.TH "KEA-ARM" "1" "Jul 09, 2026" "3.2.0" "Kea"
.SH "نام (NAME)"
kea-arm \- Kea Administrator Reference Manual Documentation
[image]
.sp
Kea is an open source implementation of the Dynamic Host Configuration
Protocol (DHCP) servers, developed and maintained by Internet Systems
Consortium (ISC).
.sp
This is the reference guide for Kea version 3.2.0\&.
Links to the most up\-to\-date version of this document (in PDF, HTML,
and plain text formats) can be found on Read the Docs \%<https://\:kea\:.readthedocs\:.io>\&.
Other useful Kea information can be found in our
Knowledgebase \%<https://\:kb\:.isc\:.org>\&.
.SH INTRODUCTION
.sp
Kea is the next generation of DHCP software, developed by Internet Systems Consortium (ISC). It
supports both the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 protocols along with their extensions,
e.g. prefix delegation and dynamic updates to DNS.
.sp
This guide covers Kea version 3.2.0\&.
.sp
For information about supported platforms see Supported Platforms\&.
.SS Supported Platforms
.sp
In general, this version of Kea builds and runs on any POSIX\-compliant
system with a C++ compiler (with C++14 support), the Botan cryptographic library,
the log4cplus logging library and the Boost system library.
.sp
ISC regularly tests Kea on many operating systems and architectures, but
lacks the resources to test all of them. Consequently, ISC is only able to
offer support on a \(dqbest\-effort\(dq basis for some.
.SS Regularly Tested Platforms
.sp
Kea is officially supported on Alpine, Debian, Fedora, FreeBSD, RHEL, and Ubuntu
systems. Kea\-3.2.0 builds have been tested on:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Alpine — 3.21, 3.22, 3.23, 3.24
.IP \(bu 2
Debian — 11, 12, 13
.IP \(bu 2
Fedora — 44
.IP \(bu 2
FreeBSD — 15
.IP \(bu 2
RHEL — 8, 9, 10
.IP \(bu 2
Ubuntu — 22.04, 24.04, 26.04
.UNINDENT
.sp
There are currently no plans to port Kea to Windows systems.
.SS Best\-Effort
.sp
The following are platforms on which Kea is known to build and run.
ISC makes every effort to fix bugs on these platforms, but may be unable to
do so quickly due to lack of hardware, less familiarity on the part of
engineering staff, and other constraints.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
macOS — 14, 15, 26
.UNINDENT
.SS Community\-Maintained
.sp
These systems have once been regularly tested, but official support for it has
been abandoned, usually due to discontinued support on their own part. Older
versions may not have the required dependencies for building Kea easily
available, although it is possible in many cases to compile on those directly
from source. The community and interested parties may wish to help with
maintenance, and we welcome patch contributions, although we cannot guarantee
that we will accept them. All contributions are assessed against the risk of
adverse effect on officially supported platforms.
.sp
These include platforms past their respective EOL dates, such as:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Alpine — 3.20 (EOL 1 Apr 2026) and older
.IP \(bu 2
Debian — 10 (EOL 30 Jun 2024) and older
.IP \(bu 2
Fedora — 43 (EOL 9 Dec 2026) and older
.IP \(bu 2
FreeBSD — 14 (EOL 31 Dec 2026) and older
.IP \(bu 2
RHEL — 7 (EOL 30 Jun 2024) and older
.IP \(bu 2
Ubuntu — 20.04 (EOL 31 May 2025) and older
.IP \(bu 2
macOS — 13 (EOL 15 Sep 2025) and older
.UNINDENT
.SS Unsupported Platforms
.sp
These are platforms on which versions of Kea since 1.7 are known \fInot\fP to build or run:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Windows (all versions)
.IP \(bu 2
Windows Server (all versions)
.IP \(bu 2
Any platform with OpenSSL 1.0.1 or earlier, which does not also have Botan as an alternative
.IP \(bu 2
Any platform with log4cplus version 1.0.2 or earlier.
.IP \(bu 2
Any platform with a compiler that doesn\(aqt support C++14.
.UNINDENT
.SS Required Software at Runtime
.sp
Kea uses various extra software packages which may not be
provided in the default installation of some operating systems, nor in
the standard package collections. This required software may need to
be installed separately. (For the build requirements, also see Build Requirements \%<#\:build-requirements>\&.)
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Kea supports two cryptographic libraries: Botan and OpenSSL. Only one
of them is required to be installed during compilation. Kea uses the
Botan library for C++ (\%<https://\:botan\:.randombit\:.net>), version 2.0 or
later; support for Botan versions earlier than 2.0 was
removed as of Kea 1.7.0. As an alternative to Botan, Kea can
use the OpenSSL cryptographic library (\%<https://\:www\:.openssl\:.org>),
version 1.0.2 or later.
.IP \(bu 2
Kea uses the log4cplus C++ logging library
(\%<https://\:sourceforge\:.net/\:p/\:log4cplus/\:wiki/\:Home>). It requires log4cplus version
1.0.3 or later.
.IP \(bu 2
Kea requires the Boost system library (\%<https://\:www\:.boost\:.org>).
Building with the header\-only version of Boost is no longer
recommended.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some optional features of Kea have additional dependencies.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
To store lease information in a MySQL database, Kea requires
MySQL headers and libraries. This is an optional dependency;
Kea can be built without MySQL support.
.IP \(bu 2
To store lease information in a PostgreSQL database, Kea
requires PostgreSQL headers and libraries. This is an optional
dependency; Kea can be built without PostgreSQL support.
.IP \(bu 2
Kea provides a NETCONF interface with the \fBkea\-netconf\fP agent. This Kea module
requires Sysrepo software when used. Building Kea with NETCONF support
requires many dependencies to be installed, which are described in more
detail in Installing NETCONF \%<#\:netconf-install>\&. This is an optional dependency; Kea can be
built without NETCONF support.
.IP \(bu 2
To sign and verify DNS updates the Kea DDNS server may use GSS\-TSIG, which requires
MIT Kerberos 5 or Heimdal libraries. The dependencies required to be
installed are described in more detail in GSS\-TSIG Compilation \%<#\:gss-tsig-install>\&. This is an
optional dependency; Kea can be built without GSS\-TSIG support.
.UNINDENT
.SS Kea Software
.sp
Kea is a modular DHCP server solution. This modularity is accomplished using multiple
cooperating processes which, together, provide the server functionality.
The following software is included with Kea:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> — This tool starts, stops, reconfigures, and reports the status of
the Kea servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> — The DHCPv4 server process. This process responds to
DHCPv4 queries from clients.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> — The DHCPv6 server process. This process responds to
DHCPv6 queries from clients.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> — The DHCP Dynamic DNS process. This process acts
as an intermediary between the DHCP servers and external DNS servers. It
receives name update requests from the DHCP servers and sends DNS
update messages to the DNS servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> — This is a useful tool for database backend maintenance
(creating a new database, checking versions, upgrading, etc.).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> — This process removes redundant information from the
files used to provide persistent storage for the memfile database
backend. While it can be run standalone, it is normally run as and
when required by the Kea DHCP servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> \- kea\-netconf is an agent that provides a
YANG/NETCONF interface for configuring Kea.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkea\-shell\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-shell> — This simple text client uses the REST interface to
connect to the Kea server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBperfdhcp\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-perfdhcp> — This is a DHCP benchmarking tool which simulates multiple
clients to test both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 server performance.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The tools and modules are covered in full detail in this guide. In
addition, manual pages are also provided in the default installation.
.sp
Kea also provides C++ libraries and programmer interfaces for DHCP.
These include detailed developer documentation and code examples.
.SH QUICK START
.sp
This section describes the basic steps needed to get Kea up and running.
For further details, full customizations, and troubleshooting, see the
respective chapters elsewhere in this Kea Administrator Reference Manual (ARM).
.SS Quick Start Guide Using tarball
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Install required runtime and build dependencies. See
Build Requirements \%<#\:build-requirements> for details.
.IP 2. 3
Download the Kea source tarball from
the main isc.org downloads page \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:download>,
the ISC downloads site \%<https://\:downloads\:.isc\:.org/\:isc/\:kea>, or
the ISC Cloudsmith page \%<https://\:cloudsmith\:.io/\:~isc/\:packages/?\:q=format%3Araw>\&.
.IP 3. 3
Extract the tarball. For example:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ tar \-Jxvf kea\-3.2.0\&.tar.xz
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 4. 3
Go into the source directory and set up the build:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ cd kea\-3.2.0
$ meson setup build [your extra parameters]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 5. 3
Build it:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson compile \-C build
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 6. 3
Install it (by default it will be placed in \fB/usr/local/\fP, so
root privileges are likely required for this step):
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson install \-C build
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Quick Start Guide Using Native Packages
.sp
ISC provides native Alpine, deb, and RPM packages, which make Kea installation
much easier than building from source. Unless specific compilation options are desired, it is usually
easier to install Kea using native packages.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Go to Kea on cloudsmith.io \%<https://\:cloudsmith\:.io/\:~isc/\:repos>\&.
.IP 2. 3
Choose the Cloudsmith repository e.g. kea\-3\-2 for Kea 3.2.0\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
All stable releases are stored in separate repositories, such as kea\-2\-6, kea\-3\-0, and so on.
All development releases, which are not recommended for production use,
are located in the kea\-dev repository.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 3. 3
Click on the arrow beside the \(dqSet Me Up\(dq button and select the desired OS flavor:
Alpine, Debian, or RedHat.
.IP 4. 3
Follow the onscreen instructions.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
For example, the Debian setup instructions for Kea 3.0 can be found here:
\%<https://\:cloudsmith\:.io/\:~isc/\:repos/\:kea-3-0/\:setup/#\:formats-deb>
.sp
The dropdown near the top of the page offers instructions for
other operating systems.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 5. 3
Kea is split into various packages. The entire list is available on the
Cloudsmith repository page under \fBPackages\fP, or it can be retrieved
using \fBapk\fP/\fBapt\fP/\fBdnf\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Debian/Ubuntu
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ apt search isc\-kea
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp4\-server\fP and \fBisc\-kea\-dhcp6\-server\fP are empty
transitional packages. The working server packages are
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp4\fP and \fBisc\-kea\-dhcp6\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Fedora/RedHat
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ dnf search \(aqisc\-kea*\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Alpine
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ apk search isc\-kea
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
.TE
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 6. 3
Install the metapackage containing all the tools, libraries, servers,
documentation, and open source hooks:
.UNINDENT
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Debian/Ubuntu
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo apt install isc\-kea
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Fedora/RedHat
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo dnf install isc\-kea
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Alpine
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# apk add isc\-kea
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
.TE
.sp
or specific packages:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Debian/Ubuntu
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo apt install isc\-kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Fedora/RedHat
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo dnf install isc\-kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Alpine
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ apk add isc\-kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
.TE
.sp
or ALL Kea\-related packages, including development headers, debug
symbols, and subscriber hooks (if available):
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Debian/Ubuntu
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo apt install \(aqisc\-kea*\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Fedora/RedHat
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo dnf install \(aqisc\-kea*\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Alpine
T}	T{
Installing packages via globbing (\fB*\fP) is not available for Alpine,
but it can be simulated with the following command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# apk search isc\-kea | sed \(aqs/\-[0\-9].*//g\(aq | xargs apk add
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
.TE
.sp
or all packages with a specified version number:
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Debian/Ubuntu
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo apt install \(aqisc\-kea*=2.4.0\-isc20230921141113\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Fedora/RedHat
T}	T{
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo dnf install \(aqisc\-kea*2.4.0\-isc20230921141113*\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
_
T{
Alpine
T}	T{
Installing packages via globbing (\fB*\fP) is not available for Alpine,
but it can be simulated with the following command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# apk search isc\-kea | sed \(aqs/\-[0\-9].*//g\(aq | grep r20230921141113 | xargs apk add
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
T}
.TE
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 8. 3
All installed packages should be now available.
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
You can start a server manually:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# kea\-dhcp6 \-c /etc/kea/kea\-dhcp6.conf
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or using systemd:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# systemctl restart kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or using OpenRC on Alpine:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# service kea\-dhcp6 restart
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> is not available in packages, as similar functionality is provided
by the native systemctl scripts.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 9. 3
On Debian/Ubuntu systems, the service is automatically enabled at boot time
when the package is installed. On Fedora/RHEL and Alpine, the service is not
automatically enabled, so if desired, it must be enabled manually.
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
With systemd on Fedora/RedHat:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# systemctl enable kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
With OpenRC on Alpine:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# rc\-update add kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Quick Start Guide Using Docker Containers
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Go to the ISC docker repository on cloudsmith.io \%<https://\:cloudsmith\:.io/\:~isc/\:repos/\:docker/\:packages>\&.
.IP 2. 3
Create an ipvlan network attached to the client\-facing host interface and
assigned to the subnet that is served by Kea.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ docker network create \-\-driver ipvlan \-\-ipv6 \-\-subnet 2001:db8::/64 \-\-opt parent=eth0 ipvlan0
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 3. 3
Pick the desired image and pull it locally.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ docker pull docker.cloudsmith.io/isc/docker/kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 4. 3
Create a container from the image. Mount the configuration volume and the
data volume if needed.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ docker create \e
    \-\-name kea\-dhcp6 \e
    \-\-network ipvlan0 \e
    \-\-volume /local/kea/config:/etc/kea \e
    \-\-volume /local/kea/data:/var/lib/kea \e
    docker.cloudsmith.io/isc/docker/kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 5. 3
Start the docker container.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ docker start kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 6. 3
To stop the docker container, run:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ docker stop kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Refer to the kea\-docker readme \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea-docker#\:user-content-docker-files-for-building-kea-containers> for more complex scenarios.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Quick Start Guide for DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 Services
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Edit the Kea configuration files, which by default are installed in
the \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/etc/kea/\(dq\fP directory. These are:
\fBkea\-dhcp4.conf\fP, \fBkea\-dhcp6.conf\fP, \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns.conf\fP, and
\fBkeactrl.conf\fP for the DHCPv4 server, DHCPv6 server,
D2, and keactrl script, respectively.
.IP 2. 3
To start the DHCPv4 server in the background, run the
following command (as root):
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# keactrl start \-s dhcp4
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Or run the following command to start the DHCPv6 server:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# keactrl start \-s dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that it is also possible to start all servers simultaneously:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# keactrl start
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 3. 3
Verify that the Kea server(s) is/are running:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# keactrl status
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A server status of \(dqinactive\(dq may indicate a configuration error.
Please check the log file (by default named
\fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea/kea\-dhcp4.log\(dq\fP,
\fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea/kea\-dhcp6.log\(dq\fP, or
\fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea/kea\-ddns.log\(dq\fP) for the details of
any errors.
.IP 4. 3
If the server has started successfully, test that it is
responding to DHCP queries and that the client receives a
configuration from the server; for example, use the ISC DHCP
client \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:download>\&.
.IP 5. 3
To stop running the server(s):
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# keactrl stop
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For system\-specific instructions, please read the
system\-specific notes \%<https://\:kb\:.isc\:.org/\:docs/\:installing-kea>,
available in the Kea section of ISC\(aqs
Knowledgebase \%<https://\:kb\:.isc\:.org/\:docs>\&.
.sp
The details of \fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> script usage can be found in Managing Kea with keactrl \%<#\:keactrl>\&.
.sp
Once Kea services are up and running, consider deploying a dashboard solution
to monitor running services. For more details, see Monitoring Kea With Stork \%<#\:stork>\&.
.SS Running the Kea Servers Directly
.sp
The Kea servers can be started directly, without the need to use
\fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> or \fBsystemctl\fP\&. To start the DHCPv4 server run the following command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# kea\-dhcp4 \-c /path/to/your/kea4/config/file.json
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Similarly, to start the DHCPv6 server, run the following command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# kea\-dhcp6 \-c /path/to/your/kea6/config/file.json
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH INSTALLATION
.SS Packages
.sp
ISC publishes native RPM, deb, and APK packages, as well as tarballs
with the source code. The packages are available in
ISC\(aqs Cloudsmith repositories \%<https://\:cloudsmith\:.io/\:~isc/\:repos>\&.
The native packages can be downloaded
and installed using the system available in a specific distribution (such
as dpkg or rpm). The Kea repository can also be added to the system,
making it easier to install updates. For details, please
go to \%<https://\:cloudsmith\:.io/\:~isc/\:repos>, choose the desired Kea version,
and then click the \(dqSet Me Up\(dq button. For detailed
instructions, please refer to this Knowledgebase article \%<https://\:kb\:.isc\:.org/\:docs/\:isc-kea-packages>\&.
.sp
ISC maintains two types of repositories: stable and development.
The stable repositories contain a single stable release (e.g.,
kea\-2\-6 or kea\-3\-0) along with all its maintenance updates.
Separate repositories were introduced to minimize the risk of
unintentionally upgrading from one stable release to another.
.sp
The development repository, kea\-dev, includes current and
future development releases, which ISC does not recommend
for production use. Packages in the kea\-dev repository are
subject to cleanup, and older versions may be removed.
.SS Installation From Cloudsmith Packages
.sp
ISC provides Kea packages for Alpine, Debian, Fedora, RHEL, and Ubuntu.
The recommended method for installing Kea on any of these systems is to
install the \fBisc\-kea\fP metapackage from the Cloudsmith repository.
This metapackage is included on all supported distros and
installs all of the services offered by the Kea software suite.
.sp
Specific Kea components
can be installed individually, with any of the following packages:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp4\fP — Kea DHCPv4 server package
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp6\fP — Kea DHCPv6 server package
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP — Kea DHCP DDNS server
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-admin\fP — Kea database administration tools
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-hooks\fP — Kea open source DHCP hooks
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Kea subscriber hook packages are not included in the \fBisc\-kea\-hooks\fP package.
For ISC customers with access to the subscriber hooks, those packages have the
\fBisc\-kea\-subscriber\-\fP prefix. For users interested in purchasing professional
support services from ISC and getting access to the subscriber hooks, please
fill out our contact form at \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:contact>\&.
.sp
Once installed, the services can be managed through the distribution\(aqs
service manager. The services are named: \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>, \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
and \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The real service names on Debian and Ubuntu use slightly different
package names, to maintain compatibility with some older scripts. A
systemd service alias is used to allow users to refer to them with shorter
names. Calling \fBsystemctl enable\fP on these services requires
the real service names, which are: \fBisc\-kea\-dhcp4\-server\fP,
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp6\-server\fP and \fBisc\-kea\-dhcp\-ddns\-server\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Caveats When Upgrading Kea Packages
.sp
To upgrade to a current version of Kea from version 2.3.2 or earlier on Debian
and Ubuntu systems, run \fBapt dist\-upgrade\fP instead of the usual \fBapt upgrade\fP\&.
Once this upgrade has been completed, it is possible to upgrade to later versions
normally using  \fBapt upgrade\fP on Debian and Ubuntu systems.
.sp
Users may notice differences in the packages distributed in Kea versions prior to
2.3.2 and those distributed with 2.3.2 and later. As a result of an overhaul of our
package design with that release, some packages were renamed or removed.
To ensure that upgrades go as smoothly as possible, pay attention to
which packages are being removed and installed by the upgrade transaction,
and ensure that all required packages are reinstalled.
.sp
Specifically, there is a possibility for the following packages to be removed
during the upgrade, depending on which packages were originally installed:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp4\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp6\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBisc\-kea\-hooks\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
To install the entire Kea software suite, simply run
\fBapt install isc\-kea\fP after upgrading, which
will install all of the relevant subpackages that make up Kea.
.sp
This upgrade path issue does not apply to RPM and Alpine systems.
.sp
Customers with ISC support contracts who experience difficulties with upgrading
are invited to open a ticket in their support queue. Other users
are encouraged to describe their situation on the kea\-users mailing list \%<https://\:lists\:.isc\:.org/\:mailman/\:listinfo/\:kea-users>
for best\-effort support from other list members.
.SS Installation Hierarchy
.sp
The following is the directory layout of the complete Kea installation.
(All directory paths are relative to the installation directory.)
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBetc/kea/\fP — configuration files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinclude/\fP — C++ development header files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlib/\fP — libraries.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlib/kea/hooks\fP — additional hook libraries.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsbin/\fP — server software and commands used by the system administrator.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshare/doc/kea/\fP — this guide, other supplementary documentation, and examples.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshare/kea/\fP — API command examples and database schema scripts.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshare/man/\fP — manual pages (online documentation).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvar/lib/kea/\fP — server identifier (DHCPv6) and lease database files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvar/log/kea/\fP \- log files.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvar/run/kea\fP \- PID file and logger lock file.
.UNINDENT
.SS Build Requirements
.sp
In addition to the runtime requirements (listed in
Required Software at Runtime \%<#\:required-software>), building Kea from source code requires
various development include headers and program development tools.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Some operating systems have split their distribution packages into a
runtime and a development package. The
development package versions, which include header files and
libraries, must be installed to build Kea from the source code.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Building from source code requires the following software installed on
the system:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The development tools meson, ninja, and pkg\-config.
.IP \(bu 2
Boost C++ libraries (\%<https://\:www\:.boost\:.org>) (at least version 1.66).
On versions before 1.69 the Boost system library must also be installed.
.IP \(bu 2
OpenSSL (at least version 1.0.2) or Botan (at least version 3.4.0).
OpenSSL version 1.1.1 or later is strongly recommended.
.IP \(bu 2
log4cplus (at least version 1.0.3) development include headers.
.IP \(bu 2
A C++ compiler (with C++14 support) and standard development headers.
.IP \(bu 2
The MySQL client and the client development libraries, when using the
\fB\-D mysql=enabled\fP configuration flag to build the Kea MySQL database
backend. In this case, an instance of the MySQL server running locally
or on a machine reachable over a network is required. Note that running
the unit tests requires a local MySQL server.
.IP \(bu 2
The PostgreSQL client and the client development libraries, when using the
\fB\-D postgresql=enabled\fP configuration flag to build the Kea PostgreSQL database
backend. In this case an instance of the PostgreSQL server running locally
or on a machine reachable over a network is required. Note that running
the unit tests requires a local PostgreSQL server.
.IP \(bu 2
Sysrepo v1.4.140 and libyang v1.0.240, needed to connect to a Sysrepo
datastore. Earlier versions are no longer supported. When compiling from
sources, the configure switch that can be used is \fB\-D netconf=enabled\fP\&.
If these dependencies were installed in custom paths, point PKG_CONFIG_PATH
to them.
.IP \(bu 2
The MIT Kerberos 5 or Heimdal libraries are needed by Kea DDNS server to
sign and verify DNS updates using GSS\-TSIG. The configuration switch which
enables this functionality is \fB\-D krb5=enabled\fP\&. If this dependency is installed
in a custom path, point PKG_CONFIG_PATH to the .pc file or PATH to the
krb5\-config tool.
.IP \(bu 2
googletest (version 1.8 or later) is required when using the \fB\-D tests=enabled\fP
configuration option to build the unit tests.
.IP \(bu 2
The documentation generation tools Sphinx \%<https://\:www\:.sphinx-doc\:.org>,
texlive with its extensions, and Doxygen, to create the documentation.
Specifically, with Fedora, \fBpython3\-sphinx\fP, \fBpython3\-sphinx_rtd_theme\fP,
\fBtexlive\fP, and \fBtexlive\-collection\-latexextra\fP are necessary.
With Ubuntu, \fBpython3\-sphinx\fP, \fBpython3\-sphinx\-rtd\-theme\fP,
\fBtexlive\fP, and \fBtexlive\-latex\-extra\fP are needed.
If LaTeX packages are missing, Kea skips PDF generation and produces only
HTML documents.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Visit ISC\(aqs Knowledgebase at \%<https://\:kb\:.isc\:.org/\:docs/\:installing-kea> for
system\-specific installation tips.
.SS Installation From Source
.sp
Although Kea may be available in pre\-compiled, ready\-to\-use packages
from operating system vendors, it is open source software written in
C++. As such, it is freely available in source code form from ISC as a
downloadable tar file. The source code can also be obtained from the Kea
GitLab repository at \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea>\&. This
section describes how to build Kea from the source code.
.SS Download Tar File
.sp
The Kea release tarballs may be downloaded from:
\%<https://\:downloads\:.isc\:.org/\:isc/\:kea>\&.
.SS Verify The Tar File Signature
.sp
The tar file with the source code is distributed together with its
GPG signature. The signature is a file with the same name as the tar
file appended by the \fB\&.asc\fP extension. You can find the signature
file on our download page, FTP, or CloudSmith.
.sp
The signature is created using the ISC code\-signing key. The current
set of ISC code\-signing keys is available from the ISC website at
\%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:pgpkey> (the Current Set of ISC Code\-Signing Keys \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:docs/\:isc-keyblock\:.asc> link).
.sp
The signature can be verified using the GnuPGP \fBgpg\fP tool. The
following commands import the code\-signing keys
(\fBisc\-keyblock.asc\fP) and verify the signature:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ gpg \-\-import isc\-keyblock.asc
$ gpg \-\-verify kea\-X.Y.Z.tar.xz.asc kea\-X.Y.Z.tar.xz
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The verification allows users to confirm that the tar file has not
been tampered with and that it was created by ISC.
.SS Retrieve From Git
.sp
The latest development code is available on GitLab (see
\%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea>). The Kea source is public and
development is done in the “master” branch.
.sp
Downloading this \(dqbleeding edge\(dq code is recommended only for developers
or advanced users. Using development code in a production environment is
not recommended.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When building from source code retrieved via git, additional software
is required: automake (v1.11 or later), libtoolize, and autoconf
(v2.69 or later). These may need to be installed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The code can be checked out from
\fBhttps://gitlab.isc.org/isc\-projects/kea.git\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ git clone https://gitlab.isc.org/isc\-projects/kea.git
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The code checked out from the git repository does not include the build files.
They can be created by running \fBmeson setup build\fP\&.
.sp
Write access to the Kea repository is only granted to ISC staff.
Developers planning to contribute to Kea should check our
Contributor\(aqs
Guide \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea/\:blob/\:master/\:contributors-guide\:.md>\&.
The Kea Developer\(aqs
Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide> contains more
information about the process, and describes the requirements for
contributed code to be accepted by ISC.
.SS Set Up the Build
.sp
Kea uses Meson to discover build environment details. To generate the ninja
file using the defaults, simply run:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson setup build
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Run \fBmeson configure\fP to view the different build options.
Some commonly used options are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-prefix\fP
Define the installation location (the default is \fB/usr/local\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-D mysql=enabled\fP
Build Kea with code to allow it to store leases and host reservations
in a MySQL database.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-D postgresql=enabled\fP
Build Kea with code to allow it to store leases and host reservations
in a PostgreSQL database.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-D krb5=enabled\fP
Build Kea with Kerberos5 with GSS\-API support required by
\fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libddns_gss_tsig\:.so>\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-D netconf=enabled\fP
Build Kea with libyang and sysrepo support required by
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-D crypto=botan\fP
Use the Botan cryptographic library instead of OpenSSL.
Meson searches the \(aqbotan\-3\(aq and \(aqbotan\(aq pkg\-config library.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-D crypto=openssl\fP
Use the OpenSSL cryptographic library. By default the value of
the \(aqcrypto\(aq option is \(aqopenssl\(aq.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
For instructions concerning the installation and configuration of
database backends for Kea, see DHCP Database Installation and Configuration\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If \fBmeson setup build\fP fails, it may be due to missing or old dependencies.
.sp
When \fBmeson setup build\fP succeeds, it displays a report with the parameters
used to build the code. This report is saved into the file
\fBbuild/config.report\fP and is also embedded into the executable binaries, e.g.
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>\&.
.SS Build
.sp
After the setup step is complete, build the executables from the C++
code and prepare the Python scripts by running the command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson compile \-C build
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Install
.sp
To install the Kea executables, support files, and documentation, issue
the command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson install \-C build
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The install step may require superuser privileges.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It should not be required, but if shared libraries are not found at runtime,
you can run \fBldconfig\fP as root with \fB/usr/local/lib\fP (or with
\fB${prefix}/lib\fP if set up with \fB\-\-prefix\fP) in \fB/etc/ld.so.conf\fP (or
the relevant linker cache configuration file for the OS):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ldconfig
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If \fBldconfig\fP is not run where required, users may see
errors like the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
program: error while loading shared libraries: libkea\-something.so.1:
cannot open shared object file: No such file or directory
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DHCP Database Installation and Configuration
.sp
Kea stores its leases in a lease database. The software has been written
in a way that makes it possible to choose which database product should
be used to store the lease information. Kea supports three
database backends: MySQL, PostgreSQL, and memfile. To limit external
dependencies, MySQL and PostgreSQL support are disabled by default and only
memfile is available. Support for the optional external database backend must
be explicitly included when Kea is built.
This section covers the building of Kea with one of the optional backends and
the creation of the lease database.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When unit tests are built with Kea (i.e. the \fB\-D tests=enabled\fP configuration
option is specified), the databases must be manually pre\-configured
for the unit tests to run. The details of this configuration can be
found in the Kea Developer\(aqs
Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Building with MySQL Support
.sp
Install MySQL according to the instructions for the system. The client
development libraries must be installed.
.sp
Build and install Kea as described in Installation,
with the following modification. To enable the MySQL database code, at the
setup step (see Set Up the Build), the \fB\-D mysql=enabled\fP switch should be specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson setup build \-D mysql=enabled
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If MySQL was not installed in the default location, the location can be
selected by setting \fBPKG_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/mariadb.pc:${PKG_CONFIG_PATH}\fP
or \fBPATH=/path/to/mariadb\-config:${PATH}\fP prior to \fBmeson setup\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/opt/mariadb/lib/pkgconfig PATH=/opt/mariadb/bin meson setup build \-D mysql=enabled
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
See First\-Time Creation of the MySQL Database \%<#\:mysql-database-create> for details regarding MySQL
database configuration.
.SS Building with PostgreSQL Support
.sp
Install PostgreSQL according to the instructions for the system. The
client development libraries must be installed. Client development
libraries are often packaged as \fBlibpq\fP\&.
.sp
Build and install Kea as described in Installation,
with the following modification. To enable the PostgreSQL database code, at the
setup step (see Set Up the Build), the \fB\-D postgresql=enabled\fP switch should be specified.
.sp
If PostgreSQL was not installed in the default location, the location can be
selected by setting \fBPKG_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/libpq.pc:${PKG_CONFIG_PATH}\fP
or \fBPATH=/path/to/pg_config:${PATH}\fP prior to \fBmeson setup\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ PKG_CONFIG_PATH=/opt/postgresql/lib/pkgconfig PATH=/opt/postgresql/bin meson setup build \-D postgresql=enabled
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
See First\-Time Creation of the PostgreSQL Database \%<#\:pgsql-database-create> for details regarding PostgreSQL
database configuration.
.SS Hammer Building Tool
.sp
Hammer is a Python 3 script that lets users automate tasks related to building
Kea, such as setting up virtual machines, installing Kea dependencies,
compiling Kea with various options, running unit\-tests and more. This
tool was created primarily for internal QA purposes at ISC and it is not
included in the Kea distribution; however, it is available in the Kea
git repository. This tool was developed primarily for internal purposes
and ISC cannot guarantee its proper operation. Administrators who decide to use it
should do so with care.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Use of this tool is completely optional. Everything it does can be
done manually.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The first\-time user is strongly encouraged to look at Hammer\(aqs built\-in
help:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py \-\-help
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It will list available parameters.
.sp
Hammer is able to set up various operating systems running either in LXC
or in VirtualBox. For a list of supported systems, use the
\fBsupported\-systems\fP command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py supported\-systems
fedora:
  \- 37: lxc
  \- 38:
centos:
  \- 8: lxc, virtualbox
  \- 9:
rhel:
  \- 8: virtualbox
  \- 9:
ubuntu:
  \- 18.04: lxc, virtualbox
  \- 20.04: lxc
  \- 22.04: lxc
debian:
  \- 10: lxc, virtualbox
  \- 11: lxc
  \- 12: lxc
freebsd:
  \- 12.0: virtualbox
  \- 12.1:
  \- 13.0: virtualbox
alpine:
  \- 3.15: lxc
  \- 3.16: lxc
  \- 3.17: lxc
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is also possible to run the build locally, in the current system (if the OS
is supported).
.sp
First, the Hammer dependencies must be installed: Vagrant
and either VirtualBox or LXC. Hammer can install
Vagrant and the required Vagrant plugins using the command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py ensure\-hammer\-deps
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
VirtualBox and LXC must be installed manually.
.sp
The basic functions provided by Hammer are to prepare the build environment
and perform the actual build, and to run the unit tests locally in the current
system. This can be achieved by running the command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py build \-p local
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The scope of the process can be defined using the \fB\-\-with\fP (\fB\-w\fP) and \fB\-\-without\fP
(\fB\-x\fP) options. By default, the \fBbuild\fP command builds Kea with
documentation, installs it locally, and runs unit tests.
.sp
To exclude the installation and generation of docs, type:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py build \-p local \-x install docs
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The basic scope can be extended by mysql, pgsql, native\-pkg, shell, and forge.
Please refer to \fB\&./hammer.py build \-\-help\fP for more details.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If building Kea locally, Hammer dependencies like Vagrant are
not needed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Hammer can be told to set up a new virtual machine with a specified
operating system, without the build:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py prepare\-system \-p virtualbox \-s freebsd \-r 12.0
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This way, a system can be prepared for our own use.
.sp
To prepare such a system using SSH, invoke:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py ssh \-p virtualbox \-s freebsd \-r 12.0
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is possible to speed up subsequent Hammer builds via
ccache \%<https://\:ccache\:.samba\:.org>\&. During
compilation, ccache stores objects in a shared folder. In subsequent runs,
instead of doing an actual compilation, ccache returns the stored earlier
objects. The cache with these objects for reuse must be stored outside of VM
or LXC. To indicate the folder, the \fB\-\-ccache\-dir\fP
parameter for Hammer must be included. In the indicated folder, there are separate stored objects for each target
operating system.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py build \-p lxc \-s ubuntu \-r 18.04 \-\-ccache\-dir ~/kea\-ccache
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
ccache is currently only supported for LXC in Hammer; support
for VirtualBox may be added later.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information check:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./hammer.py \-\-help
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Running Kea From a Non\-root Account on Linux
.sp
Both Kea DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 servers perform operations that in general require root access
privileges. In particular, DHCPv4 opens raw sockets and both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 open UDP sockets on
privileged ports. However, with some extra system configuration, it is possible to run Kea from
non\-root accounts.
.sp
First, a regular user account must be created:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
useradd admin
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then, change the binaries\(aq ownership and group to the new user. Note that
the specific path may be different. Please refer to the \fB\-\-prefix\fP
parameter passed to \fBmeson setup\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
chown \-R admin /opt/kea
chgrp \-R admin /opt/kea
chown \-R admin /var/log/kea/kea\-dhcp4.log
chgrp \-R admin /var/log/kea/kea\-dhcp4.log
chown \-R admin /var/log/kea/kea\-dhcp6.log
chgrp \-R admin /var/log/kea/kea\-dhcp6.log
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If using systemd, modify its service file
(e.g. /etc/systemd/system/kea\-dhcp6.service):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
User=admin
Group=admin
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The most important step is to set the capabilities of the binaries. Refer to the operating system man page
for \fIcapabilities\fP for more information.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
setcap \(aqcap_net_bind_service,cap_net_raw=+ep\(aq /opt/kea/sbin/kea\-dhcp4
setcap \(aqcap_net_bind_service=+ep\(aq /opt/kea/sbin/kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If using systemd, also add this to the service file
(e.g. /etc/systemd/system/kea\-dhcp6.service):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ExecStartPre=setcap \(aqcap_net_bind_service=+ep\(aq /opt/kea/sbin/kea\-dhcp6
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After this step is complete, the admin user should be able to run Kea. Note that the DHCPv4 server by
default opens raw sockets. If the network is only using relayed traffic, Kea can be instructed to
use regular UDP sockets (refer to \fBdhcp\-socket\-type\fP parameter in the
Interface Configuration \%<#\:dhcp4-interface-configuration> section) and the \fBcap_net_raw\fP capability can be skipped.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is possible to avoid running Kea with root privileges by instructing Kea to
use non\-privileged (greater than 1024) ports and redirecting traffic. This, however, only works
for relayed traffic. This approach in general is considered experimental and has not been tested
for deployment in production environments. Use with caution!
.sp
To use this approach, configure the server to listen on other non\-privileged ports (e.g. 1547
and 1548) by running the process with the \fB\-p\fP option in \fB/etc/systemd/system/kea\-dhcp4.service\fP:
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ExecStart=/opt/kea/sbin/kea\-dhcp4 \-d \-c /etc/kea/kea\-dhcp4.conf \-p 2067
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
and \fB/etc/systemd/system/kea\-dhcp4.service\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ExecStart=/opt/kea/sbin/kea\-dhcp6 \-d \-c /etc/kea/kea\-dhcp6.conf \-p 1547
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then configure port redirection with iptables and ip6tables for new ports (e.g. 1547
and 1548). Be sure to replace \fBens4\fP with the specific interface name.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
iptables \-t nat \-A PREROUTING \-i ens4 \-p udp \-\-dport 67 \-j REDIRECT \-\-to\-port 2067
iptables \-t nat \-A PREROUTING \-i ens4 \-p udp \-\-dport 2068 \-j REDIRECT \-\-to\-port 68
ip6tables \-t nat \-A PREROUTING \-i ens4 \-p udp \-\-dport 547 \-j REDIRECT \-\-to\-port 1547
ip6tables \-t nat \-A PREROUTING \-i ens4 \-p udp \-\-dport 1548 \-j REDIRECT \-\-to\-port 548
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Deprecated Features
.sp
This section lists significant features that have been or will be removed. We try to
deprecate features before removing them, to signal
to current users to plan a migration. New users should not rely on deprecated features.
.SS Sysrepo 0.x or 1.x
.sp
Kea 2.3.2 introduced support for Sysrepo 2.x. Unfortunately,
Sysrepo continues to undergo major changes that are backward\-incompatible,
and current Kea versions do not support Sysrepo earlier than versions 2.x.
.SH KEA DATABASE ADMINISTRATION
.SS Databases and Schema Versions
.sp
Kea may be configured to use a database as storage for leases or as a
source of servers\(aq configurations and host reservations (i.e. static
assignments of addresses, prefixes, options, etc.). As Kea is
updated, new database schemas are introduced to facilitate new
features and correct discovered issues with the existing schemas.
.sp
Each version of Kea expects a particular schema structure and checks for this by
examining the version of the database it is using. Separate version numbers are
maintained for the schemas, independent of the version of Kea itself. It is
possible that the schema version will stay the same through several Kea
revisions; similarly, it is possible that the version of the schema may go up
several revisions during a single Kea version upgrade. Versions for each backend
type are also independent, so an increment in the MySQL backend version does not
imply an increment in that of PostgreSQL.
.sp
Schema versions are specified in a major.minor format. For the most recent
versions, the minor version is always zero and only the major version is
incremented.
.sp
Historically, the minor version used to be incremented when backward\-compatible
changes were introduced to the schema: for example \- when a new index is added.
This was opposed to incrementing the major version which implied an incompatible
schema change: for example \- changing the type of an existing column. If Kea
attempts to run on a schema that is too old, as indicated by a mismatched schema
version, it will fail; administrative action is required to upgrade the schema.
.SS The \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> Tool
.sp
To manage the databases, Kea provides the \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> tool. It can
initialize a new backend, check its version number, perform a backend
upgrade, and dump lease data to a text file.
.sp
\fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> takes two mandatory parameters: \fBcommand\fP and
\fBbackend\fP\&. Additional, non\-mandatory options may be specified. The
currently supported commands are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdb\-init\fP — initializes a new database schema, which is useful
during a new Kea installation. The new database is updated to
match the Kea version being installed. \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> is
automatically invoked with this command if a missing schema is
detected during startup or reconfiguration of Kea DHCP servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdb\-version\fP — reports the database backend version number. This
is not necessarily equal to the Kea version number, as each backend
has its own versioning scheme.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdb\-upgrade\fP — conducts a database schema upgrade. This is
useful when upgrading Kea.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease\-dump\fP — dumps the contents of the lease database (for MySQL or
PostgreSQL backends) to a CSV (comma\-separated values) text file.
.sp
The first line of the file contains the column names. This can be used
as a way to switch from a database backend to a memfile backend.
Alternatively, it can be used as a diagnostic tool, so it provides a portable
form of the lease data.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease\-upload\fP — uploads leases from a CSV (comma\-separated values) text
file to a MySQL or a PostgreSQL lease database. The CSV file needs to be in
memfile format.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBbackend\fP specifies the type of backend database. The currently
supported types are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmemfile\fP — lease information is stored on disk in a text file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmysql\fP — information is stored in a MySQL relational database.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpgsql\fP — information is stored in a PostgreSQL relational
database.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Additional parameters may be needed, depending on the setup and
specific operation: username, password, and database name or the
directory where specific files are located. See the appropriate manual
page for details (\fBman 8 kea\-admin\fP).
.SS Supported Backends
.sp
The following table presents the capabilities of available backends.
Please refer to the specific sections dedicated to each backend to
better understand their capabilities and limitations. Choosing the right
backend is essential for the success of the deployment.
.SS List of available backends
.TS
box center;
