.TE
.SS Memfile
.sp
The memfile backend is able to store lease information, but cannot
store host reservation details; these must be stored in the
configuration file. (There are no plans to add a host reservations
storage capability to this backend.)
.sp
No special initialization steps are necessary for the memfile backend.
During the first run, both \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> create
an empty lease file if one is not present. Necessary disk\-write
permission is required.
.SS Upgrading Memfile Lease Files From an Earlier Version of Kea
.sp
There are no special steps required to upgrade memfile lease files
between versions of Kea. During startup, the
servers check the schema version of the lease files against their
own. If there is a mismatch, the servers automatically launch the
LFC process to convert the files to the server\(aqs schema version. While
this mechanism is primarily meant to ease the process of upgrading to
newer versions of Kea, it can also be used for downgrading should the
need arise. When upgrading, any values not present in the original lease
files are assigned appropriate default values. When downgrading, any
data present in the files but not in the server\(aqs schema are
dropped. To convert the files manually prior to starting the
servers, run the lease file cleanup (LFC) process. See The LFC Process \%<#\:kea-lfc> for more information.
.SS MySQL
.sp
MySQL is able to store leases, host reservations, options defined on a
per\-host basis, and a subset of the server configuration parameters
(serving as a configuration backend).
.SS MySQL 5.7 vs MySQL 8 vs MariaDB 10 and 11
.sp
In our Kea performance testing, MySQL 8 shows a 60\-90% drop in speed
in comparison with MySQL 5.7.
Due to the upcoming MySQL 5.7 EOL, we recommend using MariaDB instead of MySQL 8.
.sp
MySQL 5.7, MySQL 8, MariaDB 10, and MariaDB 11 are fully compatible,
interchangeable, and tested with Kea.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The MySQL or MariaDB variant you use must support JSON processing. Those listed
above or newer should. This can be verified with the following SQL statement:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> SELECT JSON_EXTRACT(\(aq{ \(dqfoo\-bar\(dq: 1 }\(aq, \(aq$.\(dqfoo\-bar\(dq\(aq);
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| JSON_EXTRACT(\(aq{ \(dqfoo\-bar\(dq: 1 }\(aq, \(aq$.\(dqfoo\-bar\(dq\(aq) |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| 1                                               |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
1 row in set (0.01 sec)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Any other result indicates JSON is not supported.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS First\-Time Creation of the MySQL Database
.sp
Before preparing any Kea\-specific database and tables, the MySQL database
must be configured to use the system timezone. It is recommended to use UTC
as the timezone for both the system and the MySQL database.
.sp
To check the system timezone:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
date +%Z
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To check the MySQL timezone:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> SELECT @@system_time_zone;
mysql> SELECT @@global.time_zone;
mysql> SELECT @@session.time_zone;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To configure the MySQL timezone for a specific server, please refer to the
installed version documentation.
.sp
Usually the setting is configured in the [mysqld] section in \fB/etc/mysql/my.cnf\fP,
\fB/etc/mysql/mysql.cnf\fP, \fB/etc/mysql/mysqld.cnf\fP, or
\fB/etc/mysql/mysql.conf.d/mysqld.cnf\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[mysqld]
# using default\-time\-zone
default\-time\-zone=\(aq+00:00\(aq

# or using timezone
timezone=\(aqUTC\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When setting up the MySQL database for the first time, the
database area must be created within MySQL, and the MySQL user ID under
which Kea will access the database must be set up. This needs to be done manually,
rather than via \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin>\&.
.sp
To create the database:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log into MySQL as \(dqroot\(dq:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ mysql \-u root \-p
Enter password:
mysql>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 2. 3
Create the MySQL database:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> CREATE DATABASE database_name;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(\fBdatabase_name\fP is the name chosen for the database.)
.IP 3. 3
Create the user under which Kea will access the database (and give it
a password), then grant it access to the database tables:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> CREATE USER \(aquser\-name\(aq@\(aqlocalhost\(aq IDENTIFIED BY \(aq1234\(aq;
mysql> GRANT ALL ON database\-name.* TO \(aquser\-name\(aq@\(aqlocalhost\(aq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(\fBuser\-name\fP and \fBpassword\fP are the user ID and password used to
allow Kea access to the MySQL instance. All apostrophes in the
command lines above are required.)
.IP 4. 3
Create the database.
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
Exit the MySQL client
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> quit
Bye
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then use the  \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> tool to create the database.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-admin db\-init mysql \-u database\-user \-p database\-password \-n database\-name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
While it is possible to create the database from within the MySQL client, we recommend
using the \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> tool as it performs some necessary validations to ensure Kea can
access the database at runtime. Among those checks is verification that the schema does not contain
any pre\-existing tables; any pre\-existing tables must be removed
manually. An additional check examines the user\(aqs ability to create functions and
triggers. The following error indicates that the user does not have the necessary
permissions to create functions or triggers:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ERROR 1419 (HY000) at line 1: You do not have the SUPER privilege and binary logging is
enabled (you *might* want to use the less safe log_bin_trust_function_creators variable)
ERROR/kea\-admin: mysql_can_create cannot trigger, check user permissions, mysql status = 1
mysql: [Warning] Using a password on the command line interface can be insecure.
ERROR/kea\-admin: Create failed, the user, keatest, has insufficient privileges.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The simplest way around this is to set the global MySQL variable,
\fBlog_bin_trust_function_creators\fP, to 1 via the MySQL client.
Note this must be done as a user with SUPER privileges:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> set @@global.log_bin_trust_function_creators = 1;
Query OK, 0 rows affected (0.00 sec)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To create the database with MySQL directly, follow these steps:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> CONNECT database\-name;
mysql> SOURCE path\-to\-kea/share/kea/scripts/mysql/dhcpdb_create.mysql
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(where \fBpath\-to\-kea\fP is the location where Kea is installed.)
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
The database may also be dropped manually as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> CONNECT database\-name;
mysql> SOURCE path\-to\-kea/share/kea/scripts/mysql/dhcpdb_drop.mysql
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(where \fBpath\-to\-kea\fP is the location where Kea is installed.)
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Dropping the database results in the unrecoverable loss of any data it contains.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 5. 3
Exit MySQL:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> quit
Bye
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the tables were not created in Step 4, run the \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> tool
to create them now:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-admin db\-init mysql \-u database\-user \-p database\-password \-n database\-name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Do not do this if the tables were created in Step 4. \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin>
implements rudimentary checks; it will refuse to initialize a database
that contains any existing tables. To start from scratch,
all data must be removed manually. (This process is a manual operation
on purpose, to avoid accidentally irretrievable mistakes by \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin>\&.)
.SS Upgrading a MySQL Database From an Earlier Version of Kea
.sp
Sometimes a new Kea version uses a newer database schema, so the
existing database needs to be upgraded. This can be done using the
\fBkea\-admin db\-upgrade\fP command.
.sp
To check the current version of the database, use the following command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-admin db\-version mysql \-u database\-user \-p database\-password \-n database\-name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(See Databases and Schema Versions
for a discussion about versioning.) If the version does not match the
minimum required for the new version of Kea (as described in the release
notes), the database needs to be upgraded.
.sp
Before upgrading, please make sure that the database is backed up. The
upgrade process does not discard any data, but depending on the nature
of the changes, it may be impossible to subsequently downgrade to an
earlier version.
.sp
To perform an upgrade, issue the following command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-admin db\-upgrade mysql \-u database\-user \-p database\-password \-n database\-name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
To search host reservations by hostname, it is critical that the collation of
the hostname column in the host table be case\-insensitive. Fortunately, that
is the default in MySQL, but it can be verified via this command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> SELECT COLLATION(\(aq\(aq);
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| COLLATION(\(aq\(aq)   |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| utf8_general_ci |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
According to mysql\(aqs naming convention, when the name ends in \fB_ci\fP,
the collation is case\-insensitive.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Improved Performance With MySQL
.sp
Changing the MySQL internal value \fBinnodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit\fP from the default value
of 1 to 2 can result in a huge gain in Kea performance. In some deployments, the
gain was over 1000% (10 times faster when set to 2, compared to the default value of 1).
It can be set per\-session for testing:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
mysql> SET GLOBAL innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=2;
mysql> SHOW SESSION VARIABLES LIKE \(aqinnodb_flush_log%\(aq;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or permanently in \fB/etc/mysql/my.cnf\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[mysqld]
innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=2
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Be aware that changing this value can cause problems during data recovery
after a crash, so we recommend checking the MySQL documentation \%<https://\:dev\:.mysql\:.com/\:doc/\:refman/\:8\:.0/\:en/\:innodb-parameters\:.html#\:sysvar_innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit>\&.
With the default value of 1, MySQL writes changes to disk after every INSERT or UPDATE query
(in Kea terms, every time a client gets a new lease or renews an existing lease). When
\fBinnodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit\fP is set to 2, MySQL writes the changes at intervals
no longer than 1 second. Batching writes gives a substantial performance boost. The trade\-off,
however, is that in the worst\-case scenario, all changes in the last second before crash
could be lost. Given the fact that Kea is stable software and crashes very rarely,
most deployments find it a beneficial trade\-off.
.SS PostgreSQL
.sp
PostgreSQL can store leases, host reservations, and options
defined on a per\-host basis.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The PostgreSQL version you use must support JSON processing. Any version
beginning with 9.4 should support it. This can be verified with the
following SQL statement:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
=> SELECT proname FROM pg_proc WHERE proname = \(aqjson_extract_path\(aq;
    proname
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
json_extract_path
(1 row)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Any other result indicates JSON is not supported.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS First\-Time Creation of the PostgreSQL Database
.sp
Before preparing any Kea\-specific database and tables, the PostgreSQL database
must be configured to use the system timezone. It is recommended to use UTC
as the timezone for both the system and the PostgreSQL database.
.sp
To check the system timezone:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
date +%Z
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To check the PostgreSQL timezone:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# show timezone;
postgres=# SELECT * FROM pg_timezone_names WHERE name = current_setting(\(aqTIMEZONE\(aq);
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To configure the PostgreSQL timezone for a specific server, please refer to the
installed version documentation.
.sp
Usually the setting is configured in the \fBpostgresql.conf\fP with the varying
version path \fB/etc/postgresql/<version>/main/postgresql.conf\fP, but on some systems
the files may be located in \fB/var/lib/pgsql/data\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
timezone = \(aqUTC\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The first task is to create both the database and the user under
which the servers will access it. A number of steps are required:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Log into PostgreSQL as \(dqpostgres\(dq:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo \-u postgres psql postgres
Enter password:
postgres=#
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 2. 3
Create the database:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# CREATE DATABASE database\-name;
CREATE DATABASE
postgres=#
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(\fBdatabase\-name\fP is the name chosen for the database.)
.IP 3. 3
Create the user under which Kea will access the database (and give it
a password), then grant it access to the database:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# CREATE USER user\-name WITH PASSWORD \(aq1234\(aq;
CREATE ROLE
postgres=# GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE database\-name TO user\-name;
GRANT
postgres=# \ec database\-name
You are now connected to database \(dqdatabase\-name\(dq as user \(dqpostgres\(dq.
postgres=# GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON SCHEMA public TO user\-name;
GRANT
postgres=#
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 4. 3
Exit PostgreSQL:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# \eq
Bye
$
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 5. 3
At this point, create the database tables either
using the \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> tool, as explained in the next section
(recommended), or manually. To create the tables manually, enter the
following command. PostgreSQL will prompt the administrator to enter the
new user\(aqs password that was specified in Step 3. When the command
completes, Kea will return to the shell prompt. The
output should be similar to the following:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ psql \-d database\-name \-U user\-name \-f path\-to\-kea/share/kea/scripts/pgsql/dhcpdb_create.pgsql
Password for user user\-name:
CREATE TABLE
CREATE INDEX
CREATE INDEX
CREATE TABLE
CREATE INDEX
CREATE TABLE
START TRANSACTION
INSERT 0 1
INSERT 0 1
INSERT 0 1
COMMIT
CREATE TABLE
START TRANSACTION
INSERT 0 1
COMMIT
$
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
(\fBpath\-to\-kea\fP is the location where Kea is installed.)
.sp
If instead an error is encountered, such as:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
psql: FATAL:  no pg_hba.conf entry for host \(dq[local]\(dq, user \(dquser\-name\(dq, database \(dqdatabase\-name\(dq, SSL off
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\&... the PostgreSQL configuration will need to be altered. Kea uses
password authentication when connecting to the database and must have
the appropriate entries added to PostgreSQL\(aqs pg_hba.conf file. This
file is normally located in the primary data directory for the
PostgreSQL server. The precise path may vary depending on the
operating system and version, but the default location for PostgreSQL is
\fB/etc/postgresql/*/main/postgresql.conf\fP\&. However, on some systems, the
file may reside in \fB/var/lib/pgsql/data\fP\&.
.sp
Assuming Kea is running on the same host as PostgreSQL, adding lines
similar to the following should be sufficient to provide
password\-authenticated access to Kea\(aqs database:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
local   database\-name    user\-name                                 password
host    database\-name    user\-name          127.0.0.1/32           password
host    database\-name    user\-name          ::1/128                password
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
These edits are primarily intended as a starting point, and are not a
definitive reference on PostgreSQL administration or database
security. Please consult the PostgreSQL user manual before making
these changes, as they may expose other databases that are running. It
may be necessary to restart PostgreSQL for the changes to
take effect.
.UNINDENT
.SS Initialize the PostgreSQL Database Using \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin>
.sp
If the tables were not created manually, do so now by
running the \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin> tool:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-admin db\-init pgsql \-u database\-user \-p database\-password \-n database\-name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Do not do this if the tables were already created manually. \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin>
implements rudimentary checks; it will refuse to initialize a database
that contains any existing tables. To start from scratch,
all data must be removed manually. (This process is a manual operation
on purpose, to avoid accidentally irretrievable mistakes by \fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin>\&.)
.SS Upgrading a PostgreSQL Engine From an Earlier Version
.sp
If you upgraded your PostgreSQL from a version prior to 15.0, you need to
grant additional privileges to the user:
.sp
First, log into PostgreSQL as \(dqpostgres\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo \-u postgres psql \-d database\-name \-U postgres
Enter password:
postgres=#
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Next, grant the access to the \fBpublic\fP schema.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON SCHEMA public TO user\-name;
GRANT
postgres=#
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Now, quit the PostgreSQL client:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# \eq
Bye
$
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Upgrading a PostgreSQL Database From an Earlier Version of Kea
.sp
The PostgreSQL database schema can be upgraded using the same tool and
commands as described in Upgrading a MySQL Database From an Earlier Version of Kea, with the exception that the \(dqpgsql\(dq
database backend type must be used in the commands.
.sp
Use the following command to check the current schema version:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-admin db\-version pgsql \-u database\-user \-p database\-password \-n database\-name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Use the following command to perform an upgrade:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-admin db\-upgrade pgsql \-u database\-user \-p database\-password \-n database\-name
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Improved Performance With PostgreSQL
.sp
Changing the PostgreSQL internal value \fBsynchronous_commit\fP from the default value
of ON to OFF can result in significant gains in Kea performance; on slow systems, the gain
can be over 1000%. It can be set per\-session for testing:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# SET synchronous_commit = OFF;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or permanently via command (preferred method):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
postgres=# ALTER SYSTEM SET synchronous_commit=OFF;
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or permanently in \fB/etc/postgresql/[version]/main/postgresql.conf\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
synchronous_commit = off
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Changing this value can cause problems during data recovery
after a crash, so we recommend a careful read of the PostgreSQL documentation \%<https://\:www\:.postgresql\:.org/\:docs/\:current/\:wal-async-commit\:.html>\&.
With the default value of ON, PostgreSQL writes changes to disk after every INSERT or UPDATE query
(in Kea terms, every time a client gets a new lease or renews an existing lease). When
\fBsynchronous_commit\fP is set to OFF, PostgreSQL adds some delay before writing the changes.
Batching writes gives a substantial performance boost,
but in the worst\-case scenario, all changes in the last moment before a crash
could be lost. Since Kea is stable software and crashes very rarely,
most deployments find the performance benefits outweigh the potential risks.
.SS Using Read\-Only Databases With Host Reservations
.sp
If a read\-only database is used for storing host reservations, Kea must
be explicitly configured to operate on the database in read\-only mode.
Sections Using Read\-Only Databases for Host Reservations With DHCPv4 \%<#\:read-only-database-configuration4> and
Using Read\-Only Databases for Host Reservations with DHCPv6 \%<#\:read-only-database-configuration6> describe when such
a configuration may be required, and how to configure Kea to operate in
this way for both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6.
.SS Limitations Related to the Use of SQL Databases
.SS Year 2038 Issue
.sp
The lease expiration time in Kea is stored in the SQL database for each lease
as a timestamp value. Kea developers have observed that the MySQL database
does not accept timestamps beyond 2147483647 seconds (the maximum signed
32\-bit number) from the beginning of the UNIX epoch (00:00:00 on 1
January 1970). Some versions of PostgreSQL do accept greater values, but
the value is altered when it is read back. For this reason, the lease
database backends put a restriction on the maximum timestamp to be
stored in the database, which is equal to the maximum signed 32\-bit
number. This effectively means that the current Kea version cannot store
leases whose expiration time is later than 2147483647 seconds since the
beginning of the epoch (around the year 2038). This will be fixed when
database support for longer timestamps is available.
.SH KEA SECURITY
.sp
Kea was originally designed to be installed in a protected environment, in a network
datacenter; it did not offer hardened security features. However, due to customer demand
and evolving network requirements, support for basic HTTP authentication and Transport
Layer Security (TLS) have been added to Kea.
.SS TLS/HTTPS Support
.sp
Since Kea 1.9.6, TLS can be used to secure HTTP communication. There are three levels of
protection possible:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
No TLS. The connection is plain\-text, unencrypted HTTP. (This is
the only option available in versions prior to Kea 1.9.6.)
.IP \(bu 2
Encryption, which protects against passive attacks and
eavesdropping. In this case, the server is authenticated but the client is
not. This is the typical mode when securing a website, where
clients and servers are not under the control of a common entity.
.IP \(bu 2
Mutual authentication between the client and the server. This is the
strictest security mode and is the default when TLS is
enabled.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
TLS mutual authentication is for TLS entities only. When TLS and
an HTTP authentication scheme are used together, there is no binding between
the two security mechanisms, and therefore no proof that the TLS client and server
are the same as the HTTP authentication client and server.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is recommend to use privileged ports for HTTP/HTTPS against local attacks
(by users which are connected to the box where Kea servers/agents run). This
measure also prevents against impersonation with HTTP, and Denial of
Service in general.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The server will issue an error when changing the socket type from HTTP to HTTPS
or from HTTPS to HTTP using the same address and port. This action is not
allowed as it might introduce a security issue accidentally caused by a user
mistake.
A different address or port must be specified when using the \(dqconfig\-set\(dq
command to switch from HTTP to HTTPS or from HTTPS to HTTP. The same applies
when modyfying the configuration file and then running \(dqconfig\-reload\(dq command.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Building Kea with TLS/HTTPS Support
.sp
TLS/HTTPS support is available with either the OpenSSL or the Botan
cryptographic library. There are some constraints on the Boost library
that must be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
OpenSSL versions older than 1.0.2 are obsolete and should not be used.
Kea TLS support has not been tested with and is not supported on these versions.
.IP \(bu 2
OpenSSL version 1.0.2 has extended support, but only for OpenSSL premium
customers. Kea TLS support has been tested but is not supported on this version.
.IP \(bu 2
OpenSSL versions 1.1.x and later have been tested and are supported. Many
recent operating system versions include TLS 1.3 support.
.IP \(bu 2
OpenSSL 3.x has been released and Kea will build with it.
.IP \(bu 2
LibreSSL 3.2.4 has been tested. LibreSSL shares the OpenSSL 1.0.2 API, so
it should work, but is not supported.
.IP \(bu 2
Botan 1.x and 2.x versions are obsolete and must not be used.
.IP \(bu 2
Botan versions 3.4.0 and later have been tested and are supported. Kea TLS
support requires the four Asio header files which are included in Botan
packages and which are installed only if Botan is configured with the
\fB\-\-with\-boost\fP option.
.sp
Many packages provided by operating systems, such as Ubuntu 20.10,
do not build Botan with Boost support, making those packages
unusable for Kea with TLS.
.sp
It is still possible to take these files from the corresponding
Botan distribution and install them manually in the Botan include
directory, but this should be a last\-resort procedure.
.sp
Without these header files, or with a Botan version prior
to 3.4.0, Kea cannot build as the TLS/HTTPS support is considered
essential for security.
.IP \(bu 2
Very old Boost versions provide SSL support (based on OpenSSL)
without offering a choice of the TLS version; Kea can still use them,
but they are not recommended.
.IP \(bu 2
Boost versions prior to 1.64 provide SSL support with a fixed
choice of the TLS version; Kea enforces the use of TLS 1.2 with them.
.IP \(bu 2
Boost versions 1.64 or newer provide SSL support with a generic
TLS version; the best (highest) version available on both peers is
selected.
.UNINDENT
.SS TLS/HTTPS Configuration
.sp
The TLS configuration parameters are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtrust\-anchor\fP \- this string parameter specifies the name of a file
or directory where the certification authority (CA) certificate of
the other peer can be found. With OpenSSL, the directory must include
hash symbolic links. With Botan, the directory is recursively
searched for certificates.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcert\-file\fP \- this string parameter specifies the name of the file
containing the end\-entity certificate of the Kea instance
being configured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkey\-file\fP \- this string parameter specifies the private key of the
end\-entity certificate of the Kea instance being configured.
The file must not be encrypted; it is highly recommended to
restrict its access.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The three string parameters must be either all unspecified (TLS disabled)
or all specified (TLS enabled).
.sp
TLS is asymmetric: the authentication of the server by the client is
mandatory but the authentication of the client by the server is optional.
In TLS terms, this means the server may require the client certificate, or
may not; there is a server\-specific TLS parameter.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcert\-required\fP \- this boolean parameter allows a server to not
require the client certificate. Its default value is \fBtrue\fP, which
means the client certificate is required and the
client must be authenticated. This flag has no meaning on the client side; the server
always provides a certificate which is validated by the client.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Objects in files must be in the PEM format. Files can contain more
than one certificate, but this has not been tested and is not supported.
.sp
Botan requires CA certificates to have the standard CA certificate
attributes, verifies that end\-entity certificates are version 3
certificates (as required by the TLS standard), and supports only PKCS 8
files for the private key.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Some cryptographic libraries (e.g. Botan and recent OpenSSL) enforce
minimal strength (i.e. key length), e.g. at least 2048 for RSA.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A sample set of certificates and associated objects is available at
\fBsrc/lib/asiolink/testutils/ca\fP in the Kea sources, with a \fBdoc.txt\fP file
explaining how they were generated using the \fBopenssl\fP command. These
files are for testing purposes only. \fBDo not use them in production.\fP
.sp
TLS handshake, the phase where the cryptographic parameters are exchanged
and authentication is verified, can fail in multiple ways. Error messages
often do not help to pinpoint the source of the problem.
Both OpenSSL and Botan provide a command\-line tool with a \fBverify\fP command
which can be used to understand and fix handshake issues.
.SS OpenSSL Tuning
.sp
Kea accepts the default OpenSSL configuration parameters, but administrators can
also fine\-tune the OpenSSL settings. For example, it may be desirable to limit
the TLS version.
.sp
The default OpenSSL configuration file is named \fBopenssl.cnf\fP\&. It can
be found in a system\-dependent \fBetc\fP directory, and the location can be overridden
using the \fBOPENSSL_CONF\fP environment variable. For OpenSSL versions greater than
1.0.2, the minimum acceptable protocol can be set via the \fBMinProtocol\fP variable.
.sp
For these examples, we assume that no variables are already set and no sections already
exist; it is, of course, possible to reuse existing variables and sections.
.sp
In the default application, \fBopenssl_conf\fP, the corresponding variable
must be set to the name of the section that handles defaults: in this example,
\fBdefault_conf\fP\&. If \fBopenssl_conf\fP is not yet set, add this command
at the beginning of the OpenSSL configuration file (before the first
section):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
openssl_conf = default_conf
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the \fBdefault_conf\fP section, the \fBssl_conf\fP variable must be set
to the name of the section that handles SSL/TLS defaults: in this
example, \fBssl_sect\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[ default_conf ]
ssl_conf = ssl_sect
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the \fBssl_sect\fP section, the \fBsystem_default\fP variable must be
set to the name of the section that handles system defaults: in
this example, \fBsystem_default_sect\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[ ssl_sect ]
system_default = system_default_sect
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the \fBsystem_default_sect\fP section, the \fBMinProtocol\fP  variable must be
set to the desired minimal SSL/TLS version: in this example, \fBTLSv1.2\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[ system_default_sect ]
MinProtocol = TLSv1.2
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The same steps can be used to enforce other crypto parameters if
desired.
.sp
It is highly recommended to read the \fBopenssl.cnf\fP manual page,
normally called \fBconfig.5ssl\fP and displayed using \fBman config\fP\&.
.SS Secure Kea Control Channel
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This section was about the now obsolete (removed) Kea Control Agent but
applies to Kea control HTTPS control sockets supported by Kea servers.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The control sockets of Kea servers  natively supports secure
HTTP connections using TLS. This allows protection against users from
the node where the agent runs, something that a reverse proxy cannot
provide. More about TLS/HTTPS support in Kea can be found in TLS/HTTPS Support\&.
.sp
TLS is configured using three string parameters with file names, and
a boolean parameter:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBtrust\-anchor\fP specifies the Certification Authority file name or
directory path.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBcert\-file\fP specifies the server certificate file name.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBkey\-file\fP specifies the private key file name. The file must not
be encrypted.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBcert\-required\fP specifies whether client certificates are required
or optional. The default is to require them and to perform mutual
authentication.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The file format is PEM. Either all the string parameters are specified and
HTTP over TLS (HTTPS) is used, or none is specified and plain HTTP is used.
Configuring only one or two string parameters results in an error.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When client certificates are not required, only the server side is
authenticated, i.e. the communication is encrypted with an unknown
client. This protects only against passive attacks; active
attacks, such as \(dqman\-in\-the\-middle,\(dq are still possible.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
No standard HTTP authentication scheme cryptographically binds its end
entity with TLS. This means that the TLS client and server can be
mutually authenticated, but there is no proof they are the same as
for the HTTP authentication.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBkea\-shell\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-shell> tool also supports TLS.
.SS Securing a Kea Deployment
.sp
Below is a list of considerations for administrators wishing to improve Kea\(aqs
security. In many cases, there are trade\-offs between convenience and security.
.SS Component\-Based Design
.sp
The Kea architecture is modular, with separate daemons for separate tasks.
A Kea deployment may include DHCPv4, DHCPv6, and Dynamic DNS daemons;
the \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> utility for doing periodic lease
file cleanup; MySQL and or PostgreSQL databases, run either locally on the application
servers or accessed over the internal network; a Netconf daemon to perform config and stats
monitoring of Kea servers; and a Stork monitoring system.
This modular architecture allows the administrator to minimize the attack surface
by minimizing the code that is loaded and running.
For example, \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> should not be run unless DNS updates are required.
Similarly, \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> is never triggered (and can be safely removed or never installed) if memfile is not used.
Potential Kea security issues can be minimized by running only those processes required in the local environment.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, \fBdata\-directory\fP (DHCPv6 only) is deprecated. The lease files
(DHCPv4 and DHCPv6) and duid file (DHCPv6 only) may only be loaded from
the directory determined at compilation: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/lib/kea\(dq\fP\&.
This path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_DHCP_DATA_DIR\fP to the desired path.  If a path other than
this value is used in \fBname\fP or \fBdata\-directory\fP, Kea will emit an error and
refuse to start or, if already running, log an unrecoverable error.
This restriction applies to writing lease file using \fBlease4\-write\fP and
\fBlease6\-write\fP commands. If a path other than this value is used in \fBfilename\fP,
Kea will emit an error and refuse to start or, if already running, log an
unrecoverable error.  For ease of use in specifying a custom file name simply
omit the path portion from \fBfilename\fP\&. Same restriction also applies to
writing cache file using \fBcache\-write\fP command. For ease of use in
specifying a custom file name simply omit the path portion from \fBfilename\fP\&.
The \fBKEA_DHCP_DATA_DIR\fP environment variable is also used to change the default
location for the generated DHCPv6 server identifier file (kea\-dhcp6\-serverid).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Limiting Application Permissions
.sp
The DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 protocols assume the server opens privileged UDP port 67
(DHCPv4) or 547 (DHCPv6), which requires root access under normal circumstances. However, via the
capabilities mechanism on Linux systems, Kea can run from an unprivileged account. See
Running Kea From a Non\-root Account on Linux \%<#\:non-root> for details on how to run Kea without root access.
.SS Securing Kea Administrative Access
.sp
The three primary Kea daemons (\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>, \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> and \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>) all support a control
channel, which is implemented as a UNIX socket. The control channel, which opens a UNIX socket, is disabled by default;
however, many configuration examples have it enabled, as it is a very popular feature. To
read from or write to this socket, root access is generally required, although if Kea is configured
to run as non\-root, the owner of the process can write to it. Access can be controlled using normal
file\-access control on POSIX systems (owner, group, others, read/write).
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, control sockets may only reside in the directory
determined during compilation as \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/run/kea\(dq\fP,
which must also have \fB0750\fP access rights. This path may be overridden
at startup by setting the environment variable \fBKEA_CONTROL_SOCKET_DIR\fP
to the desired path.  If a path other than this value is used in
\fBsocket\-name\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start or, if already
running, log an unrecoverable error.  For ease of use in simply omit the
path component from \fBsocket\-name\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since Kea version 2.7.2 DHCP servers support HTTP/HTTPS control channels,
and since Kea version 3.1.8 the Control Agent has been removed.
.sp
Since Kea\-2.7.6 Kea supports multiple HTTP/HTTPS connections. Both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses can be used.
Security can be enhanced if configuring HTTPS connections for all daemons.
.sp
Kea configuration is controlled by a JSON file on the Kea server. This file can be viewed or edited
by anyone with file permissions (which are controlled by the operating system). Note that
passwords are stored in clear text in the configuration file, so anyone with access to read the
configuration file can find this information. As a practical matter, anyone with permission to edit
the configuration file has control over Kea.
Limiting user permission to read or write the Kea configuration file is an important security step.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, the config file may only be written (using the
\fBconfig\-write\fP command) to the same directory as the config file used
when starting Kea (passed as a \fB\-c\fP argument).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Securing Database Connections
.sp
Kea can use an external MySQL or PostgreSQL database to store configuration, host reservations,
or/and leases, or/and for forensic logging. The use of databases is a popular feature, but it is optional;
it is also possible to store data in a flat file on disk.
.sp
When using a database, Kea stores and uses the following credentials to authenticate with the database:
username, password, host, port, and database name. \fBThese are stored in clear text
in the configuration file.\fP
.sp
Depending on the database configuration, it is also possible to verify whether the system user matches the
database username. Consult the MySQL or PostgreSQL manual for details.
.sp
Kea supports client SSL/TLS settings for MySQL and PostgreSQL database
and it must be configured explicitly for all used connections (configuration,
reservations, leases, forensic logging). See the Database Connectivity
section (Database Connectivity \%<#\:database-connectivity>) for details.
.SS Information Leakage Through Logging
.sp
It is possible for Kea to log an entire configuration file, including passwords and secrets.
Since Kea 1.9.7, this issue has been resolved by replacing the value of all entries ending in
\fBpassword\fP or \fBsecret\fP with asterisks, as was already done for database logs.
.sp
Logs are sent to stdout, stderr, files, or syslog; system file permissions system apply to
stdout/stderr and files. Syslog may export the logs over the network, exposing them further to possible snooping.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, log files may only be written to the output directory
determined during compilation as: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea\(dq\fP\&. This
path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_LOG_FILE_DIR\fP to the desired path.  If a path other than
this value is used in \fBoutput\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start
or, if already running, log an unrecoverable error.  For ease of use simply
omit the path component from \fBoutput\fP and specify only the file name.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, legal log files may only be written to the output directory
determined during compilation as: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea\(dq\fP\&. This
path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_LEGAL_LOG_DIR\fP to the desired path.  If a path other than this value
is used in \fBpath\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start or if already
running, log an unrecoverable error. For ease of use simply omit the \fBpath\fP
parameter.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
