.sp
The following table lists all option types used in the previous two tables with a description of
what values are accepted for them.
.SS List of standard DHCP option types
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Meaning
T}
_
T{
binary
T}	T{
An arbitrary string of bytes, specified as a set
of hexadecimal digits.
T}
_
T{
boolean
T}	T{
A boolean value with allowed
values true or false.
T}
_
T{
empty
T}	T{
No value; data is carried in
sub\-options.
T}
_
T{
fqdn
T}	T{
Fully qualified domain name (e.g.
www.example.com).
T}
_
T{
ipv4\-address
T}	T{
IPv4 address in the usual
dotted\-decimal notation (e.g.
192.0.2.1).
T}
_
T{
ipv6\-address
T}	T{
IPv6 address in the usual colon
notation (e.g. 2001:db8::1).
T}
_
T{
ipv6\-prefix
T}	T{
IPv6 prefix and prefix length
specified using CIDR notation,
e.g. 2001:db8:1::/64. This data
type is used to represent an
8\-bit field conveying a prefix
length and the variable length
prefix value.
T}
_
T{
psid
T}	T{
PSID and PSID length separated by
a slash, e.g. 3/4 specifies
PSID=3 and PSID length=4. In the
wire format it is represented by
an 8\-bit field carrying PSID
length (in this case equal to 4)
and the 16\-bits\-long PSID value
field (in this case equal to
\(dq0011000000000000b\(dq using binary
notation). Allowed values for a
PSID length are 0 to 16. See RFC 7597 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc7597>
for details about the PSID wire
representation.
T}
_
T{
record
T}	T{
Structured data that may be
comprised of any types (except
\(dqrecord\(dq and \(dqempty\(dq). The array
flag applies to the last field
only.
T}
_
T{
string
T}	T{
Any text. Please note that Kea
silently discards any
terminating/trailing nulls from
the end of \(dqstring\(dq options when
unpacking received packets. This
is in keeping with RFC 2132,
Section 2 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2132#\:section-2>\&.
T}
_
T{
tuple
T}	T{
A length field encoded as an 8\-bit
or 16\-bit unsigned integer followed by
a string of this length. Typically, for DHCPv4,
the length is 8\-bit, and for DHCPv6, it is 16\-bit.
However, there are exceptions to that rule.
E.g. for the DHCPv4 SZTP Redirect Option,
bootstrap\-server\-list is encoded as a list of
tuples where the URI\-length in each tuple is
a 16\-bit unsigned integer.
T}
_
T{
uint8
T}	T{
An 8\-bit unsigned integer with
allowed values 0 to 255.
T}
_
T{
uint16
T}	T{
A 16\-bit unsigned integer with
allowed values 0 to 65535.
T}
_
T{
uint32
T}	T{
A 32\-bit unsigned integer with
allowed values 0 to 4294967295.
T}
_
T{
int8
T}	T{
An 8\-bit signed integer with allowed
values \-128 to 127.
T}
_
T{
int16
T}	T{
A 16\-bit signed integer with
allowed values \-32768 to 32767.
T}
_
T{
int32
T}	T{
A 32\-bit signed integer with
allowed values \-2147483648 to
2147483647.
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Option \fBclassless\-static\-route\fP has its own data type \(dqinternal\(dq and accepts a string with the following mask
as its value: subnet1/prefix_len1 \- router1, subnetN/prefix_lenN \- routerN.
.sp
For example: 10.229.0.128/25 \- 10.229.0.1, 10.198.122.47/32 \- 10.198.122.1
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Kea also supports the Relay Agent Information (RAI, defined in
RFC 3046 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3046>) option, sometimes referred to as the relay option, agent
option, or simply option 82. The option itself is just a container and does not convey any information
on its own. The following table contains a list of RAI sub\-options that Kea can understand. The RAI
and its sub\-options are inserted by the relay agent and received by Kea; there is no need for Kea
to be configured with those options. Kea\(aqs classification and flexible ID features in host reservations can be
used to process those and other options not listed in the table below.
.SS List of RAI sub\-options that Kea can understand
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Code
T}	T{
Comment
T}
_
T{
circuit\-id
T}	T{
1
T}	T{
Used when host\-reservation\-identifiers is set to \fIcircuit\-id\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
remote\-id
T}	T{
2
T}	T{
Can be used with flex\-id to identify hosts.
T}
_
T{
link\-selection
T}	T{
5
T}	T{
If present, used to select the appropriate subnet.
T}
_
T{
subscriber\-id
T}	T{
6
T}	T{
Can be used with flex\-id to identify hosts.
T}
_
T{
server\-id\-override
T}	T{
11
T}	T{
If sent by the relay, Kea accepts it as the \fIserver\-id\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
relay\-id
T}	T{
12
T}	T{
Identifies the relay
T}
_
T{
relay\-port
T}	T{
19
T}	T{
If sent by the relay, Kea sends back its responses to this port.
T}
.TE
.sp
All other RAI sub\-options (including those not listed here) can be used in client classification to
classify incoming packets to specific classes, and/or by \fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_flex_id\:.so> to
construct a unique device identifier. For more information about expressions used in client
classification and flexible identifiers, see Client Classification \%<#\:classify>\&. The RAI sub\-options can be
referenced using \fBrelay4[option\-code].hex\fP; for example, to classify packets based on the
\fBremote\-id\fP (sub\-option code 2), one would use \fBrelay4[2].hex\fP\&. An example client class that
includes all packets with a specific \fBremote\-id\fP value would look as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqremote\-id\-1020304\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqrelay4[2].hex == 0x01020304\(dq,
            ...
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Classes may be used to segregate traffic into a relatively small number of groups, which then
can be used to select specific subnets, pools and extra options, and more. If per\-host behavior
is necessary, using host reservations with flexible identifiers is strongly recommended.
.SS CableLabs Client Conf Suboptions
.sp
CableLabs client conf option (\(dqcablelabs\-client\-conf\(dq code 122) is a
container of suboptions in the \(dqcablelabs\-client\-conf\(dq space listed in
the table below.
.SS List of CableLabs Client Conf sub\-options that Kea can understand
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
