.TE
.SS Iterative Allocator
.sp
This is the default allocator used by the Kea DHCPv4 server. It remembers the
last offered address and offers this address, increased by one, to the next client.
For example, it may offer addresses in this order: \fB192.0.2.10\fP, \fB192.0.2.11\fP,
\fB192.0.2.12\fP, and so on. The time to find and offer the next address is very
short; thus, this is the most performant allocator when pool utilization
is low and there is a high probability that the next address is available.
.sp
The iterative allocation underperforms when multiple DHCP servers share a lease
database or are connected to a cluster. The servers tend to offer and allocate
the same blocks of addresses to different clients independently, which causes many
allocation conflicts between the servers and retransmissions by clients. A random
allocation addresses this issue by dispersing the allocation order.
.SS Random Allocator
.sp
The random allocator uses a uniform randomization function to select offered
addresses from subnet pools. It is suitable in deployments where multiple servers
are connected to a shared
database or a database cluster. By dispersing the offered addresses, the servers
minimize the risk of allocating the same address to two different clients at
the same or nearly the same time. In addition, it improves the server\(aqs resilience against
attacks based on allocation predictability.
.sp
The random allocator is, however, slightly slower than the iterative allocator.
Moreover, it increases the server\(aqs memory consumption because it must remember
randomized addresses to avoid offering them repeatedly. Memory consumption grows
with the number of offered addresses; in other words, larger pools and more
clients increase memory consumption by random allocation.
.sp
The following configuration snippet shows how to select the random allocator
for a subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
        \(dqallocator\(dq: \(dqrandom\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.0.0.0/8\(dq,
                \(dqallocator\(dq: \(dqrandom\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Free Lease Queue Allocator
.sp
This is a sophisticated allocator whose use should be considered in subnets
with highly utilized address pools. In such cases, it can take a considerable
amount of time for the iterative or random allocator to find an available
address, because they must repeatedly check whether there is a valid lease for
an address they will offer. The number of checks can be as high as the number
of addresses in the subnet when the subnet pools are exhausted, which can have a
direct negative impact on the DHCP response time for each request.
.sp
The Free Lease Queue (FLQ) allocator tracks lease allocations and de\-allocations
and maintains a running list of available addresses for each address pool.
It allows an available lease to be selected within a constant time, regardless of
the subnet pools\(aq utilization. The allocator continuously updates the list of
free leases by removing any allocated leases and adding released or
reclaimed ones.
.sp
The following configuration snippet shows how to select the FLQ allocator
for a subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqallocator\(dq: \(dqflq\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
There are several considerations that the administrator should take into account
before using this allocator. The FLQ allocator can heavily impact the server\(aqs
startup and reconfiguration time, because the allocator has to populate the
list of free leases for each subnet where it is used. These delays can be
observed both during the configuration reload and when the subnets are
created using \fBlibdhcp_subnet_cmds.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_subnet_cmds\:.so>\&. This allocator increases
memory consumption to hold the list of free leases, proportional
to the total size of the address pools for which the FLQ allocator is used.
Finally, lease reclamation must be enabled with a low value of the
\fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP parameter, to ensure that the server frequently
collects expired leases and makes them available for allocation via the
free lease queue; expired leases are not considered free by
the allocator until they are reclaimed by the server. See
Lease Reclamation \%<#\:lease-reclamation> for more details about the lease reclamation process.
.sp
We recommend that the FLQ allocator be selected
only after careful consideration. For example, using it for a subnet with a
\fB/8\fP pool may delay the server\(aqs startup by 15 seconds or more. On the
other hand, the startup delay and the memory consumption increase should
be acceptable for subnets with a \fB/16\fP pool or smaller. We also recommend
specifying another allocator type in the global configuration settings
and overriding this selection at the subnet or shared\-network level, to use
the FLQ allocator only for selected subnets. That way, when a new subnet is
added without an allocator specification, the global setting is used, thus
avoiding unnecessary impact on the server\(aqs startup time.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The FLQ allocator is not suitable for use with a shared lease database
(i.e., when multiple Kea servers store leases in the same database).
The servers are unaware of the expired leases reclaimed by the
sibling servers and never return them to their local free lease queues.
As a result, the servers will not be able to offer some of the available
leases to the clients. Only a server reclaiming a particular lease will
be able to offer it.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In Kea 3.1.1 a new parameter \fBadaptive\-lease\-time\-threshold\fP was added.
It can be specified at global, shared network and subnet levels and
takes a floating point value between \fB0.0\fP (excluded) and \fB1.0\fP\&.
It is disabled by default or when set to \fB1.0\fP\&. It is only supported
by the FLQ allocator. When the occupancy rate of pools in a subnet is
above the specified value the server decreases the lease valid lifetime
to the applicable \fBmin\-valid\-lifetime\fP for new clients. Clients
renewing an already existing lease get at least the remaining lifetime
from the current lease. Since the leases expire faster, the server may
either recover more quickly or avoid pool exhaustion entirely.
.sp
The occupancy rate is defined as being the number of already assigned
addresses (including the address being assigned) divided by the total
number of addresses in pools where the address can be allocated from
(i.e. if the query is not member of a \fBclient\-classes\fP guard of a pool
this pool is not taken into account).
.SS Shared Free Lease Queue Allocator
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Added in Kea 3.1.9, Shared Free Lease Queue Allocator is considered
experimental and is not supported for production use.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Shared Free Lease Queue (SFLQ) Allocator is intended for use sites with
shared lease back ends (MySQL or PostgreSQL) and subnets with highly
utilized address pools. In such cases, it can take a considerable
amount of time for the iterative or random allocator to find an available
address, because they must repeatedly check whether there is a valid lease for
an address they will offer. The number of checks can be as high as the number
of addresses in the subnet when the subnet pools are exhausted. Since each
check requires a database query this can have a direct negative impact on
the DHCP response time for each request.
.sp
Similar to the Free Lease Queue Allocator, the SFLQ Allocator tracks lease
allocations and de\-allocations and maintains a running list of available
addresses for each address pool. These lists are referred to as SFLQ data. It
allows an available lease to be selected within a constant time, regardless
of the subnet\(aqs pools\(aq utilization. The SFLQ data is maintained through
specialized lease updates employed by the Kea server for leases that belong
to subnets that are using SFLQ allocation.
.sp
The fundamental difference between the FLQ and SFLQ Allocators is that SFLQ
allocation stores the SFLQ data centrally in the lease back end rather than
locally in Kea server memory. This allows it be shared between servers such
that they all have the same understanding of current lease state. It also means
that once created the SFLQ data is persistent and therefore does not have to
be re\-created upon Kea server startup or reconfiguration. The following
configuration snippet shows how to select the SFLQ allocator for a subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqallocator\(dq: \(dqshared\-flq\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
There are some considerations that the administrator should take into account
before using this allocator. Like FLQ, the SFLQ allocator can heavily impact the
server\(aqs startup and reconfiguration time because the allocator has to populate the
SFLQ data for each pool in each subnet where it is used. These delays can be observed
both during the configuration reload and when the subnets are created using
\fBlibdhcp_subnet_cmds.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_subnet_cmds\:.so>\&.  Unlike FLQ, these costs only occur the first
time a subnet is configured to use SFLQ or changes are made to its pools\(aq address
ranges. In other words, following a restart or reconfiguration the SFLQ data only
needs to be created for pools for which it does not already exist. Thus the impact
on subsequent restarts or reconfigurations of subnets that already use SFLQ is minimal.
.sp
Since SFLQ data is maintained in the lease back end, the cost of storing
it is in database server disc space rather than local Kea server memory. Disk use
for the SFLQ data is a fraction of what is already stored per lease and as
pool utilization increases, the size of SFLQ data decreases.
.sp
We recommend that the SFLQ allocator be selected only after careful consideration.
As discussed above, impact on startup can be substantial when introducing the use
of SFLQ. Creating the initial SFLQ data for large pools (e.g.\(ga\(ga/8\(ga\(ga) may delay the
server\(aqs startup by several seconds or more per pool. It is faster for smaller pools
(e.g.\(ga\(ga/16\(ga\(ga or less) but per pool can add up when there are many such pools.  To
reduce this startup cost we recommend specifying another allocator type at the global
level and specifying the SFLQ allocator at the subnet or shared\-network level only
for select subnets.
.sp
There is another important aspect of the SFLQ Allocator that warrants discussion.
As mentioned above when a server is using SFLQ allocation for a given pool, it uses
specialized back end updates that maintain the SFLQ data. Thus it is critical that
all servers sharing a lease back end agree on which subnets use SFLQ allocation.
If they do not agree the SFLQ data will quickly become inaccurate. Addresses may
look free when they are not and vice versa. In the case of the former, the allocation
engine will detect an existing lease and try another. In the case of the latter,
the lease will not be offered.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is strongly recommended that when configuring subnets to use or stop
using SFLQ allocation, it be done in maintenance windows such that servers
are not actively serving clients for those subnets while the changes are made.
This will avoid inconsistencies in SFLQ data.
.sp
SFLQ data is tracked in terms of pools where each pool has a start address
and an end address. Configuration changes that change the address range
of a pool or split into multiple new pools will trigger SFLQ data creation
for the new pools when the new configuration is applied.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As mentioned before when a server restarts or is reconfigured, it will iterate
over its subnets and initialize the configured allocator for that subnet\(aqs pools.
For the SFLQ Allocator, this will cause the server to request the lease back end
to create the SFLQ data for each pool in the subnet. If the SFLQ data already exists
for a pool, the request will return immediately. If not it will return once
the SFLQ data for that pool has been created. SFLQ data creation for a specific
pool is an exclusive operation. Once it has been initiated by a server, any requests
from other servers to create the SFLQ data for the same pool will block and not
return until the first request completes creating the SFLQ data for the pool.
In this way only one server actually creates the pool\(aqs SFLQ data and all others
are prohibited from using the pool until its SFLQ data exists.  This behavior
is intended to maintain some order during concurrent server restarts and
reconfigurations.
.sp
There is the topic of overlapping pools. Kea permits the configuration
of pools whose address ranges overlap other pools. The SFLQ Allocator supports
this seamlessly as long as all such pools are configured to use the SFLQ
Allocator.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Lease reclamation must be enabled with a low value for the
\fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP parameter to ensure that the server frequently
collects expired leases and makes them available for allocation via the
SFLQ data. Expired leases are not considered free by the allocator until they are
reclaimed by the server. See Lease Reclamation \%<#\:lease-reclamation> for more details about the
lease reclamation process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
SFLQ Allocator is restricted to use in pools no larger than /8
(e.g. 16777216 addresses). Pools of larger capacity will cause a configuration
error.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
SFLQ Allocator does not support \fBadaptive\-lease\-time\-threshold\fP\&.
.SH THE DHCPV6 SERVER
.SS Starting and Stopping the DHCPv6 Server
.sp
It is recommended that the Kea DHCPv6 server be started and stopped
using \fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> (described in Managing Kea with keactrl \%<#\:keactrl>); however, it is also
possible to run the server directly via the \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> command, which accepts
the following command\-line switches:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-c file\fP \- specifies the configuration file. This is the only
mandatory switch.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-d\fP \- specifies whether the server logging should be switched to
debug/verbose mode. In verbose mode, the logging severity and debuglevel
specified in the configuration file are ignored; \(dqdebug\(dq severity
and the maximum debuglevel (99) are assumed. The flag is convenient
for temporarily switching the server into maximum verbosity, e.g.
when debugging.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-p server\-port\fP \- specifies the local UDP port on which the server
listens. This is only useful during testing, as a DHCPv6 server
listening on ports other than the standard ones is not able to
handle regular DHCPv6 queries.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-P client\-port\fP \- specifies the remote UDP port to which the
server sends all responses. This is only useful during testing,
as a DHCPv6 server sending responses to ports other than the standard
ones is not able to handle regular DHCPv6 queries.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-t file\fP \- specifies a configuration file to be tested. \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
loads it, checks it, and exits. During the test, log messages are
printed to standard output and error messages to standard error. The
result of the test is reported through the exit code (0 =
configuration looks OK, 1 = error encountered). The check is not
comprehensive; certain checks are possible only when running the
server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-T file\fP \- specifies a configuration file to be tested. \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
loads it, checks it, and exits. It performs extra checks beyond what \fB\-t\fP
offers, such as establishing database connections (for the lease backend,
host reservations backend, configuration backend, and forensic logging
backend), loading hook libraries, parsing hook\-library configurations, etc.
It does not open UNIX or TCP/UDP sockets, lock files, nor does it open or rotate
files, as any of these actions could interfere with a running process on the
same machine.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-v\fP \- displays the Kea version and exits.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-V\fP \- displays the Kea extended version with additional parameters
and exits. The listing includes the versions of the libraries
dynamically linked to Kea.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-W\fP \- displays the Kea configuration report and exits. The report
is a copy of the \fBconfig.report\fP file produced by \fBmeson setup\fP;
it is embedded in the executable binary.
.sp
The contents of the \fBconfig.report\fP file may also be accessed by examining
certain libraries in the installation tree or in the source tree.
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# from installation using libkea\-process.so
$ strings ${prefix}/lib/libkea\-process.so | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libkea\-process.so
$ strings src/lib/process/.libs/libkea\-process.so | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libkea\-process.a
$ strings src/lib/process/.libs/libkea\-process.a | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libcfgrpt.a
$ strings src/lib/process/cfgrpt/.libs/libcfgrpt.a | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-X\fP \- As of Kea 3.0, disables security restrictions. The server will
still check for violations but will emit warning logs when they are found
rather than fail with an error. Please see
Kea Runtime Security Policy Checking \%<#\:sec-kea-runtime-security-policy-checking> for details.
.UNINDENT
.sp
On startup, the server detects available network interfaces and
attempts to open UDP sockets on all interfaces listed in the
configuration file. Since the DHCPv6 server opens privileged ports, it
requires root access; this daemon must be run as root.
.sp
During startup, the server attempts to create a PID file of the
form: \fB[pidfile_dir]/[conf name].kea\-dhcp6.pid\fP, where:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpidfile_dir\fP \- is \fB[prefix]/[localstatedir]/run/kea\fP where
\fBprefix\fP and \fBlocalstatedir\fP are the values passed into meson setup using
\fB\-\-prefix\fP and \fB\-\-localstatedir\fP which default to \fB/usr/local\fP and
\fBvar\fP respectively. So the whole \fBpidfile_dir\fP defaults to
\fB/usr/local/var\fP\&. Note that this value may be overridden at runtime by
setting the environment variable  \fBKEA_PIDFILE_DIR\fP intended primarily for
testing purposes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconf name\fP: The configuration file name used to start the server,
minus all preceding paths and the file extension. For example, given
a pathname of \fB/usr/local/etc/kea/myconf.txt\fP, the portion used would
be \fBmyconf\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the file already exists and contains the PID of a live process, the
server issues a \fBDHCP6_ALREADY_RUNNING\fP log message and exits. It is
possible, though unlikely, that the file is a remnant of a system crash
and the process to which the PID belongs is unrelated to Kea. In such a
case, it would be necessary to manually delete the PID file.
.sp
The server can be stopped using the \fBkill\fP command. When running in a
console, the server can also be shut down by pressing Ctrl\-c. Kea detects
the key combination and shuts down gracefully.
.sp
The reconfiguration of each Kea server is triggered by the SIGHUP signal.
When a server receives the SIGHUP signal it rereads its configuration file and,
if the new configuration is valid, uses the new configuration.
If the new configuration proves to be invalid, the server retains its
current configuration; however, in some cases a fatal error message is logged
indicating that the server is no longer providing any service: a working
configuration must be loaded as soon as possible.
.SS DHCPv6 Server Configuration
.SS Introduction
.sp
This section explains how to configure the Kea DHCPv6 server using a
configuration file.
.sp
Before DHCPv6 is started, its configuration file must
be created. The basic configuration is as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
# DHCPv6 configuration starts on the next line
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {

# First we set up global values
    \(dqvalid\-lifetime\(dq: 4000,
    \(dqrenew\-timer\(dq: 1000,
    \(dqrebind\-timer\(dq: 2000,
    \(dqpreferred\-lifetime\(dq: 3000,

# Next we set up the interfaces to be used by the server.
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth0\(dq ]
    },

# And we specify the type of lease database
    \(dqlease\-database\(dq: {
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmemfile\(dq,
        \(dqpersist\(dq: true,
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/dhcp6.leases\(dq
    },

# Finally, we list the subnets from which we will be leasing addresses.
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\-2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq
                }
             ]
        }
    ]
# DHCPv6 configuration ends with the next line
}

}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following paragraphs provide a brief overview of the parameters in
the above example, along with their format. Subsequent sections of this
chapter go into much greater detail for these and other parameters.
.sp
The lines starting with a hash (#) are comments and are ignored by the
server; they do not impact its operation in any way.
.sp
The configuration starts in the first line with the initial opening
curly bracket (or brace). Each configuration must contain an object
specifying the configuration of the Kea module using it. In the example
above, this object is called \fBDhcp6\fP\&.
.sp
The \fBDhcp6\fP configuration starts with the \fB\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {\fP line and ends
with the corresponding closing brace (in the above example, the brace
after the last comment). Everything defined between those lines is
considered to be the \fBDhcp6\fP configuration.
.sp
In general, the order in which those parameters appear does not
matter, but there are two caveats. The first one is that the
configuration file must be well\-formed JSON, meaning that the
parameters for any given scope must be separated by a comma, and there
must not be a comma after the last parameter. When reordering a
configuration file, moving a parameter to or from the
last position in a given scope may also require moving the comma. The
second caveat is that it is uncommon — although legal JSON — to repeat
the same parameter multiple times. If that happens, the last occurrence
of a given parameter in a given scope is used, while all previous
instances are ignored. This is unlikely to cause any confusion as there
are no real\-life reasons to keep multiple copies of the same parameter
in the configuration file.
.sp
The first few DHCPv6 configuration elements
define some global parameters. \fBvalid\-lifetime\fP defines how long the
addresses (leases) given out by the server are valid; the default
is for a client to be allowed to use a given address for 4000
seconds. (Note that integer numbers are specified as is, without any
quotes around them.) The address will become deprecated in 3000 seconds,
i.e. clients are allowed to keep old connections, but cannot use this
address to create new connections. \fBrenew\-timer\fP and
\fBrebind\-timer\fP are values (also in seconds) that define T1 and T2 timers, which govern
when the client begins the renewal and rebind procedures.
.sp
The \fBinterfaces\-config\fP map specifies the network interfaces on which the
server should listen to DHCP messages. The \fBinterfaces\fP parameter specifies
a list of network interfaces on which the server should listen. Lists are
opened and closed with square brackets, with elements separated by commas. To
listen on two interfaces, the \fBinterfaces\-config\fP element should look like
this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth0\(dq, \(dqeth1\(dq ]
},
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The next lines define the lease database, the place where the
server stores its lease information. This particular example tells the
server to use memfile, which is the simplest and fastest database
backend. It uses an in\-memory database and stores leases on disk in a
CSV (comma\-separated values) file. This is a very simple configuration example;
usually the lease database configuration is more extensive and contains
additional parameters. Note that \fBlease\-database\fP is an object and opens up a
new scope, using an opening brace. Its parameters (just one in this example:
\fBtype\fP) follow. If there were more than one, they would be separated
by commas. This scope is closed with a closing brace. As more parameters
for the \fBDhcp6\fP definition follow, a trailing comma is present.
.sp
Finally, we need to define a list of IPv6 subnets. This is the most
important DHCPv6 configuration structure, as the server uses that
information to process clients\(aq requests. It defines all subnets from
which the server is expected to receive DHCP requests. The subnets are
specified with the \fBsubnet6\fP parameter. It is a list, so it starts and
ends with square brackets. Each subnet definition in the list has
several attributes associated with it, so it is a structure and is
opened and closed with braces. At a minimum, a subnet definition must
have at least two parameters: \fBsubnet\fP, which defines the whole
subnet; and \fBpools\fP, which is a list of dynamically allocated pools
that are governed by the DHCP server.
.sp
The example contains a single subnet. If more than one were defined,
additional elements in the \fBsubnet6\fP parameter would be specified and
separated by commas. For example, to define two subnets, the following
syntax would be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/112\(dq } ],
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
    },
    {
        \(dqid\(dq: 2,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::1\-2001:db8:2::ffff\(dq } ],
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/64\(dq
    }
],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that indentation is optional and is used for aesthetic purposes
only. In some cases it may be preferable to use more compact notation.
.sp
After all the parameters have been specified, there are two contexts open:
\fBglobal\fP and \fBDhcp6\fP; thus, two closing curly brackets must be used to close
them.
.SS Lease Storage
.sp
All leases issued by the server are stored in the lease database.
There are three database backends available: memfile
(the default), MySQL, PostgreSQL.
.SS Memfile \- Basic Storage for Leases
.sp
The server is able to store lease data in different repositories. Larger
deployments may elect to store leases in a database;
Lease Database Configuration describes this option. In
typical smaller deployments, though, the server stores lease
information in a CSV file rather than a database. As well as requiring
less administration, an advantage of using a file for storage is that it
eliminates a dependency on third\-party database software.
.sp
The configuration of the memfile backend is controlled through
the \fBDhcp6\fP/\fBlease\-database\fP parameters. The \fBtype\fP parameter is mandatory
and specifies which storage for leases the server should use, through
the \fB\(dqmemfile\(dq\fP value. The following list gives additional optional parameters
that can be used to configure the memfile backend.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpersist\fP: controls whether the new leases and updates to existing
leases are written to the file. It is strongly recommended that the
value of this parameter be set to \fBtrue\fP at all times during the
server\(aqs normal operation. Not writing leases to disk means that if a
server is restarted (e.g. after a power failure), it will not know
which addresses have been assigned. As a result, it may assign new clients
addresses that are already in use. The value of
\fBfalse\fP is mostly useful for performance\-testing purposes. The
default value of the \fBpersist\fP parameter is \fBtrue\fP, which enables
writing lease updates to the lease file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP: specifies the lease file in which new leases and lease updates
are recorded. The default value for this parameter is
\fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/lib/kea/kea\-leases6.csv\(dq\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, lease files may only be loaded from the data directory
determined during compilation: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/lib/kea\(dq\fP\&. This
path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_DHCP_DATA_DIR\fP to the desired path.  If a path other than
this value is used in \fBname\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start
or, if already running, log an unrecoverable error.  For ease of use in
specifying a custom file name simply omit the path component from \fBname\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlfc\-interval\fP: specifies the interval, in seconds, at which the
server will perform a lease file cleanup (LFC). This removes
redundant (historical) information from the lease file and
effectively reduces the lease file size. The cleanup process is
described in more detail later in this section. The default
value of the \fBlfc\-interval\fP is \fB3600\fP\&. A value of \fB0\fP disables the LFC.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-row\-errors\fP: specifies the number of row errors before the server
stops attempting to load a lease file. When the server loads a lease file, it is processed
row by row, each row containing a single lease. If a row is flawed and
cannot be processed correctly the server logs it, discards the row,
and goes on to the next row. This parameter can be used to set a limit on
the number of such discards that can occur, after which the server
abandons the effort and exits. The default value of \fB0\fP disables the limit
and allows the server to process the entire file, regardless of how many
rows are discarded.
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example configuration of the memfile backend is presented below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqlease\-database\(dq: {
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmemfile\(dq,
        \(dqpersist\(dq: true,
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\-leases6.csv\(dq,
        \(dqlfc\-interval\(dq: 1800,
        \(dqmax\-row\-errors\(dq: 100
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration selects \fBkea\-leases6.csv\fP as the storage file
for lease information and enables persistence (writing lease updates to
this file). It also configures the backend to perform a periodic cleanup
of the lease file every 1800 seconds (30 minutes) and sets the maximum number of
row errors to 100.
.SS Why Is Lease File Cleanup Necessary?
.sp
It is important to know how the lease file contents are organized to
understand why the periodic lease file cleanup is needed. Every time the
server updates a lease or creates a new lease for a client, the new
lease information must be recorded in the lease file. For performance
reasons, the server does not update the existing client\(aqs lease in the
file, as this would potentially require rewriting the entire file.
Instead, it simply appends the new lease information to the end of the
file; the previous lease entries for the client are not removed. When
the server loads leases from the lease file, e.g. at server startup,
it assumes that the latest lease entry for the client is the valid one.
Previous entries are discarded, meaning that the server can
reconstruct accurate information about the leases even though there
may be many lease entries for each client. However, storing many entries
for each client results in a bloated lease file and impairs the
performance of the server\(aqs startup and reconfiguration, as it needs to
process a larger number of lease entries.
.sp
Lease file cleanup (LFC) removes all previous entries for each client
and leaves only the latest ones. The interval at which the cleanup is
performed is configurable, and it should be selected according to the
frequency of lease renewals initiated by the clients. The more frequent
the renewals, the smaller the value of \fBlfc\-interval\fP should be. Note,
however, that the LFC takes time and thus it is possible (although
unlikely) that, if the \fBlfc\-interval\fP is too short, a new cleanup may
be started while the previous one is still running. The server would
recover from this by skipping the new cleanup when it detected that the
previous cleanup was still in progress, but it implies that the actual
cleanups will be triggered more rarely than the configured interval. Moreover,
triggering a new cleanup adds overhead to the server, which is not
able to respond to new requests for a short period of time when the new
cleanup process is spawned. Therefore, it is recommended that the
\fBlfc\-interval\fP value be selected in a way that allows the LFC
to complete the cleanup before a new cleanup is triggered.
.sp
Lease file cleanup is performed by a separate process (in the
background) to avoid a performance impact on the server process. To
avoid conflicts between two processes using the same lease
files, the LFC process starts with Kea opening a new lease file; the
actual LFC process operates on the lease file that is no longer used by
the server. There are also other files created as a side effect of the
lease file cleanup. The detailed description of the LFC process is located later
in this Kea Administrator\(aqs Reference Manual: The LFC Process \%<#\:kea-lfc>\&.
.SS Lease Database Configuration
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Lease database access information must be configured for the DHCPv6
server, even if it has already been configured for the DHCPv4 server.
The servers store their information independently, so each server can
use a separate database or both servers can use the same database.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Kea requires the database timezone to match the system timezone.
For more details, see First\-Time Creation of the MySQL Database \%<#\:mysql-database-create> and
First\-Time Creation of the PostgreSQL Database \%<#\:pgsql-database-create>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Lease database configuration is controlled through the
\fBDhcp6\fP/\fBlease\-database\fP parameters. The database type must be set to
\fBmemfile\fP, \fBmysql\fP or \fBpostgresql\fP, e.g.:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Next, the name of the database to hold the leases must be set; this is
the name used when the database was created (see
First\-Time Creation of the MySQL Database \%<#\:mysql-database-create> or First\-Time Creation of the PostgreSQL Database \%<#\:pgsql-database-create>).
.sp
For MySQL or PostgreSQL:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdatabase\-name\(dq , ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the database is located on a different system from the DHCPv6 server,
the database host name must also be specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqhost\(dq: \(dqremote\-host\-name\(dq, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Normally, the database is on the same machine as the DHCPv6 server.
In this case, set the value to the empty string:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqhost\(dq : \(dq\(dq, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Should the database use a port other than the default, it may be
specified as well:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqport\(dq : 12345, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Should the database be located on a different system, the administrator may need to
specify a longer interval for the connection timeout:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqconnect\-timeout\(dq : timeout\-in\-seconds, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The default value of five seconds should be more than adequate for local
connections. If a timeout is given, though, it should be an integer
greater than zero.
.sp
The maximum number of times the server automatically attempts to
reconnect to the lease database after connectivity has been lost may be
specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqmax\-reconnect\-tries\(dq : number\-of\-tries, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the server is unable to reconnect to the database after making the
maximum number of attempts, the server will exit. A value of 0 (the
default) disables automatic recovery and the server will exit
immediately upon detecting a loss of connectivity (MySQL and PostgreSQL
only).
.sp
The number of milliseconds the server waits between attempts to
reconnect to the lease database after connectivity has been lost may
also be specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqreconnect\-wait\-time\(dq : number\-of\-milliseconds, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The default value for MySQL and PostgreSQL is 0, which disables automatic
recovery and causes the server to exit immediately upon detecting the
loss of connectivity.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqon\-fail\(dq : \(dqstop\-retry\-exit\(dq, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The possible values are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstop\-retry\-exit\fP \- disables the DHCP service while trying to automatically
recover lost connections, and shuts down the server on failure after exhausting
\fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP\&. This is the default value for the lease backend,
the host backend, and the configuration backend.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserve\-retry\-exit\fP \- continues the DHCP service while trying to
automatically recover lost connections, and shuts down the server on failure
after exhausting \fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserve\-retry\-continue\fP \- continues the DHCP service and does not shut down
the server even if the recovery fails. This is the default value for forensic
logging.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Automatic reconnection to database backends is configured individually per
backend; this allows users to tailor the recovery parameters to each backend
they use. We suggest that users enable it either for all backends or none,
so behavior is consistent.
.sp
Losing connectivity to a backend for which reconnection is disabled results
(if configured) in the server shutting itself down. This includes cases when
the lease database backend and the hosts database backend are connected to
the same database instance.
.sp
It is highly recommended not to change the \fBstop\-retry\-exit\fP default
setting for the lease manager, as it is critical for the connection to be
active while processing DHCP traffic. Change this only if the server is used
exclusively as a configuration tool.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqretry\-on\-startup\(dq : true, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
During server startup, the inability to connect to any of the configured
backends is considered fatal only if \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBfalse\fP
(the default). A fatal error is logged and the server exits, based on the idea
that the configuration should be valid at startup. Exiting to the operating
system allows nanny scripts to detect the problem.
If \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBtrue\fP, the server starts reconnection
attempts even at server startup or on reconfigure events, and honors the
action specified in the \fBon\-fail\fP parameter.
.sp
The host parameter is used by the MySQL and PostgreSQL backends.
.sp
Finally, the credentials of the account under which the server will
access the database should be set:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqlease\-database\(dq: {
        \(dquser\(dq: \(dquser\-name\(dq,
        \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
        ...
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If there is no password to the account, set the password to the empty
string \fB\(dq\(dq\fP\&. (This is the default.)
.SS Tuning Database Timeouts
.sp
In rare cases, reading or writing to the database may hang. This can be
caused by a temporary network issue, or by misconfiguration of the proxy
server switching the connection between different database instances.
These situations are rare, but users have reported
that Kea sometimes hangs while performing database IO operations.
Setting appropriate timeout values can mitigate such issues.
.sp
MySQL exposes two distinct connection options to configure the read and
write timeouts. Kea\(aqs corresponding \fBread\-timeout\fP and  \fBwrite\-timeout\fP
configuration parameters specify the timeouts in seconds. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqread\-timeout\(dq : 10, \(dqwrite\-timeout\(dq: 20, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Setting these parameters to 0 is equivalent to not specifying them, and
causes the Kea server to establish a connection to the database with the
MySQL defaults. In this case, Kea waits indefinitely for the completion of
the read and write operations.
.sp
MySQL versions earlier than 5.6 do not support setting timeouts for
read and write operations. Moreover, the \fBread\-timeout\fP and \fBwrite\-timeout\fP
parameters can only be specified for the MySQL backend; setting them for
any other backend database type causes a configuration error.
.sp
To set a timeout in seconds for PostgreSQL, use the \fBtcp\-user\-timeout\fP
parameter. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqlease\-database\(dq: { \(dqtcp\-user\-timeout\(dq : 10, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Specifying this parameter for other backend types causes a configuration
error.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The timeouts described here are only effective for TCP connections.
Please note that the MySQL client library used by the Kea servers
typically connects to the database via a UNIX domain socket when the
\fBhost\fP parameter is \fBlocalhost\fP, but establishes a TCP connection
for \fB127.0.0.1\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since Kea.2.7.4, the libdhcp_mysql.so hook library must be loaded in order to
store leases in the MySQL Lease Database Backend.
Specify the lease backend hook library location:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
    {
        // the MySQL lease backend hook library required for lease storage.
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_mysql.so\(dq
    }, ... ], ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since Kea.2.7.4, the libdhcp_pgsql.so hook library must be loaded in order to
store leases in the PostgreSQL Lease Database Backend.
Specify the lease backend hook library location.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
    {
        // the PostgreSQL lease backend hook library required for lease storage.
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_pgsql.so\(dq
    }, ... ], ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Hosts Storage
.sp
Kea is also able to store information about host reservations in the
database. The hosts database configuration uses the same syntax as the
lease database. In fact, the Kea server opens independent connections for
each purpose, be it lease or hosts information, which gives
the most flexibility. Kea can keep leases and host reservations
separately, but can also point to the same database. Currently the
supported hosts database types are MySQL and PostgreSQL.
.sp
The following configuration can be used to configure a
connection to MySQL:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: {
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\(dq,
        \(dquser\(dq: \(dqkea\(dq,
        \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
        \(dqhost\(dq: \(dqlocalhost\(dq,
        \(dqport\(dq: 3306
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Depending on the database configuration, many of the
parameters may be optional.
.sp
Please note that usage of hosts storage is optional. A user can define
all host reservations in the configuration file, and that is the
recommended way if the number of reservations is small. However, when
the number of reservations grows, it is more convenient to use host
storage. Please note that both storage methods (the configuration file and
one of the supported databases) can be used together. If hosts are
defined in both places, the definitions from the configuration file are
checked first and external storage is checked later, if necessary.
.sp
Host information can be placed in multiple stores. Operations
are performed on the stores in the order they are defined in the
configuration file, although this leads to a restriction in ordering
in the case of a host reservation addition; read\-only stores must be
configured after a (required) read\-write store, or the addition will
fail.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Kea requires the database timezone to match the system timezone.
For more details, see First\-Time Creation of the MySQL Database \%<#\:mysql-database-create> and
First\-Time Creation of the PostgreSQL Database \%<#\:pgsql-database-create>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DHCPv6 Hosts Database Configuration
.sp
Hosts database configuration is controlled through the
\fBDhcp6\fP/\fBhosts\-database\fP parameters. If enabled, the type of database must
be set to \fBmysql\fP or \fBpostgresql\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Next, the name of the database to hold the reservations must be set;
this is the name used when the lease database was created (see
Supported Backends \%<#\:supported-databases> for instructions on how to set up the
desired database type):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdatabase\-name\(dq , ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the database is located on a different system than the DHCPv6 server,
the database host name must also be specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqhost\(dq: remote\-host\-name, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Normally, the database is on the same machine as the DHCPv6 server.
In this case, set the value to the empty string:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqhost\(dq : \(dq\(dq, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Should the database use a port different than the default, it may be
specified as well:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqport\(dq : 12345, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The maximum number of times the server automatically attempts to
reconnect to the host database after connectivity has been lost may be
specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqmax\-reconnect\-tries\(dq : number\-of\-tries, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the server is unable to reconnect to the database after making the
maximum number of attempts, the server will exit. A value of 0 (the
default) disables automatic recovery and the server will exit
immediately upon detecting a loss of connectivity (MySQL and PostgreSQL
only).
.sp
The number of milliseconds the server waits between attempts to
reconnect to the host database after connectivity has been lost may also
be specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqreconnect\-wait\-time\(dq : number\-of\-milliseconds, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The default value for MySQL and PostgreSQL is 0, which disables automatic
recovery and causes the server to exit immediately upon detecting the
loss of connectivity.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqon\-fail\(dq : \(dqstop\-retry\-exit\(dq, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The possible values are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstop\-retry\-exit\fP \- disables the DHCP service while trying to automatically
recover lost connections. Shuts down the server on failure after exhausting
\fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP\&. This is the default value for MySQL and PostgreSQL.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserve\-retry\-exit\fP \- continues the DHCP service while trying to automatically
recover lost connections. Shuts down the server on failure after exhausting
\fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserve\-retry\-continue\fP \- continues the DHCP service and does not shut down the
server even if the recovery fails.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Automatic reconnection to database backends is configured individually per
backend. This allows users to tailor the recovery parameters to each backend
they use. We suggest that users enable it either for all backends or none,
so behavior is consistent.
.sp
Losing connectivity to a backend for which reconnection is disabled results
(if configured) in the server shutting itself down. This includes cases when
the lease database backend and the hosts database backend are connected to
the same database instance.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqretry\-on\-startup\(dq : true, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
During server startup, the inability to connect to any of the configured
backends is considered fatal only if \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBfalse\fP
(the default). A fatal error is logged and the server exits, based on the idea
that the configuration should be valid at startup. Exiting to the operating
system allows nanny scripts to detect the problem.
If \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBtrue\fP, the server starts reconnection
attempts even at server startup or on reconfigure events, and honors the
action specified in the \fBon\-fail\fP parameter.
.sp
Finally, the credentials of the account under which the server will
access the database should be set:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: {
        \(dquser\(dq: \(dquser\-name\(dq,
        \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
        ...
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If there is no password to the account, set the password to the empty
string \fB\(dq\(dq\fP\&. (This is the default.)
.sp
The multiple\-storage extension uses a similar syntax; a configuration is
placed into a \fBhosts\-databases\fP list instead of into a \fBhosts\-database\fP
entry, as in:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-databases\(dq: [ { \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq, ... }, ... ], ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the same host is configured both in\-file and in\-database, Kea does not issue a warning,
as it would if both were specified in the same data source.
Instead, the host configured in\-file has priority over the one configured
in\-database.
.SS Using Read\-Only Databases for Host Reservations with DHCPv6
.sp
In some deployments, the user whose name is specified in the
database backend configuration may not have write privileges to the
database. This is often required by the policy within a given network to
secure the data from being unintentionally modified. In many cases
administrators have deployed inventory databases, which contain
substantially more information about the hosts than just the static
reservations assigned to them. The inventory database can be used to
create a view of a Kea hosts database and such a view is often
read\-only.
.sp
Kea host\-database backends operate with an implicit configuration to
both read from and write to the database. If the user does not
have write access to the host database, the backend will fail to start
and the server will refuse to start (or reconfigure). However, if access
to a read\-only host database is required for retrieving reservations
for clients and/or assigning specific addresses and options, it is
possible to explicitly configure Kea to start in \(dqread\-only\(dq mode. This
is controlled by the \fBreadonly\fP boolean parameter as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhosts\-database\(dq: { \(dqreadonly\(dq: true, ... }, ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Setting this parameter to \fBfalse\fP configures the database backend to
operate in \(dqread\-write\(dq mode, which is also the default configuration if
the parameter is not specified.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBreadonly\fP parameter is only supported for MySQL and
PostgreSQL databases.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since Kea.2.7.4, the libdhcp_mysql.so hook library must be loaded in order to
store host reservations in the MySQL Host Database Backend.
Specify the lease backend hook library location:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
    {
        // the MySQL host backend hook library required for host storage.
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_mysql.so\(dq
    }, ... ], ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since Kea.2.7.4, the libdhcp_pgsql.so hook library must be loaded in order to
store host reservations in the PostgreSQL Host Database Backend.
Specify the lease backend hook library location.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: { \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
    {
        // the PostgreSQL host backend hook library required for host storage.
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_pgsql.so\(dq
    }, ... ], ... }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Tuning Database Timeouts for Hosts Storage
.sp
See Tuning Database Timeouts\&.
.SS Interface Configuration
.sp
The DHCPv6 server must be configured to listen on specific network
interfaces. The simplest network interface configuration tells the
server to listen on all available interfaces:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dq*\(dq ]
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Kea by default does not listen on global IPv6 addresses. To enable
unicast traffic see Unicast Traffic Support\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The asterisk plays the role of a wildcard and means \(dqlisten on all
interfaces.\(dq However, it is usually a good idea to explicitly specify
interface names:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth1\(dq, \(dqeth3\(dq ]
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is possible to use an interface wildcard (*) concurrently
with explicit interface names:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth1\(dq, \(dqeth3\(dq, \(dq*\(dq ]
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This format should only be used when it is
desired to temporarily override a list of interface names and listen on
all interfaces.
.sp
As with the DHCPv4 server, binding to specific addresses and disabling
re\-detection of interfaces are supported. But \fBdhcp\-socket\-type\fP is
not supported, because DHCPv6 uses only UDP/IPv6 sockets.
.sp
Interfaces are re\-detected at each reconfiguration. This behavior can be
disabled by setting the \fBre\-detect\fP value to \fBfalse\fP, for instance:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth1\(dq, \(dqeth3\(dq ],
        \(dqre\-detect\(dq: false
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The re\-detect mechanism usually removes previously detected interfaces and
then adds all currently detected interfaces.
Note that interfaces are not re\-detected when the \fBconfig\-test\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-test>
or \fBconfig\-set\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-set> commands are run.
However, new interfaces are detected and are available for use by
the new configuration passed to the \fBconfig\-test\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-test> and
\fBconfig\-set\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-set> commands.
.sp
The loopback interfaces (i.e. the \fBlo\fP or \fBlo0\fP interface) are not
configured by default, unless explicitly mentioned in the
configuration. Note that Kea requires a link\-local address (which does
not exist on all systems) or a specified unicast address, as in:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqenp0s2/2001:db8::1234:abcd\(dq ]
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Kea binds the service sockets for each interface on startup. If another
process is already using a port, then Kea logs the message and suppresses an
error. DHCP service runs, but it is unavailable on some interfaces.
.sp
The \(dqservice\-sockets\-require\-all\(dq option makes Kea require all sockets to
be successfully bound. If any opening fails, Kea interrupts the
initialization and exits with a non\-zero status. (Default is false).
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth1\(dq, \(dqeth3\(dq ],
        \(dqservice\-sockets\-require\-all\(dq: true
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Sometimes, immediate interruption isn\(aqt a good choice. The port can be
unavailable only temporary. In this case, retrying the opening may resolve
the problem. Kea provides two options to specify the retrying:
\fBservice\-sockets\-max\-retries\fP and \fBservice\-sockets\-retry\-wait\-time\fP\&.
.sp
The first defines a maximal number of retries that Kea makes to open a socket.
The zero value (default) means that the Kea doesn\(aqt retry the process.
.sp
The second defines a wait time (in milliseconds) between attempts. The default
value is 5000 (5 seconds).
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth1\(dq, \(dqeth3\(dq ],
        \(dqservice\-sockets\-max\-retries\(dq: 5,
        \(dqservice\-sockets\-retry\-wait\-time\(dq: 5000
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If \(dqservice\-sockets\-max\-retries\(dq is non\-zero and \(dqservice\-sockets\-require\-all\(dq
is false, then Kea retries the opening (if needed) but does not fail if any
socket is still not opened.
.SS IPv6 Subnet Identifier
.sp
The subnet identifier (subnet ID) is a unique number associated with a particular
subnet. In principle, it is used to associate clients\(aq leases with their
respective subnets. The server configuration must contain unique and stable
identifiers for all subnets.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Subnet IDs must be greater than zero and less than 4294967295.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following configuration assigns the specified subnet identifier
to a newly configured subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
            \(dqid\(dq: 1024,
            ...
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS IPv6 Subnet Prefix
.sp
The subnet prefix is the second way to identify a subnet. Kea can
accept non\-canonical subnet addresses; for instance,
this configuration is accepted:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
   \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
       {
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1/64\(dq,
            ...
       }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This works even if there is another subnet with the \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq prefix;
only the textual form of subnets are compared to avoid duplicates.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Abuse of this feature can lead to incorrect subnet selection
(see IPv6 Subnet Selection).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Unicast Traffic Support
.sp
When the DHCPv6 server starts, by default it listens to the DHCP traffic
sent to multicast address ff02::1:2 on each interface that it is
configured to listen on (see Interface Configuration). In some cases it is
useful to configure a server to handle incoming traffic sent to global
unicast addresses as well; the most common reason for this is to have
relays send their traffic to the server directly. To configure the
server to listen on a specific unicast address, add a slash (/) after the interface name,
followed by the global unicast
address on which the server should listen. The server will listen to this
address in addition to normal link\-local binding and listening on the
ff02::1:2 address. The sample configuration below shows how to listen on
2001:db8::1 (a global address) configured on the \fBeth1\fP interface.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth1/2001:db8::1\(dq ]
    },
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqunicast\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::1\(dq
        } ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration will cause the server to listen on \fBeth1\fP on the
link\-local address, the multicast group (ff02::1:2), and 2001:db8::1.
.sp
Usually, unicast support is associated with a server unicast option which
allows clients to send unicast messages to the server.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The unicast option was deprecated by the RFC 9915 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc9915>, trying
to add one in responses triggers a warning and the server no
longer accepts unicast direct queries (vs unicast relayed queries).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The example above
includes a server unicast option specification which causes the
client to send messages to the specified unicast address.
.sp
It is possible to mix interface names, wildcards, and interface
names/addresses in the list of interfaces. It is not possible, however,
to specify more than one unicast address on a given interface.
.sp
Care should be taken to specify proper unicast addresses, as the server
will attempt to bind to the addresses specified without any additional
checks. This approach was selected intentionally, to allow the software to
communicate over uncommon addresses if so desired.
.SS Configuration of IPv6 Address Pools
.sp
The main role of a DHCPv6 server is address assignment. For this, the
server must be configured with at least one subnet and one pool of
dynamic addresses to be managed. For example, assume that the server is
connected to a network segment that uses the 2001:db8:1::/64 prefix. The
administrator of that network decides that addresses from the range
2001:db8:1::1 to 2001:db8:1::ffff are going to be managed by the DHCPv6
server. Such a configuration can be achieved in the following way:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
       {
           \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
           \(dqpools\(dq: [
               {
                   \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\-2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq
               }
           ],
           ...
       }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that \fBsubnet\fP is defined as a simple string, but the \fBpools\fP
parameter is actually a list of pools; for this reason, the pool
definition is enclosed in square brackets, even though only one range of
addresses is specified.
.sp
Each \fBpool\fP is a structure that contains the parameters that describe
a single pool. Currently there is only one parameter, \fBpool\fP, which
gives the range of addresses in the pool.
.sp
It is possible to define more than one pool in a subnet; continuing the
previous example, further assume that 2001:db8:1:0:5::/80 should also be
managed by the server. It could be written as 2001:db8:1:0:5:: to
2001:db8:1::5:ffff:ffff:ffff, but typing so many \fBf\fP characters is cumbersome.
The pool can be expressed more simply as 2001:db8:1:0:5::/80. Both formats are
supported by \fBDhcp6\fP and they can be mixed in the pool list. For example,
the following pools could be defined:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\-2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq },
            { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1:05::/80\(dq }
        ],
        ...
    }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
White space in pool definitions is ignored, so spaces before and after
the hyphen are optional. They can be used to improve readability.
.sp
The number of pools is not limited, but for performance reasons it is
recommended to use as few as possible.
.sp
The server may be configured to serve more than one subnet. To add a
second subnet, use a command similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\-2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq }
        ]
    },
    {
        \(dqid\(dq: 2,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/64\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/64\(dq }
        ]
    },
    ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In this example, we allow the server to dynamically assign all addresses
available in the whole subnet. Although rather wasteful, it is certainly
a valid configuration to dedicate the whole /64 subnet for that purpose.
Note that the Kea server does not preallocate the leases, so there is no
danger in using gigantic address pools.
.sp
When configuring a DHCPv6 server using prefix/length notation, please
pay attention to the boundary values. When specifying that the server
can use a given pool, it is also able to allocate the first
(typically a network address) address from that pool. For example, for
pool 2001:db8:2::/64, the 2001:db8:2:: address may be assigned as well.
To avoid this, use the \fBmin\-max\fP notation.
.SS Subnet and Prefix Delegation Pools
.sp
Subnets may also be configured to delegate prefixes, as defined in RFC
9915 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc9915>, section 6.3. A subnet may
have one or more prefix delegation pools. Each pool has a prefixed
address, which is specified as a prefix (\fBprefix\fP) and a prefix length
(\fBprefix\-len\fP), as well as a delegated prefix length
(\fBdelegated\-len\fP). The delegated length must not be shorter than
(i.e. it must be numerically greater than or equal to) the prefix length.
If both the delegated and prefix lengths are equal, the server will be
able to delegate only one prefix. The delegated prefix does not have to
match the subnet prefix.
.sp
Below is a sample subnet configuration which enables prefix delegation
for the subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:d8b:1::/64\(dq,
            \(dqpd\-pools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqprefix\(dq: \(dq3000:1::\(dq,
                    \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 64,
                    \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 96
                }
            ]
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Prefix Exclude Option
.sp
For each delegated prefix, the delegating router may choose to exclude a
single prefix out of the delegated prefix as specified in RFC
6603 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc6603>\&. The requesting router must
not assign the excluded prefix to any of its downstream interfaces.
The excluded prefix is intended to be used on a link through which the delegating router
exchanges DHCPv6 messages with the requesting router. The configuration
example below demonstrates how to specify an excluded prefix within a
prefix pool definition. The excluded prefix
\fB2001:db8:1:8000:cafe:80::/72\fP will be sent to a requesting router which
includes the Prefix Exclude option in the Option Request option (ORO),
and which is delegated a prefix from this pool.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/48\(dq,
            \(dqpd\-pools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqprefix\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1:8000::\(dq,
                    \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 56,
                    \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 64,
                    \(dqexcluded\-prefix\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1:8000:cafe:80::\(dq,
                    \(dqexcluded\-prefix\-len\(dq: 72
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Overlapping Pools
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Here are some liberties and limits to the values that subnets and pools can
take in Kea configurations that are out of the ordinary:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Kea configuration case
T}	T{
Allowed
T}	T{
Comment
T}
_
T{
Overlapping subnets
T}	T{
Yes
T}	T{
Administrator consideration needs to be given to how clients are matched to
these subnets.
T}
_
T{
Overlapping address pools in one subnet
T}	T{
No
T}	T{
Startup error: DHCP6_PARSER_FAIL
T}
_
T{
Overlapping address pools in different subnets
T}	T{
Yes
T}	T{
Specifying the same address pool in different subnets can be used as an equivalent
of the global address pool. In that case, the server can assign addresses from the
same range regardless of the client\(aqs subnet. If an address from such a pool is
assigned to a client in one subnet, the same address will be renewed for this
client if it moves to another subnet. Another client in a different subnet will
not be assigned an address already assigned to the client in any of the subnets.
T}
_
T{
Address pools that are outside the subnet they are configured under
T}	T{
No
T}	T{
Startup error: DHCP6_PARSER_FAIL
T}
_
T{
Overlapping prefix delegation pools in one subnet
T}	T{
No
T}	T{
Startup error: DHCP6_PARSER_FAIL
T}
_
T{
Overlapping prefix delegation pools in different subnets
T}	T{
Yes
T}	T{
Specifying the same prefix delegation pool in different subnets can be used as an
equivalent of the global pool. In that case, the server can delegate the same
prefixes regardless of the client\(aqs subnet. If a prefix from such a pool is
delegated to a client in one subnet, the same prefix will be renewed for this
client if it moves to another subnet. Another client in a different subnet will
not be delegated a prefix already delegated to the client in any of the subnets.
T}
_
T{
Prefix delegation pools not matching the subnet prefix
T}	T{
Yes
T}	T{
It is common in many deployments to configure the prefix delegation pools not
matching the subnet prefix, e.g. a prefix pool of 3000::/96 within the
2001:db8:1::/64 subnet. Such use cases are supported by the Kea DHCPv6 server.
T}
.TE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Standard DHCPv6 Options
.sp
One of the major features of the DHCPv6 server is the ability to provide
configuration options to clients. Although there are several options
that require special behavior, most options are sent by the server only
if the client explicitly requests them. The following example shows how
to configure the addresses of DNS servers, one of the most frequently used options.
Options specified in this way are considered global and apply to all configured subnets.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
           \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
           \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
           \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
           \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::cafe, 2001:db8::babe\(dq
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBoption\-data\fP line creates a new entry in the option\-data table.
This table contains information on all global options that the server is
supposed to configure in all subnets. The \fBname\fP line specifies the
option name. (For a complete list of currently supported names, see
List of standard DHCPv6 options configurable by an administrator\&.) The next line specifies the
option code, which must match one of the values from that list. The line
beginning with \fBspace\fP specifies the option space, which must always
be set to \fBdhcp6\fP as these are standard DHCPv6 options. For other name
spaces, including custom option spaces, see Nested DHCPv6 Options (Custom Option Spaces)\&. The following line
specifies the format in which the data will be entered; use of CSV
(comma\-separated values) is recommended. Finally, the \fBdata\fP line
gives the actual value to be sent to clients. The data parameter is specified as
normal text, with values separated by commas if more than one value is
allowed.
.sp
Options can also be configured as hexadecimal values. If \fBcsv\-format\fP is
set to \fBfalse\fP, the option data must be specified as a hexadecimal string.
The following commands configure the \fBdns\-servers\fP option for all subnets
with the addresses 2001:db8:1::cafe and 2001:db8:1::babe.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
           \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
           \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
           \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: false,
           \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq20 01 0D B8 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 CA FE \e
                    20 01 0D B8 00 01 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 00 BA BE\(dq
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The value for the setting of the \fBdata\fP element is split across two
lines in this example for clarity; when entering the command, the
whole string should be entered on the same line.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Kea supports the following formats when specifying hexadecimal data:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDelimited octets\fP \- one or more octets separated by either colons or
spaces (\(dq:\(dq or \(dq \(dq). While each octet may contain one or two digits,
we strongly recommend always using two digits. Valid examples are
\(dqab:cd:ef\(dq and \(dqab cd ef\(dq.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBString of digits\fP \- a continuous string of hexadecimal digits with
or without a \(dq0x\(dq prefix. Valid examples are \(dq0xabcdef\(dq and \(dqabcdef\(dq.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Care should be taken to use proper encoding when using hexadecimal
format; Kea\(aqs ability to validate data correctness in hexadecimal is
limited.
.sp
It is also possible to specify data for binary options as
a single\-quoted text string within double quotes, as shown (note that
\fBcsv\-format\fP must be set to \fBfalse\fP):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqsubscriber\-id\(dq,
            \(dqcode\(dq: 38,
            \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
            \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: false,
            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq\(aqconvert this text to binary\(aq\(dq
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Most of the parameters in the \fBoption\-data\fP structure are optional and
can be omitted in some circumstances, as discussed in Unspecified Parameters for DHCPv6 Option Configuration\&.
Only one of \fBname\fP or \fBcode\fP
is required; it is not necessary to specify both. Space has a default value
of \fBdhcp6\fP, so this can be skipped as well if a regular (not
encapsulated) DHCPv6 option is defined. Finally, \fBcsv\-format\fP defaults to \fBtrue\fP, so it
too can be skipped, unless the option value is specified as
hexstring. Therefore, the above example can be simplified to:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
           \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::cafe, 2001:db8::babe\(dq
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Defined options are added to the response when the client requests them,
as well as any options required by a protocol. An administrator can also
specify that an option is always sent, even if a client did not
specifically request it. To enforce the addition of a particular option,
set the \fBalways\-send\fP flag to \fBtrue\fP, as in:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
           \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::cafe, 2001:db8::babe\(dq,
           \(dqalways\-send\(dq: true
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The effect is the same as if the client added the option code in the
Option Request Option (or its equivalent for vendor options), as in:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
           \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::cafe, 2001:db8::babe\(dq,
           \(dqalways\-send\(dq: true
        },
        ...
    ],
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
           \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
               {
                   \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                   \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::cafe, 2001:db8:1::babe\(dq
               },
               ...
           ],
           ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the example above, the \fBdns\-servers\fP option respects the global
\fBalways\-send\fP flag and is always added to responses, but for subnet
\fB2001:db8:1::/64\fP, the value is taken from the subnet\-level option data
specification.
.sp
Contrary to \fBalways\-send\fP, if the \fBnever\-send\fP flag is set to
\fBtrue\fP for a particular option, the server does not add it to the response.
The effect is the same as if the client removed the option code in the
Option Request Option (or its equivalent for vendor options):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
           \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::cafe, 2001:db8::babe\(dq
        },
        ...
    ],
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
           \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
           \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
               {
                   \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                   \(dqnever\-send\(dq: true
               },
               ...
           ],
           ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the example above, the \fBdns\-server\fP option is never added to responses
on subnet \fB2001:db8:1::/64\fP\&. \fBnever\-send\fP has precedence over
\fBalways\-send\fP, so if both are \fBtrue\fP the option is not added.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBalways\-send\fP and \fBnever\-send\fP flags are sticky, meaning
they do not follow the usual configuration inheritance rules.
Instead, if they are enabled at least once along the configuration
inheritance chain, they are applied \- even if they are
disabled in other places which would normally receive a higher priority.
For instance, if one of the flags is enabled in the global scope,
but disabled at the subnet level, it is enabled,
disregarding the subnet\-level setting.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBnever\-send\fP flag is less powerful than \fBlibdhcp_flex_option.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_flex_option\:.so>;
for instance, it has no effect on options managed by the server itself.
Both \fBalways\-send\fP and \fBnever\-send\fP have no effect on options
which cannot be requested, for instance from a custom space.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is possible to override options on a per\-subnet basis. If clients
connected to most subnets are expected to get the same values of
a given option, administrators should use global options; it is possible to override
specific values for a small number of subnets. On the other hand, if
different values are used in each subnet, it does not make sense to specify
global option values; rather, only subnet\-specific ones should be set.
.sp
The following commands override the global \fBdns\-servers\fP option for a
particular subnet, setting a single DNS server with address
2001:db8:1::3.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::3\(dq
                },
                ...
            ],
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In some cases it is useful to associate some options with an address or
prefix pool from which a client is assigned a lease. Pool\-specific
option values override subnet\-specific and global option values. If the
client is assigned multiple leases from different pools, the server
assigns options from all pools from which the leases have been obtained.
However, if the particular option is specified in multiple pools from
which the client obtains the leases, only one instance of this option
is handed out to the client. The server\(aqs administrator must not
try to prioritize assignment of pool\-specific options by trying to order
pool declarations in the server configuration.
.sp
The following configuration snippet demonstrates how to specify the
\fBdns\-servers\fP option, which will be assigned to a client only if the client
obtains an address from the given pool:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::100\-2001:db8:1::300\(dq,
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::10\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Options can also be specified in class or host\-reservation scope. The
current Kea options precedence order is (from most important to least): host
reservation, pool, subnet, shared network, class, global.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Beginning with Kea 2.7.4, option inclusion can also be controlled through
option class\-tagging, see Option Class\-Tagging \%<#\:option-class-tagging>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When a data field is a string and that string contains the comma (\fB,\fP;
U+002C) character, the comma must be escaped with two backslashes (\fB\e\e,\fP;
U+005C). This double escape is required because both the routine
splitting of CSV data into fields and JSON use the same escape character; a
single escape (\fB\e,\fP) would make the JSON invalid. For example, the string
\(dqEST5EDT4,M3.2.0/02:00,M11.1.0/02:00\(dq must be represented as:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqnew\-posix\-timezone\(dq,
                            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dqEST5EDT4\e\e,M3.2.0/02:00\e\e,M11.1.0/02:00\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                },
                ...
            ],
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some options are designated as arrays, which means that more than one
value is allowed. For example, the option \fBdns\-servers\fP
allows the specification of more than one IPv6 address, enabling clients
to obtain the addresses of multiple DNS servers.
.sp
Custom DHCPv6 Options describes the
configuration syntax to create custom option definitions (formats).
Creation of custom definitions for standard options is generally not
permitted, even if the definition being created matches the actual
option format defined in the RFCs. However, there is an exception to this rule
for standard options for which Kea currently does not provide a
definition. To use such options, a server administrator must
create a definition as described in Custom DHCPv6 Options in the \fBdhcp6\fP option space. This
definition should match the option format described in the relevant RFC,
but the configuration mechanism allows any option format as there is
currently no way to validate it.
.sp
The currently supported standard DHCPv6 options are listed in
the table below. \(dqName\(dq and \(dqCode\(dq are the
values that should be used as a name/code in the option\-data structures.
\(dqType\(dq designates the format of the data; the meanings of the various
types are given in List of standard DHCP option types \%<#\:dhcp-types>\&.
.SS List of standard DHCPv6 options configurable by an administrator
.TS
box center;
