.TE
.SS Enabling the Configuration Backend
.sp
Consider the following configuration snippet, which uses a MySQL configuration
database:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqmy DHCPv6 server\(dq,
        \(dqconfig\-control\(dq: {
            \(dqconfig\-databases\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\(dq,
                    \(dquser\(dq: \(dqkea\(dq,
                    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
                    \(dqhost\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq,
                    \(dqport\(dq: 3302
                }
            ],
            \(dqconfig\-fetch\-wait\-time\(dq: 20
        },
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_mysql.so\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following snippet illustrates the use of a PostgreSQL database:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqmy DHCPv6 server\(dq,
        \(dqconfig\-control\(dq: {
            \(dqconfig\-databases\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqpostgresql\(dq,
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\(dq,
                    \(dquser\(dq: \(dqkea\(dq,
                    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
                    \(dqhost\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq,
                    \(dqport\(dq: 3302
                }
            ],
            \(dqconfig\-fetch\-wait\-time\(dq: 20
        },
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_pgsql.so\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The configuration structure is almost identical to that of the DHCPv4 server
(see Enabling the Configuration Backend \%<#\:dhcp4-cb-json> for the detailed description).
.SS Kea DHCPv6 Compatibility Configuration Parameters
.sp
ISC\(aqs intention is for Kea to follow the RFC documents to promote better standards
compliance. However, many buggy DHCP implementations already exist that cannot be
easily fixed or upgraded. Therefore, Kea provides an easy\-to\-use compatibility
mode for broken or non\-compliant clients. For that purpose, the compatibility option must be
enabled to permit uncommon practices:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqcompatibility\(dq: {
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Lenient Option Parsing
.sp
By default, DHCPv6 option 16\(aqs \fBvendor\-class\-data\fP field is parsed as a set of
length\-value pairs; the same is true for tuple fields defined in custom options.
.sp
With \fB\(dqlenient\-option\-parsing\(dq: true\fP, if a length ever exceeds the rest of
the option\(aqs buffer, previous versions of Kea returned a log message \fBunable to
parse the opaque data tuple, the buffer length is x, but the tuple length is y\fP
with \fBx < y\fP; this no longer occurs. Instead, the value is considered to be the rest of the buffer,
or in terms of the log message above, the tuple length \fBy\fP becomes \fBx\fP\&.
.sp
Enabling this flag is expected to improve compatibility with devices such as RAD
MiNID.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqcompatibility\(dq: {
      \(dqlenient\-option\-parsing\(dq: true
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Starting with Kea version 2.5.8, this parsing is extended to silently ignore
client\-fqdn (39) options with some invalid domain names, and starting with Kea
version 3.1.9 to fix invalid flags, i.e. when \(aqS\(aq and \(aqN\(aq flags set to 1
the \(aqN\(aq flag is reset to 0 for compatibility with ISC DHCP behavior.
.SS Allocation Strategies in DHCPv6
.sp
A DHCP server follows a complicated algorithm to select a DHCPv6 lease for a client.
It prefers assigning specific addresses or delegated prefixes requested by the client
and the ones for which the client has reservations.
.sp
When the client requests a specific delegated prefix,
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> follows a series of steps to try to satisfy the request, in this
order:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
It searches for a lease that matches the requested prefix and prefix length.
.IP 2. 3
It searches for a lease that matches the prefix length.
.IP 3. 3
It searches for a lease with a larger address space (smaller prefix length).
.IP 4. 3
It searches for a lease with a smaller address space (larger prefix length).
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the client requests no particular
lease and has no reservations, or other clients are already using any requested leases, the server must
find another available lease within the configured pools. A server function called
an \(dqallocator\(dq is responsible in Kea for finding an available lease in such a case.
.sp
The Kea DHCPv6 server provides configuration parameters to select different allocators
at the global, shared\-network, and subnet levels. It also
allows different allocation strategies to be selected for address assignments and
prefix delegation.
.sp
Consider the following example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqallocator\(dq: \(dqiterative\(dq,
        \(dqpd\-allocator\(dq: \(dqrandom\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
                \(dqallocator\(dq: \(dqrandom\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 2,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/64\(dq,
                \(dqpd\-allocator\(dq: \(dqiterative\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The iterative allocator is globally selected for address assignments, while the
random allocator is globally selected for prefix delegation. These settings
are selectively overridden at the subnet level.
.sp
The following sections describe the supported allocators and their
recommended uses.
.SS Allocators Comparison
.sp
In the table below, we briefly compare the supported allocators, all of which
are described in detail in later sections.
.SS Comparison of the lease allocators supported by Kea DHCPv6
.TS
