.TE
.SS Iterative Allocator
.sp
This is the default allocator used by the Kea DHCPv6 server. It remembers the
last offered lease and offers the following sequential lease to the next client.
For example, it may offer addresses in this order: \fB2001:db8:1::10\fP,
\fB2001:db8:1::11\fP, \fB2001:db8:1::12\fP, and so on. Similarly, it offers the
next sequential delegated prefix after the previous one to the next client. The time to
find and offer the next lease or delegated prefix is very short; thus, this is the most performant
allocator when pool utilization is low and there is a high probability
that the next selected lease is available.
.sp
The iterative allocation underperforms when multiple DHCP servers share a lease
database or are connected to a cluster. The servers tend to offer and allocate
the same blocks of addresses to different clients independently, which causes many
allocation conflicts between the servers and retransmissions by clients. A random
allocation addresses this issue by dispersing the allocation order.
.SS Random Allocator
.sp
The random allocator uses a uniform randomization function to select offered
addresses and delegated prefixes from subnet pools. It is suitable in deployments
where multiple servers are connected
to a shared database or a database cluster. By dispersing the offered leases, the
servers minimize the risk of allocating the same lease to two different clients at
the same or nearly the same time. In addition, it improves the server\(aqs
resilience against attacks based on allocation predictability.
.sp
The random allocator is, however, slightly slower than the iterative allocator.
Moreover, it increases the server\(aqs memory consumption because it must remember
randomized leases to avoid offering them repeatedly. Memory consumption grows
with the number of offered leases; in other words, larger pools and more
clients increase memory consumption by random allocation.
.SS Free Lease Queue Allocator (Prefix Delegation Only)
.sp
This is a sophisticated allocator whose use should be considered in subnets
with highly utilized delegated prefix pools. In such cases, it can take a
considerable amount of time for the iterative or random allocator to find
an available prefix, because they must repeatedly check whether there is a
valid lease for a prefix they will offer. The number of checks can be as
high as the number of delegated prefixes in the subnet when the subnet pools
are exhausted, which can have a direct negative impact on the DHCP response time for
each request.
.sp
The Free Lease Queue (FLQ) allocator tracks lease allocations and de\-allocations
and maintains a running list of available delegated prefixes for each pool.
It allows an available lease to be selected within a constant time, regardless of
the subnet pools\(aq utilization. The allocator continuously updates the list of
free leases by removing any allocated leases and adding released or
reclaimed ones.
.sp
The following configuration snippet shows how to select the FLQ allocator
for prefix delegation in a subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
                \(dqpd\-allocator\(dq: \(dqflq\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The Free Lease Queue allocator can only be used for DHCPv6 prefix delegation.
An attempt to use this allocator for address assignment (with the \fBallocator\fP
parameter) will cause a configuration error. DHCPv6 address pools are
typically very large and their utilization is low; in this situation, the benefits
of using the FLQ allocator diminish. The amount of time required for the
allocator to populate the free lease queue would cause the server to freeze
upon startup.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
There are several considerations that the administrator should take into account
before using this allocator for prefix delegation. The FLQ allocator can heavily
impact the server\(aqs startup and reconfiguration time, because the allocator
has to populate the list of free leases for each subnet where it is used.
These delays can be observed both during the configuration reload and when
the subnets are created using \fBlibdhcp_subnet_cmds.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_subnet_cmds\:.so>\&. This allocator
increases memory consumption to hold the list of free leases,
proportional to the total size of the pools for which this allocator is used.
Finally, lease reclamation must be enabled with a low value of the
\fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP parameter, to ensure that the server frequently
collects expired leases and makes them available for allocation via the
free lease queue. Expired leases are not considered free by
the allocator until they are reclaimed by the server. See
Lease Reclamation \%<#\:lease-reclamation> for more details about the lease reclamation process.
.sp
We recommend that the FLQ allocator be selected
only after careful consideration. The server puts no restrictions on the
delegated\-prefix pool sizes used with the FLQ allocator, so we advise users to
test how long it takes for the server to load the pools before deploying the
configuration using the FLQ allocator in production. We also recommend
specifying another allocator type in the global configuration settings
and overriding this selection at the subnet or shared\-network level, to use
the FLQ allocator only for selected subnets. That way, when a new subnet is
added without an allocator specification, the global setting is used, thus
avoiding unnecessary impact on the server\(aqs startup time.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The FLQ allocator is not suitable for use with a shared lease database
(i.e., when multiple Kea servers store leases in the same database).
The servers are unaware of the expired leases reclaimed by the
sibling servers and never return them to their local free lease queues.
As a result, the servers will not be able to offer some of the available
leases to the clients. Only a server reclaiming a particular lease will
be able to offer it.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In Kea 3.1.1 a new parameter \fBadaptive\-lease\-time\-threshold\fP was added.
It can be specified at global, shared network and subnet levels and
takes a floating point value between \fB0.0\fP (excluded) and \fB1.0\fP\&.
It is disabled by default or when set to \fB1.0\fP\&. It is  only supported
by the FLQ allocator. When the occupancy rate of pools in a subnet is
above the specified value the server decreases the lease valid lifetime
to the applicable \fBmin\-valid\-lifetime\fP for new clients. Clients
renewing an already existing lease get at least the remaining lifetime
from the current lease. Since the leases expire faster, the server may
either recover more quickly or avoid pool exhaustion entirely.
.sp
The occupancy rate is defined as being the number of already assigned
delegated prefixes (including the address being assigned) divided by the total
number of prefixes in pools where the address can be allocated from
(i.e. if the query is not member of a \fBclient\-classes\fP guard of a pool,
or if the delegated prefix length is not compatible this pool is not taken
into account).
.SS Shared Free Lease Queue Allocator
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Added in Kea 3.1.9, Shared Free Lease Queue Allocator is considered
experimental and is not supported for production use.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The SFLQ Allocator may be used for address and/or prefix pools.
For clarity the feature is described below in terms of the former but
it applies equally to the latter.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Shared Free Lease Queue (SFLQ) Allocator is intended for use sites with
shared lease back ends (MySQL or PostgreSQL) and subnets with highly
utilized address pools. In such cases, it can take a considerable
amount of time for the iterative or random allocator to find an available
address, because they must repeatedly check whether there is a valid lease for
an address they will offer. The number of checks can be as high as the number
of addresses in the subnet when the subnet pools are exhausted. Since each
check requires a database query this can have a direct negative impact on
the DHCP response time for each request.
.sp
Similar to the Free Lease Queue Allocator, the SFLQ Allocator tracks lease
allocations and de\-allocations and maintains a running list of available
addresses for each address pool. These lists are referred to as SFLQ data. It
allows an available lease to be selected within a constant time, regardless
of the subnet\(aqs pools\(aq utilization. The SFLQ data is maintained through
specialized lease updates employed by the Kea server for leases that belong
to subnets that are using SFLQ allocation.
.sp
The fundamental difference between the FLQ and SFLQ Allocators is that SFLQ
allocation stores the SFLQ data centrally in the lease back end rather than
locally in Kea server memory. This allows it be shared between servers such
that they all have the same understanding of current lease state. It also means
that once created the SFLQ data is persistent and therefore does not have to
be re\-created upon Kea server startup or reconfiguration.
.sp
Because v6 address pools can be quite large and generally have a low rate of
utilization, the default Iterative Allocator should provide adequate performance.
The SFLQ Allocator can be very helpful for prefix delegation pools where the
number of prefixes in a given pool is more constrained and pool utilization
is can be quite high.
.sp
Allocators for v6 are specified independently for addresses and delegated prefixes.
SFLQ Allocator may be used for either pool type.  The following configuration snippet
shows how to select the SFLQ allocators for all pools of both types in a subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                 \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
                 \(dqallocator\(dq: \(dqshared\-flq\(dq,
                 \(dqpd\-allocator\(dq: \(dqshared\-flq\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
There are some considerations that the administrator should take into account
before using this allocator. Like FLQ, the SFLQ allocator can heavily impact the
server\(aqs startup and reconfiguration time because the allocator has to populate the
SFLQ data for each pool in each subnet where it is used. These delays can be observed
both during the configuration reload and when the subnets are created using
\fBlibdhcp_subnet_cmds.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_subnet_cmds\:.so>\&.  Unlike FLQ, these costs only occur the first
time a subnet is configured to use SFLQ or changes are made to its pools\(aq address
ranges. In other words, following a restart or reconfiguration the SFLQ data only
needs to be created for pools for which it does not already exist. Thus the impact
on subsequent restarts or reconfigurations of subnets that already use SFLQ is minimal.
.sp
Since SFLQ data is maintained in the lease back end, the cost of storing
it is in database server disc space rather than local Kea server memory. Disk use
for the SFLQ data is a fraction of what is already stored per lease and as
pool utilization increases, the size of SFLQ data decreases.
.sp
We recommend that the SFLQ allocator be selected only after careful consideration.
As discussed above, impact on startup can be substantial when introducing the use
of SFLQ. Creating the initial SFLQ data for large pools (e.g. > 500K addresses or
prefixes) may delay the server\(aqs startup by several seconds or more per pool. It is
faster for smaller pools (e.g. 65K addresses or prefixes) but per pool can add up
when there are many such pools. To reduce this startup cost we recommend specifying
another allocator type at the global level and specifying the SFLQ allocator at the
subnet or shared\-network level only for select subnets.
.sp
There is another important aspect of the SFLQ Allocator that warrants discussion.
As mentioned above when a server is using SFLQ allocation for a given pool, it uses
specialized back end updates that maintain the SFLQ data. Thus it is critical that
all servers sharing a lease back end agree on which subnets use SFLQ allocation.
If they do not agree the SFLQ data will quickly become inaccurate. Addresses may
look free when they are not and vice versa. In the case of the former, the allocation
engine will detect an existing lease and try another. In the case of the latter,
the lease will not be offered.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is strongly recommended that when configuring subnets to use or stop using
SFLQ allocation, it be done in maintenance windows such that servers are not
actively serving clients for those subnets while the changes are made. This
will avoid inconsistencies in SFLQ data.
.sp
SFLQ data is tracked in terms of pools where each pool has a start address
and an end address. Configuration changes that change the address range
of a pool or split into multiple new pools will trigger SFLQ data creation
for the new pools when the new configuration is applied.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As mentioned before when a server restarts or is reconfigured, it will iterate
over its subnets and initialize the configured allocator for that subnet\(aqs pools.
For the SFLQ Allocator, this will cause the server to request the lease back end
to create the SFLQ data for each pool in the subnet. If the SFLQ data already exists
for a pool, the request will return immediately. If not it will return once
the SFLQ data for that pool has been created. SFLQ data creation for a specific
pool is an exclusive operation. Once it has been initiated by a server, any requests
from other servers to create the SFLQ data for the same pool will block and not
return until the first request completes creating the SFLQ data for the pool.
In this way only one server actually creates the pool\(aqs SFLQ data and all others
are prohibited from using the pool until its SFLQ data exists.  This behavior
is intended to maintain some order during concurrent server restarts and
reconfigurations.
.sp
Finally, there is the topic of overlapping pools. Kea permits the configuration
of pools whose address ranges overlap other pools (see Overlapping Pools).
SFLQ Allocator generally supports these use cases as long as all such pools on
all servers:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Are configured to use the SFLQ Allocator
.IP \(bu 2
Are of the same lease type (all \fBTYPE_NA\fP or all \fBTYPE_PD\fP)
.IP \(bu 2
For prefix delegation pools, must have the same delegated_len
.UNINDENT
.sp
Overlapping prefix delegation pools in any situation other than having the
same pool declared in multiple subnets is discouraged.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Lease reclamation must be enabled with a low value for the
\fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP parameter to ensure that the server frequently
collects expired leases and makes them available for allocation via the
SFLQ data. Expired leases are not considered free by the allocator until they are
reclaimed by the server. See Lease Reclamation \%<#\:lease-reclamation> for more details about the
lease reclamation process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
SFLQ Allocator is restricted to use in pools no larger than 16777216 addresses
or prefixes.  Pools of larger capacity will cause a configuration error.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
SFLQ Allocator does not support \fBadaptive\-lease\-time\-threshold\fP\&.
.SH DATABASE CONNECTIVITY
.sp
The Kea servers (\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>) can be configured to use a variety of
database backends for leases, hosts, and configuration. They can be
configured to support automatic recovery when connectivity is lost, via
the \fBon\-fail\fP and \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP parameters.
(The \fBreconnect\-wait\-time\fP and \fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP parameters are
described in Lease Database Configuration \%<#\:database-configuration4> and Lease Database Configuration \%<#\:database-configuration6>\&.)
.sp
It is important to understand how and when automatic recovery comes into play.
Automatic recovery, when configured, only operates after a successful startup
or reconfiguration during which connectivity to all backends has been
successfully established.
.sp
During server startup, the inability to connect to any of the configured
backends is considered fatal only if \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBfalse\fP
(the default). A fatal error is logged and the server exits, based on the idea
that the configuration should be valid at startup. Exiting to the operating
system allows nanny scripts to detect the problem.
If \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBtrue\fP, the server will start reconnection
attempts even at server startup or on reconfigure events, and will honor the
action specified in the \fBon\-fail\fP parameter.
.sp
During dynamic reconfiguration, all backends are disconnected and then
reconnected using the new configuration. If connectivity to any of the
backends cannot be established, the server logs a fatal error but remains
up. It is able to process commands but does not serve clients. This
allows the configuration to be corrected via the \fBconfig\-set\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-set> or
\fBremote\-*\fP commands, if required.
.sp
During normal operations, if connectivity to any of the backends is lost and
automatic recovery for that backend is enabled, the server disconnects from the
respective backend and then attempts to reconnect. During the recovery process,
the server ceases to serve clients according to the \fBon\-fail\fP configured
option but continues to respond to commands.
.sp
The \fBon\-fail\fP parameter configures the actions the server should take when a
connection is lost. It can have one of the following values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstop\-retry\-exit\fP \- indicates that the server should stop the service
while it tries to recover the connection, and exit if recovery is not
successful after \fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserve\-retry\-exit\fP \- indicates that the server should not stop the
service while it tries to recover the connection, and exit if recovery is not
successful after \fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserve\-retry\-continue\fP \- indicates that the server should not stop the
service while it tries to recover the connection, and not exit if recovery is
not successful after \fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
If connectivity to all backends is restored, the server returns to normal
operations. If the connection cannot be restored and the server is configured
to exit, it issues a fatal error before shutdown.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 3.1.5, the memfile lease backend supports the \fBon\-fail\fP
parameter.  If an error such as \(dqno space on device\(dq occurs while writing
to the lease file and \fBon\-fail\fP is either \fBstop\-retry\-exit\fP (the
default) or \fBserve\-retry\-exit\fP the server will exit immediately.  If
\fBon\-fail\fP is \fBserve\-retry\-continue\fP the server will remain up, though
the write errors will continue until corrective action is taken.  Values other
than zero for \fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP and \fBreconnect\-wait\-time\fP are not
supported by memfile.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For Kea DHCP servers to work with database backends, the database schema must be
created and must match the version of the Kea
server. If the version check fails and the database backend is not configured
as read\-only, Kea attempts to initialize the schema.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Kea does not automatically attempt to upgrade an existing schema; doing so
might deprive users of the opportunity to take precautions, such as
backing up the database or temporarily shutting off running
Kea servers that are currently operating on the database.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The connection to the database server can optionally be protected by TLS.
Corresponding database configuration parameters for Kea servers are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBtrust\-anchor\fP specifies the Certification Authority file name or
directory path.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBcert\-file\fP specifies the client certificate file name.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBkey\-file\fP specifies the private key file name.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBssl\-mode\fP specifies the SSL mode: \fBdisable\fP, \fBprefer\fP,
\fBrequire\fP, \fBverify\-ca\fP or \fBverify\-full\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBcipher\-list\fP specifies the list of TLS ciphers (the syntax of
the content of this parameter is described in the OpenSSL ciphers
manual).
.UNINDENT
.sp
Currently the support for each database is:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
MySQL supports the whole set, except \fBssl\-mode\fP, additional configuration
must be done in the MySQL local setup, for instance certificate revocation
list, choice of a specific TLS version, mutual authentication, etc.
When a TLS connection was required but the actual connection is in
clear text an error log is emitted.
.IP \(bu 2
PostgreSQL supports the whole set, except \fBcipher\-list\fP, additional
configuration must be done in the client library (libpq). Anything else must
be done in the PostgreSQL local configuration.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more details about securing the database connection, see Securing Database Connections \%<#\:sec-database-connection>\&.
.SH LEASE EXPIRATION
.sp
The primary role of the DHCP server is to assign addresses and/or
delegate prefixes to DHCP clients. These addresses and prefixes are
often referred to as \(dqleases.\(dq Leases are typically assigned to clients
for a finite amount of time, known as the \(dqvalid lifetime.\(dq DHCP clients
who wish to continue using their assigned leases periodically renew
them by sending the appropriate message to the DHCP server. The DHCP
server records the time when these leases are renewed and calculates new
expiration times for them.
.sp
If the client does not renew a lease before its valid lifetime elapses,
the lease is considered expired. There are many situations when the
client may cease lease renewals; common scenarios include when the machine
running the client shuts down for an extended period of time, or when a
mobile device leaves the vicinity of a network.
.sp
The process through which the DHCP server makes expired leases available
for reassignment is referred to as \(dqlease reclamation,\(dq and expired
leases returned to availability through this process are referred to as
\(dqreclaimed.\(dq The DHCP server attempts to reclaim an expired lease as
soon as it detects that it has expired. The server has several possible
ways to detect expiration: it may attempt to allocate a lease to a
client but find this lease already present in the database and expired;
or it can periodically query the lease database for expired leases.
Regardless of how an expired lease is detected, it must be reclaimed
before it can be assigned to a client.
.sp
This chapter explains how to configure the server to periodically query
for the expired leases, and how to minimize the impact of the periodic
lease\-reclamation process on the server\(aqs responsiveness. Finally, it
explains \(dqlease affinity,\(dq which provides the means to assign the same
lease to a returning client after its lease has expired.
.sp
Although all configuration examples in this section are provided for the
DHCPv4 server, the same parameters may be used for DHCPv6 server
configuration.
.SS Lease Reclamation
.sp
Lease reclamation is the process through which an expired lease becomes
available for assignment to the same or a different client. This process
involves the following steps for each reclaimed lease:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Invoke callouts for the \fBlease4_expire\fP or \fBlease6_expire\fP hook
points, if hook libraries supporting those callouts are currently
loaded.
.IP \(bu 2
Update the DNS, i.e. remove any DNS entries associated with the
expired lease.
.IP \(bu 2
Update lease information in the lease database to indicate that the
lease is now available for reassignment.
.IP \(bu 2
Update counters on the server, a process that includes increasing the
number of reclaimed leases and decreasing the number of assigned
addresses or delegated prefixes.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Please refer to The DHCP\-DDNS Server \%<#\:dhcp-ddns-server> to see how to configure DNS
updates in Kea, and to Hook Libraries \%<#\:hooks-libraries> for information about
using hook libraries.
.SS Lease Reclamation Configuration Parameters
.sp
The following list presents all the configuration parameters pertaining to
processing expired leases, with their default values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP \- this parameter governs intervals
between the completion of the previous reclamation cycle and the start of the
next one. Specified in seconds; the default value is 10.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBflush\-reclaimed\-timer\-wait\-time\fP \- this parameter controls how
often the server initiates the lease reclamation procedure. Expressed in
seconds; the default value is 25. If both \fBflush\-reclaimed\-timer\-wait\-time\fP
and \fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP are not 0, when the client sends a release
message the lease is expired instead of being deleted from lease storage.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP \- this parameter governs how long the lease
should be kept after it is reclaimed. This enables lease affinity
when set to a non\-zero value. Expressed in seconds; the default value
is 3600. If both \fBflush\-reclaimed\-timer\-wait\-time\fP and
\fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP are not 0, when the client sends a release message
the lease is expired instead of being deleted from lease storage.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-reclaim\-leases\fP \- this parameter specifies the maximum number
of reclaimed leases that can be processed at one time. Zero means
unlimited (i.e. process all reclaimed leases). The default value is
100.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-reclaim\-time\fP \- this parameter specifies an upper limit to the
length of time a lease reclamation procedure can take. Zero means no time
limit. Expressed in milliseconds; the default value is 250.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBunwarned\-reclaim\-cycles\fP \- if lease reclamation limits are
specified (\fBmax\-reclaim\-leases\fP and/or \fBmax\-reclaim\-time\fP), then
under certain circumstances the server may not be able to deal with
the leases to be reclaimed fast enough. This parameter specifies how many
consecutive clean\-up cycles must end with remaining leases to be
processed before a warning is printed. The default is 5 cycles.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The parameters are explained in more detail in the rest of this chapter.
.sp
The default value for any parameter is used when the parameter is not
explicitly specified in the configuration. If the
\fBexpired\-leases\-processing\fP map is omitted entirely in the
configuration, the default values are used for all
parameters listed above.
.SS Configuring Lease Reclamation
.sp
Kea can be configured to periodically detect and reclaim expired leases.
During this process the lease entries in the database are modified or
removed. While this is happening the server does not process incoming
DHCP messages, to avoid issues with concurrent access to database
information. As a result, the server is unresponsive while lease
reclamation is performed and DHCP queries will accumulate; responses
will be sent once the lease\-reclamation cycle is complete.
.sp
In deployments where response time is critical, administrators may wish
to minimize the interruptions in service caused by lease reclamation.
To this end, Kea provides configuration parameters to control the
frequency of lease reclamation cycles, the maximum number of leases
processed in a single reclamation cycle, and the maximum amount of time
a single reclamation cycle is allowed to run before being interrupted.
The following examples demonstrate how these parameters can be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqexpired\-leases\-processing\(dq: {
        \(dqreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\(dq: 5,
        \(dqmax\-reclaim\-leases\(dq: 0,
        \(dqmax\-reclaim\-time\(dq: 0
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The first parameter is expressed in seconds and specifies an interval
between the two consecutive lease reclamation cycles. This is explained
by the following diagram:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
|  c1  |            | c2 |            |c3|            | c4 |
|<\-\-\-\->|<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<\-\->|<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<>|<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<\-\->|<\-\-
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->
|      |     5s     |    |     5s     |  |     5s     |    | time
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This diagram shows four lease\-reclamation cycles (c1 through c4) of
variable duration. The duration of the reclamation cycle
depends on the number of expired leases detected and processed in a
particular cycle. This duration is usually significantly shorter than
the interval between the cycles.
.sp
According to the \fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP, the server keeps fixed
intervals of five seconds between the end of one cycle and the start of
the next cycle. This guarantees the presence of 5\-second\-long periods during
which the server remains responsive to DHCP queries and does not perform
lease reclamation. The \fBmax\-reclaim\-leases\fP and \fBmax\-reclaim\-time\fP
are set to 0, which sets no restriction on the maximum number of leases
reclaimed in the particular cycle, or on the maximum duration of each
cycle.
.sp
In deployments with high lease\-pool utilization, relatively short valid
lifetimes, and frequently disconnecting clients which allow leases to
expire, the number of expired leases requiring reclamation at any given
time may rise significantly. In this case, it is often desirable to
apply restrictions to the maximum duration of a reclamation cycle or the
maximum number of leases reclaimed in a cycle. The following
configuration demonstrates how this can be done:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqexpired\-leases\-processing\(dq: {
        \(dqreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\(dq: 3,
        \(dqmax\-reclaim\-leases\(dq: 100,
        \(dqmax\-reclaim\-time\(dq: 50,
        \(dqunwarned\-reclaim\-cycles\(dq: 10
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In this example, the \fBmax\-reclaim\-leases\fP parameter limits the number of leases
reclaimed in a single cycle to 100, and the \fBmax\-reclaim\-time\fP limits the
maximum duration of each cycle to 50ms. The lease\-reclamation cycle will
be interrupted if either of these limitations is reached. The
reclamation of any unreclaimed leases will be attempted in subsequent
cycles.
.sp
The following diagram illustrates the behavior of the system in the
presence of many expired leases, when the limits are applied for the
reclamation cycles:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
| c1 |                | c2 |                | c3 |                | c4 |
|<\-\->|<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<\-\->|<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<\-\->|<\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->|<\-\->|<\-\-
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\->
|50ms|       3s       |50ms|       3s       |50ms|       3s       |50ms|  time
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In this case, if any reclamation cycle takes
more than 50ms, it is interrupted according to the value of the
\fBmax\-reclaim\-time\fP\&. This results in equal durations of all reclamation
cycles over time. In this example, the limitation of the
maximum 100 leases is not reached. This may be the case when database
transactions or callouts in the hook libraries attached to the
server are slow. Regardless, the chosen values for either the maximum
number of leases or a maximum cycle time strongly depend on the
particular deployment, the lease database backend being used, any
hook libraries, etc. Administrators may need to experiment to tune the
system to suit the dynamics of their deployment.
.sp
It is important to realize that with the use of these limits, there is a
risk that expired leases will accumulate faster than the server can
reclaim them. This should not be a problem if the server is dealing with
a temporary burst of expirations, because it should be able to
eventually deal with them over time. However, if leases expire at a high
rate for a long period of time, the unreclaimed leases will pile up in
the database. To notify the administrator that the current configuration
does not satisfy the needs for reclamation of expired leases, the server
issues a warning message in the log if it is unable to reclaim all
leases within several reclamation cycles. The number of cycles after
which such a warning is issued is specified with the
\fBunwarned\-reclaim\-cycles\fP configuration parameter.
.sp
Setting the \fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP to 0 disables periodic
reclamation of the expired leases.
.SS Configuring Lease Affinity
.sp
Suppose that a laptop goes into sleep mode after a period of user
inactivity. While the laptop is in sleep mode, its DHCP client does not
renew leases obtained from the server and these leases will eventually
expire. When the laptop wakes up, it is often desirable for it to
continue using its previous assigned IP addresses. To facilitate this,
the server needs to correlate returning clients with their expired
leases. When the client returns, the server first checks for those
leases and reassigns them if they have not been assigned to another
client. The ability of the server to reassign the same lease to a
returning client is referred to as \(dqlease affinity.\(dq
.sp
When lease affinity is enabled (i.e. when \fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP is configured
to a value greater than zero), the server still reclaims leases according to the
parameters described in Configuring Lease Reclamation, but the reclaimed leases
are held in the database for a specified amount of time rather than removed.
If both \fBflush\-reclaimed\-timer\-wait\-time\fP and \fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP are
greater than zero, the lease is expired immediately when the client sends a
release message, instead of being deleted from lease storage. When the client
returns, the server first verifies whether there are any reclaimed leases
associated with this client and then reassigns them if possible. However, it is
important to note that any reclaimed lease may be assigned to another client if
that client specifically asks for it. Therefore, lease affinity does not
guarantee that the reclaimed lease will be available for the client who used it
before; it merely increases the chances of the client being assigned the same
lease. If the lease pool is small \- namely, in DHCPv4, for which address space
is limited \- there is an increased likelihood that the expired lease will be
assigned to another client.
.sp
Consider the following configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqexpired\-leases\-processing\(dq: {
        \(dqreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\(dq: 3,
        \(dqhold\-reclaimed\-time\(dq: 1800,
        \(dqflush\-reclaimed\-timer\-wait\-time\(dq: 5
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP specifies how many seconds after an expiration
a reclaimed lease should be held in the database for reassignment to
the same client. In the example given above, reclaimed leases are
held for 30 minutes (1800 seconds) after their expiration. During this time,
the server will likely be able to reassign the same lease to the
returning client, unless another client specifically requests this lease and the
server assigns it.
.sp
The server must periodically remove reclaimed leases for which the time
indicated by \fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP has elapsed. The
\fBflush\-reclaimed\-timer\-wait\-time\fP parameter controls how often the
server removes such leases. In the example provided above, the server
initiates removal of such leases five seconds after the previous
removal attempt was completed. Setting this value to 0 disables lease
affinity, meaning leases are removed from the lease database
when they are reclaimed. If lease affinity is enabled, it is recommended
that the \fBhold\-reclaimed\-time\fP be set to a value significantly higher than
the \fBreclaim\-timer\-wait\-time\fP, as timely removal of expired\-reclaimed
leases is less critical than the removal process, which may impact
server responsiveness.
.sp
There is no guarantee that lease affinity will work every time; if a
server is running out of addresses, it will reassign expired addresses
to new clients. Also, clients can request specific addresses and the
server tries to honor such requests if possible. Administrators who want to
ensure a client keeps its address, even after periods of inactivity,
should consider using host reservations or leases with very long lifetimes.
.SS Reclaiming Expired Leases via Command
.sp
The \fBleases\-reclaim\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-leases-reclaim> command can be used to trigger lease reclamation at
any time. Please consult the The leases\-reclaim Command \%<#\:command-leases-reclaim> section
for details about using this command.
.SH CONGESTION HANDLING
.SS What is Congestion?
.sp
Congestion occurs when servers are subjected to client queries faster
than those queries can be processed. As a result, the servers begin accumulating
a backlog of pending queries. The longer the high rate of traffic
continues, the farther behind the servers fall. Depending on the client
implementations, those that fail to get leases either give up or simply
continue to retry forever. In the former case, the server may eventually
recover, but the latter case is a vicious cycle from which the server is
unable to escape.
.sp
Congestion typically occurs when there is a network event that causes overly large
numbers of clients to simultaneously need leases, such as recovery after
a network outage. In a well\-planned deployment, the number and capacity of servers is
matched to the maximum expected client load. If the load is routinely too
heavy, then the deployment needs to be re\-evaluated.
.sp
The goal of congestion handling is to help servers mitigate the peak in
traffic by fulfilling as many of the most relevant requests as possible
until the congestion subsides.
.SS Configuring Congestion Handling
.sp
Congestion handling
offers the ability to configure the server to use a separate thread to
read packets from the interface socket buffers. As the thread reads
packets from the buffers, they are added to an internal packet queue,
and the server\(aqs main application thread processes packets from this
queue rather than from the socket buffers. By structuring it this way, a
configurable layer has been introduced which can make decisions on which
packets to process, how to store them, and the order in which they are
processed by the server.
.sp
The default packet queue implementation for both \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
is a simple ring buffer. Once it reaches capacity, new packets get added
to the back of the queue by discarding packets from the front of the
queue. Rather than always discarding the newest packets, Kea now always
discards the oldest packets. The capacity of the buffer, i.e. the maximum
number of packets the buffer can contain, is configurable. A reasonable
starting point is to match the capacity to the number of leases
per second a specific installation of Kea can handle. This
figure varies widely depending on the specifics of an individual deployment.
.sp
As there is no one algorithm that can best handle the dynamics of all
sites, and because over time new approaches will evolve, the packet
queue is implemented as a plug\-in, which can be replaced by a custom queue
implementation via a hook library. This should make it straightforward
for interested parties to experiment with their own solutions.
(Developers can refer to \fBisc::dhcp::PacketQueue\fP and
\fBisc::dhcp::PacketQueueMgr\fP, described in the
Kea Developer\(aqs Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide/\:index\:.html>\&.)
.sp
Packet queue behavior is configured in both \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
servers through an optional, top\-level, configuration element,
\fBdhcp\-queue\-control\fP\&. Omitting this element disables packet queueing:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqdhcp\-queue\-control\(dq: {
    \(dqenable\-queue\(dq: true, // true|false
    \(dqqueue\-type\(dq: \(dqqueue type\(dq,
    \(dqcapacity\(dq : 256 // n packets
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenable\-queue\fP \- enables or disables packet queueing.
When \fBtrue\fP, the server processes packets from the packet queue, which
is filled by a separate thread. When \fBfalse\fP, the server processes
packets directly from the socket buffers in the main thread. It is
disabled (\fBfalse\fP) by default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBqueue\-type\fP \- the name of the queue implementation to use. This value
exists so that custom implementations can be registered (via a hook
library) and then selected. There is a default packet queue
implementation that is pre\-registered during server start up:
\(dqkea\-ring4\(dq for \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \(dqkea\-ring6\(dq for \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcapacity\fP \- this is the maximum number of packets the
queue can hold before packets are discarded. The optimal value for
this is extremely site\-dependent. The default value is 64 for both
\(dqkea\-ring4\(dq and \(dqkea\-ring6\(dq.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following example enables the default packet queue for \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>,
with a queue capacity of 250 packets:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq:
{
    \(dqdhcp\-queue\-control\(dq: {
       \(dqenable\-queue\(dq: true,
       \(dqqueue\-type\(dq: \(dqkea\-ring4\(dq,
       \(dqcapacity\(dq : 250
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following example enables the default packet queue for \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>,
with a queue capacity of 300 packets:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq:
{
    \(dqdhcp\-queue\-control\(dq: {
       \(dqenable\-queue\(dq: true,
       \(dqqueue\-type\(dq: \(dqkea\-ring6\(dq,
       \(dqcapacity\(dq : 300
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Congestion handling is currently incompatible with multi\-threading;
when both are enabled, congestion handling is silently disabled.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH THE DHCP-DDNS SERVER
.SS Overview
.sp
The DHCP\-DDNS Server (\fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>, known informally as D2) conducts
the client side of the Dynamic DNS protocol (DDNS, defined in RFC
2136 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2136>) on behalf of the DHCPv4
and DHCPv6 servers (\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>, respectively).
The DHCP servers construct DDNS update requests, known as NameChangeRequests
(NCRs), based on DHCP lease change events and then post them to D2. D2
attempts to match each request to the appropriate DNS server(s) and
carries out the necessary conversation with those servers to update the
DNS data.
.sp
The Kea hook library \fBlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_ddns_tuning\:.so> provides the ability
for both \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> to generate host names
procedurally based on an expression, to skip DDNS updates on a per\-client basis,
or to fine\-tune various DNS update aspects. Please refer to the libdhcp_ddns_tuning.so: DDNS Tuning \%<#\:hooks-ddns-tuning>
documentation for the configuration options.
.SS DNS Server Selection
.sp
To match a request to the appropriate DNS servers, D2 must have
a catalog of servers from which to select. In fact, D2 has two such
catalogs, one for forward DNS and one for reverse DNS; these catalogs
are referred to as \(dqDDNS domain lists.\(dq Each list consists of one or more
named DDNS domains. Further, each DDNS domain has a list of one or more
DNS servers that publish the DNS data for that domain.
.sp
When conducting forward\-domain matching, D2 compares the fully qualified
domain name (FQDN) in the request against the name of each forward DDNS
domain in its catalog. The domain whose name matches the longest portion
of the FQDN is considered the best match. For example, if the FQDN is
\(dqmyhost.sample.example.com.\(dq, and there are two forward domains in the
catalog, \(dqsample.example.com.\(dq and \(dqexample.com.\(dq, the former is
regarded as the best match. In some cases, it may not be possible to
find a suitable match. Given the same two forward domains there would be
no match for the FQDN \(dqbogus.net\(dq, so the request would be rejected.
Finally, if there are no forward DDNS domains defined, D2 simply
disregards the forward\-update portion of requests.
.sp
When conducting reverse\-domain matching, D2 constructs a reverse FQDN
from the lease address in the request and compares that against the name
of each reverse DDNS domain. Again, the domain whose name matches the
longest portion of the FQDN is considered the best match. For instance,
if the lease address is \(dq172.16.1.40\(dq and there are two reverse domains
in the catalog, \(dq1.16.172.in\-addr.arpa.\(dq and \(dq16.172.in\-addr.arpa\(dq, the
former is the best match. As with forward matching, D2 may not find a
suitable match. Given the same two domains, there would be no match for
the lease address, \(dq192.168.1.50\(dq, and the request would be rejected.
As with forward\-domain matching, if there are no reverse DDNS domains defined, D2 simply
disregards the reverse\-update portion of requests.
.SS Conflict Resolution
.sp
D2 implements the conflict resolution strategy prescribed by RFC
4703 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4703>\&. Conflict resolution is
intended to prevent different clients from mapping to the same FQDN at
the same time. To make this possible, the RFC requires that forward DNS
entries for a given FQDN must be accompanied by a DHCID resource record
(RR). This record contains a client identifier that uniquely identifies
the client to whom the name belongs. Furthermore, any DNS updater that
wishes to update or remove existing forward entries for an FQDN may only
do so if their client matches that of the DHCID RR.
.sp
In other words, the DHCID RR maps an FQDN to the client to whom it
belongs, and thereafter changes to that mapping can only be done by
or at the behest of that client.
.sp
Conflict resolution can be indirectly enabled or disabled via
the configuration parameter \fBddns\-use\-conflict\-resolution\fP, supported
by both \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>\&. These servers use this parameter to
set a flag within each NameChangeRequest they send that tells D2
whether conflict resolution should be employed for that request.
By default, conflict resolution is enabled. For more details, please refer
to discussions of \fBddns\-use\-conflict\-resolution\fP in DDNS for DHCPv4 \%<#\:dhcp4-ddns-config> and DDNS for DHCPv6 \%<#\:dhcp6-ddns-config>\&.
.sp
When conflict resolution is disabled, D2 still adds DHCID RRs but does
not use them to enforce client ownership of DNS entries. Disabling it should
only be used after careful consideration.
.SS Dual\-Stack Environments
.sp
RFC 4703, section
5.2, \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4703#\:section-5.2> describes
issues that may arise with dual\-stack clients. These are clients that
wish to have both IPv4 and IPv6 mappings for the same FQDN.
To work properly, clients must embed their IPv6 DUID
within their IPv4 client identifier option, as described in RFC
4361 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4361>\&. In this way, DNS updates
for both IPv4 and IPv6 can be managed under the same DHCID RR. This feature
is supported by Kea beginning with release 2.1.2.
.SS Starting and Stopping the DHCP\-DDNS Server
.sp
\fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> is the Kea DHCP\-DDNS server and, due to the nature of
DDNS, it runs alongside either the DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 component (or both).
Like other parts of Kea, it is a separate binary that can be run on its
own or through \fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> (see Managing Kea with keactrl \%<#\:keactrl>). In normal
operation, controlling \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> with \fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> is
recommended; however, it is also possible to run the DHCP\-DDNS server
directly. It accepts the following command\-line switches:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-c file\fP \- specifies the configuration file. This is the only
mandatory switch.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-d\fP \- specifies whether server logging should be switched to
debug/verbose mode. In verbose mode, the logging severity and
debuglevel specified in the configuration file are ignored and
\(dqdebug\(dq severity and the maximum debuglevel (99) are assumed. The
flag is convenient for temporarily switching the server into maximum
verbosity, e.g. when debugging.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-t file\fP \- specifies the configuration file to be tested.
\fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> attempts to load it and conducts sanity checks.
Certain checks are possible only while running the actual
server. The actual status is reported with an exit code (0 =
configuration looks okay, 1 = error encountered). Kea prints out log
messages to standard output and errors to standard error when testing
the configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-v\fP \- displays the Kea version and exits.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-V\fP \- displays the extended Kea version and exits.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-W\fP \- displays the Kea configuration report and exits. The report
is a copy of the \fBconfig.report\fP file produced by \fBmeson setup\fP;
it is embedded in the executable binary.
.sp
The contents of the \fBconfig.report\fP file may also be accessed by examining
certain libraries in the installation tree or in the source tree.
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# from installation using libkea\-process.so
$ strings ${prefix}/lib/libkea\-process.so | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libkea\-process.so
$ strings src/lib/process/.libs/libkea\-process.so | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libkea\-process.a
$ strings src/lib/process/.libs/libkea\-process.a | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libcfgrpt.a
$ strings src/lib/process/cfgrpt/.libs/libcfgrpt.a | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-X\fP \- As of Kea 3.0, disables security restrictions. The server will
still check for violations but will emit warning logs when they are found
rather than fail with an error. Please see
Kea Runtime Security Policy Checking \%<#\:sec-kea-runtime-security-policy-checking> for details.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Upon startup, the module loads its configuration and begins listening
for NCRs based on that configuration.
.sp
During startup, the server attempts to create a PID file of the
form: \fB[pidfile_dir]/[conf name].kea\-dhcp\-ddns.pid\fP where:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpidfile_dir\fP \- is \fB[prefix]/[localstatedir]/run/kea\fP where
\fBprefix\fP and \fBlocalstatedir\fP are the values passed into meson setup using
\fB\-\-prefix\fP and \fB\-\-localstatedir\fP which default to \fB/usr/local\fP and
\fBvar\fP respectively. So the whole \fBpidfile_dir\fP defaults to
\fB/usr/local/var\fP\&. Note that this value may be overridden at runtime by
setting the environment variable  \fBKEA_PIDFILE_DIR\fP intended primarily for
testing purposes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconf name\fP \- is the configuration file name used to start the server,
minus all preceding paths and the file extension. For example, given
a pathname of \fB/usr/local/etc/kea/myconf.txt\fP, the portion used would
be \fBmyconf\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the file already exists and contains the PID of a live process, the
server issues a \fBDHCP_DDNS_ALREADY_RUNNING\fP log message and exits. It
is possible, though unlikely, that the file is a remnant of a system
crash and the process to which the PID belongs is unrelated to Kea. In
such a case it is necessary to manually delete the PID file.
.SS Configuring the DHCP\-DDNS Server
.sp
Before starting the \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> module for the first time, a
configuration file must be created. The following default configuration
is a template that can be customized to individual requirements.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq,
    \(dqport\(dq: 53001,
    \(dqdns\-server\-timeout\(dq: 500,
    \(dqncr\-protocol\(dq: \(dqUDP\(dq,
    \(dqncr\-format\(dq: \(dqJSON\(dq,
    \(dqtsig\-keys\(dq: [ ],
    \(dqforward\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [ ]
    },
    \(dqreverse\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [ ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The configuration can be divided into the following sections, each of
which is described below:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fIGlobal Server Parameters\fP \- define values which control connectivity and
global server behavior.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIControl Sockets\fP \- defines the Control Socket list.
.IP \(bu 2
\fITSIG Key Info\fP \- defines the TSIG keys used for secure traffic with
DNS servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIForward DDNS\fP \- defines the catalog of forward DDNS domains.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIReverse DDNS\fP \- defines the catalog of reverse DDNS domains.
.UNINDENT
.SS Global Server Parameters
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBip\-address\fP \- the IP address on which D2 listens for requests. The
default is the local loopback interface at address 127.0.0.1.
Either an IPv4 or IPv6 address may be specified.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBport\fP \- the port on which D2 listens for requests. The default value
is 53001.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdns\-server\-timeout\fP \- the maximum amount of time, in milliseconds,
that D2 will wait for a response from a DNS server to a single DNS
update message.  The default is 500 ms.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBncr\-protocol\fP \- the socket protocol to use when sending requests to
D2. Currently only UDP is supported.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBncr\-format\fP \- the packet format to use when sending requests to D2.
Currently only JSON format is supported.
.UNINDENT
.sp
D2 must listen for change requests on a known address and port. By
default it listens at 127.0.0.1 on port 53001. The following example
illustrates how to change D2\(aqs global parameters so it will listen at
192.168.1.10 port 900:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.168.1.10\(dq,
    \(dqport\(dq: 900,
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is possible for a malicious attacker to send bogus
NameChangeRequests to the DHCP\-DDNS server. Addresses other than the
IPv4 or IPv6 loopback addresses (127.0.0.1 or ::1) should only be
used for testing purposes; note that local users may still
communicate with the DHCP\-DDNS server.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If the \fBip\-address\fP and \fBport\fP are changed, the corresponding values in
the DHCP servers\(aq \fBdhcp\-ddns\fP configuration section must be changed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Management API for the D2 Server
.sp
The management API allows the issuing of specific management commands,
such as configuration retrieval or shutdown. For more details, see
Management API \%<#\:ctrl-channel>\&. By default there are no sockets
open; to instruct Kea to open a socket, the following entry in the
configuration file can be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqcontrol\-sockets\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq,
            \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dq/path/to/the/unix/socket\(dq
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS UNIX Control Socket
.sp
Until Kea server 2.7.2 the only supported communication channel type was
the UNIX stream socket with \fBsocket\-type\fP set to \fBunix\fP and
\fBsocket\-name\fP to the file path of the UNIX/LOCAL socket.
.sp
The length of the path specified by the \fBsocket\-name\fP parameter is
restricted by the maximum length for the UNIX socket name on the
operating system, i.e. the size of the \fBsun_path\fP field in the
\fBsockaddr_un\fP structure, decreased by 1. This value varies on
different operating systems, between 91 and 107 characters. Typical
values are 107 on Linux and 103 on FreeBSD.
.sp
Kea supports only one \fBunix\fP control socket in the \(dqcontrol\-sockets\(dq list.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, control sockets may only reside in the directory
determined during compilation as \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/run/kea\(dq\fP,
which must also have \fB0750\fP access rights. This path may be overridden
at startup by setting the environment variable \fBKEA_CONTROL_SOCKET_DIR\fP
to the desired path.  If a path other than this value is used in
\fBsocket\-name\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start or, if already
running, log an unrecoverable error.  For ease of use in simply omit the
path component from \fBsocket\-name\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Communication over the control channel is conducted using JSON structures.
See the Control Channel section in the Kea Developer\(aqs
Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide/\:d2/\:d96/\:ctrlSocket\:.html>
for more details.
.sp
The D2 server supports the following operational commands:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbuild\-report\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-build-report>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-get>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-hash\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-hash-get>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-reload\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-reload>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-set\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-set>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-test\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-test>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-write\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-write>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlist\-commands\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-list-commands>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshutdown\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-shutdown>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstatus\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-status-get>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBversion\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-version-get>
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since Kea version 2.0.0, the D2 server also supports the following
operational commands for statistics:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstatistic\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-statistic-get>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstatistic\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-statistic-get>\-all
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstatistic\-reset\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-statistic-reset>
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstatistic\-reset\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-statistic-reset>\-all
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBshutdown\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-shutdown> command supports the extra \fBtype\fP argument, which controls the
way the D2 server cleans up on exit.
The supported shutdown types are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnormal\fP \- stops the queue manager and finishes all current transactions
before exiting. This is the default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdrain_first\fP \- stops the queue manager but continues processing requests
from the queue until it is empty.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnow\fP \- exits immediately.
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqshutdown\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqexit\-value\(dq: 3,
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqdrain_first\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS HTTP/HTTPS Control Socket
.sp
The \fBsocket\-type\fP must be \fBhttp\fP or \fBhttps\fP (when the type is \fBhttps\fP
TLS is required). The \fBsocket\-address\fP (default \fB127.0.0.1\fP) and
\fBsocket\-port\fP (default 8000) specify an IP address and port to which
the HTTP service will be bound.
.sp
The \fBtrust\-anchor\fP, \fBcert\-file\fP, \fBkey\-file\fP, and \fBcert\-required\fP
parameters specify the TLS setup for HTTP, i.e. HTTPS. If these parameters
are not specified, HTTP is used. The TLS/HTTPS support in Kea is
described in TLS/HTTPS Support \%<#\:tls>\&.
.sp
Basic HTTP authentication protects
against unauthorized uses of the server by local users. For
protection against remote attackers, HTTPS and reverse proxy
provide stronger security.
.sp
The authentication is described in the \fBauthentication\fP block
with the mandatory \fBtype\fP parameter, which selects the authentication.
Currently only the basic HTTP authentication (type basic) is supported.
.sp
The \fBrealm\fP authentication parameter (default \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\-server\fP
is used for error messages when the basic HTTP authentication is required
but the client is not authorized.
.sp
When the \fBclients\fP authentication list is configured and not empty,
basic HTTP authentication is required. Each element of the list
specifies a user ID and a password. The user ID is mandatory, must
not be empty, and must not contain the colon (:) character. The
password is optional; when it is not specified an empty password
is used.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The basic HTTP authentication user ID and password are encoded
in UTF\-8, but the current Kea JSON syntax only supports the Latin\-1
(i.e. 0x00..0xff) Unicode subset.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To avoid exposing the user ID and/or the associated
password, these values can be read from files. The syntax is extended by:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBdirectory\fP authentication parameter, which handles the common
part of file paths. The default value is the empty string.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBpassword\-file\fP client parameter, which, alongside the \fBdirectory\fP
parameter, specifies the path of a file that can contain the password,
or when no user ID is given, the whole basic HTTP authentication secret.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBuser\-file\fP client parameter, which, with the \fBdirectory\fP parameter,
specifies the path of a file where the user ID can be read.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since Kea\-2.7.6 Kea supports multiple HTTP/HTTPS connections.
Both IPv4 and IPv6 addresses can be used.
The server will issue an error when changing the socket type from HTTP to HTTPS
or from HTTPS to HTTP using the same address and port. This action is not
allowed as it might introduce a security issue accidentally caused by a user
mistake.
A different address or port must be specified when using the \(dqconfig\-set\(dq
command to switch from HTTP to HTTPS or from HTTPS to HTTP. The same applies
when modyfying the configuration file and then running \(dqconfig\-reload\(dq command.
.sp
When files are used, they are read when the configuration is loaded,
to detect configuration errors as soon as possible.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqcontrol\-sockets\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqhttps\(dq,
            \(dqsocket\-address\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.40\(dq,
            \(dqsocket\-port\(dq: 8005,
            \(dqtrust\-anchor\(dq: \(dq/path/to/the/ca\-cert.pem\(dq,
            \(dqcert\-file\(dq: \(dq/path/to/the/agent\-cert.pem\(dq,
            \(dqkey\-file\(dq: \(dq/path/to/the/agent\-key.pem\(dq,
            \(dqcert\-required\(dq: true,
            \(dqauthentication\(dq: {
                \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqbasic\(dq,
                \(dqrealm\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp\-ddns\-server\(dq,
                \(dqclients\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dquser\(dq: \(dqadmin\(dq,
                    \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq
                } ]
            }
        },
        {
            \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqhttp\(dq,
            \(dqsocket\-address\(dq: \(dq2010:30:40::50\(dq,
            \(dqsocket\-port\(dq: 8004
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS TSIG Key List
.sp
A DDNS protocol exchange can be conducted with or without a transaction
signature, or TSIG (defined
in RFC 2845 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2845>). This
configuration section allows the administrator to define the set of TSIG
keys that may be used in such exchanges.
.sp
To use TSIG when updating entries in a DNS domain, a key must be defined
in the TSIG key list and referenced by name in that domain\(aqs
configuration entry. When D2 matches a change request to a domain, it
checks whether the domain has a TSIG key associated with it. If so, D2
uses that key to sign DNS update messages sent to and verify
responses received from the domain\(aqs DNS server(s). For each TSIG key
required by the DNS servers that D2 is working with, there must be
a corresponding TSIG key in the TSIG key list.
.sp
As one might gather from the name, the \fBtsig\-key\fP section of the D2
configuration lists the TSIG keys. Each entry describes a TSIG key used
by one or more DNS servers to authenticate requests and sign responses.
Every entry in the list has three parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP \- is a unique text label used to identify this key within the
list. This value is used to specify which key (if any) should be used
when updating a specific domain. As long as the name is unique its
content is arbitrary, although for clarity and ease of maintenance it
is recommended that it match the name used on the DNS server(s). This
field cannot be blank.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBalgorithm\fP \- specifies which hashing algorithm should be used with
this key. This value must specify the same algorithm used for the key
on the DNS server(s). The supported algorithms are listed below:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
HMAC\-MD5
.IP \(bu 2
HMAC\-SHA1
.IP \(bu 2
HMAC\-SHA224
.IP \(bu 2
HMAC\-SHA256
.IP \(bu 2
HMAC\-SHA384
.IP \(bu 2
HMAC\-SHA512
.UNINDENT
.sp
This value is not case\-sensitive.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdigest\-bits\fP \- is used to specify the minimum truncated length in
bits. The default value 0 means truncation is forbidden; non\-zero
values must be an integral number of octets, and be greater than both
80 and half of the full length. (Note that in BIND 9 this parameter
is appended to the algorithm name, after a dash.)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsecret\fP \- is used to specify the shared secret key code for this
key. This value is case\-sensitive and must exactly match the value
specified on the DNS server(s). It is a base64\-encoded text value.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsecret\-file\fP \- since Kea 2.5.8, this more secure alternative is supported.
This value specifies the file name where the secret can be found, i.e. the base64\-encoded
secret is the content of the file.
.UNINDENT
.sp
As an example, suppose that a domain D2 will be updating is maintained
by a BIND 9 DNS server, which requires dynamic updates to be secured
with TSIG. Suppose further that the entry for the TSIG key in BIND 9\(aqs
named.conf file looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
:
key \(dqkey.four.example.com.\(dq {
    algorithm hmac\-sha224;
    secret \(dqbZEG7Ow8OgAUPfLWV3aAUQ==\(dq;
};
:
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
By default, the TSIG key list is empty:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
   \(dqtsig\-keys\(dq: [ ],
   ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A new key must be added to the list:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqtsig\-keys\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkey.four.example.com.\(dq,
            \(dqalgorithm\(dq: \(dqHMAC\-SHA224\(dq,
            \(dqsecret\(dq: \(dqbZEG7Ow8OgAUPfLWV3aAUQ==\(dq
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
These steps must be repeated for each TSIG key needed, although the
same TSIG key can be used with more than one domain.
.SS Forward DDNS
.sp
The forward DDNS section is used to configure D2\(aqs forward\-update
behavior. Currently it contains a single parameter, the catalog of
forward DDNS domains, which is a list of structures.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqforward\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [ ]
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
By default, this list is empty, which causes the server to ignore
the forward\-update portions of requests.
.SS Adding Forward DDNS Domains
.sp
A forward DDNS domain maps a forward DNS zone to a set of DNS servers
which maintain the forward DNS data (i.e. name\-to\-address mapping) for
that zone. Each zone served needs one forward DDNS domain.
Some or all of the zones may be maintained by the same
servers, but one DDNS domain is still needed for each zone. Remember that
matching a request to the appropriate server(s) is done by zone and a
DDNS domain only defines a single zone.
.sp
This section describes how to add forward DDNS domains; repeat these
steps for each forward DDNS domain desired. Each forward DDNS domain has
the following parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP \- this is the fully qualified domain name (or zone) that this DDNS
domain can update. This value is compared against the request FQDN
during forward matching. It must be unique within the catalog.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkey\-name\fP \- if TSIG is used with this domain\(aqs servers, this value
should be the name of the key from the TSIG key list. If the
value is blank (the default), TSIG will not be used in DDNS
conversations with this domain\(aqs servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdns\-servers\fP \- this is a list of one or more DNS servers which can conduct
the server side of the DDNS protocol for this domain. The servers are
used in a first\-to\-last preference; in other words, when D2 begins to
process a request for this domain, it will pick the first server in
this list and attempt to communicate with it. If that attempt fails,
D2 will move to the next one in the list and so on, until either it
is successful or the list is exhausted.
.UNINDENT
.sp
To create a new forward DDNS domain, add a new domain element and set
its parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqforward\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqother.example.com.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is possible to add a domain without any servers; however, if that
domain matches a request, the request will fail. To make the domain
useful, at least one DNS server must be added to it.
.SS Adding Forward DNS Servers
.sp
This section describes how to add DNS servers to a forward DDNS domain.
Repeat these instructions as needed for all the servers in each domain.
.sp
Forward DNS server entries represent actual DNS servers which support
the server side of the DDNS protocol. Each forward DNS server has the
following parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhostname\fP \- the resolvable host name of the DNS server; this
parameter is not yet implemented.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBip\-address\fP \- the IP address at which the server listens for DDNS
requests. This may be either an IPv4 or an IPv6 address.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBport\fP \- the port on which the server listens for DDNS requests. It
defaults to the standard DNS service port of 53.
.UNINDENT
.sp
To create a new forward DNS server, a new server element must be added to
the domain and its parameters filled in. If, for example, the service is
running at \(dq172.88.99.10\(dq, set the forward DNS server as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqforward\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqother.example.com.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.88.99.10\(dq,
                        \(dqport\(dq: 53
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Since \fBhostname\fP is not yet supported, the parameter \fBip\-address\fP
must be set to the address of the DNS server.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Reverse DDNS
.sp
The reverse DDNS section is used to configure D2\(aqs reverse update
behavior, and the concepts are the same as for the forward DDNS section.
Currently it contains a single parameter, the catalog of reverse DDNS
domains, which is a list of structures.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqreverse\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [ ]
    },
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
By default, this list is empty, which causes the server to ignore
the reverse\-update portions of requests.
.SS Adding Reverse DDNS Domains
.sp
A reverse DDNS domain maps a reverse DNS zone to a set of DNS servers
which maintain the reverse DNS data (address\-to\-name mapping) for that
zone. Each zone served needs one reverse DDNS domain.
Some or all of the zones may be maintained by the same servers, but
one DDNS domain entry is needed for each zone. Remember that
matching a request to the appropriate server(s) is done by zone and a
DDNS domain only defines a single zone.
.sp
This section describes how to add reverse DDNS domains; repeat these
steps for each reverse DDNS domain desired. Each reverse DDNS domain has
the following parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP \- this is the fully qualified reverse zone that this DDNS domain can
update. This is the value used during reverse matching, which
compares it with a reversed version of the request\(aqs lease address.
The zone name should follow the appropriate standards; for example,
to support the IPv4 subnet 172.16.1, the name should be
\(dq1.16.172.in\-addr.arpa.\(dq. Similarly, to support an IPv6 subnet of
2001:db8:1, the name should be \(dq1.0.0.0.8.B.D.0.1.0.0.2.ip6.arpa.\(dq
The name must be unique within the catalog.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkey\-name\fP \- if TSIG is used with this domain\(aqs servers,
this value should be the name of the key from the TSIG key list. If
the value is blank (the default), TSIG will not be used in DDNS
conversations with this domain\(aqs servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdns\-servers\fP \- this is a list of one or more DNS servers which can conduct
the server side of the DDNS protocol for this domain. Currently, the
servers are used in a first\-to\-last preference; in other words, when
D2 begins to process a request for this domain, it will pick the
first server in this list and attempt to communicate with it. If that
attempt fails, D2 will move to the next one in the list and so on,
until either it is successful or the list is exhausted.
.UNINDENT
.sp
To create a new reverse DDNS domain, a new domain element must be added
and its parameters set. For example, to support subnet 2001:db8:1::, the
following configuration could be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqreverse\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq1.0.0.0.8.B.D.0.1.0.0.2.ip6.arpa.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is possible to add a domain without any servers; however, if that
domain matches a request, the request will fail. To make the domain
useful, at least one DNS server must be added to it.
.SS Adding Reverse DNS Servers
.sp
This section describes how to add DNS servers to a reverse DDNS domain.
Repeat these instructions as needed for all the servers in each domain.
.sp
Reverse DNS server entries represent actual DNS servers which support
the server side of the DDNS protocol. Each reverse DNS server has the
following parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhostname\fP \- the resolvable host name of the DNS server; this value
is currently ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBip\-address\fP \- the IP address at which the server listens for DDNS
requests.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBport\fP \- the port on which the server listens for DDNS requests. It
defaults to the standard DNS service port of 53.
.UNINDENT
.sp
To create a new reverse DNS server, a new server
element must be added to the domain and its parameters specified. If, for example, the
service is running at \(dq172.88.99.10\(dq, then set it as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqreverse\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq1.0.0.0.8.B.D.0.1.0.0.2.ip6.arpa.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.88.99.10\(dq,
                        \(dqport\(dq: 53
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Since \fBhostname\fP is not yet supported, the parameter \fBip\-address\fP
must be set to the address of the DNS server.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Per\-DNS\-Server TSIG Keys
.sp
Since Kea version 2.0.0, a TSIG key can be specified in a DNS server
configuration. The priority rule is:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
if a not\-empty key name is specified in a DNS server entry, this TSIG
key protects DNS updates sent to this server.
.IP \(bu 2
if the DNS server entry is empty, but a
not\-empty key name is specified in the parent\(aqs domain entry, the parent domain\(aqs
TSIG key protects DNS updates sent to this server.
.IP \(bu 2
if the DNS server entry is empty, and no key name is specified in its parent
domain entry, no TSIG protects DNS updates sent to this server.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For instance, in this configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqforward\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqother.example.com.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.88.99.10\(dq,
                        \(dqport\(dq: 53
                    },
                    {
                        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.88.99.11\(dq,
                        \(dqport\(dq: 53,
                        \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqreverse\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq1.0.0.0.8.B.D.0.1.0.0.2.ip6.arpa.\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.88.99.12\(dq,
                        \(dqport\(dq: 53
                    },
                    {
                        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.88.99.13\(dq,
                        \(dqport\(dq: 53,
                        \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq
                    }
                ]
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqtsig\-keys\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
            \(dqalgorithm\(dq: \(dqHMAC\-MD5\(dq,
            \(dqsecret\(dq: \(dqLSWXnfkKZjdPJI5QxlpnfQ==\(dq
        },
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq,
            \(dqalgorithm\(dq: \(dqHMAC\-SHA224\(dq,
            \(dqsecret\(dq: \(dqbZEG7Ow8OgAUPfLWV3aAUQ==\(dq
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The 172.88.99.10 server will use the \(dqfoo\(dq TSIG key, the 172.88.99.11 and
172.88.99.13 servers will use the \(dqbar\(dq key. and 172.88.99.12 will not use TSIG.
.SS User Contexts in DDNS
.sp
See Comments and User Context \%<#\:user-context> for additional background regarding the user
context idea.
.sp
User contexts can be specified on a global scope, a DDNS domain, a DNS server,
a TSIG key, and loggers. One other useful usage is the ability to store
comments or descriptions; the parser translates a \(dqcomment\(dq entry into a
user context with the entry, which allows a comment to be attached
inside the configuration itself.
.SS Example DHCP\-DDNS Server Configuration
.sp
This section provides a sample DHCP\-DDNS server configuration, based on
a small example network. Let\(aqs suppose our example network has three
domains, each with their own subnet.
.SS Our example network
.TS
box center;
l|l|l|l.
T{
Domain
T}	T{
Subnet
T}	T{
Forward DNS
Servers
T}	T{
Reverse DNS
Servers
T}
_
T{
four.example.com
T}	T{
192.0.2.0/24
T}	T{
172.16.1.5,
172.16.2.5
T}	T{
172.16.1.5,
172.16.2.5
T}
_
T{
six.example.com
T}	T{
2001:db8:1::/64
T}	T{
3001:1::50
T}	T{
3001:1::51
T}
_
T{
example.com
T}	T{
192.0.0.0/16
T}	T{
172.16.2.5
T}	T{
172.16.2.5
T}
.TE
.sp
We need to construct three forward DDNS domains:
.SS Forward DDNS domains needed
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
#
T}	T{
DDNS Domain Name
T}	T{
DNS Servers
T}
_
T{
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
.UNINDENT
T}	T{
four.example.com.
T}	T{
172.16.1.5, 172.16.2.5
T}
_
T{
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 2. 3
.UNINDENT
T}	T{
six.example.com.
T}	T{
3001:1::50
T}
_
T{
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 3. 3
.UNINDENT
T}	T{
example.com.
T}	T{
172.16.2.5
T}
.TE
.sp
As discussed earlier, FQDN\-to\-domain matching is based on the longest
match. The FQDN \(dqmyhost.four.example.com.\(dq matches the first domain
(\(dqfour.example.com\(dq), while \(dqadmin.example.com.\(dq matches the third
domain (\(dqexample.com\(dq). The FQDN \(dqother.example.net.\(dq fails to
match any domain and is rejected.
.sp
The following example configuration specifies the forward DDNS domains.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqcomment\(dq: \(dqexample configuration: forward part\(dq,
    \(dqforward\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfour.example.com.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.16.1.5\(dq },
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.16.2.5\(dq }
                ]
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqsix.example.com.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::1\(dq }
                ]
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqexample.com.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.16.2.5\(dq }
                ],
                \(dquser\-context\(dq: { \(dqbackup\(dq: false }
            },
            ...
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Similarly, we need to construct the three reverse DDNS domains:
.SS Reverse DDNS domains needed
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
#
T}	T{
DDNS Domain Name
T}	T{
DNS Servers
T}
_
T{
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
.UNINDENT
T}	T{
2.0.192.in\-addr.arpa.
T}	T{
172.16.1.5, 172.16.2.5
T}
_
T{
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 2. 3
.UNINDENT
T}	T{
1.0.0.0.8.d.b.0.1.0.0.2.ip6.arpa.
T}	T{
3001:1::50
T}
_
T{
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 3. 3
.UNINDENT
T}	T{
0.182.in\-addr.arpa.
T}	T{
172.16.2.5
T}
.TE
.sp
An address of \(dq192.0.2.150\(dq matches the first domain,
\(dq2001:db8:1::10\(dq matches the second domain, and \(dq192.0.50.77\(dq matches the
third domain.
.sp
These reverse DDNS domains are specified as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
    \(dqcomment\(dq: \(dqexample configuration: reverse part\(dq,
    \(dqreverse\-ddns\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-domains\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq2.0.192.in\-addr.arpa.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.16.1.5\(dq },
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.16.2.5\(dq }
                ]
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq1.0.0.0.8.B.D.0.1.0.0.2.ip6.arpa.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::1\(dq }
                ]
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq0.192.in\-addr.arpa.\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqdns\-servers\(dq: [
                    { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq172.16.2.5\(dq }
                ]
            },
            ...
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DHCP\-DDNS Server Statistics
.sp
Kea version 2.0.0 introduced statistics support for DHCP\-DDNS.
.sp
Statistics are divided into three groups: NameChangeRequests, DNS updates,
and per\-TSIG\-key DNS updates. While the statistics of the first two groups
are cumulative, i.e. not affected by configuration change or reload,
per\-key statistics are reset to 0 when the underlying object is
(re)created.
.sp
Currently Kea\(aqs statistics management has the following limitations:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
only integer samples (i.e. a counter and a timestamp) are used;
.IP \(bu 2
the maximum sample count is 1;
.IP \(bu 2
there is no API to remove one or all statistics;
.IP \(bu 2
there is no API to set the maximum sample count or age.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Hook libraries, such as the GSS\-TSIG library,
make new statistics available in Kea.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
More information about Kea statistics can be found at Statistics \%<#\:stats>\&.
.SS NCR Statistics
.sp
The NameChangeRequest statistics are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBncr\-received\fP \- the number of received valid NCRs
.IP \(bu 2
\fBncr\-invalid\fP \- the number of received invalid NCRs
.IP \(bu 2
\fBncr\-error\fP \- the number of errors in NCR receptions other than an I/O cancel on shutdown
.IP \(bu 2
\fBqueue\-mgr\-queue\-full\fP \- the number of times the NCR receive queue reached maxium capacity
.UNINDENT
.SS DNS Update Statistics
.sp
The global DNS update statistics are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-sent\fP \- the number of DNS updates sent
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-signed\fP \- the number of DNS updates sent and protected by TSIG
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-unsigned\fP \- the number of DNS updates sent and not protected by TSIG
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-success\fP \- the number of DNS updates which successfully completed
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-timeout\fP \- the number of DNS updates which completed on timeout
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-error\fP \- the number of DNS updates which completed with an error other than
timeout
.UNINDENT
.SS Per\-TSIG\-Key DNS Update Statistics
.sp
The per TSIG key DNS update statistics are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-sent\fP \- the number of DNS updates sent
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-success\fP \- the number of DNS updates which successfully completed
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-timeout\fP \- the number of DNS updates which completed on timeout
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdate\-error\fP \- the number of DNS updates which completed with an error other than
timeout
.UNINDENT
.sp
The name format for per\-key statistics is \fBkey[<key\-DNS\-name>].<stat\-name>\fP:
for instance, the name of the \fBupdate\-sent\fP statistics for the
\fBkey.example.com.\fP TSIG key is \fBkey[key.example.com.].update\-sent\fP\&.
.SS DHCP\-DDNS Server Limitations
.sp
The following are the current limitations of the DHCP\-DDNS server.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Requests received from the DHCP servers are placed in a queue until
they are processed. Currently, all queued requests are lost if the
server shuts down.
.UNINDENT
.SS Supported Standards
.sp
The following RFCs are supported by the DHCP\-DDNS server:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fISecret Key Transaction Authentication for DNS (TSIG)\fP, RFC 2845 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2845>: All DNS update packets sent and
received by the DHCP\-DDNS server can be protected by TSIG signatures.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIDynamic Updates in the Domain Name System (DNS UPDATE)\fP, RFC 2136 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2136>: The complete DNS update mechanism is
supported.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIResolution of Fully Qualified Domain Name (FQDN) Conflicts among Dynamic Host
Configuration Protocol (DHCP) Clients\fP, RFC 4703 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4703>: DHCP\-DDNS takes care of
conflict resolution, for both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIA DNS Resource Record (RR) for Encoding Dynamic Host Configuration Protocol
(DHCP) Information (DHCID RR)\fP, RFC 4701 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4701>: The DHCP\-DDNS server uses DHCID
records.
.UNINDENT
.SH THE LFC PROCESS
.SS Overview
.sp
\fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> is a service process that removes redundant information from
the files used to provide persistent storage for the memfile database
backend. This service is written to run as a standalone process.
.sp
While \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> can be started externally, there is usually no need to
do so. \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> is run on a periodic basis by the Kea DHCP servers,
and since Kea 3.1.2 can be started using the \fBkea\-lfc\-start\fP command.
.sp
The process operates on a set of files, using them to receive input and
output of the lease entries and to indicate what stage the process is
in, in the event of an interruption. Currently the caller must supply
names for all of the files.
.SS Command\-Line Options
.sp
\fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> is run as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kea\-lfc [\-4 | \-6] \-c config\-file \-p pid\-file \-x previous\-file \-i copy\-file \-o output\-file \-f finish\-file
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The argument \fB\-4\fP or \fB\-6\fP selects the protocol version of the lease
files.
.sp
The \fB\-c\fP argument specifies the configuration file. This is required,
but is not currently used by the process.
.sp
The \fB\-p\fP argument specifies the PID file. When the \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> process
starts, it attempts to determine whether another instance of the process
is already running by examining the PID file. If one is already running,
the new process is terminated; if one is not running, Kea writes its PID
into the PID file.
.sp
The other filenames specify where the \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> process should look
for input, write its output, and perform its bookkeeping:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprevious\fP — when \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> starts, this is the result of any
previous run of \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc>\&. When \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> finishes, it is the
result of this run. If \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> is interrupted before completing,
this file may not exist.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinput\fP — before the DHCP server invokes \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc>, it moves
the current lease file here and then calls \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> with this file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBoutput\fP — this is the temporary file where \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> writes the
leases. Once the file has finished writing, it is moved to the
\fBfinish\fP file (see below).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfinish\fP — this is another temporary file \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> uses for
bookkeeping. When \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> completes writing the \fBoutput\fP file, it
moves the contents to the file of this name. After \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> finishes deleting the
other files (\fBprevious\fP and \fBinput\fP), it moves this file to the \fBprevious\fP
lease file. By moving the files in this fashion, \fBkea\-lfc\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-lfc> and
the DHCP server processes can determine the correct file to use even
if one of the processes is interrupted before completing its task.
.UNINDENT
.sp
There are several additional arguments, mostly for debugging purposes.
\fB\-d\fP sets the logging level to debug. \fB\-v\fP and \fB\-V\fP print out
version stamps, with \fB\-V\fP providing a longer form. \fB\-h\fP prints out
the usage string.
.SH CLIENT CLASSIFICATION
.SS Client Classification Overview
.sp
In certain cases it is useful to differentiate among different types
of clients and treat them accordingly. Common reasons include:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The clients represent different pieces of topology, e.g. a cable
modem is not the same as the clients behind that modem.
.IP \(bu 2
The clients have different behavior, e.g. a smartphone behaves
differently from a laptop.
.IP \(bu 2
The clients require different values for some options, e.g. a
docsis3.0 cable modem requires different settings from a docsis2.0
cable modem.
.UNINDENT
.sp
To make management easier, different clients can be grouped into a
client class to receive common options.
.sp
An incoming packet can be associated with a client class in several
ways:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Implicitly, using a vendor class option or another built\-in condition.
.IP \(bu 2
Using an expression which evaluates to \fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
Using static host reservations, a shared network, a subnet, etc.
.IP \(bu 2
Using a hook.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Client classification can be used to change the behavior of almost any
part of the DHCP message processing. There are currently nine
mechanisms that take advantage of client classification:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Dropping queries.
.IP \(bu 2
Subnet selection.
.IP \(bu 2
Pool selection.
.IP \(bu 2
Lease limiting.
.IP \(bu 2
Rate limiting.
.IP \(bu 2
DDNS tuning.
.IP \(bu 2
Defining DHCPv4 private (codes 224\-254) and code 43 options.
.IP \(bu 2
Assigning different options.
.IP \(bu 2
Setting specific options for use with the TFTP server address
and the boot file field for DHCPv4 cable modems.
.UNINDENT
.SS Classification Steps
.sp
The classification process is conducted in several steps:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
The \fBALL\fP class is associated with the incoming packet.
.IP 2. 3
Vendor class options are processed.
.IP 3. 3
Classes with matching expressions and not marked for later evaluation (\(dqon
request\(dq or depending on the \fBKNOWN\fP/\fBUNKNOWN\fP built\-in classes)
are processed in the order they are defined in the
configuration; the boolean expression is evaluated and, if it
returns \fBtrue\fP (a match), the incoming packet is associated with the
class.
.IP 4. 3
If a private or code 43 DHCPv4 option is received, it is decoded
following its client\-class or global (or, for option 43,
last\-resort) definition.
.IP 5. 3
When the incoming packet belongs to the special class \fBDROP\fP, it is
dropped and an informational message is logged with the packet
information.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBpkt4_receive\fP and \fBpkt6_receive\fP callouts are called here.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 6. 3
When the \fBearly\-global\-reservations\-lookup\fP global parameter is
configured to \fBtrue\fP, the process looks up global reservations and
partially performs steps 8, 9, and 10. The lookup is limited to
global reservations; if one is found the \fBKNOWN\fP class is set,
but if none is found the \fBUNKNOWN\fP class is \fBnot\fP set.
.IP 7. 3
A subnet is chosen, possibly based on the class information when
some subnets are reserved. More precisely: when choosing a subnet,
the server iterates over all of the subnets that are feasible given
the information found in the packet (client address, relay address,
etc.). It uses the first subnet it finds that either has no
class associated with it, or has a class which matches one of the
packet\(aqs classes.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBsubnet4_select\fP and \fBsubnet6_select\fP callouts are called here.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 8. 3
The server looks for host reservations. If an identifier from the
incoming packet matches a host reservation in the subnet or shared
network, the packet is associated with the \fBKNOWN\fP class and all
classes of the host reservation. If a reservation is not found, the
packet is assigned to the \fBUNKNOWN\fP class.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If there is a matching reservation from the selected subnet and that
subnet belongs to a shared\-network, then the assignment of any classes
specified in the reservation is deferred until after lease allocation.
This is done to account for the possibility that the subnet selection may
change during lease allocation and that would negate and possibly replace
the original reservation match.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 9. 3
Classes with matching expressions \- directly, or indirectly using the
\fBKNOWN\fP/\fBUNKNOWN\fP built\-in classes and not marked for later evaluation
(\(dqon request\(dq) \- are processed in the order they are defined
in the configuration; the boolean expression is evaluated and, if it
returns \fBtrue\fP (a match), the incoming packet is associated with the
class. After a subnet is selected, the server determines whether
there is a reservation for a given client. Therefore, it is not
possible to use the \fBUNKNOWN\fP class to select a shared network or
a subnet. For the \fBKNOWN\fP class, only global reservations are used and the
\fBearly\-global\-reservations\-lookup\fP parameter must be configured to
\fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP 10. 3
When the incoming packet belongs to the special class \fBDROP\fP, it is
dropped and an informational message is logged with the packet
information. Since Kea version 1.9.8, it is permissible to make the \fBDROP\fP
class dependent on the \fBKNOWN\fP/\fBUNKNOWN\fP classes.
.IP 11. 3
If needed, addresses and prefixes from pools are assigned, possibly
based on the class information when some pools are reserved for
class members.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBlease4_select\fP, \fBlease4_renew\fP, \fBlease6_select\fP, \fBlease6_renew\fP,
and \fBlease6_rebind\fP callouts are called here.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If there is a matching host reservation from the selected subnet and that
subnet belongs to a shared\-network, then any classes specified in the
reservation are assigned.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 12. 4
The \fBevaluate\-additional\-classes\fP lists (if any) are evaluated first
for the pool, then the subnet, and the finally shared\-network to which the
assigned resources belong. Classes are evaluated in the order they appear
within each list.
.IP 13. 4
Options are assigned, again possibly based on the class information
in the order that classes were associated with the incoming packet.
The first class matched to the packet which specifies a given
option wins. For DHCPv4 private and code 43 options, this includes
option definitions specified within classes.  The order of precedence
for options is as follows:
.INDENT 4.0
.IP 1. 3
Host options
.IP 2. 3
Pool options
.IP 3. 3
Subnet options
.IP 4. 3
Shared network options
.IP 5. 3
Options from classes assigned during normal classification:
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP a. 3
\fBALL\fP class
.IP b. 3
Vendor classes
.IP c. 3
Host classes
.IP d. 3
Evaluated classes that do not depend on \fBKNOWN\fP class
.IP e. 3
\fBKNOWN\fP and \fBUNKNOWN\fP classes
.IP f. 3
Evaluated classes that depend on \fBKNOWN\fP class
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 6. 3
Options from classes in \fBevaluate\-additional\-classes\fP from:
.INDENT 4.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP a. 3
Pool
.IP b. 3
Subnet
.IP c. 3
Shared network
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 7. 3
Global options
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Care should be taken with client classification, as it is easy for
clients that do not meet any class criteria to be denied service
altogether.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Built\-in Client Classes
.sp
Some classes are built\-in, so they do not need to be explicitly defined. They
can be defined if there is a need to associate lease lifetimes, option data,
etc. with them.
.sp
Vendor class information is the primary example: the server checks whether an
incoming DHCPv4 packet includes the vendor class identifier option (60)
or an incoming DHCPv6 packet includes the vendor class option (16). If
it does, the content of that option is prepended with \fBVENDOR_CLASS_\fP
and the result is interpreted as a class for that packet. The content that is
considered is the whole class identifier for DHCPv4, and the first vendor class
data field for DHCPv6. The enterprise number and subsequent vendor class data
fields are not used for the purpose of classification. For example, modern cable
modems send such options with value \fBdocsis3.0\fP, so the packet belongs to
class \fBVENDOR_CLASS_docsis3.0\fP\&.
.sp
The \fBHA_\fP prefix is used by \fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_ha\:.so> to
designate certain servers to process DHCP packets as a result of load
balancing. The class name is constructed by prepending the \fBHA_\fP prefix
to the name of the server which should process the DHCP packet. This
server uses an appropriate pool or subnet to allocate IP addresses
(and/or prefixes), based on the assigned client classes. The details can
be found in libdhcp_ha.so: High Availability Outage Resilience for Kea Servers \%<#\:hooks-high-availability>\&.
.sp
The \fBSPAWN_\fP prefix is used by template classes to generate spawned class
names at runtime. The spawned class name is constructed by prepending the
\fBSPAWN_\fP prefix to the template class name and the evaluated value:
\fBSPAWN_<template\-class\-name>_<evaluated\-value>\fP\&.
More details can be found in Configuring Classes\&.
.sp
The \fBBOOTP\fP class is used by \fBlibdhcp_bootp.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_bootp\:.so> to classify and
respond to inbound BOOTP queries.
.sp
The \fBSKIP_DDNS\fP class is used by the DDNS\-tuning hook library to suppress
DDNS updates on a per client basis.
.sp
Other examples are the \fBALL\fP class, to which all incoming packets belong,
and the \fBKNOWN\fP class, assigned when host reservations exist for a
particular client. By convention, the names of built\-in classes begin with all
capital letters.
.sp
Currently recognized built\-in class names are \fBALL\fP, \fBKNOWN\fP and \fBUNKNOWN\fP,
and the prefixes \fBVENDOR_CLASS_\fP, \fBHA_\fP, \fBAFTER_\fP, \fBEXTERNAL_\fP,
\fBSKIP_DDNS\fP\&. Although the \fBAFTER_\fP prefix is a provision for an
as\-yet\-unwritten hook, the \fBEXTERNAL_\fP prefix can be freely used; built\-in
classes are implicitly defined so they never raise warnings if they do not
appear in the configuration.
.SS Using Expressions in Classification
.sp
The expression portion of a classification definition contains operators
and values. All values are currently strings; operators take a string or
strings and return another string. When all the operations have
completed, the result should be a value of \fBtrue\fP or \fBfalse\fP\&. The packet
belongs to the class (and the class name is added to the list of
classes) if the result is \fBtrue\fP\&. Expressions are written in standard
format and can be nested.
.sp
Expressions are pre\-processed during the parsing of the configuration
file and converted to an internal representation. This allows certain
types of errors to be caught and logged during parsing. Examples of
these errors include an incorrect number or type of argument to an
operator. The evaluation code also checks for this class of error and
generally throws an exception, though this should not occur in a
normally functioning system.
.sp
Other issues, such as the starting position of a substring being
outside of the substring or an option not existing in the packet, result
in the operator returning an empty string.
.sp
Dependencies between classes are also checked. For instance, forward
dependencies are rejected when the configuration is parsed; an
expression can only depend on already\-defined classes (including built\-in
classes) which are evaluated in a previous or the same evaluation phase.
This does not apply to the \fBKNOWN\fP or \fBUNKNOWN\fP classes.
