.sp
Notes:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Hexadecimal strings are converted into a string as expected. The
starting \fB0X\fP or \fB0x\fP is removed, and if the string is an odd number
of characters a \(dq0\(dq is prepended to it.
.IP \(bu 2
IP addresses are converted into strings of length 4 or 16. IPv4,
IPv6, and IPv4\-embedded IPv6 (e.g. IPv4\-mapped IPv6) addresses are
supported.
.IP \(bu 2
Integers in an expression are converted to 32\-bit unsigned integers
and are represented as four\-byte strings; for example, 123 is
represented as \fB0x0000007b\fP\&. All expressions that return numeric values
use 32\-bit unsigned integers, even if the field in the packet is
smaller. In general, it is easier to use decimal notation to
represent integers, but it is also possible to use hexadecimal
notation. When writing an integer in hexadecimal, care should be
taken to make sure the value is represented as 32 bits, e.g. use
\fB0x00000001\fP instead of \fB0x1\fP or \fB0x01\fP\&. Also, make sure the value is
specified in network order, e.g. 1 is represented as \fB0x00000001\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBoption[code].hex\fP extracts the value of the option with the code
\fBcode\fP from the incoming packet. If the packet does not contain the
option, it returns an empty string. The string is presented as a byte
string of the option payload, without the type code or length fields.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBoption[code].exists\fP checks whether an option with the code \fBcode\fP
is present in the incoming packet. It can be used with empty options.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmember(\(aqfoobar\(aq)\fP checks whether the packet belongs to the client
class \fBfoobar\fP\&. To avoid dependency loops, the configuration file
parser verifies whether client classes were already defined or are
built\-in, i.e., beginning with \fBVENDOR_CLASS_\fP, \fBAFTER_\fP (for the
to\-come \(dqafter\(dq hook) and \fBEXTERNAL_\fP or equal to \fBALL\fP, \fBKNOWN\fP,
\fBUNKNOWN\fP, etc.
.sp
\fBknown\fP and \fBunknown\fP are shorthand for \fBmember(\(aqKNOWN\(aq)\fP and \fBnot
member(\(aqKNOWN\(aq)\fP\&. Note that the evaluation of any expression using
the \fBKNOWN\fP class (directly or indirectly) is deferred after the host
reservation lookup (i.e. when the \fBKNOWN\fP or \fBUNKNOWN\fP partition is
determined).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay4[code].hex\fP attempts to extract the value of the sub\-option
\fBcode\fP from the option inserted as the DHCPv4 Relay Agent Information
(82) option. If the packet does not contain a RAI option, or the RAI
option does not contain the requested sub\-option, the expression
returns an empty string. The string is presented as a byte string of
the option payload without the type code or length fields. This
expression is allowed in DHCPv4 only.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay4\fP shares the same representation types as \fBoption\fP; for
instance, \fBrelay4[code].exists\fP is supported.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay6[nest]\fP allows access to the encapsulations used by any DHCPv6
relays that forwarded the packet. The \fBnest\fP level specifies the
relay from which to extract the information, with a value of 0
indicating the relay closest to the DHCPv6 server. Negative values
allow relays to be specified counting from the DHCPv6 client, with \-1 indicating
the relay closest to the client. If the requested
encapsulation does not exist, an empty string \fB\(dq\(dq\fP is returned. This
expression is allowed in DHCPv6 only.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay6[nest].option[code]\fP shares the same representation types as
\fBoption\fP; for instance, \fBrelay6[nest].option[code].exists\fP is
supported.
.IP \(bu 2
Expressions starting with \fBpkt4\fP can be used only in DHCPv4. They
allow access to DHCPv4 message fields.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkt6\fP refers to information from the client request. To access any
information from an intermediate relay, use \fBrelay6\fP\&. \fBpkt6.msgtype\fP
and \fBpkt6.transid\fP output a 4\-byte binary string for the message type
or transaction ID. For example, the message type \fBSOLICIT\fP is
\fB0x00000001\fP or simply 1, as in \fBpkt6.msgtype == 1\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqVendor option\(dq means the Vendor\-Identifying Vendor\-Specific Information
option in DHCPv4 (code 125; see Section 4 of RFC
3925 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3925#\:section-4>) and the
Vendor\-Specific Information Option in DHCPv6 (code 17, defined in
Section 21.17 of RFC
9915 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc9915#\:section-21\:.17>). \(dqVendor
class option\(dq means the Vendor\-Identifying Vendor Class Option in DHCPv4
(code 124; see Section 3 of RFC
3925 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3925#\:section-3>) in DHCPv4 and
the Class Option in DHCPv6 (code 16; see Section 21.16 of RFC
9915 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc9915#\:section-21\:.16>). Vendor
options may have sub\-options that are referenced by their codes.
Vendor class options do not have sub\-options, but rather data chunks,
which are referenced by index value. Index 0 means the first data
chunk, index 1 is for the second data chunk (if present), etc.
.IP \(bu 2
In the vendor and vendor\-class constructs an asterisk (*) or 0 can be
used to specify a wildcard \fBenterprise\-id\fP value, i.e. it will match
any \fBenterprise\-id\fP value.
.IP \(bu 2
Vendor Class Identifier (option 60 in DHCPv4) can be accessed using the
option[60] expression.
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 3925 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3925> and RFC
9915 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc9915> allow for multiple
instances of vendor options to appear in a single message. The client
classification code currently examines the first instance if more
than one appear. For the \fBvendor.enterprise\fP and \fBvendor\-class.enterprise\fP
expressions, the value from the first instance is returned. Please
submit a feature request on the
Kea GitLab site \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea> to request
support for multiple instances.
.UNINDENT
.SS List of classification expressions
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Example
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
Equal
T}	T{
\(aqfoo\(aq == \(aqbar\(aq
T}	T{
Compare the two
values and return
\fBtrue\fP or \fBfalse\fP
T}
_
T{
Match
T}	T{
match(\(aqfoo.*\(aq, \(aqfoobar\(aq)
T}	T{
Match a regular
expression with a
value.
T}
_
T{
Not
T}	T{
not (\(aqfoo\(aq == \(aqbar\(aq)
T}	T{
Logical negation
T}
_
T{
And
T}	T{
(\(aqfoo\(aq == \(aqbar\(aq) and
(\(aqbar\(aq == \(aqfoo\(aq)
T}	T{
Logical and
T}
_
T{
Or
T}	T{
(\(aqfoo\(aq == \(aqbar\(aq) or
(\(aqbar\(aq == \(aqfoo\(aq)
T}	T{
Logical or
T}
_
T{
Substring
T}	T{
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq,0,3)
T}	T{
Return the requested
substring
T}
_
T{
Concat
T}	T{
concat(\(aqfoo\(aq,\(aqbar\(aq)
T}	T{
Return the
concatenation of the
strings
T}
_
T{
Concat (operator +)
T}	T{
\(aqfoo\(aq + \(aqbar\(aq
T}	T{
Return the
concatenation of the
strings
T}
_
T{
Ifelse
T}	T{
ifelse(\(aqfoo\(aq ==
\(aqbar\(aq,\(aqus\(aq,\(aqthem\(aq)
T}	T{
Return the branch
value according to
the condition
T}
_
T{
Hexstring
T}	T{
hexstring(\(aqfoo\(aq, \(aq\-\(aq)
T}	T{
Converts the value to
a hexadecimal string,
e.g. 66\-6F\-6F
T}
_
T{
Lcase
T}	T{
lcase(\(aqLoWeR\(aq)
T}	T{
Converts the value of
a string expression
to lower case, e.g.
\(aqlower\(aq
T}
_
T{
Ucase
T}	T{
ucase(\(aquPpEr\(aq)
T}	T{
Converts the value of
a string expression
to upper case, e.g.
\(aqUPPER\(aq
T}
_
T{
Split
T}	T{
split(\(aqfoo.bar\(aq, \(aq.\(aq, 2)
T}	T{
Return the second
field, splitting on
dots.
T}
.TE
.SS List of conversion\-to\-text expressions
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Example
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
AddressToText
T}	T{
addrtotext (192.10.0.1)
addrtotext (2003:db8::)
T}	T{
Represent the 4 bytes
of an IPv4 address or
the 16 bytes of an
IPv6 address in human
readable format
T}
_
T{
Int8ToText
T}	T{
int8totext (\-1)
T}	T{
Represents the 8\-bit
signed integer in text
format
T}
_
T{
Int16ToText
T}	T{
int16totext (\-1)
T}	T{
Represents the 16\-bit
signed integer in text
format
T}
_
T{
Int32ToText
T}	T{
int32totext (\-1)
T}	T{
Represents the 32\-bit
signed integer in text
format
T}
_
T{
UInt8ToText
T}	T{
uint8totext (255)
T}	T{
Represents the 8\-bit
unsigned integer in
text format
T}
_
T{
UInt16ToText
T}	T{
uint16totext (65535)
T}	T{
Represents the 16\-bit
unsigned integer in
text format
T}
_
T{
UInt32ToText
T}	T{
uint32totext (4294967295)
T}	T{
Represents the 32\-bit
unsigned integer in
text format
T}
.TE
.sp
Notes:
.sp
The conversion operators can be used to transform data from binary to the text
representation. The only requirement is that the input data type length matches
an expected value.
.sp
The \fBAddressToText\fP token expects 4 bytes for IPv4 addresses or 16 bytes for IPv6
addresses. The \fBInt8ToText\fP and \fBUInt8ToText\fP tokens expect 1 byte, the \fBInt16ToText\fP and
\fBUInt16ToText\fP tokens expect 2 bytes, and \fBInt32ToText\fP and \fBUInt32ToText\fP expect 4 bytes.
For all conversion tokens, if the data length is 0, the result string is empty.
.SS Predicates
.sp
The two predicates are Equal and Match. They can be used to build other
common predicates, for instance:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
not (substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, 3, 3) == \(aqbar\(aq)
match(\(aqfoo.*\(aq, lcase(\(aqFooBar\(aq))
match(\(aq.*foo.*\(aq, \(aqis it foo or bar\(aq)
match(\(aq^.*foo.*$\(aq, \(aqis it foo or bar\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
So inequality, case insensitive pattern matching or pattern search.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Detected invalid regular expressions are considered as syntax errors,
runtime exceptions during match are handled as no match.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Be careful with the match operator as it can result in very poor
performance leading to regular expression denial of service (ReDoS).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Logical Operators
.sp
The Not, And, and Or logical operators are the common operators. Not has
the highest precedence and Or the lowest. And and Or are (left)
associative. Parentheses around a logical expression can be used to
enforce a specific grouping; for instance, in \(dqA and (B or C)\(dq. Without
parentheses, \(dqA and B or C\(dq means \(dq(A and B) or C\(dq.
.SS Substring
.sp
The substring operator \fBsubstring(value, start, length)\fP accepts both
positive and negative values for the starting position and the length.
For \fBstart\fP, a value of 0 is the first byte in the string while \-1 is
the last byte. If the starting point is outside of the original string
an empty string is returned. \fBlength\fP is the number of bytes to extract.
A negative number means to count towards the beginning of the string but
does not include the byte pointed to by \fBstart\fP\&. The special value \fBall\fP
means to return all bytes from start to the end of the string. If the length
is longer than the remaining portion of the string, then the entire
remaining portion is returned. Some examples may be helpful:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, 0, 6) == \(aqfoobar\(aq
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, 3, 3) == \(aqbar\(aq
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, 3, all) == \(aqbar\(aq
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, 1, 4) == \(aqooba\(aq
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, \-5, 4) == \(aqooba\(aq
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, \-1, \-3) == \(aqoba\(aq
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, 4, \-2) == \(aqob\(aq
substring(\(aqfoobar\(aq, 10, 2) == \(aq\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Concat
.sp
The concat function \fBconcat(string1, string2)\fP returns the concatenation
of its two arguments. For instance:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
concat(\(aqfoo\(aq, \(aqbar\(aq) == \(aqfoobar\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For user convenience, Kea version 1.9.8 added an associative operator
version of the concat function. For instance:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(aqabc\(aq + \(aqdef\(aq + \(aqghi\(aq + \(aqjkl\(aq + \(aq...\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
is the same as:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
concat(concat(concat(concat(\(aqabc\(aq, \(aqdef\(aq), \(aqghi\(aq), \(aqjkl\(aq), \(aq...\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
concat(\(aqabc\(aq, concat(\(aqdef\(aq, concat(\(aqghi\(aq, concat(\(aqjkl\(aq, \(aq...\(aq))))
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(aqabcdefghijkl...\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Split
.sp
The split operator \fBsplit(value, delimiters, field\-number)\fP accepts a list
of characters to use as delimiters and a positive field number of the
desired field when the value is split into fields separated by the delimiters.
Adjacent delimiters are not compressed out, rather they result in an empty
string for that field number. If value is an empty string, the result will be an
empty string. If the delimiters list is empty, the result will be the original
value. If the field\-number is less than one or larger than the number of
fields, the result will be an empty string. Some examples follow:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
split (\(aqone.two..four\(aq, \(aq.\(aq, 1) == \(aqone\(aq
split (\(aqone.two..four\(aq, \(aq.\(aq, 2) == \(aqtwo\(aq
split (\(aqone.two..four\(aq, \(aq.\(aq, 3) == \(aq\(aq
split (\(aqone.two..four\(aq, \(aq.\(aq, 4) == \(aqfour\(aq
split (\(aqone.two..four\(aq, \(aq.\(aq, 5) == \(aq\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
To use a hard\-to\-escape character as a delimiter, use its ASCII hex value.
For example, split by \fBsingle quote\fP using \fB0x27\fP:
\fBsplit(option[39].text, 0x27, 1)\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Ifelse
.sp
The ifelse function \fBifelse(cond, iftrue, ifelse)\fP returns the \fBiftrue\fP
or \fBifelse\fP branch value following the boolean condition \fBcond\fP\&. For
instance:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ifelse(option[230].exists, option[230].hex, \(aqnone\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Hexstring
.sp
The hexstring function \fBhexstring(binary, separator)\fP returns the binary
value as its hexadecimal string representation: pairs of hexadecimal
digits separated by the separator, e.g \fB\(aq:\(aq\fP, \fB\(aq\-\(aq\fP, \fB\(aq\(aq\fP (empty separator).
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq:\(aq)
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The expression for each class is executed on each packet received. If
the expressions are overly complex, the time taken to execute them
may impact the performance of the server. Administrators who need complex or
time\-consuming expressions should consider writing a
hook \%<#\:hooks-libraries> to perform the necessary work.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuring Classes
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B A client class definition can contain the following properties:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBname\fP parameter is mandatory and must be unique among all classes.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBtest\fP expression is not mandatory and represents a string containing the
logical expression used to determine membership in the class. The entire
expression is included in double quotes (\fB\(dq\fP). The result should evaluate
to a boolean value (\fBtrue\fP or \fBfalse\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBtemplate\-test\fP expression is not mandatory and represents a string
containing the logical expression used to generate a spawning class. The
entire expression is included in double quotes (\fB\(dq\fP). The result should
evaluate to a string value representing the variable part of the spawned
class name. If the resulting string is empty, no spawning class is generated.
The resulting spawned class has the following generated name format:
\fBSPAWN_<template\-class\-name>_<evaluated\-value>\fP\&.
After classes are evaluated and a spawned class is generated, the corresponding
template class name is also associated with the packet. An Option specified in a
spawned class will take precedence over the same option if set in its template class.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBoption\-data\fP list is not mandatory and contains options that should be
assigned to members of this class. In the case of a template class, these
options are assigned to the generated spawned class.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBoption\-def\fP list is not mandatory and is used to define custom options.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBonly\-if\-required\fP has been replaced with \fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP and
is now deprecated. It will still be accepted as input for a time to allow users
to migrate but will eventually be unsupported.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP flag is not mandatory; when its value is set to
\fBfalse\fP (the default), membership is determined during classification and is
available for subnet selection, for instance. When the value is set to
\fBtrue\fP, membership is evaluated only if the class appears in an
\fBevaluate\-additional\-classes\fP list and is usable only for option configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBuser\-context\fP is not mandatory and represents a map with user\-defined data
and possibly configuration options for hook libraries.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBnext\-server\fP parameter is not mandatory and configures the \fBsiaddr\fP field in
packets associated with this class. It is used in DHCPv4 only.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBserver\-hostname\fP is not mandatory and configures the \fBsname\fP field in
packets associated with this class. It is used in DHCPv4 only.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBboot\-file\-name\fP is not mandatory and configures the \fBfile\fP field in
packets associated with this class. It is used in DHCPv4 only.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBvalid\-lifetime\fP, \fBmin\-valid\-lifetime\fP, and \fBmax\-valid\-lifetime\fP are
not mandatory and configure the valid lifetime fields for this client class.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBpreferred\-lifetime\fP, \fBmin\-preferred\-lifetime\fP, and
\fBmax\-preferred\-lifetime\fP are not mandatory and configure the preferred
lifetime fields for this client class. It is used in DHCPv6 only.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBtest\fP and \fBtemplate\-test\fP are mutually exclusive in a client class
definition. Use either one, or neither, but not both. If both are provided,
the configuration is rejected.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the following example, the class named \fBClient_foo\fP is defined. It is
comprised of all clients whose client IDs (option 61) start with the string
\fBfoo\fP\&. Members of this class will be given 192.0.2.1 and 192.0.2.2 as their
domain name servers.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient_foo\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[61].hex,0,3) == \(aqfoo\(aq\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 6,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1, 192.0.2.2\(dq
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The next example shows a client class named \(dqClient_enterprise\(dq being defined
for use by the DHCPv6 server. It is
comprised of all clients whose client identifiers start with the given
hex string, which would indicate a DUID based on an enterprise ID of
\fB0x0002AABBCCDD\fP\&. Members of this class will be given 2001:db8:0::1 and
2001:db8:2::1 as their domain name servers.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient_enterprise\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[1].hex,0,6) == 0x0002AABBCCDD\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:0::1, 2001:db8:2::1\(dq
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is also possible to have both left and right operands of the evaluated
expression processed at runtime. Expressions related to packets can appear in
the expression as many times as needed; there is no limit. However, each token
has a small impact on performance and excessively complex expressions may cause a
bottleneck.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqInfrastructure\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqoption[82].option[2].hex == pkt4.mac\(dq,
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Template Classes
.sp
The \fBtemplate\-test\fP parameter indicates that the class is a template class.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient\-ID\(dq,
            \(dqtemplate\-test\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[61].hex,0,3)\(dq,
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the received DHCPv4 packet contains option 61, then the first three bytes represent
the value \fBfoo\fP in ASCII, and the spawned class uses the
\fBSPAWN_Client\-ID_foo\fP name.
Both the \fBSPAWN_Client\-ID_foo\fP and \fBClient\-ID\fP classes are associated with
the packet.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Template classes can also be used to spawn classes which match regular
classes, effectively associating the regular class to the packet.
To achieve this, the regular class must also contain the fixed part of the
spawned class name:
.sp
\fBSPAWN_<template\-class\-name\-used\-to\-activate\-this\-regular\-class>_<evaluated\-value\-filtering\-this\-regular\-class>\fP
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqSPAWN_Client\-ID_foobar\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[1].hex,0,6) == 0x0002AABBCCDD\(dq,
            ...
        },
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient\-ID\(dq,
            \(dqtemplate\-test\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[1].hex,0,6)\(dq,
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the received DHCPv6 packet contains option 1 (client identifier) with hex
value \fB0x0002AABBCCDD\fP, then the \fBSPAWN_Client\-ID_foobar\fP is associated
with the packet. Moreover, if the first six bytes represent value \fBfoobar\fP in
ASCII, then the spawned class uses the \fBSPAWN_Client\-ID_foobar\fP name,
effectively associating the regular class to the packet. In this second case,
both the \fBSPAWN_Client\-ID_foobar\fP and \fBClient\-ID\fP classes are associated
with the packet.
The \fBtest\fP expression on the regular class \fBSPAWN_Client\-ID_foobar\fP is not
mandatory and can be omitted, but it is used here with a different match
expression for example purposes.
.sp
Usually the \fBtest\fP and \fBtemplate\-test\fP expressions are evaluated before
subnet selection, but in some cases it is useful to evaluate it later when the
subnet, shared network, or pools are known but output\-option processing has not
yet been done. For this purpose, the \fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP flag, which is
\fBfalse\fP by default, allows the evaluation of the \fBtest\fP expression or the
\fBtemplate\-test\fP expression only when it is required by the class\(aqs presence
in the \fBevaluate\-additional\-classes\fP list of the selected pool, subnet, or
shared network.
.sp
The \fBevaluate\-additional\-classes\fP list, which is valid for pool, subnet,
and shared\-network scope, specifies the classes which are evaluated in
the second pass before output\-option processing. The list is built in
same precedence order as the option data, i.e. an option data item in
a subnet takes precedence over one in a shared network. An
additional class in a subnet is added before one in a shared
network. The mechanism is related to the \fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP flag but
it is not mandatory that the flag be set to \fBtrue\fP\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBtemplate\-test\fP expression can also be used to filter generated spawned
classes, so that they are created only when needed by using the \fBifelse\fP
instruction.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient\-ID\(dq,
            \(dqtemplate\-test\(dq: \(dqifelse(substring(option[61].hex,4,3) == \(aqfoo\(aq, substring(option[12].hex,0,12), \(aq\(aq)\(dq,
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The template classes can be used to configure limits which, just like
options, are associated with the spawned class. This permits the configuration of
limits that apply to all packets associated with a class spawned at
runtime, according to the \fBtemplate\-test\fP expression in the parent template
class. For a more detailed description of how to configure limits using the
limits hook library, see Configuration \%<#\:hooks-limits-configuration>\&.
For example, using the configuration below, ingress DHCPv6 packets that have
client ID values (in the format expressed by the Kea evaluator) \fBfoobar\fP
and \fBfoofoo\fP both amount to the same limit of 60 packets per day, while
other packets that have the first three hextets different than \fBfoo\fP are put
in separate rate\-limiting buckets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient\-ID\(dq,
            \(dqtemplate\-test\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[1].hex,0,3)\(dq,
            \(dquser\-context\(dq : {
                \(dqlimits\(dq: {
                    \(dqrate\-limit\(dq: \(dq60 packets per day\(dq
                }
            },
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Using Static Host Reservations in Classification
.sp
Classes can be statically assigned to clients using techniques
described in Reserving Client Classes in DHCPv4 \%<#\:reservation4-client-classes> and
Reserving Client Classes in DHCPv6 \%<#\:reservation6-client-classes>\&.
.sp
Subnet host reservations are searched after subnet selection.
Global host reservations are searched at the same time by default but
the \fBearly\-global\-reservations\-lookup\fP allows to change this behavior
into searching them before the subnet selection.
.sp
Pool selection is performed after all host reservations lookups.
.SS Configuring Subnets With Class Information
.sp
In certain cases it is beneficial to restrict access to certain subnets
only to clients that belong to a given class, using the \fBclient\-classes\fP
parameter when defining the subnet.  This parameter may be used to specify
a list of one or more classes to which clients must belong in order to
use the subnet. This can be referred to as a class guard for the subnet, or in
other words the subnet is guarded by a client class.
.sp
Let\(aqs assume that the server is connected to a network segment that uses the
192.0.2.0/24 prefix. The administrator of that network has decided that
addresses from the range 192.0.2.10 to 192.0.2.20 will be managed by the DHCPv4
server. Only clients belonging to client class \fBClient_foo\fP are allowed to use
this subnet. Such a configuration can be achieved in the following way:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient_foo\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[61].hex,0,3) == \(aqfoo\(aq\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 6,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1, 192.0.2.2\(dq
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
            \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10 \- 192.0.2.20\(dq } ],
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqClient_foo\(dq ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following example shows how to restrict access to a DHCPv6 subnet. This
configuration restricts use of the addresses in the range 2001:db8:1::1 to
2001:db8:1::FFFF to members of the \(dqClient_enterprise\(dq class.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient_enterprise\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[1].hex,0,6) == 0x0002AABBCCDD\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:0::1, 2001:db8:2::1\(dq
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
            \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::\-2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq } ],
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: \(dqClient_enterprise\(dq
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuring Pools With Class Information
.sp
Similar to subnets, in certain cases access to certain address or prefix
pools must be restricted to only clients that belong to at least one of a
list of one or more classes, using the \fBclient\-classes\fP when defining
the pool. This can be referred to as a class guard for the pool, or in other
words the pool is guarded by a client class.
.sp
Let\(aqs assume that the server is connected to a network segment that uses the
192.0.2.0/24 prefix. The administrator of that network has decided that
addresses from the range 192.0.2.10 to 192.0.2.20 are going to be managed by the
DHCPv4 server. Only clients belonging to client class \fBClient_foo\fP are allowed
to use this pool. Such a configuration can be achieved in the following way:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient_foo\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[61].hex,0,3) == \(aqfoo\(aq\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 6,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1, 192.0.2.2\(dq
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10 \- 192.0.2.20\(dq,
                    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqClient_foo\(dq ]
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following example shows how to restrict access to an address pool. This
configuration restricts use of the addresses in the range 2001:db8:1::1 to
2001:db8:1::FFFF to members of the \(dqClient_enterprise\(dq class.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqClient_enterprise_\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[1].hex,0,6) == 0x0002AABBCCDD\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:0::1, 2001:db8:2::1\(dq
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,

            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,

            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::\-2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq,
                    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqClient_foo\(dq ]
                }
            ]
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Class Priority
.sp
Client classes in Kea follow the order in which they are specified in the
configuration (vs. alphabetical order). Additional classes are ordered by
pool, subnet, and then shared\-network and within each scope by the order in
which they appear in \fBevaluate\-additional\-classes\fP\&.
.sp
When determining which client\-class information (comprised of
options, lease lifetimes, or DHCPv4 field values) is part of the class
definitions to be included in the response, the server examines the union of
options from all of the assigned classes. If two or more classes include the
same class information, the value from the first assigned class is used.
\fBALL\fP is always the first class, i.e. the class with the highest
priority, and matching required classes are last, so they have the
lowest priority.
.sp
Options defined in classes override any global options, and in turn are
overridden by options defined for an individual subnet, shared network, pool, or
reservation.
.sp
On the other hand, lease lifetimes and DHCPv4 field values defined at class
scope override any values defined globally, in a subnet scope, or in a
shared\-network scope.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Because additional evaluation occurs after lease assignment, parameters
that would otherwise impact lease life times (e.g. \fBvalid\-lifetime\fP,
\fBoffer\-lifetime\fP) will have no effect when specified in a class that
also sets \fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP true.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As an example, imagine that an incoming packet matches two classes.
Class \fBfoo\fP defines values for an NTP server (option 42 in DHCPv4) and
an SMTP server (option 69 in DHCPv4), while class \fBbar\fP defines values
for an NTP server and a POP3 server (option 70 in DHCPv4). The server
examines the three options \- NTP, SMTP, and POP3 \- and returns any that
the client requested. As the NTP server was defined twice, the server
chooses only one of the values for the reply; the class from which the
value is obtained is determined as explained in the previous paragraphs.
.SS Option Class\-Tagging
.sp
Option class\-tagging allows an option value to be conditionally applied
to the response based on the client\(aqs class membership.  The effect is
similar to using an if\-block in ISC DHCP to conditionally include
options at a given scope.  Class\-tagging is done by specifying a list of
one of more class names in the option\(aqs \fBclient\-classes\fP entry.
.sp
Consider a case where members of a given class need the same value for
one option but a subnet\-specific value for another option.  The following
example shows class\-tagging used to give clients who belong to \(dqGROUP1\(dq
a subnet\-specific value for option \(dqbar\(dq, while giving all members of \(dqGROUP1\(dq
the same value for option \(dqfoo\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqGROUP1\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq:\(dqsubstring(option[123].hex,0,4) == \(aqONE\(aq\(dq,
        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dqsomefile.txt\(dq
        }]
    }],
    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq178.16.1.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [\(dqGROUP1\(dq],
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: 123
        }]
    },
    {
        \(dqid\(dq: 2,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq178.16.2.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [\(dqGROUP1\(dq],
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: 789
        }]
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBclient\-classes\fP list is allowed in an option specification at
any scope.  Option class\-tagging is enforced at the time options are
being added to the response which occurs after lease assignment just
before the response is to be sent to the client.
.sp
When \fBnever\-send\fP for an option is true at any scope, all
\fBclient\-classes\fP entries for that option are ignored. The
option will not be included.
.sp
When \fBalways\-send\fP is true at any scope, the option will be
included unless, the option determined by scope specifies
a \fBclient\-classes\fP list that does not contain any of the
client\(aqs classes.
.sp
Otherwise, an option requested by the client will be included in
the response if  the option either does not specify \fBclient\-classes\fP
or the client belongs to at least one of the classes in \fBclient\-classes\fP\&.
.sp
When an option\(aqs class\-tag does not match, it is as though
the option was not specified at that scope.  In the following
example the option \(dqfoo\(dq is specified for both the subnet and
the pool.  The pool specification includes a class\-tag that limits
the option to members of class \(dqmelon\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqid\(dq: 100,
    \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq178.16.1.0/24\(dq,
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
        \(dqdata\(dq: 456
    }],
    \(dqpools\(dq: [{
        \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq178.16.1.100 \- 178.16.1.200\(dq,
        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: 123,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq : [ \(dqmelon\(dq ]
        }]
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Clients that match class \(dqmelon\(dq will have a value of 123 for option \(dqfoo\(dq,
while clients that do not match \(dqmelon\(dq will have a value of 456 for option
\(dqfoo\(dq.
.sp
It is possible to achieve an if\-elseif\-else effect but specifying an option
more than once with different class tags and values. Consider the following
configuration for a subnet which provides three possible values for the
\(dqserver\-str\(dq option:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqid\(dq: 1,
    \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.1.0/24\(dq,
    \(dqpools\(dq: [{ \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.1.1 \- 192.0.1.255\(dq }],
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq:  [ \(dqclass\-one\(dq ],
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver\-str\(dq,
        \(dqdata\(dq: \(dqstring.one\(dq
    },{
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq:  [ \(dqclass\-two\(dq ],
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver\-str\(dq,
        \(dqdata\(dq: \(dqstring.two\(dq
    },{
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver\-str\(dq,
        \(dqdata\(dq: \(dqstring.other\(dq
   }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Clients belonging to \(dqclass\-one\(dq will get a value of \(dqstring.one\(dq, clients
belonging to \(dqclass\-two\(dq will get a value of \(dqstring.two\(dq, and clients that
belong to neither \(dqclass\-one\(dq nor \(dqclass\-two\(dq will get a value of
\(dqstring.other\(dq.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The order that the options are tested is not guaranteed other than an option
with an empty \(dqclient\-classes\(dq list is checked last.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Though examples above are for DHCPv4, class\-tagging syntax and
behavior is the same for DHCPv6.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Classes and Hooks
.sp
Hooks may be used to classify packets. This may be useful if the
expression would be complex or time\-consuming to write, and could be
better or more easily written as code. Once the hook has added the proper class name
to the packet, the rest of the classification system will work as expected
in choosing a subnet and selecting options. For a description of hooks,
see Hook Libraries \%<#\:hooks-libraries>; for information on configuring classes,
see Configuring Classes and Configuring Subnets With Class Information\&.
.SS Debugging Expressions
.sp
While constructing classification expressions, administrators may find
it useful to enable logging; see Logging \%<#\:logging> for a more complete
description of the logging facility.
.sp
To enable the debug statements in the classification system,
the severity must be set to \fBDEBUG\fP and the debug level to at least 55.
The specific loggers are \fBkea\-dhcp4.eval\fP and \fBkea\-dhcp6.eval\fP\&.
.sp
To understand the logging statements, it is essential to understand a bit about
how expressions are evaluated; for a more complete description, refer to
[the design document](\%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea/\:-/\:wikis/\:Designs/\:client-classification-design>).
In brief, there are two structures used during the evaluation of an
expression: a list of tokens which represent the expressions, and a value
stack which represents the values being manipulated.
.sp
The list of tokens is created when the configuration file is processed,
with most expressions and values being converted to a token. The list is
organized in reverse Polish notation. During execution, the list is
traversed in order; as each token is executed, it is able to pop
values from the top of the stack and eventually push its result on the
top of the stack. Imagine the following expression:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[61].hex,0,3) == \(aqfoo\(aq\(dq,
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will result in the following tokens:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
option, number (0), number (3), substring, text (\(aqfoo\(aq), equals
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In this example, the first three tokens will simply push values onto the
stack. The substring token will then remove those three values and
compute a result that it places on the stack. The text option also
places a value on the stack, and finally the equals token removes the two
tokens on the stack and places its result on the stack.
.sp
When debug logging is enabled, each time a token is evaluated it
emits a log message indicating the values of any objects that were popped
off of the value stack, and any objects that were pushed onto the value
stack.
.sp
The values are displayed as either text, if the command is known to
use text values, or hexadecimal, if the command either uses binary values
or can manipulate either text or binary values. For expressions that pop
multiple values off the stack, the values are displayed in the order
they were popped. For most expressions this will not matter, but for the
concat expression the values are displayed in reverse order from their
written order in the expression.
.sp
Let us assume that the following test has been entered into the
configuration. This example skips most of the configuration to
concentrate on the test.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[61].hex,0,3) == \(aqfoo\(aq\(dq,
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The logging might then resemble this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2016\-05\-19 13:35:04.163 DEBUG [kea.eval/44478] EVAL_DEBUG_OPTION Pushing option 61 with value 0x666F6F626172
2016\-05\-19 13:35:04.164 DEBUG [kea.eval/44478] EVAL_DEBUG_STRING Pushing text string \(aq0\(aq
2016\-05\-19 13:35:04.165 DEBUG [kea.eval/44478] EVAL_DEBUG_STRING Pushing text string \(aq3\(aq
2016\-05\-19 13:35:04.166 DEBUG [kea.eval/44478] EVAL_DEBUG_SUBSTRING Popping length 3, start 0, string 0x666F6F626172 pushing result 0x666F6F
2016\-05\-19 13:35:04.167 DEBUG [kea.eval/44478] EVAL_DEBUG_STRING Pushing text string \(aqfoo\(aq
2016\-05\-19 13:35:04.168 DEBUG [kea.eval/44478] EVAL_DEBUG_EQUAL Popping 0x666F6F and 0x666F6F pushing result \(aqtrue\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The debug logging may be quite verbose if there are multiple
expressions to evaluate; it is intended as an aid in helping
create and debug expressions. Administrators should plan to disable debug
logging when expressions are working correctly. Users may also
wish to include only one set of expressions at a time in the
configuration file while debugging them, to limit the log
statements. For example, when adding a new set of expressions, an administrator
might find it more convenient to create a configuration file that
only includes the new expressions until they are working
correctly, and then add the new set to the main configuration file.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH HOOK LIBRARIES
.SS Introduction
.sp
Kea is both flexible and customizable, via the use of \(dqhooks.\(dq This feature
lets Kea load one or more
dynamically linked libraries (known as \(dqhook libraries\(dq) and call functions
in them at various points in its processing (\(dqhook points\(dq).
Those functions perform whatever custom processing is required.
.sp
The hooks concept allows the core Kea code to remain reasonably small
by moving features that only some, but not all, users find useful to
external libraries. Those with no need for certain functions can simply choose not
to load those libraries.
.sp
Hook libraries are loaded by individual Kea processes, not by Kea as a
whole. This means, among other things, that it is possible to associate one set
of libraries with the DHCPv4 server and a different set with the DHCPv6
server.
.sp
It is also possible for a process to load
multiple libraries. When processing reaches a hook point, Kea calls the
hook library functions attached to it. If multiple libraries have
attached a function to a given hook point, Kea calls all of them, in the
order in which the libraries are specified in the configuration file.
The order may be important; consult the documentation of the libraries
for specifics.
.sp
When a Kea process unloads a library, it expects the \fBdlclose\fP function
to remove all library symbols, as well as the library code, from address space.
Although most OSes implement the \fBdlclose\fP function, this behavior is not
required by the POSIX standard and not all systems support it; for example, the musl
library, used by default by Alpine Linux, implements the \fBdlclose\fP function
as a no operation. On such systems a library actually remains loaded for the
lifetime of the process, which means that it must be restarted
to update libraries with newer versions; it is not sufficient to simply
reconfigure or reload the Kea process.
.sp
The next sections describe how to install and configure hook libraries. Users who are
interested in writing their own hook library can find information
in the Hooks Developer\(aqs Guide section of the Kea Developer\(aqs
Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide/\:df/\:d46/\:hooksdgDevelopersGuide\:.html>\&.
.sp
Note that some libraries are available under different licenses.
.sp
Please also note that some libraries may require additional dependencies and/or
compilation switches to be enabled.
.SS Installing Hook Packages
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
For more details about installing the Kea Subscriber Hooks, please read
this Knowledgebase article \%<https://\:kb\:.isc\:.org/\:docs/\:aa-01587>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Most hook packages are now included in the base Kea sources. There is no
need to do anything special to compile or install them, as they are covered
by the usual building and installation procedures. Please
refer to Installation \%<#\:installation> for a general overview of the installation process.
.sp
ISC provides several additional subscriber\-only hooks in the form of packages, which
follow a similar installation procedure but with several additional steps.
For our users\(aq convenience, the subscriber hooks\(aq installation procedure is described in this section.
.sp
1. Download the package; detailed instructions are provided in the KB article
above. The package will be a file with a name similar to
\fBkea\-subscriber\-|release|.tar.xz\fP\&.
.sp
2. Administrators who have the sources for the corresponding version of the
open\-source Kea package on their system from the initial Kea installation
should skip this step. Otherwise, extract the Kea source from the original
tarball that was downloaded. For example, with a download of Kea 3.2.0,
there should be a tarball called \fBkea\-|release|.tar.xz\fP on the system.
Unpack this tarball:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ tar \-Jxvf kea\-3.2.0\&.tar.xz
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will unpack the tarball into the \fBkea\-|release|\fP subdirectory of
the current working directory.
.sp
3. Unpack the Kea subscriber hooks tarball into the same directory where the
original Kea source is located. Once Kea 3.2.0 has been unpacked into a \fBkea\-|release|\fP
subdirectory and the Kea subscriber tarball is in the current directory, the following
steps will unpack the subscriber tarball into the correct location:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ cd kea\-3.2.0
$ tar \-Jxvf ../kea\-subscriber\-3.2.0\&.tar.xz
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that unpacking the Kea subscriber package puts the files into a
directory named \fBpremium\fP\&. Regardless of the name of the package, the
directory is always called \fBpremium\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 4. 3
Set up the build. Make sure \fBmeson\fP and \fBninja\fP are installed on your
system. The first section of the output should look something like this:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson setup build
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Package:
  Name:               kea
  Version:            3.2.0
  Extended version:   3.2.0 (tarball)
  Version type:       development
  OS Family:          Linux

  Prefix:             /opt/kea
  Hooks directory:    /opt/kea/lib/kea/hooks
  Premium hooks:      yes
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 6. 3
Compile Kea.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson compile \-C build
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 7. 3
Install Kea sources along with the hooks:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sudo make install
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The installation location of the hook libraries depends on whether the
\fB\-\-prefix\fP parameter was specified at the \fBmeson setup\fP step. If not,
the default location is \fB/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks\fP\&. The proper installation
of the libraries can be verified with this command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ls \-l /usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/*.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libddns_gss_tsig.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_bootp.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_class_cmds.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_ddns_tuning.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_flex_id.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_flex_option.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_ha.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_host_cache.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_host_cmds.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_lease_cmds.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_lease_query.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_legal_log.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_limits.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_mysql.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_perfmon.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_pgsql.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_ping_check.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_radius.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_run_script.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_stat_cmds.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_subnet_cmds.so
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
with the following subscriber libraries:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_cb_cmds.so
/usr/local/lib/kea/hooks/libdhcp_rbac.so
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The exact list returned depends on the packages installed. If the
directory was specified via \fB\-\-prefix\fP, the hook libraries will be located in
\fB{prefix directory}/lib/kea/hooks\fP\&.
.SS Configuring Hook Libraries
.sp
The hook libraries for a given process are configured using the
\fBhooks\-libraries\fP keyword in the configuration for that process. (Note
that the word \(dqhooks\(dq is plural.) The value of the keyword is an array
of map structures, with each structure corresponding to a hook library. For
example, to set up two hook libraries for the DHCPv4 server, the
configuration would be:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqfirst_custom_hooks_example.so\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqsecond_custom_hooks_example.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqmail\(dq: \(dqspam@example.com\(dq,
                    \(dqfloor\(dq: 13,
                    \(dqdebug\(dq: false,
                    \(dqusers\(dq: [
                        \(dqalice\(dq,
                        \(dqbob\(dq,
                        \(dqcharlie\(dq
                    ],
                    \(dqlanguages\(dq: {
                        \(dqfrench\(dq: \(dqbonjour\(dq,
                        \(dqklingon\(dq: \(dqyl\(aqel\(dq
                    }
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Libraries are reloaded even if their lists have not changed,
because the parameters specified for the library (or the files those
parameters point to) may have changed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As of Kea 2.7.9, hook libraries may only be loaded from the default installation
directory determined during compilation and shown in the config report as
\(dqHooks directory\(dq.  This value may be overridden at startup by setting the
environment variable \fBKEA_HOOKS_PATH\fP to the desired path.  If a path other
than this value is used in a \fBlibrary\fP element Kea will emit an error and refuse
to load the library. For ease of use \fBlibrary\fP elements should simply omit path
components, specifying the file name only as shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqfirst_custom_hooks_example.so\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqsecond_custom_hooks_example.so\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The support for path components in \fBlibrary\fP elements is deprecated as of 2.7.8, and will
eventually be removed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Libraries may have additional parameters that are not mandatory, in the
sense that there may be libraries that do not require them. However, for any
given library there is often a requirement to specify a certain
set of parameters. Please consult the documentation for each individual library for
details. In the example above, the first library (\fB/opt/first_custom_hooks_example.so\fP)
has no parameters. The second library (\fB/opt/local/second_custom_hooks_example.so\fP)
has five parameters: specifying mail (string parameter), floor (integer parameter),
debug (boolean parameter), lists (list of strings), and maps (containing strings).
Nested parameters can be used if the library supports it. This topic is explained in detail
in the Hooks Developer\(aqs Guide section of the Kea Developer\(aqs Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide/\:df/\:d46/\:hooksdgDevelopersGuide\:.html>\&.
.sp
Some hooks use user context to set the parameters. See User Contexts in Hooks\&.
.sp
Notes:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The full path to each library should be given.
.IP \(bu 2
As noted above, the order in which the hooks are called may be important;
consult the documentation for each library for specifics.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty list has the same effect as omitting the \fBhooks\-libraries\fP
configuration element altogether.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
There is one case where this is not true: if Kea is running with a
configuration that contains a \fBhooks\-libraries\fP item, and that
item is removed and the configuration reloaded, the removal is
ignored and the libraries remain loaded. As a workaround,
instead of removing the \fBhooks\-libraries\fP item, change it to an
empty list.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
At the moment, only the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> processes support
hook libraries.
.SS Order of Configuration:
.sp
It is important to recognize that the order in which hook libraries are
configured determines the order in which their callouts will be executed,
in cases where more than one hook library implements the same callout. For
example, to use the Flexible Identifier (also called Flex ID) hook library to formulate the client
IDs in conjunction with the High Availability (HA) hook library for load\-balanced HA, it is essential
that the Flex ID library be specified first in the server\(aqs \fBhooks\-libraries\fP
section.  This ensures that the client ID is formulated by the Flex ID library
before the HA library uses it for load\-balancing. Similarly, it is best to
specify the Forensic Logging library last, to ensure that any other installed hooks have already made
their contributions to the packet processing before that one is loaded.
.SS User Contexts in Hooks
.sp
Hook libraries can have their own configuration parameters, which is
convenient if the parameter applies to the whole library. However,
sometimes it is useful to extend certain configuration entities
with additional configuration data. This is where the concept
of user contexts comes in. A system administrator can define an arbitrary set of
data and attach it to Kea structures, as long as the data is specified
as a JSON map. In particular, it is possible to define fields that are
integers, strings, boolean, lists, or maps. It is possible to define
nested structures of arbitrary complexity. Kea does not use that data on
its own; it simply stores it and makes it available for the hook libraries.
.sp
Another use case for user contexts may be storing comments and other
information that will be retained by Kea. Regular comments are discarded
when the configuration is loaded, but user contexts are retained. This is
useful if administrators want their comments to survive \fBconfig\-set\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-set> or \fBconfig\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-get>
operations, for example.
.sp
If user context is supported in a given context, the parser translates
\(dqcomment\(dq entries into user context with a \(dqcomment\(dq entry.
.sp
User context can store configuration for multiple hooks and comments at once.
.sp
Some hooks use user context for a configuration that can be easily edited
without the need to restart the server.
.sp
The DDNS Tuning hook uses user context to configure per\-subnet behavior. Here\(aqs an example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
    \(dqid\(dq: 1,
    \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
    \(dqpools\(dq: [{
        \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10 \- 192.0.2.20\(dq
    } ],
    \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
        \(dqddns\-tuning\(dq: {
            \(dqhostname\-expr\(dq: \(dq\(aqguest\-\(aq+int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 0,1))+\(aq\-\(aq \e
                                      +int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 1,2))+\(aq\-\(aq \e
                                      +int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 2,3))+\(aq\-\(aq \e
                                      +int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 3,4))\(dq
        },
        \(dqlast\-modified\(dq: \(dq2017\-09\-04 13:32\(dq,
        \(dqphones\(dq: [ \(dqx1234\(dq, \(dqx2345\(dq ],
        \(dqdevices\-registered\(dq: 42,
        \(dqbilling\(dq: false
    }
}]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Limits hook uses user\-context in classes and subnets to set parameters. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqgold\(dq,
        \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
          \(dqlimits\(dq: {
            \(dqaddress\-limit\(dq: 2,
            \(dqprefix\-limit\(dq: 1,
            \(dqrate\-limit\(dq: \(dq1000 packets per second\(dq
          }
        }
      }
    ],
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_limits.so\(dq
      }
    ],
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
          {
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::/64\(dq
          }
        ],
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::/64\(dq,
        \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
          \(dqlimits\(dq: {
            \(dqaddress\-limit\(dq: 4,
            \(dqprefix\-limit\(dq: 2,
            \(dqrate\-limit\(dq: \(dq10 packets per minute\(dq
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Parked\-Packet Limit
.sp
Kea servers contain a mechanism by which the response to a client packet may
be held, pending completion of hook library work. We refer to this as \(dqparking
the packet.\(dq When work is ready to continue, the server unparks the response
and continues processing.
.sp
There is a global parameter, \fBparked\-packet\-limit\fP, that may be used to limit
the number of responses that may be parked at any given time. This acts as a
form of congestion handling and protects the server from being swamped when the
volume of client queries is outpacing the server\(aqs ability to respond. Once the
limit is reached, the server emits a log and drops any new responses until
parking spaces are available.
.sp
In general, smaller values for the parking lot limit are likely to cause more
drops but with shorter response times; larger values are likely to result in
fewer drops but with longer response times. Currently, the default value for
\fBparked\-packet\-limit\fP is 256.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Using too small a value may result in an unnecessarily high drop rate, while
using too large a value may lead to response times that are simply too long
to be useful. A value of 0, while allowed, disables the limit altogether, but
this is highly discouraged as it may lead to Kea servers becoming
unresponsive to clients. Choosing the best value is very site\-specific; we
recommend users initially leave it at the default value of 256 and observe
how the system behaves over time with varying load conditions.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of the global parameter used with \fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP\&.
It lowers the number of concurrently parked packets to 128.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqparked\-packet\-limit\(dq: 128
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq
      },
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
          \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [
            {
              \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqhot\-standby\(dq,
              \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                {
                  \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                  \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                  \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://127.0.0.1:8080/\(dq
                },
                {
                  \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                  \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqstandby\(dq,
                  \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://127.0.0.1:8088/\(dq
                }
              ],
              \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Available Hook Libraries
.sp
As described above, the hook functionality provides a way to customize
a Kea server without modifying the core code. ISC has chosen to take
advantage of this feature to provide functions that may only be useful
to a subset of Kea users. To this end, ISC has created some hook
libraries, discussed in the following sections.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 3.0.0, most of these libraries are available at no cost with the open source base code;
a few are only available to organizations
that contribute to Kea\(aqs development through paid ISC support contracts. Paid support
includes professional engineering assistance, advance security notifications, input
into ISC\(aqs roadmap planning, and many other benefits, while helping
keep Kea sustainable in the long term. ISC encourages companies and organizations
to consider purchasing a paid support contract; further information can be
obtained by completing the form at \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:contact>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following table provides a list of hook libraries currently available
from ISC. It is important to pay attention to which libraries may be
loaded by which Kea processes. If a library
from ISC does not work as expected, please make sure that it has been
loaded by the correct process per the table below.
.SS List of available hook libraries
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Availability
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
BOOTP
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library adds BOOTP support, as defined in
RFC 1497. It recognizes received BOOTP requests:
they are translated into DHCPREQUEST packets, put into the
BOOTP client class, and receive infinite lifetime leases.
T}
_
T{
Class Commands
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library allows configured DHCP client classes to
be added, updated, deleted, and fetched without
needing to restart the DHCP server.
T}
_
T{
Configuration Backend Commands
T}	T{
ISC support
customers
T}	T{
This hook
library implements a collection of commands to manage
Kea configuration information in a
database. This library may only be used in conjunction with
one of the supported Configuration Backend implementations.
T}
_
T{
DDNS Tuning
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library adds custom behaviors related to Dynamic
DNS updates on a per\-client basis. Its primary feature is to
allow the host name used for DNS to be
calculated using an expression.
T}
_
T{
Flexible Identifier
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
Kea software provides a way to handle host reservations that
include addresses, prefixes, options, client classes and
other features. The reservation can be based on hardware
address, DUID, circuit\-id, or client\-id in DHCPv4 and on
hardware address or DUID in DHCPv6. However, there are
sometimes scenarios where the reservation is more complex,
e.g. uses other options than mentioned above, uses parts of
specific options, or perhaps uses a combination of several
options and fields to uniquely identify a client. Those
scenarios are addressed by the Flexible Identifier hook
application. It allows defining an expression, similar to
the one used in client classification,
e.g. \fBsubstring(relay6[0].option[37],0,6)\fP\&. Each incoming
packet is evaluated against that expression and its value is
then searched in the reservations database.
T}
_
T{
Flexible Option
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This library provides hooks that compute option values
instead of static configured values. An expression is
evaluated on the query packet. Defined add, supersede, and
remove actions are applied on the response packet before
it is sent using the evaluation result.
T}
_
T{
Forensic Logging
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This library provides hooks that record a detailed log of
lease assignments and renewals in a set of log files. In
many legal jurisdictions, companies \- especially ISPs \- must
record information about the addresses they have leased to
DHCP clients. This library is designed to help with that
requirement. If the information that it records is
sufficient, it may be used directly. If a jurisdiction
requires a different set of information to be saved, it can
be used as a template or example to create
custom logging hooks. In Kea 1.9.8, additional parameters
were added to give users more flexibility regarding
what information should be logged.
T}
_
T{
GSS\-TSIG
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library adds support to the Kea D2 server
(kea\-dhcp\-ddns) for using GSS\-TSIG to sign DNS updates.
T}
_
T{
High Availability
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
The risk of DHCP service unavailability can be minimized
by setting up a pair of DHCP servers in a network. Two
modes of operation are supported. The first one is called
load\-balancing, and is sometimes referred to as
\(dqactive\-active.\(dq Each server can handle selected groups of
clients in this network, or all clients if it detects that
its partner has become unavailable. It is also possible to
designate one server to serve all DHCP clients, and leave
another server as standby. This mode is called \(dqhot standby\(dq
and is sometimes referred to as \(dqactive\-passive.\(dq This
server activates its DHCP function only when it detects that
its partner is not available. Such cooperation between the
DHCP servers requires that these servers constantly
communicate with each other to send updates about allocated
leases, and to periodically test whether their partners are
still operational. The hook library also provides an ability
to send lease updates to external backup servers, making it
much easier to have a replacement that is up to date.
T}
_
T{
Host Cache
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
Some database backends, such as RADIUS,
may take a long time to respond. Since
Kea in general is synchronous, backend performance
directly affects DHCP performance. To minimize
performance impact, this library
provides a way to cache responses from other hosts. This
includes negative caching, i.e. the ability to remember that
there is no client information in the database.
T}
_
T{
Host Commands
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
Kea provides a way to store host reservations in a
database. In many larger deployments it is useful to be able
to manage that information while the server is running. This
library provides management commands for adding, querying,
and deleting host reservations in a safe way without
restarting the server. In particular, it validates the
parameters, so an attempt to insert incorrect data, e.g. add
a host with conflicting identifier in the same subnet, is
rejected. Those commands are exposed via the command channel
(JSON over UNIX or HTTP/HTTPS sockets),
T}
_
T{
Lease Commands
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library offers a number of commands used to
manage leases. Kea can store lease information in various
backends: memfile, MySQL, PostgreSQL. This library provides
a unified interface to manipulate leases in an unified, safe
way. In particular, it allows manipulation of memfile leases
while Kea is running, sanity check changes, lease existence
checks, and removal of all leases belonging to a specific
subnet. It can also catch obscure errors, like the addition
of a lease with subnet\-id that does not exist in the
configuration, or configuration of a lease to use an address
that is outside of the subnet to which it is supposed to
belong. This library allows easy management of user contexts
associated with leases.
T}
_
T{
Leasequery
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This library adds support for DHCPv4 Leasequery (RFC 4388),
DHCPv4 Bulk Leasequery (RFC6926); DHCPv6 Leasequery
(RFC 5007), and DHCPv6 Bulk Leasequery (RFC5460).
T}
_
T{
Limits
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
With this hook library, \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> servers can apply a limit to the rate at
which packets receive a response. The limit can be applied
per\-client class or per\-subnet.
T}
_
T{
MySQL Support Library
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library provides support for using MySQL
database(s) for Kea Lease, Host, and Configuration Backends.
T}
_
T{
PerfMon
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
With this hook library, \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> servers can track and report performance
data.
T}
_
T{
Ping Check
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
With this hook library, the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> server can
perform ping checks of candidate lease addresses before
offering them to clients.
T}
_
T{
PostgreSQL Support Library
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library provides support for using PostgreSQL
database(s) for Kea Lease, Host, and Configuration Backends.
T}
_
T{
RADIUS
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
The RADIUS hook library allows Kea to interact with
RADIUS servers using access and accounting mechanisms. The
access mechanism may be used for access control, assigning
specific IPv4 or IPv6 addresses reserved by RADIUS,
dynamically assigning addresses from designated pools chosen
by RADIUS, or rejecting the client\(aqs messages altogether.
The accounting mechanism allows a RADIUS server to keep
track of device activity over time.
T}
_
T{
RBAC
T}	T{
ISC support
customers
T}	T{
This hook library adds support to Kea servers (kea\-dhcp4,
kea\-dhcp6 and kea\-dhcp\-ddns) for Role\-Based Access Control
filtering of commands.
T}
_
T{
Run Script
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This hook library adds support to run external
scripts for specific packet\-processing hook points. There
are several exported environment variables available for
the script.
T}
_
T{
Statistics Commands
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
This library provides additional
commands for retrieving accurate DHCP lease statistics, for
Kea DHCP servers that share the same lease database. This
setup is common in deployments where DHCP service redundancy
is required and a shared lease database is used to avoid
lease\-data replication between the DHCP servers.
This hook library returns lease statistics
for each subnet.
T}
_
T{
Subnet Commands
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
In deployments in which subnet configuration needs to be
frequently updated, it is a hard requirement that such
updates be performed without the need for a full DHCP server
reconfiguration or restart. This hook library allows for
incremental changes to the subnet configuration such as
adding or removing a subnet. It also allows for
listing all available subnets and fetching detailed
information about a selected subnet. The commands exposed by
this library do not affect other subnets or configuration
parameters currently used by the server.
T}
_
T{
User Check
T}	T{
Kea open
source
T}	T{
Reads known users list from a file. Unknown users will be
assigned a lease from the last subnet defined in the
configuration file, e.g. to redirect them to a captive
portal. This demonstrates how an external source of
information can be used to influence the Kea allocation
engine. This hook is part of the Kea source code and is
available in the \fBsrc/hooks/dhcp/user_chk\fP directory.
T}
.TE
.sp
ISC hopes to see more hook libraries become available as time
progresses, developed both internally and externally. Since this list
may evolve dynamically, it is maintained on a wiki page, available
at \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea/\:-/\:wikis/\:Hooks-Available>\&.
Developers or others who are aware of any hook libraries not listed there
are asked to please send a note to the kea\-users or kea\-dev mailing lists for
updating. (Information on all of ISC\(aqs public mailing lists can be found
at \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:mailinglists>\&.)
.sp
The libraries developed by ISC are described in detail in the following
sections.
.SS \fBlibdhcp_bootp.so\fP: Support for BOOTP Clients
.sp
This hook library adds support for BOOTP with vendor\-information extensions
(RFC 1497 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc1497>). Received BOOTP
requests are recognized, translated into DHCPREQUEST packets by adding
a \fBdhcp\-message\-type\fP option, and put into the \(dqBOOTP\(dq client class.
Members of this class get infinite lifetime leases but the class can
also be used to guard a pool of addresses.
.sp
The DHCP\-specific options, such as \fBdhcp\-message\-type\fP, are removed from
the server\(aqs responses; responses shorter than the BOOTP minimum
size of 300 octets are padded to this size.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_bootp.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> process,
as there is no BOOTP protocol for IPv6.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This library is loaded similarly to other hook libraries, and
it takes no parameters.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {   \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_bootp.so\(dq },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
A host reservation for a BOOTP client should use the hardware address
as the identifier (the \fBclient\-id\fP option is a DHCP\-specific option).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Incoming BOOTP packets are added to the BOOTP class, allowing administrators
to segregate BOOTP clients into separate pools. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
        {
            // The DHCP class is the complement of the BOOTP class
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqDHCP\(dq,
            \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqnot member(\(aqBOOTP\(aq)\(dq
        }
    ],
    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                // BOOTP clients will be handled here
                \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.200 \- 192.0.2.254\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqBOOTP\(dq ]
            },
            {
                // Regular DHCP clients will be handled here
                \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1 \- 192.0.2.199\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqDHCP\(dq ]
            }],
            ...
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS BOOTP Hooks Limitations
.sp
Currently the BOOTP library has the following limitation:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Basic BOOTP, as defined in RFC 951 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc951>, is not supported. Kea only
supports BOOTP with vendor\-information extensions.
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP: Configuration Backend Commands
.sp
This hook library is used to manage Kea servers\(aq configurations in a
configuration backend database. This library must be used in conjunction with
the available CB hook libraries implementing the common APIs to create, read,
update, and delete (CRUD) the configuration information in the respective
databases. For example: \fBlibdhcp_mysql.so\fP implements this API for
MySQL while \fBlibdhcp_pgsql.so\fP implements this API for PostgreSQL.
To manage the configuration information in a MySQL database, both
\fBlibdhcp_mysql.so\fP and \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP must be
loaded by the server used for configuration management.
To manage the configuration information in a PostgreSQL database, both
\fBlibdhcp_pgsql.so\fP and \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP must be
loaded by the server used for configuration management.
.sp
More information on how to configure the Configuration Backend hook library for
use with a MySQL or PostgreSQL database can be found in the Configuration Backend in DHCPv4 \%<#\:dhcp4-cb>
and Configuration Backend in DHCPv6 \%<#\:dhcp6-cb> sections.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP is available only to ISC customers with
a paid support contract. For more information on subscription options,
please complete the form at \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:contact>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Please read about Limitations and Warnings \%<#\:cb-limitations> before using the commands
described in this section.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Command Structure
.sp
There are 5 types of commands supported by this library:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdel\fP \- delete the selected object from the database, e.g.
\fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-del\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBget\fP \- fetch the selected object from the database, e.g.
\fBremote\-subnet4\-get\-by\-id\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBget\-all\fP \- fetch all objects of the particular type from the
database, e.g. \fBremote\-option\-def4\-get\-all\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlist\fP \- list all objects of the particular type in the database,
e.g. \fBremote\-network4\-list\fP; this class of commands returns brief
information about each object compared to the output of \fBget\-all\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBset\fP \- creates or replaces an object of the given type in the
database, e.g. \fBremote\-option4\-global\-set\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
All types of commands accept an optional \fBremote\fP map which selects the
database instance to which the command refers. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet4\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
            \(dqhost\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.33\(dq,
            \(dqport\(dq: 3302
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
selects the MySQL database, running on host 192.0.2.33 and port 3302, to
fetch the list of subnets from. All parameters in the \fBremote\fP argument are
optional. The \fBport\fP parameter can be only specified in conjunction
with the \fBhost\fP\&. If no options in the \fBremote\fP parameter are to
be specified, the parameter should be omitted. In this case, the server
will use the first backend listed in the \fBconfig\-control\fP map within
the configuration of the server receiving the command.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
In the current Kea release, it is only possible to configure the Kea server
to use a single configuration backend. Strictly speaking, it is
possible to point the Kea server to at most one database (either MySQL or
PostgreSQL) using the \fBconfig\-control\fP parameter. Therefore, the \fBremote\fP
parameter may be omitted in the commands and \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP
uses the sole backend by default.  The example commands below most often show a
value of \(dqmysql\(dq for the \fBtype\fP parameter; it should be assumed that the
value is \(dqpostgresql\(dq for installations using a PostgreSQL database.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Control Commands for DHCP Servers
.sp
This section describes and gives some examples of the control commands
implemented by \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP, to manage the
configuration information of the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 servers. Many of the
commands are almost identical between DHCPv4 and DHCPv6; they only
differ by the command name. Other commands differ slightly by the
structure of the inserted data; for example, the structure of the IPv4 subnet
information is different than that of the IPv6 subnet.
Nevertheless, they still share the structure of their command arguments
and thus it makes sense to describe them together.
.sp
In addition, whenever the text in the subsequent sections refers to a
DHCP command or DHCP parameter, it refers to both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6
variants. The text specific to the particular server type refers to them
as: DHCPv4 command, DHCPv4 parameter, DHCPv6 command, DHCPv6 parameter,
etc.
.SS Metadata
.sp
The typical response to the \fBget\fP or \fBlist\fP command includes a list
of returned objects (e.g. subnets), and each such object contains the
\fBmetadata\fP map with some database\-specific information describing this
object. In other words, the metadata contains any information about the
fetched object which may be useful for an administrator but which is not
part of the object specification from the DHCP server standpoint. In the
present Kea release, the metadata is limited to the \fBserver\-tag\fP\&. It
describes the association of the object with a particular server or
all servers.
.sp
The following is the example response to the \fBremote\-network4\-list\fP
command, which includes the metadata:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 IPv4 shared network(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqlevel3\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Client implementations must not assume that the metadata contains only
the \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter. In future releases, it is expected that this
map will be extended with additional information, e.g. object modification
time, log message created during the last modification, etc.
.SS Option Class\-tags as Keys
.sp
Option Class\-Tagging \%<#\:option-class-tagging> added a list of client classes, \fBclient\-classes\fP,
to option specifications. Thus in any list of option specifications (e.g.
\fIoption\-data\fP), there may be more than one specification for the same \fBcode\fP
and \fBspace\fP\(aq but with different \fBclient\-classes\fP lists and different
data content.
.sp
In order to support this construct, Kea uses the option\(aqs \fBclient\-classes\fP
list in addition to code and space to uniquely identifiy each option. This
is important to keep in mind when using the Management API to alter your
configuration. As of Kea 3.1.1 commands which get, set, or delete an
individual option now accept an optional \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter in
addition to \fBcode\fP and \fBspace\fP parameters.
.sp
If the \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter is omitted:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Get \- Fetches one of n that matches code and space. Which option is returned
is indeterminate when more than one match exists.
.IP 2. 3
Set \- Updates only the option that matches code and space and has an empty
client\-classes list. If no match is found then the option is created.
.IP 3. 3
Delete \- Deletes all options that match the code and space, if they exist.
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter is specified:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Get \- Fetches the option that matches code, space and client\-classes if
one exists.
.IP 2. 3
Set \- Updates only the option that matches code, space and client\-classes.
If no match is found then the option is created.
.IP 3. 3
Delete \- Deletes only the option that matches code, space and client\-classes,
if one exists.
.UNINDENT
.sp
An empty \fBclient\-classes\fP list is specified as \(aq[  ]\(aq with or without white spaces
between the brackets. When managing lists of options that do not employ client class\-
tagging, the \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter may simply be omitted.
.SS The \fBremote\-server4\-del\fP, \fBremote\-server6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
This command is used to delete the information about a selected DHCP server from
the configuration database. The server is identified by a unique case
insensitive server tag. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-server4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqservers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqpostgresql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As a result of this command, all associations of the configuration for the
user\-defined server called \(dqserver1\(dq are removed from the database, including
non\-shareable configuration information, such as global parameters, option
definitions, and global options. Any shareable configuration information,
i.e. the configuration elements which may
be associated with more than one server, is preserved. In particular, the
subnets and shared networks associated with the deleted servers are
preserved. If any of the shareable configuration elements was associated only
with the deleted server, this object becomes unassigned (orphaned). For
example: if a subnet has been created and associated with \(dqserver1\(dq using
the \fBremote\-subnet4\-set\fP command and \(dqserver1\(dq is subsequently deleted, the
subnet remains in the database but no servers can use this subnet. The
subnet can be updated using the \fBremote\-subnet4\-set\fP command, and can be
associated with either another server or with all servers, using the special
server tag \(dqall\(dq. Such a subnet can be also deleted from the database
using the \fBremote\-subnet4\-del\-by\-id\fP or
\fBremote\-subnet4\-del\-by\-prefix\fP command, if it is no longer needed.
.sp
The following is the successful response to the \fBremote\-server4\-del\fP command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv4 server(s) deleted.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBremote\-server4\-del\fP and
\fBremote\-server6\-del\fP commands must be used with
care, because an accidental deletion of the server can cause some parts of the
existing configurations to be lost permanently from the database. This
operation is not reversible. Re\-creation of the accidentally deleted server
does not revert the lost configuration for that server and such configuration
must be re\-created manually by the user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-server4\-get\fP, \fBremote\-server6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
This command is used to fetch the information about the selected DHCP server
from the configuration database. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-server6\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqservers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command fetches the information about the DHCPv6 server identified by the
server tag \(dqserver1\(dq. The server tag is case\-insensitive. A successful response
returns basic information about the server, such as the server tag and the user\(aqs
description of the server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCP server server1 found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqservers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqA DHCPv6 server located on the first floor.\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-server4\-get\-all\fP, \fBremote\-server6\-get\-all\fP Commands
.sp
This command is used to fetch all user\-defined DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 servers from the
database. The command structure is very simple:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-server4\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response includes basic information about each server, such as its server
tag and description:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 servers found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqservers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqA DHCP server located on the first floor.\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqAn old DHCP server to be soon replaced.\(dq
            }
        ],
       \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-server4\-set\fP, \fBremote\-server6\-set\fP Commands
.sp
This command is used to create or replace an information about a DHCP server in
the database. The information about the server must be created when there is a
need to differentiate the configurations used by various Kea instances
connecting to the same database. Various configuration elements, e.g. global
parameters, subnets, etc. may be explicitly associated with the selected servers
(using server tags as identifiers), allowing only these servers to use the
respective configuration elements. Using the particular server tag to make such
associations is only possible when the server information has been stored in the
database via the \fBremote\-server4\-set\fP or
\fBremote\-server6\-set\fP commands. The
following command creates a new (or updates an existing) DHCPv6 server in the
database:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-server6\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqservers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqA DHCP server on the ground floor.\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The server tag must be unique across all servers in the database. When the
server information under the given server tag already exists, it is replaced
with the new information. The specified server tag is case\-insensitive, and the
maximum length of the server tag is 256 characters. The following keywords are
reserved and cannot be used as server tags: \(dqall\(dq and \(dqany\(dq.
.sp
The following is the example response to the above command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv6 server successfully set.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqservers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqserver\-tag\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqdescription\(dq: \(dqA DHCP server on the ground floor.\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-del\fP, \fBremote\-global\-parameter6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete a global DHCP parameter from the
configuration database. When the parameter is deleted from the database,
the server uses the value specified in the configuration file for
this parameter, or a default value if the parameter is not specified in
the configuration file.
.sp
The following command attempts to delete the DHCPv4 \fBrenew\-timer\fP
parameter common for all servers from the database:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: [ \(dqrenew\-timer\(dq ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
         },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If a server\-specific parameter is to be deleted, the
\fBserver\-tags\fP list must contain the tag of the appropriate
server. There must be exactly one server tag specified in this list.
.SS The \fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\fP, \fBremote\-global\-parameter6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to fetch a scalar global DHCP parameter from the
configuration database.
.sp
The following command attempts to fetch the \fBboot\-file\-name\fP
parameter for \(dqserver1\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: [ \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq ],
         \(dqremote\(dq: {
             \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
         },
         \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned value has one of the four scalar types: string, integer,
real, or boolean. Non\-scalar global configuration parameters, such as map
or list, are not returned by this command.
.sp
In the case of the example above, the string value is returned, e.g.:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv4 global parameter found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq,
            \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
            }
        },
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that the response above indicates that the returned parameter is associated
with \(dqall\(dq servers rather than \(dqserver1\(dq, used in the command. This indicates
that there is no \(dqserver1\(dq\-specific value in the database and therefore, the value
shared by all servers is returned. If there were a \(dqserver1\(dq\-specific value
in the database, that value would be returned instead.
.sp
The example response for the integer value is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv4 global parameter found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqrenew\-timer\(dq: 2000,
            \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
            }
        },
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The real value:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv4 global parameter found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqt1\-percent\(dq: 0.85,
            \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
            }
        },
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Finally, the boolean value:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv4 global parameter found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqmatch\-client\-id\(dq: true,
            \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver2\(dq ]
            }
        },
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\-all\fP, \fBremote\-global\-parameter6\-get\-all\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to fetch all global DHCP parameters from the database
for the specified server. The following example demonstrates how to fetch all
global parameters to be used by the server \(dqserver1\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The example response may look as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 global parameters found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqmatch\-client\-id\(dq: true,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The example response contains two parameters: one string parameter and one
boolean parameter. The metadata returned for each parameter indicates
whether this parameter is specific to \(dqserver1\(dq or applies to all servers. Since the
\fBmatch\-client\-id\fP value is associated with \(dqall\(dq servers,
it indicates that there is no \(dqserver1\(dq\-specific setting for this parameter.
Each parameter always has exactly one server tag associated with it, because
global parameters are non\-shareable configuration elements.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If the server tag is set to \(dqall\(dq in the command, the response will
contain only the global parameters associated with the logical server
\(dqall\(dq. When the server tag points to the specific server (as in the
example above), the returned list combines parameters associated with
this server and all servers, but the former take precedence.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-set\fP, \fBremote\-global\-parameter6\-set\fP Commands
.sp
This command is used to create scalar global DHCP parameters in the
database. If any of the parameters already exists, its value is replaced
as a result of this command. It is possible to set multiple parameters
within a single command, each having one of the four types: string,
integer, real, or boolean. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq,
            \(dqrenew\-timer\(dq: 2000,
            \(dqt1\-percent\(dq: 0.85,
            \(dqmatch\-client\-id\(dq: true
        },
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An error is returned if any of the parameters is not supported by the DHCP
server or its type does not match. Care should be taken when multiple parameters
are specified in a single command, because it is possible that only some of the
parameters will be stored successfully and some will fail. If an error occurs when
processing this command, it is recommended to use
\fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\-all\fP or
\fBremote\-global\-parameter6\-get\-all\fP
to check which of the parameters have
been stored/updated successfully and which have failed.
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain a single server tag or
the keyword \(dqall\(dq. In the example above, all specified parameters are associated
with the \(dqserver1\(dq server.
.SS The \fBremote\-network4\-del\fP, \fBremote\-network6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete an IPv4 or IPv6 shared network from
the database. The optional parameter \fBsubnets\-action\fP determines
whether the subnets belonging to the deleted shared network should also
be deleted or preserved. The \fBsubnets\-action\fP parameter defaults to \fBkeep\fP,
which preserves the subnets. If it is set to \fBdelete\fP, the subnets are
deleted along with the shared network.
.sp
The following command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network6\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqlevel3\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnets\-action\(dq: \(dqkeep\(dq,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
deletes the \(dqlevel3\(dq IPv6 shared network. The subnets are preserved, but
they are disassociated from the deleted shared network and become
global. This behavior corresponds to the behavior of the
\fBnetwork4\-del\fP, \fBnetwork6\-del\fP commands with respect to the
\fBsubnets\-action\fP parameter.
.sp
Note that the \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used for this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-network4\-get\fP, \fBremote\-network6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to retrieve information about an IPv4 or
IPv6 shared network. The optional parameter \fBsubnets\-include\fP denotes
whether the subnets belonging to the shared network should also be
returned. This parameter defaults to \fBno\fP, in which case the subnets
are not returned. If this parameter is set to \fBfull\fP, the subnets are
returned together with the shared network.
.sp
The following command fetches the \(dqlevel3\(dq IPv6 shared network along
with the full information about the subnets belonging to it:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network6\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqlevel3\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnets\-include\(dq: \(dqfull\(dq,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that the \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used for this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-network4\-list\fP, \fBremote\-network6\-list\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to list all IPv4 or IPv6 shared networks for a server.
.sp
The following command retrieves all shared networks to be used by
\(dqserver1\(dq and \(dqserver2\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network4\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq, \(dqserver2\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter is mandatory and contains one or more server
tags. It may contain the keyword \(dqall\(dq to fetch the shared networks associated
with all servers. When the \fBserver\-tags\fP list contains the
\fBnull\fP value, the returned response contains a list of unassigned shared
networks, i.e. the networks which are associated with no servers. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network4\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ null ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The example response to this command when non\-null server tags are specified
looks similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq3 IPv4 shared network(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqground floor\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor2\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor3\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver2\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 3
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned information about each shared network merely contains the shared
network name and the metadata. To fetch detailed information about
the selected shared network, use the \fBremote\-network4\-get\fP or
\fBremote\-network6\-get\fP command.
.sp
The example response above contains three shared networks. One of the
shared networks is associated with all servers, so it is included in
the list of shared networks to be used by \(dqserver1\(dq and \(dqserver2\(dq.
The remaining two shared networks are returned because one of them
is associated with \(dqserver1\(dq and another one is associated with
\(dqserver2\(dq.
.sp
When listing unassigned shared networks, the response looks similar
to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 IPv4 shared network(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfancy\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ null ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBnull\fP value in the metadata indicates that the
returned shared network is unassigned.
.SS The \fBremote\-network4\-set\fP, \fBremote\-network6\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands create a new or replace an existing IPv4 or IPv6 shared
network in the database. The structure of the shared network information
is the same as in the Kea configuration file (see
Shared Networks in DHCPv4 \%<#\:shared-network4> and Shared Networks in DHCPv6 \%<#\:shared-network6> for details),
except that specifying subnets along with the shared
network information is not allowed. Including the \fBsubnet4\fP or \fBsubnet6\fP parameter
within the shared network information results in an error.
.sp
These commands are intended to be used for managing the shared
network\-specific information and DHCP options. To associate and
disassociate the subnets with the shared networks, the
\fBremote\-subnet4\-set\fP, \fBremote\-subnet6\-set\fP
commands should be used.
.sp
The following command adds the IPv6 shared network \(dqlevel3\(dq to the
database:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network6\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqlevel3\(dq,
                \(dqinterface\(dq: \(dqeth0\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqsntp\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq
                } ]
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes the \fBinterface\fP parameter, which sets the shared
network\-level interface name. Any remaining shared\-network\-level parameters,
which are not specified with the command, will be marked as
\(dqunspecified\(dq in the database. The DHCP server uses the global
values for unspecified parameters or, if the global values are not
specified, the default values are used.
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory for this command and must include one or
more server tags. As a result, the shared network is associated with all listed
servers. The shared network may be associated with all servers connecting to the
database when the keyword \(dqall\(dq is included.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As with other \(dqset\(dq commands, this command replaces all the
information about the given shared network in the database, if the
shared network already exists. Therefore, when sending this command,
make sure to always include all parameters that must be specified for
the updated shared\-network instance. Any unspecified parameter will
be marked unspecified in the database, even if its value was present
prior to sending the command.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-option\-def4\-del\fP, \fBremote\-option\-def6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete a DHCP option definition from the
database. The option definition is identified by an option code and
option space. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def6\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 1,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqisc\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
deletes the definition of the option associated with \(dqserver1\(dq, having the
code of 1 and belonging to the option space \(dqisc\(dq. The default option spaces are
\(dqdhcp4\(dq and \(dqdhcp6\(dq for the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 top\-level options, respectively. If
there is no such option definition explicitly associated with \(dqserver1\(dq, no option
definition is deleted. To delete an option definition belonging to \(dqall\(dq servers,
the keyword \(dqall\(dq must be used as the server tag. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list must
contain exactly one tag and cannot include the \fBnull\fP value.
.sp
As of Kea 3.1.2, before deleting an option definition, the server will first
check if there are any options specified that depend upon that definition. If
so the delete command will be rejected with an error message explaining why.
This default behavior may be overridden by setting the optional \fBforce\fP
parameter to true as shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def6\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 1,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqisc\(dq,
                \(dqforce\(dq: true
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBforce\fP parameter should only be used after careful consideration.
Removing an option definition while a dependent option specification exists
will cause that option to be excluded from the running configuration.
This parameter is provided to handle use cases where a definition may
need to be corrected while leaving the option in place.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-option\-def4\-get\fP, \fBremote\-option\-def6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to fetch a specified DHCP option definition from
the database. The option definition is identified by the option code and
option space. The default option spaces are \(dqdhcp4\(dq and \(dqdhcp6\(dq for the
DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 top\-level options, respectively.
.sp
The following command retrieves a DHCPv4 option definition associated with all
servers, having the code of 1 and belonging to the option space \(dqisc\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 1,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqisc\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list must include exactly one server tag or the keyword
\(dqall\(dq, and cannot contain the \fInull\fP value.
.SS The \fBremote\-option\-def4\-get\-all\fP, \fBremote\-option\-def6\-get\-all\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to fetch all DHCP option definitions from the database
for the given server or all servers. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def6\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command attempts to fetch all DHCPv6 option definitions associated
with \(dqall\(dq servers. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory for
this command and must include exactly one server tag or the keyword \(dqall\(dq.
It cannot include the \fBnull\fP value.
.sp
The following is the example response to this command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv6 option definition(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq,
                \(dqcode\(dq: 1012,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
                \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqrecord\(dq,
                \(dqarray\(dq: true,
                \(dqrecord\-types\(dq: \(dqipv6\-address, uint16\(dq,
                \(dqencapsulate\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response contains an option definition associated with all servers, as
indicated by the metadata.
.SS The \fBremote\-option\-def4\-set\fP, \fBremote\-option\-def6\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands create a new DHCP option definition or replace an
existing option definition in the database. The structure of the option
definition information is the same as in the Kea configuration file (see
Custom DHCPv4 Options \%<#\:dhcp4-custom-options> and Custom DHCPv6 Options \%<#\:dhcp6-custom-options>).
The following command creates the DHCPv4 option definition at the
top\-level \(dqdhcp4\(dq option space and associates it with \(dqserver1\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                \(dqcode\(dq: 222,
                \(dqtype\(dq: \(dquint32\(dq,
                \(dqarray\(dq: false,
                \(dqrecord\-types\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq,
                \(dqencapsulate\(dq: \(dq\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list must include exactly one
server tag or the keyword \(dqall\(dq, and cannot contain the
\fBnull\fP value.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-global\-del\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-global\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete a global DHCP option from the
database. The option is identified by an option code and option space.
As of Kea 3.1.1 an optional \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter may also be
specified (see Option Class\-tags as Keys).
.sp
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option4\-global\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 5,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqclass\-one\(dq ]
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \(dqdhcp4\(dq value represents the top\-level option space where the standard DHCPv4
options belong. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter is mandatory and must include a
single option tag or the keyword \(dqall\(dq. If the explicit server tag is specified,
this command attempts to delete a global option associated with this
server. If there is no such option associated with the given server, no option
is deleted. To delete an option associated with all servers, the
keyword \(dqall\(dq must be specified.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-global\-get\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-global\-get\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to fetch a global DHCP option from the database.
The option is identified by the code and option space. The top\-level
option spaces where DHCP standard options belong are called \(dqdhcp4\(dq and
\(dqdhcp6\(dq for the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 servers, respectively.
As of Kea 3.1.1, an optional \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter may also be
specified (see Option Class\-tags as Keys).
.sp
The following command retrieves the IPv6 \(dqDNS Servers\(dq (code 23) option
associated with all servers:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-global\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqclass\-one\(dq ]
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter is mandatory and must include exactly one
server tag or the keyword \(dqall\(dq. It cannot contain the \fBnull\fP
value.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-global\-get\-all\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-global\-get\-all\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to fetch all global DHCP options from the configuration
database for the given server or for all servers. The following command
fetches all global DHCPv4 options for \(dqserver1\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-global\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory for this command and
must contain exactly one server tag or a keyword \(dqall\(dq; it cannot contain
the \fBnull\fP value.
.sp
The following is a example response to this
command with a single option being associated with \(dqserver1\(dq returned:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 options found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\-servers\(dq,
                \(dqcode\(dq: 6,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq,
                \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: false,
                \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.3\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-global\-set\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-global\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands create a new global DHCP option or replace an existing
option in the database. The structure of the option information is the
same as in the Kea configuration file (see Standard DHCPv4 Options \%<#\:dhcp4-std-options>
and Standard DHCPv6 Options \%<#\:dhcp6-std-options>). For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-global\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory for this command
and must include exactly one server tag or the keyword \(dqall\(dq; it cannot
include the \fBnull\fP value. The command above associates
the option with the \(dqserver1\(dq server.
.sp
Note that specifying an option name instead of the option code only
works reliably for standard DHCP options. When specifying a value
for a user\-defined DHCP option, the option code should be indicated
instead of the name. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-global\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 1,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqisc\(dq,
                \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-network\-del\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-network\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete a shared\-network\-specific DHCP
option from the database. The option is identified by an option code
and option space, and as of Kea 3.1.1, an optional \fBclient\-classes\fP
parameter may also be specified (see Option Class\-tags as Keys).
These parameters are passed within the \fBoptions\fP list.
Another list, \fBshared\-networks\fP, contains a map
with the name of the shared network from which the option is to
be deleted. If the option is not explicitly specified for this
shared network, no option is deleted. In particular, the given
option may be present for a subnet belonging to the shared network.
Such an option instance is not affected by this command as this
command merely deletes the shared\-network\-level option. To
delete a subnet\-level option, the \fBremote\-option4\-subnet\-del\fP,
\fBremote\-option6\-subnet\-del\fP commands must be used instead.
.sp
The following command attempts to delete an option having the
option code 5 in the top\-level option space and an empty client\-classes
list from the shared network \(dqfancy\(dq.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option4\-network\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfancy\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 5,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq : [ ]
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \(dqdhcp4\(dq value represents the top\-level option space where the standard DHCPv4
options belong. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-network\-set\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-network\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands create a new shared\-network\-specific DHCP option or replace
an existing option in the database. The structure of the option information
is the same as in the Kea configuration file (see Standard DHCPv4 Options \%<#\:dhcp4-std-options>
and Standard DHCPv6 Options \%<#\:dhcp6-std-options>). The option information is carried in the
\fBoptions\fP list.  Another list, \fBshared\-networks\fP, contains a map with the
name of the shared network for which the option is to be set. If such an option
already exists for the shared network, it is replaced with the new instance.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-network\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfancy\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.sp
Specifying an option name instead of the option code only works reliably
for standard DHCP options. When specifying a value for a user\-defined
DHCP option, the option code should be indicated instead of the name.
.SS The \fBremote\-option6\-pd\-pool\-del\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to delete a prefix delegation pool\-specific DHCPv6
option from the database. The option is identified by an option code and
option space, and as of Kea 3.1.1, an optional \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter
may also be specified (see Option Class\-tags as Keys).
These parameters are passed within the \fBoptions\fP list.
Another list, \fBpd\-pools\fP, contains a map with the
prefix\-delegation\-pool prefix and length identifying the pool. If the
option is not explicitly specified for this pool, no option is deleted.
In particular, the given option may exist for a subnet containing
the specified pool. Such an option instance is not affected by this
command, as this command merely deletes a prefix delegation pool\-level
option. To delete a subnet\-level option, the
\fBremote\-option6\-subnet\-del\fP command must be used instead.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-pd\-pool\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpd\-pools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqprefix\(dq: \(dq3000::\(dq,
                \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 64
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \(dqdhcp6\(dq value represents the top\-level option space where the standard DHCPv6
options belong. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-option6\-pd\-pool\-set\fP Command
.sp
This command creates a new prefix delegation pool\-specific DHCPv6 option or
replaces an existing option in the database. The structure of the option
information is the same as in the Kea configuration file (see Standard DHCPv4 Options \%<#\:dhcp4-std-options>
and Standard DHCPv6 Options \%<#\:dhcp6-std-options>). The option information is carried in the
\fBoptions\fP list. Another list, \fBpd\-pools\fP, contains a map with the
prefix\-delegation\-pool prefix and the prefix length identifying the pool. If such an
option already exists for the prefix delegation pool, it is replaced with
the new instance.
.sp
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-pd\-pool\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpd\-pools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqprefix\(dq: \(dq3001:1::\(dq,
                \(dqlength\(dq: 64
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.sp
Specifying an option name instead of the option code only works reliably
for standard DHCP options. When specifying a value for a user\-defined
DHCP option, the option code should be indicated instead of the name.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-pool\-del\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-pool\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete an address\-pool\-specific DHCP
option from the database. The option is identified by an option code
and option space, and as of Kea 3.1.1, an optional \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter
may also be specified (see Option Class\-tags as Keys).
These parameters are passed within the \fBoptions\fP list.
Another list, \fBpools\fP, contains a map with the
IP address range or prefix identifying the pool. If the option
is not explicitly specified for this pool, no option is deleted.
In particular, the given option may exist for a subnet containing
the specified pool. Such an option instance is not affected by this
command, as this command merely deletes a pool\-level option. To
delete a subnet\-level option, the \fBremote\-option4\-subnet\-del\fP
or \fBremote\-option6\-subnet\-del\fP command must be used instead.
.sp
The following command attempts to delete an option having the
option code 5 in the top\-level option space from an IPv4 address
pool:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option4\-pool\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10 \- 192.0.2.100\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 5,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \(dqdhcp4\(dq value represents the top\-level option space where the standard DHCPv4
options belong. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-pool\-set\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-pool\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands create a new address\-pool\-specific DHCP option or replace
an existing option in the database. The structure of the option information
is the same as in the Kea configuration file (see Standard DHCPv4 Options \%<#\:dhcp4-std-options>
and Standard DHCPv6 Options \%<#\:dhcp6-std-options>). The option information is carried in the
\fBoptions\fP list. Another list, \fBpools\fP, contains a map with the IP address
range or prefix identifying the pool. If such an option already exists for
the pool, it is replaced with the new instance.
.sp
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option4\-pool\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10 \- 192.0.2.100\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\-servers\(dq,
                \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq10.0.0.1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.sp
Specifying an option name instead of the option code only works reliably
for standard DHCP options. When specifying a value for a user\-defined
DHCP option, the option code should be indicated instead of the name.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-subnet\-del\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-subnet\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete a subnet\-specific DHCP option
from the database. The option is identified by an option code
and option space, and, as of Kea 3.1.1, an optional \fBclient\-classes\fP
parameter (see Option Class\-tags as Keys).
These parameters are passed within the \fBoptions\fP list.
Another list, \fBsubnets\fP, contains a map with the
identifier of the subnet from which the option is to be deleted.
If the option is not explicitly specified for this subnet, no
option is deleted.
.sp
The following command attempts to delete an option having the
option code 5 in the top\-level option space from the subnet
having an identifier of 123.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option4\-subnet\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 123
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: 5,
                \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \(dqdhcp4\(dq value represents the top\-level option space where the standard DHCPv4
options belong. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-option4\-subnet\-set\fP, \fBremote\-option6\-subnet\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands create a new subnet\-specific DHCP option or replace an existing
option in the database. The structure of the option information is the same as
in the Kea configuration file (see Standard DHCPv4 Options \%<#\:dhcp4-std-options>
and Standard DHCPv6 Options \%<#\:dhcp6-std-options>). The option information is carried in the
\fBoptions\fP list. Another list, \fBsubnets\fP, contains a map with the identifier of
the subnet for which the option is to be set. If such an option already exists
for the subnet, it is replaced with the new instance.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option6\-subnet\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 123
            }
        ],
        \(dqoptions\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be specified for this command.
.sp
Specifying an option name instead of the option code only works reliably
for the standard DHCP options. When specifying a value for the user\-defined
DHCP option, the option code should be indicated instead of the name.
.SS The \fBremote\-subnet4\-del\-by\-id\fP, \fBremote\-subnet6\-del\-by\-id\fP Commands
.sp
This is the first variant of the commands used to delete an IPv4 or IPv6
subnet from the database. It uses the subnet ID to identify the subnet. For
example, to delete the IPv4 subnet with an ID of 5:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet4\-del\-by\-id\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 5
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used with this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-subnet4\-del\-by\-prefix\fP, \fBremote\-subnet6\-del\-by\-prefix\fP Commands
.sp
This is the second variant of the commands used to delete an IPv4 or
IPv6 subnet from the database. It uses the subnet prefix to identify the
subnet. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet6\-del\-by\-prefix\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used with this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-subnet4\-get\-by\-id\fP, \fBremote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-id\fP Commands
.sp
This is the first variant of the commands used to fetch an IPv4 or IPv6
subnet from the database. It uses a subnet ID to identify the subnet.
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet4\-get\-by\-id\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 5
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used with this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-subnet4\-get\-by\-prefix\fP, \fBremote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-prefix\fP Commands
.sp
This is the second variant of the commands used to fetch an IPv4 or IPv6
subnet from the database. It uses a subnet prefix to identify the
subnet. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-prefix\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used with this command.
.SS The \fBremote\-subnet4\-list\fP, \fBremote\-subnet6\-list\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to list all IPv4 or IPv6 subnets from the database for
selected servers or all servers. The following command retrieves all servers to
be used by \(dqserver1\(dq and \(dqserver2\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet4\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq, \(dqserver2\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter is mandatory and contains one or
more server tags. It may contain the keyword \(dqall\(dq, to fetch the subnets
associated with all servers. When the \fBserver\-tags\fP list
contains the \fBnull\fP value, the returned response contains a list
of unassigned subnets, i.e. the subnets which are associated with no servers.
For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet4\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ null ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The example response to this command when non\-null server tags are specified
looks similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv4 subnet(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: null,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq, \(dqserver2\(dq ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 2,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: null,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned information about each subnet is limited to the subnet identifier,
prefix, and associated shared network name. To retrieve full
information about the selected subnet, use
the \fBremote\-subnet4\-get\-by\-id\fP / \fBremote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-id\fP command
or the \fBremote\-subnet4\-get\-by\-prefix\fP / \fBremote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-prefix\fP command.
.sp
The example response above contains two subnets. One of the subnets is
associated with both servers: \(dqserver1\(dq and \(dqserver2\(dq. The second subnet is
associated with all servers, so it is also present in the configurations for
\(dqserver1\(dq and \(dqserver2\(dq.
.sp
When listing unassigned subnets, the response will look similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 IPv4 subnet(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 3,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.4.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: null,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ null ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBnull\fP value in the metadata indicates that the
returned subnet is unassigned.
.SS The \fBremote\-subnet4\-set\fP, \fBremote\-subnet6\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to create a new IPv4 or IPv6 subnet or replace
an existing subnet in the database. Setting the subnet also associates
or disassociates the subnet with a shared network.
.sp
The structure of the subnet information is similar to the structure used
in the configuration file (see DHCPv4 Server Configuration \%<#\:dhcp4-configuration> and
DHCPv6 Server Configuration \%<#\:dhcp6-configuration>). The subnet information conveyed in the
\fBremote\-subnet4\-set\fP or \fBremote\-subnet6\-set\fP command
must include the additional parameter
\fBshared\-network\-name\fP, which denotes whether the subnet belongs to a
shared network.
.sp
Consider the following example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 5,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: \(dqlevel3\(dq,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\-192.0.2.200\(dq } ],
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq
                } ]
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It creates the subnet and associates it with the \(dqlevel3\(dq shared
network. The \(dqlevel3\(dq shared network must be created with the \fBremote\-network4\-set\fP
command prior to creating the subnet.
.sp
If the created subnet must be global \- that is, not associated with any shared
network \- the \fBshared\-network\-name\fP must be explicitly set to
\fBnull\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 5,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: null,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\-192.0.2.200\(dq } ],
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq
                } ]
           }
        ],
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The subnet created in the previous example is replaced with the new
subnet having the same parameters, but it becomes global.
.sp
The \fBshared\-network\-name\fP parameter is mandatory for the
\fBremote\-subnet4\-set\fP command. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must
include one or more server tags. As a result, the subnet is associated with all
of the listed servers. It may also be associated with all servers connecting
to the database when the keyword \(dqall\(dq is used as the server tag.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As with other \(dqset\(dq commands, this command replaces all the
information about the particular subnet in the database, if the
subnet information is already present. Therefore, when sending this
command, make sure to always include all parameters that must be
specified for the updated subnet instance. Any unspecified parameter
will be marked as unspecified in the database, even if its value was
present prior to sending the command.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-class4\-del\fP, \fBremote\-class6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands delete a DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 client class by name. If any client
classes in the database depend on the deleted class, an error is returned in
response to this command. In this case, to successfully delete the class,
the dependent client classes must be deleted first. Use the
\fBremote\-class4\-get\-all\fP command to fetch all client classes and find
the dependent ones.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used for this command because client
classes are uniquely identified by name.
.SS The \fBremote\-class4\-get\fP, \fBremote\-class6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
These commands retrieve DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 client class information by a
client\-class name.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter cannot be used for this command because client
classes are uniquely identified by name.
.sp
A response to the command looks similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 client class \(aqfoo\(aq found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-class4\-get\-all\fP, \fBremote\-class6\-get\-all\fP Commands
.sp
These commands retrieve all DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 client classes for a particular server,
multiple explicitly listed servers, and/or all servers. A given server has its own
server\-specific tag and also has the \(dqall\(dq server tag; these commands retrieve
the classes for both an individual server and for \(dqall\(dq servers. For example, the
following command retrieves all client classes defined for \(dqserver1\(dq as well as
the client classes defined for \(dqall\(dq servers:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter is mandatory and contains one or more server
tags. If other server tags are specified, \(dqall\(dq does not need to be included
in \fBserver\-tags\fP, as every server automatically also has the \(dqall\(dq server tag.
If \fBserver\-tags\fP contains only the keyword \(dqall\(dq, only the client classes associated
with \(dqall\(dq servers are returned. When the \fBserver\-tags\fP list contains the
\fBnull\fP value, the returned response contains a list of unassigned client
classes, i.e. the networks which are associated with no servers.
.sp
A response to the command looks similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 DHCPv4 client class(es) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq,
                \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqfoo\(aq)\(dq,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBremote\-class4\-set\fP, \fBremote\-class6\-set\fP Commands
.sp
These commands insert a new or replace an existing DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 client class in
the database. The client class information structure is the same as in the Kea
configuration file (see Client Classification in DHCPv4 \%<#\:dhcp4-client-classifier> and
Client Classification in DHCPv6 \%<#\:dhcp6-client-classifier> for details).
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqKNOWN\(aq) or member(\(aqbar\(aq)\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-def\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqconfigfile\(dq,
                        \(dqcode\(dq: 224,
                        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq
                    }
                ],
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqconfigfile\(dq,
                        \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq1APC\(dq
                    }
                ]
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Client\-class ordering rules described in Using Expressions in Classification \%<#\:classification-using-expressions>
apply to the classes inserted into the database. They imply that the class \fIbar\fP
referenced in the test expression must exist in the database when issuing the
above command.
.sp
By default, a new client class is inserted at the end of the class hierarchy in
the database and can reference any class associated with the same server tag or
with the special server tag \(dqall\(dq. If an existing class is updated, it remains
at its current position within the class hierarchy.
.sp
However, the class commands allow the position of the inserted
or updated client class to be specified. The optional \fBfollow\-class\-name\fP parameter can be
included in the command to indicate the name of the existing class after which
the managed class should be placed. Suppose there are two DHCPv6 classes in the
database: \fIfirst\-class\fP and \fIsecond\-class\fP\&. To add a new class, \fIthird\-class\fP,
between these two, use a command similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class6\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqthird\-class\(dq,
                \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqfirst\-class\(aq)\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqfollow\-class\-name\(dq: \(dqfirst\-class\(dq,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that \fIthird\-class\fP can depend on \fIfirst\-class\fP because it is placed
after \fIfirst\-class\fP; \fIthird\-class\fP cannot depend on \fIsecond\-class\fP
because it is placed before it. However, \fIsecond\-class\fP could be updated to
depend on \fIthird\-class\fP\&.
.sp
The \fBfollow\-class\-name\fP parameter can be explicitly set to \fBnull\fP, e.g.:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class6\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqthird\-class\(dq,
                \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqfirst\-class\(aq)\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqfollow\-class\-name\(dq: null,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ \(dqall\(dq ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It yields the same behavior as if the \fBfollow\-class\-name\fP parameter were not included,
i.e. the new class is appended at the end of the class hierarchy, and the updated
class remains at the current position.
.SS \fBlibdhcp_class_cmds.so\fP: Class Commands
.sp
This hook library exposes
several control commands for manipulating client classes (part of the
Kea DHCP servers\(aq configurations) without the need to restart those
servers. Using these commands it is possible to add, update, delete, and
list the client classes configured for a given server.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_class_cmds.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBclass\-add\fP Command
.sp
The \fBclass\-add\fP command adds a new client class to the DHCP server
configuration. This class is appended at the end of the list of classes
used by the server and may depend on any of the already\-configured
client classes.
.sp
The following example demonstrates how to add a new client class to the
DHCPv4 server configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqclass\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqipxe_efi_x64\(dq,
                \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqoption[93].hex == 0x0009\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.254\(dq,
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqhal9000\(dq,
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that the \fBclient\-classes\fP parameter is a JSON list, but it allows
only a single client class to be present.
.sp
Here is the response to the \fBclass\-add\fP command in our example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqClass \(aqipxe_efi_x64\(aq added.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBclass\-update\fP Command
.sp
The \fBclass\-update\fP command updates an existing client class in the
DHCP server configuration. If the client class with the given name
does not exist, the server returns the result code of 3, which means that
the server configuration is not modified and the client class does not
exist. The \fBclass\-add\fP command must be used instead to create the new
client class.
.sp
The \fBclass\-update\fP command has the same argument structure as the
\fBclass\-add\fP command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqclass\-update\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqipxe_efi_x64\(dq,
                \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqoption[93].hex == 0x0017\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqxfce\(dq,
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is the response for our example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqClass \(aqipxe_efi_x64\(aq updated.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Any parameter of the client class can be modified with this command,
except \fBname\fP\&. There is currently no way to rename the class, because
the class name is used as a key for searching the class to be updated.
To achieve a similar effect to renaming the class, an existing class can
be removed with the \fBclass\-del\fP command and then added again with a
different name using \fBclass\-add\fP\&. Note, however, that the class with
the new name will be added at the end of the list of configured classes.
.sp
As with other update commands, this command overwrites all the contents of an
entry. If the client class previously had a resource assigned to it, and the
\fBclass\-update\fP command is missing the resource, it is deleted from the server
configuration. If an incremental update of the class is desired, it can
be achieved by issuing a \fBclass\-get\fP to get the current state
of the client class, selecting the client class from the response, modifying it
to the required outcome, and then issuing the \fBclient\-update\fP command with the
resulting client class attached.
.SS The \fBclass\-del\fP Command
.sp
The \fBclass\-del\fP command is used to remove a particular class from the server
configuration. The class to be removed is identified by name. The class
is not removed if there are other classes depending on it; to remove
such a class, the dependent classes must be removed first.
.sp
The following is a sample command removing the \fBipxe_efi_x64\fP class:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqclass\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqipxe_efi_x64\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is the response to the \fBclass\-del\fP command in our example, when
the specified client class has been found:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqClass \(aqipxe_efi_x64\(aq deleted.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the class does not exist, the result of 3 is returned.
.SS The \fBclass\-list\fP Command
.sp
\fBclass\-list\fP is used to retrieve a list of all client classes. This
command includes no arguments:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqclass\-list\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is the response of the server in our example, including the list of
client classes:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 classes found\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqipxe_efi_x64\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpxeclient\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that the returned list does not contain full class definitions, but
merely class names. To retrieve full class information, the
\fBclass\-get\fP command should be used.
.SS The \fBclass\-get\fP Command
.sp
\fBclass\-get\fP is used to retrieve detailed information about a specified
class. The command structure is very simple:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqclass\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpxeclient\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the class with the specified name does not exist, the status code of
3 is returned. If the specified client class exists, the class details
are returned in the following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqClass \(aqpxeclient\(aq definition returned\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpxeclient\(dq,
                \(dqonly\-in\-additional\-list\(dq: true,
                \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqoption[vendor\-class\-identifier].text == \(aqPXEClient\(aq\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-def\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqconfigfile\(dq,
                        \(dqcode\(dq: 209,
                        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqstring\(dq
                    }
                ],
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqxfce\(dq,
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that the example above is DHCPv4\-specific; the last three
parameters are only returned by the DHCPv4 server and are never returned
by the DHCPv6 server. Also, some of the parameters provided in this
example may not be returned if they are not specified for the class.
Specifically, \fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP, \fBtest\fP, and \fBoption\-def\fP are not
returned if they are not specified for the class.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea version 2.7.4, \fBonly\-if\-required\fP has been replaced with
\fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP and deprecated. In order to allow users time
to migrate class commands will still accept it as input but translate it
to \fBonly\-in\-additional\-list\fP on output.  Eventually support for the
old name will be removed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\fP: DDNS Tuning
.sp
This hook library adds support for fine\-tuning various DNS update aspects.
It currently supports procedural host\-name generation and the ability to skip
performing DDNS updates for select clients.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The library, which was added in Kea 2.1.5, can be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>
and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> daemons by adding it to the \fBhooks\-libraries\fP element of the
server\(aqs configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                ...
            }
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Procedural Host\-Name Generation
.sp
This hook library provides the ability to generate host names procedurally, based on
an expression. The expression can be defined globally in the hook parameters, using
\fIhostname\-expr\fP\&. If defined globally, it applies to all hosts in all subnets. The
expressions can use all tokens defined in Client Classification \%<#\:classify>\&. An example of a global
expression is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqhostname\-expr\(dq: \(dq\(aqhost\-\(aq+hexstring(pkt4.mac,\(aq\-\(aq)\(dq,
                ...
            }
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The default behavior of \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> is to prefer the FQDN option (code 81) over the
host name option (code 12) when a client sends both.   The following example shows
the \fBhostname\-expr\fP one would use to reverse this rule:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqhostname\-expr\(dq : \(dqifelse(option[12].exists, option[host\-name].text, option[81].text)\(dq
            }
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is also possible to define this parameter in a subnet, using the user\-context mechanism.
If defined at the subnet level, the expression applies to a specific subnet only. If the
subnet expression is defined as empty, \fB\(dq\(dq\fP, it suppresses (or disables) the use of a
global expression for that subnet. An example subnet expression is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
 \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
     \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
     \(dqpools\(dq: [{
         \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10 \- 192.0.2.20\(dq
     } ],

     \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
         \(dqddns\-tuning\(dq: {
             \(dqhostname\-expr\(dq: \(dq\(aqguest\-\(aq+int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 0,1))+\(aq\-\(aq \e
                                       +int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 1,2))+\(aq\-\(aq \e
                                       +int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 2,3))+\(aq\-\(aq \e
                                       +int8totext(substring(pkt4.yiaddr, 3,4))\(dq
         }
     }
 }],
 ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The expression value above uses a backslash, \fB\e\fP, to show line continuation. This is for
clarity only and is not valid JSON supported by Kea parsing. The actual value must
be expressed on a single line.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Privacy should be taken into consideration when generating a host name. The host name
is usually inserted into the DNS, which is a public system. Exposing identifiers that
can be used to track devices, such as a MAC address, are usually a very bad idea.
The global expression example here used a MAC address for simplicity.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DHCPv4 Host\-Name Generation
.sp
With this library installed, the behavior for \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> when forming host names in
response to a client query (e.g. DISCOVER, REQUEST) is as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
1. If a host name is supplied via a host reservation, use it with the DDNS
behavioral parameters to form the final host name. Go to step 4.
.sp
2. If the client supplied an FQDN option (option 81), use the domain name value
specified within it, with the DDNS behavioral parameters, to form the final
host name. Go to step 4.
.sp
3. If the client supplied a host\-name option (option 12), use the host name specified
within it, with the DDNS behavioral parameters, to form the final host name.
.sp
4. If there is a \fBddns\-tuning\fP in\-scope host\-name expression (either global or subnet),
calculate the host name using the expression. If the calculated value is not a fully
qualified name and there is an in\-scope \fBddns\-qualifying\-suffix\fP, append the suffix.
.sp
5. If the value calculated by the hook is not an empty string and is different than
the host name formed in steps 1 or 2, the calculated value becomes the
final host name.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DHCPv6 Host\-Name Generation
.sp
With this library installed, the behavior for \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> when forming host names in
response to a client query (e.g. SOLICIT, REQUEST, RENEW, REBIND) is as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
1. If the client supplied an FQDN option (option 39), use the domain name value
specified within it, with the DDNS behavioral parameters, to form the final
host name. Go to step 4.
.sp
2. If the client did not supply an FQDN but \fBddns\-replace\-client\-name\fP is either
\fBalways\fP or \fBwhen\-not\-present\fP, then calculate the final form of the host
name and use it to create an outbound FQDN. Go to step 4.
.sp
3. If there is no outbound FQDN at this point, client\-name processing for this
packet stops. Without an outbound FQDN there is no way to communicate a host
name to the client.
.sp
4. If a host name is supplied via a host reservation, use it along with the DDNS
behavioral parameters to form the final host name; it supersedes the FQDN value
calculated in steps 1 or 2.
.sp
5. If there is a \fBddns\-tuning\fP in\-scope host name expression (either global or subnet),
calculate the host name using the expression. If the calculated value is not a fully
qualified name and there is an in\-scope \fBddns\-qualifying\-suffix\fP, append the suffix.
.sp
6. If the value calculated by the hook is not an empty string and is different than
the host name formed in steps 1 or 2, the calculated value becomes the
final host name.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Skipping DDNS Updates
.sp
\fBlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\fP also provides the ability to skip DDNS updates on a
per\-client basis. The library recognizes a special client class, \(dqSKIP_DDNS\(dq; when a
client is matched to this class, the Kea servers (\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>) do not
send DDNS update requests (NCRs) to \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>\&. A common use case would be
to skip DDNS updates for fixed\-address host reservations. This is done easily by
simply assigning the class to the host reservation as shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqreservations\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq,
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqSKIP_DDNS\(dq, \(dqfoo\(dq, \(dqbar\(dq ]
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\fP hook library notes the
presence of the \fB\(dqSKIP_DDNS\(dq\fP class in the
client\(aqs class list each time the client requests, renews, or releases its lease,
and instructs \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> to bypass sending DDNS updates. A similar workflow is
supported for \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqreservations\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::1\(dq,
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqSKIP_DDNS\(dq, \(dqfoo\(dq, \(dqbar\(dq ]
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Although \(dqSKIP_DDNS\(dq is a special class, it can be defined with a test
expression. Defining it as shown below would omit DDNS updates for all KNOWN
clients:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq:[
    {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqSKIP_DDNS\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqKNOWN\(aq)\(dq
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBlibdhcp_ddns_tuning.so\fP hook library must be
loaded for the \fB\(dqSKIP_DDNS\(dq\fP class to have an effect.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP: Flexible Identifier for Host Reservations
.sp
The Kea software provides a way to handle
host reservations that include addresses, prefixes, options, client
classes, and other features. The reservation can be based on hardware
address, DUID, circuit\-id, or client\-id in DHCPv4 and on hardware
address or DUID in DHCPv6. However, there are sometimes scenarios where
the reservation is more complex; it may use options other than those mentioned
above, use parts of specific options, or perhaps even use a combination of
several options and fields to uniquely identify a client. Those
scenarios are addressed by the Flexible Identifiers hook application.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP allows the definition of an expression, using notation initially
used only for client classification. (See
Using Expressions in Classification \%<#\:classification-using-expressions> for a detailed description of
the syntax available.) One notable difference is that for client
classification, the expression currently has to evaluate to either \fBtrue\fP
or \fBfalse\fP, while the flexible identifier expression is expected to
evaluate to a string that will be used as an identifier. It is a valid case
for the expression to evaluate to an empty string (e.g. in cases where a
client does not send specific options). This expression is then
evaluated for each incoming packet, and this evaluation generates an
identifier that is used to identify the client. In particular, there may
be host reservations that are tied to specific values of the flexible
identifier.
.sp
The library can be loaded similarly to other hook libraries. It
supports the following parameters: \fBidentifier\-expression\fP, \fBreplace\-client\-id\fP,
and \fBignore\-iaid\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_flex_id.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqidentifier\-expression\(dq: \(dqexpression\(dq,
                \(dqreplace\-client\-id\(dq: false,
                \(dqignore\-iaid\(dq: false
            }
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The flexible identifier library supports both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6.
.sp
Let\(aqs consider a case of an IPv6 network that has an
independent interface for each of its connected customers. Customers are
able to plug in whatever device they want, so any type of identifier
(e.g. a client\-id) is unreliable. Therefore, the operator may decide to
use an option inserted by a relay agent to differentiate between
clients. In this particular deployment, the operator has verified that the
interface\-id is unique for each customer\-facing interface, so it
is suitable for usage as a reservation. However, only the first six bytes of
the interface\-id are interesting, because the remaining bytes are either
randomly changed or not unique between devices. Therefore, the customer
decides to use the first six bytes of the interface\-id option inserted by the
relay agent. After adding \fBflex\-id\fP, the \fBhost\-reservation\-identifiers\fP goal
can be achieved by using the following configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [{
    # subnet definition starts here
    \(dqreservations\(dq: [{
        \(dqflex\-id\(dq: \(dq\(aqport1234\(aq\(dq,
        # value of the first 8 bytes of the interface\-id
        \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8::1\(dq ]
    },
    ...
    ],
    ...
    },
    ...
    ],
    # end of subnet definitions
    \(dqhost\-reservation\-identifiers\(dq: [\(dqduid\(dq, \(dqflex\-id\(dq],
    # add \(dqflex\-id\(dq to reservation identifiers
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_flex_id.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqidentifier\-expression\(dq: \(dqsubstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex,0,8)\(dq
            }
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Care should be taken when adjusting the expression. If the expression
changes, then all the \fBflex\-id\fP values may change, possibly rendering
all reservations based on \fBflex\-id\fP unusable until they are manually updated.
It is strongly recommended that administrators start with the expression and a
handful of reservations, and then adjust the expression as needed. Once
the desired result is obtained with the expression, host reservations
can be deployed on a broader scale.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBflex\-id\fP values in host reservations can be specified in two ways. First,
they can be expressed as a hex string, e.g. the string \(dqbar\(dq can be represented
as 626174. Alternatively, it can be expressed as a quoted value (using
double and single quotes), e.g. \(dq\(aqbar\(aq\(dq. The former is more convenient
for printable characters, while hex string values are more convenient
for non\-printable characters and do not require the use of the
\fBhexstring\fP operator.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [{
    # subnet definition starts here
    \(dqreservations\(dq: [{
        \(dqflex\-id\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        # value of the first 8 bytes of the interface\-id
        \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8::1\(dq ]
    },
    ...
    ],
    ...
    },
    ...
    ],
    # end of subnet definitions
    \(dqhost\-reservation\-identifiers\(dq: [\(dqduid\(dq, \(dqflex\-id\(dq],
    # add \(dqflex\-id\(dq to reservation identifiers
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_flex_id.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqidentifier\-expression\(dq: \(dqvendor[4491].option[1026].hex\(dq
            }
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
One less common scenario where the examples above may prove useful is for
DHCPv6 clients that change their DUIDs between exchanges. Certain PXE
clients are known to behave this way.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBreplace\-client\-id\fP Flag
.sp
When \fBreplace\-client\-id\fP is set to \fBfalse\fP (which is the default setting),
\fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP uses the evaluated flexible identifier solely for
identifying host reservations, i.e. searching for reservations within a
database. This is the functional equivalent of other identifiers, similar
to hardware address or circuit\-id. However, this mode of operation
implies that if a client device is replaced, it may cause a
conflict between an existing lease (allocated to the old device) and the
new lease being allocated to the new device. The conflict arises
because the same flexible identifier is computed for the replaced device,
so the server will try to allocate the same lease. The mismatch between
client identifiers sent by the new device and the old device causes the server
to refuse this new allocation until the old lease expires. A
manifestation of this problem is dependent on the specific expression used
as the flexible identifier, and is likely to appear if only options
and other parameters are used that identify where the device is connected
(e.g. circuit\-id), rather than the device identification itself (e.g.
MAC address).
.sp
\fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP offers a way to overcome the problem with lease
conflicts by dynamically replacing the client identifier (or DUID in DHCPv6)
with a value derived from the flexible identifier. The server
processes the client\(aqs query as if the flexible identifier were sent in the
client identifier (or DUID) option. This guarantees that a returning
client (for which the same flexible identifier is evaluated) will be
assigned the same lease, despite the client identifier and/or MAC address
change.
.sp
The following is a stub configuration that enables this behavior:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_flex_id.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqidentifier\-expression\(dq: \(dqexpression\(dq,
                \(dqreplace\-client\-id\(dq: true
            }
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the DHCPv4 case, the value derived from the flexible identifier is
formed by prepending one byte with a value of zero to the flexible identifier.
In the DHCPv6 case, it is formed by prepending two zero bytes before the
flexible identifier.
.sp
Note that for this mechanism to take effect, the DHCPv4 server must be
configured to respect the client identifier option value during lease
allocation, i.e. \fBmatch\-client\-id\fP must be set to \fBtrue\fP\&. See
Using Client Identifier and Hardware Address \%<#\:dhcp4-match-client-id> for details. No additional settings are
required for DHCPv6.
.sp
If the \fBreplace\-client\-id\fP option is set to \fBtrue\fP, the value of the
\fBecho\-client\-id\fP parameter (which governs whether to send back a
client\-id option) is ignored.
.sp
The libdhcp_lease_cmds.so: Lease Commands for Easier Lease Management section describes commands used to retrieve,
update, and delete leases using various identifiers, such as \fBhw\-address\fP and
\fBclient\-id\fP\&. \fBlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\fP does not natively support querying
for leases by flexible identifier. However, when \fBreplace\-client\-id\fP is
set to \fBtrue\fP, it makes it possible to query for leases using a value
derived from the flexible identifier. In DHCPv4, the query
looks similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqclient\-id\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq00:54:64:45:66\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where the hexadecimal value of \(dq54:64:45:66\(dq is a flexible identifier
computed for the client.
.sp
In DHCPv6, the corresponding query looks something like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqduid\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq00:00:54:64:45:66\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 10
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBignore\-iaid\fP Flag
.sp
When \fBignore\-iaid\fP is set to \fBtrue\fP (the default value is \fBfalse\fP),
\fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP causes the Kea DHCPv6 server to ignore the IAID value
from incoming IPv6 packets. This parameter is ignored by the Kea DHCPv4 server.
.sp
If the packet contains only one IA_NA, the IAID value will be changed to \fB0\fP
and stored as such in the lease storage. Similarly, if the packet contains only
one IA_PD, the IAID value will be changed to \fB0\fP and stored as such in the
lease storage. The IAID is restored to its initial value in the response back
to the client. The change is visible in the identifier expression if the IAID is
part of the expression.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
To avoid lease conflicts, if the incoming packet contains more than one
IA_NA, the IAID value is not changed on any of the IA_NAs. Similarly,
if the incoming packet contains more than one IA_PD, the IAID value is not
changed on any of the IA_PDs.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This functionality breaks RFC compliance and should be enabled only if
required. When enabled, a warning message is issued at configure time.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBignore\-iaid\fP parameter operates independently of the flexible identifier
feature and may be used without specifying a value for \fBidentifier\-expression\fP\&.
When \fBidentifier\-expression\fP is omitted or specified as an empty string, \fI\(dq\(dq\fP,
the flexible identifier feature is disabled. Kea versions prior to 3.1.2 require
a value for \fBidentifier\-expression\fP but accept the empty string value. As of
Kea 3.1.2, the parameter is optional.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_flex_option.so\fP: Flexible Option Actions for Option Value Settings
.sp
This library allows administrators to define an action to take, for a given
option, based upon on the result of an expression. These actions are carried
out during the final stages of constructing a query response packet, just
before it is sent to the client. The three actions currently supported are
\fBadd\fP, \fBsupersede\fP, and \fBremove\fP\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_flex_option.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The syntax used for the action expressions is the same syntax used
for client classification and the Flexible Identifier hook library;
see either Using Expressions in Classification \%<#\:classification-using-expressions> or libdhcp_flex_id.so: Flexible Identifier for Host Reservations
for a detailed description of the syntax.
.sp
The \fBadd\fP and \fBsupersede\fP actions use an expression returning a
string, and do nothing if the string is empty. The
\fBremove\fP application uses an expression returning \fBtrue\fP or \fBfalse\fP,
and does nothing on \fBfalse\fP\&. When it is necessary to set an option to the
empty value this mechanism does not work, but a client class can be
used instead.
.sp
The \fBadd\fP action adds an option only when the option does not already
exist and the expression does not evaluate to the empty string.
The \fBsupersede\fP action is similar, but it overwrites the option value
if it already exists. The \fBremove\fP action removes the option from
the response packet if it already exists and the expression evaluates to
true.
.sp
The option to which an action applies may be specified by either its
numeric code or its name; either the code or the name must be
specified. The option space is DHCPv4 or DHCPv6, depending
on the server where the hook library is loaded.
.sp
\fBlibdhcp_flex_option.so\fP can be loaded
by either the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
process. It takes a mandatory \fBoptions\fP parameter with a list of
per\-option parameter maps, with \fBcode\fP, \fBname\fP, \fBadd\fP, \fBsupersede\fP, and
\fBremove\fP actions. Action entries take a string value representing an
expression.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
   \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
       \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
           {
               \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_flex_option.so\(dq,
               \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                   \(dqoptions\(dq: [
                       {
                           \(dqcode\(dq: 67,
                           \(dqadd\(dq: \(dqifelse(option[host\-name].exists,concat(option[host\-name].text,\(aq.boot\(aq),\(aq\(aq)\(dq
                       }
                   ]
               }
           }
       ]
   }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If (and only if) the \fBquery\fP includes a \fBhost\-name\fP option (code 12), a
\fBboot\-file\-name\fP option (code 67) is added to the response with the host name
followed by \fB\&.boot\fP for content.
.sp
A commonly discussed use case is modifying the DHCPv4 subnet mask option
(code 1). The following example demonstrates that capability, as all ingress
packets identified by the gateway address 192.168.0.1 are met with a /32 subnet
mask in the response:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_flex_option.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqoptions\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqcode\(dq: 1,
                            \(dqsupersede\(dq: \(dqifelse(pkt4.giaddr==192.168.0.1, \(aq255.255.255.255\(aq, \(aq\(aq)\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Flexible Option library supports both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6.
.sp
The \fBadd\fP and \fBsupersede\fP actions take an optional
\fBcsv\-format\fP boolean parameter. If not specified or set to \fBfalse\fP, the
option data is set using the raw value of the evaluated expression. When it is
configured to \fBtrue\fP, this value is parsed using the option definition from
the option data specified in the configuration file. This eases option setting
for options using complex record formats or fully qualified domain names.
.sp
For instance, if the expression evaluation returns \(dqexample.com\(dq and
the option is defined with the \fBfqdn\fP type, the domain name is
encoded into DNS binary format.
.sp
Since Kea 2.1.4, the \fBclient\-class\fP parameter specifies a class guard,
and takes a client\-class name. If not empty, the client\(aqs packet needs to
belong to tne specified class for this entry to be used.
.sp
It is also possible to have multiple entries for the same option,
but each entry must have exactly one action. If the option is not defined
in \fBdhcp4\fP for DHCPv4 or \fBdhcp6\fP for DHCPv6, the location of
the option definition can be specified using its name, with the new
\fBspace\fP parameter.
.sp
Since Kea 2.1.4, sub\-options are supported with a new entry, \fBsub\-options\fP,
which replaces the action in the configuration of the container option,
i.e. the option where sub\-options are located.
.sp
The \fBsub\-options\fP parameter takes a list of sub\-option entries, each
containing:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcode\fP \- specifies the sub\-option code; either the \fBcode\fP or \fBname\fP
must be specified. If both are given, they must match or the configuration
is rejected at load time.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP \- specifies the sub\-option name; either the \fBcode\fP or \fBname\fP
must be specified. If both are given, they must match or the configuration
is rejected at load time.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBspace\fP \- specifies the space where the sub\-option can be defined. This
parameter is optional, because it can be found in the container option
definition. The configuration is rejected if no valid space name is
available at load time. Note that vendor spaces are supported for the
DHCPv4 \fBvivso\-suboptions\fP and for the DHCPv6 \fBvendor\-opts\fP, both
pre\-defined (e.g. DoCSIS vendor id 4491) or custom.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBadd\fP \- (action) adds a sub\-option only if it does not already exist
and the expression does not evaluate to the empty string.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsupersede\fP \- (action) adds or overwrites a sub\-option if the expression
does not evaluate to the empty string.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremove\fP \- (action) removes a sub\-option if it already exists and the
expression evaluates to true.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontainer\-add\fP \- a boolean value that specifies whether the container option
should be created, if it does not exist in the \fBadd\fP and \fBsupersede\fP
action. If not specified, it defaults to \fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontainer\-remove\fP \- a boolean value that specifies whether the container option
should be deleted, if it remains empty after the removal of a sub\-option by
the \fBremove\fP action. If not specified, it defaults to \fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcsv\-format\fP \- a boolean value that specifies whether the raw value of the
evaluated expression is used (false, default) or parsed using the sub\-option
definition (true).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-class\fP \- specifies whether the sub\-option entry must be skipped when
the \fBquery\fP does not belong to the specified client class. Note that the similar
parameter in the container option entry applies to the whole \fBsub\-options\fP
list.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For instance, this configuration adds a string sub\-option in the DHCPv4
\fBvendor\-encapsulated\-options\fP (code 43) option. This option
encapsulates the \fBvendor\-encapsulated\-options\fP space.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
   \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
       \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
           {
               \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_flex_option.so\(dq,
               \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                   \(dqoptions\(dq: [
                       {
                           \(dqcode\(dq: 43,
                           \(dqsub\-options\(dq: [
                               {
                                  \(dqcode\(dq: 1,
                                  \(dqadd\(dq: \(dq\(aqfoobar\(aq\(dq
                               }
                           ]
                       }
                   ]
               }
           }
       ]
   }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP: Sign DNS Updates With GSS\-TSIG
.sp
This hook library allows the \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> server to use
GSS\-TSIG to sign DNS updates. For a full discussion of GSS\-TSIG in Kea,
please see GSS\-TSIG \%<#\:gss-tsig>\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP: High Availability Outage Resilience for Kea Servers
.sp
This hook library can be loaded on a pair of DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 servers, to
increase the reliability of the DHCP service in the event of an outage on one
server.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
High Availability (HA) of the DHCP service is provided by running multiple
cooperating server instances. If any of these instances becomes unavailable for
any reason (DHCP software crash, power outage,
hardware failure), a surviving server instance can continue providing reliable
service to clients. Many DHCP server implementations include the \(dqDHCP Failover\(dq
protocol, whose most significant features are communication between the servers,
partner failure detection, and lease synchronization between the servers.
However, the DHCPv4 failover standardization process was never completed by the
IETF. The DHCPv6 failover standard (RFC 8156) was published, but it is complex
and difficult to use, has significant operational constraints, and is different
from its v4 counterpart. Although it may be useful to use a \(dqstandard\(dq failover
protocol, most Kea users are simply interested in a working solution which
guarantees high availability of the DHCP service. Therefore, the Kea HA hook
library derives major concepts from the DHCP failover protocol but uses its own
solutions for communication and configuration. It offers its own state machine,
which greatly simplifies its implementation and generally fits better into Kea,
and it provides the same features in both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6. This document
intentionally uses the term \(dqhigh availability\(dq rather than \(dqfailover\(dq to
emphasize that it is not the failover protocol implementation.
.sp
The following sections describe the configuration and operation of the Kea HA
hook library.
.SS Supported Configurations
.sp
The Kea HA hook library supports three configurations, also known as HA modes:
\fBload\-balancing\fP, \fBhot\-standby\fP, and \fBpassive\-backup\fP\&. In the
\fBload\-balancing\fP mode, two servers respond to DHCP requests. The
\fBload\-balancing\fP function is implemented as described in RFC
3074 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3074>, with each server responding to
half the received DHCP queries. When one of the servers allocates a lease for a
client, it notifies the partner server over the control channel (via the RESTful
API), so the partner can save the lease information in its own database. If the
communication with the partner is unsuccessful, the DHCP query is dropped and
the response is not returned to the DHCP client. If the lease update is
successful, the response is returned to the DHCP client by the server which has
allocated the lease. By exchanging lease updates, both servers get a copy of all
leases allocated by the entire HA setup, and either server can be switched to
handle the entire DHCP traffic if its partner becomes unavailable.
.sp
In the \fBload\-balancing\fP configuration, one of the servers must be designated
as \fBprimary\fP and the other as \fBsecondary\fP\&. Functionally, there is no
difference between the two during normal operation. However, this distinction is
required when the two servers are started at (nearly) the same time and have to
synchronize their lease databases. The primary server synchronizes the database
first. The secondary server waits for the primary server to complete the lease
database synchronization before it starts the synchronization.
.sp
In the \fBhot\-standby\fP configuration, one of the servers is designated as
\fBprimary\fP and the other as \fBstandby\fP\&. During normal operation, the primary
server is the only one that responds to DHCP requests. The standby server
receives lease updates from the primary over the control channel; however, it
does not respond to any DHCP queries as long as the primary is running or, more
accurately, until the standby considers the primary to be offline. If the
standby server detects the failure of the primary, it starts responding to all
DHCP queries.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Operators often wonder whether to use \fBload\-balancing\fP or \fBhot\-standby\fP
mode. The \fBload\-balancing\fP mode has the benefit of splitting the DHCP load
between two instances, reducing the traffic processed by each of them.
However, it is not always clear to the operators that using the
\fBload\-balancing\fP mode requires manually splitting the address pools between
two Kea instances using client classification, to preclude both servers from
allocating the same address to different clients.
Such a split is not needed in the \fBhot\-standby\fP mode. Thus, the benefit
of using \fBhot\-standby\fP over \fBload\-balancing\fP is that the former has a
simpler configuration. Conversely, \fBload\-balancing\fP has higher performance
potential at the cost of more complex configuration.
See Load\-Balancing Configuration for details on how to split the pools
using client classification.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the configurations described above, both the primary and secondary/standby
are referred to as \fBactive\fP servers, because they receive lease updates and
can automatically react to the partner\(aqs failures by responding to the DHCP
queries which would normally be handled by the partner. The HA hook library
supports another server type/role: \fBbackup\fP\&. The use of a backup server is
optional, and can be implemented in both \fBload\-balancing\fP and \fBhot\-standby\fP
setup, in addition to the active servers. There is no limit on the number of
backup servers in the HA setup; however, the presence of backup servers may
increase the latency of DHCP responses, because not only do active servers send
lease updates to each other, but also to the backup servers. The active servers
do not expect acknowledgments from the backup servers before responding to the
DHCP clients, so the overhead of sending lease updates to the backup servers is
minimized.
.sp
In the last supported configuration, \fBpassive\-backup\fP, there is only one
active server and typically one or more backup servers. A \fBpassive\-backup\fP
configuration with no backup servers is also accepted, but it is no different
than running a single server with no HA function at all.
.sp
The \fBpassive\-backup\fP configuration is used in situations when an administrator
wants to take advantage of the backup server(s) as an additional storage for
leases without running the full\-blown failover setup. In this case, if the
primary server fails, the DHCP service is lost; it requires the administrator to
manually restart the primary to resume DHCP service. The administrator may also
configure one of the backup servers to provide DHCP service to the clients, as
these servers should have accurate or nearly accurate information about the
allocated leases. The major advantage of the \fBpassive\-backup\fP mode is that it
provides some redundancy of the lease information but with better performance of
the primary server responding to the DHCP queries.
The primary server does not have to wait for acknowledgments to the lease
updates from the backup servers before it sends a response to the DHCP client.
This reduces the response time compared to the \fBload\-balancing\fP and
\fBhot\-standby\fP cases, in which the server responding to the DHCP query has to
wait for the acknowledgment from the other active server before it can respond
to the client.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
An interesting use case for a single active server running in the
\fBpassive\-backup\fP mode is a notification service, in which software
pretending to be a backup server receives live notifications about allocated
and deleted leases from the primary server and can display them on a
monitoring screen, trigger alerts, etc.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Clocks on Active Servers
.sp
Synchronized clocks are essential for the HA setup to operate reliably.
The servers share lease information \- via lease updates and during
synchronization of the databases \- including the time when the lease was
allocated and when it expires. Some clock skew between the servers participating
in the HA setup usually exists; this is acceptable as long as the clock skew is
relatively low, compared to the lease lifetimes. However, if the clock skew
becomes too high, the different lease expiration times on different servers may
cause the HA system to malfunction. For example, one server may consider a lease
to be expired when it is actually still valid. The lease reclamation process may
remove a name associated with this lease from the DNS, causing problems when the
client later attempts to renew the lease.
.sp
Each active server monitors the clock skew by comparing its current time with
the time returned by its partner in response to the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command. This
gives a good approximation of the clock skew, although it does not take into
account the time between the partner sending the response and the receipt of
this response by the server which sent the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command. If the clock skew
exceeds 30 seconds, a warning log message is issued. The administrator may
correct this problem by synchronizing the clocks (e.g. using NTP); the servers
should notice the clock skew correction and stop issuing the warning.
.sp
If the clock skew is not corrected and exceeds 60 seconds, the HA service on
each of the servers is terminated, i.e. the state machine enters the
\fBterminated\fP state. The servers will continue to respond to DHCP clients (as
in the \fBload\-balancing\fP or \fBhot\-standby\fP mode), but will exchange neither
lease updates nor heartbeats and their lease databases will diverge. In this
case, the administrator should synchronize the clocks and restart the servers.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is possible to restart the servers one at a time, in no particular order.
The clocks must be in sync before restarting the servers. A restarted server
remains in the \fBwaiting\fP state until the partner is also restarted. If the
partner is not restarted within 10 minutes, the restarted server transitions
back to the \fBterminated\fP state to continue serving the DHCP clients. Restart
both servers to resume the normal HA service.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The clock skew is only assessed between two active servers, and only the
active servers enter the \fBterminated\fP state if the skew is too high. The
clock skew between active and backup servers is not assessed, because active
servers do not exchange heartbeat messages with backup servers.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS HTTPS Support
.sp
Since Kea 1.9.7, the High Availability hook library supports HTTPS via TLS, as
described in TLS/HTTPS Support \%<#\:tls>\&.
.sp
The HTTPS configuration parameters are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtrust\-anchor\fP \- specifies the name of a file or directory where the
certification authority certificate of a Kea entity can be found.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcert\-file\fP \- specifies the name of the file containing the end\-entity
certificate to use.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkey\-file\fP \- specifies the private key of the end\-entity certificate to use.
.UNINDENT
.sp
These parameters can be configured at the global and peer levels. When
configured at both levels the peer value is used, allowing common values to be
shared.
.sp
The three parameters must be either all not specified (HTTPS disabled) or all
specified (HTTPS enabled). Specification of the empty string is considered not
specified; this can be used, for instance, to disable HTTPS for a particular
peer when it is enabled at the global level.
.sp
As the High Availability hook library is an HTTPS client, there is no
\fBcert\-required\fP parameter in this hook configuration.
This parameter can be set at the server side to require and verify a client
certificate in client\-server communication. It does not affect communication
between HA peers at the client side; see below for information on the server
side.
.sp
Since Kea 2.1.7 the HTTPS server side is supported:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
the peer entry for the server name is used for the TLS setting.
.IP \(bu 2
the new \fBrequire\-client\-certs\fP parameter specifies whether client
certificates are required and verified, i.e. like \fBcert\-required\fP\&. It
defaults to \fBtrue\fP and is an HA config (vs. peer config) parameter.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Kea 2.1.7 added a new security feature with the \fBrestrict\-commands\fP HA config
parameter: when set to \fBtrue\fP, commands which are not used by the hook are
rejected. The default is \fBtrue\fP since Kea 3.0.0.
.sp
The following is an example of an HA server pair for \fBhot\-standby\fP with TLS.
.sp
Server 1:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
    }, {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [{
                \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqtrust\-anchor\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/CA.pem\(dq,
                \(dqcert\-file\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/server1_cert.pem\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-file\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/server1_key.pem\(dq,
                \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqhot\-standby\(dq,
                \(dqheartbeat\-delay\(dq: 10000,
                \(dqmax\-response\-delay\(dq: 60000,
                \(dqmax\-ack\-delay\(dq: 5000,
                \(dqmax\-unacked\-clients\(dq: 5,
                \(dqpeers\(dq: [{
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqstandby\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }]
            }]
        }
    }],

    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [{
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.100 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq
            }]
    }],

    \(dqcontrol\-sockets\(dq: [{
        \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqhttps\(dq,
        \(dqsocket\-address\(dq: \(dq192.168.56.33\(dq,
        \(dqsocket\-port\(dq: 8000,
        \(dqtrust\-anchor\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/CA.pem\(dq,
        \(dqcert\-file\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/server1_cert.pem\(dq,
        \(dqkey\-file\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/server1_key.pem\(dq,
        \(dqcert\-required\(dq: true
     }]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Server 2:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
    }, {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [{
                \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                \(dqtrust\-anchor\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/CA.pem\(dq,
                \(dqcert\-file\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/server2_cert.pem\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-file\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/server2_key.pem\(dq,
                \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqhot\-standby\(dq,
                \(dqheartbeat\-delay\(dq: 10000,
                \(dqmax\-response\-delay\(dq: 60000,
                \(dqmax\-ack\-delay\(dq: 5000,
                \(dqmax\-unacked\-clients\(dq: 5,
                \(dqpeers\(dq: [{
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqstandby\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }]
            }]
        }
    }],

    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [{
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.100 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq
            }]
    }],

    \(dqcontrol\-sockets\(dq:[{
        \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqhttps\(dq,
        \(dqsocket\-address\(dq: \(dq192.168.56.66\(dq,
        \(dqsocket\-port\(dq: 8000,
        \(dqtrust\-anchor\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/CA.pem\(dq,
        \(dqcert\-file\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/server2_cert.pem\(dq,
        \(dqkey\-file\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/server2_key.pem\(dq,
        \(dqcert\-required\(dq: true
    }]

  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Server States
.sp
A DHCP server operating within an HA setup runs a state machine, and the state
of the server can be retrieved by its peers using the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command
sent over the RESTful API. If the partner server does not respond to the
\fBha\-heartbeat\fP command within the specified amount of time, the communication
is considered interrupted and the server may, depending on the configuration,
use additional measures (described later in this document) to verify that the
partner is still operating. If it finds that the partner is not operating, the
server transitions to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state to handle all the DHCP traffic
directed to the system.
.sp
In this case, the surviving server continues to send the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP
command to detect when the partner wakes up. At that time, the partner
synchronizes the lease database. When it is again ready to operate, the
surviving server returns to normal operation, i.e. the \fBload\-balancing\fP or
\fBhot\-standby\fP state.
.sp
The following is the list of all possible server states:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbackup\fP \- normal operation of the backup server. In this state it receives
lease updates from the active server(s).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP \- an active server running in \fBload\-balancing\fP
mode may transition to this state when it experiences communication issues
with a partner server over the control channel. This is an intermediate state
between the \fBload\-balancing\fP and \fBpartner\-down\fP states. In this state the
server continues to respond to DHCP queries but does not send lease updates
to the partner; lease updates are queued and are sent when normal
communication is resumed. If communication does not resume within the time
specified, the primary server then transitions to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state.
The \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state was introduced to ensure reliable DHCP
service when both active servers remain operational but the communication
between them is interrupted for a prolonged period of time. Either server can
be configured to never enter this state by setting the
\fBdelayed\-updates\-limit\fP to 0 (please refer to
Load\-Balancing Configuration, later in this chapter, for details on this
parameter). Disabling entry into the \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state causes
the server to begin testing for the \fBpartner\-down\fP state as soon as the
server is unable to communicate with its partner.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
In Kea 1.9.4, with the introduction of \fBdelayed\-updates\-limit\fP, the default
server\(aqs behavior in \fBload\-balancing\fP mode changed. When a server
experiences communication issues with its partner, it now enters the
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state and queues lease updates until communication
is resumed. Prior to Kea 1.9.4, a server that could not communicate with its
partner in \fBload\-balancing\fP mode would immediately begin the transition to
the \fBpartner\-down\fP state.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhot\-standby\fP \- normal operation of the active server running in the
\fBhot\-standby\fP mode; both the primary and the standby server are in this
state during their normal operation. The primary server responds to DHCP
queries and sends lease updates to the standby server and to any backup
servers that are present.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBload\-balancing\fP \- normal operation of the active server running in the
\fBload\-balancing\fP mode; both the primary and the secondary server are in
this state during their normal operation. Both servers respond to DHCP
queries and send lease updates to each other and to any backup servers that
are present.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBin\-maintenance\fP \- an active server transitions to this state as a result
of being notified by its partner that the administrator requested maintenance
of the HA setup. The administrator requests the maintenance by sending the
\fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command to the server which is supposed to take over
the responsibility for responding to the DHCP clients while the other server
is taken offline for maintenance. If the server is in the \fBin\-maintenance\fP
state it can be safely shut down. The partner transitions to the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state immediately after discovering that the server in
maintenance has been shut down.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpartner\-down\fP \- an active server transitions to this state after detecting
that its partner (another active server) is offline. The server does not
transition to this state if only a backup server is unavailable. In the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state the active server responds to all DHCP queries,
including those queries which are normally handled by the server that is now
unavailable.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP \- an active server transitions to this state
after receiving a \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command from the administrator.
The server in this state becomes responsible for responding to all DHCP
requests. The server sends a \fBha\-maintenance\-notify\fP command to the partner,
which should enter the \fBin\-maintenance\fP state. The server remaining in the
\fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state keeps sending lease updates to the partner
until it finds that the partner has stopped responding to those lease updates,
heartbeats, or any other commands. In this case, the server in the
\fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state transitions to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state
and keeps responding to the queries, but no longer sends lease updates.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpassive\-backup\fP \- a primary server running in the \fBpassive\-backup\fP HA
mode transitions to this state immediately after it boots up. The primary
server in this state responds to all DHCP traffic and sends lease updates to
the backup servers it is connected to. By default, the primary server does
not wait for acknowledgments from the backup servers and responds to a DHCP
query right after sending lease updates to all backup servers. If any of the
lease updates fail, a backup server misses the lease update but the DHCP
client is still provisioned. This default configuration can be changed by
setting the \fBwait\-backup\-ack\fP configuration parameter to \fBtrue\fP, in which
case the primary server always waits for the acknowledgements and drops the
DHCP query if sending any of the corresponding lease updates fails. This
improves lease database consistency between the primary and the secondary.
However, if a communication failure between the active server and any of the
backups occurs, it effectively causes the failure of the DHCP service from
the DHCP clients\(aq perspective.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBready\fP \- an active server transitions to this state after synchronizing
its lease database with an active partner. This state indicates to the
partner (which may be in the \fBpartner\-down\fP state) that it should return to
normal operation. If and when it does, the server in the \fBready\fP state also
starts normal operation.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsyncing\fP \- an active server transitions to this state to fetch leases from
the active partner and update the local lease database. When in this state,
the server issues the \fBdhcp\-disable\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-dhcp-disable> command to disable the DHCP service of
the partner from which the leases are fetched. The DHCP service is disabled
for a maximum time of 60 seconds, after which it is automatically re\-enabled,
in case the syncing partner was unable to re\-enable the service. If the
synchronization completes successfully, the synchronizing server issues the
\fBha\-sync\-complete\-notify\fP command to notify the partner. In most states,
the partner re\-enables its DHCP service to continue responding to the DHCP
queries. In the \fBpartner\-down\fP state, the partner first ensures that
communication between the servers is re\-established before enabling the DHCP
service. The syncing operation is synchronous; the server waits for an answer
from the partner and does nothing else while the lease synchronization takes
place. A server that is configured not to synchronize the lease database with
its partner, i.e. when the \fBsync\-leases\fP configuration parameter is set to
\fBfalse\fP, will never transition to this state. Instead, it transitions
directly from the \fBwaiting\fP state to the \fBready\fP state.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBterminated\fP \- an active server transitions to this state when the High
Availability hook library is unable to further provide reliable service and a
manual intervention of the administrator is required to correct the problem.
Various issues with the HA setup may cause the server to transition to this
state. While in this state, the server continues responding to DHCP clients
based on the HA mode selected (\fBload\-balancing\fP or \fBhot\-standby\fP), but
lease updates are not exchanged and heartbeats are not sent. Once a server
has entered the \fBterminated\fP state, it remains in this state until it is
restarted. The administrator must correct the issue which caused this
situation prior to restarting the server (e.g. synchronize the clocks);
otherwise, the server will return to the \fBterminated\fP state once it finds
that the issue persists.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBwaiting\fP \- each started server instance enters this state. A backup server
transitions directly from this state to the \fBbackup\fP state. An active
server sends a heartbeat to its partner to check its state; if the partner
appears to be unavailable, the server transitions to the \fBpartner\-down\fP
state. If the partner is available, the server transitions to the \fBsyncing\fP
or \fBready\fP state, depending on the setting of the \fBsync\-leases\fP
configuration parameter. If both servers appear to be in the \fBwaiting\fP
state (concurrent startup), the primary server transitions to the next state
first. The secondary or standby server remains in the \fBwaiting\fP state until
the primary transitions to the \fBready\fP state.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Currently, restarting the HA service from the \fBterminated\fP state requires
restarting the DHCP server or reloading its configuration.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Whether the server responds to DHCP queries and which queries it responds to is
a matter of the server\(aqs state, if no administrative action is performed to
configure the server otherwise. The following table provides the default
behavior for various states.
.sp
The \fBDHCP Service Scopes\fP denote which group of received DHCP queries the
server responds to in the given state. The HA configuration must specify a
unique name for each server within the HA setup. This document uses the
following convention within the provided examples: \(dqserver1\(dq for a primary
server, \(dqserver2\(dq for the secondary or standby server, and \(dqserver3\(dq for the
backup server. In real life any names can be used as long as they remain unique.
.sp
An in\-depth explanation of the scopes can be found below.
.SS Default behavior of the server in various HA states
.TS
box center;
