.TE
.sp
In the \fBload\-balancing\fP mode there are two scopes specified for the active
servers: \(dqHA_server1\(dq and \(dqHA_server2\(dq. The DHCP queries load\-balanced to
\fBserver1\fP belong to the \(dqHA_server1\(dq scope and the queries load\-balanced to
\fBserver2\fP belong to the \(dqHA_server2\(dq scope. If either server is in the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state, the active partner is responsible for serving both
scopes.
.sp
In the \fBhot\-standby\fP mode, there is only one scope \- \(dqHA_server1\(dq \- because
only \fBserver1\fP is responding to DHCP queries. If that server becomes
unavailable, \fBserver2\fP becomes responsible for this scope.
.sp
The backup servers do not have their own scopes. In some cases they can be used
to respond to queries belonging to the scopes of the active servers. Also, a
backup server which is neither in the \fBpartner\-down\fP state nor in normal
operation serves no scopes.
.sp
The scope names can be used to associate pools, subnets, and networks with
certain servers, so that only these servers can allocate addresses or prefixes
from those pools, subnets, or networks. This is done via the client
classification mechanism (see Load Balancing With Advanced Classification for more
details).
.SS Scope Transition in a Partner\-Down Case
.sp
When one of the servers finds that its partner is unavailable, it starts serving
clients from both its own scope and the scope of the unavailable partner. This
is straightforward for new clients, i.e. those sending DHCPDISCOVER (DHCPv4) or
Solicit (DHCPv6), because those requests are not sent to any particular server.
The available server responds to all such queries when it is in the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state.
.sp
When a client renews a lease, it sends its DHCPREQUEST (DHCPv4) or Renew (DHCPv6)
message directly to the server which has allocated the lease being renewed. If
this server is no longer available, the client will get no response. In that
case, the client continues to use its lease and attempts to renew until the
rebind timer (T2) elapses. The client then enters the rebinding phase, in which
it sends a DHCPREQUEST (DHCPv4) or Rebind (DHCPv6) message to any available
server. The surviving server receives the rebinding request and typically
extends the lifetime of the lease. The client then continues to contact that new
server to renew its lease as appropriate.
.sp
If and when the other server once again becomes available, both active servers
will eventually transition to the \fBload\-balancing\fP or \fBhot\-standby\fP state,
in which they will again be responsible for their own scopes. Some clients
belonging to the scope of the restarted server will try to renew their leases
via the surviving server, but this server will no longer respond to them; the
client will eventually transition back to the correct server via the rebinding
mechanism.
.SS Load\-Balancing Configuration
.sp
The following is the configuration snippet to enable high availability on the
primary server within the \fBload\-balancing\fP configuration. The same
configuration should be applied on the secondary and backup servers, with the
only difference that \fBthis\-server\-name\fP should be set to \(dqserver2\(dq and
\(dqserver3\(dq on those servers, respectively.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Remember that \fBload\-balancing\fP mode requires the address pools and
delegated prefix pools to be split between the active servers. During normal
operation, the servers use non\-overlapping pools to avoid allocating the same
lease to different clients by both instances. A server only uses the pool
fragments owned by the partner when the partner is not running. See the notes
in Supported Configurations highlighting differences between the
\fBload\-balancing\fP and \fBhot\-standby\fP modes. The semantics of pool
partitioning is explained further in this section.
The Load Balancing With Advanced Classification section provides advanced
pool\-partitioning examples.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
    }, {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [{
                \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                \(dqheartbeat\-delay\(dq: 10000,
                \(dqmax\-response\-delay\(dq: 60000,
                \(dqmax\-ack\-delay\(dq: 5000,
                \(dqmax\-unacked\-clients\(dq: 5,
                \(dqmax\-rejected\-lease\-updates\(dq: 10,
                \(dqdelayed\-updates\-limit\(dq: 100,
                \(dqpeers\(dq: [{
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqsecondary\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver3\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.99:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqbackup\(dq,
                    \(dqbasic\-auth\-user\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                    \(dqbasic\-auth\-password\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: false
                }]
            }]
        }
    }],

    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [{
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.100 \- 192.0.3.150\(dq,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqHA_server1\(dq ]
         }, {
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.200 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqHA_server2\(dq ]
         }],

         \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.1\(dq
         }],

         \(dqrelay\(dq: { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq10.1.2.3\(dq }
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Two hook libraries must be loaded to enable HA: \fBlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\fP and
\fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP\&. The latter implements the HA feature, while the former
enables control commands required by HA to fetch and manipulate leases on the
remote servers. In the example provided above, it is assumed that Kea libraries
are installed in the \fB/usr/lib\fP directory. If Kea is not installed in the
\fB/usr\fP directory, the hook libraries\(aq locations must be updated accordingly.
.sp
The HA configuration is specified within the scope of \fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP\&.
Note that while the top\-level parameter \fBhigh\-availability\fP is a list, only a
single entry is currently supported.
.sp
The following are the global parameters which control the server\(aqs behavior with
respect to HA:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthis\-server\-name\fP \- is a unique identifier of the server within this HA
setup. It must match one of the servers specified within the \fBpeers\fP list.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmode\fP \- specifies an HA mode of operation. The currently supported modes
are \fBload\-balancing\fP and \fBhot\-standby\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBheartbeat\-delay\fP \- specifies a duration in milliseconds between sending
the last heartbeat (or other command sent to the partner) and the next
heartbeat. Heartbeats are sent periodically to gather the status of the
partner and to verify whether the partner is still operating. The default
value of this parameter is 10000 ms.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-response\-delay\fP \- specifies a duration in milliseconds since the last
successful communication with the partner, after which the server assumes
that communication with the partner is interrupted. This duration should be
greater than the \fBheartbeat\-delay\fP; typically it should be a multiple of
\fBheartbeat\-delay\fP\&. When the server detects that communication is
interrupted, it may transition to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state (when
\fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP is 0) or trigger the failure\-detection procedure
using the values of the two parameters below. The default value of this
parameter is 60000 ms.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-ack\-delay\fP \- is one of the parameters controlling partner
failure\-detection. When communication with the partner is interrupted, the
server examines the values of the \(dqsecs\(dq field (DHCPv4) or \(dqelapsed time\(dq
option (DHCPv6), which denote how long the DHCP client has been trying to
communicate with the DHCP server. This parameter specifies the maximum time
in milliseconds for the client to try to communicate with the DHCP server,
after which this server assumes that the client failed to communicate with
the DHCP server (is unacknowledged or \(dqunacked\(dq). The default value of this
parameter is 10000.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP \- specifies how many \(dqunacked\(dq clients are allowed
(see \fBmax\-ack\-delay\fP) before this server assumes that the partner is
offline and transitions to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state. The special value of 0
is allowed for this parameter, which disables the failure\-detection mechanism.
In this case, a server that cannot communicate with its partner over the
control channel assumes that the partner server is down and transitions to
the \fBpartner\-down\fP state immediately. The default value of this parameter
is 10.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-rejected\-lease\-updates\fP \- specifies how many lease updates for distinct
clients can fail, due to a conflict between the lease and the partner configuration
or state, before the server transitions to the \fBterminated\fP state. Conflict
can be a sign of a misconfiguration; usually, a small number of conflicted
leases are acceptable because they affect only a few devices. However, if
the conflicts occur for many devices (e.g., an entire subnet), the HA service
becomes unreliable and should be terminated, and the problem must be manually
corrected by an administrator. It is up to the administrator to select
the highest acceptable value of \fBmax\-rejected\-lease\-updates\fP\&. The default
value is 10. The special value of 0 configures the server to never terminate
the HA service due to lease conflicts. If the value is 1, the server
transitions to the \fBterminated\fP state when the first conflict occurs.
This parameter does not pertain to conflicting lease updates sent to
the backup servers.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdelayed\-updates\-limit\fP \- specifies the maximum number of lease updates
which can be queued while the server is in the \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP
state. This parameter was introduced in Kea 1.9.4. The special value of 0
configures the server to never transition to the \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP
state and the server behaves as in earlier Kea versions, i.e. if the server
cannot reach its partner, it goes straight into the \fBpartner\-down\fP state.
The default value of this parameter is 100.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBmax\-rejected\-lease\-updates\fP parameter was introduced in Kea 2.3.1.
Previously, the server did not differentiate between a lease update
failure due to a non\-functioning partner and a failure due to a conflict
(e.g., configuration issues).
As a result, the server could sometimes transition to the \fBpartner\-down\fP
state even though the partner was operating normally, but only certain leases
had issues. Conflicts should no longer cause such a transition. However,
depending on the \fBmax\-rejected\-lease\-updates\fP setting, too many conflicts
can lead to termination of the High Availability service. In that case, both
servers continue to respond to DHCP queries but no longer send lease updates.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The values of \fBmax\-ack\-delay\fP and \fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP must be selected
carefully, taking into account the specifics of the network in which the DHCP
servers are operating. The server in question may not respond to some DHCP
clients following administrative policy, or the server may drop malformed
queries from clients. Therefore, selecting too low a value for the
\fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP parameter may result in a transition to the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state even though the partner is still operating. On the other
hand, selecting too high a value may result in never transitioning to the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state if the DHCP traffic in the network is very low (e.g. at
night), because the number of distinct clients trying to communicate with the
server could be lower than the \fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP setting.
.sp
In some cases it may be useful to disable the failure\-detection mechanism
altogether, if the servers are located very close to each other and network
partitioning is unlikely, i.e. failure to respond to heartbeats is only possible
when the partner is offline. In such cases, set \fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP to 0.
.sp
The \fBdelayed\-updates\-limit\fP parameter is used to enable or disable the
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP procedure, and controls the server\(aqs behavior in the
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state. This parameter can only be used in the
\fBload\-balancing\fP mode.
.sp
If a server in the \fBload\-balancing\fP state experiences communication issues
with its partner (a heartbeat or lease\-update failure), the server transitions
to the \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state. In this state, the server keeps
responding to DHCP queries but does not send lease updates to the partner. The
lease updates are queued until communication is re\-established, to ensure that
DHCP service remains available even in the event of the communication loss
between the partners. There may appear to be communication loss when either one
of the servers has terminated, or when both servers remain available but cannot
communicate with each other. In the former case, the surviving server will
follow the normal procedure and should eventually transition to the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state. In the latter case, both servers should transition to
the \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state and should never transition to the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state (if \fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP is set to a non\-zero value),
because all DHCP queries are answered and neither server would see any unacked
DHCP queries.
.sp
Introduction of the \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP procedure was motivated by issues
which may appear when two servers remain online but the communication between
them remains interrupted for a period of time. In earlier Kea versions, the
servers having communication issues used to drop DHCP packets before
transitioning to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state. In some cases they both
transitioned to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state, which could potentially result in
allocations of the same IP addresses or delegated prefixes to different clients
by both servers. By entering the intermediate \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state,
these problems are avoided.
.sp
If a server in the \fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state re\-establishes communication
with its partner, it tries to send the partner all of the outstanding lease
updates it has queued. This is done synchronously and may take a considerable
amount of time before the server transitions to the \fBload\-balancing\fP state and
resumes normal operation.
The maximum number of lease updates which can be queued in the
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state is controlled by \fBdelayed\-updates\-limit\fP\&.
If the limit is exceeded, the server stops queuing lease updates and performs a
full database synchronization after re\-establishing the connection with the
partner, instead of sending outstanding lease updates before transitioning to
the \fBload\-balancing\fP state. Even if the limit is exceeded, the server in the
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state remains responsive to DHCP clients.
.sp
It may be preferable to set higher values of \fBdelayed\-updates\-limit\fP when
there is a risk of prolonged communication interruption between the servers and
when the lease database is large, to avoid costly lease\-database synchronization.
On the other hand, if the lease database is small, the time required to send
outstanding lease updates may be longer than the lease\-database synchronization.
In such cases it may be better to use a lower value, e.g. 10. The default value
of 100 is a reasonable compromise and should work well in most deployments with
moderate traffic.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This parameter is new and values for it that work well in some environments
may not work well in others. Feedback from users will help us build a better
working set of recommendations.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBpeers\fP parameter contains a list of servers within this HA setup.
This configuration must contain at least one primary and one secondary server.
It may also contain an unlimited number of backup servers. In this example,
there is one backup server which receives lease updates from the active servers.
.sp
These are the parameters specified for each of the peers within this
list:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP \- specifies a unique name for the server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBurl\fP \- specifies the URL to be used to contact this server over the
control channel. Other servers use this URL to send control commands to that
server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbasic\-auth\-user\fP \- specifies the user ID for basic HTTP authentication. If
not specified or specified as an empty string, no authentication header is
added to HTTP transactions. It must not contain the colon (:) character.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbasic\-auth\-user\-file\fP \- is an alternative to \fBbasic\-auth\-user\fP:
instead of presenting the user ID in the configuration file it is specified
in the first line of the file indicated by this parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbasic\-auth\-password\fP \- specifies the password for basic HTTP
authentication. This parameter is ignored when the user ID is not specified
or is empty. The password is optional; if not specified, an empty password is
used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbasic\-auth\-password\-file\fP \- is an alternative to \fBbasic\-auth\-password\fP:
instead of presenting the password in the configuration file it is specified
in the first line of the file indicated by this parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrole\fP \- denotes the role of the server in the HA setup. The following
roles are supported in the \fBload\-balancing\fP configuration: \fBprimary\fP,
\fBsecondary\fP, and \fBbackup\fP\&. There must be exactly one primary and one
secondary server in the \fBload\-balancing\fP setup.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauto\-failover\fP \- a boolean value which denotes whether a server detecting
a partner\(aqs failure should automatically start serving the partner\(aqs clients.
The default value of this parameter is \fBtrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
In our example configuration above, both active servers can allocate leases from
the subnet \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq. This subnet contains two address pools:
\(dq192.0.3.100 \- 192.0.3.150\(dq and \(dq192.0.3.200 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq, which are
associated with HA server scopes using client classification. When \fBserver1\fP
processes a DHCP query, it uses the first pool for lease allocation. Conversely,
when \fBserver2\fP processes a DHCP query it uses the second pool. If either of
the servers is in the \fBpartner\-down\fP state, the other can serve leases from
both pools; it selects the pool which is appropriate for the received query. In
other words, if the query would normally be processed by \fBserver2\fP but this
server is not available, \fBserver1\fP allocates the lease from the pool of
\(dq192.0.3.200 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq. The \fIserver3\(ga\fP HTTP control socket
requires basic HTTP authentication, and authorizes the user ID \(dqfoo\(dq with the
password \(dq1234\(dq.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBurl\fP schema can be \fBhttp\fP or \fBhttps\fP, but since Kea version 1.9.6
the \fBhttps\fP schema requires a TLS setup. The hostname part must be an IPv4
address or an IPv6 address between square brackets, e.g.
\fBhttp://[2001:db8::1]:8080/\fP\&. Names are not accepted.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Load Balancing With Advanced Classification
.sp
In the previous section, we provided an example of a \fBload\-balancing\fP
configuration with client classification limited to the \(dqHA_server1\(dq and
\(dqHA_server2\(dq classes, which are dynamically assigned to the received DHCP
queries. In many cases, HA is needed in deployments which already use some other
client classification.
.sp
Suppose there is a system which classifies devices into two groups: \(dqphones\(dq and
\(dqlaptops\(dq, based on some classification criteria specified in the Kea
configuration file. Both types of devices are allocated leases from different
address pools. Introducing HA in \fBload\-balancing\fP mode results in a further
split of each of those pools, as each server allocates leases for some phones
and some laptops. This requires each of the existing pools to be split between
\(dqHA_server1\(dq and \(dqHA_server2\(dq, so we end up with the following classes:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqphones_server1\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqlaptops_server1\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqphones_server2\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqlaptops_server2\(dq
.UNINDENT
.sp
The corresponding server configuration, using advanced classification (and the
\fBmember\fP expression), is provided below. For brevity\(aqs sake, the HA hook
library configuration has been removed from this example.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [{
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqphones\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqsubstring(option[60].hex,0,6) == \(aqAastra\(aq\(dq
    }, {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqlaptops\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqnot member(\(aqphones\(aq)\(dq
    }, {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqphones_server1\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqphones\(aq) and member(\(aqHA_server1\(aq)\(dq
    }, {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqphones_server2\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqphones\(aq) and member(\(aqHA_server2\(aq)\(dq
    }, {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqlaptops_server1\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqlaptops\(aq) and member(\(aqHA_server1\(aq)\(dq
    }, {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqlaptops_server2\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqmember(\(aqlaptops\(aq) and member(\(aqHA_server2\(aq)\(dq
    }],

    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
    }, {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [{
            }]
        }
    }],

    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [{
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.100 \- 192.0.3.125\(dq,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqphones_server1\(dq ]
        }, {
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.126 \- 192.0.3.150\(dq,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqlaptops_server1\(dq ]
        }, {
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.200 \- 192.0.3.225\(dq,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqphones_server2\(dq ]
        }, {
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.226 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqlaptops_server2\(dq ]
        }],

        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.1\(dq
        }],

        \(dqrelay\(dq: { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq10.1.2.3\(dq }
    }]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The configuration provided above splits the address range into four pools: two
pools dedicated to \(dqHA_server1\(dq and two to \(dqHA_server2\(dq. Each server can assign
leases to both phones and laptops. Both groups of devices are assigned addresses
from different pools. The \(dqHA_server1\(dq and \(dqHA_server2\(dq classes are built\-in
(see Built\-in Client Classes \%<#\:built-in-client-classes>) and do not need to be declared.
They are assigned dynamically by the HA hook library as a result of the
\fBload\-balancing\fP algorithm. \(dqphones_*\(dq and \(dqlaptop_*\(dq evaluate to \fBtrue\fP
when the query belongs to a given combination of other classes, e.g. \(dqHA_server1\(dq
and \(dqphones\(dq. The pool is selected accordingly as a result of such an evaluation.
.sp
Consult Client Classification \%<#\:classify> for details on how to use the \fBmember\fP expression and
class dependencies.
.SS Hot\-Standby Configuration
.sp
The following is an example configuration of the primary server in a
\fBhot\-standby\fP configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
    }, {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [{
                \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqhot\-standby\(dq,
                \(dqheartbeat\-delay\(dq: 10000,
                \(dqmax\-response\-delay\(dq: 60000,
                \(dqmax\-ack\-delay\(dq: 5000,
                \(dqmax\-unacked\-clients\(dq: 5,
                \(dqmax\-rejected\-lease\-updates\(dq: 10,
                \(dqpeers\(dq: [{
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqstandby\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver3\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.99:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqbasic\-auth\-user\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                    \(dqbasic\-auth\-password\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqbackup\(dq,
                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: false
                }]
            }]
        }
    }],

    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [{
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.100 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq,
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqHA_server1\(dq ]
        }],

        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.1\(dq
        }],

        \(dqrelay\(dq: { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq10.1.2.3\(dq }
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration is very similar to the \fBload\-balancing\fP configuration
described in Load\-Balancing Configuration, with a few notable differences.
.sp
The \fBmode\fP is now set to \fBhot\-standby\fP, in which only one server responds to
DHCP clients. If the primary server is online, it responds to all DHCP queries.
The \fBstandby\fP server takes over all DHCP traffic only if it discovers that the
primary is unavailable.
.sp
In this mode, the non\-primary active server is called \fBstandby\fP and that is
its role.
.sp
Finally, because there is always only one server responding to DHCP queries,
there is only one scope \- \(dqHA_server1\(dq \- in use within pool definitions. In fact,
the \fBclient\-class\fP parameter could be removed from this configuration without
harm, because there can be no conflicts in lease allocations by different
servers as they do not allocate leases concurrently. The \fBclient\-class\fP
remains in this example mostly for demonstration purposes, to highlight the
differences between the \fBhot\-standby\fP and \fBload\-balancing\fP modes of
operation.
.SS Passive\-Backup Configuration
.sp
The following is an example configuration file for the primary server in a
\fBpassive\-backup\fP configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
    }, {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [{
                \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqpassive\-backup\(dq,
                \(dqwait\-backup\-ack\(dq: false,
                \(dqpeers\(dq: [{
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqbackup\(dq
                }, {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver3\(dq,
                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.99:8005/\(dq,
                    \(dqbasic\-auth\-user\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                    \(dqbasic\-auth\-password\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqbackup\(dq
                }]
            }]
        }
    }],

    \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [{
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.100 \- 192.0.3.250\(dq
        }],

        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [{
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.1\(dq
        }],

        \(dqrelay\(dq: { \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq10.1.2.3\(dq }
    }]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The configurations of three peers are included: one for the primary and two for
the backup servers.
.sp
Many of the parameters present in the \fBload\-balancing\fP and \fBhot\-standby\fP
configuration examples are not relevant in the \fBpassive\-backup\fP mode, thus
they are not specified here. For example: \fBheartbeat\-delay\fP,
\fBmax\-unacked\-clients\fP, \fBmax\-rejected\-lease\-updates\fP, and others related to
the failover mechanism should not be specified in the \fBpassive\-backup\fP mode.
.sp
The \fBwait\-backup\-ack\fP is a boolean parameter not present in previous examples.
It defaults to \fBfalse\fP and must not be modified in the \fBload\-balancing\fP and
\fBhot\-standby\fP modes. In the \fBpassive\-backup\fP mode this parameter can be set
to \fBtrue\fP, which causes the primary server to expect acknowledgments to the
lease updates from the backup servers prior to responding to the DHCP client. It
ensures that the lease has propagated to all servers before the client is given
the lease, but it poses a risk of losing a DHCP service if there is a
communication problem with one of the backup servers. This setting also
increases the latency of the DHCP response, because of the time that the primary
spends waiting for the acknowledgements. We recommend that the
\fBwait\-backup\-ack\fP setting be left at its default value (\fBfalse\fP) if the DHCP
service reliability is more important than consistency of the lease information
between the primary and the backups, and in all cases when the DHCP service
latency should be minimal.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Currently, active servers place lease updates to be sent to peers onto
internal queues (one queue per peer/URL). In \fBpassive\-backup\fP mode, active
servers do not wait for lease updates to be acknowledged; thus during times
of heavy client traffic it is possible for the number of lease updates queued
for transmission to accumulate faster than they can be delivered. As client
traffic lessens the queues begin to empty. Since Kea 2.0.0, active servers
monitor the size of these queues and emit periodic warnings (see
HTTP_CLIENT_QUEUE_SIZE_GROWING in Kea Messages Manual \%<#\:kea-messages>) if they perceive a
queue as growing too quickly. The warnings cease once the queue size begins
to shrink. These messages are intended as a bellwether and seeing them
sporadically during times of heavy traffic load does not necessarily indicate
a problem. If, however, they occur continually during times of routine
traffic load, they likely indicate potential mismatches in server
capabilities and/or configuration; this should be investigated, as the size
of the queues may eventually impair an active server\(aqs ability to respond to
clients in a timely manner.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Lease Information Sharing
.sp
An HA\-enabled server informs its active partner about allocated or renewed
leases by sending appropriate control commands, and the partner updates the
lease information in its own database. When the server starts up for the first
time or recovers after a failure, it synchronizes its lease database with its
partner. These two mechanisms guarantee consistency of the lease information
between the servers and allow the designation of one of the servers to handle
the entire DHCP traffic load if the other server becomes unavailable.
.sp
In some cases, though, it is desirable to disable lease updates and/or database
synchronization between the active servers, if the exchange of information about
the allocated leases is performed using some other mechanism. Kea supports
various database types that can be used to store leases, including MySQL and
PostgreSQL. Those databases include built\-in solutions for data replication
which are often used by Kea administrators to provide redundancy.
.sp
The HA hook library supports such scenarios by disabling lease updates over the
control channel and/or lease\-database synchronization, leaving the server to
rely on the database replication mechanism. This is controlled by the two
boolean parameters \fBsend\-lease\-updates\fP and \fBsync\-leases\fP, whose values
default to \fBtrue\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
        },
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                    \(dqsend\-lease\-updates\(dq: false,
                    \(dqsync\-leases\(dq: false,
                    \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq
                        },
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqsecondary\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                } ]
            }
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the most typical use case, both parameters are set to the same value, i.e.
both are \fBfalse\fP if database replication is in use, or both are \fBtrue\fP
otherwise. Introducing two separate parameters to control lease updates and
lease\-database synchronization is aimed at possible special use cases; for
example, when synchronization is performed by copying a lease file (therefore
\fBsync\-leases\fP is set to \fBfalse\fP), but lease updates should be conducted as
usual (\fBsend\-lease\-updates\fP is set to \fBtrue\fP). It should be noted that Kea
does not natively support such use cases, but users may develop their own
scripts and tools around Kea to provide such mechanisms. The HA hook library
configuration is designed to maximize flexibility of administration.
.SS Controlling Lease\-Page Size Limit
.sp
An HA\-enabled server initiates synchronization of the lease database after
downtime or upon receiving the \fBha\-sync\fP command. The server uses the commands
\fBlease4\-get\-page\fP and \fBlease6\-get\-page\fP
to fetch leases from its partner server (lease queries). The size of the results
page (the maximum number of leases to be returned in a single response to one of
these commands) can be controlled via configuration of the HA hook library.
Increasing the page size decreases the number of lease queries sent to the
partner server, but it causes the partner server to generate larger responses,
which lengthens transmission time as well as increases memory and CPU
utilization on both servers. Decreasing the page size helps to decrease resource
utilization, but requires more lease queries to be issued to fetch the entire
lease database.
.sp
The default value of the \fBsync\-page\-limit\fP command controlling the page size
is 10000. This means that the entire lease database can be fetched with a single
command if the size of the database is equal to or less than 10000 lines.
.SS Timeouts
.sp
In deployments with a large number of clients connected to the network,
lease\-database synchronization after a server failure may be a time\-consuming
operation. The synchronizing server must gather all leases from its partner,
which yields a large response over the RESTful interface. The server receives
leases using the paging mechanism described in Controlling Lease\-Page Size Limit\&.
Before the page of leases is fetched, the synchronizing server sends a
\fBdhcp\-disable\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-dhcp-disable> command to disable the DHCP service on the partner server. If
the service is already disabled, this command resets the timeout for the DHCP
service being disabled, which by default is set to 60 seconds. If fetching a
single page of leases takes longer than the specified time, the partner server
assumes that the synchronizing server has died and resumes its DHCP service. The
connection of the synchronizing server with its partner is also protected by the
timeout. If the synchronization of a single page of leases takes longer than the
specified time, the synchronizing server terminates the connection and the
synchronization fails. Both timeout values are controlled by a single
configuration parameter, \fBsync\-timeout\fP\&. The following configuration snippet
demonstrates how to modify the timeout for automatic re\-enabling of the DHCP
service on the partner server and how to increase the timeout for fetching a
single page of leases from 60 seconds to 90 seconds:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
        },
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                    \(dqsync\-timeout\(dq: 90000,
                    \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq
                        },
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqsecondary\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                } ]
            }
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is important to note that extending this \fBsync\-timeout\fP value may sometimes
be insufficient to prevent issues with timeouts during lease\-database
synchronization.  The DHCP server also monitors the connection from the
peer for timeouts. Those timeouts cannot currently be modified via
configuration; extending these timeouts is only possible by modifying them in
the Kea code and recompiling the server. The relevant constants are located in
the Kea source at: \fBsrc/lib/config/timeouts.h\fP\&.
.SS Pausing the HA State Machine
.sp
The \fBhigh\-availability\fP state machine includes many different states described
in detail in Server States\&. The server enters each state when certain
conditions are met, most often taking into account the partner server\(aqs state.
In some states the server performs specific actions, e.g. synchronization of the
lease database in the \fBsyncing\fP state, or responding to DHCP queries according
to the configured mode of operation in the \fBload\-balancing\fP and \fBhot\-standby\fP
states.
.sp
By default, transitions between the states are performed automatically and the
server administrator has no direct control over when the transitions take place;
in most cases, the administrator does not need such control. In some situations,
however, the administrator may want to \(dqpause\(dq the HA state machine in a
selected state to perform some additional administrative actions before the
server transitions to the next state.
.sp
Consider a server failure which results in the loss of the entire lease database.
Typically, the server rebuilds its lease database when it enters the \fBsyncing\fP
state by querying the partner server for leases, but it is possible that the
partner was also experiencing a failure and lacks lease information. In this
case, it may be required to reconstruct lease databases on both servers from
some external source, e.g. a backup server. If the lease database is to be
reconstructed via the RESTful API, the servers should be started in the initial,
i.e. \fBwaiting\fP, state and remain in this state while leases are being added.
In particular, the servers should not attempt to synchronize their lease
databases nor start serving DHCP clients.
.sp
The HA hook library provides configuration parameters and a command to control
pausing and resuming the HA state machine. The following configuration causes
the HA state machine to pause in the \fBwaiting\fP state after server startup.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
        },
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                    \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq
                        },
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqsecondary\(dq
                        }
                    ],
                    \(dqstate\-machine\(dq: {
                        \(dqstates\(dq: [
                            {
                                \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqwaiting\(dq,
                                \(dqpause\(dq: \(dqonce\(dq
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                } ]
            }
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBpause\fP parameter value \fBonce\fP denotes that the state machine should be
paused upon the first transition to the \fBwaiting\fP state; later transitions to
this state will not cause the state machine to pause. Two other supported values
of the \fBpause\fP parameter are \fBalways\fP and \fBnever\fP\&. The latter is the
default value for each state, which instructs the server never to pause the
state machine.
.sp
In order to \(dqunpause\(dq the state machine, the \fBha\-continue\fP command must be
sent to the paused server. This command does not take any arguments. See
Control Commands for High Availability for details about commands specific to \fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP\&.
.sp
It is possible to configure the state machine to pause in more than one state.
Consider the following configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
        },
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                    \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq
                        },
                        {
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                            \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8005/\(dq,
                            \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqsecondary\(dq
                        }
                    ],
                    \(dqstate\-machine\(dq: {
                        \(dqstates\(dq: [
                            {
                                \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqready\(dq,
                                \(dqpause\(dq: \(dqalways\(dq
                            },
                            {
                                \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqpartner\-down\(dq,
                                \(dqpause\(dq: \(dqonce\(dq
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                } ]
            }
        }
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration instructs the server to pause the state machine every time it
transitions to the \fBready\fP state and upon the first transition to the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state.
.sp
Refer to Server States for a complete list of server states. The state
machine can be paused in any of the supported states; however, it is not
practical to pause in the \fBbackup\fP or \fBterminated\fP states because the server
never transitions out of these states anyway.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
In the \fBsyncing\fP state the server is paused before it makes an attempt to
synchronize the lease database with a partner. To pause the state machine
after lease\-database synchronization, use the \fBready\fP state instead.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The state of the HA state machine depends on the state of the cooperating
server. Therefore, pausing the state machine of one server may affect the
operation of the partner server. For example: if the primary server is paused
in the \fBwaiting\fP state, the partner server will also remain in the
\fBwaiting\fP state until the state machine of the primary server is resumed
and that server transitions to the \fBready\fP state.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Multi\-Threaded Configuration (HA+MT)
.sp
It is possible to configure HA to use direct
multi\-threaded communication between peers. We refer to this mode as HA+MT.
With HA+MT enabled, each peer runs its own dedicated, internal HTTP listener
(i.e. server) which receives and responds to commands directly, thus eliminating
the sequentialization of commands including for the HA protocol between peers. In addition, both
the listener and client components use multi\-threading to support multiple
concurrent connections between peers. By eliminating the overloading of
the control channel and executing
multiple command exchanges in parallel, HA throughput between peers
improves considerably over earlier versions of Kea in most situations.
.sp
The following parameters have been added to the HA configuration, to support
HA+MT operation:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenable\-multi\-threading\fP \- enables or disables multi\-threading HA peer
communication (HA+MT). Kea core multi\-threading must be enabled for HA+MT to
operate. When \fBfalse\fP, the server relies on a HTTP control socket for
communication with its peer, and uses single\-threaded HTTP client processing.
The default is \fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhttp\-dedicated\-listener\fP \- enables or disables the creation of a dedicated,
internal HTTP listener through which the server receives HA messages from its
peers. The internal listener replaces the role of control socket traffic,
allowing peers to send their HA commands directly to each other. The listener
listens on the peer\(aqs \fBurl\fP\&. When \fBfalse\fP, the server
relies on HTTP control socket. This parameter has been provided largely for
flexibility and testing; running HA+MT without dedicated listeners enabled
will substantially limit HA throughput. The default is \fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhttp\-listener\-threads\fP \- indicates the maximum number of threads the
dedicated listener should use. A value of \fB0\fP instructs the server to use the
same number of threads that the Kea core is using for DHCP multi\-threading.
The default is \fB0\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhttp\-client\-threads\fP \- indicates the maximum number of threads that should
be used to send HA messages to its peers. A value of \fB0\fP instructs the server
to use the same number of threads that the Kea core is using for DHCP
multi\-threading. The default is \fB0\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
These parameters are grouped together under a map element, \fBmulti\-threading\fP,
as illustrated below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: { }
        },
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [ {
                    \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                    \(dqmulti\-threading\(dq: {
                        \(dqenable\-multi\-threading\(dq: true,
                        \(dqhttp\-dedicated\-listener\(dq: true,
                        \(dqhttp\-listener\-threads\(dq: 4,
                        \(dqhttp\-client\-threads\(dq: 4
                    },
                    \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                      // This is the configuration of this server instance.
                      {
                          \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                          // This specifies the URL of our server instance.
                          // Since the HA+MT uses a direct connection, the
                          // DHCPv4 server open its own socket. Note that it
                          // must be different than the one used by the control
                          // socket (typically 8000). In this example, 8005 is used.
                          \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.0.2.1:8005/\(dq,
                          // This server is primary. The other one must be
                          // secondary.
                          \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq
                      },
                      // This is the configuration of our HA peer.
                      {
                          \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                          // This specifies the URL of our server instance.
                          // Since the HA+MT uses a direct connection, the
                          // DHCPv4 server open its own socket. Note that it
                          // must be different than the one used by the control
                          // socket (typically 8000). In this example, 8005 is used.
                          \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.0.2.2:8005/\(dq,
                          // The partner is a secondary. This server is a
                          // primary as specified in the previous \(dqpeers\(dq
                          // entry and in \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq before that.
                          \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqsecondary\(dq
                      },
                      ...
                    ],
                    ...
                },
                ...
                ]
            }
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the example above, HA+MT is enabled with four threads for the listener and
four threads for the client.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is essential to configure the ports correctly. One common mistake is to
configure control socket to listen on port 8000 and also configure dedicated
listeners on port 8000. In such a configuration, the communication will still
work over control socket (in single\-threaded mode), but it will be slow and
the DHCP server will fail to bind sockets (in multi\-threading mode).
Administrators should ensure that dedicated listeners use a different port
(8001 is a suggested alternative); if ports are misconfigured or the ports
dedicated to control socket are used, the performance bottlenecks caused by the
single\-threaded mode of the DHCP server or the sequential nature of the control
socket of the DHCP servers will nullify any performance gains offered by HA+MT.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Parked\-Packet Limit
.sp
Refer to Parked\-Packet Limit for a basic introduction to packet parking.
.sp
The HA hook library uses this mechanism. When an HA server
needs to send a lease update to its peers to notify them of the change to the
lease, it parks the client response until the peers acknowledge the lease
update. At that point, the server unparks the response and sends it to the
client. This applies to client queries that cause lease changes, such as
DHCPREQUEST for DHCPv4 and Request, Renew, and Rebind for DHCPv6. It does not
apply to DHCPDISCOVERs (v4) or Solicits (v6).
.SS Controlled Shutdown and Maintenance of DHCP Servers
.sp
Having a pair of servers providing High Availability allows for controlled
shutdown and maintenance of those servers without disrupting the DHCP service.
For example, an administrator can perform an upgrade of one of the servers while
the other one continues to respond to DHCP queries. When the first server is
upgraded and back online, the upgrade can be performed for the second server.
.sp
A typical problem reported with early versions of the High Availability hook
library was that the administrator did not have direct control over the state of
the DHCP server. Shutting down one of the servers for maintenance did not
necessarily cause the other server to start responding to all DHCP queries,
because the failure\-detection algorithm described in Scope Transition in a Partner\-Down Case
requires that the partner not respond for a configured period of time and,
depending on the configuration, may also require that a number of DHCP requests
not be responded to for a specified period of time. The maintenance procedure,
however, requires that the administrator be able to instruct one of the servers
to instantly start serving all DHCP clients, and the other server to instantly
stop serving any DHCP clients, so it can be safely shut down.
.sp
The maintenance feature of the High Availability hook library addresses this
situation. The \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command allows the
administrator to put the pairs of the active servers into a state in which one of
them is responding to all DHCP queries and the other one is awaiting shutdown.
.sp
Suppose that the HA setup includes two active servers, \fBserver1\fP and
\fBserver2\fP, and the latter needs to be shut down for maintenance.
The administrator can send the \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command to \fBserver1\fP,
as this is the server which is going to handle the DHCP traffic while the other
one is offline. \fBserver1\fP responds with an error if its state or the partner\(aqs
state does not allow for a maintenance shutdown: for example, if maintenance is
not supported for the backup server or if the server is in the \fBterminated\fP
state. Also, an error is returned if the \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP request was
already sent to the other server.
.sp
Upon receiving the \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command, \fBserver1\fP sends the
\fBha\-maintenance\-notify\fP command to \fBserver2\fP to put it in the
\fBin\-maintenance\fP state. If \fBserver2\fP confirms, \fBserver1\fP transitions to
the \fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state. This is similar to the \fBpartner\-down\fP
state, except that in the \fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state \fBserver1\fP continues
to send lease updates to \fBserver2\fP until the administrator shuts down
\fBserver2\fP\&. \fBserver1\fP now responds to all DHCP queries.
.sp
The administrator can now safely shut down \fBserver2\fP in the \fBin\-maintenance\fP
state and perform any necessary maintenance actions. While \fBserver2\fP is
offline, \fBserver1\fP will obviously not be able to communicate with its partner,
so it will immediately transition to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state; it will
continue to respond to all DHCP queries but will no longer send lease updates to
\fBserver2\fP\&. Restarting \fBserver2\fP after the maintenance will trigger normal
state negotiation, lease\-database synchronization, and, ultimately, a transition
to the normal \fBload\-balancing\fP or \fBhot\-standby\fP state. Maintenance can then
be performed on \fBserver1\fP, after sending the \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command
to \fBserver2\fP\&.
.sp
If the \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command was sent to the server and the server
has transitioned to the \fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state, it is possible to
transition both it and its partner back to their previous states to resume the
normal operation of the HA pair. This is achieved by sending the
\fBha\-maintenance\-cancel\fP command to the server that is in the
\fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state. However, if the server has already
transitioned to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state as a result of detecting that the
partner is offline, canceling the maintenance is no longer possible. In that
case, it is necessary to restart the other server and allow it to complete its
normal state negotiation process.
.sp
If the server has many relationships with different partners, the \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP
command attempts to transition all of the relationships into the
\fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state by sending \fBha\-maintenance\-notify\fP to all
partner servers. If this step fails for any server, an error is returned.
If that happens, the \fBha\-maintenance\-cancel\fP command can be used to resume normal
operations and fix the issue.
.SS Control Commands for High Availability
.sp
Even though the HA hook library is designed to automatically resolve issues with
DHCP service interruptions by redirecting the DHCP traffic to a surviving server
and synchronizing the lease database as needed, it may be useful for the
administrator to have more control over both servers\(aq behavior. In particular,
it may be useful to be able to trigger lease\-database synchronization on demand,
or to manually set the HA scopes that are being served.
.sp
The backup server can sometimes be used to handle DHCP traffic if both active
servers are down. The backup server does not perform the failover function
automatically; thus, in order to use the backup server to respond to DHCP
queries, the server administrator must enable this function manually.
.sp
The following sections describe commands supported by the HA hook library which
are available for the administrator.
.SS The \fBha\-sync\fP Command
.sp
The \fBha\-sync\fP command instructs the server to synchronize its local lease
database with the selected peer. The server fetches all leases from the peer and
updates any locally stored leases which are older than those fetched. It also
creates new leases when any of those fetched do not exist in the local database.
All leases that are not returned by the peer but are in the local database are
preserved. The database synchronization is unidirectional; only the database on
the server to which the command has been sent is updated. To synchronize the
peer\(aqs database, a separate \fBha\-sync\fP command must be issued to that peer.
.sp
Database synchronization may be triggered for both active and backup server
types. The \fBha\-sync\fP command has the following structure (in a DHCPv4 example):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-sync\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4 \(dq],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
        \(dqmax\-period\(dq: 60
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When the server receives this command it first disables the DHCP service of the
server from which it will be fetching leases, by sending the \fBdhcp\-disable\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-dhcp-disable>
command to that server. The \fBmax\-period\fP parameter specifies the maximum
duration (in seconds) for which the DHCP service should be disabled. If the DHCP
service is successfully disabled, the synchronizing server fetches leases from
the remote server by issuing one or more \fBlease4\-get\-page\fP commands. When the
lease\-database synchronization is complete, the synchronizing server sends the
\fBdhcp\-enable\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-dhcp-enable> command to the peer to re\-enable its DHCP service.
.sp
The \fBmax\-period\fP value should be sufficiently long to guarantee that it does
not elapse before the synchronization is completed. Otherwise, the DHCP server
will automatically enable its DHCP function while the synchronization is still
in progress. If the DHCP server subsequently allocates any leases during the
synchronization, those new (or updated) leases will not be fetched by the
synchronizing server, leading to database inconsistencies.
.SS The \fBha\-scopes\fP Command
.sp
This command allows an administrator to modify the HA scopes being served.
It can be useful when recovering from a failure or when performing maintenance.
Consult Load\-Balancing Configuration and Hot\-Standby Configuration to
learn which scopes are available for the different HA modes of operation.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBha\-scopes\fP command can put Kea servers into conflicting states,
which can lead to unexpected behavior. Changing scopes does not automatically
change the state of the server or its relationship with a partner.
.sp
For example, when we add primary scopes to the standby server it will start
responding to DHCP traffic for those scopes. This can lead to a situation where
both servers are responding to the same traffic, which can lead to IP address
collisions.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBha\-scopes\fP command has the following structure;
using an example in DHCPv4.:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-scopes\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqscopes\(dq: [ \(dqHA_server1\(dq, \(dqHA_server2\(dq ],
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command configures the server to handle traffic from both the \(dqHA_server1\(dq
and \(dqHA_server2\(dq scopes. To disable all scopes specify an empty list:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-scopes\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4 \(dq],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqscopes\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The optional \fBserver\-name\fP parameter specifies the name of one of the partners in
the HA relationship that this command pertains to. This parameter can be omitted if the
server receiving this command has only one HA relationship in its configuration.
.SS The \fBha\-continue\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to resume the operation of the paused HA state machine, as
described in Pausing the HA State Machine\&. It takes no arguments, so the
command structure is simply:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-continue\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqscopes\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The optional \fBserver\-name\fP parameter specifies the name of one of the partners in
the HA relationship that this command pertains to. This parameter can be omitted if the
server receiving this command has only one HA relationship in its configuration.
.SS The \fBha\-heartbeat\fP Command
.sp
The Server States section describes how the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command
is used by a pair of active HA servers to detect one partner\(aqs failure. This
command, however, can also be sent by the system administrator to one or both
servers to check their HA state. This allows a monitoring system to be deployed
on the HA enabled servers to periodically check whether they are operational or
whether any manual intervention is required. The \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command takes
no arguments:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-heartbeat\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqscopes\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The optional \fBserver\-name\fP parameter specifies the name of one of the partners in
the HA relationship that this command pertains to. This parameter can be omitted if the
server receiving this command has only one HA relationship in its configuration.
.sp
Upon successful communication with the server, a response similar to this should
be returned:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
   \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
   \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHA peer status returned.\(dq,
   \(dqarguments\(dq:
       {
           \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqpartner\-down\(dq,
           \(dqdate\-time\(dq: \(dqThu, 07 Nov 2019 08:49:37 GMT\(dq,
           \(dqscopes\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ],
           \(dqunsent\-update\-count\(dq: 123
       }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned \fBstate\fP value should be one of the values listed in
Server States\&. In the example above, the \fBpartner\-down\fP state is
returned, which indicates that the server which responded to the command
believes that its partner is offline; thus, it is serving all DHCP requests sent
to the servers. To ensure that the partner is indeed offline, the administrator
should send the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command to the second server. If sending the
command fails, e.g. due to an inability to establish a TCP connection to the
DHCP server, it is very likely that the server is not running.
.sp
The \fBdate\-time\fP parameter conveys the server\(aqs notion of time.
.sp
The \fBunsent\-update\-count\fP value is a cumulative count of all unsent lease
updates since the server was booted; its value is set to 0 when the server is
started. It is never reset to 0 during the server\(aqs operation, even after the
partner synchronizes the database. It is incremented by the partner sending the
heartbeat response when it cannot send the lease update. For example, suppose
the failure is a result of a temporary communication interruption. In that case,
the partner receiving the \fBpartner\-down\fP heartbeat response tracks the value
changes and can determine, once communication is reestablished, whether there
are any new lease updates that it did not receive. If the values on both servers
do not match, it is an indication that the partner should synchronize its lease
database. A non\-zero value itself is not an indication of any present issues
with lease updates, but a constantly incrementing value is.
.sp
The typical response returned by one server when both are
operational is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
   \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
   \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHA peer status returned.\(dq,
   \(dqarguments\(dq:
       {
           \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
           \(dqdate\-time\(dq: \(dqThu, 07 Nov 2019 08:49:37 GMT\(dq,
           \(dqscopes\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ],
           \(dqunsent\-update\-count\(dq: 0
       }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In most cases, the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command should be sent to both HA\-enabled
servers to verify the state of the entire HA setup. In particular, if one of the
servers indicates that it is in the \fBload\-balancing\fP state, it means that this
server is operating as if its partner is functional. When a partner goes down,
it takes some time for the surviving server to realize it. The
Scope Transition in a Partner\-Down Case section describes the algorithm which the surviving
server follows before it transitions to the \fBpartner\-down\fP state. If the
\fBha\-heartbeat\fP command is sent during the time window between the failure of
one of the servers and the transition of the surviving server to the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state, the response from the surviving server does not reflect
the failure. Resending the command detects the failure once the surviving server
has entered the \fBpartner\-down\fP state.
.SS The \fBstatus\-get\fP Command
.sp
\fBstatus\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-status-get> is a general\-purpose command supported by several Kea daemons,
not only the DHCP servers. However, when sent to a DHCP server with HA enabled,
it can be used to get insight into the details of the HA\-specific server status.
Not only does the response contain the status information of the server
receiving this command, but also the information about its partner if it is
available.
.sp
The following is an example response to the \fBstatus\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-status-get> command, including
the HA status of two \fBload\-balancing\fP servers:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpid\(dq: 1234,
        \(dquptime\(dq: 3024,
        \(dqreload\(dq: 1111,
        \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqha\-mode\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                \(dqha\-servers\(dq: {
                    \(dqlocal\(dq: {
                        \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                        \(dqscopes\(dq: [ \(dqserver1\(dq ],
                        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                        \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                        \(dqsystem\-time\(dq: \(dq2024\-01\-01 12:00:00\(dq
                    },
                    \(dqremote\(dq: {
                        \(dqage\(dq: 10,
                        \(dqanalyzed\-packets\(dq: 8,
                        \(dqclock\-skew\(dq: 0,
                        \(dqcommunication\-interrupted\(dq: true,
                        \(dqconnecting\-clients\(dq: 2,
                        \(dqin\-touch\(dq: true,
                        \(dqlast\-scopes\(dq: [ \(dqserver2\(dq ],
                        \(dqlast\-state\(dq: \(dqload\-balancing\(dq,
                        \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqsecondary\(dq,
                        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                        \(dqsystem\-time\(dq: \(dq2024\-01\-01 12:00:00\(dq,
                        \(dqunacked\-clients\(dq: 1,
                        \(dqunacked\-clients\-left\(dq: 2
                    }
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqmulti\-threading\-enabled\(dq: true,
        \(dqthread\-pool\-size\(dq: 4,
        \(dqpacket\-queue\-size\(dq: 64,
        \(dqpacket\-queue\-statistics\(dq: [ 0.2, 0.1, 0.1 ],
        \(dqsockets\(dq: {
            \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqready\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBhigh\-availability\fP argument is a list which currently comprises only one
element.
.sp
The \fBha\-servers\fP map contains two structures: \fBlocal\fP and \fBremote\fP\&. The
former contains the status information of the server which received the command,
while the latter contains the status information known to the local server about
the partner. The \fBrole\fP of the partner server is gathered from the local
configuration file, and thus should always be available. The remaining status
information, such as \fBlast\-scopes\fP and \fBlast\-state\fP, is not available until
the local server communicates with the remote by successfully sending the
\fBha\-heartbeat\fP command. If at least one such communication has taken place,
the returned value of the \fBin\-touch\fP parameter is set to \fBtrue\fP\&. By
examining this value, the command\(aqs sender can determine whether the information
about the remote server is reliable.
.sp
The \fBlast\-scopes\fP and \fBlast\-state\fP parameters contain information about the
HA scopes served by the partner and its state. This information is gathered
during the \fBha\-heartbeat\fP command exchange, so it may not be accurate if a
communication problem occurs between the partners and this status information is
not refreshed. In such a case, it may be useful to send the \fBstatus\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-status-get>
command to the partner server directly to check its current state. The \fBage\fP
parameter specifies the age of the information from the partner, in seconds.
.sp
The \fBcommunication\-interrupted\fP boolean value indicates whether the server
receiving the \fBstatus\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-status-get> command (the local server) has been unable to
communicate with the partner longer than the duration specified as
\fBmax\-response\-delay\fP\&. In such a situation, the active servers are considered
to be in the \fBcommunication\-interrupted\fP state. At this point, the local
server may start monitoring the DHCP traffic directed to the partner to see if
the partner is responding to this traffic. More about the failover procedure can
be found in Load\-Balancing Configuration\&.
.sp
The \fBsystem\-time\fP parameter holds the UTC time when skew between local and
partner node was last calculated. It is displayed in \fB%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S\fP format
for each active node: local, and remote, respectively. The \fBclock\-skew\fP
parameter is available in the \fBremote\fP map and holds the difference in seconds
between the two times. Local time is subtracted from the partner\(aqs time.
A positive value means that the partner is ahead, while a negative value means
that the partner is behind. Both \fBsystem\-time\fP and \fBclock\-skew\fP parameters
can be null if the clock skew was not calculated yet.
.sp
The \fBconnecting\-clients\fP, \fBunacked\-clients\fP, \fBunacked\-clients\-left\fP, and
\fBanalyzed\-packets\fP parameters were introduced along with the
\fBcommunication\-interrupted\fP parameter and they convey useful information about
the state of the DHCP traffic monitoring in the \fBcommunication\-interrupted\fP
state. Once the server leaves the \fBcommunication\-interrupted\fP state, these
parameters are all reset to 0.
.sp
These parameters have the following meaning in the \fBcommunication\-interrupted\fP
state:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconnecting\-clients\fP \- this is the number of different clients which have
attempted to get a lease from the remote server. These clients are
differentiated by their MAC address and client identifier (in DHCPv4) or DUID
(in DHCPv6). This number includes \(dqunacked\(dq clients (for which the \(dqsecs\(dq
field or \(dqelapsed time\(dq value exceeded the \fBmax\-response\-delay\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBunacked\-clients\fP \- this is the number of different clients which have been
considered \(dqunacked\(dq, i.e. the clients which have been trying to get the
lease longer than the value of the \(dqsecs\(dq field, or for which the
\(dqelapsed time\(dq exceeded the \fBmax\-response\-delay\fP setting.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBunacked\-clients\-left\fP \- this indicates the number of additional clients
which have to be considered \(dqunacked\(dq before the server enters the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state. This value decreases when the \fBunacked\-clients\fP
value increases. The local server enters the \fBpartner\-down\fP state when this
value decreases to 0.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBanalyzed\-packets\fP \- this is the total number of packets directed to the
partner server and analyzed by the local server since entering the
communication interrupted state. It includes retransmissions from the same
clients.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Monitoring these values helps to predict when the local server will enter the
\fBpartner\-down\fP state or to understand why the server has not yet entered this
state.
.sp
The \fBha\-mode\fP parameter returns the HA mode of operation selected using the
\fBmode\fP parameter in the configuration file. It can hold one of the following
values: \fBload\-balancing\fP, \fBhot\-standby\fP, or \fBpassive\-backup\fP\&.
.sp
The \fBstatus\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-status-get> response has the format described above only in the
\fBload\-balancing\fP and \fBhot\-standby\fP modes. In the \fBpassive\-backup\fP mode the
\fBremote\fP map is not included in the response because in this mode there is
only one active server (local). The response includes no information about the
status of the backup servers.
.SS The \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP Command
.sp
This command initiates the transition of the server\(aqs partners into the
\fBin\-maintenance\fP state, and the transition of the server receiving the command
into the \fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state, in each HA relationship. See the
Controlled Shutdown and Maintenance of DHCP Servers section for details.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-maintenance\-start\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBha\-maintenance\-cancel\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to cancel the maintenance previously initiated using the
\fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP command. The server receiving this command first
sends \fBha\-maintenance\-notify\fP, with the \fBcancel\fP flag set to \fBtrue\fP, to its
partners. Next, the server reverts from the \fBpartner\-in\-maintenance\fP state to
its previous state. See the Controlled Shutdown and Maintenance of DHCP Servers section for details.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-maintenance\-cancel\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBha\-maintenance\-notify\fP Command
.sp
This command is sent by the server receiving the \fBha\-maintenance\-start\fP or the
\fBha\-maintenance\-cancel\fP command to its partner, to cause the partner to
transition to the \fBin\-maintenance\fP state or to revert from this state to a
previous state. See the Controlled Shutdown and Maintenance of DHCP Servers section for details.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-maintenance\-notify\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcancel\(dq: false,
        \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqready\(dq,
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBstate\fP argument informs the recipient about the state of the sending server. The
recipient can instantly resume the operation of the state machine without sending
a heartbeat to check the partner\(aqs state.
.sp
The optional \fBserver\-name\fP parameter specifies the name of one of the partners in
the HA relationship that this command pertains to. This parameter can be omitted if the
server receiving this command has only one HA relationship in its configuration.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBha\-maintenance\-notify\fP command is not meant to be used by system
administrators. It is used for internal communication between a pair of
HA\-enabled DHCP servers. Direct use of this command is not supported and may
produce unintended consequences.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBha\-reset\fP Command
.sp
This command causes the server to reset its High Availability state machine by
transitioning it to the \fBwaiting\fP state. A partner in the
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state may send this command to cause the server
to synchronize its lease database. Database synchronization is required when the
partner has failed to send all lease database updates after re\-establishing
connection after a temporary connection failure. It is also required when the
\fBdelayed\-updates\-limit\fP is exceeded, when the server is in the
\fBcommunication\-recovery\fP state.
.sp
A server administrator may send this command to reset a misbehaving state
machine.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-reset\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The optional \fBserver\-name\fP parameter specifies the name of one of the partners in
the HA relationship that this command pertains to. This parameter can be omitted if the
server receiving this command has only one HA relationship in its configuration.
.sp
It elicits the response:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHA state machine reset.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the server receiving this command is already in the \fBwaiting\fP state, the
command has no effect.
.SS The \fBha\-sync\-complete\-notify\fP Command
.sp
A server sends this command to its partner to signal that it has completed
lease\-database synchronization. The partner may enable its DHCP service if it
can allocate new leases in its current state. The partner does not enable the
DHCP service in the \fBpartner\-down\fP state until it sends a successful \fBha\-heartbeat\fP
test to its partner server. If the connection is still unavailable, the server
in the \fBpartner\-down\fP state enables its own DHCP service to continue
responding to clients.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-sync\-complete\-notify\(dq,
    \(dqservice\(dq: [ \(dqdhcp4\(dq ],
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqorigin\-id\(dq: 2000,
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The optional \fBserver\-name\fP parameter specifies the name of one of the partners in
the HA relationship that this command pertains to. This parameter can be omitted if the
server receiving this command has only one HA relationship in its configuration.
.sp
The \fBorigin\-id\fP parameter is used to select the HA service for which the receiving server should
enable the DHCP service when it receives this notification. This is the same origin the
sending server used previously to disable the DHCP service before synchronization.
The \fBorigin\-id\fP parameter deprecates the \fBorigin\fP parameter used in some earlier
Kea versions.
.sp
It elicits the response:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqServer successfully notified about the synchronization completion.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBha\-sync\-complete\-notify\fP command is not meant to be used by system
administrators. It is used for internal communication between a pair of
HA\-enabled DHCP servers. Direct use of this command is not supported and may
produce unintended consequences.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Hub and Spoke Configuration
.sp
A hub\-and\-spoke arrangement, with one central server and multiple branch servers,
is a common way to ensure resiliency of the DHCP servers. The branch servers are the primary servers
in the \fBhot\-standby\fP mode and respond to the local DHCP traffic in their respective
locations. The central server acts as a standby server for each branch server.
It maintains independent state machines with the branch servers, called relationships.
If one of the branch servers experiences a failure, the central server can take over its
DHCP traffic. In this case, one of the central server\(aqs relationships is in
the \fBpartner\-down\fP state. The remaining relationships may still be in the \fBhot\-standby\fP
state and not actively respond to DHCP traffic. When the branch server becomes active again,
it synchronizes the lease database with the central server and the central server becomes
fully passive again. In rare cases, if multiple branch servers stop, the central server
takes responsibility for all their traffic, and potentially all DHCP traffic in the entire network
if every branch server is down. A simple hub\-and\-spoke arrangement consisting of two
branch servers and one central server is shown below.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
                            +\-\-\-\-\- Central Server \-\-\-\-\-\-+
                            |                           |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+ relationship 1 |  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  | relationship 2 +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
| Server 1 |===================| Server 2 | Server 4 |===================| Server 3 |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+                |  +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+  |                +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
                            |                           |
                            +\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Each branch server\(aqs configuration comprises a set of subnets appropriate for the branch
server; different branch servers serve different subnets. The central server\(aqs configuration
includes all subnets of the branch servers, so that it can respond to DHCP traffic
directed to any branch servers that fail. The subnets in the central server must be
grouped into relationships as in the snippet below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
            \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqenp0s8\(dq, \(dqenp0s9\(dq ]
        },
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {}
            },
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                            \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqhot\-standby\(dq,
                            \(dqmulti\-threading\(dq: {
                                \(dqenable\-multi\-threading\(dq: true,
                                \(dqhttp\-dedicated\-listener\(dq: true,
                                \(dqhttp\-listener\-threads\(dq: 4,
                                \(dqhttp\-client\-threads\(dq: 4
                            },
                            \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                                {
                                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.66:8007/\(dq,
                                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                                },
                                {
                                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.56.33:8007/\(dq,
                                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqstandby\(dq,
                                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                                }
                            ]
                        },
                        {
                            \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver4\(dq,
                            \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqhot\-standby\(dq,
                            \(dqmulti\-threading\(dq: {
                                \(dqenable\-multi\-threading\(dq: true,
                                \(dqhttp\-dedicated\-listener\(dq: true,
                                \(dqhttp\-listener\-threads\(dq: 4,
                                \(dqhttp\-client\-threads\(dq: 4
                            },
                            \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                                {
                                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver3\(dq,
                                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.57.99:8007/\(dq,
                                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                                },
                                {
                                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver4\(dq,
                                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.57.33:8007/\(dq,
                                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqstandby\(dq,
                                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/80\(dq } ],
                \(dqinterface\(dq: \(dqenp0s8\(dq,
                \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
                    \(dqha\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 2,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/64\(dq,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/80\(dq } ],
                \(dqinterface\(dq: \(dqenp0s9\(dq,
                \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
                    \(dqha\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver4\(dq
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The peer names in the relationships must be unique. The user context for each subnet contains
the \fBha\-server\-name\fP parameter, associating a subnet with a relationship. The \fBha\-server\-name\fP
can be any of the peer names in the relationship. For example, if a relationship contains peer names
\fBserver1\fP and \fBserver2\fP, it does not matter whether the \fBha\-server\-name\fP is \fBserver1\fP or
\fBserver2\fP\&. In both cases, it associates a subnet with that relationship.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It is important to note that each peer on a given Kea server must use a
unique listener address/port combination.  If the same address and port are
defined for multiple peers, the Kea service will fail to start with an
error similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
DHCP4_INIT_FAIL failed to initialize Kea server: configuration error using
file \(aqkea\-dhcp4.conf\(aq: Error initializing hooks: CmdHttpListener::run failed:
unable to setup TCP acceptor for listening to the incoming HTTP requests:
bind: Address already in use
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is not required to specify the \fBha\-server\-name\fP in the branch servers, assuming that the
branch servers only contain the subnets they serve. Consider the following configuration for
branch \fBserver3\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
            \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqenp0s8\(dq ]
        },
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {}
            },
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ha.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqhigh\-availability\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqthis\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver3\(dq,
                            \(dqmode\(dq: \(dqhot\-standby\(dq,
                            \(dqmulti\-threading\(dq: {
                                \(dqenable\-multi\-threading\(dq: true,
                                \(dqhttp\-dedicated\-listener\(dq: true,
                                \(dqhttp\-listener\-threads\(dq: 4,
                                \(dqhttp\-client\-threads\(dq: 4
                            },
                            \(dqpeers\(dq: [
                                {
                                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver3\(dq,
                                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.57.99:8007/\(dq,
                                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqprimary\(dq,
                                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                                },
                                {
                                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqserver4\(dq,
                                    \(dqurl\(dq: \(dqhttp://192.168.57.33:8007/\(dq,
                                    \(dqrole\(dq: \(dqstandby\(dq,
                                    \(dqauto\-failover\(dq: true
                                }
                            ]
                        }
                    ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 2,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/64\(dq,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::/80\(dq
                    }
                ],
                \(dqinterface\(dq: \(dqenp0s8\(dq,
                \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
                    \(dqha\-server\-name\(dq: \(dqserver3\(dq
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Even though it is possible to omit the \fBha\-server\-name\fP user context parameters in the
branch servers, we recommend including them. The servers fetch all leases from the
partners during the database synchronization; if the subnets are not explicitly associated with
the relationship, the branch server inserts all fetched leases from the central server (including
those from other relationships) into its database. Specifying the \fBha\-server\-name\fP parameter for
each configured subnet in the branch server guarantees that only the leases belonging to its
relationship are inserted into the branch server\(aqs database.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The peer names in the branch servers must match the peer names in the respective central
server\(aqs relationships, because these names are used for signaling between the HA partners.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The hub\-and\-spoke configuration is currently incompatible with the \fBreselect\-subnet\-address\fP
and \fBreselect\-subnet\-pool\fP parameters of the \fBlibdhcp_radius.so\fP\&. The High
Availability hook library uses an originally selected subnet for choosing an HA relationship
to process a packet. The subnet reselection may interfere with this choice. See the
RADIUS Hook Library Configuration \%<#\:radius-config> for details.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_host_cache.so\fP: Host Cache Reservations for Improved Performance
.sp
Some database backends, such as RADIUS, are slow and may take
a long time to respond. Since Kea in general is synchronous, backend
performance directly affects DHCP performance. To minimize the
impact and improve performance, the Host Cache library provides a way to
cache information from the database locally. This includes negative
caching, i.e. the ability to remember that there is no client
information in the database.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_host_cache.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In principle, this hook library can be used with any backend that may
introduce performance degradation (MySQL, PostgreSQL or RADIUS). Host Cache
must be loaded for the RADIUS accounting mechanism to work.
.sp
The Host Cache hook library is very simple. It takes only one
optional parameter (\fBmaximum\fP), which defines the maximum number of hosts
to be cached. If not specified, the default value of 0 is used, which
means there is no limit. This hook library can be loaded the same way as
any other hook library; for example, this configuration could be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp4\(dq: {

# Your regular DHCPv4 configuration parameters here.

\(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
{
    \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_host_cache.so\(dq,
    \(dqparameters\(dq: {

        # Tells Kea to never cache more than 1000 hosts.
        \(dqmaximum\(dq: 1000

    }
} ],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Once loaded, the Host Cache hook library provides a number of new
commands which can be used either over the control channel (see
Using the Control Channel \%<#\:ctrl-channel-client>).
An example RESTful API client is described in
Overview of the Kea Shell \%<#\:shell-overview>\&. The following sections describe the commands
available.
.SS The \fBcache\-flush\fP Command
.sp
This command allows removal of a specified number of cached host
entries. It takes one parameter, which defines the number of hosts to be
removed. An example usage looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-flush\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: 1000
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command removes 1000 hosts; to delete \fIall\fP cached
hosts, use \fBcache\-clear\fP instead. The hosts are stored in FIFO
(first\-in, first\-out) order, so the oldest entries are always removed.
.SS The \fBcache\-clear\fP Command
.sp
This command allows removal of all cached host entries. An example usage
looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-clear\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command removes all hosts. To delete only a certain
number of cached hosts, please use \fBcache\-flush\fP instead.
.SS The \fBcache\-size\fP Command
.sp
This command returns the number of host entries. An example usage looks
as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-size\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBcache\-write\fP Command
.sp
In general, the cache content is considered a runtime state and the
server can be shut down or restarted as usual; the cache is then
repopulated after restart. However, there are some cases when it is
useful to store the contents of the cache. One such case is RADIUS,
where the cached hosts also retain additional cached RADIUS attributes;
there is no easy way to obtain this information again, because renewing
clients send their packet to the DHCP server directly. Another use case
is when an administrator wants to restart the server and, for performance reasons,
wants it to start with a hot (populated) cache.
.sp
This command allows writing the contents of the in\-memory cache to a
file on disk. It takes one parameter, which defines the filename. An
example usage looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-write\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: \(dq/var/lib/kea/kea\-host\-cache.json\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This causes the contents to be stored in the \fB/var/lib/kea/kea\-host\-cache.json\fP
file. That file can then be loaded with the \fBcache\-load\fP command or
processed by any other tool that is able to understand JSON format.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, the cache file may only be written to the data directory
determined during compilation: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/lib/kea\(dq\fP\&. This
path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_DHCP_DATA_DIR\fP to the desired path. For ease of use in
specifying a custom file name simply omit the path portion from \fBfilename\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBcache\-load\fP Command
.sp
See the previous section for a discussion of use cases where it may be
useful to write and load contents of the host cache to disk.
.sp
This command allows the contents of a file on disk to be loaded into an
in\-memory cache. It takes one parameter, which defines the filename. An
example usage looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-load\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: \(dq/usr/local/share/kea/kea\-host\-cache.json\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command stores the contents to the \fB/usr/local/share/kea/kea\-host\-cache.json\fP
file. That file can then be loaded with the \fBcache\-load\fP command or
processed by any other tool that is able to understand JSON format.
.SS The \fBcache\-get\fP Command
.sp
This command is similar to \fBcache\-write\fP, but instead of writing the cache
contents to disk, it returns the contents to whoever sent the command.
.sp
This command allows the contents of a file on disk to be loaded into an
in\-memory cache. It takes one parameter, which defines the filename. An
example usage looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-get\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command returns all the cached hosts; the response
may be large.
.SS The \fBcache\-get\-by\-id\fP Command
.sp
This command is similar to \fBcache\-get\fP, but instead of returning the whole
content it returns only the entries matching the given identifier.
.sp
It takes one parameter, which defines the identifier of wanted cached
host reservations. An example usage looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-get\-by\-id\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command returns all the cached hosts with the given hardware
address.
.SS The \fBcache\-insert\fP Command
.sp
This command may be used to manually insert a host into the cache; there
are very few use cases when this command might be useful. This command
expects its arguments to follow the usual syntax for specifying host
reservations (see Host Reservations in DHCPv4 \%<#\:host-reservation-v4> or
Host Reservations in DHCPv6 \%<#\:host-reservation-v6>), with one difference: the \fBsubnet\-id\fP
value must be explicitly specified.
.sp
An example command to insert an IPv4 host into the host cache
looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-insert\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id4\(dq: 4,
        \(dqsubnet\-id6\(dq: 0,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\(dq,
        \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqsomehost.example.org\(dq,
        \(dqclient\-classes4\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqclient\-classes6\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqoption\-data4\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqoption\-data6\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
        \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq,
        \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
        \(dqhost\-id\(dq: 0
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example command to insert an IPv6 host into the host cache
looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-insert\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id4\(dq: 0,
        \(dqsubnet\-id6\(dq: 6,
        \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8::cafe:babe\(dq ],
        \(dqprefixes\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8:dead:beef::/64\(dq ],
        \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
        \(dqclient\-classes4\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqclient\-classes6\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqoption\-data4\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqoption\-data6\(dq: [ ],
        \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
        \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
        \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
        \(dqhost\-id\(dq: 0
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBcache\-remove\fP Command
.sp
Sometimes it is useful to remove a single entry from the host cache: for
example, consider a situation where the device is active, Kea has already
provided configuration, and the host entry is in cache. As a result of
administrative action (e.g. the customer hasn\(aqt paid their bills or has
been upgraded to better service), the information in the backend database
(e.g. MySQL or RADIUS) is being updated. However, since the cache is in use,
Kea does not notice the change as the cached values are used. The
\fBcache\-remove\fP command can solve this problem by removing a cached entry
after administrative changes.
.sp
The \fBcache\-remove\fP command works similarly to the \fBreservation\-get\fP command.
It allows querying by two parameters: either \fBsubnet\-id4\fP or \fBsubnet\-id6\fP;
or \fBip\-address\fP (may be an IPv4 or IPv6 address), \fBhw\-address\fP (specifies
a hardware/MAC address), \fBduid\fP, \fBcircuit\-id\fP, \fBclient\-id\fP, or \fBflex\-id\fP\&.
.sp
An example command to remove an IPv4 host with reserved address
192.0.2.1 from a subnet with a \fBsubnet\-id\fP 123 looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-remove\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 123
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Another example that removes an IPv6 host identifier by DUID and
specific \fBsubnet\-id\fP is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqcache\-remove\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq00:01:ab:cd:f0:a1:c2:d3:e4\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 123
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_host_cmds.so\fP: Host Commands
.sp
Kea can store host reservations in a database; in many larger deployments,
it is useful to be able to manage that information while the server is
running. The Host Commands library provides management commands for adding, querying,
and deleting host reservations in a safe way without restarting the
server. In particular, it validates the parameters, so an attempt to
insert incorrect data \- such as adding a host with a conflicting identifier in the
same subnet \- is rejected. Those commands are exposed via the command
channel (JSON over UNIX or HTTP/HTTPS sockets).
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_host_cmds.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Currently, the following commands are supported:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-add\fP, which adds a new host reservation.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-get\fP, which returns an existing reservation if specified
criteria are matched.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-get\-all\fP, which returns all reservations in a specified subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-get\-page\fP, a variant of \fBreservation\-get\-all\fP that returns
reservations by pages, either all or in a specified subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-get\-by\-address\fP, which returns all reservations with a
specified IP address or a delegated prefix (without a prefix length), and optionally a subnet id.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-get\-by\-hostname\fP, which returns all reservations with a
specified hostname and optionally in a subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-get\-by\-id\fP, which returns all reservations with a specified
identifier (since Kea version 1.9.0).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-del\fP, which attempts to delete a reservation matching specified
criteria.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreservation\-update\fP, which updates (replaces) an existing reservation
matching the given identifiers in a subnet.
.UNINDENT
.sp
To use the commands that change reservation information
(i.e. \fBreservation\-add\fP, \fBreservation\-del\fP, and \fBreservation\-update\fP) to
modify data stored in the host database, the hosts database must be specified
and it must not operate in read\-only mode (for details, see the
\fBhosts\-databases\fP descriptions in DHCPv4 Hosts Database Configuration \%<#\:hosts-databases-configuration4> and
DHCPv6 Hosts Database Configuration \%<#\:hosts-databases-configuration6>). If the \fBhosts\-databases\fP are not
specified or are running in read\-only mode, \fBlibdhcp_host_cmds.so\fP will
load, but any attempts to use \fBreservation\-add\fP, \fBreservation\-del\fP, and
\fBreservation\-update\fP to modify data in that database will fail.
.sp
These commands can also modify hosts from the JSON configuration independently
from the hosts database. The changes provided in the JSON configuration are
volatile and can be made permanent by sending the \fBconfig\-write\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-write> command.
.sp
For a description of proposed future commands, see the Control API
Requirements \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea/\:-/\:wikis/\:Designs/\:commands>
document.
.sp
All host commands use JSON syntax. They can be issued either using the
control channel (see Management API \%<#\:ctrl-channel>).
.sp
The library can be loaded similarly to other hook libraries. It
does not take any parameters, and it supports both the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6
servers.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_host_cmds.so\(dq
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet\-id\fP Parameter
.sp
Before examining the individual commands, it is worth discussing the
parameter \fBsubnet\-id\fP\&. Currently, this parameter is mandatory for all of the
commands supplied by this library, with the exception of
\fBreservation\-get\-by\-hostname\fP and \fBreservation\-get\-by\-address\fP,
where it is optional. \fBsubnet\-id\fP is also optional in
\fBreservation\-get\-page\fP, and it is forbidden
in \fBreservation\-get\-by\-id\fP\&.
.sp
Reservations can be specified globally, and are not necessarily specific to any
subnet. When reservations are supplied via the configuration file, the
ID of the containing subnet (or lack thereof) is implicit in the
configuration structure. However, when managing reservations using
host commands, it is necessary to explicitly identify the scope to which
the reservation belongs. This is done via the \fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter.
For global reservations, use a value of zero (0). For reservations
scoped to a specific subnet, use that subnet\(aqs ID. See also the note
in The reservation\-add Command about the \fBreservation\-add\fP
command for an empty (i.e. with no reserved address or prefix) reservation.
.sp
On the other hand, when the \fBsubnet\-id\fP is not specified in the command
parameters, it is added to each host in responses. If the \fBsubnet\-id\fP
has the unused special value, this means the host entry belongs only
to the other IP version (i.e. IPv6 in the DHCPv4 server or IPv4 in the DHCPv6
server) and this entry is ignored.
.SS The \fBoperation\-target\fP Parameter
.sp
Most host commands accept the \fBoperation\-target\fP parameter, which selects
the host data source. The commands may process data from the server
configuration (i.e., memory operation target), a database (database target),
or both (all sources). The operation\-target parameter is optional.
By default, the commands that only read the data use all data sources
(memory and database); the commands that modify the state (i.e. \fBreservation\-add\fP,
\fBreservation\-del\fP, and \fBreservation\-update\fP) only use the
database target.
.sp
The \fBoperation\-target\fP parameter accepts the following values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmemory\fP \- query or update the runtime server configuration
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdatabase\fP \- query or update host database(s)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBall\fP \- query or update both runtime configuration and host database(s)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdefault\fP \- query or update a default host data source \- it is command\-specific
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBreservation\-add\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-add\fP allows for the insertion of a new host. It takes a
set of arguments that vary depending on the nature of the host
reservation. Any parameters allowed in the configuration file that
pertain to host reservation are permitted here. For details regarding
IPv4 reservations, see Host Reservations in DHCPv4 \%<#\:host-reservation-v4>; for IPv6 reservations, see
Host Reservations in DHCPv6 \%<#\:host-reservation-v6>\&. The \fBsubnet\-id\fP is mandatory. Use a
value of zero (0) to add a global reservation, or the ID of the subnet
to which the reservation should be added. The command can be as simple as having
only the two mandatory entries:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In that case, however, it does not assign any resources to the host. An IPv4
address can be assigned as in this example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f\(dq,
            \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It can also take many more parameters, such as:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqclient\-id\(dq: \(dq01:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f\(dq,
            \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.205\(dq,
            \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq,
            \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqhal9000\(dq,
            \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq10.1.1.202,10.1.1.203\(dq
                }
            ],
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ \(dqspecial_snowflake\(dq, \(dqoffice\(dq ]
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a complex IPv6 reservation:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06:07:08:09:0A\(dq,
            \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8:1:cafe::1\(dq ],
            \(dqprefixes\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8:2:abcd::/64\(dq ],
            \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoo.example.com\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqvendor\-opts\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq4491\(dq
                },
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqtftp\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqvendor\-4491\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq3000:1::234\(dq
                }
            ]
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it adds the
reservation to the hosts database only.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {},
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: \(dqall\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command returns a status that indicates either success (result 0)
or failure (result 1). A failed command always includes a text parameter
that explains the cause of the failure. Here\(aqs an example of a successful
addition:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHost added.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And here\(aqs an example of a failure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 1,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqMandatory \(aqsubnet\-id\(aq parameter missing.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As \fBreservation\-add\fP is expected to store the host, the \fBhosts\-databases\fP
parameter must be specified in the configuration, and databases must not
run in read\-only mode.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Since kea version 2.7.0 an empty reservation is implicitly global, i.e.
if no \fBsubnet\-id\fP entry is present and for DHCPv4 \fBip\-address\fP or
for DHCPv6 \fBip\-addresses\fP and \fBprefixes\fP do not specify a reserved
address or prefix the command is valid and adds a global (subnet\-id 0)
host reservation.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBreservation\-get\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-get\fP can be used to query the host database and retrieve
existing reservations. This command supports two types of parameters:
(\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBaddress\fP) or (\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBidentifier\-type\fP,
\fBidentifier\fP). The first type of query is used when the address (either
IPv4 or IPv6) is known, but the details of the reservation are not. One
common use for this type of query is to find out whether a given
address is reserved. The second query uses identifiers. For
maximum flexibility, Kea stores the host identifying information as a
pair of values: the type and the actual identifier. Currently supported
identifiers are \fB\(dqhw\-address\(dq\fP, \fB\(dqduid\(dq\fP, \fB\(dqcircuit\-id\(dq\fP, \fB\(dqclient\-id\(dq\fP, and
\fB\(dqflex\-id\(dq\fP\&. The \fBsubnet\-id\fP is mandatory. Use a value
of zero (0) to fetch a global reservation, or the ID of the subnet to
which the reservation belongs.
.sp
An example command for getting a host reservation by a (\fBsubnet\-id\fP,
\fBaddress\fP) pair looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example query by (\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBidentifier\-type\fP, \fBidentifier\fP) looks as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 4,
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqhw\-address\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it gets the
reservation from both the in memory storage and the hosts database.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: \(dqdatabase\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Command \fBreservation\-get\fP typically returns the result 0 when a query was
conducted properly. In particular, 0 is returned when the host was not
found. If the query was successful, the host parameters are
returned. An example of a query that did not find the host looks as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 0, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHost not found.\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here\(aqs an example of a result returned when the host was found successfully:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [

    ],
    \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqsomehost.example.org\(dq,
    \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\(dq,
    \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [

    ],
    \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq,
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 4
  },
  \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
  \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHost found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example result returned when the query was malformed might look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 1, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqNo \(aqip\-address\(aq provided and \(aqidentifier\-type\(aq is either missing or not a string.\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBreservation\-get\-all\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-get\-all\fP can be used to query the host database and
retrieve all reservations in a specified subnet. This command uses
parameters providing the mandatory \fBsubnet\-id\fP\&. Global host reservations
can be retrieved by using a \fBsubnet\-id\fP value of zero (0).
.sp
For instance, retrieving host reservations for the subnet 1:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
     }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
returns some IPv4 hosts:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqsomehost.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqotherhost.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:ff\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.200\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            },
            ...
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq72 IPv4 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response returned by \fBreservation\-get\-all\fP can be very long. The
DHCP server does not handle DHCP traffic while preparing a response to
\fBreservation\-get\-all\fP, so if there are many reservations in a subnet, this
may be disruptive; use with caution. For larger deployments, please
consider using \fBreservation\-get\-page\fP instead.
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it gets the
reservation from both the in memory storage and the hosts database.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: \(dqdatabase\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information, see The reservation\-get\-all Command\&.
.SS The \fBreservation\-get\-page\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-get\-page\fP can be used to query the host database and
retrieve all reservations in a specified subnet, by pages. This command
uses parameters providing the mandatory \fBsubnet\-id\fP\&. Use a value of zero
(0) to fetch global reservations. The second mandatory parameter is the
page size limit. The optional \fBsource\-index\fP and \fBfrom\-host\-id\fP parameters, both
of which default to 0, are used to chain page queries.
Since Kea version 1.9.0, the \fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter is optional.
.sp
The usage of the \fBfrom\fP and \fBsource\-index\fP parameters requires additional
explanation. For the first call, those parameters should not be specified
(or should be specified as zeros). For any follow\-up calls, they should be set to
the values returned in previous calls, in a next map holding \fBfrom\fP and
\fBsource\-index\fP values. Subsequent calls should be issued until all
reservations are returned. The end is reached once the returned list is
empty, the count is 0, no next map is present, and result status 3 (empty) is
returned.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBfrom\fP and \fBsource\-index\fP parameters reflect the internal state of
the search. There is no need to understand what they represent; it is
simply a value that should be copied from one response to the
next query. However, for those who are curious, the \fBfrom\fP field represents a
64\-bit representation of the host identifier used by a host backend. The
\fBsource\-index\fP is an internal representation of multiple host
backends: 0 is used to represent hosts defined in a configuration
file, and 1 represents the first database backend. In some uncommon cases
there may be more than one database backend configured, so
potentially there may be a 2. In any case, Kea iterates over all
backends configured.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For instance, retrieving host reservations for the subnet 1 and
requesting the first page can be done by:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-page\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqlimit\(dq: 10
     }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Since this is the first call, \fBsource\-index\fP and \fBfrom\fP should not be
specified. They are set to their zero default values.
.sp
Some hosts are returned with information to get the next page:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 72,
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqsomehost.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqotherhost.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:ff\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.200\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq
            },
            ...
        ],
        \(dqnext\(dq: {
            \(dqfrom\(dq: 1234567,
            \(dqsource\-index\(dq: 1
        }
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq72 IPv4 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that the \fBfrom\fP and \fBsource\-index\fP fields were specified in the response in
the next map. Those two must be copied to the next command, so Kea
continues from the place where the last command finished. To get the
next page the following command can be sent:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-page\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsource\-index\(dq: 1,
        \(dqfrom\(dq: 1234567,
        \(dqlimit\(dq: 10
     }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response will contain a list of hosts with updated \fBsource\-index\fP
and \fBfrom\fP fields. Continue calling the command until the last
page is received. Its response will look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 0,
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [ ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 3,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq0 IPv4 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command does not accept the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument.
.sp
This command is more complex than \fBreservation\-get\-all\fP, but it lets
users retrieve larger host reservations lists in smaller chunks. For
small deployments with few reservations, it is easier to use
\fBreservation\-get\-all\fP\&.
.SS The \fBreservation\-get\-by\-address\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-get\-by\-address\fP can be used to query the host database and
retrieve all reservations for a given IP address or a delegated prefix (without
a prefix length), in a specified subnet or in all subnets. This command uses
parameters providing the mandatory \fBip\-address\fP and the optional \fBsubnet\-id\fP
and \fBoperation\-target\fP\&.
.sp
For instance, retrieving host reservations for the IPv4 address \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq
in the subnet 1:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-address\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
    },
    \(dqservice\(dq: [
        \(dqdhcp4\(dq
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
can return two IPv4 hosts:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq99:99:99:99:99:01\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqcircuit\-id\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv4 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To search for all reservations in all subnets, simply skip the \fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-address\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq
    },
    \(dqservice\(dq: [
        \(dqdhcp4\(dq
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example response could be:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq99:99:99:99:99:01\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqcircuit\-id\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq99:99:99:99:99:02\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 0
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq99:99:99:99:99:03\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 2
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq4 IPv4 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When using the command for retrieving DHCP6 host reservations, one can provide
either an IPv6 address or a delegated prefix as the \fBip\-address\fP parameter.
Please notice that this command does not take prefix length as a parameter in the
current implementation. Thus, searching by an IP address \fB2001:db8:2:cafe::\fP
can return host reservations with delegated prefixes of different lengths matching
this search. For example: \fB2001:db8:2:cafe::/63\fP, \fB2001:db8:2:cafe::/64\fP, etc.
Please consider the example below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-address\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2:cafa::\(dq
    },
    \(dqservice\(dq: [
        \(dqdhcp6\(dq
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Response:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06:07:88:98:fa\(dq,
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoo.example.com\(dq,
                \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [
                    \(dq2001:db8:1:cafe::2\(dq
                ],
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqprefixes\(dq: [
                    \(dq2001:db8:2:abcd::/64\(dq,
                    \(dq2001:db8:2:cafa::/63\(dq
                ],
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 8
            },
            {
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [],
                \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06:07:88:98:fb\(dq,
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoo.example.com\(dq,
                \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [
                    \(dq2001:db8:1:cafe::2\(dq
                ],
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [],
                \(dqprefixes\(dq: [
                    \(dq2001:db8:2:abcd::/64\(dq,
                    \(dq2001:db8:2:cafa::/64\(dq
                ],
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 8
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv6 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it gets the
reservation from both the in memory storage and the hosts database.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-address\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.200.181\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: \(dqdatabase\(dq
    },
    \(dqservice\(dq: [
        \(dqdhcp4\(dq
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This command is useful in specific cases. By default, having more than
one host reservation for the same IP address in a given subnet is not allowed,
as explained in
Multiple Reservations for the Same IPv4 \%<#\:multiple-reservations-same-ip4>
or
Multiple Reservations for the Same IPv6 \%<#\:multiple-reservations-same-ip6>\&.
That\(aqs why this command comes in handy
when the \fBip\-reservations\-unique\fP boolean parameter is set to \fBfalse\fP\&.
Another use case of this command is having overlapping subnets with
the same IP address reservations (but with different identifiers) in different
subnets.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBreservation\-get\-by\-hostname\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-get\-by\-hostname\fP can be used to query the host database and
retrieve all reservations with a specified hostname or in
a specified subnet. This command uses parameters providing the mandatory
\fBhostname\fP and the optional \fBsubnet\-id\fP\&. Global host reservations
can be retrieved by using a \fBsubnet\-id\fP value of zero (0).
Hostname matching is case\-insensitive.
.sp
For instance, retrieving host reservations for \(dqfoobar\(dq in the subnet 1:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-hostname\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoobar.example.org\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
     }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
returns some IPv4 hosts:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoobar.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoobar.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:ff\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.200\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq
            },
            ...
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq5 IPv4 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response returned by \fBreservation\-get\-by\-hostname\fP can be long,
particularly when responses are not limited to a subnet.
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it gets the
reservation from both the in memory storage and the hosts database.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-hostname\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoobar.example.org\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: \(dqdatabase\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information, see The reservation\-get\-by\-hostname Command\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When using MySQL as the host backend, this command relies on the fact
that the hostname column in the hosts table uses a case\-insensitive
collation, as explained in the MySQL \%<#\:mysql-database> section of
Kea Database Administration \%<#\:admin>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBreservation\-get\-by\-id\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-get\-by\-id\fP can be used to query the host database and
retrieve all reservations with a specified identifier (\fBidentifier\-type\fP
and \fBidentifier\fP parameters), independently of subnets. The syntax for
parameters is the same as for \fBreservation\-get\fP\&.
The \fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter cannot be used, to avoid confusion.
This command is available since Kea version 1.9.0.
.sp
For instance, retrieving host reservations for the 01:02:03:04:05:06 MAC
address:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-id\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqhw\-address\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq
     }
 }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
returns some IPv4 hosts:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhosts\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoo.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.100\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 123
            },
            {
                \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dqbootfile.efi\(dq,
                \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqbar.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.200\(dq,
                \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.0.2\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
                \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqserver\-hostname.example.org\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 345
            },
            ...
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq5 IPv4 host(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response returned by \fBreservation\-get\-by\-id\fP can be long,
particularly when responses are not limited to a subnet.
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it gets the
reservation from both the in memory storage and the hosts database.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\-by\-id\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqhw\-address\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: \(dqdatabase\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more information, see The reservation\-get\-by\-id Command\&.
.SS The \fBreservation\-del\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-del\fP can be used to delete a reservation from the host
database and/or JSON configuration. This command supports two types of parameters:
(\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBaddress\fP) or (\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBidentifier\-type\fP, \fBidentifier\fP). The
first type of query is used when the address (either IPv4 or IPv6) is
known, but the details of the reservation are not. One common use for
this type of query is to remove a reservation (e.g. a specific
address should no longer be reserved). The second query uses identifiers.
For maximum flexibility, Kea stores the host identifying information as
a pair of values: the type and the actual identifier. Currently supported
identifiers are \fB\(dqhw\-address\(dq\fP, \fB\(dqduid\(dq\fP, \fB\(dqcircuit\-id\(dq\fP, \fB\(dqclient\-id\(dq\fP, and
\fB\(dqflex\-id\(dq\fP\&. The \fBsubnet\-id\fP is mandatory. Use a value
of zero (0) to delete a global reservation, or the ID of the subnet from
which the reservation should be deleted.
.sp
An example command for deleting a host reservation by (\fBsubnet\-id\fP,
\fBaddress\fP) pair looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example deletion by (\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBidentifier\-type\fP, \fBidentifier\fP) looks as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 4,
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqhw\-address\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Command \fBreservation\-del\fP returns a result of 0 when the host deletion was
successful, or 1 if it failed. Descriptive text is provided in the event of
an error. Here are some examples of possible results:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 1,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHost not deleted (not found).\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHost deleted.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 1,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqUnable to delete a host because there is no hosts\-database configured.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it removes
the reservation from the hosts database only.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 4,
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqhw\-address\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: \(dqmemory\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBreservation\-update\fP Command
.sp
\fBreservation\-update\fP allows for the update of an existing host. It takes the
same set of arguments as \fBreservation\-add\fP, and also
requires a host identifier and a subnet ID to identify the host that is being
updated. The command can be as simple as having only the two mandatory entries:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-update\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In that case, however, it does not assign any resources to the host. An IPv4
address can be assigned as in this example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-update\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f\(dq,
            \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It can also take many more parameters, such as:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-update\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqclient\-id\(dq: \(dq01:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f\(dq,
            \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.205\(dq,
            \(dqnext\-server\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq,
            \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: \(dqhal9000\(dq,
            \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: \(dq/dev/null\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq10.1.1.202,10.1.1.203\(dq
                }
            ],
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
                \(dqoffice\(dq,
                \(dqspecial_snowflake\(dq
            ]
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a complex IPv6 reservation update:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-update\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1,
            \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06:07:08:09:0A\(dq,
            \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [
                \(dq2001:db8:1:cafe::1\(dq
            ],
            \(dqprefixes\(dq: [
                \(dq2001:db8:2:abcd::/64\(dq
            ],
            \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqfoo.example.com\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqvendor\-opts\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq4491\(dq
                },
                {
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqtftp\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqvendor\-4491\(dq,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq3000:1::234\(dq
                }
            ]
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command returns a status that indicates either success (result \fB0\fP) or
failure (result \fB1\fP), and a text parameter that confirms success or explains
the cause of the failure. Here\(aqs an example of a successful update:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHost updated.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And here\(aqs an example of a failure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 1,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqMandatory \(aqsubnet\-id\(aq parameter missing.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command accepts the \fBoperation\-target\fP argument. By default, it adds the
reservation to the hosts database only. As \fBreservation\-update\fP is expected
to store the host, the \fBhosts\-databases\fP parameter must be specified in the
configuration, and databases must not run in read\-only mode if the operation
target is not the JSON configuration.
.sp
As with other update and set commands, this command overwrites all the contents
of the entry. If the host previously had a resource assigned to it, and the
\fBreservation\-update\fP command is missing the resource, it is deleted from the
database. If an incremental update of the host is desired, this can be
achieved by issuing a \fBreservation\-get\-by\-id\fP to get the current state of the
host, selecting the host from the response, modifying it to the required
outcome, and then issuing the \fBreservation\-update\fP command with the resulting
host attached.
.SS General Mentions
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The host backends for the host cache and RADIUS hook libraries do not
respond to commands that return a collection of host reservations, such as
\fBreservation\-get\-all\fP\&. Commands returning one host entry or dedicated host
cache commands should be used instead.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\fP: Lease Commands for Easier Lease Management
.sp
Kea allows users to store lease information in several
backends (memfile, MySQL, and PostgreSQL), and the Lease Commands library provides an
interface that can manipulate leases in a unified, safe way.
In particular, it allows things that were previously impossible: lease
manipulation in memfile while Kea is running, sanity check changes,
lease existence checks, and removal of all leases belonging to a
specific subnet. The hook library can also catch more obscure errors, like an attempt
to add a lease with a \fBsubnet\-id\fP that does not exist in the
configuration, or configuring a lease to use an address that is outside
of the subnet to which it is supposed to belong. The library also
provides a non\-programmatic way to manage user contexts associated with
leases.
.sp
As of Kea 2.7.7, the library also provides support for \fBbinding\-variables\fP\&. Binding
variables allow the user to store custom values for each lease. They are discussed here:
Binding Variables\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or the
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
There are many situations where an administrative command may be useful;
for example, during migration between servers or different vendors, when
a certain network is being retired, or when a device has been
disconnected and the system administrator knows that it will not be coming
back. The \fBget\fP queries may be useful for automating certain management
and monitoring tasks, and they can also act as preparatory steps for lease
updates and removals.
.sp
This library provides the following commands:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-add\fP \- adds a new IPv4 lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-add\fP \- adds a new IPv6 lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-bulk\-apply\fP \- creates, updates, and/or deletes multiple
IPv6 leases in a single transaction.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-get\fP \- checks whether an IPv4 lease with the specified
parameters exists and returns it if it does.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-get\fP \- checks whether an IPv6 lease with the specified
parameters exists and returns it if it does.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-get\-all\fP \- returns all IPv4 leases or all IPv4 leases for
the specified subnets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-get\-all\fP \- returns all IPv6 leases or all IPv6 leases for
the specified subnets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-get\-page\fP \- returns a set (\(dqpage\(dq) of leases from the list
of all IPv4 leases in the database. By iterating through the pages it
is possible to retrieve all the leases.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-get\-page\fP \- returns a set (\(dqpage\(dq) of leases from the list
of all IPv6 leases in the database. By iterating through the pages it
is possible to retrieve all the leases.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-get\-by\-hw\-address\fP \- returns all IPv4 leases with the specified
hardware address.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-get\-by\-hw\-address\fP \- returns all IPv6 leases with the specified
hardware address.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-get\-by\-client\-id\fP \- returns all IPv4 leases with the specified
\fBclient\-id\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-get\-by\-duid\fP \- returns all IPv6 leases with the specified DUID.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-get\-by\-state\fP \- returns all IPv4 leases with the specified state.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-get\-by\-state\fP \- returns all IPv6 leases with the specified state.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-get\-by\-hostname\fP \- returns all IPv4 leases with the specified
hostname.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-get\-by\-hostname\fP \- returns all IPv6 leases with the specified
hostname.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-del\fP \- deletes an IPv4 lease with the specified parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-del\fP \- deletes an IPv6 lease with the specified parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-update\fP \- updates (replaces) an existing IPv4 lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-update\fP \- updates (replaces) an existing IPv6 lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-wipe\fP \- removes all leases from a specific IPv4 subnet or
from all subnets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-wipe\fP \- removes all leases from a specific IPv6 subnet or
from all subnets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-resend\-ddns\fP \- resends a request to update DNS entries for
an existing lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-resend\-ddns\fP \- resends a request to update DNS entries for
an existing lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4\-write\fP \- writes the IPv4 memfile lease database into a file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6\-write\fP \- writes the IPv6 memfile lease database into a file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool4\-rebuild\fP \- rebuilds an IPv4 SFLQ pool in the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool6\-rebuild\fP \- rebuilds an IPv6 SFLQ pool in the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-all\fP \- fetches all IPv4 SFLQ pools from the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-all\fP \- fetches all IPv6 SFLQ pools from the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-subnet\fP \- fetches all IPv4 SFLQ pools that belong to
a subnet from the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-subnet\fP \- fetches all IPv6 SFLQ pools that belong to
a subnet from the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-range\fP \- fetches all IPv4 SFLQ pools that overlap an
address range from the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-range\fP \- fetches all IPv6 SFLQ pools that overlap an
address range from the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool4\-del\fP \- deletes an IPv4 SFLQ pool that matches an address
range from the lease back end.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsflq\-pool6\-del\fP \- deletes an IPv6 SFLQ pool that matches an address
range from the lease back end.
.UNINDENT
.sp
All commands use JSON syntax and can be issued either using the control
channel (see Management API \%<#\:ctrl-channel>).
.sp
The library can be loaded in the same way as other hook libraries, and
it does not take any parameters. It supports both the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6
servers.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBlease4\-add\fP, \fBlease6\-add\fP Commands
.sp
The \fBlease4\-add\fP and
\fBlease6\-add\fP commands allow a new lease
to be created. Typically Kea creates a lease when it first sees a new
device; however, sometimes it may be convenient to create the lease
manually. The \fBlease4\-add\fP command requires at least two parameters:
an IPv4 address and an identifier, i.e. hardware (MAC) address. A third
parameter, \fBsubnet\-id\fP, is optional. If the \fBsubnet\-id\fP is not specified or
the specified value is 0, Kea tries to determine the value by running
a subnet\-selection procedure. If specified, however, its value must
match the existing subnet. The simplest successful call might look as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq,
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBlease6\-add\fP command requires three parameters: an IPv6 address,
an IAID value (identity association identifier, a value sent by
clients), and a DUID. As with \fBlease4\-add\fP, the \fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter is
optional. If the \fBsubnet\-id\fP is not specified or the provided value is 0,
Kea tries to determine the value by running a subnet\-selection
procedure. If specified, however, its value must match the existing
subnet. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 66,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::3\(dq,
        \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f:20:21:22:23:24\(dq,
        \(dqiaid\(dq: 1234
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBlease6\-add\fP command can also be used to add leases for IPv6 prefixes.
In this case there are three additional parameters that must be specified:
\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBtype\fP (set to \(dqIA_PD\(dq), and prefix length. The actual
prefix is set using the \fBip\-address\fP field. Note that Kea cannot guess
\fBsubnet\-id\fP values for prefixes; they must be specified explicitly. For
example, to configure a lease for prefix 2001:db8:abcd::/48, the
following command can be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 66,
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:abcd::\(dq,
        \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 48,
        \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f:20:21:22:23:24\(dq,
        \(dqiaid\(dq: 1234
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The commands can take several additional optional parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvalid\-lft\fP \- specifies the lifetime of the lease, expressed in
seconds. If not specified, the value configured in the subnet related
to the specified \fBsubnet\-id\fP is used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexpire\fP \- creates a timestamp of the lease expiration time,
expressed in UNIX format (seconds since 1 Jan 1970). If not
specified, the default value is the current time plus the lease lifetime (the value
of \fBvalid\-lft\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfqdn\-fwd\fP \- specifies whether the lease should be marked as if a
forward DNS update were conducted. This only affects the
data stored in the lease database, and no DNS update will be
performed. If configured, a DNS update to remove the A or AAAA
records will be conducted when the lease is removed due to expiration
or being released by a client. If not specified, the default value is
\fBfalse\fP\&. The hostname parameter must be specified if \fBfqdn\-fwd\fP is set to
\fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfqdn\-rev\fP \- specifies whether the lease should be marked as if
reverse DNS update were conducted. This only affects the
data stored in the lease database, and no DNS update will be
performed.. If configured, a DNS update to remove the PTR record will
be conducted when the lease is removed due to expiration or being
released by a client. If not specified, the default value is \fBfalse\fP\&.
The hostname parameter must be specified if \fBfqdn\-fwd\fP is set to \fBtrue\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhostname\fP \- specifies the hostname to be associated with this
lease. Its value must be non\-empty if either \fBfqdn\-fwd\fP or \fBfqdn\-rev\fP are
set to \fBtrue\fP\&. If not specified, the default value is an empty string.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhw\-address\fP \- optionally specifies a hardware (MAC) address for an
IPv6 lease. It is a mandatory parameter for an IPv4 lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-id\fP \- optionally specifies a client identifier for an IPv4
lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpreferred\-lft\fP \- optionally specifies a preferred lifetime for
IPv6 leases. If not specified, the value configured for the subnet
corresponding to the specified \fBsubnet\-id\fP is used. This parameter is
not used when adding an IPv4 lease.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstate\fP \- specifies the state of an added lease, which can be 0 for \fBdefault\fP,
1 for \fBdeclined\fP, 2 for \fBexpired\-reclaimed\fP, and 3 for the \fBreleased\fP state.
Any other value causes an error. Using 1 for a \fB\(dqIA_PD\(dq\fP lease type is
illegal and will be rejected.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuser\-context\fP \- specifies the user context to be associated with
this lease. It must be a JSON map.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a fairly complex lease addition:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 66,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::3\(dq,
        \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04:05:06:07:08\(dq,
        \(dqiaid\(dq: 1234,
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f\(dq,
        \(dqpreferred\-lft\(dq: 500,
        \(dqvalid\-lft\(dq: 1000,
        \(dqexpire\(dq: 12345678,
        \(dqfqdn\-fwd\(dq: true,
        \(dqfqdn\-rev\(dq: true,
        \(dqstate\(dq: 0,
        \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqurania.example.org\(dq,
        \(dquser\-context\(dq: { \(dqversion\(dq: 1 }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command returns a status that indicates either success (result 0)
or failure (result 1). A failed command always includes a text
parameter that explains the cause of failure. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 0, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqLease added.\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Example failure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 1, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqmissing parameter \(aqip\-address\(aq (<string>:3:19)\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBlease6\-bulk\-apply\fP Command
.sp
The \fBlease6\-bulk\-apply\fP was implemented to address
the performance penalty in High\-Availability mode when a single DHCPv6
transaction resulted in multiple lease updates sent to the partner, if
multiple address and/or prefix leases were allocated. Consider the case
when a DHCPv6 client requests the assignment of two IPv6 addresses and two IPv6
prefixes: it may result in the allocation of four leases. In addition,
DHCPv6 may assign a different address than the one requested by the client during
the renew or rebind stage, and delete the leases previously used by this client.
There are six lease changes sent between the HA partners in this case.
Sending these updates as individual commands, e.g. via \fBlease6\-update\fP,
is highly inefficient and produces unnecessary delays in communication,
both between the HA partners and in sending the response to the DHCPv6 client.
.sp
The \fBlease6\-bulk\-apply\fP command deals with this
problem by aggregating all lease changes \- both deleted leases and
new or updated leases \- in a single command.
The receiving server iterates over the deleted leases and deletes them
from its lease database. Next, it iterates over the new/updated leases
and adds them to the database or updates them if they already exist.
.sp
Even though High Availability is the major application for
this command, it can be freely used in all cases when it is desirable to
send multiple lease changes in a single command.
.sp
In the following example, we delete two leases and add
or update two other leases in the database:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
 {
   \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-bulk\-apply\(dq,
   \(dqarguments\(dq: {
       \(dqdeleted\-leases\(dq: [
           {
               \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:abcd::\(dq,
               \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
               ...
           },
           {
               \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:abcd::234\(dq,
               \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_NA\(dq,
               ...
           }
       ],
       \(dqleases\(dq: [
           {
               \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 66,
               \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:cafe::\(dq,
               \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
                ...
           },
           {
               \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 66,
               \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:abcd::333\(dq,
               \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_NA\(dq,
               ...
           }
       ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If any of the leases are malformed, no lease changes are applied
to the lease database. If the leases are well\-formed but there is a
failure to apply any of the lease changes to the database, the command
continues to be processed for other leases. All the leases for which
the command was unable to apply the changes in the database are
listed in the response. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqBulk apply of 2 IPv6 leases completed\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqfailed\-deleted\-leases\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:abcd::\(dq,
                \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
                \(dqresult\(dq: 3,
                \(dqerror\-message\(dq: \(dqno lease found\(dq
            }
        ],
        \(dqfailed\-leases\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:cafe::\(dq,
                \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
                \(dqresult\(dq: 1,
                \(dqerror\-message\(dq: \(dqunable to communicate with the lease database\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response above indicates that the hook library was unable to
delete the lease for prefix \(dq2001:db8:abcd::\(dq and add or update the lease
for prefix \(dq2001:db8:cafe::\(dq. However, there are two other lease changes
which have been applied as indicated by the text message. The
\fBresult\fP is the status constant that indicates the type
of the error experienced for the particular lease. The meanings of the
returned codes are the same as the results returned for the commands.
In particular, the result of 1 indicates an error while processing the
lease, e.g. a communication error with the database. The result of 3
indicates that an attempt to delete the lease was unsuccessful because
such a lease doesn\(aqt exist (an empty result).
.SS The \fBlease4\-get\fP, \fBlease6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
\fBlease4\-get\fP and \fBlease6\-get\fP can be used to query the lease database
and retrieve existing leases. There are two types of parameters the
\fBlease4\-get\fP command supports: (\fBaddress\fP) or (\fBsubnet\-id\fP,
\fBidentifier\-type\fP, \fBidentifier\fP). There are also two types for
\fBlease6\-get\fP: (\fBaddress\fP, \fBtype\fP) or (\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBidentifier\-type\fP,
\fBidentifier\fP, \fBIAID\fP, \fBtype\fP). The first type of query is used when the
address (either IPv4 or IPv6) is known, but the details of the lease are
not; one common use case of this type of query is to find out whether a
given address is being used. The second query uses identifiers;
currently supported identifiers for leases are: \fB\(dqhw\-address\(dq\fP (IPv4
only), \fB\(dqclient\-id\(dq\fP (IPv4 only), and \fB\(dqduid\(dq\fP (IPv6 only).
.sp
An example \fBlease4\-get\fP command for getting a lease using an IPv4
address is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example of the \fBlease6\-get\fP query is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-get\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1234:ab::\(dq,
    \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example query by \fB\(dqhw\-address\(dq\fP for an IPv4 lease looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqhw\-address\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq08:08:08:08:08:08\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example query by \fB\(dqclient\-id\(dq\fP for an IPv4 lease looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqclient\-id\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq01:01:02:03:04:05:06\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example query by (\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBidentifier\-type\fP, \fBidentifier\fP, \fBiaid\fP, \fBtype\fP)
for an IPv6 lease is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqduid\(dq,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq08:08:08:08:08:08\(dq,
        \(dqiaid\(dq: 1234567,
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_NA\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBtype\fP is an optional parameter. Supported values are: \fBIA_NA\fP
(non\-temporary address) and \fBIA_PD\fP (IPv6 prefix). If not specified, \fBIA_NA\fP
is assumed.
.sp
\fBlease4\-get\fP and \fBlease6\-get\fP return an indication of the result of the operation
and lease details, if found. The result has one of the following values: 0
(success), 1 (error), or 3 (empty). An empty result means that a query
has been completed properly, but the object (a lease in this case) has
not been found.
The lease parameters, if found, are returned as arguments.
\fBclient\-id\fP is not returned if empty.
.sp
An example result returned when the host was found:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-id\(dq: \(dq42:42:42:42:42:42:42:42\(dq,
    \(dqcltt\(dq: 12345678,
    \(dqfqdn\-fwd\(dq: false,
    \(dqfqdn\-rev\(dq: true,
    \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqmyhost.example.com.\(dq,
    \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq08:08:08:08:08:08\(dq,
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq,
    \(dqstate\(dq: 0,
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44,
    \(dqvalid\-lft\(dq: 3600
  },
  \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
  \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 lease found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The client last transaction time (\fBcltt\fP field) is bound to the
valid lifetime (\fBvalid\-lft\fP) and to the expire date (not reported
here but stored in databases) by the equation
cltt + valid\e_lft = expire
.sp
at the exception of the infinite valid lifetime coded by the
0xfffffff (4294967295) special value which makes the expire value
to overflow on MySQL and old PostgreSQL backends where timestamps
are 32 bit long. So in these lease databases the expire date is the
same as the cltt i.e.
cltt = expire when valid\e_lft = 4294967295 and the
lease backend is MySQL or PostgreSQL.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBlease4\-get\-all\fP, \fBlease6\-get\-all\fP Commands
.sp
\fBlease4\-get\-all\fP and \fBlease6\-get\-all\fP are used to retrieve all IPv4
or IPv6 leases, or all leases for the specified set of subnets. All
leases are returned when there are no arguments specified with the
command, as in the following example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\-all\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If arguments are provided, it is expected that they contain the
\fB\(dqsubnets\(dq\fP parameter, which is a list of subnet identifiers for which
leases should be returned. For example, to retrieve all IPv6
leases belonging to the subnets with identifiers 1, 2, 3, and 4:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [ 1, 2, 3, 4 ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a detailed list of leases in the
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqleases\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcltt\(dq: 12345678,
                \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq42:42:42:42:42:42:42:42\(dq,
                \(dqfqdn\-fwd\(dq: false,
                \(dqfqdn\-rev\(dq: true,
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqmyhost.example.com.\(dq,
                \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq08:08:08:08:08:08\(dq,
                \(dqiaid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::1\(dq,
                \(dqpreferred\-lft\(dq: 500,
                \(dqstate\(dq: 0,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44,
                \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_NA\(dq,
                \(dqvalid\-lft\(dq: 3600
            },
            {
                \(dqcltt\(dq: 12345678,
                \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq21:21:21:21:21:21:21:21\(dq,
                \(dqfqdn\-fwd\(dq: false,
                \(dqfqdn\-rev\(dq: true,
                \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dq\(dq,
                \(dqiaid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:0:0:2::\(dq,
                \(dqpreferred\-lft\(dq: 500,
                \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 80,
                \(dqstate\(dq: 0,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44,
                \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
                \(dqvalid\-lft\(dq: 3600
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv6 lease(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBlease4\-get\-all\fP and
\fBlease6\-get\-all\fP commands may result in
very large responses. This may have a negative impact on the DHCP
server\(aqs responsiveness while the response is generated and
transmitted over the control channel, as the server imposes no
restriction on the number of leases returned as a result of this
command.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBlease4\-get\-page\fP, \fBlease6\-get\-page\fP Commands
.sp
The \fBlease4\-get\-all\fP and
\fBlease6\-get\-all\fP commands may result in
very large responses; generating such a response may consume CPU
bandwidth as well as memory. It may even cause the server to become
unresponsive. In the case of large lease databases it is usually better to
retrieve leases in chunks, using the paging mechanism.
\fBlease4\-get\-page\fP and \fBlease6\-get\-page\fP implement a paging mechanism
for DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 servers, respectively. The following command
retrieves the first 1024 IPv4 leases:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\-page\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqfrom\(dq: \(dqstart\(dq,
        \(dqlimit\(dq: 1024
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The keyword \fBstart\fP denotes that the first page of leases should be
retrieved. Alternatively, an IPv4 zero address can be specified to
retrieve the first page:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\-page\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqfrom\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq,
        \(dqlimit\(dq: 1024
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Similarly, the IPv6 zero address can be specified in the
\fBlease6\-get\-page\fP command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-get\-page\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqfrom\(dq: \(dq::\(dq,
        \(dqlimit\(dq: 6
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqleases\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::1\(dq,
                ...
            },
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::9\(dq,
                ...
            },
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:3::1\(dq,
                ...
            },
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:5::3\(dq,
                ...
            },
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:4::1\(dq,
                ...
            },
            {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::7\(dq,
                ...
            },
            ...
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 6
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq6 IPv6 lease(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that the leases\(aq details were excluded from the response above for
brevity.
.sp
Generally, the returned list is not sorted in any particular order. Some
lease database backends may sort leases in ascending order of addresses,
but the controlling client must not rely on this behavior.
.sp
The \fBcount\fP parameter contains the number of returned leases on the
page.
.sp
To fetch the next page, the client must use the last address of the
current page as an input to the next \fBlease4\-get\-page\fP or
\fBlease6\-get\-page\fP command call. In this example it is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-get\-page\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqfrom\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::7\(dq,
        \(dqcount\(dq: 6
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
because 2001:db8:2::7 is the last address on the current page.
.sp
The client may assume that it has reached the last page when the
\fBcount\fP value is lower than that specified in the command; this
includes the case when the \fBcount\fP is equal to 0, meaning that no
leases were found.
.SS The \fBlease4\-get\-by\-*\fP, \fBlease6\-get\-by\-*\fP Commands
.sp
\fBlease4\-get\-by\-*\fP and \fBlease6\-get\-by\-*\fP can be used to query the lease database and
retrieve all existing leases matching a given feature (denoted by the \fB*\fP). These
can include a specified hardware address (IPv4
only), \fBclient\-id\fP IPv4 only), \fBduid\fP (IPv6 only) identifiers, or hostname.
.sp
An example \fBlease4\-get\-by\-hw\-address\fP command for getting IPv4 leases
with a given hardware address is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-get\-by\-hw\-address\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq08:08:08:08:08:08\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example of the \fBlease6\-get\-by\-hostname\fP is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-get\-by\-hostname\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqmyhost.example.org\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBby\fP key is the only parameter. The returned response contains a detailed
list of leases in the same format as \fBlease4\-get\-all\fP or \fBlease6\-get\-all\fP\&. This list can be
empty and is usually not large.
.sp
Since Kea version 3.1.5 the \fBby\-state\fP commands were added with:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
the \fBstate\fP parameter also accepts a string (vs. a number) for
usual state names (default (or assigned) (0), declined (1),
expired\-reclaimed (2), released (3), and registered (4)).
.IP \(bu 2
an optional subnet\-id parameter limits to leases in the specified subnet.
When omitted or set to 0 all leases with the given state are returned.
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBlease4\-del\fP, \fBlease6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
\fBlease4\-del\fP and \fBlease6\-del\fP can be used to delete a lease from the lease database.
There are two types of parameters these commands support, similar to the
\fBlease4\-get\fP and \fBlease6\-get\fP commands: (\fBaddress\fP) for both v4 and v6, (\fBsubnet\-id\fP,
\fBidentifier\-type\fP, \fBidentifier\fP) for v4, and (\fBsubnet\-id\fP, \fBidentifier\-type\fP,
\fBidentifier\fP, \fBtype\fP, \fBIAID\fP) for v6. The first type of query is used when the
address (either IPv4 or IPv6) is known, but the details of the lease are
not. One common use case is where an administrator wants a specified
address to no longer be used. The second form of the command uses
identifiers. For maximum flexibility, this interface uses identifiers as
a pair of values: the type and the actual identifier. The currently
supported identifiers are \fB\(dqhw\-address\(dq\fP (IPv4 only), \fB\(dqclient\-id\(dq\fP (IPv4
only), and \fB\(dqduid\(dq\fP (IPv6 only).
.sp
An example command for deleting an IPv4 lease by address is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example IPv4 lease deletion by \fB\(dqhw\-address\(dq\fP is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-del\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqidentifier\(dq: \(dq08:08:08:08:08:08\(dq,
    \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: \(dqhw\-address\(dq,
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The IPv6 address leases are deleted the same way, but using \fBlease6\-del\fP\&. The
IPv6 prefix leases are also deleted using \fBlease6\-del\fP, but with some extra
steps. The prefix should be referenced by its address and prefix length should be ignored.
For example, to delete 2001:db8:1::/48, the following command can be used. The
\fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter is optional.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::\(dq,
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Another parameter called \fBupdate\-ddns\fP, when \fBtrue\fP, instructs the server to
queue a request to \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> to remove DNS entries after the lease is
successfully deleted if:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
DDNS updating is enabled (i.e. \fB\(dqdhcp\-ddns\(dq:{ \(dqenable\-updates\(dq: true }\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
The lease\(aqs hostname is not empty.
.IP \(bu 2
At least one of the lease\(aqs DNS direction flags (\fBfqdn_fwd\fP or \fBfqdn_rev\fP) is true.
.UNINDENT
.sp
This parameter defaults to \fBfalse\fP\&. An example of its use is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.202\(dq,
        \(dqupdate\-ddns\(dq: true
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Commands \fBlease4\-del\fP and \fBlease6\-del\fP return a result that indicates the outcome
of the operation. It has one of the following values: 0 (success), 1 (error),
or 3 (empty). The empty result means that a query has been completed properly,
but the object (a lease, in this case) has not been found.
.SS The \fBlease4\-update\fP, \fBlease6\-update\fP Commands
.sp
The \fBlease4\-update\fP and
\fBlease6\-update\fP commands can be used to
update existing leases. Since all lease database backends are indexed by
IP addresses, it is not possible to update an address, but all other
fields may be altered. If an address needs to be changed, please use
\fBlease4\-del\fP / \fBlease6\-del\fP followed by \fBlease4\-add\fP / \fBlease6\-add\fP\&.
.sp
The \fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter is optional. If not specified, or if the
specified value is 0, Kea tries to determine its value by running a
subnet\-selection procedure. If specified, however, its value must match
the existing subnet.
.sp
The optional boolean parameter \fB\(dqforce\-create\(dq\fP specifies whether the
lease should be created if it does not exist in the database. It defaults
to \fBfalse\fP, which indicates that the lease is not created if it does not
exist. In such a case, an error is returned when trying to
update a non\-existing lease. If the \fB\(dqforce\-create\(dq\fP parameter is set to
\fBtrue\fP and the updated lease does not exist, the new lease is created as a
result of receiving the \fBlease4\-update\fP / \fBlease6\-update\fP command.
.sp
An example of a command to update an IPv4 lease is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-update\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq,
    \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqnewhostname.example.org\(dq,
    \(dqhw\-address\(dq: \(dq1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f\(dq,
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44,
    \(dqforce\-create\(dq: true
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example of a command to update an IPv6 lease is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-update\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::1\(dq,
    \(dqduid\(dq: \(dq88:88:88:88:88:88:88:88\(dq,
    \(dqiaid\(dq: 7654321,
    \(dqhostname\(dq: \(dqnewhostname.example.org\(dq,
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 66,
    \(dqforce\-create\(dq: false
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As with other update commands, this command overwrites all the contents of the
entry. If the lease previously had a resource assigned to it, and the
\fBlease4\-update\fP / \fBlease6\-update\fP command is missing the resource, it is
deleted from the lease database. If an incremental update of the lease is
desired, then this can be achieved by issuing a \fBlease4\-get\fP / \fBlease6\-get\fP
command to get the current state of the lease, selecting the lease from the
response, modifying it to the required outcome, and then issuing the
\fBlease4\-update\fP / \fBlease6\-update\fP command with the resulting lease attached.
.SS The \fBlease4\-wipe\fP, \fBlease6\-wipe\fP Commands
.sp
\fBlease4\-wipe\fP and \fBlease6\-wipe\fP are designed to remove all leases
associated with a given subnet. This administrative task is expected to
be used when an existing subnet is being retired. The leases
are not properly expired; no DNS updates are carried out, no log
messages are created, and hooks are not called for the leases being
removed.
.sp
An example of \fBlease4\-wipe\fP is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-wipe\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 44
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example of \fBlease6\-wipe\fP is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-wipe\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 66
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The commands return a text description of the number of leases removed,
plus the status code 0 (success) if any leases were removed or 3 (empty)
if there were no leases. Status code 1 (error) may be returned if the
parameters are incorrect or some other exception is encountered.
.sp
\fBsubnet\-id\fP 0 has a special meaning; it tells Kea to delete leases from
all configured subnets. Also, the \fBsubnet\-id\fP parameter may be omitted. If
not specified, leases from all subnets are wiped.
.sp
Note: currently only memfile lease storage supports this command.
.SS The \fBlease4\-resend\-ddns\fP, \fBlease6\-resend\-ddns\fP Commands
.sp
\fBlease4\-resend\-ddns\fP and \fBlease6\-resend\-ddns\fP can be used to generate
a request to \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns> to update the DNS entries for an existing
lease.  The desired lease is selected by a single parameter, \fB\(dqip\-address\(dq\fP\&.
For an update request to be generated, DDNS updating must be enabled
and DNS entries must have already been made (or attempted) for the lease.
In other words, all of the following must be true:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
DDNS updating must be enabled (i.e. \fB\(dqdhcp\-ddns\(dq:{ \(dqenable\-updates\(dq: true\(dq}\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
The lease\(aqs hostname must not be empty.
.IP \(bu 2
At least one of the lease\(aqs DNS direction flags (\fBfqdn_fwd\fP or \fBfqdn_rev\fP) must be true.
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example \fBlease4\-resend\-ddns\fP command for getting a lease using an IPv4
address is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease4\-resend\-ddns\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example of the \fBlease6\-resend\-ddns\fP query is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlease6\-resend\-ddns\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Commands \fBlease4\-resend\-ddns\fP and \fBlease6\-resend\-ddns\fP return an indication of the
result of the operation. It has one of the following values: 0 (success), 1 (error),
or 3 (empty). An empty result means that a query has been completed properly, but the
object (a lease in this case) has not been found.
.sp
A successful result does not mean that DNS has been successfully updated; it
indicates that a request to update DNS has been successfully created and
queued for transmission to \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>\&.
.sp
Here\(aqs an example of a result returned when the lease was found:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
  \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqNCR generated for: 2001:db8:1::1, hostname: example.com.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBlease4\-write\fP, \fBlease6\-write\fP Commands
.sp
\fBlease4\-write\fP and \fBlease6\-write\fP can be used for recovery in emergency
situations where the memfile lease file is damaged, e.g. removed by
accident or truncated by a full file system, but the in\-memory database
is still valid. These commands are supported only by the memfile database
backend and write the lease database into a CSV file. They take the path
of the file as the \fBfilename\fP argument. If the specified output file
is the same as the configured memfile one, the backend closes and reopens
the file in an attempt to synchronize both the files and the in\-memory images
of the lease database. The extension \fB\&.bak\fP and the server PID number are added
to the previous filename: for example, \fB\&.bak14326\fP\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, the lease file may only be written to the data directory
determined during compilation: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/lib/kea\(dq\fP\&. This
path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_DHCP_DATA_DIR\fP to the desired path.  If a path other than
this value is used in \fBfilename\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start
or, if already running, log an unrecoverable error.  For ease of use in
specifying a custom file name simply omit the path portion from \fBfilename\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
These commands do not replace the LFC mechanism; they should be used
only in exceptional circumstances, such as when recovering after
running out of disk space.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Commands for Managing SFLQ Pools
.sp
The following commands can be used to manage the SFLQ data (e.g. pools and
free leases) that exists in the lease back end for subnets that use
the Shared FLQ Allocator. Under normal circumstances this information
is created and maintained automatically by the SFLQ Allocator mechanisms.
With these API commands it is possible view, (re)create, and delete
SFLQ pools.
.sp
First, it is important to understand that SFLQ pools are uniquely identified
by their start and end address. For any valid IPv4 or IPv6 range, there can
only be one pool. Pools can however, have overlapping ranges.
.sp
Commands that fetch pools, return the following values for each pool:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstart\-address\fP \- start address of the pool
.IP \(bu 2
\fBend\-address\fP \- end address of the pool
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet\-id\fP \- subnet ID to which the pool was created
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease\-type\fP \- for IPv4 it is always \(dqV4\(dq, for IPv6 is is either \(dqIA_NA\(dq or \(dqIA_PD\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdelegated\-len\fP \- for \(dqV4\(dq and \(dqIA_NA\(dq pools it is always 128.  For \(dqIA_PD\(dq pools
it is the delegated prefix length.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcreated\-ts\fP  \- time the pool was created.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmodified\-ts\fP \- time the pool was last updated. It is initially set when the
pool creation completes. Thereafter it is updated each time SFLQ allocation
picks a free lease from the pool.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfree\-leases\fP the number of currently free addresses in the pool.
.UNINDENT
.sp
There at least three cases where these commands are likely to prove most
useful. First, as a means to look for and remove obsolete pools. Because
the pools can be be shared by an arbitrary number of servers, none of whom
can know for certain when a pool is no longer needed, the SFLQ data for a pool
cannot be automatically removed when its allocation scheme is changed to
something other than SFLQ Allocator. While inactive pools should not cause
servers to malfunction they do consume data and index space within the lease
back end.
.sp
The second case is that of mixed allocation schemes. In order for SFLQ
Allocation to work properly, all servers allocating from a given pool (i.e.
address or prefix range), must use SFLQ. If some servers do while others
do not, the SFLQ data can quickly become inaccurate. This can be remedied
by stopping any servers that are not using SFLQ for the pool(s) in question
and rebuilding the pool(s) using the sflq\-pool*\-rebuild commands, changing
those servers to use SFLQ for the pool(s) and then restarting them.
.sp
The third use case is that of overlapping pools, (i.e. pools whose
address/prefix ranges overlap each other). Kea supports this as deliberate
configuration choice but it may also occur inadvertently when configuration
changes are not made consistently across multiple servers. Fetching the SFLQ
pools by an address range will fetch all SFLQ pools that overlap the input
range.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Creating or deleting SFLQ pools while servers are actively allocating
from their address ranges could lead to inconsistent results and is generally
not recommended.  Commands that fetch SFLQ pools may be safely used at anytime.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsflq\-pool4\-rebuild\fP, \fBsflq\-pool6\-rebuild\fP Commands
.sp
\fBsflq\-pool4\-rebuild\fP and \fBsflq\-pool6\-rebuild\fP can be used to
rebuild the free lease data for existing SFLQ pools.  This is useful in cases
where one or more servers have been using other allocator schemes for
the address ranges that include SFLQ pools. Issuing the command will cause
the lease back end to discard and then rebuild the free lease data for the
given pool. Only a pool that exactly matches the start and end adddress
given will be rebuilt.
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool4\-rebuild\fP command for rebuilding an IPv4 SFLQ
Pool is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool4\-rebuild\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0\(dq,
        \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.255\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool6\-rebuild\fP command for rebuilding an IPv6
SFLQ pool for an address pool is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool6\-rebuild\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::\(dq,
        \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::FFFF\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
For large pools (e.g. >= 1 million address/prefixes), this command can take
twenty seconds or more. During this time the server will not process
client queries. Make sure the tool with which you send the commands
is configured for long time outs. Once submitted to the lease back
end the query will continue to run, even if the API client disconnects.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command returns a status that indicates either success (result 0)
or failure (result 1). A failed command always includes a text
parameter that explains the cause of failure. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 0, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqSFLQ pool rebuilt.\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-all\fP, \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-all\fP Commands
.sp
\fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-all\fP and \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-all\fP can be used
to fetch a list of all IPv4 or IPv6 SFLQ pools.  They take no arguments.
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-all\fP command for fetching IPv4 SFLQ
pools:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool4\-get\-all\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a detailed list of all IPv4 SLFQ pools. In the
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:16:36.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 128,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq178.0.255.255\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 65535,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqV4\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:16:36.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq178.0.0.1\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            },
        {
            \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:18:02.000000\(dq,
            \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 128,
            \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.255\(dq,
            \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 256,
            \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqV4\(dq,
            \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:18:02.000000\(dq,
            \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0\(dq,
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 2
        }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 pool(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-all\fP command for fetching IPv6 SFLQ
pools:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool6\-get\-all\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a detailed list of all IPv6 SLFQ pools. In the
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 13:21:10.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 120,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 256,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 13:21:10.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            },
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 08:40:40.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 128,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq3001::ffff\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 65520,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqIA_NA\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 08:40:41.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq3001::10\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 2
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 pool(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-subnet\fP, \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-subnet\fP Commands
.sp
\fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-subnet\fP and \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-subnet\fP
can be used to fetch a list of all IPv4 or IPv6 SFLQ pools that were
created for a subnet.
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-subnet\fP command for fetching IPv4 SFLQ
pools is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-subnet\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq : 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a detailed list of IPv4 SLFQ pools in
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:18:02.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 128,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.255\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 256,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqV4\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:18:02.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 pool(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-subnet\fP command for fetching IPv6 SFLQ
pools is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-subnet\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq : 100
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a detailed list of IPv6 SLFQ pools in
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 13:21:10.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 120,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 256,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 13:21:10.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 100
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 pool(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-range\fP, \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-range\fP Commands
.sp
\fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-range\fP and \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-range\fP
can be used to fetch a list of all IPv4 or IPv6 SFLQ pools that were
created for a subnet.
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-range\fP command for fetching IPv4 SFLQ
pools is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool4\-get\-by\-range\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq178.0.0.0\(dq,
        \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq178.255.255.255\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a detailed list of IPv4 SLFQ pools in
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:16:36.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 128,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq178.0.255.255\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 65535,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqV4\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 11:16:36.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq178.0.0.1\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 1
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 pool(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-range\fP command for fetching IPv6 SFLQ
pools is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool6\-get\-by\-range\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq3001::\(dq,
        \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq3002::\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a detailed list of IPv6 SLFQ pools in
following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 08:40:40.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 128,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq3001::ffff\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 65520,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqIA_NA\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 08:40:41.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq3001::10\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 3001
            },
            {
                \(dqcreated\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 08:40:41.000000\(dq,
                \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 64,
                \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq3001:20:0:ff:ffff:ffff:ffff:ffff\(dq,
                \(dqfree\-leases\(dq: 256,
                \(dqlease\-type\(dq: \(dqIA_PD\(dq,
                \(dqmodified\-ts\(dq: \(dq2026\-05\-05 08:40:41.000000\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq3001:20::\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 3001
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 pool(s) found.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsflq\-pool4\-del\fP, \fBsflq\-pool6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
\fBsflq\-pool4\-del\fP and \fBsflq\-pool6\-del\fP can be used to delete
an IPv4 or IPv6 SFLQ pool. They require two arguments, \fBstart\-address\fP and
and \fBend\-address\fP\&.  Only a pool whose range matches these values will be
deleted and only if no other SFLQ pools overlap the range described by these
values.  If any overlapping pools are detected the delete will fail with an
error message stating this to be the case. There is an optional \fBforce\fP
parameter can be used too override this safeguard.  When set to \fBtrue\fP the
matching pool will be deleted without checking for overlapping pools.
.sp
Deleting a pool when there are overlapping pools can produce holes in the
free lease data where the overlaps occur. If the overlapping pools are to
remain in use, the \fBsflq\-poolX\-rebuild\fP commands should be used to rebuild the
free lease data for the remaining pools.
.sp
Alternatively, all of the overlapping pools can be deleted and the next
Kea server (re)start or (re)configure will recreate any needed pools
automatically.
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool4\-del\fP command for deleting an IPv4 SFLQ
pool is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10\(dq,
        \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.20\(dq,
        \(dqforce\(dq: false
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response, if there were overlapping pools might look as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 1,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDelete would affect 2 overlapping pools\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And if there were no overlapping pools or \fBforce\fP were set true, and
the matching pool exists, the result would be similar to that shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqSFLQ pool deleted\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example \fBsflq\-pool6\-del\fP command for deleting an IPv6 SFLQ
pool is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsflq\-pool6\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqstart\-address\(dq: \(dq3001::\(dq,
        \(dqend\-address\(dq: \(dq3001::10:FFFF\(dq,
        \(dqforce\(dq: false
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response, if there were overlapping pools might look as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 1,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDelete would affect 3 overlapping pools\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And if there were no overlapping pools or \fBforce\fP were set true, and
the matching pool exists, the result would be similar to that shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqSFLQ pool deleted\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Binding Variables
.sp
Binding variables allow users to store custom values with each lease. The
values are calculated using expressions and stored in the lease\(aqs
\fBuser\-context\fP\&.  The feature is similar to ISC DHCP\(aqs \fBset\fP statement.
.sp
They are configured as parameters for the lease\-cmds hook library.  Each
variable has the following parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B name
The name of the variable that will appear in the \fBuser\-context\fP\&. The
name must be unique within the list of binding variables..
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B expression
The expression used to calculate the variable\(aqs value (see :ref:
\fIclassification\-using\-expressions\fP).
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B source
The packet to use as input for the  expression. It is either \fBquery\fP
(the packet sent by the client) or \fBresponse\fP (the packet the server
is sending in response).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following example would store a user\-defined option sent by the
client along with the domain\-name sent by the server for each DCHPv4
lease:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqbinding\-variables\(dq: [{
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqopt\-222\(dq,
                \(dqexpression\(dq: \(dqhexstring(option[222].hex, \(aq:\(aq)\(dq,
                \(dqsource\(dq: \(dqquery\(dq
            },{
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdomain\-name\(dq,
                \(dqexpression\(dq: \(dqoption[15].text\(dq,
                \(dqsource\(dq: \(dqresponse\(dq
            }]
        }
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The values are stored as name\-value pairs in the \fBISC\fP map in the lease\(aqs \fBuser\-context\fP
contents would look similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqISC\(dq: {
        \(dqbinding\-variables\(dq: [{
            \(dqdomain\-name\(dq: \(dqexample.org\(dq,
            \(dqopt\-222\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04\(dq
        }
    ]}
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When stored in database back ends, the user\-context will not contain line breaks
as shown above and would appear as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqISC\(dq: { \(dqbinding\-variables\(dq: { \(dqdomain\-name\(dq: \(dqexample.org\(dq, \(dqopt\-222\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04\(dq } } }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When using memfile lease storage, the \fBuser\-context\fP is output with commas replaced by
the escape sequence \(dq&#x2c\(dq.  This is necessary to prevent interference with the lease file
parsing.  The example output above would appear as follows in a memfile lease file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqISC\(dq: { \(dqbinding\-variables\(dq: { \(dqdomain\-name\(dq: \(dqexample.org\(dq&#x2c \(dqopt\-222\(dq: \(dq01:02:03:04\(dq } } }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Binding variable values are evaluated whenever a lease is assigned or renewed.
For \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>, they are also added if \fBoffer\-lifetime\fP is greater than zero.
The lease is only updated in the back end if the values have changed.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When used in conjunction with the Ping Check hook library, the
the lease\-cmds hook library should be listed after Ping Check in
the server\(aqs \fBhooks\-libraries\fP section of the configuration.
.sp
When used in conjunction with the HA hook library, the lease\-cmds
hook library must be listed before HA in the server\(aqs \fBhooks\-libraries\fP
section of the configuration.  This ensures that the binding variables
are evaluated before HA sends lease updates to its peer(s).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_lease_query.so\fP: Leasequery Support
.sp
This library provides support for DHCPv4 Leasequery as described in
RFC 4388 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4388>; and for DHCPv6
Leasequery as described in (RFC 5007 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc5007>).
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_lease_query.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Kea version 2.3.4 added support for DHCPv6 Bulk Leasequery
(RFC 5460 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc5460>);
Kea version 2.3.5 added support for DHCPv4 Bulk Leasequery
(RFC 6926 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc6926>) using
the memfile lease backend.
.SS DHCPv4 Leasequery
.sp
DHCPv4 simple Leasequery provides a requester the ability to query for
active lease information for either a single IP address or a single client.
RFC 4388 calls for three such queries:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Query by IP address
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
The IP address of interest is contained within the \fBciaddr\fP field of
the query.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Query by hardware address
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
The hardware address of interest is contained with the \fBchaddr\fP field
of the query.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Query by client identifier
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
The client identifier of interest is sent in the \fBdhcp\-client\-identifier\fP
option (61) of the query.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The inbound DHCPLEASEQUERY packet must supply only one of the three values
above. Queries which supply more than one of these values are dropped.
.sp
In addition, the query must contain the IP address of the requester in
\fBgiaddr\fP\&. This value is used not only as the destination for the
query response but also to validate the requester against a known
list of IP addresses which are permitted to query. This list of valid
requester addresses is specified as part of the Leasequery hook library\(aqs
configuration (see the section on configuration below).
.sp
In response to a valid query, the server returns one of three message
types:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
DHCPLEASEUNKNOWN
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
Returned when the IP address of interest is not one the server knows
about (query by IP address); or there are no active leases for the
client of interest (query by hardware address or client ID).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
DHCPLEASEUNASSIGNED
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
Returned when the IP address is one the server knows of but for which
there are no active leases (applies only to query by IP address).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
DHCPLEASEACTIVE
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
Returned when there is at least one active lease found matching the
criteria.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For both DHCPLEASEUNKNOWN and DHCPLEASEUNASSIGNED responses, the only
information sent back to the requester in response is the query parameter
itself (i.e. one of: IP address, hardware address, or client identifier).
.sp
For DHCPLEASEACTIVE the server provides the following information
for the newest active lease that matches the criteria, in the response:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBciaddr\fP \- set to the lease\(aqs IP address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBchaddr\fP \- set to the lease\(aqs hardware address
.UNINDENT
.sp
In addition, one or more of the following options are included:
.SS DHCPLEASEACTIVE options
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Option
T}	T{
Code
T}	T{
Content
T}
_
T{
dhcp\-client\-identifier
T}	T{
61
T}	T{
copied from the lease (if appropriate)
T}
_
T{
client\-last\-transaction\-time
T}	T{
91
T}	T{
the amount of time that has elapsed since the
lease\(aqs client\-last\-transaction\-time (CLTT).
This value is also used by the server to
adjust lifetime and timer values.
T}
_
T{
dhcp\-lease\-time
T}	T{
51
T}	T{
lease\(aqs lifetime reduced by CLTT
T}
_
T{
dhcp\-renewal\-time
T}	T{
58
T}	T{
as controlled by kea\-dhcp4 configuration and
then reduced by CLTT
T}
_
T{
dhcp\-rebind\-time
T}	T{
59
T}	T{
as dictated by kea\-dhcp4 configuration and
then reduced by CLTT
T}
_
T{
dhcp\-agent\-options
T}	T{
82
T}	T{
if stored on the lease. (See
Storing Extended Lease Information \%<#\:dhcp4-store-extended-info>)
T}
_
T{
associated\-ip
T}	T{
92
T}	T{
a list of all other IP addresses for which
the client has active leases. (Does not apply
to query by IP address)
T}
.TE
.sp
The \fBdhcp\-server\-identifier\fP option (54) is returned in all responses in keeping with
RFC 2131, section 4.3.1.
.sp
RFC 4388 allows requesters to ask for specific options via the
\fBdhcp\-parameter\-request\-list\fP (PRL, option 55). This is not currently supported in Kea.
.SS DHCPv4 Leasequery Configuration
.sp
Configuring the Leasequery hook library for use is straightforward. It
supports a single parameter, \fBrequesters\fP, which is a list of IP addresses from
which DHCPLEASEQUERY packets are accepted. In other words, it is a list of
known requesters. The following code shows an example configuration with two requester
addresses:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
 \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
     {
         \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_query.so\(dq,
         \(dqparameters\(dq: {
             \(dqrequesters\(dq: [ \(dq192.0.1.1\(dq, \(dq10.0.0.2\(dq ]
         }
     }
 ],
 ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 3.1.4, it is also possible to include ranges of requester addresses in CIDR
format such as \(dq192.0.1.0/24\(dq. Please note that specifying ranges may be less secure
than only using explicit IP addresses.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DHCPv6 Leasequery
.sp
DHCPv6 simple Leasequery gives a requester the ability to query for
active lease information for either a single IP address or a single client
DUID. The query type and parameters are conveyed in an \fBlq\-query\fP option (44)
attached to a \fBDHCPV6_LEASEQUERY\fP message:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBquery\-type\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is either \fBquery\-by\-address\fP (1) or \fBquery\-by\-clientid\fP (2)
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlink\-address\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
The global link address, when not empty, instructs the query to be
limited to leases within that \(dqlink.\(dq Kea uses this value to
select only leases that belong to subnets whose prefix matches
this value. Active leases for prefix delegations for
a matched subnet are included in the query reply, even if the
delegated prefix itself falls outside the subnet prefix.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBquery\-options\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
A single \fBiaaddr\fP option (12) must be supplied when querying by address.
When querying by client ID, a single \fBclientid\fP option (1) must be
supplied. RFC 5007 also calls for an optional, \fBoro\fP option (6), to
request specific options be returned for matched leases. This is
not currently implemented.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
RFC 5007, Section 3.3 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc5007#\:section-3.3>
states that querying by IP address should return either a lease (e.g.
binding) for the address itself or a lease for a delegated prefix that
contains the address.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBDHCPV6_LEASEQUERY\fP queries are only honored if the source address of
the query matches an entry in a list of known IP addresses which are
permitted to query. This list of valid requester addresses is specified
as part of the Leasequery hook library’s configuration (see the section
on configuration below). Queries received from unknown requesters are
logged and dropped.
.sp
In response to a valid query, the server carries out the requisite
activities and returns a \fBDHCPV6_LEASEQUERY_REPLY\fP\&. All replies contain
at least a \fBstatus\-code\fP option (13) that indicates the outcome of the query
as detailed in the following table:
.SS DHCPV6_LEASEQUERY_REPLY status option values per query outcome
.TS
box center;
l|l|l|l.
T{
Query Outcome
T}	T{
Status
Label
T}	T{
Status
Code
T}	T{
Status
Text
T}
_
T{
Invalid query type field
T}	T{
STATUS_UnknownQueryType
T}	T{
7
T}	T{
\(dqunknown query\-type\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by IP address that does not
contain an address option
T}	T{
STATUS_Malformed
T}	T{
10
T}	T{
\(dqmissing D6O_IAADDR\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by IP address for an address
that does fall within any configured
pools
T}	T{
STATUS_NotConfigured
T}	T{
9
T}	T{
\(dqaddress not in a configured
pool\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by IP address which found only
an inactive lease (e.g. expired,
declined, reclaimed\-expired)
T}	T{
STATUS_Success
T}	T{
0
T}	T{
\(dqinactive lease exists\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by IP address that found no
leases (active or otherwise)
T}	T{
STATUS_Success
T}	T{
0
T}	T{
\(dqno active lease\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by IP address that found an
active lease for the address
T}	T{
STATUS_Success
T}	T{
0
T}	T{
\(dqactive lease found\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by Client ID that does not
contain a client ID option
T}	T{
STATUS_Malformed
T}	T{
10
T}	T{
\(dqmissing D6O_CLIENTID\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by Client ID with a link
address that does not match any
configured subnets
T}	T{
STATUS_NotConfigured
T}	T{
9
T}	T{
\(dqnot a configured link\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by client ID which found no
matching leases
T}	T{
STATUS_Success
T}	T{
0
T}	T{
\(dqno active leases\(dq
T}
_
T{
Query by client ID which found one
or more active leases
T}	T{
STATUS_Success
T}	T{
0
T}	T{
\(dqactive lease(s) found\(dq
T}
.TE
.sp
For those scenarios where the query was either invalid or for which no matching active
leases were found, the \fBDHCPV6_LEASEQUERY_REPLY\fP only contains the \fBstatus\-code\fP
option (12) per the above table.
.sp
When a query finds active leases in more than one subnet and the query\(aqs \fBlink\-address\fP
is empty, then, in addition to the status\-code, the \fBDHCPV6_LEASEQUERY_REPLY\fP
contains an \fBlq\-client\-link\fP option (48). The \fBlq\-client\-link\fP contains a list of
IPv6 addresses, one for each subnet in which a lease was found (see
RFC 5007, Section 4.1.2.5 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc5007#\:section-4\:.1\:.2.5>)
If, however, the query\(aqs \fBlink\-address\fP is not empty, the list of queries is
pruned to contain only leases that belong to that subnet.
.sp
When the query results in one or more active leases which all belong to a single
subnet, in addition to the \fBstatus\-code\fP, the \fBDHCPV6_LEASEQUERY_REPLY\fP contains a
\fBclient\-data\fP option (45) (see
RFC 5007, Section 4.1.2.2 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc5007#\:section-4\:.1\:.2.2>).
The client\-data option encapsulates the following options:
.SS OPTION_CLIENT_DATA returned when active lease(s) are found
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Option
T}	T{
Code
T}	T{
Content
T}
_
T{
clientid
T}	T{
1
T}	T{
copied from the lease (if one exists)
T}
_
T{
clt\-time
T}	T{
46
T}	T{
amount of time that has elapsed since the
lease\(aqs client\-last\-transaction\-time (CLTT).
This value will also be used by the server to
adjust lifetime and timer values.
T}
_
T{
iaaddr
T}	T{
5
T}	T{
One option per matched address. Fields in
each option:
\- lease address
\- valid lifetime reduced by CLTT
\- preferred lifetime reduced by CLTT
T}
_
T{
iaprefix
T}	T{
26
T}	T{
One option per matched prefix. Fields in
each option:
\- prefix
\- prefix length
\- valid lifetime reduced by CLTT
\- preferred lifetime reduced by CLTT
T}
.TE
.sp
If the lease with the most recent client\-last\-transaction\-time (CLTT)
value has relay information in its user context (see
Storing Extended Lease Information \%<#\:store-extended-info-v6>), then an \fBOPTION_LQ_RELAY_DATA\fP option is
added to the reply (see
RFC 5007, Section 4.1.2.4 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc5007#\:section-4\:.1\:.2.4>).
.sp
The relay information on the lease is a list with an entry for each
relay layer the client packet (e.g. \fBDHCPV6_REQUEST\fP) traversed, with the
first entry in the list being the outermost layer (closest to the server). The
\fBpeer\-address\fP field of the \fBlq\-rely\-option\fP is set to the peer address of this
relay. The list of relays is then used to construct a \fBDHCPV6_RELAY_FORW\fP message
equivalent to that which contained the client packet, minus the client packet.
This message is stored in the \fBDHCP\-relay\-message\fP field of the \fBlq\-relay\-data\fP option.
.SS DHCPv6 Leasequery Configuration
.sp
Configuring the Leasequery hook library for use is straightforward. It
supports a single parameter, \fBrequesters\fP, which is a list of IP addresses from
which DHCPV6_LEASEQUERY packets are accepted. In other words, it is a list of
known requesters. The following code shows an example configuration with two requester
addresses:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
 \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
     {
         \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_query.so\(dq,
         \(dqparameters\(dq: {
             \(dqrequesters\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq, \(dq2001:db8:2::1\(dq ],
             \(dqprefix\-lengths\(dq: [ 72 ]
         }
     }
 ],
 ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 3.1.4, it is also possible to include ranges of requester addresses in CIDR
format such as \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq. Please note that specifying ranges may be less secure
than only using explicit IP addresses.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When a query by IP address does not match an existing address lease,
a search for a matching delegated prefix is conducted. This is carried
out by iterating over a list of prefix lengths in descending order,
extracting a prefix of that length from the query address, and searching
for a delegation matching the resulting prefix. This continues for each
length in the list until a match is found or the list is exhausted.
.sp
By default, the list of prefix lengths to use in the search is determined
dynamically after (re)configuration events. The resulting list
contains unique values of \fBdelegated\-len\fP, gleaned from the currently
configured set of PD pools.
.sp
There is an optional parameter, \fBprefix\-lengths\fP, which
provides the ability to explicitly configure the list rather than having
it be determined dynamically. This provides tighter control over which
prefix lengths are searched. In the above example, the prefix\-length
search is restricted to a single pass, using a length of 72, regardless
of whether there are pools using other values for \fBdelegated\-len\fP\&.
Specifying an empty list, as shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
:
               \(dqprefix\-lengths\(dq: [ ]
:
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
disables the search for delegated prefixes for query by IP address.
.SS DHCPv4 Bulk Leasequery
.sp
DHCPv4 Bulk Leasequery gives a requester the ability to query for
active lease information over a TCP connection. This allows the server
to return all leases matching a given query.
.sp
Two of the query types identified by RFC 4388 \- Query by MAC address and
Query by Client\-identifier \- are Bulk Leasequery types specified by RFC
6926. That RFC also defines these new Bulk Leasequery types:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Query by Relay Identifier
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
The query carries an RAI (dhcp\-agent\-options (82)) option with
a relay\-id (12) sub\-option.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Query by Remote ID
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
The query carries an RAI (dhcp\-agent\-options (82) option) with
a remote\-id (2) sub\-option.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
Query for All Configured IP Addresses
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This query type is selected when no other query type is specified.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
RFC 6926 also defines new options for Bulk Leasequery:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
status\-code (151)
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This reply option carries a status code such as MalformedQuery or
NotAllowed, with an optional text message.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
base\-time (152)
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This reply option carries the absolute current time that the response
was created. All other time\-based reply options are related to
this value.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
start\-time\-of\-state (153)
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This reply option carries the time of the lease\(aqs transition into its
current state.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
query\-start\-time (154)
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This query option specifies a start\-query time; replies will only
contain leases that are older than this value.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
query\-end\-time (155)
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This query option specifies an end\-query time; replies will only
contain leases that are newer than this value.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
dhcp\-state (156)
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This reply option carries the lease state.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
data\-source (157)
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This reply option carries the source of the data as a remote flag.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
RFC 6926 reuses and extends the Virtual Subnet Selection option (221)
defined in RFC 6607.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Kea does not yet support querying for all configured IP addresses,
so the dhcp\-state option cannot be used, as only active leases can be
returned in replies. Kea does not keep the start time of the lease\(aqs state,
nor the local/remote information, so it cannot emit the corresponding
start\-time\-of\-state and data\-source options. Kea does not support VPNs
so the presence of option 221 in the query is considered a
(NotAllowed) error.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS DHCPv6 Bulk Leasequery
.sp
DHCPv6 Bulk Leasequery gives a requester the ability to query for
active lease information over a TCP connection. This allows the server
to return all active leases matching a query.
.sp
New query types are available: \fBquery\-by\-relay\-id\fP (3),
\fBquery\-by\-link\-address\fP (4), and \fBquery\-by\-remote\-id\fP (5).
.sp
A new status code, \fBSTATUS_QueryTerminated\fP (11), has been defined but it is
not yet used by the hook library.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Kea attempts to map link address parameters to the prefixes of configured
subnets. If a given address falls outside all configured subnet prefixes,
the query fails with a status code of \fBSTATUS_NotConfigured\fP\&. If
the link address parameter for \fBquery\-by\-relay\-id\fP or \fBquery\-by\-remote\-id\fP
is not \fB::\fP (i.e. not empty), only delegated prefixes that lie within matching
subnet prefixes are returned. Currently, \fBquery\-by\-address\fP does not
support finding delegated prefixes by specifying an address that lies within
the prefix.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Bulk Leasequery Configuration
.sp
Bulk Leasequery configuration is specified via a new map parameter, \fBadvanced\fP,
with these possible entries:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbulk\-query\-enabled\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
When \fBtrue\fP, Kea accepts connections from IP addresses in the requesters
list and processes received bulk leasequeries. The default is \fBfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBactive\-query\-enabled\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is an anticipated parameter: if set, it must be \fBfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBextended\-info\-tables\-enabled\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
When \fBtrue\fP, the lease backend manages DHCPv6 lease extended info
(relay info) in tables to support the new DHCPv6 Bulk Leasequery
by\-relay\-id and by\-remote\-id types. The default is to use the
same value as \fBbulk\-query\-enabled\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease\-query\-ip\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is the IP address upon which to listen for connections. The address must be
of the same family as the server, e.g. IPv6 for the DHCPv6 server.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease\-query\-port\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is the port upon which to listen. The default is 67 for IPv4 and 547 for IPv6,
i.e. the same value as for the UDP DHCP service, but for TCP.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-bulk\-query\-threads\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This indicates the maximum number of threads that bulk leasequery processing
should use. A value of 0 instructs the server to use the same number of
threads that the Kea core is using for DHCP multi\-threading.
The default is 0.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-requester\-connections\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is the maximum number of concurrent requester connections. The default
is 10; the value must be greater than 0.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-concurrent\-queries\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is the maximum number of concurrent queries per connection. The value 0
allows Kea to determine the number, and is the default.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-requester\-idle\-time\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is the amount of time that may elapse after receiving data from a requester
before its connection is closed as idle, in seconds. The default
is 300.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\-leases\-per\-fetch\fP
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
This is the maximum number of leases to return in a single fetch. The default is 100.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Once TLS is supported, we expect to implement common TLS parameters.
.sp
For instance, for DHCPv4:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
          \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_query.so\(dq,
          \(dqparameters\(dq: {
              \(dqrequesters\(dq: [ \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq, \(dq192.0.2.2\(dq ],
              \(dqadvanced\(dq : {
                   \(dqbulk\-query\-enabled\(dq: true,
                   \(dqactive\-query\-enabled\(dq: false,

                   \(dqlease\-query\-ip\(dq: \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq,
                   \(dqlease\-query\-tcp\-port\(dq: 67,

                   \(dqmax\-bulk\-query\-threads\(dq: 0,
                   \(dqmax\-requester\-connections\(dq: 10,
                   \(dqmax\-concurrent\-queries\(dq: 4,
                   \(dqmax\-requester\-idle\-time\(dq: 300,
                   \(dqmax\-leases\-per\-fetch\(dq: 100
              }
          }
      }
  ],
  ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or for DHCPv6:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
          \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_lease_query.so\(dq,
          \(dqparameters\(dq: {
              \(dqrequesters\(dq: [ \(dq2001:db8:1::1\(dq, \(dq2001:db8:2::1\(dq ],
              \(dqadvanced\(dq : {
                   \(dqbulk\-query\-enabled\(dq: true,
                   \(dqactive\-query\-enabled\(dq: false,

                   \(dqextended\-info\-tables\-enabled\(dq: true,

                   \(dqlease\-query\-ip\(dq: \(dq::1\(dq,
                   \(dqlease\-query\-tcp\-port\(dq: 547,

                   \(dqmax\-bulk\-query\-threads\(dq: 0,
                   \(dqmax\-requester\-connections\(dq: 10,
                   \(dqmax\-concurrent\-queries\(dq: 4,
                   \(dqmax\-requester\-idle\-time\(dq: 300,
                   \(dqmax\-leases\-per\-fetch\(dq: 100
              }
          }
      }
  ],
  ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Updating Existing Leases in SQL Lease Backends
.sp
Bulk Leasequery requires additions to the lease data that is stored. With SQL lease
backends, leases created prior to the server being configured for bulk lease
query do not contain the new data required. In order to populate this data,
it is necessary to run these API commands:
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Existing leases must have been created by Kea with \fBstore\-extended\-info\fP
enabled, in order for the new data from extended info to be extracted
and stored.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For DHCPv4 lease data, the command is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqextended\-info4\-upgrade\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For DHCPv6 lease data, the command is:
.sp
For extended info used by relay ID and by remote ID, the command is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqextended\-info6\-upgrade\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In all cases, the response indicates whether it succeeded or failed
and includes either the count of leases updated or the nature of the failure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqUpgraded 1000 leases\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This \fBextended\-info6\-upgrade\fP command must be called when:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
the database schema was upgraded from 2.4.1 or older version.
\fBextended\-info4\-upgrade\fP can be used when upgrading from 2.3.8 or older version.
.IP \(bu 2
Bulk Leasequery was not enabled; tables are maintained only when v6 BLQ is
enabled.
.IP \(bu 2
data in tables does not seem to be consistent; tables are not maintained in
an atomic way, so consistency is not guaranteed. For instance, when a database
is shared between several servers, races can happen between updates.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The operation of the extended info command is governed by the \fBextended\-info\-checks\fP
parameter, under the \fBsanity\-checks\fP element. Please see Sanity Checks in DHCPv4 \%<#\:sanity-checks4>
or Sanity Checks in DHCPv6 \%<#\:sanity-checks6>\&.
.sp
For large numbers of leases, this command may take some time to complete.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Both the \fBextended\-info4\-upgrade\fP and \fBextended\-info6\-upgrade\fP commands
are intended for one\-time use after upgrading to a version of Kea that fully
supports Bulk Leasequery in database backends. These commands are not meant
for regular use and will be removed in future Kea versions.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_legal_log.so\fP: Forensic Logging
.sp
The Forensic Logging hook library provides
hooks that record a detailed log of assignments, renewals, releases, and other
lease events into a set of log files.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_legal_log.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In many legal jurisdictions, companies \- especially ISPs \- must record
information about the addresses they have leased to DHCP clients. This
library is designed to help with that requirement. If the information
that it records is sufficient, it may be used directly.
.sp
If a jurisdiction requires that different information be saved, users
may use the custom formatting capability to extract information from the inbound
request packet, or from the outbound response packet. Administrators are advised
to use this feature with caution, as it may affect server performance.
The custom format cannot be used for control channel commands.
.sp
Alternatively, this library may be used as a template or an example for the
user\(aqs own custom logging hook. The logging is done as a set of hooks to allow
it to be customized to any particular need; modifying a hook library is easier
and safer than updating the core code. In addition, by using the hooks features,
users who do not need to log this information can leave it out and avoid
any performance penalties.
.SS Log File Naming
.sp
The names of the log files follow a set pattern.
.sp
If using \fBday\fP, \fBmonth\fP, or \fByear\fP as the time unit, the file name follows
the format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
path/base\-name.CCYYMMDD.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where \fBCC\fP represents the century, \fBYY\fP represents the year,
\fBMM\fP represents the month, and \fBDD\fP represents the day.
.sp
If using \fBsecond\fP as the time unit the file name follows the format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
path/base\-name.TXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX.txt
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where \fBXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX\fP represents the time in seconds since the beginning
of the UNIX epoch.
.sp
When using \fBsecond\fP as the time unit, the file is rotated when
the \fBcount\fP number of seconds pass. In contrast, when using \fBday\fP, \fBmonth\fP,
or \fByear\fP as the time unit, the file is rotated whenever the \fBcount\fP of day,
month, or year starts, as applicable.
.sp
The \fB\(dqpath\(dq\fP and \fB\(dqbase\-name\(dq\fP are supplied in the configuration as
described below; see Configuring the Forensic Logging Hooks\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When running Kea servers for both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6, the log names
must be distinct. See the examples in Configuring the Forensic Logging Hooks\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuring the Forensic Logging Hooks
.sp
To use this functionality, the hook library must be included in the
configuration of the desired DHCP server modules. \fBlibdhcp_legal_log.so\fP
can save logs to a text file or to a database (created using
\fBkea\-admin\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-admin>; see First\-Time Creation of the MySQL Database \%<#\:mysql-database-create> and First\-Time Creation of the PostgreSQL Database \%<#\:pgsql-database-create>).
The library is installed alongside the Kea libraries in
\fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea\(dq\fP, where \fBkea\-install\-dir\fP is determined
by the \fB\-\-prefix\fP meson setup option which defaults to
\fB/usr/local\fP\&. Assuming the default value, \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> can be configured to load
\fBlibdhcp_legal_log.so\fP like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_legal_log.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/var/log/kea\(dq,
                    \(dqbase\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-forensic4\(dq
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>, the configuration is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_legal_log.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/var/log/kea\(dq,
                    \(dqbase\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-forensic6\(dq
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The hook library parameters for the text file configuration are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpath\fP \- the directory in which the forensic file(s) will be written.
The default value is \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea\(dq\fP\&. The directory
must exist.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, legal log files may only be written to the output directory
determined during compilation as: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea\(dq\fP\&. This
path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_LEGAL_LOG_DIR\fP to the desired path.  If a path other than this value
is used in \fBpath\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start or if already
running, log an unrecoverable error. For ease of use simply omit the \fBpath\fP
parameter.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbase\-name\fP \- an arbitrary value which is used in conjunction with the
current system date to form the current forensic file name. It
defaults to \fBkea\-legal\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtime\-unit\fP \- configures the time unit used to rotate the log file. Valid
values are \fBsecond\fP, \fBday\fP, \fBmonth\fP, or \fByear\fP\&. It defaults to
\fBday\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcount\fP \- configures the number of time units that need to pass until the
log file is rotated. It can be any positive number, or 0, which disables log
rotation. It defaults to 1.
.UNINDENT
.sp
If log rotation is disabled, a new file is created when the library is
loaded; the new file name is different from any previous file name.
.sp
Additional actions can be performed just before closing the old file and after
opening the new file. These actions must point to an external executable or
script and are configured with the following settings:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprerotate\fP \- an external executable or script called with the name of the
file that will be closed. Kea does not wait for the process to finish.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpostrotate\fP \- an external executable or script called with the name of the
file that was opened. Kea does not wait for the process to finish.
.UNINDENT
.sp
These executables must be stored in the \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/share/kea/scripts/\(dq\fP
directory which can be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_HOOK_SCRIPTS_PATH\fP to a different path.
.sp
Custom formatting can be enabled for logging information that can be extracted
either from the client\(aqs request packet or from the server\(aqs response packet.
Use with caution as this might affect server performance.
The custom format cannot be used for control channel commands.
Two parameters can be used towards this goal, either together or separately:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrequest\-parser\-format\fP \- an evaluated parsed expression used to extract and
log data from the incoming packet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBresponse\-parser\-format\fP \- an evaluated parsed expression used to extract and
log data from the server response packet.
.UNINDENT
.sp
See Using Expressions in Classification \%<#\:classification-using-expressions> for a list of expressions.
If either \fBrequest\-parser\-format\fP or \fBresponse\-parser\-format\fP is
configured, the default logging format is not used. If both of them are
configured, the resulting log message is constructed by concatenating the
data extracted from the request and the data extracted from the response.
.sp
The custom formatting permits logging on multiple lines using the hexstring 0x0a
(ASCII code for new line). In the log file, each line is prepended
with the log timestamp. For the database backend, the data is stored
(including the newline character) in the same entry.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_legal_log.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/var/log/kea\(dq,
                    \(dqbase\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-forensic6\(dq,
                    \(dqrequest\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dq\(aqfirst line\(aq + 0x0a + \(aqsecond line\(aq\(dq,
                    \(dqresponse\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dq\(aqalso second line\(aq + 0x0a + \(aqthird line\(aq\(dq
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some data might be available in the request or only in the response; the
data in the request packet might differ from that in the response packet.
.sp
The lease\-client context can only be printed using the default format, as this
information is not directly stored in the request packet or in the response
packet.
.sp
The \fBtimestamp\-format\fP parameter can be used to change the timestamp logged
at the beginning of each line. Permissible formatting is the one supported by
strftime plus the \(aq%Q\(aq extra format which adds the microseconds subunits. The
default is: \(dq%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z\(dq. This parameter has no effect for the
database backends, where the timestamp is defined at the schema level.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_legal_log.so\(dq,
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqpath\(dq: \(dq/var/log/kea\(dq,
                    \(dqbase\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-forensic6\(dq,
                    \(dqtimestamp\-format\(dq: \(dq%H%t%w %F%%\(dq
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Additional parameters for the database connection can be specified, e.g:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_legal_log.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdatabase\-name\(dq,
          \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
          \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
          \(dquser\(dq: \(dquser\-name\(dq
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For more specific information about database\-related parameters, please refer to
Lease Database Configuration \%<#\:database-configuration4> and Lease Database Configuration \%<#\:database-configuration6>\&.
.sp
If it is desired to restrict forensic logging to certain subnets, the
\fB\(dqlegal\-logging\(dq\fP boolean parameter can be specified within a user context
of these subnets. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1 \- 192.0.2.200\(dq
                    }
                ],
                \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
                    \(dqlegal\-logging\(dq: false
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration disables legal logging for the subnet \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq. If the
\fB\(dqlegal\-logging\(dq\fP parameter is not specified, it defaults to \fBtrue\fP, which
enables legal logging for the subnet.
.sp
The following example demonstrates how to selectively disable legal
logging for an IPv6 subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\-2001:db8:1::ffff\(dq
                    }
                ],
                \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
                    \(dqlegal\-logging\(dq: false
                }
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
See User Contexts in IPv4 \%<#\:dhcp4-user-contexts> and User Contexts in IPv6 \%<#\:dhcp6-user-contexts> to
learn more about user contexts in Kea configuration.
.SS DHCPv4 Log Entries
.sp
For DHCPv4, the library creates entries based on DHCPREQUEST, DHCPDECLINE,
and DHCPRELEASE messages, et al., and their responses. The resulting packets and
leases are taken into account, intercepted through the following hook points:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkt4_receive\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBleases4_committed\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkt4_send\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4_release\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4_decline\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
An entry is a single string with no embedded end\-of\-line markers and a
prepended timestamp, and has the following sections:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
timestamp address duration device\-id {client\-info} {relay\-info} {user\-context}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Where:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtimestamp\fP \- the date and time the log entry was written, in
\(dq%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z\(dq strftime format (\(dq%Z\(dq is the time zone name).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaddress\fP \- the leased IPv4 address given out, and whether it was
assigned, renewed, or released.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBduration\fP \- the lease lifetime expressed in days (if present), hours,
minutes, and seconds. A lease lifetime of 0xFFFFFFFF will be denoted
with the text \(dqinfinite duration.\(dq This information is not given
when the lease is released.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdevice\-id\fP \- the client\(aqs hardware address shown as a numerical type and
hex\-digit string.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-info\fP \- the DHCP client id option (61) if present, shown as a
hex string. When its content is printable it is displayed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay\-info\fP \- for relayed packets, the \fBgiaddr\fP and the RAI \fBcircuit\-id\fP,
\fBremote\-id\fP, and \fBsubscriber\-id\fP options (option 82 sub options: 1, 2 and 6),
if present. The \fBcircuit\-id\fP and \fBremote\-id\fP are presented as hex
strings. When their content is printable it is displayed.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuser\-context\fP \- the optional user context associated with the lease.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For instance (line breaks are added here for readability; they are not
present in the log file):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 CET Address: 192.2.1.100 has been renewed for 1 hrs 52 min 15 secs to a device with hardware address:
hwtype=1 08:00:2b:02:3f:4e, client\-id: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: 192.2.16.33,
identified by circuit\-id: 68:6f:77:64:79 (howdy) and remote\-id: 87:f6:79:77:ef
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or for a release:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 CET Address: 192.2.1.100 has been released from a device with hardware address:
hwtype=1 08:00:2b:02:3f:4e, client\-id: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: 192.2.16.33,
identified by circuit\-id: 68:6f:77:64:79 (howdy) and remote\-id: 87:f6:79:77:ef
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In addition to logging lease activity driven by DHCPv4 client traffic,
the hook library also logs entries for the following lease management control
channel commands: \fBlease4\-add\fP, \fBlease4\-update\fP, and \fBlease4\-del\fP\&. These cannot have
custom formatting. Each entry is a single string with no embedded end\-of\-line
markers, and it will typically have the following form:
.sp
\fBlease4\-add:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator added a lease of address: *address* to a device with hardware address: *device\-id*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Depending on the arguments of the add command, it may also include the
client\-id and duration.
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 CET Administrator added a lease of address: 192.0.2.202 to a device with hardware address:
1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f for 1 days 0 hrs 0 mins 0 secs
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlease4\-update:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator updated information on the lease of address: *address* to a device with hardware address: *device\-id*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Depending on the arguments of the update command, it may also include
the client\-id and lease duration.
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 CET Administrator updated information on the lease of address: 192.0.2.202 to a device
with hardware address: 1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f, client\-id: 1234567890
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlease4\-del:\fP deletes have two forms, one by address and one by
identifier and identifier type:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator deleted the lease for address: *address*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator deleted a lease for a device identified by: *identifier\-type* of *identifier*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Currently only a type of \fB@b hw\-address\fP (hardware address) is supported.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 CET Administrator deleted the lease for address: 192.0.2.202

2018\-01\-06 01:02:12 CET Administrator deleted a lease for a device identified by: hw\-address of 1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the High Availability hook library is enabled, the partner periodically sends lease
commands in a similar format; the only difference is that the issuer of
the command is \(dqHA partner\(dq instead of \(dqAdministrator.\(dq
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* HA partner added ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* HA partner updated ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* HA partner deleted ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBrequest\-parser\-format\fP and \fBresponse\-parser\-format\fP options can be used to
extract and log data from the incoming packet and server response packet,
respectively. The configured value is an evaluated parsed expression returning a
string. A list of tokens is described in the server classification process.
Use with caution as this might affect server performance.
If either of them is configured, the default logging format is not used.
If both of them are configured, the resulting log message is constructed by
concatenating the logged data extracted from the request and the logged data
extracted from the response.
.sp
The custom formatting permits logging on multiple lines using the hexstring 0x0a
(ASCII code for new line). In the case of the log file, each line is prepended
with the log timestamp. For the database backend, the data is stored
(including the newline character) in the same entry.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_legal_log.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdatabase\-name\(dq,
          \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
          \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
          \(dquser\(dq: \(dquser\-name\(dq,
          \(dqrequest\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dq\(aqlog entry\(aq + 0x0a + \(aqsame log entry\(aq\(dq,
          \(dqresponse\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dq\(aqalso same log entry\(aq + 0x0a + \(aqagain same log entry\(aq\(dq
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some data might be available in the request or in the response only, and some
data might differ in the incoming packet from the one in the response packet.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqrequest\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dqifelse(pkt4.msgtype == 4 or pkt4.msgtype == 7, \(aqAddress: \(aq + ifelse(option[50].exists, addrtotext(option[50].hex), addrtotext(pkt4.ciaddr)) + \(aq has been released from a device with hardware address: hwtype=\(aq + substring(hexstring(pkt4.htype, \(aq\(aq), 7, 1) + \(aq \(aq + hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(option[61].exists, \(aq, client\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[61].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(pkt4.giaddr == 0.0.0.0, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(pkt4.giaddr) + ifelse(option[82].option[1].exists, \(aq, circuit\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[2].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[2].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[6].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[6].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq)\(dq,
    \(dqresponse\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dqifelse(pkt4.msgtype == 5, \(aqAddress: \(aq + addrtotext(pkt4.yiaddr) + \(aq has been assigned for \(aq + uint32totext(option[51].hex) + \(aq seconds to a device with hardware address: hwtype=\(aq + substring(hexstring(pkt4.htype, \(aq\(aq), 7, 1) + \(aq \(aq + hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(option[61].exists, \(aq, client\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[61].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(pkt4.giaddr == 0.0.0.0, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(pkt4.giaddr) + ifelse(option[82].option[1].exists, \(aq, circuit\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[2].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[2].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[6].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[6].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq)\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Details:
.sp
This will log the following data on request and renew:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Address: 192.2.1.100 has been assigned for 6735 seconds to a device with hardware address: hwtype=1 08:00:2b:02:3f:4e, client\-id: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: 192.2.16.33, circuit\-id: 68:6f:77:64:79, remote\-id: 87:f6:79:77:ef, subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will log the following data on release and decline:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Address: 192.2.1.100 has been released from a device with hardware address: hwtype=1 08:00:2b:02:3f:4e, client\-id: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: 192.2.16.33, circuit\-id: 68:6f:77:64:79, remote\-id: 87:f6:79:77:ef, subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A similar result can be obtained by configuring only \fBrequest\-parser\-format\fP\&.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqrequest\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dqifelse(pkt4.msgtype == 3, \(aqAddress: \(aq + ifelse(option[50].exists, addrtotext(option[50].hex), addrtotext(pkt4.ciaddr)) + \(aq has been assigned\(aq + ifelse(option[51].exists, \(aq for \(aq + uint32totext(option[51].hex) + \(aq seconds\(aq, \(aq\(aq) + \(aq to a device with hardware address: hwtype=\(aq + substring(hexstring(pkt4.htype, \(aq\(aq), 7, 1) + \(aq \(aq + hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(option[61].exists, \(aq, client\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[61].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(pkt4.giaddr == 0.0.0.0, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(pkt4.giaddr) + ifelse(option[82].option[1].exists, \(aq, circuit\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[2].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[2].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[6].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[6].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), ifelse(pkt4.msgtype == 4 or pkt4.msgtype == 7, \(aqAddress: \(aq + ifelse(option[50].exists, addrtotext(option[50].hex), addrtotext(pkt4.ciaddr)) + \(aq has been released from a device with hardware address: hwtype=\(aq + substring(hexstring(pkt4.htype, \(aq\(aq), 7, 1) + \(aq \(aq + hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(option[61].exists, \(aq, client\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[61].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(pkt4.giaddr == 0.0.0.0, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(pkt4.giaddr) + ifelse(option[82].option[1].exists, \(aq, circuit\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[2].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[2].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[82].option[6].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(option[82].option[6].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq))\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Details:
.SS DHCPv6 Log Entries
.sp
For DHCPv6, the library creates entries based on REQUEST, RENEW, RELEASE,
and DECLINE messages, et al. and their responses. The resulting packets and leases
are taken into account, intercepted through the following hook points:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkt6_receive\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBleases6_committed\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkt6_send\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6_release\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6_decline\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
An entry is a single string with no embedded end\-of\-line markers and a
prepended timestamp, and has the following sections:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
timestamp address duration device\-id {relay\-info}* {user\-context}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Where:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtimestamp\fP \- the date and time the log entry was written, in
\(dq%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S %Z\(dq strftime format (\(dq%Z\(dq is the time zone name).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaddress\fP \- the leased IPv6 address or prefix given out, and whether it
was assigned, renewed, or released.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBduration\fP \- the lease lifetime expressed in days (if present), hours,
minutes, and seconds. A lease lifetime of 0xFFFFFFFF will be denoted
with the text \(dqinfinite duration.\(dq This information is not given
when the lease is released.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdevice\-id\fP \- the client\(aqs DUID and hardware address (if present).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay\-info\fP \- for relayed packets the content of relay agent messages, and the
\fBremote\-id\fP (code 37), \fBsubscriber\-id\fP (code 38), and \fBinterface\-id\fP (code 18)
options, if present. Note that the \fBinterface\-id\fP option, if present,
identifies the whole interface on which the relay agent received the message.
This typically translates to a single link in the network, but
it depends on the specific network topology. Nevertheless, this is
useful information to better pinpoint the location of the device,
so it is recorded, if present.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuser\-context\fP \- the optional user context associated with the lease.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For instance (line breaks are added here for readability; they are not
present in the log file):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 PST Address:2001:db8:1:: has been assigned for 0 hrs 11 mins 53 secs
to a device with DUID: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 and hardware address: hwtype=1 08:00:2b:02:3f:4e
(from Raw Socket) connected via relay at address: fe80::abcd for client on link address: 3001::1,
hop count: 1, identified by remote\-id: 01:02:03:04:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f and subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or for a release:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 PST Address:2001:db8:1:: has been released
from a device with DUID: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 and hardware address: hwtype=1 08:00:2b:02:3f:4e
(from Raw Socket) connected via relay at address: fe80::abcd for client on link address: 3001::1,
hop count: 1, identified by remote\-id: 01:02:03:04:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f and subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In addition to logging lease activity driven by DHCPv6 client traffic,
the hook library also logs entries for the following lease management control channel
commands: \fBlease6\-add\fP, \fBlease6\-update\fP, and \fBlease6\-del\fP\&. Each entry is a
single string with no embedded end\-of\-line markers, and it will
typically have the following form:
.sp
\fBlease6\-add:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator added a lease of address: *address* to a device with DUID: *DUID*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Depending on the arguments of the add command, it may also include the
hardware address and duration.
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 PST Administrator added a lease of address: 2001:db8::3 to a device with DUID:
1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f:20:21:22:23:24 for 1 days 0 hrs 0 mins 0 secs
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlease6\-update:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator updated information on the lease of address: *address* to a device with DUID: *DUID*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Depending on the arguments of the update command, it may also include
the hardware address and lease duration.
.sp
Example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 PST Administrator updated information on the lease of address: 2001:db8::3 to a device with
DUID: 1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f:20:21:22:23:24, hardware address: 1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlease6\-del:\fP deletes have two forms, one by address and one by
identifier and identifier type:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator deleted the lease for address: *address*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* Administrator deleted a lease for a device identified by: *identifier\-type* of *identifier*
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Currently only a type of \fBDUID\fP is supported.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2018\-01\-06 01:02:03 PST Administrator deleted the lease for address: 2001:db8::3

2018\-01\-06 01:02:11 PST Administrator deleted a lease for a device identified by: duid of 1a:1b:1c:1d:1e:1f:20:21:22:23:24
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the High Availability hook library is enabled, the partner periodically sends lease
commands in a similar format; the only difference is that the issuer of
the command is \(dqHA partner\(dq instead of \(dqAdministrator.\(dq
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* HA partner added ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* HA partner updated ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
*timestamp* HA partner deleted ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBrequest\-parser\-format\fP and \fBresponse\-parser\-format\fP options can be used to
extract and log data from the incoming packet and server response packet,
respectively. The configured value is an evaluated parsed expression returning a
string. A list of tokens is described in the server classification process.
Use with caution as this might affect server performance.
If either of them is configured, the default logging format is not used.
If both of them are configured, the resulting log message is constructed by
concatenating the logged data extracted from the request and the logged data
extracted from the response.
.sp
The custom formatting permits logging on multiple lines using the hexstring 0x0a
(ASCII code for new line). In the case of the log file, each line is prepended
with the log timestamp. For the database backend, the data is stored
(including the newline character) in the same entry.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_legal_log.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdatabase\-name\(dq,
          \(dqpassword\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq,
          \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqmysql\(dq,
          \(dquser\(dq: \(dquser\-name\(dq,
          \(dqrequest\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dq\(aqlog entry\(aq + 0x0a + \(aqsame log entry\(aq\(dq,
          \(dqresponse\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dq\(aqalso same log entry\(aq + 0x0a + \(aqagain same log entry\(aq\(dq
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Some data might be available in the request or in the response only, and some
data might differ in the incoming packet from the one in the response packet.
.sp
Notes:
.sp
In the case of IPv6, the packets can contain multiple IA_NA (3) or IA_PD (25)
options, each containing multiple options, including OPTION_IAADDR (5) or
OPTION_IAPREFIX (25) suboptions.
To be able to print the current lease associated with the log entry, the
forensic log hook library internally isolates the corresponding IA_NA or IA_PD
option and respective suboption matching the current lease.
The hook library will iterate over all new allocated addresses and all deleted
addresses, making each address available for logging as the current lease for
the respective logged entry.
.sp
They are accessible using the following parser expressions:
.sp
Current lease associated with OPTION_IAADDR:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
addrtotext(substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 0, 16))
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Current lease associated with OPTION_IAPREFIX:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
addrtotext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 9, 16))
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
All other parameters of the options are available at their respective offsets
in the option. Please read RFC9915 for more details.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqrequest\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dqifelse(pkt6.msgtype == 8 or pkt6.msgtype == 9, ifelse(option[3].option[5].exists, \(aqAddress: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 0, 16)) + \(aq has been released from a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[25].option[26].exists, \(aqPrefix: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 9, 16)) + \(aq/\(aq + uint8totext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 8, 1)) + \(aq has been released from a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq), \(aq\(aq)\(dq,
    \(dqresponse\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dqifelse(pkt6.msgtype == 7, ifelse(option[3].option[5].exists and not (substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 20, 4) == 0), \(aqAddress: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 0, 16)) + \(aq has been assigned for \(aq + uint32totext(substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 20, 4)) + \(aq seconds to a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[25].option[26].exists and not (substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 4, 4) == 0), \(aqPrefix: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 9, 16)) + \(aq/\(aq + uint8totext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 8, 1)) + \(aq has been assigned for \(aq + uint32totext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 4, 4)) + \(aq seconds to a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq), \(aq\(aq)\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Details:
.sp
This will log the following data on request, renew, and rebind for NA:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Address: 2001:db8:1:: has been assigned for 713 seconds to a device with DUID: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: fe80::abcd for client on link address: 3001::1, remote\-id: 01:02:03:04:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f, subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f, connected at location interface\-id: 72:65:6c:61:79:31:3a:65:74:68:30
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will log the following data on request, renew and rebind for PD:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Prefix: 2001:db8:1::/64 has been assigned for 713 seconds to a device with DUID: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: fe80::abcd for client on link address: 3001::1, remote\-id: 01:02:03:04:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f, subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f, connected at location interface\-id: 72:65:6c:61:79:31:3a:65:74:68:30
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will log the following data on release and decline for NA:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Address: 2001:db8:1:: has been released from a device with DUID: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: fe80::abcd for client on link address: 3001::1, remote\-id: 01:02:03:04:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f, subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f, connected at location interface\-id: 72:65:6c:61:79:31:3a:65:74:68:30
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This will log the following data on release and decline for PD:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Prefix: 2001:db8:1::/64 has been released from a device with DUID: 17:34:e2:ff:09:92:54 connected via relay at address: fe80::abcd for client on link address: 3001::1, remote\-id: 01:02:03:04:0a:0b:0c:0d:0e:0f, subscriber\-id: 1a:2b:3c:4d:5e:6f, connected at location interface\-id: 72:65:6c:61:79:31:3a:65:74:68:30
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A similar result can be obtained by configuring only \fBrequest\-parser\-format\fP\&.
.sp
Examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqrequest\-parser\-format\(dq: \(dqifelse(pkt6.msgtype == 3 or pkt6.msgtype == 5 or pkt6.msgtype == 6, ifelse(option[3].option[5].exists, \(aqAddress: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 0, 16)) + \(aq has been assigned for \(aq + uint32totext(substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 20, 4)) + \(aq seconds to a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[25].option[26].exists, \(aqPrefix: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 9, 16)) + \(aq/\(aq + uint8totext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 8, 1)) + \(aq has been assigned for \(aq + uint32totext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 4, 4)) + \(aq seconds to a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq), ifelse(pkt6.msgtype == 8 or pkt6.msgtype == 9, ifelse(option[3].option[5].exists, \(aqAddress: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[3].option[5].hex, 0, 16)) + \(aq has been released from a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(option[25].option[26].exists, \(aqPrefix: \(aq + addrtotext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 9, 16)) + \(aq/\(aq + uint8totext(substring(option[25].option[26].hex, 8, 1)) + \(aq has been released from a device with DUID: \(aq + hexstring(option[1].hex, \(aq:\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].peeraddr == \(aq\(aq, \(aq\(aq, \(aq connected via relay at address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].peeraddr) + \(aq for client on link address: \(aq + addrtotext(relay6[0].linkaddr) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[37].exists, \(aq, remote\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[37].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[38].exists, \(aq, subscriber\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[38].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq) + ifelse(relay6[0].option[18].exists, \(aq, connected at location interface\-id: \(aq + hexstring(relay6[0].option[18].hex, \(aq:\(aq), \(aq\(aq)), \(aq\(aq), \(aq\(aq))\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Details:
.SS Database Backend
.sp
Log entries can be inserted into a database when Kea is configured with
database backend support. Kea uses a table named \fBlogs\fP, that includes a
timestamp generated by the database software, and a text log with the same
format as files without the timestamp.
.sp
Please refer to MySQL \%<#\:mysql-database> for information on using a MySQL database;
or to PostgreSQL \%<#\:pgsql-database> for PostgreSQL database information. The \fBlogs\fP
table is part of the Kea database schemas.
.sp
Configuration parameters are extended by standard lease database
parameters as defined in Lease Database Configuration \%<#\:database-configuration4>\&. The \fBtype\fP
parameter should be \fBmysql\fP, \fBpostgresql\fP, \fBlogfile\fP or \fBsyslog\fP; when
it is absent or set to \fBlogfile\fP, files are used.
.sp
No specific tools are provided to operate the database, but standard
tools may be used, for example, to dump the logs table from a MYSQL database:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ mysql \-\-user keatest \-\-password 1234 \-e \(dqselect * from logs;\(dq
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+
| timestamp           | address      | log                                                                                                                                                             | id |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+
| 2022\-03\-30 17:38:41 | 192.168.50.1 | Address: 192.168.50.1 has been assigned for 0 hrs 10 mins 0 secs to a device with hardware address: hwtype=1 ff:01:02:03:ff:04, client\-id: 00:01:02:03:04:05:06 | 31 |
| 2022\-03\-30 17:38:43 | 192.168.50.1 | Address: 192.168.50.1 has been assigned for 0 hrs 10 mins 0 secs to a device with hardware address: hwtype=1 ff:01:02:03:ff:04, client\-id: 00:01:02:03:04:05:06 | 32 |
| 2022\-03\-30 17:38:45 | 192.168.50.1 | Address: 192.168.50.1 has been assigned for 0 hrs 10 mins 0 secs to a device with hardware address: hwtype=1 ff:01:02:03:ff:04, client\-id: 00:01:02:03:04:05:06 | 33 |
+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-+
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Like all the other database\-centric features, forensic logging supports database
connection recovery, which can be enabled by setting the \fBon\-fail\fP parameter.
If not specified, the \fBon\-fail\fP parameter in forensic logging defaults to
\fBserve\-retry\-continue\fP\&. This is different than for
\fBlibdhcp_lease_cmds.so\fP, \fBlibdhcp_host_cmds.so\fP, and
\fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP, where
\fBon\-fail\fP defaults to \fBstop\-retry\-exit\fP\&. In this case, the server continues
serving clients and does not shut down even if the recovery mechanism fails.
If \fBon\-fail\fP is set to \fBserve\-retry\-exit\fP, the server will shut down if
the connection to the database backend is not restored according to the
\fBmax\-reconnect\-tries\fP and \fBreconnect\-wait\-time\fP parameters, but it
continues serving clients while this mechanism is activated.
.sp
During server startup, the inability to connect to any of the configured
backends is considered fatal only if \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBfalse\fP
(the default). A fatal error is logged and the server exits, based on the idea
that the configuration should be valid at startup. Exiting to the operating
system allows nanny scripts to detect the problem.
If \fBretry\-on\-startup\fP is set to \fBtrue\fP, the server starts reconnection
attempts even at server startup or on reconfigure events, and honors the
action specified in the \fBon\-fail\fP parameter.
.SS Syslog Backend
.sp
Log entries can be inserted into syslog by setting the \fBtype\fP to \fBsyslog\fP\&.
When syslog type is configured, the \fBpattern\fP parameter specifies the details that
are used for logging. For more details see Logging \%<#\:logging>\&. If not configured, it defaults
to:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dq%\-5p [%c.%t] %m\en\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBfacility\fP parameter specifies the syslog facility and it defaults to \fBlocal0\fP\&.
.SS \fBlibdhcp_limits.so\fP: Limits to Manage Lease Allocation and Packet Processing
.sp
This hook library enables two types of limits:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Lease limiting: allow a maximum of \fBn\fP leases assigned at any one time.
.IP 2. 3
Rate limiting: allow a maximum of \fBn\fP packets per \fBtime_unit\fP to receive a response.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_limits.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuration
.sp
The following examples are for \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>, but they apply equally to
\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>\&. The wildcards \fB\(dq<limit\-type>\(dq\fP and \fB\(dq<limit\-value>\(dq\fP need to be replaced
with the respective keys and values for each limit type described in the sections following this
one.
.sp
The library can be loaded by both \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> servers by adding its path in the
\fB\(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq\fP element of the server\(aqs configuration.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_limits.so\(dq
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This alone does not limit anything. The desired limits are added to the user context in the
configuration portion of the element that identifies the clients to be limited: a client class or a
subnet. Upon reconfiguration, if Kea picked up on the configured limits, it logs one line for
each configured limit. The log message contains \fBLIMITS_CONFIGURED\fP in its identifier.
.sp
This is how a lease limit is defined for a client class:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqcable\-modem\-1\(dq,
        \(dqtest\(dq: \(dqoption[123].hex == 0x000C4B1E\(dq,
        \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
          \(dqlimits\(dq: {
            \(dq<limit>\(dq: \(dq<limit\-value>\(dq
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This is how a lease limit is defined for a global subnet:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::/64\(dq,
        \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
          \(dqlimits\(dq: {
            \(dq<limit>\(dq: \(dq<limit\-value>\(dq
          }
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This is how a lease limit is defined for a subnet inside a shared network:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
          {
            \(dqid\(dq: 1,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::/64\(dq,
            \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
              \(dqlimits\(dq: {
                \(dq<limit>\(dq: \(dq<limit\-value>\(dq
              }
            }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The Limits hook library uses the class name to identify a client class and the subnet ID to
identify a subnet.  Changing a test expression in a client class or the network range of a
subnet while leaving the name or ID unchanged does not reset the lease count for the
respective client class or subnet. To reset the lease count, change the client class name
or the subnet ID.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Lease Limiting
.sp
It is possible to limit the number of leases that a group of clients can get from a Kea DHCP server
or from a set of collaborating Kea DHCP servers.
.sp
The value of a lease limit can be specified as an unsigned integer in 32 bits, i.e. between \fB0\fP and
\fB4,294,967,295\fP\&. Each lease type can be limited individually. IPv4 leases and IPv6 IA_NA leases
are limited through the \fB\(dqaddress\-limit\(dq\fP configuration entry. IPv6 IA_PD leases are limited
through the \fB\(dqprefix\-limit\(dq\fP configuration entry. Here are some examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dqaddress\-limit\(dq: 4\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dqprefix\-limit\(dq: 2\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
For lease limiting, client classes and the associated lease counts \- which are
checked against the configured limits \- are updated for each lease in the following hook callouts:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4_select\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease4_renew\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6_select\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6_renew\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlease6_rebind\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
As a result, classes for which \fB\(dqonly\-in\-additional\-list\(dq\fP is \(dqtrue\(dq cannot be lease\-limited.
Please refer to the classification steps \%<#\:classify-classification-steps> for more information on which
client classes can be used to limit the number of leases.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Under load, a Kea DHCP server may allocate more leases than the limit strictly allows. This only has a chance of
happening during high traffic surges, coming from clients belonging to the same class or the
same subnet, depending on what is limited. Users may be interested in following the development of
atomic lease limits \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:kea/\:-/\:issues/\:2449> in ISC\(aqs GitLab instance.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Rate Limiting
.sp
It is possible to limit the frequency or rate at which inbound packets receive a response.
.sp
The value of a rate limit can be specified in the format \fB\(dq<p> packets per <time\-unit>\(dq\fP\&. \fB<p>\fP
is any number that can be represented by an unsigned integer in 32 bits, i.e. between \fB0\fP and
\fB4,294,967,295\fP\&. \fB<time\-unit>\fP can be any of \fBsecond\fP, \fBminute\fP, \fBhour\fP, \fBday\fP,
\fBweek\fP, \fBmonth\fP, or \fByear\fP\&. A \fBmonth\fP is considered to be 30 days for
simplicity; similarly, a \fByear\fP is 365 days for limiting purposes. This syntax
covers a wide range of rates, from one lease per year to four billion leases per
second. This value is assigned to the \fB\(dqrate\-limit\(dq\fP configuration entry.
Here are some examples:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dqrate\-limit\(dq: 1 packet per second\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dqrate\-limit\(dq: 4 packets per minute\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\(dqrate\-limit\(dq: 16 packets per hour\fP
.UNINDENT
.sp
The configured value of \fB0\fP packets is a convenient way of disabling packet processing for certain
clients entirely. As such, it means its literal value and is not a special value for disabling
limiting altogether, as might be imagined. Disabling limiting entirely is achieved by removing
the \fB\(dqrate\-limit\(dq\fP leaf configuration entry, the \fB\(dqlimits\(dq\fP map or user context
around it, or the hook library configuration. The same applies to the value of \fB0\fP in lease
limiting. However, that use case is best achieved with rate limiting; it puts less computational
strain on Kea, since the action of dropping the request or sending a NAK is decided earlier.
.sp
In terms of rate limiting, client classes are evaluated at the \fBpkt4_receive\fP and the
\fBpkt6_receive\fP callout, respectively, so that rate limits are checked as early as possible in the
packet\-processing cycle. Thus, only those classes which are assigned to the packet solely via an
independent test expression can be used. Classes that depend on host reservations or the special
\fBBOOTP\fP or \fBKNOWN\fP classes, and classes that are marked with \fB\(dqonly\-in\-additional\-list\(dq: true\fP,
cannot be rate limited. See the classification steps \%<#\:classify-classification-steps> for
more details on which client classes can be used to limit the packet rate.
.sp
Rate limits based on subnet are enforced only on the initially selected subnet for a given packet.
If the selected subnet is subsequently changed, as may be the case for subnets in a
shared network or when reselection is enabled in libraries such as the RADIUS hook, rate
limits on the newly selected subnet are ignored. In other words, packets are gated only by
the rate limit on the original subnet.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
It may seem logical to think that assigning a rate limit of \fBn\fP packets per time unit results
in \fBn\fP DORA or \fBn\fP SARR exchanges. However, by default, all inbound packets are counted \- meaning
that a full message exchange accounts for two packets. To achieve the effect of counting an
exchange only once, use client\-class rate\-limiting with a test expression that binds
\fBpkt4.msgtype\fP to DHCPDISCOVER messages or \fBpkt6.msgtype\fP to SOLICIT messages.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_mysql.so\fP: Database Backend for MySQL
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B This hook library implements MySQL database storage for:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
Lease Backend
.IP \(bu 2
Host Backend
.IP \(bu 2
Configuration Backend
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS MySQL Configuration Backend
.sp
This hook library works in conjunction with \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP to
implement the API to create, read, update, and delete (CRUD) the configuration
in a MySQL database. Please see libdhcp_cb_cmds.so: Configuration Backend Commands for more details.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_mysql.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user. However, this hook library only works with
\fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP, which allows easy configuration
management with the use of API commands. \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP
is available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract; for more
information on subscription options, please complete the form at
\%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:contact>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_perfmon.so\fP: Performance Monitoring
.sp
The Performance Monitoring (perfmon) hook library gives the Kea DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 server
the ability to track and report performance\-related data.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_perfmon.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Overview
.sp
The perfmon library, added in Kea 2.5.6, can be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> daemon by adding it to the \fBhooks\-libraries\fP element of
the server\(aqs configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_perfmon.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                ...
            }
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The library tracks the lifecycle of client query packets as they are processed by Kea,
from when the query was received by the kernel to when the response
is ready to be sent. This tracking is driven by a packet event stack, which
contains a list of event/timestamp pairs for significant milestones that
occur during the processing of a client query. The list of possible events is
shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
socket_received \- kernel placed the client query into the socket buffer
.IP 2. 3
buffer_read \- server read the client query from the socket buffer
.IP 3. 3
mt_queued \- server placed the client query into its thread\-pool work queue (multi\-threaded mode only)
.IP 4. 3
process_started \- server has begun processing the query
.IP 5. 3
process_completed \- server has constructed the response and is ready to send it
.UNINDENT
.sp
This list is likely to expand over time; it will also be possible for hook developers
to add their own events. This will be detailed in future releases.
.SS Passive Event Logging
.sp
When the perfmon hook library is loaded, it logs the packet event stack
contents for each client query that generates a response packet. The log entry
contains client query identifiers, followed by the list of event/timestamp pairs
in the order that they occurred.
.sp
For \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>, the log is identified by the label \fBPERFMON_DHCP4_PKT_EVENTS\fP,
and emitted at logger debug level 50 or higher. For a DHCPDISCOVER it is emitted
once the DHCPOFFER is ready to send, and looks similar to the following (see
the second entry):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2024\-03\-20 10:52:20.069 INFO  [kea\-dhcp4.leases/50033.140261252249344] DHCP4_LEASE_OFFER [hwtype=1 08:00:27:25:d3:f4], cid=[no info], tid=0xc288f9: lease 178.16.2.0 will be offered
2024\-03\-20 10:52:20.070 DEBUG [kea\-dhcp4.perfmon\-hooks/50033.140261252249344] PERFMON_DHCP4_PKT_EVENTS query: [hwtype=1 08:00:27:25:d3:f4], cid=[no info], tid=0xc288f9 events=[2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067563 : socket_received, 2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067810 : buffer_read, 2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067897 : mt_queued, 2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067952 : process_started, 2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.069614 : process_completed]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>, the log is identified by the label \fBPERFMON_DHCP6_PKT_EVENTS\fP,
and emitted at logger debug level 50 or higher. For a DHCPV6_SOLICIT it is emitted
once the DHCPV6_ADVERTISE is ready to send, and looks similar to the following (see
the second entry):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2024\-03\-20 10:22:14.030 INFO  [kea\-dhcp6.leases/47195.139913679886272] DHCP6_LEASE_ADVERT duid=[00:03:00:01:08:00:27:25:d3:f4], [no hwaddr info], tid=0xb54806: lease for address 3002:: and iaid=11189196 will be advertised
2024\-03\-20 10:22:14.031 DEBUG [kea\-dhcp6.perfmon\-hooks/47195.139913679886272] PERFMON_DHCP6_PKT_EVENTS query: duid=[00:03:00:01:08:00:27:25:d3:f4], [no hwaddr info], tid=0xb54806 events=[2024\-Mar\-20 14:22:14.028729 : socket_received, 2024\-Mar\-20 14:22:14.028924 : buffer_read, 2024\-Mar\-20 14:22:14.029005 : process_started, 2024\-Mar\-20 14:22:14.030566 : process_completed]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Duration Monitoring
.sp
When monitoring is enabled, stack event data is aggregated over a specified interval. These
aggregates are referred to as \(dqmonitored durations\(dq or simply \(dqdurations\(dq for ease. Durations are
uniquely identified by a \(dqduration key\(dq which consists of the following values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
query message type \- message type of the client query (e.g.DHCPDISCOVER, DHCPV6_REQUEST)
.IP \(bu 2
response message type \- message type of the server response (e.g. DHCPOFFER, DHCPV6_REPLY)
.IP \(bu 2
start event label \- event that defines the beginning of the task (e.g. socket_received, process_started)
.IP \(bu 2
stop event label \- event that defines the end of the task (e.g. buffer_read, process_completed)
.IP \(bu 2
subnet id \- subnet selected during message processing (or 0 for global durations)
.UNINDENT
.sp
Once the server has finished constructing a response to a query, the query\(aqs event stack
is processed into a series of updates to monitored durations. If, upon updating, a
duration\(aqs sample interval is found to have been completed, it is sent to reporting
and a new sample interval is begun. The interval width is dictated by configuration
parameter \fBinterval\-width\-secs\fP\&.
.sp
The event stack for the multi\-threaded mode DHCPDISCOVER/DHCPOFFER cycle shown above
contains the following events:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Event Timestamp
T}	T{
Event Label
T}
_
T{
2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067563
T}	T{
socket_received
T}
_
T{
2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067810
T}	T{
buffer_read
T}
_
T{
2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067897
T}	T{
mt_queued
T}
_
T{
2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.067952
T}	T{
process_started
T}
_
T{
2024\-Mar\-20 14:52:20.069614
T}	T{
process_completed
T}
.TE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Assuming the selected subnet\(aqs ID was 100, the duration updates created by perfmon
from these events are shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Duration Keys for SubnetID 100
T}	T{
Update in
microseconds
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.socket_received\-buffer_read.100
T}	T{
247
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.buffer_read\-mt_queue.100
T}	T{
87
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.mt_queued\-process_started.100
T}	T{
55
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.process_started\-process_completed.100
T}	T{
1662
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.composite\-total_response.100
T}	T{
2051
T}
.TE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Notice that in addition to the adjacent event updates, there is an additional duration
update for the total duration of the entire stack whose key contains the event\-pair
\fBcomposite\-total_response\fP\&. This tracks the total time to respond from when the query
is received until the response is ready to send. Finally, there are also global
duration updates for each of the above:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Global Duration Keys
T}	T{
Update in
microseconds
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.socket_received\-buffer_read.0
T}	T{
247
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.buffer_read\-mt_queue.0
T}	T{
87
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.mt_queued\-process_started.0
T}	T{
55
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.process_started\-process_completed.0
T}	T{
1662
T}
_
T{
DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.composite\-total_response.0
T}	T{
2051
T}
.TE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Statistics Reporting
.sp
When enabled, perfmon reports a duration\(aqs data each time the duration completes a
sampling interval (see \fBstats\-mgr\-reporting\fP under Configuration). Each
statistic employs the following naming convention:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{subnet\-id[x]}.perfmon.<query type>\-<response type>.<start event>\-<end event>.<value\-name>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
There is both a global and a subnet\-specific value for each. Currently, the only
value reported for a given duration key is \fBmean\-usecs\fP; this statistic is the mean time
between the duration\(aqs event pair over the most recently completed interval. In other
words, if during a given interval there were seven occurrences (i.e. updates) totaling
3500us, the \fBmean\-usecs\fP reported would be 500us. Continuing with the example above, the
statistics reported are named as follows for the subnet\-level values:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
subnet[100].perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.socket_received\-buffer_read.mean\-usecs
subnet[100].perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.buffer_read\-mt_queue.mean\-usecs
subnet[100].perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.mt_queued\-process_started.mean\-usecs
subnet[100].perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.process_started\-process_completed.mean\-usecs
subnet[100].perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.composite\-total_response.mean\-usecs
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
and as shown for global values:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.socket_received\-buffer_read.mean\-usecs
perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.buffer_read\-mt_queue.mean\-usecs
perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.mt_queued\-process_started.mean\-usecs
perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.process_started\-process_completed.mean\-usecs
perfmon.DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.composite\-total_response.mean\-usecs
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The results are reported to StatsMgr, an internal Kea component that reports data as statistics
that can be retrieved using statistics commands. They can be fetched using the commands
\fBstatistic\-get\-all\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-statistic-get-all>, \fBstatistic\-global\-get\-all\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-statistic-global-get-all>, or \fBstatistic\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-statistic-get>\&.
.SS Alarms
.sp
Alarms may be defined to watch specific durations. Each alarm defines a high\-water mark,
\fBhigh\-water\-ms\fP, and a low\-water mark, \fBlow\-water\-ms\fP\&. If the reported mean value
for the duration exceeds the high\-water mark a WARN level alarm log is emitted, at which
point the alarm is considered \(dqtriggered.\(dq Once triggered, the WARN level log is
repeated at the alarm report interval specified by \fBalarm\-report\-secs\fP, as long as the reported
mean for the duration remains above the low\-water mark. Once the mean falls below the
low\-water mark the alarm is cleared and an INFO level log is emitted.
.sp
The alarm\-triggered WARN log looks similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2024\-03\-20 10:22:14.030 WARN [kea\-dhcp6.leases/47195.139913679886272] PERFMON_ALARM_TRIGGERED Alarm for DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.composite\-total_response.0 has been triggered since 2024\-03\-20 10:18:20.070000, reported mean duration 00:00:00.700000 exceeds high\-water\-ms: 500
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The alarm\-cleared INFO log looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2024\-03\-20 10:30:14.030 INFO [kea\-dhcp6.leases/47195.139913679886272] PERFMON_ALARM_CLEARED Alarm for DHCPDISCOVER.DHCPOFFER.composite\-total_response.0 has been cleared, reported mean duration 00:00:00.010000 is now below low\-water\-ms: 25
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS API Commands
.SS The \fBperfmon\-control\fP Command
.sp
This command can be used to enable or disable active monitoring and statistics
reporting at runtime without altering or reloading configuration.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
     \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-control\(dq,
     \(dqarguments\(dq: {
         \(dqenable\-monitoring\(dq: true,
         \(dqstats\-mgr\-reporting\(dq: false
     }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Regardless of the arguments (if any) supplied, the resulting values of both
flags are always returned:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-control success\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
         \(dqenable\-monitoring\(dq: true,
         \(dqstats\-mgr\-reporting\(dq: false
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBperfmon\-get\-all\-durations\fP Command
.sp
This command fetches all monitored duration data currently held in memory by
the Perfmon hook library.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-get\-all\-durations\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqresult\-set\-format\(dq: true
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A result of 0 is returned if command succeeds along with monitored duration data,
while a result of 1 is returned if command is invalid or command processing
encounters an error.
.sp
The format of the monitored duration data returned is determined by the
optional argument, \fBresult\-set\-format\fP\&.  When false, (the default), the list
of durations will be returned as a list of individual elements as shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
       \(dqdurations\(dq: [{
           \(dqduration\-key\(dq: {
               \(dqquery\-type\(dq: \(dqDHCPDISCOVER\(dq,
               \(dqresponse\-type\(dq: \(dqDHCPOFFER\(dq,
               \(dqstart\-event\(dq: \(dqbuffer_read\(dq,
               \(dqstop\-event\(dq: \(dqmt_queued\(dq,
               \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 0
           },
           \(dqmax\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 118,
           \(dqmin\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 31,
           \(dqoccurrences\(dq: 501,
           \(dqstart\-time\(dq: \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:06.814884\(dq,
           \(dqtotal\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 23951,
           \(dqmean\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 47
        },
        ...
       ],
       \(dqresult\-set\-format\(dq: false,
       \(dqinterval\-width\-secs\(dq: 5,
       \(dqtimestamp\(dq: \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:22.397233\(dq
   },
\(dqresult\(dq: 0,
\(dqtext\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-get\-all\-durations: n found\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When \fBresult\-set\-format\fP is true, the list of durations will be returned in
a format similar to an SQL result set as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqdurations\-result\-set\(dq: {
            \(dqcolumns\(dq: [
                \(dqquery\-type\(dq,
                \(dqresponse\-type\(dq,
                \(dqstart\-event\(dq,
                \(dqend\-event\(dq,
                \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq,
                \(dqinterval\-start\(dq,
                \(dqoccurrence\(dq,
                \(dqmin\-duration\-usecs\(dq,
                \(dqmax\-duration\-usecs\(dq,
                \(dqtotal\-duration\-usecs\(dq,
                \(dqmean\-duration\-usecs\(dq
            ],
            \(dqrows\(dq: [ [
                \(dqDHCPDISCOVER\(dq,
                \(dqDHCPOFFER\(dq,
                \(dqbuffer_read\(dq,
                \(dqmt_queued\(dq,
                0,
                \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:06.814884\(dq,
                501,
                31,
                118,
                23951,
                47
                ],
                ...
            ]
        },
        \(dqresult\-set\-format\(dq: true,
        \(dqinterval\-width\-secs\(dq: 5,
        \(dqtimestamp\(dq: \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:22.397233\(dq
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-get\-all\-durations: n found\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The data values for each duration will be from the duration\(aqs last completed data
interval.  If a duration has no such interval, \fBinterval\-start\fP will be reported
as \(dq<none>\(dq and the remaining values will be zero.
.SS Configuration
.sp
Use of the performance monitoring hook library is configured via several parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B enable\-monitoring
This parameter enables event data aggregation for reporting, statistics, and alarms. It defaults to \fBfalse\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B interval\-width\-secs
This specifies the amount of time, in seconds, that individual task durations are accumulated into an
aggregate before they are reported. The default is 60 seconds.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B stats\-mgr\-reporting
This enables the reporting of aggregates to StatsMgr. It defaults to \fBtrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B alarm\-report\-secs
This specifies the amount of time, in seconds, between logging instances for an alarm once it has been triggered.
It defaults to 300 seconds.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B alarms
This is an optional list of alarms that monitor specific duration aggregates. Each alarm is
defined by these four parameters:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B duration\-key
Identifies the monitored duration for the following:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
query\-type \- message type of the client query (e.g.DHCPDISCOVER, DHCPV6_REQUEST)
.IP \(bu 2
response\-type \- message type of the server response (e.g. DHCPOFFER, DHCPV6_REPLY)
.IP \(bu 2
start\-event \- event that defines the beginning of the task (e.g. socket_received, process_started)
.IP \(bu 2
stop\-event \- event that defines the end of the task
.IP \(bu 2
subnet\-id \- subnet selected during message processing (or 0 for global durations)
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B enable\-alarm
Enables or disables this alarm; defaults to \fBtrue\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B high\-water\-ms
Specifies the value, in milliseconds, over which the duration must occur to trigger this alarm;
must be greater than zero.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
.INDENT 2.0
.TP
.B low\-water\-ms
Specifies the value, in milliseconds, under which the duration must fall to clear this alarm;
must be greater than zero but less than \fBhigh\-water\-ms\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Passive event logging is always enabled, even without specifying the \(dqparameters\(dq section.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A sample configuration is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_perfmon.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqenable\-monitoring\(dq: true,
            \(dqinterval\-width\-secs\(dq: 5,
            \(dqstats\-mgr\-reporting\(dq: true,
            \(dqalarm\-report\-secs\(dq: 600,
            \(dqalarms\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqduration\-key\(dq: {
                    \(dqquery\-type\(dq: \(dqDHCPDISCOVER\(dq,
                    \(dqresponse\-type\(dq: \(dqDHCPOFFER\(dq,
                    \(dqstart\-event\(dq: \(dqprocess\-started\(dq,
                    \(dqstop\-event\(dq: \(dqprocess\-completed\(dq,
                    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 0
                },
                \(dqenable\-alarm\(dq: true,
                \(dqhigh\-water\-ms\(dq: 500,
                \(dqlow\-water\-ms\(dq: 25
            }]
        }
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_ping_check.so\fP: Ping Check
.sp
This hook library adds the ability to perform a \(dqping check\(dq of a candidate
IPv4 address prior to offering it to a DHCP client. This feature is similar
to a behavior available in ISC DHCP and one suggested in RFC
2131 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2131>, section 3.1, item 2.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_ping_check.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Overview
.sp
The Ping Check library, added in Kea 2.5.4, can be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> daemon
by adding it to the \fBhooks\-libraries\fP element of the server\(aqs configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ping_check.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                ...
            }
        },
        ...
    ],
    ...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When the library is loaded, \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> conducts a ping check prior to
offering a lease to a client if all of the following conditions are true:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
The ping check hook library is loaded.
.IP 2. 3
Ping checking is enabled.
.IP 3. 3
The server is responding to a DHCPDISCOVER.
.IP 4. 3
The candidate lease is neither active nor reserved.
.IP 5. 3
Any of the following are true:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
a. This is the first offer of this lease to this client. This check
can only be done if \fBoffer\-lifetime\fP is greater than zero (i.e. temporary
allocation on DHCPDISCOVER is enabled). If \fBoffer\-lifetime\fP is zero
ping checks are done for every DHCPOFFER, as the server has no way to
know whether prior offers were made.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP b. 3
The lease is being offered to a client other than its previous owner.
.UNINDENT
.sp
c. The lease is being offered to its previous owner and more than the
number of seconds specified by \fBping\-cltt\-secs\fP have elapsed since the
CLTT of the original lease.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When the ping check library is loaded, the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> server
responds to a DHCPDISCOVER by:
.sp
1. Selecting a candidate IPv4 address through normal processes and using it to
construct a DHCPOFFER.
.sp
2. Parking the DHCPOFFER and requesting a ping check from the ping check hook
library, via its \fBlease4_offer\fP callout. Refer to Parked\-Packet Limit for
a basic introduction to packet parking.
.sp
3. Testing the callout conditions described above: if they are not
satisfied, it returns without conducting a check, and the server
sends the DHCPOFFER to the client. Otherwise, the callout
initiates a ping check for the lease address.
.sp
4. Upon conclusion of the ping check, the server either sends the DHCPOFFER
to the client, if the check concluded that the address is available, or discards
the DHCPFOFFER and creates a DECLINED lease for the address.
.sp
Each ping check consists of the following steps:
.sp
1. If the number of ECHO REPLYs sent is less than the configured
minimum number to send, send an ICMP ECHO REQUEST to the lease address.
Otherwise, conclude that the address is available.
.sp
2. If no ECHO REPLY is received within a configurable amount of time,
return to step 1.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 3. 3
Upon receipt of an ICMP ECHO REPLY, conclude that the lease is NOT available.
.IP 4. 3
If any of the following occur:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP a. 3
Receipt of an ICMP DESTINATION UNREACHABLE message
.IP b. 3
Send failure of an ICMP ECHO REQUEST due to a network error (e.g. network is unreachable)
.IP c. 3
Send failure of an ICMP ECHO REQUEST due to a permissions error (e.g. lease address is a broadcast address)
.IP d. 3
Send failure of an ICMP ECHO REQUEST with socket buffer full error
.UNINDENT
.sp
skip the address check and treat it as available.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\(dqSocket buffer full\(dq errors indicate that the OS rate limits on ICMP are
being exceeded. The server does not retry them, as this would likely only
exacerbate the situation. If this occurs continuously, then the client load
on the server may be too high to accommodate ping checking. Ping checking is
not recommended for systems with high throughput demands.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuration
.sp
The ping check hook library currently supports the following configuration parameters
that may be set at the global and subnet levels; subnet values override global values:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenable\-ping\-check\fP \- This parameter enables or disables ping checking at a given scope.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmin\-ping\-requests\fP \- This is the minimum number of ECHO REQUESTs sent without receiving a reply, before an address is declared available. The default is 1; it must be greater than zero.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreply\-timeout\fP \- This is the maximum amount of time to wait for a reply to a single ECHO REQUEST. Specified in milliseconds, it must be greater than zero; the default is 100.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBping\-cltt\-secs\fP \- This specifies the number of seconds that must elapse after the lease\(aqs CLTT before a ping check is conducted, when the client is the lease\(aqs previous owner. The default value is 60 seconds.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following parameter is only supported at the global level:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBping\-channel\-threads\fP \- In multi\-threaded mode, this is the number of threads in the channel\(aqs thread pool. The default is 0, which instructs the library to use the same number of threads as the Kea core. This value is ignored if given when Kea is in single\-threaded mode.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following configuration excerpt illustrates a global\-level configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [{
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_ping_check.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            \(dqenable\-ping\-check\(dq : true,
            \(dqmin\-ping\-requests\(dq : 1,
            \(dqreply\-timeout\(dq : 100,
            \(dqping\-cltt\-secs\(dq : 60,
            \(dqping\-channel\-threads\(dq : 0
        }
    }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following excerpt demonstrates a subnet\-level configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
 {
 \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [{
     \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
     \(dqpools\(dq: [{
         \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.10 \- 192.0.2.20\(dq
     }],

     \(dquser\-context\(dq: {
         \(dqping\-check\(dq: {
             \(dqenable\-ping\-check\(dq : true,
             \(dqmin\-ping\-requests\(dq : 2,
             \(dqreply\-timeout\(dq : 250,
             \(dqping\-cltt\-secs\(dq : 120
         }
     }
 }]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_pgsql.so\fP: Database Backend for PostgreSQL
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B This hook library implements PostgreSQL database storage for:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
Lease Backend
.IP \(bu 2
Host Backend
.IP \(bu 2
Configuration Backend
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS PostgreSQL Configuration Backend
.sp
This hook library works in conjunction with \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP to
implement the API to create, read, update, and delete (CRUD) the configuration
in a PostgreSQL database. Please see libdhcp_cb_cmds.so: Configuration Backend Commands for more details.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_pgsql.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user. However, this hook library only works with
\fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP, which allows easy configuration
management with the use of API commands. \fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP
is available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract; for more
information on subscription options, please complete the form at
\%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:contact>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_radius.so\fP: RADIUS Server Support
.sp
This hook library allows the Kea DHCP servers to use the RADIUS protocol to
authorize DHCP clients through the access service, or for DHCP lease journaling
through the accounting service. For details on RADIUS in Kea, please see
RADIUS \%<#\:radius>\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_radius.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_rbac.so\fP: Role\-Based Access Control
.SS Role\-Based Access Control (RBAC) Overview
.sp
Before the processing of commands in received HTTP requests, \fBlibdhcp_rbac.so\fP
takes specific parameters, e.g. the common\-name part of the client
certificate subject name, to assign a role to the request.
The configuration associated with this role is used to accept or reject
the command. After processing, the response can be rewritten, e.g.
parts can be removed.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Here is a summary of the steps in processing a request:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
The HTTP library records some information to be used later, e.g.
the remote address.
.IP \(bu 2
When TLS is required but the request was not protected by TLS,
the request is rejected by sending an \(dqunauthorized\(dq response.
.IP \(bu 2
The command is extracted from the request.
.IP \(bu 2
A role is assigned using recorded information in the request.
.IP \(bu 2
The role is used to accept (pass through) or reject (send
a forbidden response to) the command.
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Here is a summary of the steps in processing a response:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
The information attached to the request is retrieved during the
request processing (when the request was accepted).
.IP \(bu 2
Request filters are applied to the response.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_rbac.so\fP is available only to ISC customers with
a paid support contract. For more information on subscription options,
please complete the form at \%<https://\:www\:.isc\:.org/\:contact>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Since Kea 2.7.2 the RBAC is no longer limited to the Control Agent:
it can be used by any other server supporting HTTP/HTTPS control
sockets e.g. DHCPv4, DHCPv6 and DDNS servers.
Since Kea 3.1.8 the obsolete Control Agent has been removed.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Role\-Based Access Control Configuration
.SS Role Assignment
.sp
Role assignment is governed by the configured role\-assignment method.
.SS Role assignment methods
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
remote\-address
T}	T{
remote/client IP address
T}
_
T{
cert\-subject
T}	T{
common\-name part of the client certificate subject name
T}
_
T{
cert\-issuer
T}	T{
common\-name part of the client certificate issuer name
T}
_
T{
basic\-authentication
T}	T{
user ID of basic HTTP authentication
T}
_
T{
custom\-value
T}	T{
another role can be designed in external hooks
T}
.TE
.SS Role Configuration
.SS Role configuration parameters
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
name
T}	T{
the role name (with the exception of the default
and unknown roles)
T}
_
T{
accept\-commands
T}	T{
the accept access list
T}
_
T{
reject\-commands
T}	T{
the reject access list
T}
_
T{
other\-commands
T}	T{
specifies what to do for commands not matching
accept and reject lists (default: reject)
T}
_
T{
list\-match\-first
T}	T{
specifies what to do for commands matching both
the accept and reject list by giving the list to
check and apply first (default: accept)
T}
_
T{
response\-filters
T}	T{
the filters to apply to responses
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The role assignment may fail with \fBcert\-subject\fP when
the client certificate was not required, or it may have no subject common
name and instead have a DNS alternative subject name. In this case, the role
assignment returns the empty role and the \fBdefault\-role\fP entry is used.
.sp
The role assignment can return an unexpected value, e.g. with an
unregistered role name or a typing error. In this case the \fBunknown\-role\fP
entry is used.
.sp
The default for both \fBdefault\-role\fP and \fBunknown\-role\fP is to reject all commands.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS API Commands
.sp
All commands of the REST API are described in files in the source directory
\fBsrc/share/api\fP, or in installed Kea
in \fB\&.../share/kea/api\fP\&. \fBlibdhcp_rbac.so\fP reads these files to
take the name, the access right (i.e. \fBread\fP or \fBwrite\fP), and the hook
name. The access right can be modified in the file but changes are only
applied after the server or agent restarts. Removing command definitions
from \fB\&.../share/kea/api\fP has consequences: if the access control list is
based on \fBread\fP or \fBwrite\fP and the definition file is missing, the
server or agent always rejects such a command. If the access controls list
is using \fBcommands\fP to specify the name of a command and the definition
file from \fB\&.../share/kea/api\fP of this particular command is missing,
the Kea server logs an error on startup and exit.
.SS Extra command\-definition parameters
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
name
T}	T{
(mandatory) the command name
T}
_
T{
access
T}	T{
(mandatory) the access right i.e. \fBread\fP or \fBwrite\fP
T}
_
T{
hook
T}	T{
(optional) the hook name (empty or not\-present for
commands of servers or agents)
T}
.TE
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
These command description files are security\-sensitive, e.g. with
too\-permissive access rights a local attacker may modify them and
defeat the RBAC goal.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Access Control Lists
.sp
Access control lists can be specified using a name (string) or a
single entry map.
.SS Predefined named access list
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
ALL
T}	T{
matches everything
T}
_
T{
NONE
T}	T{
matches nothing
T}
_
T{
READ
T}	T{
matches commands with the read\-access right
T}
_
T{
WRITE
T}	T{
matches commands with the write\-access right
T}
.TE
.sp
Map access list specifications use a list type in the name of the single entry
and parameter in the value.
.SS Access list types
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Description
T}	T{
Parameter
T}
_
T{
not
T}	T{
logical not
T}	T{
access list
T}
_
T{
and
T}	T{
logical and
T}	T{
list of access lists
T}
_
T{
or
T}	T{
logical or
T}	T{
list of access lists
T}
_
T{
command
T}	T{
explicit list
T}	T{
list of command names
T}
_
T{
access
T}	T{
by access right
T}	T{
access right (\fBread\fP or \fBwrite\fP)
T}
_
T{
hook
T}	T{
by hook
T}	T{
hook name (can be empty)
T}
.TE
.SS Response Filters
.SS Predefined response filters
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
list\-commands
T}	T{
Removes not\-allowed commands from the
list\-commands response
T}
.TE
.SS Global Parameters
.sp
The global parameters are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBassign\-role\-method\fP: the name of the method
which is used for role assignment. This parameter is mandatory.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBapi\-files\fP: the path of the directory where
the API files describing commands can be found. This parameter is mandatory.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrequire\-tls\fP: the specification of whether received requests on HTTP (vs HTTPS) are
rejected. It defaults to \fBfalse\fP when the role\-assignment method is not
based on certificates.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcommands\fP: the list of extra command configurations.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaccess\-control\-lists\fP: the named access control list definitions
(each definition is a single entry map; the name of the entry is
the name of the access list, and the value is the specification).
The name is used in other parts of the configuration, such as \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBroles\fP: the role configurations.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdefault\-role\fP: the configuration of the default role (used
when \(dq\(dq is assigned).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBunknown\-role\fP: the configuration of the unknown role
(used when the not\-empty assigned role has no configuration).
.UNINDENT
.SS Sample Configurations
.sp
Sample configurations are available at kea4/hooks\-rbac.json \%<#\:config-example-kea4-hooks-rbac-json>
and kea6/hooks\-rbac.json \%<#\:config-example-kea6-hooks-rbac-json>\&.
.SS Accept/Reject Algorithm
.sp
This is the pseudo\-code of the accept/reject decision algorithm which returns
\fBtrue\fP (accept) or \fBfalse\fP (reject).
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
bool match(command) {
    if (list\-match\-first == accept) {
        if (accept_list && accept_list\->match(command)) {
            return (true);
        }
        if (reject_list && reject_list\->match(command)) {
            return (false);
        }
    } else {
        if (reject_list && reject_list\->match(command)) {
            return (false);
        }
        if (accept_list && accept_list\->match(command)) {
            return (true);
        }
    }
    if (others == reject) {
       return (false);
    } else {
       return (true);
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Custom Hook Commands and Command Redefinition
.sp
It is possible to have a custom hook with new commands. In this case,
Role Based Access Control can be used to manage a new command in two ways.
.sp
The \fBcommand\fP global parameter can be used to define its name, access type,
and hook name:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqcommands\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqmy\-new\-command\(dq,
            \(dqaccess\(dq: \(dqwrite\(dq,
            \(dqhook\(dq: \(dqmy\-custom\-hook\(dq
        }
    ],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The new command can then be specified in \fBroles\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqroles\(dq: [
    {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dquser1\(dq,
        \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq: {
            \(dqcommands\(dq: [ \(dqmy\-new\-command\(dq ] },
        \(dqreject\-commands\(dq: \(dqWRITE\(dq,
        \(dqlist\-match\-first\(dq: \(dqaccept\(dq
    },
    {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dquser2\(dq,
        \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq: { \(dqhook\(dq: \(dqmy\-custom\-hook\(dq },
        \(dqreject\-commands\(dq: \(dqALL\(dq,
        \(dqlist\-match\-first\(dq: \(dqaccept\(dq
    }
],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The second method is to create a custom file in \fB\&.../share/kea/api\fP and define
the access type of the custom command(s).
.sp
It is also possible to redefine an existing command by removing its definition
file from \fB\&.../share/kea/api\fP and defining it in the \fBcommands\fP global parameter:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqcommands\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdhcp\-disable\(dq,
            \(dqaccess\(dq: \(dqread\(dq,
            \(dqhook\(dq: \(dqmy\-custom\-hook\-3\(dq
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
With this approach, an administrator can put the configurations of all existing
commands inside the server\(aqs configuration file.
.SS Extensive Example
.sp
Here is an extensive example for a role accepting all read commands, with
the exception of \fBconfig\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-get>, e.g. for hiding passwords. For any remote
user who is not recognized as \(dquser1\(dq, all commands should be rejected.
.sp
The first option is to put the allowed commands in the \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq
list and to reject anything else:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqroles\(dq: [
{
    \(dqname\(dq: \(dquser1\(dq,
    \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq:
    {
        \(dqand\(dq: [
            \(dqREAD\(dq,
            { \(dqnot\(dq:
                { \(dqcommands\(dq: [ \(dqconfig\-get\(dq ] }
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqreject\-commands\(dq: \(dqALL\(dq,
    // This is the default but as the config relies on it
    // it is explicitly set.
    \(dqlist\-match\-first\(dq: \(dqaccept\(dq
},
\&...
],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A common alternative is not to set the \(dqreject\-commands\(dq list, i.e. leave
it empty and rely on \(dqother\-commands\(dq to reject anything else.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqroles\(dq: [
{
    \(dqname\(dq: \(dquser2\(dq,
    \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq:
    {
        \(dqand\(dq: [
            \(dqREAD\(dq,
            { \(dqnot\(dq:
                { \(dqcommands\(dq: [ \(dqconfig\-get\(dq ] }
            }
        ]
    },
    // This is the default but as the config relies on it
    // it is explicitly set.
    \(dqother\-commands\(dq: \(dqreject\(dq
},
\&...
],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is also possible to do the opposite, i.e. to set only the \(dqreject\-commands\(dq list:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqroles\(dq: [
{
    \(dqname\(dq: \(dquser3\(dq,
    \(dqreject\-commands\(dq:
    {
        \(dqor\(dq: [
            \(dqWRITE\(dq,
            { \(dqcommands\(dq: [ \(dqconfig\-get\(dq ] }
        ]
    },
    \(dqother\-commands\(dq: \(dqaccept\(dq
},
\&...
],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Or use both lists with the exception in the \(dqreject\-commands\(dq list,
which must be checked first as \(dqconfig\-get\(dq has the read\-access right.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqroles\(dq: [
{
    \(dqname\(dq: \(dquser4\(dq,
    \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq: \(dqREAD\(dq,
    \(dqreject\-commands\(dq: { \(dqcommands\(dq: [ \(dqconfig\-get\(dq ] },
    \(dqlist\-match\-first\(dq: \(dqreject\(dq
},
\&...
],
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To check any configuration, it is a good idea to use the \(dqlist\-commands\(dq
response filter, which shows errors such as missing (rejected) commands
and extra (accepted) commands.
.sp
\fBaccess\-control\-lists\fP can be used for definitions of access control lists
and later reused in \fBroles\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
\(dqaccess\-control\-lists\(dq:[
    {
        \(dqmy\-list\-one\(dq:{
            \(dqor\(dq:[
            {
                \(dqhook\(dq: \(dqsubnet_cmds\(dq
            },
            {
                \(dqcommands\(dq:[ \(dqlist\-commands\(dq ]
            }
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        \(dqmy\-list\-two\(dq:{
            \(dqand\(dq:[
            \(dqREAD\(dq,
            {
                \(dqnot\(dq:{
                    \(dqcommands\(dq:[ \(dqconfig\-get\(dq ]
                }
            }
            ]
        }
    },
    {
        \(dqmy\-list\-three\(dq:{
            \(dqor\(dq:[
            { \(dqhook\(dq:\(dqsubnet_cmds\(dq },
            { \(dqhook\(dq:\(dqclass_cmds\(dq },
            { \(dqhook\(dq:\(dqlease_cmds\(dq }
            ]
        }
    }
],
\(dqroles\(dq:[
    {
        \(dqname\(dq:\(dqadmin\(dq,
        \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq:\(dqmy\-list\-one\(dq,
        \(dqreject\-commands\(dq:\(dqALL\(dq,
        \(dqlist\-match\-first\(dq:\(dqaccept\(dq
    },
    {
        \(dqname\(dq:\(dqadmin2\(dq,
        \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq:\(dqmy\-list\-two\(dq,
        \(dqreject\-commands\(dq:\(dqALL\(dq,
        \(dqlist\-match\-first\(dq:\(dqaccept\(dq
    }
],
\(dqunknown\-role\(dq:{
    \(dqaccept\-commands\(dq:\(dqmy\-list\-three\(dq,
    \(dqreject\-commands\(dq:\(dqALL\(dq
},
\&...
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_run_script.so\fP: Run Script Support for External Hook Scripts
.sp
The Run Script hook library adds support for calling an external script for specific
packet\-processing hook points.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_run_script.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_run_script.so\(dq,
            \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq/full_path_to/script_name.sh\(dq,
                \(dqsync\(dq: false
            }
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The parameters contain the \fBname\fP, which indicates the full path to the external
script to be called on each hook point, and also the \fBsync\fP option, to be able
to wait synchronously for the script to finish execution.
If the \fBsync\fP parameter is \fBfalse\fP, then the script will launch and Kea
will not wait for the execution to finish, causing all the OUT parameters of
the script (including the next step) to be ignored.
.sp
The external script must be stored in the \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/share/kea/scripts/\(dq\fP
directory which can be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_HOOK_SCRIPTS_PATH\fP to a different path.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The script inherits all privileges from the server which calls it.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Currently, enabling synchronous calls to external scripts is not supported.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This library has several hook\-point functions implemented, which are
called at the specific packet\-processing stage.
.sp
The dhcpv4 hook points:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
lease4_renew
lease4_expire
lease4_recover
leases4_committed
lease4_release
lease4_decline
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The dhcpv6 hook points:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
lease6_renew
lease6_rebind
lease6_expire
lease6_recover
leases6_committed
lease6_release
lease6_decline
addr6_register
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Each hook point extracts the Kea internal data and exports it as string
environment variables. These parameters are shared with the target script
using the child process environment.
The only parameter passed to the call of the target script is the name of
the hook point.
.sp
An example of a script implementing all hook points is presented below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/bin/sh

unknown_handle() {
    echo \(dqUnhandled function call ${*}\(dq
    exit 123
}


lease4_renew () {
    ...
}

lease4_expire () {
    ...
}

lease4_recover () {
    ...
}

leases4_committed () {
    ...
}

lease4_release () {
    ...
}

lease4_decline () {
    ...
}

lease6_renew () {
    ...
}

lease6_rebind () {
    ...
}

lease6_expire () {
    ...
}

lease6_recover () {
    ...
}

leases6_committed () {
    ...
}

lease6_release () {
    ...
}

lease6_decline () {
    ...
}

addr6_register () {
    ...
}

case \(dq$1\(dq in
    \(dqlease4_renew\(dq)
        lease4_renew
        ;;
    \(dqlease4_expire\(dq)
        lease4_expire
        ;;
    \(dqlease4_recover\(dq)
        lease4_recover
        ;;
    \(dqleases4_committed\(dq)
        leases4_committed
        ;;
    \(dqlease4_release\(dq)
        lease4_release
        ;;
    \(dqlease4_decline\(dq)
        lease4_decline
        ;;
    \(dqlease6_renew\(dq)
        lease6_renew
        ;;
    \(dqlease6_rebind\(dq)
        lease6_rebind
        ;;
    \(dqlease6_expire\(dq)
        lease6_expire
        ;;
    \(dqlease6_recover\(dq)
        lease6_recover
        ;;
    \(dqleases6_committed\(dq)
        leases6_committed
        ;;
    \(dqlease6_release\(dq)
        lease6_release
        ;;
    \(dqlease6_decline\(dq)
        lease6_decline
        ;;
    \(dqaddr6_register\(dq)
        addr6_register
        ;;
    *)
        unknown_handle \(dq${@}\(dq
        ;;
esac
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Available parameters for each hook point are presented below.
.sp
DHCPv4:
.sp
\fBlease4_renew\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
QUERY4_TYPE
QUERY4_TXID
QUERY4_LOCAL_ADDR
QUERY4_LOCAL_PORT
QUERY4_REMOTE_ADDR
QUERY4_REMOTE_PORT
QUERY4_IFACE_INDEX
QUERY4_IFACE_NAME
QUERY4_HOPS
QUERY4_SECS
QUERY4_FLAGS
QUERY4_CIADDR
QUERY4_SIADDR
QUERY4_YIADDR
QUERY4_GIADDR
QUERY4_RELAYED
QUERY4_HWADDR
QUERY4_HWADDR_TYPE
QUERY4_LOCAL_HWADDR
QUERY4_LOCAL_HWADDR_TYPE
QUERY4_REMOTE_HWADDR
QUERY4_REMOTE_HWADDR_TYPE
QUERY4_OPTION_82
QUERY4_OPTION_82_SUB_OPTION_1
QUERY4_OPTION_82_SUB_OPTION_2
SUBNET4_ID
SUBNET4_NAME
SUBNET4_PREFIX
SUBNET4_PREFIX_LEN
PKT4_CLIENT_ID
PKT4_HWADDR
PKT4_HWADDR_TYPE
LEASE4_ADDRESS
LEASE4_CLTT
LEASE4_HOSTNAME
LEASE4_HWADDR
LEASE4_HWADDR_TYPE
LEASE4_STATE
LEASE4_SUBNET_ID
LEASE4_VALID_LIFETIME
LEASE4_CLIENT_ID
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlease4_expire\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
LEASE4_ADDRESS
LEASE4_CLTT
LEASE4_HOSTNAME
LEASE4_HWADDR
LEASE4_HWADDR_TYPE
LEASE4_STATE
LEASE4_SUBNET_ID
LEASE4_VALID_LIFETIME
LEASE4_CLIENT_ID
REMOVE_LEASE
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
