.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The OLD_LEASE6 does not always exist.
.sp
The leases4_committed hook point needs for loops to handle the list of addresses.
This can be achieved in the following way:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
leases4_committed() {
    for i in $(seq 0 $((LEASES4_SIZE\-1))); do
        LEASE4_ADDRESS=$(eval \(dqecho \e$LEASES4_AT${i}_ADDRESS\(dq)
        ...
    done

    for i in $(seq 0 $((DELETED_LEASES4_SIZE\-1))); do
        DELETED_LEASE4_ADDRESS=$(eval \(dqecho \e$DELETED_LEASES4_AT${i}_ADDRESS\(dq)
        ...
    done
    exit 0
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The leases6_committed hook point needs for loops to handle the list of addresses.
This can be achieved in the following way:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
leases6_committed() {
    for i in $(seq 0 $((LEASES6_SIZE\-1))); do
        LEASE6_ADDRESS=$(eval \(dqecho \e$LEASES6_AT${i}_ADDRESS\(dq)
        ...
    done

    for i in $(seq 0 $((DELETED_LEASES6_SIZE\-1))); do
        DELETED_LEASE6_ADDRESS=$(eval \(dqecho \e$DELETED_LEASES6_AT${i}_ADDRESS\(dq)
        ...
    done
    exit 0
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_stat_cmds.so\fP: Statistics Commands for Supplemental Lease Statistics
.sp
This library provides additional commands for retrieving lease
statistics from Kea DHCP servers. These commands were added to address
an issue with obtaining accurate lease statistics in deployments running
multiple Kea servers that use a shared lease backend. The in\-memory
statistics kept by individual servers only track lease changes made by
that server; thus, in a deployment with multiple servers (e.g. two
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> servers using the same PostgreSQL database for lease storage),
these statistics are incomplete. The MySQL and PostgreSQL backends in
Kea track lease allocation changes as they occur via database triggers.
Additionally, all the lease backends were extended to support
retrieving lease statistics for a single subnet, a range
of subnets, or all subnets. Finally, this library provides commands
for retrieving these statistics.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_stat_cmds.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The commands provided by this library are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstat\-lease4\-get\fP \- fetches DHCPv4 lease statistics.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstat\-lease6\-get\fP \- fetches DHCPv6 lease statistics.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Statistics Commands library is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.sp
All commands use JSON syntax and can be issued directly to the servers
via the control channel (see Management API \%<#\:ctrl-channel>).
.sp
This library is loaded in the same way as other libraries and currently has no
parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_stat_cmds.so\(dq
        },
        ...
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In a deployment with multiple Kea DHCP servers sharing a common lease
storage, this hook library can be loaded by any or all of the servers. However,
a server\(aqs response to a \fBstat\-lease4\-get\fP / \fBstat\-lease6\-get\fP
command will only contain data for subnets known to
that server. In other words, if a subnet does not appear in a server\(aqs
configuration, Kea will not retrieve statistics for it.
.SS The \fBstat\-lease4\-get\fP, \fBstat\-lease6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
The \fBstat\-lease4\-get\fP and
\fBstat\-lease6\-get\fP commands fetch lease
statistics for a range of known subnets. The range of subnets is
determined through the use of optional command input parameters:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet\-id\fP \- the ID of the subnet for which lease statistics
should be fetched; used to get statistics for a single subnet. If
the subnet does not exist, the command result code is 3 (i.e.
\fBCONTROL_RESULT_EMPTY\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet\-range\fP \- a pair of subnet IDs which describe an inclusive
range of subnets for which statistics should be retrieved. The range
may include one or more IDs that correspond to no subnet; in this
case, the command only outputs lease statistics for those that
exist. However, if the range does not include any known subnets, the
command result code is 3 (i.e. \fBCONTROL_RESULT_EMPTY\fP).
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfirst\-subnet\-id\fP \- the ID of the first subnet in the range.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlast\-subnet\-id\fP \- the ID of the last subnet in the range.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The use of \fBsubnet\-id\fP and \fBsubnet\-range\fP are mutually exclusive. If no
parameters are given, the result will contain data for all known
subnets. Note that in configurations with many subnets, this
can result in a large response.
.sp
The following command fetches lease statistics for all known subnets
from a \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstat\-lease4\-get\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following command fetches lease statistics for subnet ID 10 from a
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstat\-lease6\-get\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq : 10
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following command fetches lease statistics for all subnets with IDs
in the range 10 through 50 from a \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstat\-lease4\-get\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnet\-range\(dq: {
      \(dqfirst\-subnet\-id\(dq: 10,
      \(dqlast\-subnet\-id\(dq: 50
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to either command will contain three elements:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBresult\fP \- a numeric value indicating the outcome of the command
where:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fP \- the command was successful;
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fP \- an error occurred, and an explanation is the \(dqtext\(dq
element; or
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2\fP \- the fetch found no matching data.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtext\fP \- an explanation of the command outcome. When the command
succeeds, it contains the command name along with the number of
rows returned.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBarguments\fP \- a map containing the data returned by the command as
the element \(dqresult\-set\(dq, which is patterned after SQL statement
responses:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcolumns\fP \- a list of text column labels.
.sp
The columns returned for DHCPv4 are:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet\-id\fP \- the ID of the subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtotal\-addresses\fP \- the total number of addresses available for
DHCPv4 management in the subnet. In other words, this is the
count of all addresses in all the configured pools in the subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcumulative\-assigned\-addresses\fP \- the cumulative number of addresses
in the subnet that have been assigned to a client by the server
since it started.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBassigned\-addresses\fP \- the number of addresses in the subnet that
are currently assigned to a client.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdeclined\-addresses\fP \- the number of addresses in the subnet that
are currently declined and are thus unavailable for assignment.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The columns returned for DHCPv6 are:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet\-id\fP \- the ID of the subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtotal\-nas\fP \- the number of NA addresses available for DHCPv6
management in the subnet. In other words, this is the count of
all the NA addresses in all the configured NA pools in the
subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcumulative\-assigned\-nas\fP \- the cumulative number of NA addresses
in the subnet that have been assigned to a client by the server
since it started.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBassigned\-nas\fP \- the number of NA addresses in the subnet that
are currently assigned to a client.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdeclined\-addresses\fP \- the number of NA addresses that are currently
declined and are thus unavailable for assignment.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtotal\-pds\fP \- the total number of PD prefixes available of DHCPv6
management in the subnet. In other words, this is the count of
all prefixes in all the configured prefix pools in the subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcumulative\-assigned\-pds\fP \- the cumulative number of PD prefixes
in the subnet that have been assigned to a client by the server
since it started.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBassigned\-pds\fP \- the number of PD prefixes in the subnet that are
currently assigned to a client.
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrows\fP \- a list of rows, one per subnet ID. Each row contains a
data value corresponding to and in the same order as each column
listed in \(dqcolumns\(dq for a given subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtimestamp\fP \- the textual date and time the data were fetched,
expressed as GMT.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to a DHCPv4 command might look as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
  \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqstat\-lease4\-get: 2 rows found\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqresult\-set\(dq: {
      \(dqcolumns\(dq: [ \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq, \(dqtotal\-addresses\(dq, \(dqcumulative\-assigned\-addresses\(dq, \(dqassigned\-addresses\(dq, \(dqdeclined\-addresses\(dq ],
      \(dqrows\(dq: [
        [ 10, 256, 300, 111, 0 ],
        [ 20, 4098, 2034, 2034, 4 ]
      ],
      \(dqtimestamp\(dq: \(dq2018\-05\-04 15:03:37.000000\(dq
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to a DHCPv6 command might look as follows, assuming subnet 10 has no
prefix pools, subnet 20 has no NA pools, and subnet 30 has both NA and
PD pools:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
  \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqstat\-lease6\-get: 2 rows found\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqresult\-set\(dq: {
      \(dqcolumns\(dq: [ \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq, \(dqtotal\-nas\(dq, \(dqcumulative\-assigned\-nas\(dq, \(dqassigned\-nas\(dq, \(dqdeclined\-addresses\(dq, \(dqtotal\-pds\(dq, \(dqcumulative\-assigned\-pds\(dq, \(dqassigned\-pds\(dq ],
      \(dqrows\(dq: [
        [ 10, 4096, 5000, 2400, 3, 0, 0, 0],
        [ 20, 0, 0, 0, 0, 1048, 300, 233 ],
        [ 30, 256, 60, 60, 0, 1048, 15, 15 ]
      ],
      \(dqtimestamp\(dq: \(dq2018\-05\-04 15:03:37.000000\(dq
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS \fBlibdhcp_subnet_cmds.so\fP: Subnet Commands to Manage Subnets and Shared Networks
.sp
This library offers commands used to query and manipulate subnet and shared network
configurations in Kea. These can be very useful in deployments
with a large number of subnets being managed by the DHCP servers,
when those subnets are frequently updated. The commands offer a lightweight
approach for manipulating subnets without needing to fully reconfigure
the server, and without affecting existing servers\(aq configurations. An
ability to manage shared networks (listing, retrieving details, adding
new ones, removing existing ones, and adding subnets to and removing them from
shared networks) is also provided.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_subnet_cmds.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
It was previously available only to ISC customers with a paid support contract.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>
process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following commands are currently supported:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet4\-list\fP / \fBsubnet6\-list\fP \- lists all configured subnets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet4\-get\fP / \fBsubnet6\-get\fP \- retrieves detailed information about a
specified subnet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet4\-add\fP / \fBsubnet6\-add\fP \- adds a new subnet into the server\(aqs
configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet4\-update\fP / \fBsubnet6\-update\fP \- updates (replaces) a single subnet in
the server\(aqs configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet4\-del\fP / \fBsubnet6\-del\fP \- removes a subnet from the server\(aqs
configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet4\-delta\-add\fP / \fBsubnet6\-delta\-add\fP \- updates (replaces) parts of a
single subnet in the server\(aqs configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet4\-delta\-del\fP / \fBsubnet6\-delta\-del\fP \- removes parts of a single subnet in
the server\(aqs configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnetwork4\-list\fP / \fBnetwork6\-list\fP \- lists all configured shared networks.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnetwork4\-get\fP / \fBnetwork6\-get\fP \- retrieves detailed information about a
specified shared network.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnetwork4\-add\fP / \fBnetwork6\-add\fP \- adds a new shared network to the
server\(aqs configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnetwork4\-del\fP / \fBnetwork6\-del\fP \- removes a shared network from the
server\(aqs configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnetwork4\-subnet\-add\fP / \fBnetwork6\-subnet\-add\fP \- adds an existing subnet to
an existing shared network.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnetwork4\-subnet\-del\fP / \fBnetwork6\-subnet\-del\fP \- removes a subnet from
an existing shared network and demotes it to a plain subnet.
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-list\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to list all currently configured subnets. Each subnet
is returned with a subnet identifier, subnet prefix and shared network name,
if available. To retrieve detailed information about the subnet, use the
\fBsubnet4\-get\fP command.
.sp
This command has a simple structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-list\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The list of subnets is returned in the following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv4 subnets found\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 10,
            \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: \(dqnet1\(dq,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.0.0.0/8\(dq
        },
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 100,
            \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: null,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq
        }
    ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If no IPv4 subnets are found, an error code is returned along with the
error description.
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-list\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to list all currently configured subnets. Each subnet
is returned with a subnet identifier, subnet prefix and shared network name,
if available. To retrieve detailed information about the subnet, use the
\fBsubnet6\-get\fP command.
.sp
This command has a simple structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-list\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The list of subnets is returned in the following format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv6 subnets found\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 11,
            \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: null,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
        },
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 233,
            \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: \(dqsome\-net\(dq,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq3000::/16\(dq
        }
    ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If no IPv6 subnets are found, an error code is returned along with the
error description.
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-get\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to retrieve detailed information about the
specified subnet. This command usually follows \fBsubnet4\-list\fP,
which is used to discover available subnets with their respective subnet
identifiers and prefixes. Any of those parameters can then be used in
\fBsubnet4\-get\fP to fetch subnet information:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqid\(dq: 10
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.0.0.0/8\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the subnet exists, the response will be similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqInfo about IPv4 subnet 10.0.0.0/8 (id 10) returned\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.0.0.0/8\(dq,
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                    {
                        ...
                    },
                    ...
                ],
                ...
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-get\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to retrieve detailed information about the
specified subnet. This command usually follows \fBsubnet6\-list\fP,
which is used to discover available subnets with their respective subnet
identifiers and prefixes. Any of those parameters can be then used in
\fBsubnet6\-get\fP to fetch subnet information:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqid\(dq: 11
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the subnet exists, the response will be similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqInfo about IPv6 subnet 2001:db8:1::/64 (id 11) returned\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
                \(dqid\(dq: 1,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                    {
                        ...
                    },
                    ...
                ],
                ...
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-add\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to create and add a new subnet to the existing server
configuration. This operation has no impact on other subnets. The subnet
identifier must be specified and must be unique among all subnets. If
the identifier or a subnet prefix is not unique, an error is reported and
the subnet is not added.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet4\-add\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should be used to
add, remove, and modify static reservations.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [ {
            \(dqid\(dq: 123,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq,
            ...
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 subnet added\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 123,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-add\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to create and add a new subnet to the existing server
configuration. This operation has no impact on other subnets. The subnet
identifier must be specified and must be unique among all subnets. If
the identifier or a subnet prefix is not unique, an error is reported and
the subnet is not added.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet6\-add\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should be used
to add, remove, and modify static reservations.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [ {
            \(dqid\(dq: 234,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
            ...
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet added\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 234,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As of Kea 2.6.0, Kea no longer automatically generates subnet IDS; they must
be specified for \fBsubnet4\-add\fP and \fBsubnet6\-add\fP\&. If not
specified, Kea tries to assign the next \fBsubnet\-id\fP value. This
automatic ID value generator is simple; it returns the previous
automatically assigned value, increased by 1. This works well, unless
a subnet is manually created with a larger value than one previously used. For
example, if \fBsubnet4\-add\fP is called five times, each without an ID, Kea will
assign IDs 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5 and everything will work properly. However, if
\fBsubnet4\-add\fP is called five times, with the first subnet having the
\fBsubnet\-id\fP of value 3 and the remaining ones having no \fBsubnet\-id\fP, the operation will
fail. The first command (with the explicit value) will use \fBsubnet\-id\fP 3; the
second command will create a subnet with an ID of 1; the third will use a
value of 2; and finally the fourth will have its \fBsubnet\-id\fP value
auto\-generated as 3. However, since there is already a subnet with that
ID, the process will fail.
.sp
The general recommendation is either never to use explicit values, so
that auto\-generated values will always work; or always use explicit
values, so that auto\-generation is never used. The two
approaches can be mixed only if the administrator understands how internal
automatic \fBsubnet\-id\fP generation works in Kea.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Subnet IDs must be greater than zero and less than 4294967295.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-update\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to update (overwrite) a single subnet in the existing
server configuration. This operation has no impact on other subnets. The
subnet identifier is used to identify the subnet to replace; it must be
specified and must be unique among all subnets. The subnet prefix should
not be updated.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet4\-update\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should be
used to update, remove, and modify static reservations.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-update\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [ {
            \(dqid\(dq: 123,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq,
            ...
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 subnet updated\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 123,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As with other update commands, this command overwrites all the contents of the
entry. If the IPv4 subnet previously had a resource assigned to it and the
\fBsubnet4\-update\fP command is missing the resource, it is deleted from the
server configuration. If an incremental update of the subnet is desired,
use \fBsubnet4\-delta\-add\fP\&.
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-update\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to update (overwrite) a single subnet in the existing
server configuration. This operation has no impact on other subnets. The
subnet identifier is used to identify the subnet to replace; it must be
specified and must be unique among all subnets. The subnet prefix should
not be updated.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet6\-update\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should be
used to update, remove, and modify static reservations.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-update\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [ {
            \(dqid\(dq: 234,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
            ...
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet updated\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 234,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As with other update commands, this command overwrites all the contents of the
entry. If the IPv6 subnet previously had a resource assigned to it and the
\fBsubnet6\-update\fP command is missing the resource, it is deleted from the
server configuration. If an incremental update of the subnet is desired,
use \fBsubnet6\-delta\-add\fP\&.
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-del\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to remove a subnet from the server\(aqs configuration.
This command has no effect on other configured subnets, but removing a
subnet does have certain implications.
.sp
In most cases the server has assigned some leases to the clients
belonging to the subnet. The server may also be configured with static
host reservations which are associated with this subnet. The current
implementation of the \fBsubnet4\-del\fP command removes neither the leases nor
the host reservations associated with a subnet. This is the safest approach
because the server does not lose track of leases assigned to clients
from this subnet. However, removal of the subnet may still cause
configuration errors and conflicts. For example: after removal of the
subnet, the server administrator may update a new subnet with the ID
used previously for the removed subnet. This means that the existing
leases and static reservations will be in conflict with this new subnet.
Thus, we recommend that this command be used with extreme caution.
.sp
This command can also be used to completely delete an IPv4 subnet that
is part of a shared network. To simply remove the subnet
from a shared network and keep the subnet configuration, use the
\fBnetwork4\-subnet\-del\fP command instead.
.sp
The command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqid\(dq: 123
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A successful response may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 subnet 192.0.2.0/24 (id 123) deleted\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 123,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-del\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to remove a subnet from the server\(aqs configuration.
This command has no effect on other configured subnets, but removing a
subnet does have certain implications.
.sp
In most cases the server has assigned some leases to the clients
belonging to the subnet. The server may also be configured with static
host reservations which are associated with this subnet. The current
implementation of the \fBsubnet6\-del\fP command removes neither the leases nor
the host reservations associated with a subnet. This is the safest approach
because the server does not lose track of leases assigned to clients
from this subnet. However, removal of the subnet may still cause
configuration errors and conflicts. For example: after removal of the
subnet, the server administrator may add a new subnet with the ID used
previously for the removed subnet. This means that the existing leases
and static reservations will be in conflict with this new subnet. Thus,
we recommend that this command be used with extreme caution.
.sp
This command can also be used to completely delete an IPv6 subnet that
is part of a shared network. To simply remove the subnet
from a shared network and keep the subnet configuration, use the
\fBnetwork6\-subnet\-del\fP command instead.
.sp
The command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqid\(dq: 234
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
A successful response may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet 2001:db8:1::/64 (id 234) deleted\(dq,
    \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqid\(dq: 234,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-delta\-add\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to update a subnet by adding or overwriting its parts in
the existing server configuration. This operation has no impact on other
subnets. The subnet identifier is used to identify the subnet to update; it must
be specified and must be unique among all subnets. The subnet prefix should not
be updated.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet4\-delta\-add\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should
be used to update, remove, and modify static reservations.
.sp
The command uses keys to identify the respective object. The keys cannot be updated.
Objects must first be deleted in order to add other objects with conflicting keys.
The address pools are identified by the \fIpool\fP parameter; the options are identified
by the \fIname\fP or \fIcode\fP, and \fIspace\fP parameters.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-delta\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [ {
            \(dqvalid\-lifetime\(dq: 120,
            \(dqid\(dq: 123,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqalways\-send\(dq: false,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 3,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.1\(dq,
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq
                }
            ],
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.1\-10.20.30.10\(dq,
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqalways\-send\(dq: false,
                            \(dqcode\(dq: 4,
                            \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.4.1\(dq,
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqtime\-servers\(dq,
                            \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp4\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ]
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 subnet updated\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 123,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command updates subnet \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq with ID 123 by changing the valid
lifetime, by adding or changing the subnet level option 3 (\(dqrouters\(dq), by adding
or changing parameters of the pool \(dq10.20.30.1\-10.20.30.10\(dq, and by adding or
changing the pool level option 4 (\(dqtime\-servers\(dq).
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-delta\-add\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to update a subnet by adding or overwriting its parts in
the existing server configuration. This operation has no impact on other
subnets. The subnet identifier is used to identify the subnet to update; it must
be specified and must be unique among all subnets. The subnet prefix should not
be updated.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet6\-delta\-add\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should
be used to update, remove, and modify static reservations.
.sp
The command uses keys to identify the respective object. The keys cannot be updated.
Objects must first be deleted in order to add other objects with conflicting keys.
The address pools are identified by the \fIpool\fP parameter; the prefix pools are identified
by the \fIprefix\fP, \fIprefix\-len\fP, and \fIdelegated\-len\fP parameters; and the options are identified
by the \fIname\fP or \fIcode\fP, and \fIspace\fP parameters.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-delta\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [ {
            \(dqvalid\-lifetime\(dq: 120,
            \(dqid\(dq: 243,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqalways\-send\(dq: false,
                    \(dqcode\(dq: 23,
                    \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                    \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq3000::3:1\(dq,
                    \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq,
                    \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq
                }
            ],
            \(dqpd\-pools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqprefix\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:2::\(dq,
                    \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 48,
                    \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 64,
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqalways\-send\(dq: false,
                            \(dqcode\(dq: 22,
                            \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq3000::4:1\(dq,
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqsip\-server\-addr\(dq,
                            \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::1\-2001:db8:1::10\(dq,
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        {
                            \(dqalways\-send\(dq: false,
                            \(dqcode\(dq: 31,
                            \(dqcsv\-format\(dq: true,
                            \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq3000::5:1\(dq,
                            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqsntp\-servers\(dq,
                            \(dqspace\(dq: \(dqdhcp6\(dq
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ]
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet updated\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 234,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command updates subnet \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq with ID 243 by changing the valid
lifetime, by adding or changing the subnet level option 23 (\(dqdns\-servers\(dq), by
adding or changing parameters of the pool \(dq2001:db8:1::1\-2001:db8:1::10\(dq, by
adding or changing the pool level option 31 (\(dqsntp\-servers\(dq), by adding or
changing parameters of the pd\-pool \(dq2001:db8:2::\(dq with prefix\-len 48, and by adding
or changing the pd\-pool level option 22 (\(dqsip\-server\-addr\(dq).
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-delta\-del\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to update a subnet by removing its parts in the existing
server configuration. This operation has no impact on other subnets.
The subnet identifier is used to identify the subnet to update; it must be
specified and must be unique among all subnets. The subnet prefix should not be
updated.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet4\-delta\-del\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should
be used to update, remove, and modify static reservations.
.sp
The command is flexible and can delete part of the subnet by either
specifying the entire object that needs to be deleted, or just the keys
identifying the respective object. The address pools are identified by the
\fIpool\fP parameter; and the options are identified by the \fIname\fP or \fIcode\fP, and
\fIspace\fP parameters. The \fIspace\fP parameter can be omitted if the option belongs
to the default \fIdhcp4\fP space.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet4\-delta\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [ {
            \(dqvalid\-lifetime\(dq: 0,
            \(dqid\(dq: 123,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                { \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq }
            ],
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        { \(dqcode\(dq: 4 }
                    ],
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.11\-10.20.30.20\(dq
                },
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.21\-10.20.30.30\(dq
                }
            ]
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 subnet updated\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 123,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command updates subnet \(dq10.20.30.0/24\(dq with id 123 by removing the valid
lifetime, removing the subnet level option 3 (\(dqrouters\(dq), by removing the pool
\(dq10.20.30.21\-10.20.30.30\(dq and by removing the pool level option 4
(\(dqtime\-servers\(dq) in pool \(dq10.20.30.11\-10.20.30.20\(dq.
The scalar values don\(aqt need to match what is configured, but still need to be
present to maintain a valid json structure and to be a valid value to be able to
be parsed.
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-delta\-del\fP Command
.sp
This command is used to update a subnet by removing its parts in the existing
server configuration. This operation has no impact on other subnets.
The subnet identifier is used to identify the subnet to update; it must be
specified and must be unique among all subnets. The subnet prefix should not be
updated.
.sp
The subnet information within this command has the same structure as the
subnet information in the server configuration file, with the exception
that static host reservations cannot be specified within
\fBsubnet6\-delta\-del\fP\&. The commands described in libdhcp_host_cmds.so: Host Commands should
be used to update, remove, and modify static reservations.
.sp
The command is flexible and can delete the part of the subnet by either
specifying the entire object that needs to be deleted, or just the keys
identifying the respective object. The address pools are identified by the
\fIpool\fP parameter; the prefix pools are identified by the \fIprefix\fP, \fIprefix\-len\fP
and \fIdelegated\-len\fP parameters; and the options are identified by the \fIname\fP or
\fIcode\fP, and \fIspace\fP parameters. The \fIspace\fP parameter can be omitted if the
option belongs to the default \fIdhcp6\fP space.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqsubnet6\-delta\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [ {
            \(dqvalid\-lifetime\(dq: 0,
            \(dqid\(dq: 234,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                { \(dqname\(dq: \(dqdns\-servers\(dq }
            ],
            \(dqpd\-pools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqprefix\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:3::\(dq,
                    \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 48,
                    \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 64,
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        { \(dqcode\(dq: 22 }
                    ]
                },
                {
                    \(dqprefix\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:4::\(dq,
                    \(dqprefix\-len\(dq: 48,
                    \(dqdelegated\-len\(dq: 64
                }
            ],
            \(dqpools\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                        { \(dqcode\(dq: 31 }
                    ],
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::11\-2001:db8:1::20\(dq
                },
                {
                    \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::21\-2001:db8:1::30\(dq
                }
            ]
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response to this command has the following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet updated\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 234,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The command updates subnet \(dq2001:db8:1::/64\(dq with id 243 by removing the valid
lifetime, removing the subnet level option 23 (\(dqdns\-servers\(dq), by removing the
pool \(dq2001:db8:1::21\-2001:db8:1::30\(dq, by removing the pool level option 31
(\(dqsntp\-servers\(dq) in pool \(dq2001:db8:1::11\-2001:db8:1::20\(dq, by removing the
pd\-pool \(dq2001:db8:4::\(dq with prefix\-len 48, by removing the pd\-pool level option
22 (\(dqsip\-server\-addr\(dq) in pd\-pool \(dq2001:db8:3::\(dq with prefix\-len 48.
The scalar values don\(aqt need to match what is configured, but still need to be
present to maintain a valid json structure and to be a valid value to be able to
be parsed.
.SS The \fBnetwork4\-list\fP, \fBnetwork6\-list\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to retrieve the full list of currently configured
shared networks. The list contains only very basic information about
each shared network. If more details are needed, please use
\fBnetwork4\-get\fP or \fBnetwork6\-get\fP to retrieve all information
available. This command does not require any parameters and its
invocation is very simple:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqnetwork4\-list\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example response for \fBnetwork4\-list\fP looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            { \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor1\(dq },
            { \(dqname\(dq: \(dqoffice\(dq }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv4 network(s) found\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBnetwork6\-list\fP command uses exactly the same syntax for both the
command and the response.
.SS The \fBnetwork4\-get\fP, \fBnetwork6\-get\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to retrieve detailed information about shared
networks, including subnets that are currently part of a given network.
Both commands take one mandatory parameter, \fBname\fP, which specifies the
name of the shared network. An example command to retrieve details about
an IPv4 shared network with the name \(dqfloor13\(dq looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqnetwork4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example response could look as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqInfo about IPv4 shared network \(aqfloor13\(aq returned\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqmatch\-client\-id\(dq: true,
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ ],
            \(dqrebind\-timer\(dq: 90,
            \(dqrelay\(dq: {
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq0.0.0.0\(dq
            },
            \(dqrenew\-timer\(dq: 60,
            # Specify if the server should look up global reservations.
            \(dqreservations\-global\(dq: false,
            # Specify if the server should look up in\-subnet reservations.
            \(dqreservations\-in\-subnet\(dq: true,
            # Specify if the server can assume that all reserved addresses
            # are out\-of\-pool.
            \(dqreservations\-out\-of\-pool\(dq: false,
            \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                    \(dqid\(dq: 5,
                    ...
                    # many other subnet\-specific details here
                },
                {
                    \(dqid\(dq: 6,
                    \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/31\(dq,
                    ...
                    # many other subnet\-specific details here
                }
            ],
            \(dqvalid\-lifetime\(dq: 120
        }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The actual response contains many additional fields that are
omitted here for clarity. The response format is exactly the same as
used in \fBconfig\-get\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-get>, just limited to returning the shared network\(aqs
information.
.SS The \fBnetwork4\-add\fP, \fBnetwork6\-add\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to add a new shared network, which must
have a unique name. This command requires one parameter,
\fBshared\-networks\fP, which is a list and should contain exactly one
entry that defines the network. The only mandatory element for a network
is its name. Although it does not make operational sense, it is possible
to add an empty shared network that does not have any subnets in it.
That is allowed for testing purposes, but having empty networks (or networks with
only one subnet) is discouraged in production environments. For details
regarding syntax, see Shared Networks in DHCPv4 \%<#\:shared-network4> and
Shared Networks in DHCPv6 \%<#\:shared-network6>\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As opposed to parameter inheritance during the processing of a full new
configuration, this command does not fully handle parameter inheritance.
Any missing parameters will be filled with default values, rather
than inherited from the global scope.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example that showcases how to add a new IPv4 shared network looks as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqnetwork4\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [ {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq,
            \(dqsubnet4\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 100,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.2\-192.0.2.99\(dq } ],
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
                        \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: 101,
                \(dqpools\(dq: [ { \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.2\-192.0.3.99\(dq } ],
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.0/24\(dq,
                \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqrouters\(dq,
                        \(dqdata\(dq: \(dq192.0.3.1\(dq
                    }
                ]
            } ]
        } ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Assuming there was no shared network with a name \(dqfloor13\(dq and no subnets
with IDs 100 and 101 previously configured, the command will be
successful and will return the following response:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [ { \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq } ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqA new IPv4 shared network \(aqfloor13\(aq added\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBnetwork6\-add\fP command uses the same syntax for both the query and the
response. However, there are some parameters that are IPv4\-only (e.g.
\fBmatch\-client\-id\fP) and some that are IPv6\-only (e.g. \fBinterface\-id\fP). The same
applies to subnets within the network.
.SS The \fBnetwork4\-del\fP, \fBnetwork6\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to delete existing shared networks. Both
commands take exactly one parameter, \fBname\fP, that specifies the name of
the network to be removed. An example invocation of the \fBnetwork4\-del\fP
command looks as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqnetwork4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Assuming such a network was configured, the response looks
similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq
            }
        ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 shared network \(aqfloor13\(aq deleted\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBnetwork6\-del\fP command uses exactly the same syntax for both the
command and the response.
.sp
If there are any subnets belonging to the shared network being deleted,
they will be demoted to a plain subnet. There is an optional parameter
called \fBsubnets\-action\fP that, if specified, takes one of two possible
values: \fBkeep\fP (which is the default) and \fBdelete\fP\&. It controls
whether the subnets are demoted to plain subnets or removed. An example
usage in the \fBnetwork6\-del\fP command that deletes the shared network and all
subnets in it could look as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqnetwork4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq,
        \(dqsubnets\-action\(dq: \(dqdelete\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Alternatively, to completely remove the subnets, it is possible to use the
\fBsubnet4\-del\fP or \fBsubnet6\-del\fP commands.
.SS The \fBnetwork4\-subnet\-add\fP, \fBnetwork6\-subnet\-add\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to add existing subnets to existing shared
networks. There are several ways to add a new shared network. The system
administrator can add the whole shared network at once, either by
editing a configuration file or by calling the \fBnetwork4\-add\fP or
\fBnetwork6\-add\fP command with the desired subnets in it. This approach
works well for completely new shared subnets. However, there may be
cases when an existing subnet is running out of addresses and needs to
be extended with additional address space; in other words, another subnet
needs to be added on top of it. For this scenario, a system administrator
can use \fBnetwork4\-add\fP or \fBnetwork6\-add\fP, and then add an existing
subnet to this newly created shared network using
\fBnetwork4\-subnet\-add\fP or \fBnetwork6\-subnet\-add\fP\&.
.sp
The \fBnetwork4\-subnet\-add\fP and
\fBnetwork6\-subnet\-add\fP commands take
two parameters: \fBid\fP, which is an integer and specifies the ID of
an existing subnet to be added to a shared network; and \fBname\fP, which
specifies the name of the shared network to which the subnet will be added. The
subnet must not belong to any existing network; to
reassign a subnet from one shared network to another, use the
\fBnetwork4\-subnet\-del\fP or
\fBnetwork6\-subnet\-del\fP command commands first.
.sp
An example invocation of the \fBnetwork4\-subnet\-add\fP command looks as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqnetwork4\-subnet\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq,
        \(dqid\(dq: 5
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Assuming there is a network named \(dqfloor13\(dq, and there is a subnet with
\fBsubnet\-id\fP 5 that is not a part of the existing network, the command will
return a response similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 subnet 10.0.0.0/8 (id 5) is now part of shared network \(aqfloor13\(aq\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBnetwork6\-subnet\-add\fP command uses exactly the same syntax for both the
command and the response.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As opposed to parameter inheritance during the processing of a full new
configuration or when adding a new shared network with new subnets,
this command does not fully handle parameter inheritance.
Any missing parameters will be filled with default values, rather
than inherited from the global scope or from the shared network.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBnetwork4\-subnet\-del\fP, \fBnetwork6\-subnet\-del\fP Commands
.sp
These commands are used to remove a subnet that is part of an existing
shared network and demote it to a plain, stand\-alone subnet.
To remove a subnet completely, use the \fBsubnet4\-del\fP or \fBsubnet6\-del\fP
commands instead. The \fBnetwork4\-subnet\-del\fP and
\fBnetwork6\-subnet\-del\fP commands take two parameters: \fBid\fP, which is
an integer and specifies the ID of an existing subnet to be removed from
a shared network; and \fBname\fP, which specifies the name of the shared
network from which the subnet will be removed.
.sp
An example invocation of the \fBnetwork4\-subnet\-del\fP command looks as
follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
   \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqnetwork4\-subnet\-del\(dq,
   \(dqarguments\(dq: {
       \(dqname\(dq: \(dqfloor13\(dq,
       \(dqid\(dq: 5
   }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Assuming there was a subnet with \fBsubnet\-id\fP 5, that was part of a
shared network named \(dqfloor13\(dq, the response would look similar to the
following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 subnet 10.0.0.0/8 (id 5) is now removed from shared network \(aqfloor13\(aq\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBnetwork6\-subnet\-del\fP command uses exactly the same syntax for both the
command and the response.
.SS \fBlibdhcp_user_chk.so\fP: User Check
.sp
This library serves several purposes:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
To assign \(dqnew\(dq or \(dqunregistered\(dq users to a restricted subnet, while
\(dqknown\(dq or \(dqregistered\(dq users are assigned to unrestricted subnets.
.IP \(bu 2
To allow DHCP response options or vendor option values to be
customized based on user identity.
.IP \(bu 2
To provide a real\-time record of user registration activity, which
can be sampled by an external consumer.
.IP \(bu 2
To serve as a demonstration of various capabilities possible using
the hooks interface.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBlibdhcp_user_chk.so\fP is part of the open source code and is
available to every Kea user.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Once loaded, the library allows the separation of incoming requests into known
and unknown clients. For known clients, packets are processed
as usual, although it is possible to override the sending of certain options
on a per\-host basis. Clients that are not on the known
hosts list are treated as unknown and are assigned to the last
subnet defined in the configuration file.
.sp
As an example of a use case, this behavior may be implemented to put unknown users
into a separate subnet that leads to a \(dqwalled garden,\(dq where they can
only access a registration portal. Once they fill in necessary data,
their details are added to the known clients file and they get a proper
address after their device is restarted.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library was developed several years before the host reservation
mechanism became available. Host reservation is much
more powerful and flexible, but the ability of \fBuser_chk\fP
to consult an external source of information about clients and alter
Kea\(aqs behavior remains useful and of educational value.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The library reads the \fB/usr/local/share/kea/user_chk_registry.txt\fP file while being loaded
and each time an incoming packet is processed. Each line of the file is expected to
contain a self\-contained JSON snippet which must have the
following two entries:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype\fP \- whose value is \fB\(dqHW_ADDR\(dq\fP for IPv4 users or \fB\(dqDUID\(dq\fP for IPv6
users.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBid\fP \- whose value is either the hardware address or the DUID from
the request formatted as a string of hex digits, with or without \(dq:\(dq
delimiters.
.UNINDENT
.sp
and may have zero or more of the following entries:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbootfile\fP \- whose value is the pathname of the desired file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtftp_server\fP \- whose value is the hostname or IP address of the
desired server.
.UNINDENT
.sp
A sample user registry file is shown below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqtype\(dq : \(dqHW_ADDR\(dq, \(dqid\(dq : \(dq0c:0e:0a:01:ff:04\(dq, \(dqbootfile\(dq : \(dq/usr/local/share/kea/v4bootfile\(dq }
{ \(dqtype\(dq : \(dqHW_ADDR\(dq, \(dqid\(dq : \(dq0c:0e:0a:01:ff:06\(dq, \(dqtftp_server\(dq : \(dqtftp.v4.example.com\(dq }
{ \(dqtype\(dq : \(dqDUID\(dq, \(dqid\(dq : \(dq00:01:00:01:19:ef:e6:3b:00:0c:01:02:03:04\(dq, \(dqbootfile\(dq : \(dq/usr/local/share/kea/v6bootfile\(dq }
{ \(dqtype\(dq : \(dqDUID\(dq, \(dqid\(dq : \(dq00:01:00:01:19:ef:e6:3b:00:0c:01:02:03:06\(dq, \(dqtftp_server\(dq : \(dqtftp.v6.example.com\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As with any other hook libraries provided by ISC, internals of the
\fBuser_chk\fP code are well\-documented. Users may refer to the user_chk
library section of the Kea Developer\(aqs Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide/\:d8/\:db2/\:libdhcp_user_chk\:.html>
for information on how the code works internally. That, together with the
Hooks Framework section of the Kea Developer\(aqs Guide \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide/\:index\:.html#\:hooksFramework> should give users
some pointers on how to extend this library and perhaps even write one
from scratch.
.SH STATISTICS
.SS Statistics Overview
.sp
Both Kea DHCP servers support statistics gathering. A working DHCP
server encounters various events that can cause certain statistics to be
collected. For example, a DHCPv4 server may receive a packet
(the \fBpkt4\-received\fP statistic increases by one) that after parsing is
identified as a DHCPDISCOVER (\fBpkt4\-discover\-received\fP). The server
processes it and decides to send a DHCPOFFER representing its answer
(the \fBpkt4\-offer\-sent\fP and \fBpkt4\-sent statistics\fP increase by one). Such
events happen frequently, so it is not uncommon for the statistics to have
values in the high thousands. They can serve as an easy and powerful
tool for observing a server\(aqs and a network\(aqs health. For example, if
the \fBpkt4\-received\fP statistic stops growing, it means that the clients\(aq
packets are not reaching the server.
.sp
There are four types of statistics:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fIinteger\fP \- this is the most common type. It is implemented as a
64\-bit integer (int64_t in C++), so it can hold any value between
\-2^63 to 2^63\-1.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIbig integer\fP \- this type is intended to hold large numbers. It is
implemented as a 128\-bit integer (boost::multiprecision::int128_t in C++), so
it can hold any value between \-2^127 to 2^127\-1.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIfloating point\fP \- this type is intended to store floating\-point
precision. It is implemented as a C++ double type.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIduration\fP \- this type is intended for recording time periods. It
uses the \(gaboost::posix_time::time_duration type, which stores hours,
minutes, seconds, and microseconds.
.IP \(bu 2
\fIstring\fP \- this type is intended for recording statistics in text
form. It uses the C++ std::string type.
.UNINDENT
.sp
During normal operation, the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 servers gather
statistics. For a list of DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 statistics, see
Statistics in the DHCPv4 Server \%<#\:dhcp4-stats> and Statistics in the DHCPv6 Server \%<#\:dhcp6-stats>, respectively.
.sp
To extract data from the statistics module, the control channel can be
used. See Management API \%<#\:ctrl-channel> for details. It is possible to
retrieve a single statistic or all statistics, reset the statistics (i.e.
set them to a neutral value, typically zero), or even completely remove a
single statistic or all statistics. See the section Commands for Manipulating Statistics
for a list of statistics\-oriented commands.
.sp
Statistics can be used by external tools to monitor Kea. One example of such a tool is Stork.
See Monitoring Kea With Stork \%<#\:stork> for details on how to use it and other data sources to retrieve statistics periodically
to get better insight into Kea\(aqs health and operational status.
.SS Statistics Lifecycle
.sp
All of the statistics supported by Kea\(aqs servers are initialized upon the servers\(aq startup
and are returned in response to the commands such as
\fBstatistic\-get\-all\fP\&. The runtime statistics concerning DHCP packets
processed are initially set to 0 and are reset upon the server
restart.
.sp
Per\-subnet statistics are recalculated when reconfiguration takes place.
.sp
In general, once a statistic is initialized it is held in the manager until
explicitly removed, via \fBstatistic\-remove\fP or
\fBstatistic\-remove\-all\fP,
or when the server is shut down.
.sp
Removing a statistic that is updated frequently makes little sense, as
it will be re\-added when the server code next records that statistic.
The \fBstatistic\-remove\fP and
\fBstatistic\-remove\-all\fP commands are
intended to remove statistics that are not expected to be observed in
the near future. For example, a misconfigured device in a network may
cause clients to report duplicate addresses, so the server will report
increasing values of \fBpkt4\-decline\-received\fP\&. Once the problem is found
and the device is removed, the system administrator may want to remove
the \fBpkt4\-decline\-received\fP statistic so that it is no longer reported, until
and unless a duplicate address is again detected.
.SS Commands for Manipulating Statistics
.sp
There are several commands defined that can be used for accessing
(\fB\-get\fP), resetting to zero or a neutral value (\fB\-reset\fP), or removing a
statistic completely (\fB\-remove\fP). The statistics time\-based
limit (\fB\-sample\-age\-set\fP) and size\-based limit (\fB\-sample\-count\-set\fP), which
control how long or how many samples of a given statistic are retained, can also
be changed.
.sp
The difference between \fB\-reset\fP and \fB\-remove\fP is somewhat subtle.
The \fB\-reset\fP command sets the value of the statistic to zero or a neutral value,
so that after this operation, the statistic has a value of 0 (integer),
0.0 (float), 0h0m0s0us (duration), or \(dq\(dq (string).
When requested, a statistic with the values mentioned is returned.
\fB\-remove\fP removes a statistic completely, so the statistic is no longer
reported. However, the server code may add it back if there is a reason
to record it.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The following sections describe commands that can be sent to the
server; the examples are not fragments of a configuration file. For
more information on sending commands to Kea, see
Management API \%<#\:ctrl-channel>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBstatistic\-get\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-get\fP command retrieves a single statistic. It takes a
single\-string parameter called \fBname\fP, which specifies the statistic
name. An example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpkt4\-received\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The server returns details of the requested statistic, with a result of
0 indicating success and the specified statistic as the value of the
\fBarguments\fP parameter. If the requested statistic is not found, the
response contains an empty map, i.e. only { } as an argument, but
the status code still indicates success (0).
.sp
Here is an example response:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqpkt4\-received\(dq: [ [ 125, \(dq2019\-07\-30 10:11:19.498739\(dq ], [ 100, \(dq2019\-07\-30 10:11:19.498662\(dq ] ]
    },
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBstatistic\-reset\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-reset\fP command sets the specified statistic to its
neutral value: 0 for integer, 0.0 for float, 0h0m0s0us for time
duration, and \(dq\(dq for string type. It takes a single\-string parameter
called \fBname\fP, which specifies the statistic name. An example command
may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-reset\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpkt4\-received\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the specific statistic is found and the reset is successful, the
server responds with a status of 0, indicating success, and an empty
parameters field. If an error is encountered (e.g. the requested
statistic was not found), the server returns a status code of 1 (error)
and the text field contains the error description.
.SS The \fBstatistic\-remove\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-remove\fP command deletes a single statistic. It
takes a single\-string parameter called \fBname\fP, which specifies the
statistic name. An example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-remove\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpkt4\-received\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the specific statistic is found and its removal is successful, the
server responds with a status of 0, indicating success, and an empty
parameters field. If an error is encountered (e.g. the requested
statistic was not found), the server returns a status code of 1 (error)
and the text field contains the error description.
.SS The \fBstatistic\-get\-all\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-get\-all\fP command retrieves all statistics recorded. An
example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: { }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example response returning all collected statistics:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
