.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBstatistic\-reset\-all\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-reset\fP command sets all statistics to their neutral
values: 0 for integer, 0.0 for float, 0h0m0s0us for time duration, and
\(dq\(dq for string type. An example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-reset\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: { }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the operation is successful, the server responds with a status of 0,
indicating success, and an empty parameters field. If an error is
encountered, the server returns a status code of 1 (error) and the text
field contains the error description.
.SS The \fBstatistic\-remove\-all\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-remove\-all\fP command attempts to delete all statistics. An
example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-remove\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: { }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the removal of all statistics is successful, the server responds with
a status of 0, indicating success, and an empty parameters field. If an
error is encountered, the server returns a status code of 1 (error) and
the text field contains the error description.
.SS The \fBstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\fP command sets a time\-based limit
on samples for a given statistic. It takes two parameters: a string
called \fBname\fP, which specifies the statistic name, and an integer value called
\fBduration\fP, which specifies the time limit for the given statistic in seconds.
An example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpkt4\-received\(dq,
        \(dqduration\(dq: 1245
    }

}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the command is successful, the server responds with a status of
0, indicating success,
and an empty parameters field. If an error is encountered (e.g. the
requested statistic was not found), the server returns a status code
of 1 (error) and the text field contains the error description.
.SS The \fBstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\-all\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\-all\fP command sets time\-based limits
on samples for all statistics. It takes a single\-integer parameter
called \fBduration\fP, which specifies the time limit for the statistic
in seconds. An example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqduration\(dq: 1245
    }

}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the command is successful, the server responds with a status of
0, indicating success,
and an empty parameters field. If an error is encountered, the server returns
a status code of 1 (error) and the text field contains the error description.
.SS The \fBstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\fP command sets a size\-based limit
on samples for a given statistic. An example command may look
like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqpkt4\-received\(dq,
        \(dqmax\-samples\(dq: 100
    }

}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the command is successful, the server responds with a status of
0, indicating success,
and an empty parameters field. If an error is encountered (e.g. the
requested statistic was not found), the server returns a status code
of 1 (error) and the text field contains the error description.
.SS The \fBstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\-all\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\-all\fP command sets size\-based limits
on samples for all statistics. An example command may look
like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqmax\-samples\(dq: 100
    }

}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the command is successful, the server responds with a status of
0, indicating success,
and an empty parameters field. If an error is encountered, the server returns
a status code of 1 (error) and the text field contains the error description.
.SS Time Series
.sp
With certain statistics, a single isolated data point may be useful. However,
some statistics, such as received
packet size, packet processing time, or number of database queries needed to
process a packet, are not cumulative and it is useful to keep many data
points, perhaps to do some statistical analysis afterwards.
.sp
Each Kea statistic holds 20 data points; setting such
a limit prevents unlimited memory growth.
There are two ways to define the limits: time\-based (e.g. keep samples from
the last 5 minutes) and size\-based. The size\-based
limit can be changed using one of two commands: \fBstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\fP,
to set a size limit for a single statistic, and \fBstatistic\-sample\-count\-set\-all\fP,
to set size\-based limits for all statistics. To set time\-based
limits for a single statistic, use \fBstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\fP; use
\fBstatistic\-sample\-age\-set\-all\fP to set time\-based limits for all statistics.
For a given statistic only one type of limit can be active; storage
is limited by either time or size, not both.
.SH MANAGEMENT API
.sp
A classic approach to daemon configuration assumes that the server\(aqs
configuration is stored in configuration files and, when the
configuration is changed, the daemon is restarted. This approach has the
significant disadvantage of introducing periods of downtime when client
traffic is not handled. Another risk is that if the new configuration is
invalid for any reason, the server may refuse to start, which will
further extend the downtime period until the issue is resolved.
.sp
To avoid such problems, the DHCPv4, DHCPv6, and D2 servers in Kea include
support for a mechanism that allows online reconfiguration without
requiring server shutdown. Both servers can be instructed to open
control sockets, which is a communications channel. The server is able
to receive commands on that channel, act on them, and report back
status.
.sp
The DHCPv4, DHCPv6, and D2 servers receive commands over the UNIX domain
sockets. For details on how to configure these sockets, see
UNIX Control Socket \%<#\:dhcp4-unix-ctrl-channel>, UNIX Control Socket \%<#\:dhcp6-unix-ctrl-channel> and
UNIX Control Socket \%<#\:d2-unix-ctrl-channel>\&. While
it is possible to control the servers directly using UNIX domain sockets,
that requires that the controlling client be running on the same machine
as the server. SSH is usually used to connect remotely to the controlled
machine.
.sp
Network administrators usually prefer using some form of a RESTful API
to control the servers, rather than using UNIX domain sockets directly.
Kea version 3 extends servers by HTTP/HTTPS sockets. For details on
how to configure these sockets, see HTTP/HTTPS Control Socket \%<#\:dhcp4-http-ctrl-channel>,
HTTP/HTTPS Control Socket \%<#\:dhcp6-http-ctrl-channel> and HTTP/HTTPS Control Socket \%<#\:d2-http-ctrl-channel>\&.
.sp
Control connections over both HTTP and UNIX domain sockets are guarded
with timeouts. The timeout value is set to 10 seconds and is not
configurable.
.sp
This API can be used by external tools to manage and monitor Kea operation.
An example of such a monitoring tool is ISC\(aqs Stork. For details, see
Monitoring Kea With Stork \%<#\:stork>\&.
.SS Data Syntax
.sp
Communication over the control channel is conducted using JSON
structures. If configured, Kea opens a socket and listens for
incoming connections. A process connecting to this socket is expected to
send JSON commands structured as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparam1\(dq: \(dqvalue1\(dq,
        \(dqparam2\(dq: \(dqvalue2\(dq,
        ...
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The same command sent over the RESTful interface to the server has the
following structure:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
POST / HTTP/1.1\er\en
Content\-Type: application/json\er\en
Content\-Length: 147\er\en\er\en
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparam1\(dq: \(dqvalue1\(dq,
        \(dqparam2\(dq: \(dqvalue2\(dq,
        ...
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBcommand\fP parameter contains the name of the command to execute and it
is mandatory.
The \fBarguments\fP map contains the parameters required to carry out the
given command. The exact content and format of the map are command\-specific.
.sp
The server processing the incoming command sends a response of the
form:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0, // 0|1|2|3|4
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqtextual description\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqargument1\(dq: \(dqvalue1\(dq,
        \(dqargument2\(dq: \(dqvalue2\(dq,
        ...
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBresult\fP value is a status code indicating a result of the command. The
following general status codes are currently supported:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fP \- the command has been processed successfully.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fP \- a general error or failure has occurred during the command processing.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2\fP \- the specified command is unsupported by the server receiving it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB3\fP \- the requested operation has been completed but the requested
resource was not found. This status code is returned when a command
returns no resources or affects no resources.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB4\fP \- the well\-formed command has been processed but the requested
changes could not be applied, because they were in conflict with the
server state or its notion of the configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB5\fP \- the command processed has failed and the configuration has been
partially altered. The rollback mechanism was not able to restore the previous
configuration.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For example, a well\-formed command that requests a subnet that exists
in a server\(aqs configuration returns the result 0. If the server encounters
an error condition, it returns 1. If the command asks for an IPv6 subnet,
but was sent to a DHCPv4 server, it returns 2. If the query asks for a
subnet with \fBsubnet\-id\fP that has matches, the result is 3.
If the command attempts to update a lease but the specified \fBsubnet\-id\fP
does not match the identifier in the server\(aqs configuration, the result
is 4.
.sp
Hook libraries can sometimes return additional status codes specific
to their use cases.
.sp
The \fBtext\fP field typically appears when the result is non\-zero and
contains a description of the error encountered, but it often also
appears for successful outcomes. The exact text is command\-specific, but
in general uses plain English to describe the outcome of the command.
The \fBarguments\fP map contains additional data values returned by the server
which are specific to the command issued. The map may be present, but that
depends on the specific command.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Since Kea 1.9.7, it is possible to put comments in commands as
in the configuration file. For instance:
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
    # command arguments are here.
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparam1\(dq: \(dqvalue1\(dq,
        ...
        /*
        \(dqparam2\(dq: \(dqvalue2\(dq,
        ...
        */
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Since Kea 2.7.5 it is possible to specify extra HTTP headers which
are added to HTTP responses. Each header is specified by its name
and value with optionally a user context. For instance:
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqhttp\-headers\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqStrict\-Transport\-Security\(dq,
            \(dqvalue\(dq: \(dqmax\-age=31536000\(dq
        }
     ],
 ...
 }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
adds a HSTS header declaring that HTTPS (vs HTTP) must be used for one year.
.SS HTTP Command Response Format
.sp
For backward compatibility with the obsolete (removed) Control Agent
responses received by HTTP are always wrapped in an array (JSON list) of
one element.
.sp
For example, the response for a command sent would be structured as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[
    {
        \(dqresult\(dq: 0, // 0|1|2|3|4
        \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqtextual description\(dq,
        \(dqarguments\(dq: {
            \(dqargument1\(dq: \(dqvalue1\(dq,
            \(dqargument2\(dq: \(dqvalue2\(dq,
            ...
        }
    }
]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An exception to this are authentication or authorization errors which cause server
to reject the command entirely.  The response to such an error is formatted
as a single entry (JSON map) as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 403,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqForbidden\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
These types of errors are possible on systems configured for either basic
authentication or servers that load \fBlibdhcp_rbac.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_rbac\:.so>\&.
.SS Using the Control Channel
.sp
The easiest way to start interacting with the control API is to use
common UNIX/Linux tools such as \fBsocat\fP and \fBcurl\fP\&.
.sp
In order to control the given Kea service via a UNIX domain socket, use
\fBsocat\fP in interactive mode as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ socat UNIX:/path/to/the/kea/socket \-
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or in batch mode, include the \(dqignoreeof\(dq option as shown below to
ensure \fBsocat\fP waits long enough for the server to respond:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ echo \(dq{ some command...}\(dq | socat UNIX:/path/to/the/kea/socket \-,ignoreeof
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where \fB/path/to/the/kea/socket\fP is the path specified in the
\fBDhcp4/control\-socket/socket\-name\fP parameter in the Kea configuration
file. Text passed to \fBsocat\fP is sent to Kea and the responses received
from Kea are printed to standard output. This approach communicates with
the specific server directly.
.sp
It is also easy to open a UNIX socket programmatically. An example of a
simple client written in C is available in the Kea Developer\(aqs Guide, in
the Control Channel Overview chapter, in the
Using Control Channel \%<https://\:reports\:.kea\:.isc\:.org/\:dev_guide/\:d2/\:d96/\:ctrlSocket\:.html#\:ctrlSocketClient>
section.
.sp
To use Kea\(aqs RESTful API with \fBcurl\fP, use the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ curl \-X POST \-H \(dqContent\-Type: application/json\(dq \-d \(aq{ \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-get\(dq }\(aq http://kea.example.org:8000/
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This assumes that the server is running on host
\fBkea.example.org\fP and is running the RESTful service on port 8000.
.SS Commands Supported by Both the DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 Servers
.SS The \fBbuild\-report\fP Command
.sp
The \fBbuild\-report\fP command returns on the control channel what the
command line \fB\-W\fP argument displays, i.e. the embedded content of the
\fBconfig.report\fP file. This command does not take any parameters.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqbuild\-report\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBconfig\-get\fP Command
.sp
The \fBconfig\-get\fP command retrieves the current configuration used by the
server. This command does not take any parameters. The configuration
returned is roughly equal to the configuration that was loaded using the
\fB\-c\fP command\-line option during server start\-up, or was later set using the
\fBconfig\-set\fP command. However, there may be certain differences, as
comments are not retained. If the original configuration used file
inclusion, the returned configuration includes all parameters from
all included files. In Kea 2.4.0 and later, the successful response also
contains a SHA\-256 digest that can be used to easily determine whether a
configuration has changed.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The returned configuration is not redacted, i.e. it
contains database passwords in plain text, if those were specified in the
original configuration. Care should be taken not to expose the command
channel to unprivileged users.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-get\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBconfig\-hash\-get\fP Command
.sp
The \fBconfig\-hash\-get\fP command retrieves the SHA\-256 hash of the current
configuration used by the server. This command does not take any parameters.
The returned hash can be used to detect configuration changes.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-hash\-get\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And the server\(aqs response:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqhash\(dq: \(dq5C3C90EF7035249E2FF74D003C19F34EE0B83A3D329E741B52B2EF95A2C9CC5C\(dq
     }
 }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In Kea 2.4.0 and later, \fBconfig\-set\fP and \fBconfig\-get\fP also return the SHA\-256 hash
of the new or current configuration. This may be used to determine whether a configuration
has changed.
.SS The \fBconfig\-reload\fP Command
.sp
The \fBconfig\-reload\fP command instructs Kea to load again the
configuration file that was used previously. This operation is useful if
the configuration file has been changed by some external source; for
example, a system administrator can tweak the configuration file and use this
command to force Kea to pick up the changes.
.sp
Care should be taken when using this in conjunction with the \fBconfig\-set\fP command. Kea
remembers the location of the configuration file it was started with,
and this configuration can be significantly changed using the \fBconfig\-set\fP
command. When \fBconfig\-reload\fP is issued after \fBconfig\-set\fP, Kea attempts
to reload its original configuration from the file, possibly losing all
changes introduced using \fBconfig\-set\fP or other commands.
.sp
The \fBconfig\-reload\fP command does not take any parameters. An example command
invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-reload\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the configuration file is incorrect, reloading it can raise an error
which leaves the server in an unusable state. See The config\-set Command
to learn how to recover from a non\-working server.
.SS The \fBconfig\-test\fP Command
.sp
The \fBconfig\-test\fP command instructs the server to check whether the new
configuration supplied in the command\(aqs arguments can be loaded. The
supplied configuration is expected to be the full configuration for the
target server, along with an optional logger configuration. The configuration
is sanity\-checked to the extent possible without the server actually
attempting to load it; it is possible for a configuration which successfully
passes this command to still fail in the \fBconfig\-set\fP command or at launch
time. The structure of the command is as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-test\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq:  {
        \(dq<server>\(dq: {
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where <server> is the configuration element name for a given server, such
as \(dqDhcp4\(dq or \(dqDhcp6\(dq. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-test\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq:  {
        \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
            ...
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The server\(aqs response contains a numeric code, \fBresult\fP (0 for
success, non\-zero on failure), and a string, \fBtext\fP, describing the
outcome:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 0, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqConfiguration seems sane...\(dq }

or

{ \(dqresult\(dq: 1, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqunsupported parameter: BOGUS (<string>:16:26)\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBconfig\-write\fP Command
.sp
The \fBconfig\-write\fP command instructs the Kea server to write its current
configuration to a file on disk. It takes one optional argument, called
\(dqfilename\(dq, that specifies the name of the file to write the
configuration to. If not specified, the name used when starting Kea
(passed as a \fB\-c\fP argument) is used. If a relative path is specified,
Kea writes its files only in the directory where it is running.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-write\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqfilename\(dq: \(dqconfig\-modified\-2017\-03\-15.json\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, the config file file may only be written to the same
directory as the config file used when starting Kea (passed as a \fB\-c\fP
argument).
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBleases\-reclaim\fP Command
.sp
The \fBleases\-reclaim\fP command instructs the server to reclaim all expired
leases immediately. The command has the following JSON syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqleases\-reclaim\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremove\(dq: true
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBremove\fP boolean parameter is mandatory and indicates whether the
reclaimed leases should be removed from the lease database (if \fBtrue\fP), or
left in the \fBexpired\-reclaimed\fP state (if \fBfalse\fP). The latter facilitates
lease affinity, i.e. the ability to re\-assign an expired lease to a
returning client that previously used that lease. See Configuring Lease Affinity \%<#\:lease-affinity>
for details. Also, see Lease Reclamation \%<#\:lease-reclamation> for general
information about the processing of expired leases (lease reclamation).
.SS The \fBinterface\-add\fP Command
.sp
The \fBinterface\-add\fP command updates the list of interfaces used
to process DHCP traffic.
The command takes as parameter the list of interfaces with respective
addresses (if specified) on which the server should start listening for
DHCP traffic. If there are duplicate entries in the command list or they
are duplicating the entries in the running configuration, these entries
will have no effect on the new configuration.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The command can only append to the existing list of interfaces.
There is no way to remove an interface once it has been added and
the server has started listening for DHCP traffic.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Please note that the new configuration is
retained in memory only; if the server is restarted or a configuration
reload is triggered via a signal, the server uses the configuration
stored in its configuration file. The server\(aqs response contains a
numeric code, \fBresult\fP (0 for success, non\-zero on failure), and a
string, \fBtext\fP, describing the outcome:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 0, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqInterface configuration successfully updated.\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The updated configuration can also be retrieved using the \fBconfig\-get\fP
command (inside the \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq configuration map).
.SS The \fBinterface\-list\fP Command
.sp
The \fBinterface\-list\fP command retrieves the list of detected interfaces.
This command does not take any parameters.
.SS The \fBinterface\-redetect\fP Command
.sp
The \fBinterface\-redetect\fP command retrieves the list of detected interfaces
after performing a re\-detect procedure which only adds newly discovered interfaces
and addresses, without removing any previously detected interfaces or addresses.
This command does not take any parameters.\(dq
.SS The \fBlist\-commands\fP Command
.sp
The \fBlist\-commands\fP command retrieves a list of all commands supported
by the server. It does not take any arguments. An example command may
look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqlist\-commands\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: { }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The server responds with a list of all supported commands. The arguments
element is a list of strings, each of which conveys one supported
command.
.SS The \fBconfig\-set\fP Command
.sp
The \fBconfig\-set\fP command instructs the server to replace its current
configuration with the new configuration supplied in the command\(aqs
arguments. The supplied configuration is expected to be the full
configuration for the target server, along with an optional logger
configuration. While optional, the logger configuration is highly
recommended, as without it the server reverts to its default logging
configuration. The structure of the command is as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq:  {
        \(dq<server>\(dq: {
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where <server> is the configuration element name for a given server, such
as \(dqDhcp4\(dq or \(dqDhcp6\(dq. For example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq:  {
        \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
            ...
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the new configuration proves to be invalid, the server retains its
current configuration; however, in some cases a fatal error message is logged
indicating that the server is no longer providing any service: a working
configuration must be loaded as soon as possible. If the control channel
is dead, the configuration file can still be reloaded using the \fBSIGHUP\fP
signal. If that is unsuccessful, restart the server.
.sp
Please note that the new configuration is
retained in memory only; if the server is restarted or a configuration
reload is triggered via a signal, the server uses the configuration
stored in its configuration file. The server\(aqs response contains a
numeric code, \fBresult\fP (0 for success, non\-zero on failure), and a
string, \fBtext\fP, describing the outcome:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{ \(dqresult\(dq: 0, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqConfiguration successful.\(dq }

or

{ \(dqresult\(dq: 1, \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqunsupported parameter: BOGUS (<string>:16:26)\(dq }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In Kea 2.4.0 and later, the successful response from a DHCPv4, DHCPv6, or DHCP\-DDNS daemon
also contains a SHA\-256 digest of the newly set configuration. The digest can be used to easily
determine whether a configuration has changed.
.SS The \fBshutdown\fP Command
.sp
The \fBshutdown\fP command instructs the server to initiate its shutdown
procedure; it is the equivalent of sending a \fBSIGTERM\fP signal to the
process. This command does not take any arguments. An example command
may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqshutdown\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqexit\-value\(dq: 3
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The server responds with a confirmation that the shutdown procedure has
been initiated.  The optional parameter, \fBexit\-value\fP, specifies the
numeric value with which the server process exits to the system.
The default value is zero.
.sp
The DDNS daemon supports an extra parameter, \fBtype\fP, which controls the way
the process cleans up on exit. The supported shutdown types are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqnormal\(dq \- stops the queue manager and finishes all current transactions
before exiting. This is the default.
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqdrain_first\(dq \- stops the queue manager but continues processing requests
from the queue until it is empty.
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqnow\(dq \- exits immediately.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
An example command may look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqshutdown\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqexit\-value\(dq: 3,
        \(dqtype\(dq: \(dqdrain_first\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBdhcp\-disable\fP Command
.sp
The \fBdhcp\-disable\fP command globally disables the DHCP service. The
server continues to operate, but it drops all received DHCP messages.
This command is useful when the server\(aqs maintenance requires that the
server temporarily stop allocating new leases and renew existing leases.
It is also useful in failover\-like configurations during a
synchronization of the lease databases at startup, or recovery after a
failure. The optional parameter \fBmax\-period\fP specifies the time in
seconds after which the DHCP service should be automatically re\-enabled,
if the \fBdhcp\-enable\fP command is not sent before this time elapses.
.sp
Since Kea 1.9.4, there is an additional \fBorigin\fP parameter that specifies the
command source. A server administrator should typically omit this parameter
because the default value \(dquser\(dq indicates that the administrator sent the
command. In Kea 2.5.5 through 2.5.7, this parameter was also used in communication
between the HA partners to specify the identifier of an HA service sending the command
in a numeric format. However, due to compatibility issues with older
Kea versions that did not properly parse numeric values, it was necessary
to introduce the new parameter, \fBorigin\-id\fP, in Kea 2.5.8.
.sp
It holds a numeric value representing the origin of the command. The same value
can still be passed using the \fBorigin\fP parameter, but it can cause the
aforementioned compatibility issues between HA partners running different
Kea versions; if both are used, \fBorigin\-id\fP takes precedence. New Kea versions
favor using \fBorigin\-id\fP in communication between the HA partners, but
overall, it is recommended that both parameters be
omitted and the default value used.
.sp
The following codes represent the supported origins in numeric format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fP \- a user command; the same as specifying \fB\(dqorigin\(dq: \(dquser\(dq\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2000\fP, \fB2001\fP, \fB2002\fP, etc. \- origins specified by HA partners where
the increments above \fB2000\fP are distinct HA service identifiers used when
the partners have many relationships.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the following example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqdhcp\-disable\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqmax\-period\(dq: 20,
        \(dqorigin\-id\(dq: 2002,
        \(dqorigin\(dq: \(dquser\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
the effective origin is \fB2002\fP, which indicates it is an HA partner
sending the command for the service with ID of \fB2\fP\&. The \fBorigin\fP
parameter will be ignored in this case.
.SS The \fBdhcp\-enable\fP Command
.sp
The \fBdhcp\-enable\fP command globally enables the DHCP service.
.sp
Since Kea 1.9.4, there is an additional \fBorigin\fP parameter that specifies the
command source. A server administrator should typically omit this parameter
because the default value \(dquser\(dq indicates that the administrator sent the
command. In Kea 2.5.5 through 2.5.7, this parameter was also used in communication
between the HA partners to specify the identifier of an HA service sending the command
in a numeric format. However, due to compatibility issues with older
Kea versions that did not properly parse numeric values, it was necessary
to introduce the new parameter, \fBorigin\-id\fP, in Kea 2.5.8.
.sp
It holds a numeric value representing the origin of the command. The same value
can still be passed using the \fBorigin\fP parameter, but it can cause the
aforementioned compatibility issues between HA partners running different
Kea versions.; if both are used, \fBorigin\-id\fP takes precedence. New Kea versions
favor using \fBorigin\-id\fP in communication between the HA partners, but
overall, it is recommended that both
.sp
The following codes represent the supported origins in numeric format:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fP \- a user command; the same as specifying \fB\(dqorigin\(dq: \(dquser\(dq\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2000\fP, \fB2001\fP, \fB2002\fP, etc. \- origins specified by HA partners where
the increments above \fB2000\fP are distinct HA service identifiers used when
the partners have many relationships.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the following example:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqdhcp\-enable\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqorigin\-id\(dq: 2002,
        \(dqorigin\(dq: \(dquser\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
the effective origin is \fB2002\fP, which indicates it is an HA partner
sending the command for the service with ID of \fB2\fP\&. The \fBorigin\fP
parameter will be ignored in this case.
.SS The \fBstatus\-get\fP Command
.sp
The \fBstatus\-get\fP command returns the server\(aqs runtime information:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpid\fP: the process ID.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuptime\fP: the number of seconds since the start of the server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreload\fP: the number of seconds since the last configuration (re)load.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhigh\-availability\fP: HA\-specific status information about the DHCP servers
configured to use the HA hook library:
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlocal\fP: the state, the role (primary,
secondary, ...), and the scopes (i.e. what the server is actually
processing) of the local server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremote\fP: the remote server\(aqs last known state, its served
HA scopes, and the role of the remote server in the HA relationship.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulti\-threading\-enabled\fP: a flag indicating whether multi\-threading is enabled.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthread\-pool\-size\fP: the number of DHCP service threads.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpacket\-queue\-size\fP: the maximum size of the packet queue. There is one queue,
regardless of the number of running threads.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpacket\-queue\-statistics\fP: the average queue size for the last 10, 100, and 1000
packets, using an approach similar to the UNIX \fBtop\fP command.
The average queue size for the last 10 packets can be considered an
instantaneous value, while the average for the last 1000 packets shows
a longer\-term trend.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBhigh\-availability\fP information is returned only when the command is
sent to the DHCP servers in an HA setup. This parameter is
never returned when the \fBstatus\-get\fP command is sent to the
DDNS daemon.
.sp
The \fBthread\-pool\-size\fP, \fBpacket\-queue\-size\fP and
\fBpacket\-queue\-statistics\fP parameters are returned only when the
command is sent to DHCP servers with multi\-threading enabled. These
three parameters and \fBmulti\-threading\-enabled\fP are never returned when
the \fBstatus\-get\fP command is sent to the DDNS daemon.
.sp
To learn more about the HA status information returned by the
\fBstatus\-get\fP command, please refer to the The status\-get Command \%<#\:command-ha-status-get>
section.
.SS The \fBserver\-tag\-get\fP Command:
.sp
The \fBserver\-tag\-get\fP command returns the configured server tag of
the DHCPv4 or DHCPv6 server (Configuration Sharing and Server Tags \%<#\:cb-sharing> explains the server tag concept).
.SS The \fBconfig\-backend\-pull\fP Command:
.sp
The \fBconfig\-backend\-pull\fP command triggers the polling of configuration backends
(which must be configured for this command to have an effect),
explained in Enabling the Configuration Backend \%<#\:dhcp4-cb-json>\&.
.SS The \fBversion\-get\fP Command
.sp
The \fBversion\-get\fP command returns extended information about the Kea
version; it is the same information available via the \fB\-V\fP
command\-line argument. This command does not take any parameters.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqversion\-get\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBkea\-lfc\-start\fP Command
.sp
The \fBkea\-lfc\-start\fP command starts the \fBkea\-lfc\fP process which
cleans up the lease file (see The LFC Process \%<#\:kea-lfc>). It takes no arguments and
does not returns success when the lease backend is not the \fBmemfile\fP, when
the \fBpersist\fP parameter of the \fBlease\-database\fP was configured to
\fBfalse\fP (but not when \fBlfc\-interval\fP is \fB0\fP), when it detected
a LFC process is currently running or an error prevented a new LFC process
to be started.
.SS Commands Supported by the DHCPv4 Server
.SS The \fBsubnet4\-select\-test\fP Command
.sp
The \fBsubnet4\-select\-test\fP provides a way to test DHCPv4 subnet selection based
on a set of input parameters typically supplied in a client packet.
Recognized parameters take strings and are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinterface\fP \- the incoming interface name
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaddress\fP \- the client address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay\fP \- the relay/gateway address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlocal\fP \- the local/destination address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremote\fP \- the remote/source address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlink\fP \- the RAI link\-selection address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet\fP \- the subnet\-selection address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclasses\fP \- (list of strings) client classes (allowing to select a guarded subnet)
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The RAI link\-selection is ignored when the \fBignore\-rai\-link\-selection\fP
compatibility flag is \fBtrue\fP\&. When it is not ignored it has precedence
over the subnet\-selection.
.sp
Outside of errors possible results are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
(empty) \(dqno selected subnet\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqselected shared network \(aq<name>\(aq starting with subnet \(aq<subnet>\(aq id <id>\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqselected subnet \(aq<subnet>\(aq id <id>\(dq
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBsubnet4o6\-select\-test\fP Command
.sp
The \fBsubnet4o6\-select\-test\fP provides a way to test DHCPv4\-over\-DHCPv6 subnet
selection based on a set of input parameters typically supplied in a client
packet. Recognized parameters take strings and are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinterface\fP \- the incoming interface name of the DHCPv6 server
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinterface\-id\fP \- the binary content of the interface\-id relay option
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaddress\fP \- the client address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrelay\fP \- the relay/gateway address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlocal\fP \- the local/destination address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremote\fP \- the remote/source IPv6 address of the DHCPv6 server
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlink\fP \- the first relay link IPv6 address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubnet\fP \- the subnet\-selection address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclasses\fP \- (list of strings) client classes (allowing to select a guarded subnet)
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
According to the code only \fBremote\fP, \fBinterface\-id\fP and \fBinterface\fP
selectors are used. In DHCPv4\-over\-DHCPv6 implementation \fBinterface\fP and
\fBremote\fP values are transmitted from the DHCPv6 server, \fBinterface\-id\fP
and \fBlink\fP are carried in the relay info part of the DHCPv6 packet so
are the same as for the DHCPv6 server.
.sp
Outside of errors possible results are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
(empty) \(dqno selected subnet\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqselected shared network \(aq<name>\(aq starting with subnet \(aq<subnet>\(aq id <id>\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqselected subnet \(aq<subnet>\(aq id <id>\(dq
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Commands Supported by the DHCPv6 Server
.SS The \fBsubnet6\-select\-test\fP Command
.sp
The \fBsubnet6\-select\-test\fP provides a way to test DHCPv6 subnet selection based
on a set of input parameters typically supplied in a client packet.
Recognized parameters take strings and are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinterface\fP \- the incoming interface name
.IP \(bu 2
\fBinterface\-id\fP \- the binary content of the interface\-id relay option
.IP \(bu 2
\fBremote\fP \- the remote/source address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlink\fP \- the first relay link address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclasses\fP \- (list of strings) client classes (allowing to select a guarded subnet)
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Outside of errors possible results are:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
(empty) \(dqno selected subnet\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqselected shared network \(aq<name>\(aq starting with subnet \(aq<subnet>\(aq id <id>\(dq
.IP \(bu 2
\(dqselected subnet \(aq<subnet>\(aq id <id>\(dq
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Commands Supported by the D2 Server
.sp
The D2 server supports only a subset of the DHCPv4/DHCPv6 server commands:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbuild\-report\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-get\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-hash\-get\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-reload\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-set\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-test\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\-write\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlist\-commands\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBshutdown\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstatus\-get\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBversion\-get\fP
.UNINDENT
.SS Migration from the obsolete (removed) Control Agent
.sp
Since Kea version 2.7.2 DHCP servers support HTTP/HTTPS control channels
so the Control Agent (CA) is no longer needed, and since Kea version
3.1.8, the CA has been removed.
.sp
The DHCPv4, DHCPv6, and D2 servers extend the \fBcontrol\-socket\fP entry
to \fBcontrol\-sockets\fP list. To migrate a CA configuration add an element
to this list with:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsocket\-type\fP set to \fBhttp\fP or \fBhttps\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsocket\-address\fP with the content of CA \fBhttp\-host\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsocket\-port\fP with the content of CA \fBhttp\-port\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtrust\-anchor\fP (unchanged)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcert\-file\fP (unchanged)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkey\-file\fP (unchanged)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcert\-required\fP (unchanged)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauthentication\fP (unchanged)
.UNINDENT
.sp
User context is supported too. Please look at respective HTTP control socket
sections for defaults and other details:
DHCP4 HTTP/HTTPS Control Socket \%<#\:dhcp4-http-ctrl-channel>,
DHCP6 HTTP/HTTPS Control Socket \%<#\:dhcp6-http-ctrl-channel> and DHCP\-DDNS HTTP/HTTPS Control Socket \%<#\:d2-http-ctrl-channel>\&.
.sp
Beware that two servers must use different address / port pairs. This applies
the HA listeners too.
.sp
For compatibility the JSON result of these HTTP/HTTPS control sockets is
still encapsulated into a list.
.SH LOGGING
.SS Logging Configuration
.sp
During its operation Kea may produce many log messages. They differ in
severity (some are more important than others) and source (different
components, like hooks, produce different messages). It is useful to
understand which log messages are critical and which are not, and to
configure logging appropriately. For example, debug\-level messages
can be safely ignored in a typical deployment. They are, however, very
useful when debugging a problem.
.sp
The logging system in Kea is configured through the \fBloggers\fP entry in the
server section of the configuration file.
.SS Loggers
.sp
Within Kea, a message is logged through an entity called a \(dqlogger.\(dq
Different components log messages through different loggers, and each
logger can be configured independently of the others. Some components,
in particular the DHCP server processes, may use multiple loggers to log
messages pertaining to different logical functions of the component. For
example, the DHCPv4 server uses one logger for messages about packet
reception and transmission, another logger for messages related to lease
allocation, and so on. Some of the libraries used by the Kea server,
such as libdhcpsrv, use their own loggers.
.sp
Users implementing hook libraries (code attached to the server at
runtime) are responsible for creating the loggers used by those
libraries. Such loggers should have unique names, different from the
logger names used by Kea. That way, the messages produced by the hook
library can be distinguished from messages issued by the core Kea code.
Unique names also allow the hook loggers to be configured independently of
loggers used by Kea. Whenever it makes sense, a hook library can use
multiple loggers to log messages pertaining to different logical parts
of the library.
.sp
In the server section of a configuration file, the
configuration for zero or more loggers (including loggers used by the
proprietary hook libraries) can be specified. If there are no loggers specified, the
code uses default values; these cause Kea to log messages of INFO
severity or greater to standard output. There is a small time window
after Kea has been started but before it has read its configuration;
logging in this short period can be controlled using environment
variables. For details, see Logging During Kea Startup\&.
.sp
The three main elements of a logger configuration are: \fBname\fP (the
component that is generating the messages), \fBseverity\fP (what to log),
and \fBoutput_commands\fP (where to log). There is also a \fBdebuglevel\fP
element, which is only relevant if debug\-level logging has been
selected.
.SS The \fBname\fP (string) Logger
.sp
Each logger in the system has a name: that of the component binary file
using it to log messages. For instance, to configure logging
for the DHCPv4 server, add an entry for a logger named “kea\-dhcp4”.
This configuration will then be used by the loggers in the DHCPv4
server and all the libraries used by it, unless a library defines its
own logger and there is a specific logger configuration that applies to
that logger.
.sp
When tracking down an issue with the server\(aqs operation, use of DEBUG
logging is required to obtain the verbose output needed for problem
diagnosis. However, the high verbosity is likely to overwhelm the
logging system in cases where the server is processing high\-volume
traffic. To mitigate this problem, Kea can use multiple loggers, for
different functional parts of the server, that can each be configured
independently. If the user is reasonably confident that a problem
originates in a specific function of the server, or that the problem is
related to a specific type of operation, they may enable high verbosity
only for the relevant logger, thereby limiting the DEBUG messages to the
required minimum.
.sp
The loggers are associated with a particular library or binary of Kea.
However, each library or binary may (and usually does) include multiple
loggers. For example, the DHCPv4 server binary contains separate loggers
for packet parsing, dropped packets, callouts, etc.
.sp
The loggers form a hierarchy. For each program in Kea, there is a \(dqroot\(dq
logger, named after the program (e.g. the root logger for \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>, the
DHCPv4 server, is named \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>). All other loggers are children of
this logger and are named accordingly, e.g. the allocation engine in the
DHCPv4 server logs messages using a logger called
\fBkea\-dhcp4.alloc\-engine\fP\&.
.sp
This relationship is important, as each child logger derives its default
configuration from its parent root logger. In the typical case, the root
logger configuration is the only logging configuration specified in the
configuration file and so applies to all loggers. If an entry is made
for a given logger, any attributes specified override those of the root
logger, whereas any not specified are inherited from it.
.sp
To illustrate this, suppose we are using the DHCPv4 server with the
root logger \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> logging at the INFO level. In order to enable
DEBUG verbosity for DHCPv4 packet drops, we must create a configuration
entry for the logger with \fB\(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp4.bad\-packets”\fP,
\fB\(dqseverity\(dq: \(dqDEBUG\(dq\fP, and an explicit debug level. All other configuration
parameters may be omitted for this logger if it should use the default values
specified in the root logger\(aqs configuration.
.sp
\fBdebuglevel\fP is inherited only if \fBseverity\fP is missing as well. For
predictable results, if \fBseverity\fP is \fB\(dqDEBUG\(dq\fP, these two attributes
should always be explicitly specified or omitted together. An entry with an
explicit \fB\(dqDEBUG\(dq\fP severity does not inherit \fBdebuglevel\fP from the root
logger and defaults to \fB0\fP if missing, resulting in no debug messages
being logged. This is a consequence of relying on the log4cplus inheritance
mechanism.
.sp
If there are multiple logger specifications in the configuration that
might match a particular logger, the specification with the more
specific logger name takes precedence. For example, if there are entries
for both \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp4.dhcpsrv\fP, the main DHCPv4 server
program — and all libraries it uses other than the \fBdhcpsrv\fP library
(libdhcpsrv) — logs messages according to the configuration in the
first entry (\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>). Messages generated by the \fBdhcpsrv\fP library
are logged according to the configuration set by the second entry.
.sp
The currently defined loggers are listed in the following table. The
\(dqSoftware Package\(dq column of this table specifies whether the particular
loggers belong to the core Kea code (open source Kea binaries and
libraries), or hook libraries (open source or subscriber).
.SS List of loggers supported by Kea servers and hook libraries shipped with Kea/subscriber packages
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
