.TE
.sp
Note that user\-defined hook libraries should not use any of the loggers
mentioned above, but should instead define new loggers with names that
correspond to the libraries using them. Suppose that a user created
a library called “libdhcp\-packet\-capture” to dump packets received and
transmitted by the server to a file. An appropriate name for the
logger could be \fBkea\-dhcp4.packet\-capture\-hooks\fP\&. (Note that the hook
library implementer only specifies the second part of this name, i.e.
“packet\-capture”. The first part is a root\-logger name and is prepended
by the Kea logging system.) It is also important to note that since this
new logger is a child of a root logger, it inherits the configuration
from the root logger, something that can be overridden by an entry in
the configuration file.
.sp
The easiest way to find a logger name is to configure all logging to go
to a single destination and look there for specific logger names. See
Logging Message Format for details.
.SS The \fBseverity\fP (string) Logger
.sp
This specifies the category of messages logged. Each message is logged
with an associated severity, which may be one of the following (in
descending order of severity):
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
FATAL \- associated with messages generated by a condition that is so
serious that the server cannot continue executing.
.IP \(bu 2
ERROR \- associated with messages generated by an error condition. The
server continues executing, but the results may not be as
expected.
.IP \(bu 2
WARN \- indicates an out\-of\-the\-ordinary condition. However, the
server continues executing normally.
.IP \(bu 2
INFO \- an informational message marking some event.
.IP \(bu 2
DEBUG \- messages produced for debugging purposes.
.UNINDENT
.sp
When the severity of a logger is set to one of these values, it
only logs messages of that severity and above (e.g. setting the logging
severity to INFO logs INFO, WARN, ERROR, and FATAL messages). The
severity may also be set to NONE, in which case all messages from that
logger are inhibited.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The \fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> tool, described in Managing Kea with keactrl \%<#\:keactrl>, can be configured
to start the servers in verbose mode. If this is the case, the
settings of the logging severity in the configuration file have
no effect; the servers use a logging severity of DEBUG
regardless of the logging settings specified in the configuration
file. To control severity via the configuration file,
please make sure that the \fBkea_verbose\fP value is set to \(dqno\(dq within
the \fBkeactrl\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-keactrl> configuration.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBdebuglevel\fP (integer) Logger
.sp
When a logger\(aqs severity is set to DEBUG, this value specifies the
level of debug messages to be printed. It ranges from 0 (least
verbose) to 99 (most verbose). If severity for the logger is not DEBUG,
this value is ignored.
.SS The \fBoutput\-options\fP (list) Logger
.sp
Each logger can have zero or more \fBoutput\-options\fP\&. These specify
where log messages are sent and are explained in detail below.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
The alias \fBoutput\-options\fP was added in Kea 2.5.1, to be used
interchangeably with the previous \fBoutput_options\fP configuration key.
This was done to keep all configuration keys consistent, i.e.
using a hyphen (\fI\-\fP) instead of an underscore (\fI_\fP) in the key name.
.sp
As of Kea 2.5.2, \fBoutput\-options\fP becomes the default configuration key
and \fBoutput_options\fP can be used as an alias.
.sp
As of Kea 2.7.4, \fBoutput_options\fP configuration key has been deprecated and
will be removed in future versions. Please consider updating your
configuration by switching to using \fBoutput\-options\fP instead.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBoutput\fP (string) Option
.sp
This value determines the type of output. There are several special
values allowed here: \fBstdout\fP (messages are printed on standard
output), \fBstderr\fP (messages are printed on stderr), \fBsyslog\fP
(messages are logged to syslog using the default name), \fBsyslog:name\fP
(messages are logged to syslog using a specified name). Any other value is
interpreted as a filename to which messages should be written.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
As of Kea 2.7.9, log files may only be written to the output directory
determined during compilation as: \fB\(dq[kea\-install\-dir]/var/log/kea\(dq\fP\&. This
path may be overridden at startup by setting the environment variable
\fBKEA_LOG_FILE_DIR\fP to the desired path.  If a path other than
this value is used in \fBoutput\fP, Kea will emit an error and refuse to start
or, if already running, log an unrecoverable error.  For ease of use simply
omit the path component from \fBoutput\fP and specify only the file name.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBflush\fP (boolean) Option
.sp
This flushes the buffers after each log message. Doing this reduces performance
but ensures that if the program terminates abnormally, all messages
up to the point of termination are recorded. The default is \fBtrue\fP\&.
.SS The \fBmaxsize\fP (integer) Option
.sp
This option is only relevant when the destination is a file; this is the maximum size
in bytes that a log file may reach. When the maximum size is reached,
the file is renamed and a new file created. Initially, a \(dq.1\(dq is
appended to the name; if a \(dq.1\(dq file exists, it is renamed \(dq.2\(dq, etc.
This is referred to as rotation.
.sp
The default value is 10240000 (10MB). The smallest value that can be
specified without disabling rotation is 204800. Any value less than
this, including 0, disables rotation. The greatest possible value is INT_MAX MB, which is
approximately 2PB.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Due to a limitation of the underlying logging library (log4cplus),
rolling over the log files (from \(dq.1\(dq to \(dq.2\(dq, etc.) may show odd
results; there can be multiple small files at the timing of rollover.
This can happen when multiple processes try to roll over the
files simultaneously. Version 1.1.0 of log4cplus solved this problem,
so if this version or later of log4cplus is used to build Kea, the
issue should not occur. Even with older versions, it is normally
expected to happen rarely unless the log messages are produced very
frequently by multiple different processes.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBmaxver\fP (integer) Option
.sp
This option is only relevant when the destination is a file and rotation is enabled
(i.e. maxsize is large enough). This is the maximum number of rotated
versions that will be kept. Once that number of files has been reached,
the oldest file, \(dqlog\-name.maxver\(dq, is discarded each time the log
rotates. In other words, at most there will be the active log file plus
maxver rotated files. The minimum and default value is 1.
.SS The \fBpattern\fP (string) Option
.sp
This option can be used to specify the layout pattern of messages for
a logger. Kea logging is implemented using the log4cplus library and its
output formatting is based, conceptually, on the printf formatting from C;
this is discussed in detail in the next section,
Logging Message Format\&.
.sp
Each output type (\fBstdout\fP, file, or \fBsyslog\fP) has a default \fBpattern\fP which
describes the content of its log messages. This parameter can be used to
specify a desired pattern. The pattern for each logger is governed
individually, so each configured logger can have its own pattern. Omitting
the \fBpattern\fP parameter or setting it to an empty string, \(dq\(dq, causes
Kea to use the default pattern for that logger\(aqs output type.
.sp
In addition to the log text itself, the default patterns used for \fBstdout\fP
and files contain information such as date and time, logger level, and
process information. The default pattern for \fBsyslog\fP is limited primarily
to log level, source, and the log text. This avoids duplicating information
which is usually supplied by syslog.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Users are strongly encouraged to test their pattern(s) on a local,
non\-production instance of Kea, running in the foreground and
logging to \fBstdout\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Logging Message Format
.sp
As mentioned above, Kea log message content is controlled via a scheme similar
to the C language\(aqs printf formatting. The \(dqpattern\(dq used for each message is
described by a string containing one or more format components as part of a
text string.  In addition to the components, the string may contain any other
useful text for the administrator.
.sp
The behavior of Kea\(aqs format strings is determined by log4cplus. The following
time format options are possible enclosed in \fB%D{}\fP or \fB%d{}\fP:
.SS List of supported time format string components by Kea\(aqs logger
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Component
T}	T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\fB%a\fP
T}	T{
Abbreviated weekday name
T}
_
T{
\fB%A\fP
T}	T{
Full weekday name
T}
_
T{
\fB%b\fP
T}	T{
Abbreviated month name
T}
_
T{
\fB%B\fP
T}	T{
Full month name
T}
_
T{
\fB%c\fP
T}	T{
Standard date and time string
T}
_
T{
\fB%d\fP
T}	T{
Day of month as a decimal(1\-31)
T}
_
T{
\fB%H\fP
T}	T{
Hour(0\-23)
T}
_
T{
\fB%I\fP
T}	T{
Hour(1\-12)
T}
_
T{
\fB%j\fP
T}	T{
Day of year as a decimal(1\-366)
T}
_
T{
\fB%m\fP
T}	T{
Month as decimal(1\-12)
T}
_
T{
\fB%M\fP
T}	T{
Minute as decimal(0\-59)
T}
_
T{
\fB%p\fP
T}	T{
Locale\(aqs equivalent of AM or PM
T}
_
T{
\fB%q\fP
T}	T{
milliseconds as decimal(0\-999)
T}
_
T{
\fB%Q\fP
T}	T{
microseconds as decimal(0\-999.999)
T}
_
T{
\fB%S\fP
T}	T{
Second as decimal(0\-59)
T}
_
T{
\fB%U\fP
T}	T{
Week of year, Sunday being first day(0\-53)
T}
_
T{
\fB%w\fP
T}	T{
Weekday as a decimal(0\-6, Sunday being 0)
T}
_
T{
\fB%W\fP
T}	T{
Week of year, Monday being first day(0\-53)
T}
_
T{
\fB%x\fP
T}	T{
Standard date string
T}
_
T{
\fB%X\fP
T}	T{
Standard time string
T}
_
T{
\fB%y\fP
T}	T{
Year in decimal without century(0\-99)
T}
_
T{
\fB%Y\fP
T}	T{
Year including century as decimal
T}
_
T{
\fB%Z\fP
T}	T{
Time zone name
T}
_
T{
\fB%%\fP
T}	T{
The percent sign
T}
.TE
.sp
Refer to the documentation for the \fBstrftime()\fP function found in the
\fB<ctime>\fP header or the \fBstrftime(3)\fP UNIX manual page for more
information.
.sp
It is probably easiest to understand this by examining the default pattern
for stdout and files; currently they are the same. That pattern is shown
below:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dq%D{%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S.%q} %\-5p [%c/%i.%t] %m\en\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
and a typical log produced by this pattern looks something like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
2019\-08\-05 14:27:45.871 DEBUG [kea\-dhcp4.dhcpsrv/8475.12345] DHCPSRV_TIMERMGR_START_TIMER starting timer: reclaim\-expired\-leases
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
That breaks down to:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%D{%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S.%q}\fP
\(dq%D\(dq is the local date and time when the log message is generated,
while everything between the curly braces, \(dq{}\(dq, are date and time components.
From the example log above this produces:
\fB2019\-08\-05 14:27:45.871\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%\-5p\fP
The severity of the message, output as a minimum of five characters,
using right\-padding with spaces. In our example log: \fBDEBUG\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%c\fP
The log source. This includes two elements: the Kea process generating the
message, in this case, \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>; and the component within the program
from which the message originated, \fBdhcpsrv\fP (e.g.  the name of the
library used by DHCP server implementations).
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%i\fP
The process ID. From the example log: \fB8475\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%t\fP
The thread ID. From the example log: \fB12345\fP\&.
The format of the thread ID is OS\-dependent: e.g. on some systems
it is an address, so it is displayed in hexadecimal.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%m\fP
The log message itself. Kea log messages all begin with a message identifier
followed by arbitrary log text. Every message in Kea has a unique
identifier, which can be used as an index to the Kea Messages Manual \%<#\:kea-messages>, where
more information can be obtained. In our example log above, the identifier
is \fBDHCPSRV_TIMERMGR_START_TIMER\fP\&. The log text is typically a brief
description detailing the condition that caused the message to be logged. In
our example, the information logged,
\fBstarting timer: reclaim\-expired\-leases\fP, explains that the timer for the
expired lease reclamation cycle has been started.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Omitting \fB%m\fP omits the log message text from the output, making it
rather useless. \fB%m\fP should be considered mandatory.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Finally, note that spacing between components, the square brackets around the
log source and PID, and the final carriage return \fB\en\fP are all literal text
specified as part of the pattern.
.sp
\fBWarning:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
To ensure that each log entry is a separate line, patterns
must end with an \fB\en\fP\&. There may be use cases where it is not desired
so we do not enforce its inclusion. If it is omitted from
the pattern, the log entries will run together in one long \(dqline\(dq.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The default pattern for \fBsyslog\fP output is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dq%\-5p [%c.%t] %m\en\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It omits the date and time as well as the process ID, as this
information is typically output by \fBsyslog\fP\&. Note that Kea uses the pattern
to construct the text it sends to \fBsyslog\fP (or any other destination). It has
no influence on the content \fBsyslog\fP may add or formatting it may do.
.sp
Consult the OS documentation for \fBsyslog\fP behavior, as there are multiple
implementations.
.sp
A complete list of logging parameters supported by Kea is shown in the table below:
.SS List of supported format string components by Kea\(aqs logger
.TS
box center;
l|l.
T{
Component
T}	T{
Value
T}
_
T{
\fB%b\fP
T}	T{
Outputs file that called the log e.g., logger_impl.cc
T}
_
T{
\fB%c\fP
T}	T{
Outputs the logger of the event e.g., kea\-dhcp4.hosts
T}
_
T{
\fB%d\fP
T}	T{
\fB%d{}\fP formats UTC time output e.g., \fB%d{%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S.%q}\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB%D\fP
T}	T{
\fB%D{}\fP formats LOCAL time output e.g., \fB%D{%Y\-%m\-%d %H:%M:%S.%q}\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB%E\fP
T}	T{
Outputs environment variables e.g., \fB%E{PATH}\fP
T}
_
T{
\fB%F\fP
T}	T{
Outputs filename where logging request was issued e.g., logger_impl.cc
T}
_
T{
\fB%h\fP
T}	T{
Outputs hostname of the system e.g., host\-1
T}
_
T{
\fB%H\fP
T}	T{
Outputs fully qualified domain name e.g., host\-1.example.com
T}
_
T{
\fB%l\fP
T}	T{
Equivalent to \fB%F:%L\fP e.g., logger_impl.cc:179
T}
_
T{
\fB%L\fP
T}	T{
Outputs the line number where the log was called e.g., 179
T}
_
T{
\fB%m\fP
T}	T{
Outputs the actual log message
T}
_
T{
\fB%M\fP
T}	T{
Outputs caller (function name) of the log message
T}
_
T{
\fB%n\fP
T}	T{
Outputs line separator supported by platform e.g., \fB\en\fP in Linux
T}
_
T{
\fB%p\fP
T}	T{
Outputs log severity e.g., INFO
T}
_
T{
\fB%r\fP
T}	T{
Outputs milliseconds since program start e.g., 1235
T}
_
T{
\fB%t\fP
T}	T{
Outputs thread ID that generated the log message e.g., 281472855306256
T}
_
T{
\fB%T\fP
T}	T{
Outputs thread name that generated the log message e.g., 168005
T}
_
T{
\fB%i\fP
T}	T{
Outputs process ID that generated the log message e.g., 168030
T}
_
T{
\fB%%\fP
T}	T{
Outputs a literal percent sign
T}
.TE
.sp
Padding and truncation are also possible with modifiers preceding the component; this is
done by placing a number and other modifier characters between the component and the %
sign. There are five ways of modifying the output:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%20p\fP
Left pads with spaces (align right) if the severity is shorter than 20 characters.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%\-15r\fP
Right pads with spaces (align left) if the milliseconds since program start is shorter
than 15 characters.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%.30m\fP
Truncates from the beginning of the message if the message is longer than 30
characters.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%10.35E{PATH}\fP
Left pad with spaces (align right) if the environment variable \(dqPATH\(dq is shorter than
10 characters. If the content is longer than 35 characters, then truncate from the
beginning of the string.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB%\-15.40m\fP
Right pad with spaces (align left) if the log message is shorter than 15 characters.
If the message is longer than 40 characters, truncate from the beginning.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Supported parameters depend on the liblog4cplus version used to compile Kea, which can
be checked by executing \fBkea\-dhcp4 \-W | grep \-i log4cplus\fP\&. This produces a result
such as: \fBLOG4CPLUS_VERSION:  2.0.5\fP\&. Consult the documentation in the
log4cplus wiki \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:log4cplus/\:log4cplus/\:wiki> for further information
about the installed version.
.SS Example Logger Configurations
.sp
In this example, we want to set the server logging to write to the
console using standard output.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqServer\(dq: {
    \(dqloggers\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp4\(dq,
            \(dqoutput\-options\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqstdout\(dq
                }
            ],
            \(dqseverity\(dq: \(dqWARN\(dq
        }
    ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
As a second example, we want to store DEBUG log messages in a file
that is at most 2MB and keep up to eight copies of old log files. Once the
logfile grows to 2MB, it should be renamed and a new file should be created.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
\(dqServer\(dq: {
    \(dqloggers\(dq: [
        {
            \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp6\(dq,
            \(dqoutput\-options\(dq: [
                {
                    \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dq/var/log/kea/kea\-debug.log\(dq,
                    \(dqmaxver\(dq: 8,
                    \(dqmaxsize\(dq: 204800,
                    \(dqflush\(dq: true,
                    \(dqpattern\(dq: \(dq%d{%j %H:%M:%S.%q} %c %m\en\(dq
                }
            ],
            \(dqseverity\(dq: \(dqDEBUG\(dq,
            \(dqdebuglevel\(dq: 99
        }
   ]
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Notice that the above configuration uses a custom pattern which produces output like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
220 13:50:31.783 kea\-dhcp4.dhcp4 DHCP4_STARTED Kea DHCPv4 server version 1.6.0\-beta2\-git started
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Logging During Kea Startup
.sp
The logging configuration is specified in the configuration file.
However, when Kea starts, the configuration file is not read until partway into the
initialization process. Prior to that, the logging settings are set to
default values, although it is possible to modify some aspects of the
settings by means of environment variables. In the absence of
any logging configuration in the configuration file, the settings of the
(possibly modified) default configuration persist while the program
is running.
.sp
The following environment variables can be used to control the behavior
of logging during startup:
.sp
\fBKEA_LOCKFILE_DIR\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Specifies a directory where the logging system should create its lock
file. If not specified, it is prefix/var/run/kea, where \(dqprefix\(dq
defaults to /usr/local. This variable must not end with a slash.
There is one special value: \(dqnone\(dq, which instructs Kea not to create
a lock file at all. This may cause issues if several processes log to
the same file.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBKEA_LOGGER_DESTINATION\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Specifies logging output. There are several special values:
.sp
\fBstdout\fP
Log to standard output.
.sp
\fBstderr\fP
Log to standard error.
.sp
\fBsyslog[:fac]\fP
Log via syslog. The optional \(dqfac\(dq (which is separated from the word
\(dqsyslog\(dq by a colon) specifies the facility to be used for the log
messages. Unless specified, messages are logged using the
facility \(dqlocal0\(dq.
.sp
Any other value is treated as a name of the output file. If not
otherwise specified, Kea logs to standard output.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Logging Levels
.sp
All Kea servers follow the overall intention to let the user
know what is going on while not overloading the logging system with too much information, as that
could easily be used as a denial\-of\-service attack.
.sp
Unlike the FATAL, ERROR, WARN and
INFO levels, DEBUG has additional parameters. The following list details
the basic information that is logged on each level. Sometimes the circumstances
determine whether a piece of information is logged on a higher
or lower level. For example, if a packet is being dropped due to configured classification, that
is an execution of the configured policy and would be logged on debuglevel 15. However, if the
packet is dropped due to an exception being thrown, it is much more important, as it may indicate
a software bug, serious problems with memory, or database connectivity problems. As such it may
be logged on much higher levels, such as WARN or even ERROR.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- singular messages printed during startup or shutdown of the server.
.IP \(bu 2
10 \- log information about received API commands.
.IP \(bu 2
15 \- information about reasons why a packet was dropped.
.IP \(bu 2
40 \- tracing information, including processing decisions, results
of expression evaluations, and more.
.IP \(bu 2
45 \- similar to level 40, but with more details, e.g. the subnet being
selected for an incoming packet.
.IP \(bu 2
50 \- evaluations of expressions, status received from hook points, lease
processing, packet processing details, including unpacking, packing, sending, etc.
.IP \(bu 2
55 \- includes all details available, including full packet contents
with all options printed.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The debug levels apply only to messages logged on DEBUG, and are configured using
the \fBdebuglevel\fP option. See the The debuglevel (integer) Logger section for details.
.SH THE KEA SHELL
.SS Overview of the Kea Shell
.sp
The Kea servers provide a RESTful control interface
over HTTP. That API is typically expected to be used by various IPAMs
and similar management systems. Nevertheless, there may be cases when an
administrator wants to send a command to the server directly, and the Kea shell
provides a way to do this. It is a simple command\-line,
scripting\-friendly, text client that is able to connect to the server, send
it commands with parameters, retrieve the responses, and display them.
.sp
As the primary purpose of the Kea shell is as a tool in a scripting
environment, it is not interactive. However, by following simple guidelines it can
be run manually.
.sp
Kea 1.9.0 introduced basic HTTP authentication support.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Kea version 2.7.2 introduces direct support of HTTP/HTTPS control channel
by Kea servers (\fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4>, \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> and
\fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>). The \fBkea\-shell\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-shell> can be used with
these servers, the only difference is the \fB\-\-service\fP argument becomes
useless and is ignored by Kea servers. Note the result is encapsulated
in a list for backward compatibility.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Shell Usage
.sp
\fBkea\-shell\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-shell> is run as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-shell [\-\-host hostname] [\-\-port number] [\-\-path path] [\-\-auth\-user] [\-\-auth\-password] [\-\-auth\-password\-file] [\-\-timeout seconds] [command]
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
where:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-host hostname\fP specifies the hostname of the server. If not
specified, \(dqlocalhost\(dq is used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-port number\fP specifies the TCP port on which the server listens. If
not specified, 8000 is used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-path path\fP specifies the path in the URL to connect to. If not
specified, an empty path is used. As the server listens at the empty
path, this parameter is useful only with a reverse proxy.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-auth\-user\fP specifies the user ID for basic HTTP authentication.
If not specified or specified as the empty string, authentication is
not used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-auth\-password\fP specifies the password for basic HTTP authentication.
If not specified but the user ID is specified, an empty password is used.
If used together with \fB\-\-auth\-password\-file\fP, it is disregarded.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-auth\-password\-file\fP specifies a text file whose first line contains
the password to be used for basic HTTP authentication. This flag takes
precedence over \fB\-\-auth\-password\fP flag.
Caution is advised when setting file permissions
for the file containing the password. Access to the file should be as strict as
possible and limited only to the Kea administrators.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-timeout seconds\fP specifies the timeout (in seconds) for the
connection. If not given, 10 seconds is used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-service service\-name\fP is now obsolete but is still recognize for
backward compatibility.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcommand\fP specifies the command to be sent. If not specified, the
\fBlist\-commands\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-list-commands> command is used.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Other switches are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-h\fP \- prints a help message.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-v\fP \- displays the Kea version.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-V\fP \- displays the extended Kea version.
.UNINDENT
.sp
See TLS Support for new command\-line arguments associated with TLS/HTTPS support.
.sp
Once started, the shell reads the parameters for the command from standard
input, which are expected to be in JSON format. When all have been read,
the shell establishes a connection with the server using HTTP, sends the
command, and awaits a response. Once that is received, it is displayed
on standard output.
.sp
For a list of available commands, see Management API \%<#\:ctrl-channel>;
additional commands may be provided by hook libraries. For a list of
all supported commands from the server, use the \fBlist\-commands\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-list-commands> command.
.sp
The following shows a simple example of usage:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-shell \-\-host 192.0.2.1 \-\-port 8001 \-\-auth\-user foo \-\-auth\-password\-file secret_file list\-commands
^D
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After the command line is entered, the program waits for command
parameters to be entered. Since \fBlist\-commands\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-list-commands> does not take any
arguments, Ctrl\-D (represented in the above example by \(dq^D\(dq)
indicates end\-of\-file and terminates the parameter input. The shell
then contacts the server and prints out the list of available commands
returned for the server.
.sp
The Kea shell will likely be most frequently used in
scripts; the next example shows a simple scripted execution. It sends
the command \fBconfig\-write\fP \%<#\:std-isccmd-config-write> to the server,
along with the parameters specified in param.json. The
result will be stored in result.json.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ cat param.json
\(dqfilename\(dq: \(dqmy\-config\-file.json\(dq
$ cat param.json | kea\-shell \-\-host 192.0.2.1 config\-write > result.json
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
When a reverse proxy is used to de\-multiplex requests to different
servers, the default empty path in the URL is not enough, so the
\fB\-\-path\fP parameter should be used. For instance, if requests to the
\(dq/kea\(dq path are forwarded to the server this can be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-shell \-\-host 192.0.2.1 \-\-port 8001 \-\-path kea ...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Kea shell requires Python to be installed on the system. It has been
tested with various versions of Python 3.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Beginning with Kea 2.4.0, \fBkea\-shell\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-shell> no longer supports Python 2.7.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Kea shell is intended to serve more as a demonstration of the
RESTful interface\(aqs capabilities (and, perhaps, an illustration for
people interested in integrating their management environments with Kea)
than as a serious management client. It is not likely to be
significantly expanded in the future; it is, and will remain, a simple
tool.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
When using this tool with basic HTTP authentication, please keep in
mind that command\-line arguments are not hidden from local users.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS TLS Support
.sp
\fBkea\-shell\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-shell> supports HTTPS connections; the TLS/HTTPS
support requires Python 3. The additional command\-line arguments are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-ca\fP specifies the file or directory name of the Certification
Authority. If not specified, HTTPS is not used.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-cert\fP specifies the file name of the user end\-entity public key
certificate. If specified, the file name of the user key must also be specified.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-\-key\fP specifies the file name of the user key file. If specified,
the file name of the user certificate must also be specified.
Encrypted key files are not supported.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For example, a basic HTTPS request to get a list of commands could
look like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ kea\-shell \-\-host 127.0.0.1 \-\-port 8000 \-\-ca ./kea\-ca.crt list\-commands
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH INTEGRATION WITH EXTERNAL SYSTEMS
.sp
Kea provides optional support for a variety of external systems, such as RADIUS, NETCONF,
YANG, and GSS\-TSIG. The following sections describe how to compile Kea with those additional
capabilities and how to configure them.
.SS YANG/NETCONF
.SS Overview
.sp
The Network Configuration Protocol, or NETCONF, is a network management protocol defined
in RFC 6241 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc6241>\&. It uses the YANG modeling language,
defined in RFC 6020 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc6020>, to provide a uniform way
of handling the configuration syntax of various networking devices. Kea provides optional
support for a YANG/NETCONF interface with the \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> agent.
.SS Installing NETCONF
.sp
To get its NETCONF capabilities, Kea requires the v3 versions of libyang and
Sysrepo. The specific versions that have been thoroughly tested with Kea are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
libyang v3.13.5 (commit efe43e3790822a3dc64d7d28db935d03fff8b81f)
.IP \(bu 2
sysrepo v3.7.11 (commit 1b720b196f630f348d9e0c131d326b3fb8c6aca7)
.IP \(bu 2
libyang\-cpp v3 (commit f3cd4e05462a16e81d6bfd0c4a5b385cf88a8549)
.IP \(bu 2
sysrepo\-cpp v3 (commit fe4edfa3998fdf312099ee1fb08a06983b6907f6)
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> may be compatible with later versions of libyang and
Sysrepo, but only the versions listed above have been thoroughly
tested by ISC.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Installing from packages is recommended, if they are provided by the system. If
not, users can build from sources following the directions below, which
should work on all popular operating systems.
.SS Installing \fBlibyang\fP From Sources
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ git clone https://github.com/CESNET/libyang.git
$ cd libyang
$ git checkout v3.13.5
$ mkdir build
$ cd build
$ cmake ..
$ make
$ make install
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Installing \fBsysrepo\fP From Sources
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ git clone https://github.com/sysrepo/sysrepo.git
$ cd sysrepo
$ git checkout v3.7.11
$ mkdir build
$ cd build
$ cmake \-DREPO_PATH=/etc/sysrepo ..
$ make
$ make install  # without sudo if you\(aqre doing development and want to run unit tests
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Installing \fBlibyang\-cpp\fP From Sources
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ git clone https://github.com/CESNET/libyang\-cpp.git
$ cd libyang\-cpp
$ git checkout v3
$ mkdir build
$ cd build
$ cmake \-DBUILD_TESTING=OFF ..
$ make
$ make install
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Installing \fBsysrepo\-cpp\fP From Sources
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ git clone https://github.com/sysrepo/sysrepo\-cpp.git
$ cd sysrepo\-cpp
$ git checkout v3
$ mkdir build
$ cd build
$ cmake \-DBUILD_TESTING=OFF ..
$ make
$ make install
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Compiling With NETCONF
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Obtain the Kea sources.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ git clone gitlab.isc.org/isc\-projects/kea.git
$ cd kea
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 2. 3
Set up the build.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ meson setup build \-D netconf=enabled \-D cpp_std=c++20
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
If any of the libraries are installed in a custom location, the
PKG_CONFIG_PATH can point to the .pc files.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
PKG_CONFIG_PATH=:/opt/libyang/lib/pkgconfig:/opt/libyang\-cpp/lib/pkgconfig:/opt/sysrepo/lib/pkgconfig:/opt/sysrepo\-cpp/lib/pkgconfig
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 3. 3
Check \fBbuild/config.report\fP to verify NETCONF support.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
NETCONF:
  YANG:               2.1.4
  YANG_PREFIX:        /opt/libyang
  YANGCPP:            1.1.0
  YANGCPP_PREFIX:     /opt/libyang\-cpp
  SYSREPO:            2.2.12
  SYSREPO_PREFIX:     /opt/sysrepo
  SYSREPOCPP:         1.1.0
  SYSREPOCPP_PREFIX:  /opt/sysrepo\-cpp
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 4. 3
Compile as usual.
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
$ meson compile \-C build
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Quick Sysrepo Overview
.sp
This section offers a brief overview of a subset of available
functions in Sysrepo. For more complete information, see the
Sysrepo homepage \%<https://\:www\:.sysrepo\:.org>\&.
.sp
In YANG, configurations and state data are described in YANG syntax
in module files named \fB<module\-name>[@<revision>].yang\fP
.sp
The revision part is optional and follows the \fBYYYY\-MM\-DD\fP format. An alternate
XML syntax YIN is defined but less user\-friendly. Top\-level modules are
named in Kea models (a short version of schema models).
.sp
There are two major modules that Kea is able to support: \fBkea\-dhcp4\-server\fP and
\fBkea\-dhcp6\-server\fP\&. While there is an active effort in the DHC working group at
IETF to develop a DHCPv6 YANG model, a similar initiative in the past for DHCPv4
failed. Therefore, Kea uses its own dedicated models for DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 but
partially supports the IETF model for DHCPv6.
.sp
All of the models have extra modules as dependencies, which are also provided.
All of the modules can be found in \fBsrc/share/yang/modules\fP in sources and in
\fBshare/kea/yang/modules\fP in the installation directory. This directory is
referred to as \fI${share_directory}\fP in the commands below.
.sp
To install modules from sources or upgrade them from older revisions,
run the following command. In the case of a revision upgrade, YANG
data will be migrated automatically to the new module schema.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ${share_directory}/yang/modules/utils/reinstall.sh
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
However, if there are any issues during the upgrade process, and data can be recreated from a NETCONF
client or through other means, Kea modules can be easily uninstalled before installing again
via this command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ${share_directory}/yang/modules/utils/reinstall.sh \-u
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This script should be able to reinstall Sysrepo. However, the \fB\-s\fP
flag can also be used to specify a path:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./build/src/share/yang/modules/utils/reinstall.sh \-s /path/to/sysrepo
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To individually install all modules:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ cd ./src/share/yang/modules
$ sysrepoctl \-i ./ietf\-dhcpv6\-server*.yang
$ sysrepoctl \-i ./kea\-dhcp4\-server*.yang
$ sysrepoctl \-i ./kea\-dhcp6\-server*.yang
\&...
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The installation should look similar to the following:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ ./build/src/share/yang/modules/utils/reinstall.sh
[INF] Connection 2 created.
[INF] Module \(dqkeatest\-module\(dq was installed.
[INF] File \(dqkeatest\-module@2022\-11\-30.yang\(dq was installed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 4 created.
[ERR] Module \(dqietf\-interfaces@2018\-02\-20\(dq already installed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 7 created.
[ERR] Module \(dqietf\-dhcpv6\-client\(dq is already in sysrepo.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 9 created.
[ERR] Module \(dqietf\-dhcpv6\-relay\(dq is already in sysrepo.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 11 created.
[ERR] Module \(dqietf\-dhcpv6\-server\(dq is already in sysrepo.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 13 created.
[ERR] Write permission \(dqietf\-yang\-types\(dq check failed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 15 created.
[ERR] Module \(dqietf\-dhcpv6\-options\(dq is already in sysrepo.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 17 created.
[ERR] Module \(dqietf\-dhcpv6\-types\(dq is already in sysrepo.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 21 created.
[INF] Module \(dqkea\-types\(dq was installed.
[INF] File \(dqkea\-types@2019\-08\-12.yang\(dq was installed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 23 created.
[INF] Module \(dqkea\-dhcp\-types\(dq was installed.
[INF] File \(dqkea\-dhcp\-types@2022\-11\-30.yang\(dq was installed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 25 created.
[INF] Module \(dqkea\-dhcp\-ddns\(dq was installed.
[INF] File \(dqkea\-dhcp\-ddns@2022\-07\-27.yang\(dq was installed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 27 created.
[INF] Connection 28 created.
[INF] Module \(dqkea\-dhcp4\-server\(dq was installed.
[INF] File \(dqkea\-dhcp4\-server@2022\-11\-30.yang\(dq was installed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
[INF] Connection 30 created.
[INF] Module \(dqkea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq was installed.
[INF] File \(dqkea\-dhcp6\-server@2022\-11\-30.yang\(dq was installed.
[INF] No datastore changes to apply.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To confirm whether the modules have been imported correctly, check the list of
currently installed YANG modules. It should be similar to this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepoctl \-l
Sysrepo repository: /etc/sysrepo

Module Name                | Revision   | Flags | Owner     | Startup Perms | Submodules | Features
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
ietf\-datastores            | 2018\-02\-14 | I     | user:user | 444           |            |
ietf\-dhcpv6\-client         | 2018\-09\-04 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
ietf\-dhcpv6\-options        | 2018\-09\-04 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
ietf\-dhcpv6\-relay          | 2018\-09\-04 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
ietf\-dhcpv6\-server         | 2018\-09\-04 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
ietf\-dhcpv6\-types          | 2018\-09\-04 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
ietf\-inet\-types            | 2013\-07\-15 | I     | user:user | 444           |            |
ietf\-interfaces            | 2018\-02\-20 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
ietf\-netconf               | 2013\-09\-29 | I     | user:user | 644           |            |
ietf\-netconf\-acm           | 2018\-02\-14 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
ietf\-netconf\-notifications | 2012\-02\-06 | I     | user:user | 644           |            |
ietf\-netconf\-with\-defaults | 2011\-06\-01 | I     | user:user | 444           |            |
ietf\-origin                | 2018\-02\-14 | I     | user:user | 444           |            |
ietf\-yang\-library          | 2019\-01\-04 | I     | user:user | 644           |            |
ietf\-yang\-metadata         | 2016\-08\-05 | i     |           |               |            |
ietf\-yang\-schema\-mount     | 2019\-01\-14 | I     | user:user | 644           |            |
ietf\-yang\-types            | 2013\-07\-15 | I     | user:user | 444           |            |
kea\-dhcp\-ddns              | 2022\-07\-27 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
kea\-dhcp\-types             | 2022\-11\-30 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
kea\-dhcp4\-server           | 2022\-11\-30 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
kea\-dhcp6\-server           | 2022\-11\-30 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
kea\-types                  | 2019\-08\-12 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
keatest\-module             | 2022\-11\-30 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
sysrepo\-monitoring         | 2022\-04\-08 | I     | user:user | 600           |            |
sysrepo\-plugind            | 2022\-03\-10 | I     | user:user | 644           |            |
yang                       | 2022\-06\-16 | I     | user:user | 444           |            |

Flags meaning: I \- Installed/i \- Imported; R \- Replay support
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To reinstall a module, if the revision YANG entry was bumped, simply installing
it will update it automatically. Otherwise, it must first be uninstalled:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepoctl \-u kea\-dhcp4\-server
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the module is used (i.e. imported) by other modules, it can be uninstalled
only after the dependent modules have first been uninstalled.
Installation and uninstallation must be done in dependency order and
reverse\-dependency order, as appropriate.
.SS Supported YANG Models
.sp
The currently supported models are \fBkea\-dhcp4\-server\fP and
\fBkea\-dhcp6\-server\fP\&. There is partial support for
\fBietf\-dhcpv6\-server\fP, but the primary focus of testing has been on Kea DHCP
servers. Other models (e.g. \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>) are currently not supported.
.SS Using the NETCONF Agent
.sp
The NETCONF agent follows this algorithm:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
For each managed server, get the initial configuration from the
server through the control socket.
.IP \(bu 2
Open a connection with the Sysrepo environment and establish two
sessions with the startup and running datastores.
.IP \(bu 2
Check that the used (not\-essential) and required (essential) modules are
installed in the Sysrepo repository at the right revision. If an
essential module \- that is, a module where the configuration schema for a
managed server is defined \- is not installed, raise a fatal error.
.IP \(bu 2
For each managed server, get the YANG configuration from the startup
datastore, translate it to JSON, and load it onto the server being
configured.
.IP \(bu 2
For each managed server, subscribe a module change callback using its
model name.
.IP \(bu 2
When a running configuration is changed, try to validate or load the
updated configuration via the callback to the managed server.
.UNINDENT
.SS Configuration
.sp
The behavior described in Using the NETCONF Agent
is controlled by several configuration flags, which can be set in the
global scope or in a specific managed\-server scope. If the latter,
the value defined in the managed\-server scope takes precedence. These
flags are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBboot\-update\fP \- controls the initial configuration phase; when
\fBtrue\fP (the default), the initial configuration retrieved from the
classic Kea server JSON configuration file is loaded first, and then
the startup YANG model is loaded. This setting lets administrators
define a control socket in the local JSON file and then download the
configuration from YANG. When set to \fBfalse\fP, this phase is skipped.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsubscribe\-changes\fP \- controls the module change
subscription; when \fBtrue\fP (the default), a module change callback is
subscribed, but when \fBfalse\fP the phase is skipped and running
configuration updates are disabled. When set to \fBtrue\fP, the running
datastore is used to subscribe for changes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvalidate\-changes\fP \- controls how Kea monitors changes in
the Sysrepo configuration. Sysrepo offers two stages where Kea can
interact: validation and application. At the validation (or
\fBSR_EV_CHANGE\fP event, in the Sysrepo naming convention) stage, Kea
retrieves the newly committed configuration and verifies it. If the
configuration is incorrect for any reason, the Kea servers reject it
and the error is propagated back to the Sysrepo, which then returns
an error. This step only takes place if \fBvalidate\-changes\fP is set to
\fBtrue\fP\&. In the application (or \fBSR_EV_UPDATE\fP event in the Sysrepo naming
convention) stage, the actual configuration is applied. At this stage
Kea can receive the configuration, but it is too late to signal back
any errors as the configuration has already been committed.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The idea behind the initial configuration phase is to boot Kea servers
with a minimal configuration which includes only a control socket,
making them manageable. For instance, for the DHCPv4 server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {
        \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq: {
           \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp4\-ctrl.sock\(dq,
           \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
With module change subscriptions enabled, the \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> daemon
monitors any configuration changes as they appear in the Sysrepo. Such
changes can be done using the \fBsysrepocfg\fP tool or remotely using any
NETCONF client. For details, please see the Sysrepo documentation or
A Step\-by\-Step NETCONF Agent Operation Example\&.
Those tools can be used to modify YANG configurations in the running
datastore. Note that committed configurations are only updated in the
running datastore; to keep them between server reboots they must be
copied to the startup datastore.
.sp
When module changes are tracked (using \fBsubscribe\-changes\fP set to
\fBtrue\fP) and the running configuration has changed (e.g. using
\fBsysrepocfg\fP or any NETCONF client), the callback validates the
modified configuration (if \fBvalidate\-changes\fP was not set to \fBfalse\fP)
and runs a second time to apply the new configuration. If the validation
fails, the callback is still called again but with an \fBSR_EV_ABORT\fP
(vs. \fBSR_EV_DONE\fP) event with rollback changes.
.sp
The returned code of the callback on an \fBSR_EV_DONE\fP event is ignored, as it is
too late to refuse a bad configuration.
.sp
There are four ways in which a modified YANG configuration might
be incorrect:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
It could be non\-compliant with the schema, e.g. an unknown entry, missing a
mandatory entry, a value with a bad type, or not matching a constraint.
.IP 2. 3
It could fail to be translated from YANG to JSON, e.g. an invalid user
context.
.IP 3. 3
It could fail Kea server sanity checks, e.g. an out\-of\-subnet\-pool range
or an unsupported database type.
.IP 4. 3
The syntax may be correct and pass server sanity checks but the
configuration could fail to run, e.g. the configuration specifies database
credentials but the database refuses the connection.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The first case is handled by Sysrepo. The second and third cases are
handled by \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> in the validation phase (if not disabled by
setting \fBvalidate\-changes\fP to \fBtrue\fP). The last case causes the
application phase to fail without a sensible report to Sysrepo.
.sp
The managed Kea servers and agents are described in the
\fBmanaged\-servers\fP section. Each sub\-section begins with the service
name: \fBdhcp4\fP, \fBdhcp6\fP, and \fBd2\fP\&.
.sp
Each managed server entry may contain:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
control flags \- \fBboot\-update\fP, \fBsubscribe\-changes\fP, and/or \fBvalidate\-changes\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmodel\fP \- specifies the YANG model/module name. For each service,
the default is the corresponding Kea YANG model, e.g. for \fB\(dqdhcp4\(dq\fP
it is \fB\(dqkea\-dhcp4\-server\(dq\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcontrol\-socket\fP \- specifies the control socket for managing the
service configuration.
.UNINDENT
.sp
A control socket is specified by:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsocket\-type\fP \- the socket type is either \fBstdout\fP, \fBunix\fP, or \fBhttp\fP\&.
\fBstdout\fP is the default;
it is not really a socket, but it allows \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> to run in
debugging mode where everything is printed on stdout, and it can also be
used to redirect commands easily. \fBunix\fP is the standard direct
server control channel, which uses UNIX sockets; \fBhttp\fP uses
a control channel, which accepts HTTP connections.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsocket\-name\fP \- the local socket name for the \fBunix\fP socket type
(default empty string).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsocket\-url\fP \- the HTTP URL for the \fBhttp\fP socket type (default
\fBhttp://127.0.0.1:8000/\fP).
.UNINDENT
.sp
User contexts can store arbitrary data as long as they are in valid JSON
syntax and their top\-level element is a map (i.e. the data must be
enclosed in curly brackets). They are accepted at the NETCONF entry,
i.e. below the top\-level, managed\-service entry, and control\-socket
entry scopes.
.sp
Hook libraries can be loaded by the NETCONF agent just as with other
servers or agents; however, currently no hook points are defined. The
\fBhooks\-libraries\fP list contains the list of hook libraries that
should be loaded by \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>, along with their configuration
information specified with \fBparameters\fP\&.
.sp
Please consult Logging \%<#\:logging> for details on how to configure
logging. The name of the NETCONF agent\(aqs main logger is \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>, as
given in the example above.
.SS A \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> Configuration Example
.sp
The following example demonstrates the basic NETCONF configuration. More
examples are available in the \fBdoc/examples/netconf\fP directory in the
Kea sources.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
// This is a simple example of a configuration for the NETCONF agent.
// This server provides a YANG interface for all Kea servers and the agent.
{
    \(dqNetconf\(dq:
    {
        // Control flags can be defined in the global scope or
        // in a managed server scope. Precedences are:
        // \- use the default value (true)
        // \- use the global value
        // \- use the local value.
        // So this overwrites the default value:
        \(dqboot\-update\(dq: false,

        // This map specifies how each server is managed. For each server there
        // is a name of the YANG model to be used and the control channel.
        // Currently three control channel types are supported:
        // \(dqstdout\(dq which outputs the configuration on the standard output,
        // \(dqunix\(dq which uses the local control channel supported by the
        // \(dqdhcp4\(dq and \(dqdhcp6\(dq servers (\(dqd2\(dq support is not yet available),
        // and \(dqhttp\(dq which uses HTTP/HTTPS control channels.
        \(dqmanaged\-servers\(dq:
        {
            // This is how kea\-netconf can communicate with the DHCPv4 server.
            \(dqdhcp4\(dq:
            {
                \(dqcomment\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 server\(dq,
                \(dqmodel\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp4\-server\(dq,
                \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq:
                {
                    \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq,
                    \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dqkea4\-ctrl\-socket\(dq
                }
            },

            // DHCPv6 parameters.
            \(dqdhcp6\(dq:
            {
                \(dqmodel\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq,
                \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq:
                {
                    \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq,
                    \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dqkea6\-ctrl\-socket\(dq
                }
            },

            \(dqd2\(dq:
            {
                \(dqmodel\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp\-ddns\(dq,
                \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq:
                {
                    \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq,
                    \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-ddns\-ctrl\-socket\(dq,
                    \(dquser\-context\(dq: { \(dqin\-use\(dq: true }
                }
            },

            \(dqdhcp4\-http\(dq:
            {
                \(dqcomment\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 server using HTTP\(dq,
                \(dqmodel\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp4\-server\(dq,
                \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq:
                {
                    \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqhttp\(dq,
                    \(dqsocket\-url\(dq: \(dqhttp://127.0.0.1:8000/\(dq
                }
            }
        },

        // kea\-netconf is able to load hook libraries that augment its operation.
        // Currently there are no hook points defined in kea\-netconf
        // processing.
        \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
            // The hook libraries list may contain more than one library.
            {
                // The only necessary parameter is the library filename.
                \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqcustom_hooks_example.so\(dq,

                // Some libraries may support parameters. Make sure you
                // type this section carefully, as kea\-netconf does not
                // validate it (because the format is library\-specific).
                \(dqparameters\(dq: {
                    \(dqparam1\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq
                }
            }
        ],

        // Similar to other Kea components, NETCONF also uses logging.
        \(dqloggers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\-netconf\(dq,
                \(dqoutput\-options\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dq/var/log/kea/kea\-netconf.log\(dq,
                        // Several additional parameters are possible in
                        // addition to the typical output.
                        // Flush determines whether logger flushes output
                        //  to a file.
                        // Maxsize determines maximum filesize before
                        // the file is rotated.
                        // Maxver specifies the maximum number of
                        //  rotated files to be kept.
                        \(dqflush\(dq: true,
                        \(dqmaxsize\(dq: 204800,
                        \(dqmaxver\(dq: 4
                    }
                ],
                \(dqseverity\(dq: \(dqINFO\(dq,
                \(dqdebuglevel\(dq: 0
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Starting and Stopping the NETCONF Agent
.sp
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> accepts the following command\-line switches:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-c file\fP \- specifies the configuration file.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-d\fP \- specifies whether the agent logging should be switched to
debug/verbose mode. In verbose mode, the logging severity and
debuglevel specified in the configuration file are ignored and
\(dqdebug\(dq severity and the maximum debuglevel (99) are assumed. The
flag is convenient for temporarily switching the server into maximum
verbosity, e.g. when debugging.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-t file\fP \- specifies the configuration file to be tested.
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> attempts to load it and conducts sanity checks;
certain checks are possible only while running the actual server. The
actual status is reported with exit code (0 = configuration appears valid,
1 = error encountered). Kea prints out log messages to standard
output and error to standard error when testing the configuration.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-v\fP \- displays the version of \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> and exits.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-V\fP \- displays the extended version information for \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>
and exits. The listing includes the versions of the libraries
dynamically linked to Kea.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-W\fP \- displays the Kea configuration report and exits. The report
is a copy of the \fBconfig.report\fP file produced by \fBmeson setup\fP;
it is embedded in the executable binary.
.sp
The contents of the \fBconfig.report\fP file may also be accessed by examining
certain libraries in the installation tree or in the source tree.
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# from installation using libkea\-process.so
$ strings ${prefix}/lib/libkea\-process.so | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libkea\-process.so
$ strings src/lib/process/.libs/libkea\-process.so | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libkea\-process.a
$ strings src/lib/process/.libs/libkea\-process.a | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq

# from sources using libcfgrpt.a
$ strings src/lib/process/cfgrpt/.libs/libcfgrpt.a | sed \-n \(aqs/;;;; //p\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS A Step\-by\-Step NETCONF Agent Operation Example
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Copies of example configurations presented within this section can be
found in the Kea source code, under
\fBdoc/examples/netconf/kea\-dhcp6\-operations\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Setup of NETCONF Agent Operation Example
.sp
The test box has an Ethernet interface named eth1. On some systems it is
possible to rename interfaces; for instance, on Linux with an ens38
interface:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# ip link set down dev ens38
# ip link set name eth1 dev ens38
# ip link set up dev eth1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The interface must have an address in the test prefix:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# ip \-6 addr add 2001:db8::1/64 dev eth1
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Kea DHCPv6 server must be launched with the configuration specifying
a control socket used to receive control commands. The \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>
process uses this socket to communicate with the DHCPv6 server, i.e. it
pushes translated configurations to that server using control commands.
The following is an example control socket specification for the Kea
DHCPv6 server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq: {
           \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock\(dq,
           \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In order to launch the Kea DHCPv6 server using the configuration
contained within the netconf/kea\-dhcp6\-operations/boot.json \%<#\:config-example-netconf-kea-dhcp6-operations-boot-json> file, run:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# kea\-dhcp6 \-d \-c boot.json
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The current configuration of the server can be fetched via a control
socket by running:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# echo \(aq{ \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqconfig\-get\(dq }\(aq | socat UNIX:/opt/kea/var/run/kea/kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock \(aq\-,ignoreeof\(aq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following is the example netconf/kea\-dhcp6\-operations/netconf.json \%<#\:config-example-netconf-kea-dhcp6-operations-netconf-json> configuration for:
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>, to manage the Kea DHCPv6 server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqNetconf\(dq: {
    \(dqloggers\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqdebuglevel\(dq: 99,
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\-netconf\(dq,
        \(dqoutput\-options\(dq: [
          {
            \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqstderr\(dq
          }
        ],
        \(dqseverity\(dq: \(dqDEBUG\(dq
      }
    ],
    \(dqmanaged\-servers\(dq: {
      \(dqdhcp6\(dq: {
        \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq: {
          \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock\(dq,
          \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that in production there should not be a need to log at the DEBUG level.
.sp
The Kea NETCONF agent is launched by:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
# kea\-netconf \-d \-c netconf.json
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Now that both \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> are running, it is
possible to populate updates to the configuration to the DHCPv6 server.
The following is the configuration extracted from \fBstartup.xml\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<config xmlns=\(dqurn:ietf:params:xml:ns:yang:kea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq>
  <subnet6>
    <id>1</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8::1:0</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8::1:ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8::1:0/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8::/64</subnet>
  </subnet6>
  <interfaces\-config>
    <interfaces>eth1</interfaces>
  </interfaces\-config>
  <control\-socket>
    <socket\-name>kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock</socket\-name>
    <socket\-type>unix</socket\-type>
  </control\-socket>
</config>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To populate this new configuration:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-d startup \-f xml \-m kea\-dhcp6\-server \-\-import=startup.xml
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> pushes the configuration found in the Sysrepo startup
datastore to all Kea servers during its initialization phase, after it
subscribes to module changes in the Sysrepo running datastore. This
action copies the configuration from the startup datastore to the
running datastore and enables the running datastore, making it
available.
.sp
Changes to the running datastore are applied after validation to the Kea
servers. Note that they are not by default copied back to the startup
datastore, i.e. changes are not permanent.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
\fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf> fetches the entire configuration from any Sysrepo datastore in a
single \fBget\-config\fP NETCONF operation. Prior to Kea 2.3.2, a \fBget\-config\fP operation
was done for each leaf and leaf\-list node.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Example of Error Handling in NETCONF Operation
.sp
There are four classes of issues with configurations applied via
NETCONF:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
The configuration does not comply with the YANG schema.
.IP 2. 3
The configuration cannot be translated from YANG to the Kea JSON.
.IP 3. 3
The configuration is rejected by the Kea server.
.IP 4. 3
The configuration was validated by the Kea server but cannot be
applied.
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the first case, consider the following \fBBAD\-schema.xml\fP
configuration file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<config xmlns=\(dqurn:ietf:params:xml:ns:yang:kea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq>
  <subnet4>
    <id>1</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8::1:0</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8::1:ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8::1:0/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8::/64</subnet>
  </subnet4>
  <interfaces\-config>
    <interfaces>eth1</interfaces>
  </interfaces\-config>
  <control\-socket>
    <socket\-name>kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock</socket\-name>
    <socket\-type>unix</socket\-type>
  </control\-socket>
</config>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is directly rejected by \fBsysrepocfg\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-d running \-f xml \-m kea\-dhcp6\-server \-\-import=BAD\-schema.xml
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the second case, the configuration is rejected by \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>\&.
For example, consider this \fBBAD\-translator.xml\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<config xmlns=\(dqurn:ietf:params:xml:ns:yang:kea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq>
  <subnet6>
    <id>1</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8::1:0</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8::1:ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8::1:0/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8::/64</subnet>
  </subnet6>
  <interfaces\-config>
    <interfaces>eth1</interfaces>
  </interfaces\-config>
  <control\-socket>
    <socket\-name>kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock</socket\-name>
    <socket\-type>unix</socket\-type>
  </control\-socket>
  <user\-context>bad</user\-context>
</config>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the third case, the configuration is presented to the Kea DHCPv6
server and fails to validate, as in this \fBBAD\-config.xml\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<config xmlns=\(dqurn:ietf:params:xml:ns:yang:kea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq>
  <subnet6>
    <id>1</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8:1::0</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8:1::ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8:1::0/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8::/64</subnet>
  </subnet6>
  <interfaces\-config>
    <interfaces>eth1</interfaces>
  </interfaces\-config>
  <control\-socket>
    <socket\-name>kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock</socket\-name>
    <socket\-type>unix</socket\-type>
  </control\-socket>
</config>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In the last case, the misconfiguration is detected too late and the
change must be reverted in Sysrepo, e.g. using the startup datastore as
a backup.
.SS NETCONF Operation Example with Two Pools
.sp
This example adds a second pool to the initial (i.e. startup)
configuration in the \fBtwopools.xml\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<config xmlns=\(dqurn:ietf:params:xml:ns:yang:kea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq>
  <subnet6>
    <id>1</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8::1:0</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8::1:ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8::1:0/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8::2:0</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8::2:ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8::2:0/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8::/64</subnet>
  </subnet6>
  <interfaces\-config>
    <interfaces>eth1</interfaces>
  </interfaces\-config>
  <control\-socket>
    <socket\-name>kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock</socket\-name>
    <socket\-type>unix</socket\-type>
  </control\-socket>
</config>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration is installed by:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-d running \-f xml \-m kea\-dhcp6\-server \-\-import=twopools.xml
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS NETCONF Operation Example with Two Subnets
.sp
This example specifies two subnets in the \fBtwosubnets.xml\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<config xmlns=\(dqurn:ietf:params:xml:ns:yang:kea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq>
  <subnet6>
    <id>1</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8:1::</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8:1::ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8:1::/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8:1::/64</subnet>
  </subnet6>
  <subnet6>
    <id>2</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8:2::</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8:2::ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8:2::/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8:2::/64</subnet>
  </subnet6>
  <interfaces\-config>
    <interfaces>eth1</interfaces>
  </interfaces\-config>
  <control\-socket>
    <socket\-name>kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock</socket\-name>
    <socket\-type>unix</socket\-type>
  </control\-socket>
</config>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration is installed by:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-d running \-f xml \-m kea\-dhcp6\-server \-\-import=twosubnets.xml
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS NETCONF Operation Example With Logging
.sp
This example adds a logger entry to the initial (i.e. startup)
configuration in the \fBlogging.xml\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
<config xmlns=\(dqurn:ietf:params:xml:ns:yang:kea\-dhcp6\-server\(dq>
  <interfaces\-config>
    <interfaces>eth1</interfaces>
  </interfaces\-config>
  <subnet6>
    <id>1</id>
    <pool>
      <start\-address>2001:db8::1:0</start\-address>
      <end\-address>2001:db8::1:ffff</end\-address>
      <prefix>2001:db8::1:0/112</prefix>
    </pool>
    <subnet>2001:db8::/64</subnet>
  </subnet6>
  <control\-socket>
    <socket\-name>kea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock</socket\-name>
    <socket\-type>unix</socket\-type>
  </control\-socket>
  <logger>
    <name>kea\-dhcp6</name>
    <output\-option>
      <output>stderr</output>
    </output\-option>
    <debuglevel>99</debuglevel>
    <severity>DEBUG</severity>
  </logger>
</config>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The corresponding Kea configuration in JSON is:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp6\(dq: {
    \(dqcontrol\-socket\(dq: {
      \(dqsocket\-name\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp6\-ctrl.sock\(dq,
      \(dqsocket\-type\(dq: \(dqunix\(dq
    },
    \(dqinterfaces\-config\(dq: {
      \(dqinterfaces\(dq: [ \(dqeth1\(dq ]
    },
    \(dqsubnet6\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqid\(dq: 1,
        \(dqpools\(dq: [
          {
            \(dqpool\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::1:0/112\(dq
          }
        ],
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: \(dq2001:db8::/64\(dq
      }
    ],
    \(dqloggers\(dq: [
      {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dqkea\-dhcp6\(dq,
        \(dqoutput\-options\(dq: [
          {
            \(dqoutput\(dq: \(dqstderr\(dq
          }
        ],
        \(dqseverity\(dq: \(dqDEBUG\(dq,
        \(dqdebuglevel\(dq: 99
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Finally, any of the previous examples can be replayed by using
\fBsysrepocfg\fP in edit mode as follows:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-d running \-f xml \-m kea\-dhcp6\-server \-\-edit
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or by using a NETCONF client like \fBnetopeer2\-cli\fP from the
Netopeer2 \%<https://\:github\:.com/\:CESNET/\:Netopeer2> NETCONF Toolset.
.SS Migrating YANG Data From a Prior Sysrepo Version
.sp
1. Shut down \fBkea\-netconf\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-netconf>\&. This ensures that backups for both datastores
are done at the same configuration state and that no change happens between exporting them.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 2. 3
Make data backups for all YANG modules, with one XML for each datastore.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-\-datastore running \-\-export=save.xml \-\-format=xml
$ sysrepocfg \-\-datastore startup \-\-export=save.xml \-\-format=xml
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Sysrepo v0 does not support import/export of all YANG modules; this capability was added in
Sysrepo v1. Users that are migrating from Sysrepo v0 will need to do per\-module backups. This has
the added benefit of isolating potential failures and preventing them from affecting all
modules. The command is the same, except it has the module name added to it at the end.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-\-datastore running \-\-export=save.xml \-\-format=xml kea\-dhcp6\-server
$ sysrepocfg \-\-datastore startup \-\-export=save.xml \-\-format=xml kea\-dhcp6\-server
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 3. 3
Upgrade Sysrepo to the newer version and then run:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ sysrepocfg \-\-datastore running \-\-import=save.xml
$ sysrepocfg \-\-datastore startup \-\-import=save.xml
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS GSS\-TSIG
.SS GSS\-TSIG Overview
.sp
Kea provides support for DNS updates, which can be protected using
Transaction Signatures (or TSIG). This protection is often adequate.
However, some systems, in particular Active Directory (AD) on Microsoft
Windows servers, have chosen to adopt a more complex GSS\-TSIG approach that offers
additional capabilities, such as using negotiated dynamic keys.
.sp
Kea supports GSS\-TSIG to protect DNS updates sent by
the Kea DHCP\-DDNS (D2) server in a hook, called \fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libddns_gss_tsig\:.so>\&.
.sp
GSS\-TSIG is defined in RFC 3645 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3645>\&.
The GSS\-TSIG protocol itself is an implementation of generic GSS\-API v2
services, defined in RFC 2743 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2743>\&.
.sp
Many protocols are involved in this mechanism:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Kerberos 5 \- RFC 4120 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4120>, which
provides the security framework;
.IP \(bu 2
GSS\-API (Generic Security Services Application Program Interface) \-
RFC 2743 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2743> for the API,
RFC 2744 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2743> for the C bindings, and
RFC 4121 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4121> for the application
to Kerberos 5;
.IP \(bu 2
SPNEGO (Simple and Protected GSS\-API Negotiation Mechanism) \-
RFC 4178 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc4178> for the negotiation;
.IP \(bu 2
DNS update RFC 2136 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2136>;
.IP \(bu 2
TSIG (Secret Key Transaction Authentication for DNS) \-
RFC 8945 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc8945>, which
protects DNS exchanges;
.IP \(bu 2
Secure Domain Name System (DNS) Dynamic Update \-
RFC 3007 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3007>, which is the
application of TSIG to DNS update protection;
.IP \(bu 2
TKEY (Secret Key Establishment for DNS) \-
RFC 2930 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2930>, which establishes
secret keys for TSIG by transmitting crypto payloads between DNS
parties; and
.IP \(bu 2
GSS\-TSIG \- RFC 3645 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3645>, which
is the application of GSS\-API to TSIG.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
To summarize, GSS\-API for Kerberos 5 with SPNEGO and TKEY are used to
negotiate a security context between the Kea D2 server and a DNS server:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 2.5
[image]
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The security context is then used by GSS\-TSIG to protect updates:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 2.5
[image]
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The Kea implementation of GSS\-TSIG uses a GSS\-API for Kerberos 5 with
the SPNEGO library. Two implementations meet this criteria: MIT Kerberos
5 and Heimdal.
.SS GSS\-TSIG Compilation
.sp
The following procedure was tested on Debian 12. A similar approach can be
applied to other systems.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Install either the MIT (\fBlibkrb5\-dev\fP) or the Heimdal (\fBheimdal\-dev\fP) library,
for instance:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sudo apt install libkrb5\-dev
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 2. 3
Obtain the Kea sources, extract the Kea sources.
.IP 3. 3
Set up the build:
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
meson setup build \-D krb5=enabled
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 4. 3
Make sure \fBbuild/config.report\fP shows the \fBKerberos5 GSS\-API\fP entry.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Kerberos5 GSS\-API:
  VERSION:         1.20.1
  VENDOR:          MIT
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Either the \fBkrb5.pc\fP file or the \fBkrb5\-config\fP tool are required to be
present. They are provided by both MIT Kerberos 5 and Heimdal; however, on some
systems where both Kerberos 5 and Heimdal are installed, it is a symbolic link
to one of them. If the tool is not in the standard location, it can be selected
by setting \fBPKG_CONFIG_PATH=/path/to/krb5.pc:${PKG_CONFIG_PATH}\fP or
\fBPATH=/path/to/krb5\-config:${PATH}\fP prior to \fBmeson setup\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 6. 3
Compile with \fBmeson compile \-C build\fP\&.
.IP 7. 3
.INDENT 3.0
.TP
.B After compilation, \fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libddns_gss_tsig\:.so> is available in the
\fBbuild/src/hooks/d2/gss_tsig\fP directory. It can be loaded by
\fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>\&.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libddns_gss_tsig\:.so> supports both MIT Kerberos 5 and Heimdal
implementations. Heimdal is picky about security\-sensitive file permissions and
is known to emit an unclear error message. It is a good idea to keep these
files plain, with one link and no access for the group or other users.
.sp
The \fBkrb5\-config\fP script should provide an \fB\-\-vendor\fP option which
identifies the implementation.
.SS GSS\-TSIG Deployment
.sp
Before using GSS\-TSIG, a GSS\-TSIG capable DNS server, such as BIND 9
or Microsoft Active Directory (AD), must be deployed. Other
GSS\-TSIG capable implementations may work, but have not been tested.
.SS Kerberos 5 Setup
.sp
There are two kinds of key tables (keytab files): the system one used
by servers, and client tables used by clients. For Kerberos 5, Kea is a
\fBclient\fP\&.
.sp
Install the Kerberos 5 client library and \fBkadmin\fP tool:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
sudo apt install krb5\-kdc krb5\-admin\-server
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The following examples use the \fBEXAMPLE.ORG\fP realm to demonstrate required
configuration steps and settings.
.sp
The Kerberos 5 client library must be configured to accept incoming requests
for the realm \fBEXAMPLE.ORG\fP by updating the \fBkrb5.conf\fP file
(e.g. on Linux: /etc/krb5.conf):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[libdefaults]
    default_realm = EXAMPLE.ORG
    kdc_timesync = 1
    ccache_type = 4
    forwardable = true
    proxiable = true

[realms]
    EXAMPLE.ORG = {
            kdc = kdc.example.org
            admin_server = kdc.example.org
    }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In addition to the \fBkrb5.conf\fP file, the \fBkdc.conf\fP file can be used
(e.g. on Linux: /etc/krb5kdc/kdc.conf):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[kdcdefaults]
    kdc_ports = 750,88

[realms]
    EXAMPLE.ORG = {
        database_name = /var/lib/krb5kdc/principal
        admin_keytab = FILE:/etc/krb5kdc/kadm5.keytab
        acl_file = /etc/krb5kdc/kadm5.acl
        key_stash_file = /etc/krb5kdc/stash
        kdc_ports = 750,88
        max_life = 10h 0m 0s
        max_renewable_life = 7d 0h 0m 0s
        master_key_type = des3\-hmac\-sha1
        #supported_enctypes = aes256\-cts:normal aes128\-cts:normal
        default_principal_flags = +preauth
    }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBkadmind\fP daemon Access Control List (ACL) must be configured to give
permissions to the DNS client principal to access the Kerberos 5 database
(e.g. on Linux: /etc/krb5kdc/kadm5.acl):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
DHCP/admin.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG       *
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The administrator password for the default realm must be set:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
krb5_newrealm
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After the following message is displayed, enter
the password for the default realm:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
This script should be run on the master KDC/admin server to initialize
a Kerberos realm.  It will ask you to type in a master key password.
This password will be used to generate a key that is stored in
/etc/krb5kdc/stash.  You should try to remember this password, but it
is much more important that it be a strong password than that it be
remembered.  However, if you lose the password and /etc/krb5kdc/stash,
you cannot decrypt your Kerberos database.
Loading random data
Initializing database \(aq/var/lib/krb5kdc/principal\(aq for realm \(aqEXAMPLE.ORG\(aq,
master key name \(aqK/M@EXAMPLE.ORG\(aq
You will be prompted for the database Master Password.
It is important that you NOT FORGET this password.
Enter KDC database master key:
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Then retype the password:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Re\-enter KDC database master key to verify:
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If successfully applied, the following message is displayed:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Now that your realm is set up you may wish to create an administrative
principal using the addprinc subcommand of the kadmin.local program.
Then, this principal can be added to /etc/krb5kdc/kadm5.acl so that
you can use the kadmin program on other computers.  Kerberos admin
principals usually belong to a single user and end in /admin.  For
example, if jruser is a Kerberos administrator, then in addition to
the normal jruser principal, a jruser/admin principal should be
created.

Don\(aqt forget to set up DNS information so your clients can find your
KDC and admin servers.  Doing so is documented in the administration
guide.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The next step is to create the principals for the BIND 9 DNS server
(the service protected by the GSS\-TSIG TKEY) and for the DNS client
(the Kea DHCP\-DDNS server).
.sp
The BIND 9 DNS server principal (used for authentication) is created the
following way:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kadmin.local \-q \(dqaddprinc \-randkey DNS/server.example.org\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If successfully created, the following message is displayed:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
No policy specified for DNS/server.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG; defaulting to no policy
Authenticating as principal root/admin@EXAMPLE.ORG with password.
Principal \(dqDNS/server.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG\(dq created.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The DNS server principal must be exported so that it can be used by the BIND 9
DNS server. Only this principal is required, and it is exported to the keytab
file with the name \fBdns.keytab\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kadmin.local \-q \(dqktadd \-k /usr/local/share/kea/dns.keytab DNS/server.example.org\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If successfully exported, the following message is displayed:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
Authenticating as principal root/admin@EXAMPLE.ORG with password.
Entry for principal DNS/server.example.org with kvno 2, encryption type aes256\-cts\-hmac\-sha1\-96 added to keytab WRFILE:/usr/local/share/kea/dns.keytab.
Entry for principal DNS/server.example.org with kvno 2, encryption type aes128\-cts\-hmac\-sha1\-96 added to keytab WRFILE:/usr/local/share/kea/dns.keytab.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The DHCP client principal (used by the Kea DHCP\-DDNS server) is created the
following way:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kadmin.local \-q \(dqaddprinc \-randkey DHCP/admin.example.org\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If successfully created, the following message is displayed:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
No policy specified for DHCP/admin.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG; defaulting to no policy
Authenticating as principal root/admin@EXAMPLE.ORG with password.
Principal \(dqDHCP/admin.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG\(dq created.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The DHCP client principal must be exported so that it can be used by the
Kea DHCP\-DDNS server and the GSS\-TSIG hook library. It is exported to the client
keytab file with the name \fBdhcp.keytab\fP\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kadmin.local \-q \(dqktadd \-k /usr/local/share/kea/dhcp.keytab DHCP/admin.example.org\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Finally, the \fBkrb5\-admin\-server\fP must be restarted:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
systemctl restart krb5\-admin\-server.service
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS BIND 9 with GSS\-TSIG Configuration
.sp
The BIND 9 DNS server must be configured to use GSS\-TSIG, and to use the
previously exported DNS server principal from the keytab file \fBdns.keytab\fP\&.
Updating the \fBnamed.conf\fP file is required:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
options {
    ...
    directory \(dq/var/cache/bind\(dq;
    dnssec\-validation auto;
    listen\-on\-v6 { any; };
    tkey\-gssapi\-keytab \(dq/etc/bind/dns.keytab\(dq;
};
zone \(dqexample.org\(dq {
    type master;
    file \(dq/var/lib/bind/db.example.org\(dq;
    update\-policy {
        grant \(dqDHCP/admin.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG\(dq zonesub any;
    };
};
zone \(dq84.102.10.in\-addr.arpa\(dq {
    type master;
    file \(dq/etc/bind/db.10\(dq;
};
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The zone files should have an entry for the server principal FQDN
\fBserver.example.org\fP\&.
.sp
The \fB/etc/bind/db.10\fP file needs to be created or updated:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
;
; BIND reverse data file for local loopback interface
;
$TTL    604800                      ; 1 week
@       IN      SOA      server.example.org. root.example.org. (
                         2          ; Serial
                         604800     ; Refresh
                         86400      ; Retry
                         2419200    ; Expire
                         604800     ; Negative Cache TTL
                         )
;
@       IN      NS      ns.
40      IN      PTR     ns.example.org.
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fB/var/lib/bind/db.example.org\fP file needs to be created or updated:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
$ORIGIN .
$TTL                604800             ; 1 week
example.org         IN SOA  server.example.org. root.example.org. (
                            8          ; serial
                            604800     ; refresh (1 week)
                            86400      ; retry (1 day)
                            2419200    ; expire (4 weeks)
                            604800     ; minimum (1 week)
                            )
                    NS      example.org.
                    A       ${BIND9_IP_ADDR}
                    AAAA    ::1
$ORIGIN example.org.
kdc                 A       ${KDC_IP_ADDR}
server              A       ${BIND9_IP_ADDR}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After any configuration change the server must be reloaded or
restarted:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
systemctl restart named.service
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
It is possible to get the status or restart the logs:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
systemctl status named.service
journalctl \-u named | tail \-n 30
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Windows Active Directory Configuration
.sp
This sub\-section is based on an Amazon AWS provided Microsoft Windows Server
2016 with Active Directory pre\-installed, so it describes only the steps used
for GSS\-TSIG deployment. (For the complete configuration process, please refer to
Microsoft\(aqs documentation or other external resources. We found this \%<https://\:www\:.tenforums\:.com/\:tutorials/\:51456-windows-server-2016-setup-local-domain-controller\:.html> tutorial very
useful during configuration of our internal QA testing systems.)
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B Two Active Directory (AD) user accounts are needed:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
the first account is used to download AD information, such as
the client key table of Kea
.IP \(bu 2
the second account is mapped to the Kea DHCP client principal
.UNINDENT
.TP
.B Kea needs to know:
.INDENT 7.0
.IP \(bu 2
the server IP address
.IP \(bu 2
the domain/realm name: the domain is in lower case, the realm in upper
case, both without a final dot
.IP \(bu 2
the server name
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The second account (named \fBkea\fP below) is used to create a Service
Principal Name (SPN):
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
setspn \-S DHCP/kea.<domain> kea
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
After a shared secret key is generated and put in a key table file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
ktpass \-princ DHCP/kea.<domain>@<REALM> \-mapuser kea +rndpass \-mapop set \-ptype KRB5_NT_PRINCIPAL \-out dhcp.keytab
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBdhcp.keytab\fP takes the same usage as for UNIX Kerberos.
.SS GSS\-TSIG Troubleshooting
.sp
While testing GSS\-TSIG integration with Active Directory we came across
one very cryptic error:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
INFO  [kea\-dhcp\-ddns.gss\-tsig\-hooks/4678.139690935890624] GSS_TSIG_VERIFY_FAILED GSS\-TSIG verify failed: gss_verify_mic failed with GSSAPI error:
Major = \(aqA token had an invalid Message Integrity Check (MIC)\(aq (393216), Minor = \(aqPacket was replayed in wrong direction\(aq (100002).
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
In our case, the problem was that the Kea D2 server was trying to perform an update of a reverse
DNS zone while it was not configured. An easy solution is to add a reverse DNS
zone similar to the one configured in Kea. To do that, open the \(dqDNS Manager\(dq and choose
\(dqDNS\(dq from the list; from the dropdown list, choose \(dqReverse Lookup Zones\(dq; then
click \(dqAction\(dq and \(dqNew Zone\(dq; finally, follow the New Zone Wizard to add a new zone.
.sp
The standard requires both anti\-replay and sequence services. Experiences with the BIND 9 nsupdate
showed the sequence service led to problems so it is disabled by default in the hook. It seems
the anti\-replay service can also lead to problems with Microsoft DNS servers so it is now
configurable. Note that these security services are useless for DNS dynamic update which was
designed to run over UDP so with out of order and duplicated messages.
.SS Using GSS\-TSIG
.sp
There are a number of steps required to enable the GSS\-TSIG mechanism:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
\fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libddns_gss_tsig\:.so> must be loaded by \fBkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp-ddns>\&.
.IP 2. 3
The GSS\-TSIG\-capable DNS servers must be specified with their parameters.
.UNINDENT
.sp
An excerpt from a D2 server configuration is provided below; more examples are available in the
\fBdoc/examples/ddns\fP directory in the Kea sources.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
 {
 \(dqDhcpDdns\(dq: {
     // The following parameters are used to receive NCRs (NameChangeRequests)
     // from the local Kea DHCP server. Make sure your kea\-dhcp4 and kea\-dhcp6
     // matches this.
     \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq,
     \(dqport\(dq: 53001,
     \(dqdns\-server\-timeout\(dq : 1000,

     // Forward zone: secure.example.org. It uses GSS\-TSIG. It is served
     // by two DNS servers, which listen for DDNS requests at 192.0.2.1
     // and 192.0.2.2.
     \(dqforward\-ddns\(dq:
     {
         \(dqddns\-domains\(dq:
         [
             // DdnsDomain for zone \(dqsecure.example.org.\(dq
             {
                 \(dqname\(dq: \(dqsecure.example.org.\(dq,
                 \(dqcomment\(dq: \(dqDdnsDomain example\(dq,
                 \(dqdns\-servers\(dq:
                 [
                     { // This server has an entry in gss/servers and
                       // thus will use GSS\-TSIG.
                         \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq
                     },
                     { // This server also has an entry there, so will
                       // use GSS\-TSIG, too.
                         \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.2\(dq,
                         \(dqport\(dq: 5300
                     }
                 ]
             }
         ]
     },

     // Reverse zone: we want to update the reverse zone \(dq2.0.192.in\-addr.arpa\(dq.
     \(dqreverse\-ddns\(dq:
     {
         \(dqddns\-domains\(dq:
         [
             {
                 \(dqname\(dq: \(dq2.0.192.in\-addr.arpa.\(dq,
                 \(dqdns\-servers\(dq:
                 [
                     {
                         // There is a GSS\-TSIG definition for this server (see
                         // DhcpDdns/gss\-tsig/servers), so it will use
                         // Krb/GSS\-TSIG.
                         \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq
                     }
                 ]
             }
         ]
     },

     // The GSS\-TSIG hook is loaded and its configuration is specified here.
     \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
     {
         \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibddns_gss_tsig.so\(dq,
         \(dqparameters\(dq: {
             // This section governs the GSS\-TSIG integration. Each server
             // mentioned in forward\-ddns and/or reverse\-ddns needs to have
             // an entry here to be able to use GSS\-TSIG defaults (optional,
             // if specified they apply to all the GSS\-TSIG servers, unless
             // overwritten on specific server level).

             \(dqserver\-principal\(dq: \(dqDNS/server.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG\(dq,
             \(dqclient\-principal\(dq: \(dqDHCP/admin.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG\(dq,

             // client\-keytab and credentials\-cache can both be used to
             // store client keys. As credentials cache is more flexible,
             // it is recommended to use it. Typically, using both at the
             // same time may cause problems.
             // \(dqclient\-keytab\(dq: \(dqFILE:/etc/dhcp.keytab\(dq, // toplevel only
             \(dqcredentials\-cache\(dq: \(dqFILE:/etc/ccache\(dq, // toplevel only
             \(dqgss\-replay\-flag\(dq: true, // GSS anti replay service
             \(dqgss\-sequence\-flag\(dq: false, // no GSS sequence service
             \(dqtkey\-lifetime\(dq: 3600, // 1 hour
             \(dqrekey\-interval\(dq: 2700, // 45 minutes
             \(dqretry\-interval\(dq: 120, // 2 minutes
             \(dqtkey\-protocol\(dq: \(dqTCP\(dq,
             \(dqfallback\(dq: false,

             // The list of GSS\-TSIG capable servers
             \(dqservers\(dq: [
                 {
                     // First server (identification is required)
                     \(dqid\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq,
                     \(dqdomain\-names\(dq: [ ], // if not specified or empty, will
                                          // match all domains that want to
                                          // use this IP+port pair
                     \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.1\(dq,
                     \(dqport\(dq: 53,
                     \(dqserver\-principal\(dq: \(dqDNS/server1.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG\(dq,
                     \(dqclient\-principal\(dq: \(dqDHCP/admin1.example.org@EXAMPLE.ORG\(dq,
                     \(dqgss\-replay\-flag\(dq: false, // no GSS anti replay service
                     \(dqgss\-sequence\-flag\(dq: false, // no GSS sequence service
                     \(dqtkey\-lifetime\(dq: 7200, // 2 hours
                     \(dqrekey\-interval\(dq: 5400, // 90 minutes
                     \(dqretry\-interval\(dq: 240, // 4 minutes
                     \(dqtkey\-protocol\(dq: \(dqTCP\(dq,
                     \(dqfallback\(dq: true // if no key is available fallback to the
                                      // standard behavior (vs skip this server)
                 },
                 {
                     // The second server (it has most of the parameters missing
                     // as those are using the defaults specified above)
                     \(dqid\(dq: \(dqserver2\(dq,
                     \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.0.2.2\(dq,
                     \(dqport\(dq: 5300
                 }
             ]
         }
     }
     ]

     // Additional parameters, such as logging, control socket and
     // others omitted for clarity.
 }

 }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This configuration file contains a number of extra elements.
.sp
First, a list of forward and/or reverse domains with related DNS servers
identified by their IP+port pairs is defined. If the port is not
specified, the default of 53 is assumed. This is similar to basic mode, with no
authentication done using TSIG keys, with the
exception that static TSIG keys are not referenced by name.
.sp
Second, \fBlibddns_gss_tsig.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libddns_gss_tsig\:.so> must be specified on the
\fBhooks\-libraries\fP list. This hook takes many parameters. The most important
one is \fBservers\fP, which is a list of GSS\-TSIG\-capable servers. If there are
several servers and they share some characteristics, the values can be specified
in the \fBparameters\fP scope as defaults. In the example above, the defaults that apply
to all servers, unless otherwise specified on a per\-server scope, are defined in
lines 63 through 68. The defaults can be skipped if there is only one server
defined, or if all servers have different values.
.SS List of available parameters
.TS
box center;
l|l|l|l|l.
T{
Name
T}	T{
Scope
T}	T{
Type
T}	T{
Default value
T}	T{
Description
T}
_
T{
client\-keytab
T}	T{
global /
server
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
empty
T}	T{
the Kerberos \fBclient\fP key
table
T}
_
T{
credentials\-cache
T}	T{
global /
server
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
empty
T}	T{
the Kerberos credentials cache
T}
_
T{
server\-principal
T}	T{
global /
server
T}	T{
string
T}	T{
empty
T}	T{
