.TE
.sp
The global parameters are described below:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-keytab\fP specifies the Kerberos \fBclient\fP key table.
For instance, \fBFILE:<filename>\fP can be used to point to a specific file.
This parameter can be specified only once, in the parameters scope,
and is the equivalent of setting the \fBKRB5_CLIENT_KTNAME\fP environment
variable. An empty value is silently ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcredentials\-cache\fP specifies the Kerberos credentials cache.
For instance, \fBFILE:<filename>\fP can be used to point to a file or,
if using a directory which supports more than one principal,
\fBDIR:<directory\-path>\fP\&.
This parameter can be specified only once, in the parameters scope,
and is the equivalent of setting the \fBKRB5CCNAME\fP environment
variable. An empty value is silently ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserver\-principal\fP is the Kerberos principal name of the DNS
server that receives updates. In other words, this is the
DNS server\(aqs name in the Kerberos system. This parameter is
mandatory, and uses the typical Kerberos notation:
\fB<SERVICE\-NAME>/<server\-domain\-name>@<REALM>\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-principal\fP is the Kerberos principal name of the Kea D2
service. It is optional, and uses the typical Kerberos notation:
\fB<SERVICE\-NAME>/<server\-domain\-name>@<REALM>\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgss\-replay\-flag\fP determines if the GSS anti replay service is
required. It is by default but this can be disabled.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgss\-sequence\-flag\fP determines if the GSS sequence service is
required. It is not by default but is required by the standard
so it can be enabled.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-protocol\fP determines which protocol is used to establish the
security context with the DNS servers. Currently, the only supported
values are TCP (the default) and UDP.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-lifetime\fP determines the lifetime of GSS\-TSIG keys in the
TKEY protocol. The value must be greater than the \fBrekey\-interval\fP
value. It is expressed in seconds and defaults to 3600 (one hour).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrekey\-interval\fP governs the time interval at which the keys for each configured
server are checked for rekeying, i.e. when a new key is created to replace the
current usable one if its age is greater than the \fBrekey\-interval\fP value.
The value must be smaller than the \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP value (it is recommended
to be set between 50% and 80% of the \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP value). It is expressed in
seconds and defaults to 2700 (45 minutes, or 75% of one hour).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBretry\-interval\fP governs the time interval at which to retry to create a key if any
error occurred previously for any configured server. The value must be smaller
than the \fBrekey\-interval\fP value, and should be at most 1/3 of the difference
between \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP and \fBrekey\-interval\fP\&. It is expressed in seconds
and defaults to 120 (2 minutes).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfallback\fP governs the behavior when GSS\-TSIG should be used (a
matching DNS server is configured) but no GSS\-TSIG key is available.
If set to \fBfalse\fP (the default), this server is skipped; if
set to \fBtrue\fP, the DNS server is ignored and the DNS update
is sent with the configured DHCP\-DDNS protection (e.g. TSIG key), or
without any protection when none was configured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexchange\-timeout\fP governs the amount of time to wait for the GSS\-TSIG TKEY
exchange to finish before the process times out. It is expressed in milliseconds and
defaults to 3000 (3 seconds).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuser\-context\fP is an optional parameter (see Comments and User Context \%<#\:user-context>
for a general description of user contexts in Kea).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcomment\fP is allowed but currently ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBservers\fP specifies the list of DNS servers where GSS\-TSIG is enabled.
.UNINDENT
.sp
The server map parameters are described below:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBid\fP assigns an identifier to a DNS server. It is used for statistics
and commands. It is required, and must be both not empty and unique.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdomain\-names\fP governs the many\-to\-one relationship between D2 DNS
servers and GSS\-TSIG DNS servers: for each domain name on this list,
Kea looks for a D2 DNS server for this domain with the specified IP address
and port. An empty list (the default) means that all domains
match.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBip\-address\fP specifies the IP address at which the GSS\-TSIG DNS server
listens for DDNS and TKEY requests. It is a mandatory parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBport\fP specifies the DNS transport port on which the GSS\-TSIG DNS server
listens for DDNS and TKEY requests. It defaults to 53.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBserver\-principal\fP is the Kerberos principal name of the DNS server
that receives updates. The \fBserver\-principal\fP parameter set at the per\-server
level takes precedence over one set at the global level. It is a mandatory parameter which must be specified at
either the global or the server level.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-principal\fP is the Kerberos principal name of the Kea D2
service for this DNS server. The \fBclient\-principal\fP parameter set at the per\-server
level takes precedence over one set at the global level. It is an optional parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgss\-replay\-flag\fP determines if the GSS anti replay service is
required. The \fBgss\-replay\-flag\fP parameter set at the per\-server
level takes precedence over one set at the global level. It is an optional parameter
which defaults to true.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgss\-sequence\-flag\fP determines if the GSS sequence service is
required. The \fBgss\-sequence\-flag\fP parameter set at the per\-server
level takes precedence over one set at the global level. It is an optional parameter
which defaults to false.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-protocol\fP determines which protocol is used to establish the
security context with the DNS server. The \fBtkey\-protocol\fP parameter set at the per\-server
level takes precedence over one set at the global level. The default and supported values
for the per\-server level parameter are the same as
for the global\-level parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-lifetime\fP determines the lifetime of GSS\-TSIG keys in the
TKEY protocol for the DNS server. The \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP parameter set at the per\-server
level takes precedence over one set at the global level. The default and supported values
for the per\-server level parameter are the same as
for the global\-level parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrekey\-interval\fP governs the time interval at which the keys for this particular
server are checked for rekeying, i.e. when a new key is created to replace the
current usable one if its age is greater than the \fBrekey\-interval\fP value.
The value must be smaller than the \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP value (it is recommended
to be set between 50% and 80% of the \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP value). The \fBrekey\-interval\fP
parameter set at the per\-server level takes precedence over one set at the global
level. The default and supported values for the per\-server level parameter are the same as
for the global\-level parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBretry\-interval\fP governs the time interval at which to retry to create a key if any
error occurred previously for this particular server. The value must be
smaller than the \fBrekey\-interval\fP value, and should be at most 1/3 of the
difference between \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP and \fBrekey\-interval\fP\&. The
\fBretry\-interval\fP parameter set at the per\-server level takes precedence over one set at the global
level. The default and supported values for the per\-server level parameter are the same as
for the global\-level parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfallback\fP governs the behavior when GSS\-TSIG should be used (a
matching DNS server is configured) but no GSS\-TSIG key is available.
The \fBfallback\fP parameter set at the per\-server level takes precedence over one set at the global
level. The default and supported values for the per\-server level parameter are the same as
for the global\-level parameter..
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexchange\-timeout\fP governs the amount of time to wait for the GSS\-TSIG TKEY
exchange to finish before the process times out. The \fBexchange\-timeout\fP parameter
set at the per\-server level takes precedence over one set at the global
level. The default and supported values for the per\-server level parameter are the same as
for the global\-level parameter.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBignore\-bad\-direction\fP governs a workaround for Microsoft server bug.
When set explicitly to true sent DNS update responses are accepted as
verified even when prerequisites fail with the request signature.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuser\-context\fP is an optional parameter (see Comments and User Context \%<#\:user-context>
for a general description of user contexts in Kea).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcomment\fP is allowed but currently ignored.
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Generally it is not recommended to specify both the client keytab (\fBclient\-keytab\fP)
and the credentials cache (\fBcredentials\-cache\fP), although this may
differ between Kerberos implementations. The client keytab is just for
the client key and is typically used to specify the key explicitly in more
static manner, while the credentials cache can be used to store multiple
credentials and can be dynamically updated by the Kerberos library. As such,
the credentials\-cache is more flexible and thus the recommended alternative.
.sp
Also note that only the read access right is needed to use the cache.
Fetching credentials and updating the cache requires the write access
right.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS GSS\-TSIG Automatic Key Removal
.sp
The server periodically deletes keys after they have been expired more than three times the
length of the maximum key lifetime (the \fBtkey\-lifetime\fP parameter).
The user has the option to purge keys on demand by using the \fBgss\-tsig\-purge\-all\fP
command or the \fBgss\-tsig\-purge\fP command.
.SS GSS\-TSIG Configuration for Deployment
.sp
When using Kerberos 5 and BIND 9 as described in GSS\-TSIG Deployment,
the local resolver must point to the BIND 9 \fBnamed\fP server address. The
local Kerberos must also be configured by putting the following text into the \fBkrb5.conf\fP file:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[libdefaults]
    default_realm = EXAMPLE.ORG
    kdc_timesync = 1
    ccache_type = 4
    forwardable = true
    proxiable = true
[realms]
    EXAMPLE.ORG = {
            kdc = kdc.example.org
            admin_server = kdc.example.org
    }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
With Windows AD, the DNS service is provided by AD, which also provides
the Kerberos service. The required text in the \fBkrb5.conf\fP file becomes:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[libdefaults]
    default_realm = <REALM>
    kdc_timesync = 1
    ccache_type = 4
    forwardable = true
    proxiable = true
[realms]
    ${REALM} = {
            kdc = <AD_IP_ADDR>
            admin_server = <AD_IP_ADDR>
    }
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Even when the GSS\-API library can use the secret from the client key
table, it is far better for performance to get and cache credentials.
.sp
This can be done manually via the command:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kinit \-k \-t /usr/local/share/kea/dhcp.keytab DHCP/admin.example.org
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
or, when using AD:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
kinit \-k \-t /usr/local/share/kea/dhcp.keytab DHCP/kea.<domain>
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The credential cache can be displayed using \fBklist\fP\&.
.sp
In production, it is better to rely on a Kerberos Credential Manager as
the System Security Services Daemon (\fBsssd\fP).
.sp
When using BIND 9, the server principal is in the form \(dq\%<DNS/\:server\:.example\:.org@\:EXAMPLE>\&.ORG¨;
with AD, the format is \(dqDNS/<server>.<domain>@<REALM>\(dq.
.SS GSS\-TSIG Statistics
.sp
The GSS\-TSIG hook library introduces new statistics at global and
per\-DNS\-server levels:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgss\-tsig\-key\-created\fP \- the number of created GSS\-TSIG keys
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-sent\fP \- the number of sent TKEY exchange initial requests
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-success\fP \- the number of TKEY exchanges which completed with a success
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-timeout\fP \- the number of TKEY exchanges which completed on timeout
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtkey\-error\fP \- the number of TKEY exchanges which completed with an error other than
a timeout
.UNINDENT
.sp
The relationship between keys and DNS servers is very different between
the D2 code and static TSIG keys, and GSS\-TSIG keys and DNS servers:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
a static TSIG key can be shared between many DNS servers;
.IP \(bu 2
a GSS\-TSIG key is only used by one DNS server inside a dedicated
set of keys.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS GSS\-TSIG Commands
.sp
The GSS\-TSIG hook library supports some commands, which are described below.
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-get\-all\fP Command
.sp
This command lists all the GSS\-TSIG servers and keys.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-get\-all\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response returning one GSS\-TSIG server and one key:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 GSS\-TSIG servers and 1 keys\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqgss\-tsig\-servers\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.1.2.3\(dq,
                \(dqport\(dq: 53,
                \(dqserver\-principal\(dq: \(dqDNS/foo.com@FOO.COM\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\-suffix\(dq: \(dqfoo.com.\(dq,
                \(dqtkey\-lifetime\(dq: 3600,
                \(dqtkey\-protocol\(dq: \(dqTCP\(dq,
                \(dqkeys\(dq: [
                    {
                        \(dqname\(dq: \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq,
                        \(dqinception\-date\(dq: \(dq2021\-09\-05 12:23:36.281176\(dq,
                        \(dqserver\-id\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                        \(dqexpire\-date\(dq: \(dq2021\-09\-05 13:23:36.281176\(dq,
                        \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqnot yet ready\(dq,
                        \(dqtkey\-exchange\(dq: true
                    }
                ]
            },
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: \(dqbar\(dq,
                \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.1.2.4\(dq,
                \(dqport\(dq: 53,
                \(dqserver\-principal\(dq: \(dqDNS/bar.com@FOO.COM\(dq,
                \(dqkey\-name\-suffix\(dq: \(dqbar.com.\(dq,
                \(dqtkey\-lifetime\(dq: 7200,
                \(dqtkey\-protocol\(dq: \(dqUDP\(dq,
                \(dqkeys\(dq: [ ]
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-get\fP Command
.sp
This command retrieves information about the specified GSS\-TSIG server.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-id\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response returning information about the server \(dqfoo\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqGSS\-TSIG server[foo] found\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqid\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: \(dq192.1.2.3\(dq,
        \(dqport\(dq: 53,
        \(dqserver\-principal\(dq: \(dqDNS/foo.com@FOO.COM\(dq,
        \(dqkey\-name\-suffix\(dq: \(dqfoo.com.\(dq,
        \(dqtkey\-lifetime\(dq: 3600,
        \(dqtkey\-protocol\(dq: \(dqTCP\(dq,
        \(dqkeys\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq,
                \(dqserver\-id\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
                \(dqinception\-date\(dq: \(dq2021\-09\-05 12:23:36.281176\(dq,
                \(dqexpire\-date\(dq: \(dq2021\-09\-05 13:23:36.281176\(dq,
                \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqnot yet ready\(dq,
                \(dqtkey\-exchange\(dq: true
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-list\fP Command
.sp
This command generates a list of GSS\-TSIG server IDs and key names.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-list\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response returning two GSS\-TSIG servers and three keys:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 GSS\-TSIG servers and 3 keys\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqgss\-tsig\-servers\(dq: [
            \(dqfoo\(dq,
            \(dqbar\(dq
        ],
        \(dqgss\-tsig\-keys\(dq: [
            \(dq1234.example.com.\(dq,
            \(dq5678.example.com.\(dq,
            \(dq43888.example.org.\(dq
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-key\-get\fP Command
.sp
This command retrieves information about the specified GSS\-TSIG key.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-key\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response returning information about GSS\-TSIG key \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqGSS\-TSIG key \(aq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(aq found\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqname\(dq: \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq,
        \(dqserver\-id\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq,
        \(dqinception\-date\(dq: \(dq2021\-09\-05 12:23:36.281176\(dq,
        \(dqexpire\-date\(dq: \(dq2021\-09\-05 13:23:36.281176\(dq,
        \(dqstatus\(dq: \(dqnot yet ready\(dq,
        \(dqtkey\-exchange\(dq: true
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-key\-expire\fP Command
.sp
This command expires the specified GSS\-TSIG key.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-key\-expire\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response indicating that GSS\-TSIG key \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq has been expired:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqGSS\-TSIG key \(aq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(aq expired\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-key\-del\fP Command
.sp
This command deletes the specified GSS\-TSIG key.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-key\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqkey\-name\(dq: \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response indicating that GSS\-TSIG key \(dq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(dq has been deleted:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqGSS\-TSIG key \(aq1234.sig\-foo.com.\(aq deleted\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-purge\-all\fP Command
.sp
This command removes all unusable GSS\-TSIG keys.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-purge\-all\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response indicating that two GSS\-TSIG keys have been purged:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 purged GSS\-TSIG keys\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-purge\fP Command
.sp
This command removes unusable GSS\-TSIG keys for the specified server.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-purge\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-id\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response indicating that two GSS\-TSIG keys for server \(dqfoo\(dq have been purged:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 purged keys for GSS\-TSIG server[foo]\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-rekey\-all\fP Command
.sp
This command unconditionally creates new GSS\-TSIG keys (rekeys) for
all DNS servers.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-rekey\-all\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response indicating that a rekey was performed:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqrekeyed\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command is useful when, for instance, the DHCP\-DDNS server is
reconnected to the network.
.SS The \fBgss\-tsig\-rekey\fP Command
.sp
This command unconditionally creates new GSS\-TSIG keys (rekeys) for
a specified DNS server.
.sp
An example command invocation looks like this:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqgss\-tsig\-rekey\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-id\(dq: \(dqfoo\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Here is an example of a response indicating that a rekey was performed:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqGSS\-TSIG server[foo] rekeyed\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command is typically used when a DNS server has been rebooted, so
that existing GSS\-TSIG keys shared with this server can no longer be used.
.SS RADIUS
.SS Security Warning
.sp
RADIUS/UDP (and RADIUS/TCP) security is based on direct use of MD5
with the shared secret and the access user password. These security
mechanisms were known to be weak but things changed with the publication
of the Blast\-RADIUS vulnerability \%<https://\:www\:.blastradius\:.fail>
(CVE\-2024\-3596 \%<https://\:www\:.cve\:.org/\:CVERecord?\:id=CVE-2024-3596>).
.sp
To summarize, when the infrastructure between the RADIUS client
(here the Kea DHCP server) and the RADIUS server is not protected,
a man\-in\-the\-middle attacker can forge a valid accept message in
response to a failed access / authentication request.
.sp
Some RADIUS servers including the popular FreeRADIUS server already
refuse by default to serve requests which are considered insecure because they
are not protected using the Message\-Authenticator attribute (based
on HMAC\-MD5 so not vulnerable and supported by Kea 3.1.5) so even when
the infrastructure is protected, the RADIUS deployment is impacted by
Blast\-RADIUS.
.sp
The solutions provided by Kea release 3.1.6 are:
.sp
1. To support RADIUS/TLS which provides a built\-in cryptographic
protection of communication between RADIUS clients and servers.
This is known as the long\-term solution.
.sp
2. To support the short\-term solution by adding a Message\-Authenticator
attribute in all sent messages and requiring it in all received messages
(note the second point is enforced only by recent RADIUS servers).
.sp
These solutions are experimental.
.SS RADIUS Overview
.sp
This hook library allows Kea to interact with two types of RADIUS
services: access and accounting. The most common DHCP and RADIUS
integration is done on the DHCP relay\-agent level: DHCP clients send
DHCP packets to DHCP relays; those relays contact the RADIUS server and
either send the packet to the DHCP server or drop it, depending on the
response. It does require DHCP relay hardware to support RADIUS
communication, though, and even if the relay has the necessary support, it is
often not flexible enough to send and receive additional RADIUS
attributes. There is a more appealing alternative: to extend the
DHCP server to talk to RADIUS directly. That is the goal of this library.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
This library can only be loaded by the \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or the
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> process.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The major feature of this hook library is the ability to use RADIUS
authorization. When a DHCP packet is received, the Kea server sends an
Access\-Request to the RADIUS server and waits for a response. The server
then sends back either an Access\-Accept with specific client attributes,
or an Access\-Reject. There are two cases supported here: first, the
Access\-Accept includes a Framed\-IP\-Address attribute (for DHCPv4) or a
Framed\-IPv6\-Address attribute/Delegated\-IPv6\-Prefix (for DHCPv6),
which are interpreted by Kea as instructions to assign the specified IPv4
or IPv6 address/IPv6 prefix. This effectively means RADIUS can act
as an address\-reservation database.
.sp
The second supported case is the ability to assign clients to specific
pools based on a RADIUS response. In this case, the RADIUS server sends
back an Access\-Accept with a Framed\-Pool attribute.
For both DHCPv4 and DHCPv6, Kea interprets this attribute as a client class.
With the addition of the ability to limit access to pools to
specific classes (see Configuring Pools With Class Information \%<#\:classification-pools>), RADIUS can be
used to force the client to be assigned a dynamic address or prefix from a
specific pool. Furthermore, the same mechanism can be used to control
what kind of options the client gets if there are DHCP options
specified for a particular class.
.SS RADIUS Hook Library Configuration
.sp
The RADIUS hook is a library that must be loaded by either \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> or
\fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6> servers. Unlike some other available hook libraries, this one
takes many parameters. For example, this configuration can be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqDhcp4\(dq: {

    // Your regular DHCPv4 configuration parameters go here.

    \(dqhooks\-libraries\(dq: [
      {
        // Note that the RADIUS access service requires host\-cache for
        // proper operation, so this library is loaded as well.
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_host_cache.so\(dq
      },
      {
        \(dqlibrary\(dq: \(dqlibdhcp_radius.so\(dq,
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {

          // Specify where the dictionary is located.
          \(dqdictionary\(dq: \(dq/etc/kea/radius/dictionary\(dq,

          // Specify which address to use to communicate with RADIUS servers
          \(dqbindaddr\(dq: \(dq*\(dq

          // More RADIUS parameters go here.
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
RADIUS is a complicated environment. As such, it is not feasible
to provide a default configuration that works for everyone.
However, we do have an example that showcases some of the more common
features; please see kea4/hooks\-radius.json \%<#\:config-example-kea4-hooks-radius-json>\&.
.sp
The RADIUS hook library supports the following global configuration
flags:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbindaddr\fP (default \fB\(dq*\(dq\fP) \- specifies the address to be used by the
hook library in communication with the RADIUS servers. The \fB\(dq*\(dq\fP special
value tells the kernel to choose the address at hook library load time.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcanonical\-mac\-address\fP (default \fBfalse\fP) \- specifies whether MAC
addresses in attributes follow the canonical RADIUS format (lowercase
pairs of hexadecimal digits separated by \fB\-\fP).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-id\-pop0\fP (default \fBfalse\fP) \- is used with
\fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_flex_id\:.so>; it removes the leading zero (or pair of zeroes
in DHCPv6) type in the client ID (DUID in DHCPv6). See
\fBclient\-id\-printable\fP for any value implications when used in conjunction
with it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBclient\-id\-printable\fP (default \fBfalse\fP) \- checks whether the
\fBclient\-id\fP/\fBduid\fP content is printable and uses it as is instead of in
hexadecimal. It implies that \fBclient\-id\-pop0\fP and \fBextract\-duid\fP as 0 and 255 are
not printable.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdeadtime\fP (default \fB0\fP) \- is a mechanism that helps in sorting the
servers so those that have proved responsive so far are contacted
first, and the servers that have proved unresponsive are left to try later. The
deadtime value specifies the number of seconds after which a server is
considered unresponsive. 0 disables the mechanism.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdictionary\fP (default \fB\(dq/etc/kea/radius/dictionary\(dq\fP) \- is the
attribute and value dictionary; note that it is a critical parameter.
A dictionary is provided by Kea and is set by default.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBextract\-duid\fP (default \fBtrue\fP) \- extracts the embedded DUID from an
RFC 4361\-compliant DHCPv4 client ID. See \fBclient\-id\-printable\fP for any
value implications when used in conjunction with it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBidentifier\-type4\fP (default \fB\(dqclient\-id\(dq\fP) \- specifies the identifier
type to build the User\-Name attribute for DHCPv4; it should be the same as the
host identifier. When \fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_flex_id\:.so> is used, then
\fBreplace\-client\-id\fP must be set to \fBtrue\fP and \fBclient\-id\fP must be used
with \fBclient\-id\-pop0\fP enabled.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBidentifier\-type6\fP (default \fB\(dqduid\(dq\fP) \- specifies the identifier type to
build the User\-Name attribute for DHCPv6; it should be the same as the host
identifier. When \fBlibdhcp_flex_id.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_flex_id\:.so> is used, then
\fBreplace\-client\-id\fP must be set to \fBtrue\fP and \fBduid\fP must be used with
\fBclient\-id\-pop0\fP enabled.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnas\-ports\fP (default \fB[]\fP), specifies the NAS port to use in place of
a subnet ID (default behavior). It is an array of maps, each map having two
elements at most: the mandatory NAS port value, and optionally, a selector
consisting of either a subnet ID, a subnet prefix, or a shared\-network name.
If the selector is applied to the packet, the NAS port is used instead of the
subnet ID. When the subnet ID is 0 or missing, the specified NAS port acts as
a default. The substitution happens for all packets that did not match a
selector.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprotocol\fP (default \fBUDP\fP) \- since Kea 3.1.6 specifies the transport
protocol. Accepted values are \fBUDP\fP and \fBTLS\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreselect\-subnet\-address\fP (default \fBfalse\fP) \- enables subnet reselection
according to the value of the Framed\-IP\-Address or, respectively,
the Framed\-IPv6\-Address attribute from the RADIUS access response. With this
flag enabled, if the IP address is not in the range of the currently selected
subnet, but is in the range of another subnet that is selectable with regards to
other criteria, the latter subnet is selected and used further in the lease
assignment process.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBreselect\-subnet\-pool\fP (default \fBfalse\fP) \- enables subnet reselection
according to the value of the Framed\-Pool attribute from the RADIUS access
response. With this flag enabled, if the currently selected subnet is not
guarded by the client class represented by the attribute value, but there is
another selectable subnet that is guarded by it, the latter subnet is
selected and used further in the lease assignment process.
This reselection is attempted first, and if successful, it prevents the
function of \fBreselect\-subnet\-address\fP from coming into effect.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBretries\fP (default \fB3\fP) \- is the number of retries before trying the
next server.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsession\-history\fP (default \fB\(dq\(dq\fP) \- is the name of the file providing
persistent storage for accounting session history.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBthread\-pool\-size\fP (default \fB0\fP) indicates the number of threads that
are used for sending RADIUS requests and processing RADIUS responses for both
access and accounting services before passing them to the core thread pool. A
value of \fB0\fP instructs the RADIUS hook library to use the same number of
threads used for core DHCP processing. This value is only relevant if the Kea
core is configured as multi\-threaded; a single\-threaded Kea core results in
single\-threaded RADIUS processing.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtimeout\fP (default \fB10\fP) \- is the number of seconds during which a
response is awaited.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuse\-message\-authenticator\fP (default \fBtrue\fP when the protocol is not
\fBTLS\fP) \- add a Message\-Authenticator attribute in all sent messages and
require it in all received messages. This can be used as a short\-term
mitigation against the BLAST vulnerability but works only with recent
versions of RADIUS servers which can be configured to put the attribute
in all responses.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Two services are supported:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaccess\fP \- the authorization service.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBaccounting\fP \- the accounting service.
.UNINDENT
.sp
At the service level, three sections can be configured:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Servers that define RADIUS services that the library is expected to
contact. Each server may have the following items specified:
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP \- specifies the IP address of the server. A domain name may be
used and will be resolved at hook library load time.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBport\fP \- specifies the UDP port of the server. By default, it is 1812
for access and 1813 for accounting.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsecret\fP \- authenticates messages.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtrust\-anchor\fP \- Since Kea 3.1.6 specifies the name of a file or
directory where the certification authority certificate can be found.
Required with the TLS protocol, ignored otherwise.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcert\-file\fP \- Since Kea 3.1.6 specifies the name of a file
containing the end\-entity certificate to use. Required with the TLS
protocol, ignored otherwise.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBkey\-file\fP \- Since Kea 3.1.6 specifies the private key of the
end\-entity certificate to use. Required with the TLS protocol,
ignored otherwise.
.UNINDENT
.sp
When no server is specified, the service is disabled.
.IP \(bu 2
Attributes which define additional information that the Kea server
sends to a RADIUS server. The parameter must be identified either
by a name or type. Its value can be specified in one of three
possible ways: \fBdata\fP (which defines a plain text value), \fBraw\fP (which
defines the value in hex), or \fBexpr\fP (which defines an expression
that is evaluated for each incoming packet independently).
.INDENT 2.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBname\fP \- is the name of the attribute.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtype\fP \- is the type of the attribute. Either the type or the name must be
provided, and the attribute must be defined in the dictionary.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdata\fP \- is the first of three ways to specify the attribute content.
It specifies the textual representation of the attribute content.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBraw\fP \- is the second of three ways to specify the attribute content.
It specifies the content in hexadecimal.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBexpr\fP \- is the last of the three ways to specify the attribute content.
It specifies an evaluation expression on the DHCP query packet.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvendor\fP \-  since Kea 3.1.2 is the vendor id of the attribute.
It always contains a string with the vendor name or an integer literal.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 2.0
.INDENT 3.5
Attributes are supported only for the access service.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Vendor\-Specific attribute can be specified in two ways: using a \fBraw\fP
value which must include the vendor and the vsa data, note that the \fBdata\fP
value is no longer supported since Kea 3.1.2, and the \fBexpr\fP value
is evaluated to the content of the attribute. The second way was added
by 3.1.2 and allows to specify a vendor attribute which is automatically
embedded into a Vendor\-Specific attribute.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBpeer\-updates\fP boolean flag (default \fBtrue\fP) controls whether lease
updates coming from an active High\-Availability (HA) partner should result in
an accounting request. This may be desirable to remove duplicates if HA
partners are configured to send requests to the same RADIUS server. The flag is
only supported by the accounting service. The lease synchronization process at
the startup of an HA node does not trigger a RADIUS accounting request,
regardless of the value of this flag.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBmax\-pending\-requests\fP positive integer (default \fB0\fP) limits the
number of pending RADIUS requests. It is supported only by the access service.
The value \fB0\fP means no limit; \fB64\fP is a recommended setting.
.IP \(bu 2
The \fBidle\-timer\-interval\fP positive integer (default \fB0\fP) specifies
the interval between the last valid response from servers and the
send of a \fBStatus\-Server\fP message. The value \fB0\fP means to disable
this mechanism added in Kea version 3.1.5.
.IP \(bu 2
Since Kea 3.1.6 with the TLS protocol, the \fBenabled\fP boolean flag
(default \fBtrue\fP) allows to enable or disable the \fBaccess\fP and
\fBaccounting\fP services.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For example, to specify a single access server available on localhost
that uses \fB\(dq1234\(dq\fP as a secret, and tell Kea to send three additional
attributes (\fBUser\-Password\fP, \fBConnect\-Info\fP, and \fBConfiguration\-Token\fP),
the following snippet could be used:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqparameters\(dq: {

    // Other RADIUS parameters here

    \(dqaccess\(dq: {

      // This starts the list of access servers.
      \(dqservers\(dq: [
        {
          // These are parameters for the first (and only) access server
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq,
          \(dqport\(dq: 1812,
          \(dqsecret\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq
        }
      // Additional access servers could be specified here.
      ],

      // This defines a list of additional attributes Kea will send to each
      // access server in Access\-Request.
      \(dqattributes\(dq: [
        {
          // This attribute is identified by name (must be present in the
          // dictionary) and has static value (i.e. the same value will be
          // sent to every server for every packet).
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dqUser\-Password\(dq,
          \(dqdata\(dq: \(dqmysecretpassword\(dq
        },
        {
          // It is also possible to specify an attribute using its type,
          // rather than a name. 77 is Connect\-Info. The value is specified
          // using hex. Again, this is a static value. It will be sent the
          // same for every packet and to every server.
          \(dqtype\(dq: 77,
          \(dqraw\(dq: \(dq65666a6a71\(dq
        },
        {
           // This example shows how an expression can be used to send dynamic value.
           // The expression (see Using Expressions in Classification \%<#\:classification-using-expressions>) may take any
           // value from the incoming packet or even its metadata e.g. the
           // interface it was received over from.
           \(dqname\(dq: \(dqConfiguration\-Token\(dq,
           \(dqexpr\(dq: \(dqhexstring(pkt4.mac,\(aq:\(aq)\(dq
        }
      ] // End of attributes
    }, // End of access

    // Accounting parameters.
    \(dqaccounting\(dq: {
      // This starts the list of accounting servers.
      \(dqservers\(dq: [
        {
          // These are parameters for the first (and only) accounting server
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq,
          \(dqport\(dq: 1813,
          \(dqsecret\(dq: \(dq1234\(dq
        }
        // Additional accounting servers could be specified here.
      ]
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Customization is sometimes required for certain attributes by devices belonging
to various vendors. This is a great way to leverage the expression evaluation
mechanism. For example, MAC addresses which might be used as a convenience
value for the \fBUser\-Password\fP attribute are most likely to appear in colon\-hexadecimal
notation (\fBde:ad:be:ef:ca:fe\fP), but they might need to be expressed in
hyphen\-hexadecimal notation (\fBde\-ad\-be\-ef\-ca\-fe\fP). Here\(aqs how to specify that:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
   \(dqparameters\(dq: {
      \(dqaccess\(dq: {
         \(dqattributes\(dq: [
            {
               \(dqname\(dq: \(dqUser\-Password\(dq,
               \(dqexpr\(dq: \(dqhexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq\-\(aq)\(dq
            }
         ]
      }
   }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
And here\(aqs how to specify period\-separated hexadecimal notation (\fBdead.beef.cafe\fP), preferred by Cisco devices:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
   \(dqparameters\(dq: {
      \(dqaccess\(dq: {
         \(dqattributes\(dq: [
            {
               \(dqname\(dq: \(dqUser\-Password\(dq,
               \(dqexpr\(dq: \(dqsubstring(hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq\(aq), 0, 4) + \(aq.\(aq + substring(hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq\(aq), 4, 4) + \(aq.\(aq + substring(hexstring(pkt4.mac, \(aq\(aq), 8, 4)\(dq
            }
         ]
      }
   }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
For the access service of \fBlibdhcp_radius.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_radius\:.so> to operate properly,
\fBlibdhcp_host_cache.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_host_cache\:.so> must also be loaded. The reason for this
is somewhat complex. In a typical deployment, DHCP clients send
their packets via DHCP relay, which inserts certain Relay Agent
Information options, such as \fBcircuit\-id\fP or \fBremote\-id\fP\&. The values of
those options are then used by the Kea DHCP server to formulate the
necessary attributes in the Access\-Request message sent to the RADIUS
server. However, once the DHCP client gets its address, it then renews
by sending packets directly to the DHCP server. The relays cannot
insert their RAI options at that point, and the DHCP server cannot send
the Access\-Request queries to the RADIUS server by using just the
information from incoming packets. Kea needs to keep the information
received during the initial Discover/Offer exchanges and use it again
later when sending accounting messages.
.sp
This mechanism is implemented based on user context in host
reservations. (See Comments and User Context \%<#\:user-context> and User Contexts in Hooks \%<#\:user-context-hooks> for
details.) The host\-cache mechanism allows the information retrieved by
RADIUS to be stored and used later instead of doing another authorization
round\-trip to the RADIUS server. In other words, the host\-cache mechanism
is mandatory for the access service. The Kea configuration is rejected if
\fBlibdhcp_host_cache.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_host_cache\:.so> is not loaded alongside \fBlibdhcp_radius.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_radius\:.so>\&.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Currently the RADIUS hook library is incompatible with the
\fBearly\-global\-reservations\-lookup\fP global parameter, i.e.
setting the parameter to \fBtrue\fP raises an error when the
hook library is loaded.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Currently the RADIUS hook library is incompatible with the
multi\-subnet shared networks that have host reservations other
than global. Loading the RADIUS hook library in a Kea DHCP server
that has this configuration raises an error.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Currently the \fBreselect\-subnet\-address\fP and \fBreselect\-subnet\-pool\fP
parameters are incompatible with the hub\-and\-spoke configuration of the
\fBlibdhcp_ha.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_ha\:.so>\&. This hook library uses an originally
selected subnet for choosing an HA relationship to process a packet.
The subnet reselection may interfere with this choice. See the
Hub and Spoke Configuration \%<#\:ha-hub-and-spoke> for details.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS RADIUS/TLS Configuration
.sp
Kea 3.1.6 introduces the support of RADIUS/TLS with some changes in
the configuration, mainly because a shared connection is used with TLS
(vs. different UDP ports).
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
the new \fBprotocol\fP parameter must be set to \fBTLS\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
a new service entry named \fBtls\fP must be used to define
\fBservers\fP and \fBidle\-timer\-interval\fP parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
these parameters are not longer allowed in \fBaccess\(ga and \(ga\(gaaccounting\fP
service entries.
.IP \(bu 2
as there is no \fBservers\fP entry, the \fBaccess\fP and \fBaccounting\fP
services can be disabled by setting the \fBenabled\fP flag to \fBfalse\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
server entries are required to set TLS \fBtrust\-anchor\fP, \fBcert\-file\fP
and \fBkey\-file\fP parameters.
.IP \(bu 2
default (and unique) server port is \fB2083\fP\&.
.IP \(bu 2
the \fBsecret\fP entry is no longer required and defaults to \fBradsec\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.sp
For example, the single access server configuration becomes with RADIUS/TLS:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqparameters\(dq: {

    // Set the protocol to TLS (default is UDP).
    \(dqprotocol\(dq: \(dqTLS\(dq,

    // Other RADIUS parameters here

    // Access parameters.
    \(dqaccess\(dq: {

      // This defines a list of additional attributes Kea will send to each
      // access server in Access\-Request.
      \(dqattributes\(dq: [
        {
          // This attribute is identified by name (must be present in the
          // dictionary) and has static value (i.e. the same value will be
          // sent to every server for every packet).
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dqUser\-Password\(dq,
          \(dqdata\(dq: \(dqmysecretpassword\(dq
        }
      ] // End of attributes
    }, // End of access

    // Accounting parameters.
    \(dqaccounting\(dq: {

      // Disabling this service.
      \(dqenabled\(dq: false
    }, // End of accounting

    // TLS parameters.
    \(dqtls\(dq: {

      // This starts the list of servers.
      \(dqservers\(dq: [
        {
          \(dqname\(dq: \(dq127.0.0.1\(dq,
          \(dqport\(dq: 2083,
          \(dqsecret\(dq: \(dqradsec\(dq,
          \(dqtrust\-anchor\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/CA.pem\(dq,
          \(dqcert\-file\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/server_cert.pem\(dq,
          \(dqkey\-file\(dq: \(dq/usr/lib/kea/server_key.pem\(dq
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Currently only the first configured server is used. The \fBretries\fP and
\fBdeadtime\fP parameters are silently ignored. From some limits in
the TCP client implementation, the local address can\(aqt be set (i.e.
setting the \fBbindaddr\fP parameter is ignored), and requests are
not pipelined i.e. a response is awaited before another request can be
sent over the same connection. When multi\-threading is enabled multiple
connections (up to the number of threads) can be opened in parallel.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS RADIUS Server Setup Example
.sp
The RADIUS hook library requires at least one RADIUS server to function. One
popular open source implementation is FreeRADIUS; here\(aqs how to
set it up to enable basic functionality in Kea.
.INDENT 0.0
.IP 1. 3
Install FreeRADIUS through the package manager or from the tarballs available
on [the freeradius.org download page](\%<https://\:freeradius\:.org/\:releases>).
.IP 2. 3
Establish the FreeRADIUS configuration directory. It\(aqs commonly
\fB/etc/freeradius\fP, but it may be \fB/etc/raddb\fP\&.
.IP 3. 3
Generate certificates. Go to \fB/etc/freeradius/certs\fP\&.
Run \fB\&./bootstrap\fP or \fBmake\fP\&.
Wait until finished. It should take a few seconds.
.IP 4. 3
Check that the server is able to start.
Running with the \fB\-X\fP flag is a good way to display potential errors.
Run \fBradiusd \-X\fP or \fBfreeradius \-X\fP, whichever is available.
It should display \fBReady to process requests\fP on the final line.
.IP 5. 3
If the Kea DHCP server and the RADIUS server are on different machines,
edit \fB/etc/freeradius/clients.conf\fP with the proper address under
\fBipadddr\fP\&. This file is also where the secret is set; it needs to match
the one set in the hook library\(aqs configuration.
.IP 6. 3
If RADIUS is used to authorize DHCP clients, each DHCP
client needs to have an entry in the authorize file, which can be commonly
found at:
.INDENT 3.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/etc/raddb/mods\-config/files/authorize\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/etc/freeradius/3.0/mods\-config/files/authorize\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/etc/freeradius/users\fP (for RADIUS 2.x series)
.UNINDENT
.sp
The passwords for entries must match the passwords
in the configuration of the RADIUS hook library under the
\fBUser\-Password\fP attribute. Each entry can have zero or more
attributes.
.sp
In the following example, there are six entries with the password set to the
client ID, which needs to be dynamically set in the hook library\(aqs
configuration. Here\(aqs how the entries might look:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
01:00:0c:01:02:03:04    Cleartext\-password := \(dq00:0c:01:02:03:04\(dq

01:00:0c:01:02:03:05    Cleartext\-password := \(dq00:0c:01:02:03:05\(dq
    Framed\-IP\-Address = \(dq192.0.2.5\(dq

01:00:0c:01:02:03:06    Cleartext\-password := \(dq00:0c:01:02:03:06\(dq
    Framed\-IP\-Address = \(dq192.0.2.6\(dq,
    Framed\-Pool = \(dqclassical\(dq

00:03:00:01:00:0c:01:02:03:07    Cleartext\-password := \(dq00:0c:01:02:03:07\(dq

00:03:00:01:00:0c:01:02:03:08    Cleartext\-password := \(dq00:0c:01:02:03:08\(dq
    Framed\-IPv6\-Address = \(dq2001:db8::8\(dq

00:03:00:01:00:0c:01:02:03:09    Cleartext\-password := \(dq00:0c:01:02:03:09\(dq
    Framed\-IPv6\-Address = \(dq2001:db8::9\(dq,
    Framed\-Pool = \(dqclassroom\(dq
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.IP 7. 3
Accounting does not need to be modified to work with Kea, but customizations are
possible in the accounting file, which can commonly be found at:
.INDENT 3.0
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/etc/radius\-config/mods\-config/files/accounting\fP
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/etc/freeradius/3.0/mods\-config/files/accounting\fP
.UNINDENT
.IP 8. 3
When RADIUS/TLS is used it is a good idea to bind the TLS credentials
with the client identity. This can be done using:
.INDENT 3.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
check_cert_cn = %{User\-Name}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
which matches the Common Name of the TLS client (i.e. Kea) certificate
subject with the RADIUS User\-Name.
.UNINDENT
.SS RADIUS Workflows for Lease Allocation
.sp
The following diagrams show a high\-level view of how RADIUS assists with the
lease allocation process in \fBkea\-dhcp4\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp4> and \fBkea\-dhcp6\fP \%<#\:std-iscman-kea-dhcp6>\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 2.5
[image]
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Somewhat tangential to lease allocation, and not shown in the diagrams above,
is the \fBcommand_processed\fP callout, which sends Accounting\-Request messages
when a lease command is received.
.SS Parked\-Packet Limit
.sp
Refer to Parked\-Packet Limit \%<#\:parked-packet-limit> for a basic introduction to packet parking.
.sp
The RADIUS hook library uses this mechanism. To allow for asynchronous
communication between Kea and the RADIUS server and concurrent processing of
DHCP packets by the Kea server, the DHCP request is parked, before the access
request is sent on the subnet select callout. When the access response becomes
available to the Kea DHCP server, the request is unparked, and the server
continues processing it.
.SS Differences Between RADIUS Hook Libraries Prior To Kea 2.4.0 and As Of 2.6.0
.sp
The RADIUS hook library in 2.4.0 and prior versions relied on the FreeRADIUS
client library to function. Starting with 2.6.0 and onward, the RADIUS hook
library is standalone, with its own RADIUS client implementation and its own
RADIUS dictionary. There are differences:
.TS
box center;
l|l|l.
T{
Feature
T}	T{
Old
T}	T{
New
T}
_
T{
Support for Attribute Data Types
T}	T{
string, ipaddr, ipv4prefix, integer, integer64, date, ifid, ipv6addr, ipv6prefix, tlv, abinary, byte, ether, short, signed, octets
T}	T{
string (can simulate any other unsupported data type too), ipaddr, integer, date (interpreted as integer), ipv6addr, ipv6prefix
T}
_
T{
Names of Standard Attributes
T}	T{
Taken from the FreeRADIUS dictionary.
T}	T{
Taken from the Kea RADIUS dictionary and the IANA registry. There is an aliasing mechanism built into the library that ensures backward compatibility, e.g. \fBPassword\fP translates to the standard name of the attribute \fBUser\-Password\fP\&.
T}
_
T{
Resolution of RADIUS Server Domain Names
T}	T{
At run time.
T}	T{
At hook library load time.
T}
_
T{
Automatic Deduction of Source Address for Reaching RADIUS Servers (configured with \fBbindaddr: \(dq*\(dq\fP)
T}	T{
At run time.
T}	T{
At hook library load time.
T}
_
T{
RADIUS Server Limit per Service
T}	T{
8
T}	T{
Unlimited
T}
_
T{
Support for Including Dictionaries Inside Dictionaries
T}	T{
Yes
T}	T{
since Kea 3.1.2
T}
_
T{
Support for Vendor Attributes
T}	T{
Yes
T}	T{
since Kea 3.1.2
T}
_
T{
Attribute Names and Attribute Values
T}	T{
Case\-insensitive
T}	T{
Case\-sensitive
T}
_
T{
Integer Values
T}	T{
Do not require an attribute definition.
T}	T{
Must have an associated attribute definition in the dictionary.
T}
_
T{
Attribute and Integer Value name spaces
T}	T{
flat name spaces allowing duplicates.
T}	T{
since Kea 3.1.2 different name spaces per vendor.
T}
_
T{
Reply\-Message Presence in the Kea Logs
T}	T{
Only as part of the aggregated list of attributes in \fBRADIUS_AUTHENTICATION_ACCEPTED\fP, \fBRADIUS_ACCESS_CACHE_INSERT\fP, \fBRADIUS_ACCESS_CACHE_GET\fP log messages.
T}	T{
Also has a dedicated \fBRADIUS_REPLY_MESSAGE_ATTRIBUTE\fP message per each Reply\-Message attribute logged after a valid RADIUS reply is received.
T}
_
T{
Behavior of Multiple Attributes of the Same Type (except Reply\-Message)
T}	T{
Experimentally, only the \fBfirst\fP attribute on the wire from an Access\-Accept message is considered.
T}	T{
Experimentally, only the \fBlast\fP attribute on the wire from an Access\-Accept message is considered.
T}
.TE
.SS Supported RADIUS Standards
.sp
The Remote Authentication Dial In User Service (RADIUS) protocol is specified
in RFC 2865 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2865>\&.
.sp
The RADIUS Accounting is specified in
RFC 2866 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc2866>\&.
.sp
The Message\-Authenticator attribute is specified in the EAP extension in
RFC 3579 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc3579>\&.
.sp
The Status\-Server message is specified in
RFC 5997 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc5997>\&.
.sp
The TCP transport (not supported by heavily referenced for TLS) is specified
in RFC 6613 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc6613>\&.
.sp
The TLS transport (as known as RADIUS/TLS or \fBradsec\fP) is specified in
RFC 6614 \%<https://\:datatracker\:.ietf\:.org/\:doc/\:html/\:rfc6614>\&.
.SH MONITORING KEA WITH STORK
.sp
Most administrators want to be able to monitor any Kea services that are running. Kea offers so many
pieces of information \- configuration files, API, statistics, logs, open database content, and more \-
that it may sometimes
be overwhelming to keep up. ISC\(aqs Stork project is intended to address this problem for both Kea
and BIND 9. Stork is useful in a variety of ways:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
Stork can be used as a dashboard. It provides insight into what exactly is happening
on the servers. In particular, it allows users to: see up\-to\-date details regarding pool
utilization in subnets and shared networks; monitor the state of the HA pair (and
provide extra insight in case of failover and recovery events); list, filter, and
search for specific host reservations; and more. Only
a single Stork server needs to be deployed, and one Stork agent on each machine to be monitored.
.IP \(bu 2
The Stork agent can integrate Kea with Prometheus and Grafana. Once the Stork
agent is active on the server, it serves as a Prometheus exporter. Users who have deployed
Prometheus in their networks can visualize statistics as time series using Grafana.
.IP \(bu 2
Stork can act as both a dashboard and an integrator for Prometheus/Grafana. Once Stork
is linked to where Grafana is deployed on the network, users can inspect the current status and
visit a customized link to Grafana to see how a given property behaves over time.
.UNINDENT
.sp
Stork is available as source code, but also as native deb and RPM packages, which makes it easy
to install on most popular systems. For more details, please see the
Stork ARM \%<https://\:stork\:.readthedocs\:.io> or the Stork project page \%<https://\:gitlab\:.isc\:.org/\:isc-projects/\:stork>\&.
The ARM has a nice collection of screenshots that is frequently updated, to give users
an idea of what is currently available. Stork is in the midst of full development with
monthly releases, so please check back frequently.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
Stork uses Kea API calls from the \fBlibdhcp_host_cmds.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_host_cmds\:.so> and
\fBlibdhcp_subnet_cmds.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_subnet_cmds\:.so> hooks to make changes to Kea\(aqs
configuration.  Such usage is incompatible with the Kea Configuration Backend \%<#\:config-backend>
(CB).  Stork does not currently implement support for the
\fBlibdhcp_cb_cmds.so\fP \%<#\:std-ischooklib-libdhcp_cb_cmds\:.so> needed to make changes to CB
configuration.  If using the Kea CB, Stork can only be safely used in a
\(dqread\-only\(dq fashion.  Support for the CB is planned for a future release of
Stork.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Kea Statistics in Grafana
.sp
The ISC Stork project provides an agent that can be deployed alongside Kea. It
exposes Kea statistics in a format that is accepted by Prometheus.
One of the major benefits of Prometheus is that it turns repeated one\-time observations into time series,
which lets users monitor how certain behaviors change over time. It is easy to use other tools
to visualize data available in Prometheus; the most common approach is to use
Grafana to provide visual dashboards. The Stork project provides dashboard
definitions for Kea that can be imported into Grafana very easily.
.sp
Learn more about Prometheus and Grafana on their websites: \fIPrometheus <https://prometheus.io>\fP
and \fIGrafana <https://grafana.com>\fP\&.
.SH API REFERENCE
.sp
Kea currently supports 219 commands in kea\-dhcp\-ddns, kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6 daemons and cb_cmds, class_cmds, gss_tsig, high_availability, host_cache, host_cmds, lease_cmds, lease_query, perfmon, stat_cmds, subnet_cmds hook libraries.
.sp
Commands supported by \fIkea\-dhcp\-ddns\fP daemon: build\-report, config\-get, config\-hash\-get, config\-reload, config\-set, config\-test, config\-write, gss\-tsig\-get, gss\-tsig\-get\-all, gss\-tsig\-key\-del, gss\-tsig\-key\-expire, gss\-tsig\-key\-get, gss\-tsig\-list, gss\-tsig\-purge, gss\-tsig\-purge\-all, gss\-tsig\-rekey, gss\-tsig\-rekey\-all, list\-commands, shutdown, statistic\-get, statistic\-get\-all, statistic\-reset, statistic\-reset\-all, status\-get, version\-get\&.
