.SS ha\-heartbeat
.sp
This command is sent internally by a Kea partner when operating in High Availability (HA) mode or by the system administrator to verify the state of the servers with regards to the High Availability. It retrieves the server\(aqs HA state and clock value.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.4.0 (high_availability hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see ha\-heartbeat command \%<#\:command-ha-heartbeat>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-heartbeat\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqserver\-name\(dq: \(dqserver1\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqHA peer status returned.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqstate\(dq: <server state>,
        \(dqdate\-time\(dq: <server notion of time>,
        \(dqscopes\(dq: [ <first scope>, <second scope>, ... ],
        \(dqunsent\-update\-count\(dq: <total number of lease allocations in partner\-down state>
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response includes a server state (see Server States \%<#\:ha-server-states>), current clock value, served scopes and the counter indicating how many leases the server has allocated without sending lease updates to its partner. The partner uses this counter to determine if it should synchronize its lease database.
.SS ha\-maintenance\-cancel
.sp
This command is sent to instruct a server in the partner\-in\-maintenance
state to transition back to the previous state, effectively canceling the
maintenance.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.7.4 (high_availability hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see ha\-maintenance\-cancel command \%<#\:command-ha-maintenance-cancel>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-maintenance\-cancel\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command takes no arguments.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: <integer>,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq<string>\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS ha\-maintenance\-notify
.sp
This command is sent by the server receiving the ha\-maintenance\-start
to its partner to cause the partner to transition to the in\-maintenance
state or to revert it from the in\-maintenance state as a result of
receiving the ha\-maintenance\-cancel command.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.7.4 (high_availability hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see ha\-maintenance\-notify command \%<#\:command-ha-maintenance-notify>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-maintenance\-notify\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcancel\(dq: true,
        \(dqstate\(dq: \(dqready\(dq
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a boolean argument which, if false, indicates
that the server should transition to the in\-maintenance state.
If the cancel argument is set to true it instructs the server to revert from
the in\-maintenance state to its previous state. The state argument informs
the recipient about the state of the sending server.
This command is not meant to be used by the administrator. It is
merely used for internal communication between the HA partners.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: <integer>,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq<string>\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The response may include a special error code of 1001
to indicate that the partner refused to enter the maintenance state.
.SS ha\-maintenance\-start
.sp
This command is sent to instruct one of the servers to transition
to the partner\-in\-maintenance state in which it will be responding to
all DHCP queries. The server receiving this command sends the
ha\-maintenance\-notify to its partner to cause the partner to
transition to the in\-maintenance state.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.7.4 (high_availability hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see ha\-maintenance\-start command \%<#\:command-ha-maintenance-start>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqha\-maintenance\-start\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command takes no arguments.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: <integer>,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq<string>\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
