.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
     \(dqenable\-monitoring\(dq: true,
     \(dqstats\-mgr\-reporting\(dq: false
  },
  \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
  \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-control success.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result 0 is returned if command succeeds along with the resultant values of both flags.
Result is 1 when parameters are malformed or missing.
.SS perfmon\-get\-all\-durations
.sp
This command fetches all monitored duration data currently held by Perfmon.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 2.7.0 (perfmon hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see perfmon\-get\-all\-durations command \%<#\:command-perfmon-get-all-durations>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
  \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-get\-all\-durations\(dq,
  \(dqarguments\(dq: {
    \(dqresult\-set\-format\(dq: true
  }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
If result\-set\-format is false, the output will be as shown below:
{
   \(dqarguments\(dq: {
       \(dqdurations\(dq: [{
           \(dqduration\-key\(dq: {
               \(dqquery\-type\(dq: \(dqDHCPDISCOVER\(dq,
               \(dqresponse\-type\(dq: \(dqDHCPOFFER\(dq,
               \(dqstart\-event\(dq: \(dqbuffer_read\(dq,
               \(dqstop\-event\(dq: \(dqmt_queued\(dq,
               \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: 0
           },
           \(dqmax\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 118,
           \(dqmean\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 47,
           \(dqmin\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 31,
           \(dqoccurrences\(dq: 501,
           \(dqstart\-time\(dq: \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:06.814884\(dq,
           \(dqtotal\-duration\-usecs\(dq: 23951
       },
       ...
       ],
       \(dqresult\-set\-format\(dq: false,
       \(dqinterval\-width\-secs\(dq: 5,
       \(dqtimestamp\(dq: \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:22.397233\(dq
   },
   \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
   \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-get\-all\-durations: n found\(dq
}
If result\-set\-format is true, the output will be as shown below:
{
   \(dqarguments\(dq: {
       \(dqdurations\-result\-set\(dq: {
           \(dqcolumns\(dq: [
               \(dqquery\-type\(dq,
               \(dqresponse\-type\(dq,
               \(dqstart\-event\(dq,
               \(dqend\-event\(dq,
               \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq,
               \(dqinterval\-start\(dq,
               \(dqoccurrences\(dq,
               \(dqmin\-duration\-usecs\(dq,
               \(dqmax\-duration\-usecs\(dq,
               \(dqtotal\-duration\-usecs\(dq,
               \(dqmean\-duration\-usecs\(dq
           ],
           \(dqrows\(dq: [
               [
                   \(dqDHCPDISCOVER\(dq,
                   \(dqDHCPOFFER\(dq,
                   \(dqbuffer_read\(dq,
                   \(dqmt_queued\(dq,
                   0,
                   \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:06.814884\(dq,
                   501,
                   31,
                   118,
                   23951,
                   47
               ],
               ...
           ]
       },
       \(dqresult\-set\-format\(dq: true,
       \(dqinterval\-width\-secs\(dq: 5,
       \(dqtimestamp\(dq: \(dq2024\-06\-12 17:52:22.397233\(dq
   },
   \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
   \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqperfmon\-get\-all\-durations: n found\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result 0 is returned if command succeeds along with monitored duration data.
Result is 1 if command is invalid or command processing encounters an error.
.SS remote\-class4\-del
.sp
This command deletes a DHCPv4 client class from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.9.10 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-class4\-del command \%<#\:command-remote-class4-del>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <client class name>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one name of the client class to be deleted. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv4 client class(es) deleted.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-class4\-get
.sp
This command fetches a selected DHCPv4 client class by name from the specified database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.9.10 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-class4\-get command \%<#\:command-remote-class4-get>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <client class name>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one name of the client class to be returned. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 client class found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <client class name>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                },
                <the rest of the client class information>
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The metadata is included in the returned shared network definition and provides the database\-specific information associated with the returned object.
.SS remote\-class4\-get\-all
.sp
This command fetches all DHCPv4 client classes for specified servers from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.9.10 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-class4\-get\-all command \%<#\:command-remote-class4-get-all>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is required for this command, and must not be empty.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 DHCPv4 client class(es) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                <first client class specification>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                }
            },
            {
                <second client class specification>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, ... ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a list of maps. Each map contains a client class name and the metadata, which provides database\-specific information associated with the client class.
.SS remote\-class4\-set
.sp
This command creates or replaces a DHCPv4 client class in the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.9.10 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-class4\-set command \%<#\:command-remote-class4-set>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-class4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-class\(dq: [
            {
                <client class specification>,
                \(dqfollow\-class\-name\(dq: <existing class name>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The provided list must contain exactly one client class specification. It may contain an optional parameter \(dqfollow\-class\-name\(dq which can specify an existing class name to indicate that the class from the command is placed right after this existing class in the hierarchy. This parameter can be omitted or set to \(dqnull\(dq to indicate that the new client class should be appended at the end of the hierarchy or an updated class should remain at the current position. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain one or more server tags as strings to explicitly associate the client class with one or more user\-defined servers. It may include the special server tag \(dqall\(dq to associate the class with all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 shared network successfully set.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <set client class name>
            }
        ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
