.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-global\-parameter4\-del
.sp
This command deletes a global DHCPv4 parameter from the configuration database. The server uses the value specified in the configuration file, or a default value if the parameter is not specified, after deleting the parameter from the database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-global\-parameter4\-del command \%<#\:command-remote-global-parameter4-del>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: [ <parameter name as string> ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <single server tag as string> ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command carries the list including exactly one name of the parameter to be deleted. If deleting a map parameter, the \fBmap\-name.parameter\-name\fP format must be used. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and it must contain exactly one server tag. Specifying an empty list, a value of \fBnull\fP, or multiple server tags will result in an error.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 global parameter(s) deleted.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-global\-parameter4\-get
.sp
This command fetches the selected global parameter for the server from the specified database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-global\-parameter4\-get command \%<#\:command-remote-global-parameter4-get>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: [ <parameter name as string> ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <single server tag as string> ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command carries a list including exactly one name of the parameter to be fetched. If retrieving a map parameter, the \fBmap\-name.parameter\-name\fP format must be used. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain exactly one server tag. Specifying an empty list, a value of \fBnull\fP, or multiple server tags will result in an error. The server tag \(dqall\(dq is allowed; it fetches the global parameter value shared by all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 global parameter found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            <parameter name>: <parameter value>,
            \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <server tag> ]
            }
        },
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a map with a global parameter name:value pair. The value may be a JSON string, integer, real, boolean, list of these scalar types or a map containing only one of these scalar types. The metadata is included and provides database\-specific information associated with the returned object. If the \(dqall\(dq server tag is specified, the command attempts to fetch the global parameter value associated with all servers. If the explicit server tag is specified, the command fetches the value associated with the given server. If the server\-specific value does not exist, the \fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\fP command fetches the value associated with all servers.
.SS remote\-global\-parameter4\-get\-all
.sp
This command fetches all global parameters for the server from the specified database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-global\-parameter4\-get\-all command \%<#\:command-remote-global-parameter4-get-all>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <single server tag as string> ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain exactly one server tag. Specifying an empty list, a value of \fBnull\fP, or multiple server tags will result in an error. The special server tag \(dqall\(dq is allowed; it fetches the global parameters shared by all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 global parameters found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: [
            {
                <first parameter name>: <first parameter value>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <server tag> ]
                }
            },
            {
                <second parameter name>: <second parameter value>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <server tag> ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a list of maps. Each map contains a global parameter name:value pair. The value may be a JSON string, integer, real, boolean, list of these scalar types or a map containing only one of these scalar types. The metadata is appended to each parameter and provides database\-specific information associated with the returned objects. If the server tag \(dqall\(dq is included in the command, the response contains the global parameters shared among all servers. It excludes server\-specific global parameters. If an explicit server tag is included in the command, the response contains all global parameters directly associated with the given server, and the global parameters associated with all servers when server\-specific values are not present.
.SS remote\-global\-parameter4\-set
.sp
This command creates or updates one or more global parameters in the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-global\-parameter4\-set command \%<#\:command-remote-global-parameter4-set>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-global\-parameter4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            <first parameter name>: <first parameter value>,
            <second parameter name>: <second parameter value>
        },
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <single server tag as string> ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command carries multiple global parameters with their values (including lists or maps with scalar parameters). Care should be taken when specifying more than one parameter; in some cases, only a subset of the parameters may be successfully stored in the database and other parameters may fail to be stored. In such cases the \fBremote\-global\-parameter4\-get\-all\fP command may be useful to verify the contents of the database after the update. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain exactly one server tag. Specifying an empty list, a value of \fBnull\fP, or multiple server tags will result in an error. The server tag \(dqall\(dq is allowed; it associates the specified parameters with all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 global parameter(s) successfully set.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqparameters\(dq: {
            <first parameter name>: <first parameter value>,
            <second parameter name>: <second parameter value>
        },
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
