.SS remote\-network4\-del
.sp
This command deletes an IPv4 shared network from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network4\-del command \%<#\:command-remote-network4-del>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <shared network name>
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnets\-action\(dq: <\(aqkeep\(aq | \(aqdelete\(aq>,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one name of the shared network to be deleted. The \fBsubnets\-action\fP parameter denotes whether the subnets in this shared network should be deleted. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 IPv4 shared network(s) deleted.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-network4\-get
.sp
This command fetches the selected IPv4 shared network for the server from the specified database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network4\-get command \%<#\:command-remote-network4-get>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <shared network name>
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnets\-include\(dq: <\(aqfull\(aq | \(aqno\(aq>,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one name of the shared network to be returned. The \fBsubnets\-include\fP optional parameter allows for specifying whether the subnets belonging to the shared network should also be returned. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 shared network found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <shared network name>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                },
                <the rest of the shared network information, potentially including subnets>
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the subnets are returned with the shared network, they are carried in the \fBsubnet4\fP list within the shared network definition. The metadata is included in the returned shared network definition and provides the database\-specific information associated with the returned object.
.SS remote\-network4\-list
.sp
This command fetches a list of all IPv4 shared networks from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network4\-list command \%<#\:command-remote-network4-list>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network4\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is required for this command, and must not be empty. It may either contain one or multiple server tags as strings, or a single \fBnull\fP value.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv4 shared network(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <first shared network name>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <second shared network name>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, ... ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains the list of maps. Each map contains the shared network name and the metadata, which provides database\-specific information associated with the shared network. The returned list does not contain full definitions of the shared networks; use \fBremote\-network4\-get\fP to fetch the full information about the selected shared networks. If the command includes explicit server tags as strings (including the special server tag \(dqall\(dq), the list contains all shared networks which are associated with any of the specified tags. A network is returned even if it is associated with multiple servers and only one of the specified tags matches. If the command includes the \fBnull\fP value in the \fBserver\-tags\fP list, the response contains all shared networks which are assigned to no servers (unassigned).
.SS remote\-network4\-set
.sp
This command creates or replaces an IPv4 shared network in the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network4\-set command \%<#\:command-remote-network4-set>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network4\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                <shared network specification excluding subnets list>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The provided list must contain exactly one shared network specification, and must not contain subnets (the \(dqsubnet4\(dq parameter). The subnets are added to the shared network using the \fBremote\-subnet4\-set\fP command. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain one or more server tags as strings to explicitly associate the shared network with one or more user\-defined servers. It may include the special server tag \(dqall\(dq to associate the network with all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv4 shared network successfully set.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-network6\-del
.sp
This command deletes an IPv6 shared network from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network6\-del command \%<#\:command-remote-network6-del>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network6\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <shared network name>
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnets\-action\(dq: <\(aqkeep\(aq | \(aqdelete\(aq>,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one name of the shared network to be deleted. The \fBsubnets\-action\fP parameter indicates whether the subnets in this shared network should be deleted. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 IPv6 shared network(s) deleted.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-network6\-get
.sp
This command fetches the selected IPv6 shared network for the server from the specified database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network6\-get command \%<#\:command-remote-network6-get>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network6\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <shared network name>
            }
        ],
        \(dqsubnets\-include\(dq: <\(aqfull\(aq | \(aqno\(aq>,
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one name of the shared network to be returned. The \fBsubnets\-include\fP optional parameter allows for specifying whether the subnets belonging to the shared network should also be returned. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 shared network found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <shared network name>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                },
                <the rest of the shared network information, potentially including subnets>
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the subnets are returned with the shared network, they are carried in the \fBsubnet6\fP list within the shared network definition. The metadata is included in the returned shared network definition and provides the database\-specific information associated with the returned object.
.SS remote\-network6\-list
.sp
This command fetches a list of all IPv6 shared networks from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network6\-list command \%<#\:command-remote-network6-list>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network6\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is required for this command, and must not be empty. It may either contain one or multiple server tags as strings, or a single \fBnull\fP value.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv6 shared network(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <first shared network name>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqname\(dq: <second shared network name>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, ... ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains the list of maps. Each map contains the shared network name and the metadata, which provides database\-specific information associated with the shared network. The returned list does not contain full definitions of the shared networks; use \fBremote\-network6\-get\fP to fetch the full information about the selected shared networks. If the command includes explicit server tags as strings (including the special server tag \(dqall\(dq), the list contains all shared networks which are associated with any of the specified tags. A network is returned even if it is associated with multiple servers and only one of the specified tags matches. If the command includes the \fBnull\fP value in the \fBserver\-tags\fP list, the response contains all shared networks which are assigned to no servers (unassigned).
.SS remote\-network6\-set
.sp
This command creates or replaces an IPv6 shared network in the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-network6\-set command \%<#\:command-remote-network6-set>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-network6\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqshared\-networks\(dq: [
            {
                <shared network specification excluding subnets list>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The provided list must contain exactly one shared network specification, and must not contain subnets (the \(dqsubnet6\(dq parameter). The subnets are added to the shared network using the \fBremote\-subnet6\-set\fP command. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain one or more server tags as strings to explicitly associate the shared network with one or more user\-defined servers. It may include the special server tag \(dqall\(dq to associate the network with all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 shared network successfully set.\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-option\-def4\-del
.sp
This command deletes a DHCPv4 option definition from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-option\-def4\-del command \%<#\:command-remote-option-def4-del>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def4\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [ {
            \(dqcode\(dq: <option code>,
            \(dqspace\(dq: <option space>,
            \(dqforce\(dq: false
        } ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <single server tag as string> ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one option definition specification, comprising an option name and code. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain exactly one server tag. Specifying an empty list, a value of \fBnull\fP, or multiple server tags will result in an error. As of Kea 3.1.2, before deleting an option definition, the server will first check if there are any options specified that depend upon that definition. If so the delete command will be rejected with an error message explaining why.  This default behavior may be overridden by setting the optional \fBforce\fP parameter to true.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 DHCPv4 option definition(s) deleted.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-option\-def4\-get
.sp
This command fetches a DHCPv4 option definition from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-option\-def4\-get command \%<#\:command-remote-option-def4-get>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def4\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqcode\(dq: <option code>,
                \(dqspace\(dq: <option space>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <single server tag as string> ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The desired option definition is identified by the pair of option code/space values. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain exactly one server tag. Specifying an empty list, a value of \fBnull\fP, or multiple server tags will result in an error. The server tag \(dqall\(dq is allowed, to fetch the option definition instance shared by all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqDHCPv4 option definition found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                <option definition>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <server tag> ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The metadata is included and provides database\-specific information associated with the returned object. If the \(dqall\(dq server tag is specified, the command attempts to fetch the option definition associated with all servers. If the explicit server tag is specified, the command fetches the option definition associated with the given server. If the server\-specific option definition does not exist, the \fBremote\-option\-def4\-get\fP command fetches the option definition associated with all servers.
.SS remote\-option\-def4\-get\-all
.sp
This command fetches all DHCPv4 option definitions from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-option\-def4\-get\-all command \%<#\:command-remote-option-def4-get-all>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-option\-def4\-get\-all\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <single server tag as string> ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain exactly one server tag. Specifying an empty list, a value of \fBnull\fP, or multiple server tags will result in an error. The special server tag \(dqall\(dq is allowed, to fetch the option definitions shared by all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 DHCPv4 option definition(s) found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqoption\-defs\(dq: [
            {
                <first option definition>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <server tag> ]
                }
            },
            {
                <second option definition>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <server tag> ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a list of maps. Each map contains an option definition specification and the metadata, including database\-specific information associated with the returned objects. If the server tag \(dqall\(dq is included in the command, the response contains the option definitions shared among all servers. It excludes server\-specific option definitions. If an explicit server tag is included in the command, the response contains all option definitions directly associated with the given server, and the option definitions associated with all servers when server\-specific option definitions are not present.
