3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-subnet6\-del\-by\-prefix
.sp
This command deletes an IPv6 subnet by prefix from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-subnet6\-del\-by\-prefix command \%<#\:command-remote-subnet6-del-by-prefix>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet6\-del\-by\-prefix\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: <subnet prefix>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one prefix of the subnet to be deleted. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq1 IPv6 subnet(s) deleted.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS remote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-id
.sp
This command fetches the selected IPv6 subnet by ID from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-id command \%<#\:command-remote-subnet6-get-by-id>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-id\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: <subnet identifier>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one ID of the subnet to be returned. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: <subnet identifier>,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: <subnet prefix>,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: <shared network name or null>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                },
                <the rest of the subnet specification here>
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the shared network name is null, it means that the returned subnet does not belong to any shared network (a global subnet). The metadata is included in the returned subnet definition and provides database\-specific information associated with the returned object.
.SS remote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-prefix
.sp
This command fetches the selected IPv6 subnet by prefix from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-prefix command \%<#\:command-remote-subnet6-get-by-prefix>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet6\-get\-by\-prefix\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: <subnet prefix>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        }
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
This command includes a list with exactly one prefix of the subnet to be returned. The \fBserver\-tags\fP parameter must not be specified for this command.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [ {
            \(dqid\(dq: <subnet identifier>,
            \(dqsubnet\(dq: <subnet prefix>,
            \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: <shared network name or null>,
            \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
            },
            <the rest of the subnet specification here>
        } ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 1
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
If the shared network name is null, it means that the returned subnet does not belong to any shared network (global subnet). The metadata is included in the returned subnet definition and provides database\-specific information associated with the returned object.
.SS remote\-subnet6\-list
.sp
This command fetches a list of all IPv6 subnets from the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-subnet6\-list command \%<#\:command-remote-subnet6-list>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet6\-list\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is required for this command, and must not be empty. It may either contain one or multiple server tags as strings, or a single \fBnull\fP value.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq2 IPv6 subnets found.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: <first subnet identifier>,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: <first subnet prefix>,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: <shared network name or null>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
                }
            },
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: <second subnet identifier>,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: <second subnet prefix>,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: <shared network name or null>,
                \(dqmetadata\(dq: {
                    \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, ... ]
                }
            }
        ],
        \(dqcount\(dq: 2
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The returned response contains a list of maps. Each map contains a subnet identifier, prefix, and shared network name to which the subnet belongs. If the subnet does not belong to a shared network, the name is null. The metadata includes database\-specific information associated with the subnets. The returned list does not contain full subnet definitions; use \fBremote\-subnet6\-get\fP to fetch the full information about the selected subnets. If the command includes explicit server tags as strings (including the special server tag \(dqall\(dq), the list contains all subnets which are associated with any of the specified tags. A subnet is returned even if it is associated with multiple servers and only one of the specified tags matches. If the command includes the \fBnull\fP value in the \fBserver\-tags\fP list, the response contains all subnets which are assigned to no servers (unassigned).
.SS remote\-subnet6\-set
.sp
This command creates or replaces an IPv6 subnet in the configuration database.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.6.0 (cb_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see remote\-subnet6\-set command \%<#\:command-remote-subnet6-set>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqremote\-subnet6\-set\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnets\(dq: [
            {
                \(dqid\(dq: <subnet identifier>,
                \(dqsubnet\(dq: <subnet prefix>,
                \(dqshared\-network\-name\(dq: <shared network name or null>,
                <the rest of the subnet specification here>
            }
        ],
        \(dqremote\(dq: {
            <specification of the database to connect to>
        },
        \(dqserver\-tags\(dq: [ <first server tag>, <second server tag>, ... ]
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The provided list must contain exactly one subnet specification. The \fBshared\-network\-name\fP parameter is required for these commands; it associates the subnet with the shared network by its name. If the subnet must not belong to any shared network (a global subnet), the \fBnull\fP value must be specified for the shared network name. The \fBserver\-tags\fP list is mandatory and must contain one or more server tags as strings to explicitly associate the subnet with one or more user\-defined servers. The \fBremote\-subnet6\-set\fP command may include the special server tag \(dqall\(dq to associate the subnet with all servers.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: 0,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dqIPv6 subnet successfully set.\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqid\(dq: <subnet identifier>,
        \(dqsubnet\(dq: <subnet prefix>
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS reservation\-add
.sp
This command adds a new host reservation. The reservation may include IPv4 addresses, IPv6 addresses, IPv6 prefixes, various identifiers, a class the client will be assigned to, DHCPv4 and DHCPv6 options, and more.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.2.0 (host_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see reservation\-add command \%<#\:command-reservation-add>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-add\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqreservation\(dq: {
            \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqclient\-id\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqcircuit\-id\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqduid\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqflex\-id\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqip\-address\(dq: <string (IPv4 address)>,
            \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [ <comma\-separated strings> ],
            \(dqhw\-address\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqhostname\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqnext\-server\(dq: <string (IPv4 address)>,
            \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ <comma\-separated structures defining options> ],
            \(dqprefixes\(dq: [ <comma\-separated IPv6 prefixes> ],
            \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ <comma\-separated strings> ],
            \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: <string>,
            \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: <integer>,
            \(dquser\-context\(dq: <any valid JSON>
        },
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: <string (memory, database, all, default)>
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Note that boot\-file\-name, circuit\-id, client\-id, ip\-address, next\-server, and server\-hostname are IPv4\-specific. ip\-addresses, and prefixes are IPv6\-specific. Operation\-target is optional; default is \(aqalternate\(aq.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: <integer>,
    \(dqtext\(dq: <string>
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS reservation\-del
.sp
This command deletes an existing host reservation.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.2.0 (host_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: write \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see reservation\-del command \%<#\:command-reservation-del>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-del\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: <integer>,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: <one of \(aqhw\-address\(aq, \(aqduid\(aq, \(aqcircuit\-id\(aq, \(aqclient\-id\(aq and \(aqflex\-id\(aq>,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: <string (memory, database, all, default)>
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The host reservation can be identified by either the (subnet\-id, ip\-address) pair or a triplet of (subnet\-id, identifier\-type, identifier). Operation\-target is optional; default is \(aqalternate\(aq.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: <integer>,
    \(dqtext\(dq: \(dq<string>\(dq
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Result is an integer representation of the status. Currently supported statuses are:
.INDENT 0.0
.IP \(bu 2
0 \- success
.IP \(bu 2
1 \- error
.IP \(bu 2
2 \- unsupported
.IP \(bu 2
3 \- empty (command was completed successfully, but no data was affected or returned)
.IP \(bu 2
4 \- conflict (command could not apply requested configuration changes because they were in conflict with the server state)
.UNINDENT
.SS reservation\-get
.sp
This command retrieves an existing host reservation.
.sp
Supported by: kea\-dhcp4, kea\-dhcp6
.sp
Availability: 1.2.0 (host_cmds hook library)
.sp
Access: read \fI(parameter ignored in this Kea version)\fP
.sp
Description and examples: see reservation\-get command \%<#\:command-reservation-get>
.sp
Command syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqcommand\(dq: \(dqreservation\-get\(dq,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: <integer>,
        \(dqidentifier\-type\(dq: <one of \(aqhw\-address\(aq, \(aqduid\(aq, \(aqcircuit\-id\(aq, \(aqclient\-id\(aq and \(aqflex\-id\(aq>,
        \(dqidentifier\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqoperation\-target\(dq: <string (memory, database, all, default)>
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The host reservation can be identified by either the (subnet\-id, ip\-address) pair or a triplet of (subnet\-id, identifier\-type, identifier). Operation\-target is optional; default is \(aqall\(aq.
.sp
Response syntax:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
{
    \(dqresult\(dq: <integer>,
    \(dqtext\(dq: <string>,
    \(dqarguments\(dq: {
        \(dqboot\-file\-name\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqcomment\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqclient\-id\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqcircuit\-id\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqduid\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqflex\-id\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqip\-address\(dq: <string (IPv4 address)>,
        \(dqip\-addresses\(dq: [ <comma\-separated strings> ],
        \(dqhw\-address\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqhostname\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqnext\-server\(dq: <string (IPv4 address)>,
        \(dqoption\-data\(dq: [ <comma\-separated structures defining options> ],
        \(dqprefixes\(dq: [ <comma\-separated IPv6 prefixes> ],
        \(dqclient\-classes\(dq: [ <comma\-separated strings> ],
        \(dqserver\-hostname\(dq: <string>,
        \(dqsubnet\-id\(dq: <integer>,
        \(dquser\-context\(dq: <any valid JSON>
    }
}
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
The arguments object appears only if a host is found. Many fields in the arguments object appear only if a specific field is set.
.SS reservation\-get\-all
.sp
This command retrieves all host reservations for a specified subnet.
